research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37464, 'Preservice teachers infer that a constant difference relationship is a directly proportional relationship based on linguistic associations with the phrase ''same rate'' and a cluster of related resources.', 'Chip states that the number of dumplings made by Allie and Sam are staying in a ''proportional ratio'' because they work at the same rate, despite recognizing Allie has a 2-dumpling head start.', 'proportional', 'distinguishing proportional from nonproportional relationships', 'constant difference relationships', 'PST', 'Grades 4-8', 'JRME_Izsak_2017_Preservice', 'Andrew Izsák and Erik Jacobson', '2017', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'primary', '317, 318, 319', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Izsak_2017_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37465, 'Preservice teachers apply direct proportion algorithms to situations describing an inversely proportional relationship.', 'Chip sets up the proportion 4/12 = 6/x and concludes 6 workers would take 18 minutes, temporarily ignoring that more workers should take less time.', 'proportional', 'inversely proportional relationships', 'constant product relationships', 'PST', 'Grades 4-8', 'JRME_Izsak_2017_Preservice', 'Andrew Izsák and Erik Jacobson', '2017', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'primary', '319, 320, 321', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Izsak_2017_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37466, 'Preservice teachers assume a ratio of two quantities will be constant simply because the elements that define their additive relationship remain constant.', 'Lisa writes the expression (x+5)/y and asserts the ratio is constant because the head start (+5) and the rate they add dumplings are constant.', 'proportional', 'distinguishing proportional from nonproportional relationships', 'constant difference relationships', 'PST', 'Grades 4-8', 'JRME_Izsak_2017_Preservice', 'Andrew Izsák and Erik Jacobson', '2017', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'primary', '324, 325, 326', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Izsak_2017_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37467, 'Preservice teachers struggle to consistently apply multiplicative relationships when analyzing inverse proportions, instead coordinating additive changes in one quantity with multiplicative changes in another.', 'Tess considers whether the number of minutes is halved when the number of workers increases additively from 2 to 3, confusing it with the multiplicative doubling from 2 to 4 workers.', 'proportional', 'inversely proportional relationships', 'multiplicative comparisons', 'PST', 'Grades 4-8', 'JRME_Izsak_2017_Preservice', 'Andrew Izsák and Erik Jacobson', '2017', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'secondary', '327, 328', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Izsak_2017_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37468, 'Teachers inaccurately evaluated a student''s erroneous additive step as correct because they lacked quantitative meaning for the scalar factor, interpreting it instead as an amount of a physical quantity.', 'Diane interpreted the scalar factor 5 as ''how many milliliters of orange that he would need'' and incorrectly accepted the student''s addition of 5 + 1 1/4 by concluding ''it''s six and one-fourth if you mix them together.''', 'proportional', 'proportional reasoning', 'multiplicative comparison', 'in_service', 'Middle school', 'IJSME_Jacobson_2018_Middle', 'Erik Jacobson & Joanne Lobato & Chandra H. Orrill', '2018', 'Int J of Sci and Math Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '1552, 1553, 1554', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Jacobson_2018_Middle.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37469, 'Students incorrectly added a scalar multiplier to a quantity instead of multiplying it, resulting in a correct value but an inaccurate quantitative relationship.', 'Thomas formed a multiplicative comparison (50/10 = 5) but then inaccurately added 5 + 1 1/4 to arrive at the answer 6 1/4.', 'proportional', 'proportional reasoning', 'multiplicative comparison', 'in_service', 'Middle school', 'IJSME_Jacobson_2018_Middle', 'Erik Jacobson & Joanne Lobato & Chandra H. Orrill', '2018', 'Int J of Sci and Math Educ', 'uncoded', 'primary', '1547, 1548', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Jacobson_2018_Middle.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37470, 'Students rely almost exclusively on standard rounding to the closest leading power of 10, selecting choices that reflect this procedure even when it yields an incorrect or less accurate estimate.', 'When estimating 98 x 2.62, a large percentage of students chose 100 x 3, which resulted from standard rounding, rather than the closer estimate of 100 x 2.5.', 'whole_number', 'computational estimation', 'standard rounding', 'student', 'grades five through eight', 'JRME_Schoen_1990_Outcomes', 'Harold L. Schoen, Glendon Blume, and H. D. Hoover', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '66, 67, 72', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Schoen_1990_Outcomes.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37471, 'When estimating sums of mixed numbers, students initially ignore the fractional parts and operate only with the whole numbers, demonstrating a weak understanding of the sizes of fractions.', 'For the problem 7 1/10 + 3 2/3 + 1 1/5, many students added the whole numbers 7, 3, and 1 to get 11, and either chose 11 as their answer or overcompensated and incorrectly chose 12 1/2.', 'fraction', 'estimation', 'mixed numbers', 'student', 'grades five through eight', 'JRME_Schoen_1990_Outcomes', 'Harold L. Schoen, Glendon Blume, and H. D. Hoover', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '68, 71', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Schoen_1990_Outcomes.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37472, 'When attempting to compensate for standard rounding, students often confuse the direction of their adjustments, leading them to compensate in the wrong direction.', 'One student rounded numbers to 5 + 14 and then, confusing the direction, compensated up because both numbers had been rounded up.', 'whole_number', 'estimation', 'compensation', 'student', 'grades five through eight', 'JRME_Schoen_1990_Outcomes', 'Harold L. Schoen, Glendon Blume, and H. D. Hoover', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '69, 70', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Schoen_1990_Outcomes.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37473, 'Students fail to encode the overarching conceptual or methodological ideas of a lecture because they prioritize copying formal proofs written on the blackboard and ignore statements made only verbally.', 'Students did not recall the main idea that proving a sequence is Cauchy shows it converges without knowing the limit, because the professor stated this verbally but did not write it on the blackboard.', 'calculus', 'proof comprehension', 'lecture encoding and note-taking', 'mixed', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Lew_2016_Lectures', 'Kristen Lew, Timothy Patrick Fukawa-Connelly, Juan Pablo Mejia-Ramos, and Keith Weber', '2016', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '179, 180', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lew_2016_Lectures.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37474, 'Students misinterpret the colloquial mathematical use of the word ''small'' in real analysis, viewing it as meaning uncomplicated or short in length rather than referring to a quantity with an arbitrarily small magnitude.', 'When asked about a ''toolbox'' of ideas to prove things are ''small'', one student thought ''small'' meant the expressions were simplified or not complicated, rather than referring to bounded magnitudes.', 'calculus', 'limits and bounds', 'colloquial mathematics', 'mixed', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Lew_2016_Lectures', 'Kristen Lew, Timothy Patrick Fukawa-Connelly, Juan Pablo Mejia-Ramos, and Keith Weber', '2016', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '182, 183', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lew_2016_Lectures.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37475, 'Students view proofs primarily as a series of sequential algebraic calculations, causing them to miss the professor''s explanation of the overarching holistic method being applied.', 'When watching a video clip where the professor explicitly laid out a plan to represent a difference as a sum of consecutive terms to show it is small, students described the clip merely as being about algebraic manipulations.', 'calculus', 'proof comprehension', 'holistic method versus calculation', 'mixed', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Lew_2016_Lectures', 'Kristen Lew, Timothy Patrick Fukawa-Connelly, Juan Pablo Mejia-Ramos, and Keith Weber', '2016', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '183, 184', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lew_2016_Lectures.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37476, 'Students confuse the total fractional amount of a partitioned quantity with the number of discrete pieces it has been cut into, struggling to conceptualize quantity without referring to the number of slices.', 'When asked how much of the total amount of pizza one person ate, a student replied ''Eight slices'' and, when pressed to describe the amount without talking about slices, could only say ''Eight, um, wedges, triangles like?''', 'fraction', 'fractional amount', 'distinguishing amount from number of pieces', 'student', 'grades four through eight', 'JRME_Lamon_1996_Development', 'Susan J. Lamon', '1996', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '183', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lamon_1996_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37477, 'Students fail to conceptualize a composite unit as a single entity, requiring perceptual support by drawing and counting individual constituent items to assure equal sharing.', 'A student drew all individual sticks of gum inside packages because she felt she had to ''count what''s inside'' to ensure equal shares, rather than treating the pack as a composite unit of five.', 'fraction', 'unitizing', 'composite units', 'student', 'grades four through eight', 'JRME_Lamon_1996_Development', 'Susan J. Lamon', '1996', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '184', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lamon_1996_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37478, 'Students do not attach the proper reference unit to their fractional results, confusing a fraction of the total combined amount with a fraction of a single constituent unit.', 'After sharing four pizzas among three people, a student stated each person would receive 1 1/3 of the total amount of pizza, instead of recognizing that they receive 1 1/3 pizzas, which is 1/3 of the total original amount.', 'fraction', 'referent wholes', 'coordinating fractional amounts', 'student', 'grades four through eight', 'JRME_Lamon_1996_Development', 'Susan J. Lamon', '1996', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '190', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lamon_1996_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37479, 'Students encounter a discrepancy between the precise results of their school-taught calculations and the rounded figures presented on an everyday artifact, causing them to question the authority of the school calculation.', 'Students calculated their average daily consumption as 839 liters, but the water company (COPASA) rounded it to 840 on the bill, leading students to state their results did not match.', 'proportional', 'averages', 'rounding and precision', 'mixed', 'seventh-grade', 'JRME_Tomaz_2015_How', 'Vanessa Sena Tomaz and Maria Manuela David', '2015', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '472, 473', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Tomaz_2015_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37480, 'When calculating a per-person average, students reject the simplified school assumption of dividing by resident family members, instead proposing complex estimations based on the proportional time various individuals spend in the home.', 'A student argued that her brother only comes for the weekend and a maid works on Mondays, asking how to realistically account for people who do not stay home for equal amounts of time.', 'proportional', 'averages', 'determining the denominator', 'mixed', 'seventh-grade', 'JRME_Tomaz_2015_How', 'Vanessa Sena Tomaz and Maria Manuela David', '2015', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '472, 474, 475', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Tomaz_2015_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37481, 'Students struggle to set up the standard rule of three algorithm when a situated problem presents multiple ambiguous magnitudes, intermediate results, and units to choose from.', 'When the teacher asked ''In what unit?'' regarding daily water consumption per person, students proposed money, days, and people, instead of liters, leading to confusion about which magnitudes to compare.', 'proportional', 'rule of three', 'identifying magnitudes and units', 'mixed', 'seventh-grade', 'JRME_Tomaz_2015_How', 'Vanessa Sena Tomaz and Maria Manuela David', '2015', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '474, 476, 477', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Tomaz_2015_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37482, 'Students and teachers experience a contradiction between the generic, algorithmic rule of three taught in school and the intuitive or specific mathematical procedures required to authentically solve everyday proportional situations.', 'When proposing new ways to save water, such as using a glass instead of a running tap, students realized the standard rule of three procedure could not be directly applied without modifying the problem''s initial conditions.', 'proportional', 'rule of three', 'algorithmic versus situated procedures', 'mixed', 'seventh-grade', 'JRME_Tomaz_2015_How', 'Vanessa Sena Tomaz and Maria Manuela David', '2015', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '479, 480, 481, 484, 486', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Tomaz_2015_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37483, 'Students assume the operator in a multiplication problem must be a whole number, leading to errors when the operator is a decimal because it violates the tacit repeated addition model.', 'When given the problem ''1 m of suit fabric costs 15 000 lire. How much does 0.75 m cost?'', students frequently incorrectly chose 15 000 ÷ 0.75 or 15 000 + 0.75 instead of 15 000 x 0.75.', 'decimal', 'multiplication', 'decimal operators', 'student', 'Grades 5, 7, and 9', 'JRME_Fischbein_1985_Role', 'Efraim Fischbein, Maria Deri, Maria Sainati Nello and Maria Sciolis Marino', '1985', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '6, 10, 11, 14', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Fischbein_1985_Role.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37484, 'Students assume that in division, the dividend must be larger than the divisor, often reversing the numbers when the divisor is larger to satisfy this partitive model constraint.', 'When given a partitive division problem like ''15 friends together bought 5 kg of cookies. How much did each one get?'', most students incorrectly chose 15 ÷ 5 rather than 5 ÷ 15.', 'whole_number', 'division', 'partitive division', 'student', 'Grades 5, 7, and 9', 'JRME_Fischbein_1985_Role', 'Efraim Fischbein, Maria Deri, Maria Sainati Nello and Maria Sciolis Marino', '1985', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '7, 11, 12', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Fischbein_1985_Role.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37485, 'Students fail to identify division as the appropriate operation in partitive contexts when the divisor is a decimal, as it violates the intuitive rule that the divisor must be a whole number.', 'For the problem ''I spent 900 lire for 0.75 hg of cocoa. What is the price of 1 hg?'', students frequently incorrectly chose 900 x 0.75 instead of 900 ÷ 0.75.', 'decimal', 'division', 'decimal divisors', 'student', 'Grades 5, 7, and 9', 'JRME_Fischbein_1985_Role', 'Efraim Fischbein, Maria Deri, Maria Sainati Nello and Maria Sciolis Marino', '1985', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '7, 12, 13', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Fischbein_1985_Role.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37486, 'Students misplace addition and multiplication operations when attempting to reconstruct the fraction addition algorithm, such as multiplying the numerators and adding the denominators.', '1/2 + 2/3 = 2/5; 1/2 + 1/4 = 1/6', 'fraction', 'fraction addition', 'algorithm execution', 'student', 'ages 13-15', 'JRME_Vinner_1981_Some', 'Shlomo Vinner, Rina Hershkowitz and Maxim Bruckheimer', '1981', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '71, 72', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Vinner_1981_Some.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37487, 'Students apply the multiplication algorithm to an addition problem, or naturally assume that they should add numerators to numerators and denominators to denominators based on the plus sign.', '1/2 + 2/3 = 3/5; 1/2 + 1/4 = 2/6', 'fraction', 'fraction addition', 'algorithm execution', 'student', 'ages 13-15', 'JRME_Vinner_1981_Some', 'Shlomo Vinner, Rina Hershkowitz and Maxim Bruckheimer', '1981', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '71, 72', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Vinner_1981_Some.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37488, 'Students substitute the formal representation of a common fraction line in place of actually finding a common denominator, simply writing the sum of the numerators over the sum of the denominators.', '1/2 + 2/3 = (1 + 2)/5', 'fraction', 'fraction addition', 'common denominator', 'student', 'ages 13-15', 'JRME_Vinner_1981_Some', 'Shlomo Vinner, Rina Hershkowitz and Maxim Bruckheimer', '1981', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '73', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Vinner_1981_Some.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37489, 'Students correctly apply the idea of finding a common denominator but incorrectly generate equivalent fractions, such as by adding the same number to the numerator and denominator instead of multiplying.', '1/2 + 2/3 = (4 + 4)/5', 'fraction', 'fraction addition', 'equivalent fractions', 'student', 'ages 13-15', 'JRME_Vinner_1981_Some', 'Shlomo Vinner, Rina Hershkowitz and Maxim Bruckheimer', '1981', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '74', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Vinner_1981_Some.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37490, 'A student claims that the number six can be both even and odd because it is composed of an odd number of groups of two.', 'Sean asserts that six is odd and even because it is made of three twos, and three is an odd number.', 'whole_number', 'parity', 'multiples and composition', 'student', 'third grade', 'JRME_Ball_2008_Making', 'Deborah Loewenberg Ball, Jennifer Lewis and Mark Hoover Thames', '2008', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'productive', 'primary', '13, 14, 25, 29, 39, 41, 199, 200', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Ball_2008_Making.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37491, 'A student argues that zero cannot be an even number because there are no two identical whole numbers that can be put together to make it, while another argues it must be even based on its position between odd numbers.', 'Sheena argues zero could be even because the numbers on each side (1 and -1) are odd. Sean argues zero is not even because ''what two things can you put together to make it?''', 'whole_number', 'parity', 'zero', 'student', 'third grade', 'JRME_Ball_2008_Making', 'Deborah Loewenberg Ball, Jennifer Lewis and Mark Hoover Thames', '2008', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'productive', 'primary', '24, 26, 38, 39, 196', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Ball_2008_Making.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37492, 'A student claims zero is ''special'' and exempt from being even or odd because, unlike other even numbers, adding zero to itself does not produce a larger, new even number.', 'Nathan claims zero is special because ''even numbers make even numbers'' like 2+2=4 and 4+4=8, implying zero doesn''t follow this generative pattern in the same way.', 'whole_number', 'parity', 'zero', 'student', 'third grade', 'JRME_Ball_2008_Making', 'Deborah Loewenberg Ball, Jennifer Lewis and Mark Hoover Thames', '2008', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'productive', 'primary', '28, 29, 34, 35, 36, 197', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Ball_2008_Making.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37493, 'Children incorrectly believe that the average of a set of values can be greater than the maximum value in the set, reasoning that pooling the individual amounts creates a large total that can be distributed to yield a higher amount for everyone.', 'When told children brought at most 3 potatoes each to a party, some students incorrectly judged that the average could be 4 potatoes, reasoning that adding up the small amounts creates a pile large enough for everyone to get more than 3.', 'statistics', 'arithmetic average', 'location between extremes', 'student', 'ages 8, 10, 12, and 14', 'JRME_Strauss_1988_Development', 'Sidney Strauss and Efraim Bichler', '1988', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '33, 40', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Strauss_1988_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37494, 'Children interpret the representative value (average) of a distribution as a categorical label for the group, often selecting the largest value in the set for social reasons rather than mathematical representativeness.', 'In a task where children brought 5, 10, 20, and 25 pieces of candy, some children answered that 25 pieces best represents the group ''because they brought a lot of candy since they were concerned that everyone would have a lot of candy.''', 'statistics', 'arithmetic average', 'representativeness', 'student', 'ages 8, 10, 12, and 14', 'JRME_Strauss_1988_Development', 'Sidney Strauss and Efraim Bichler', '1988', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '44, 45', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Strauss_1988_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37495, 'Students possess intuitive, concretely based models of operations leading to the incorrect conceptions that multiplication always makes a number bigger, division always makes a number smaller, and dividing a smaller number by a larger number is not permissible.', '', 'rational', 'multiplication and division', 'word problems', 'student', 'seventh-grade', 'JRME_Sellke_1991_Using', 'Donald H. Sellke, Merlyn J. Behr and Alan M. Voelker', '1991', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '30, 31', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Sellke_1991_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37496, 'Students inappropriately apply a figurative rule by simply concatenating numerals in the order they appear in a word problem rather than using place value to form a number.', 'When asked ''What number do 12 ones and 3 tens make?'', a student answers ''123'' by merely concatenating the 12 and 3.', 'whole_number', 'numeration', 'place value', 'mixed', 'second-grade', 'JRME_Cobb_1991_Assessment', 'Paul Cobb, Terry Wood, Erna Yackel, John Nicholls, Grayson Wheatley, Beatriz Trigatti and Marcella Perlwitz', '1991', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '16', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Cobb_1991_Assessment.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37497, 'Students interpret a missing-addend task based on the format of preceding addition tasks, mistakenly adding the given numbers together rather than finding the missing part.', 'When solving the missing-addend task 29 + _ = 51 immediately after two standard addition tasks, a student adds 29 and 51 to get an answer of 70 or greater.', 'whole_number', 'addition', 'missing addend', 'mixed', 'second-grade', 'JRME_Cobb_1991_Assessment', 'Paul Cobb, Terry Wood, Erna Yackel, John Nicholls, Grayson Wheatley, Beatriz Trigatti and Marcella Perlwitz', '1991', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '16', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Cobb_1991_Assessment.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37498, 'Students view vertical column computation as a separate mathematical context unrelated to horizontal formatting, exhibiting format dependency and failing to apply algorithms flexibly.', 'Students reliably use standard regrouping algorithms for tasks in a vertical column format but fail to apply them to identical tasks presented as horizontal sentences.', 'whole_number', 'computation', 'addition and subtraction', 'mixed', 'second-grade', 'JRME_Cobb_1991_Assessment', 'Paul Cobb, Terry Wood, Erna Yackel, John Nicholls, Grayson Wheatley, Beatriz Trigatti and Marcella Perlwitz', '1991', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '22', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Cobb_1991_Assessment.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37499, 'Students believe there is a finite number of decimals between two given decimal numbers, often counting sequentially by the last digit or asserting no numbers exist between them.', 'When asked how many decimals are between 1.42 and 1.43, a student responded, ''There are nine; 1.421, 1.422 ... and 1.429.'' Another stated, ''The number following 1.42 is 1.43. Therefore, there are no decimal numbers between 1.42 and 1.43.''', 'decimal', 'density', 'number magnitude', 'student', 'sixth- and eighth-grade', 'JRME_Reys_1998_Relationship', 'Robert E. Reys and Der-Ching Yang', '1998', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '232', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Reys_1998_Relationship.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37500, 'Students believe there are no fractions between two given fractions with consecutive numerators, treating fraction sequences like whole number counting.', 'A student reasoned, ''The difference between 2 and 3 is 1. Then the fraction after 2/5 is 3/5. Therefore, there is no fraction between 2/5 and 3/5.''', 'fraction', 'density', 'number magnitude', 'student', 'sixth- and eighth-grade', 'JRME_Reys_1998_Relationship', 'Robert E. Reys and Der-Ching Yang', '1998', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '233', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Reys_1998_Relationship.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37501, 'Students consider fractions and decimals as separate, unrelated entities when ordering them, failing to establish meaningful connections between the representations.', 'When asked to order fractions and decimals, a student stated, ''I don''t know how to compare fractions and decimals. I had learned the decimals and fractions in mathematics class, but I didn''t understand it.''', 'rational', 'ordering', 'comparing fractions and decimals', 'student', 'sixth- and eighth-grade', 'JRME_Reys_1998_Relationship', 'Robert E. Reys and Der-Ching Yang', '1998', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '233, 235, 236', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Reys_1998_Relationship.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37502, 'Students incorrectly assume that multiplication always results in a larger product, regardless of whether the multiplier is a decimal less than one.', 'A student explained, ''Since a number is multiplied by another number, the result is larger. Therefore, 72 x 0.46 is greater than 36.''', 'decimal', 'multiplication', 'relative effects of operations', 'student', 'sixth- and eighth-grade', 'JRME_Reys_1998_Relationship', 'Robert E. Reys and Der-Ching Yang', '1998', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '233', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Reys_1998_Relationship.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37503, 'Students blindly apply rule-based algorithms to place decimal points in products without using estimation or number sense to check for reasonableness.', 'When given that the digits 291357 represent the product of 534.6 and 0.545, 87% of sixth graders answered 29.1357 instead of estimating that half of 534 is around 250.', 'decimal', 'multiplication', 'decimal placement', 'student', 'sixth- and eighth-grade', 'JRME_Reys_1998_Relationship', 'Robert E. Reys and Der-Ching Yang', '1998', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '231', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Reys_1998_Relationship.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37504, 'Students alter the goals of applied problems by imposing unstated restrictions, driven by unproductive beliefs such as the assumption that all given numbers must be used in a math word problem.', 'A student assumed that the character in the problem would not want to buy boards of only one length, prompted by the belief that in math word problems one must use all the numbers.', 'other', 'problem solving', 'unproductive beliefs', 'student', 'seventh-grade', 'JRME_Hart_1993_Some', 'Lynn C. Hart', '1993', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '169', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hart_1993_Some.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37505, 'When lacking a real-world experiential framework for a problem, students invent arbitrary definitions or algorithms triggered by specific cue words rather than making sense of the actual situation.', 'When none of the students in a group understood the term ''baseboard,'' they were unable to proceed meaningfully and relied on invented approaches cued by familiar words.', 'other', 'problem solving', 'experiential framework', 'student', 'seventh-grade', 'JRME_Hart_1993_Some', 'Lynn C. Hart', '1993', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '169, 170', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hart_1993_Some.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37506, 'When attempting to generate a real-world story problem for division by one-half, prospective teachers incorrectly construct stories that represent division by two.', 'If we had one and three-quarters pizzas left and there were two of us dying to split it, then how would we be able to split that?', 'fraction', 'division', 'division by fractions', 'PST', 'prospective elementary and secondary teachers', 'JRME_Ball_1990_Prospective', 'Deborah Loewenberg Ball', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '135', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Ball_1990_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37507, 'Prospective teachers incorrectly believe that any number divided by zero results in zero, often confusing division with the rule for multiplying by zero.', 'I''d just say, ''Anything divided by zero is zero. That''s just a rule, you just know it.'' Or I''d say, ''Well, if you don''t have anything, you can''t get anything out.''', 'whole_number', 'division', 'division by zero', 'PST', 'prospective elementary and secondary teachers', 'JRME_Ball_1990_Prospective', 'Deborah Loewenberg Ball', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '138', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Ball_1990_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37508, 'Prospective teachers possess a narrow understanding of division restricted to the partitive model, which hinders their ability to conceptualize division by a fraction.', 'Participants struggled to relate 1 3/4 ÷ 1/2 to a concrete situation because they only thought of division as forming a certain number of equal parts.', 'fraction', 'division', 'models of division', 'PST', 'prospective elementary and secondary teachers', 'JRME_Ball_1990_Prospective', 'Deborah Loewenberg Ball', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '140', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Ball_1990_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37509, 'Prospective teachers equate mathematical explanation with stating procedural rules, failing to recognize the need for underlying conceptual justification.', 'I''d just say... ''It''s undefined,'' and I''d tell them that this is a rule that you should never forget that anytime you divide by zero, you can''t.', 'other', 'mathematical justification', 'rules vs. meaning', 'PST', 'prospective elementary and secondary teachers', 'JRME_Ball_1990_Prospective', 'Deborah Loewenberg Ball', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '138, 141, 142', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Ball_1990_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37510, 'Students inappropriately apply rules for whole numbers to fraction symbols, mistakenly concluding that a larger denominator indicates a larger fractional quantity.', 'When asked to compare 1/6 and 1/8, a student answered ''One eighth is bigger'' and explained, ''[Eight] is a bigger number I think. [Eight] is bigger than [six].''', 'fraction', 'fraction symbols', 'comparing fractions', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Mack_1990_Learning', 'Nancy K. Mack', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '21, 22', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mack_1990_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37511, 'When interpreting symbolic or concrete representations of fractions greater than one, students fail to identify the proper unit, instead treating the entire collection of parts or units as a single unified whole.', 'When shown a picture of two circles divided into fourths with a total of five parts shaded, a student answered that ''Five-eighths'' was shaded because she counted all eight pictured parts as the unit.', 'fraction', 'concrete representations', 'identifying the unit', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Mack_1990_Learning', 'Nancy K. Mack', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '22, 23', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mack_1990_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37512, 'Students rely on previously learned, faulty algorithmic procedures rather than drawing on their informal reasoning, leading them to trust incorrect arithmetic calculations over sensible, context-based answers.', 'When adding 3/8 and 2/8, a student correctly reasoned ''Five-eighths'' mentally, but when writing it down applied a faulty rote rule to get ''5/16'' (adding straight across) and trusted the incorrect procedural result more.', 'fraction', 'addition', 'rote procedures', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Mack_1990_Learning', 'Nancy K. Mack', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '27, 28', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mack_1990_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37513, 'When comparing fractions using an operator context, students focus on the additive difference between inputs and outputs rather than the multiplicative relationship.', 'A half would be more because a third takes away more than a half... so a six, right, then a half of it, that''s three, and a third takes away four.', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'operator construct', 'student', '8- and 9-year-old children', 'JRME_Hunting_1996_Engaging', 'Robert P. Hunting, Gary Davis, and Catherine A. Pearn', '1996', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '364', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hunting_1996_Engaging.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37514, 'Students using an Equal Outputs scheme to compare fractions forget the inverse relationship between the required input and fraction size, incorrectly concluding that the fraction requiring a larger input is the larger fraction.', 'When comparing 2/4 and 2/3, Shannon claimed 2/4 was larger because ''That one takes in four blocks... and that one takes in three.''', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'inverse relationship', 'student', '8- and 9-year-old children', 'JRME_Hunting_1996_Engaging', 'Robert P. Hunting, Gary Davis, and Catherine A. Pearn', '1996', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '366, 367', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hunting_1996_Engaging.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37515, 'Students inappropriately treat computational estimation tasks as exact mental computation tasks, relying on standard paper-and-pencil algorithms rather than employing flexible estimation strategies like rounding or using compatible numbers.', 'When asked to estimate 12/13 + 7/8, a student attempted to mentally calculate an exact common denominator (114), multiply cross terms, and output an exact mixed number (1 24/114) instead of recognizing each fraction was near 1.', 'fraction', 'computational estimation', 'addition', 'student', 'fifth- and eighth-grade', 'JRME_Reys_1991_Computational', 'Robert E. Reys, Barbara J. Reys, Nobuhiko Nohda, Junichi Ishida, Shigeo Yoshikawa and Katsuhiko Shimizu', '1991', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '51, 52', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Reys_1991_Computational.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37516, 'Students judge the reasonableness of a fraction sum by mechanically checking if the resulting denominator is a multiple of the addends'' denominators, rather than assessing the magnitude of the fractions.', 'When deciding if 5/8 + 4/7 = 8/15 is reasonable, a student rejected it because ''15 is not a multiple of 8 and 7'', completely ignoring that both fractions are around a half and the sum must be greater than one.', 'fraction', 'addition', 'reasonableness of results', 'student', 'fifth- and eighth-grade', 'JRME_Reys_1991_Computational', 'Robert E. Reys, Barbara J. Reys, Nobuhiko Nohda, Junichi Ishida, Shigeo Yoshikawa and Katsuhiko Shimizu', '1991', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '53, 54', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Reys_1991_Computational.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37517, 'When mentally calculating the sum of two fractions, students may inappropriately add the numerators together and find the average of the denominators to create the new fraction.', 'To estimate 12/13 + 7/8, a student added the numerators to get 19, then found common multiples for denominators (26 and 24), averaged them to 25, and provided an estimate of 19/25.', 'fraction', 'addition', 'algorithms', 'student', 'fifth- and eighth-grade', 'JRME_Reys_1991_Computational', 'Robert E. Reys, Barbara J. Reys, Nobuhiko Nohda, Junichi Ishida, Shigeo Yoshikawa and Katsuhiko Shimizu', '1991', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '53', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Reys_1991_Computational.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37518, 'Students overgeneralize a rule about decimal multiplication, assuming that multiplying by any decimal number automatically results in a smaller product, without considering the specific value of the decimal.', 'After rounding 486 x 0.24 to 500 x 0.24, a student produced an estimate of 50, explaining that ''A decimal number gives smaller numbers when it is multiplied''.', 'decimal', 'multiplication', 'estimating products', 'student', 'fifth- and eighth-grade', 'JRME_Reys_1991_Computational', 'Robert E. Reys, Barbara J. Reys, Nobuhiko Nohda, Junichi Ishida, Shigeo Yoshikawa and Katsuhiko Shimizu', '1991', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '51', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Reys_1991_Computational.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37519, 'A teacher misinterprets a student''s correct fractional reasoning (changing the referent whole to two pizzas, resulting in 14/24) as an error, rigidly insisting that the unit must remain a single pizza (resulting in the improper fraction 14/12).', 'When a student correctly states ''we would have, uh, 14 out of the 24... slices'', the teacher interrupts to insist ''we should think of them as 2 separate pizzas'', steering the students to answer 14/12.', 'fraction', 'fractional representation', 'referent whole', 'mixed', 'fifth-grade', 'JRME_Maher_1990_Chapter', 'Carolyn A. Maher and Robert B. Davis', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'primary', '81, 82, 83, 84, 85, 86', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Maher_1990_Chapter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37520, 'When a student correctly combines parts from two identical wholes and shifts the referent unit to the combined total (e.g., viewing two 8-piece pizzas as a 16-piece whole), the teacher incorrectly diagnoses this productive reasoning as the algorithmic error of ''adding denominators.''', 'A student combining 3/8 of one pizza and 3/8 of another states the answer is ''6/16,'' which the teacher documents in her notes as ''Brian continues to add the denominators.''', 'fraction', 'addition', 'referent whole', 'mixed', 'fifth-grade', 'JRME_Maher_1990_Chapter', 'Carolyn A. Maher and Robert B. Davis', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'productive', 'secondary', '82', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Maher_1990_Chapter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37521, 'When modeling a fraction problem by drawing physical pieces, a student incorrectly names the resulting fraction by using the total number of drawn pieces as both the numerator and the denominator.', 'To represent eight children each getting one-fourth of a candy bar, a student draws eight one-fourth blocks but describes the total amount as ''eight eighths.''', 'fraction', 'fractional representation', 'naming fractions', 'mixed', 'fifth-grade', 'JRME_Maher_1990_Chapter', 'Carolyn A. Maher and Robert B. Davis', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '80', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Maher_1990_Chapter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37522, 'Children use terms like ''half'' to refer to the action of breaking or to any resulting piece, without understanding the numerical characteristics of equality or ''one of two parts.''', 'A child uses expressions like ''break it in half in four pieces'' or calls each piece of a randomly broken cookie a ''broken cookie'' rather than knowing what to mathematically call a half.', 'fraction', 'partitioning', 'halves', 'student', 'kindergarten and the first three grades', 'JRME_Pothier_1983_Partitioning', 'Yvonne Pothier and Daiyo Sawada', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '311', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Pothier_1983_Partitioning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37523, 'Children indiscriminately apply a systematic strategy of repeated halving to partition shapes, ignoring equality, even when tasked with creating an odd number of parts.', 'To obtain thirds, a child performs repeated dichotomies by mixing halving lines, or draws parallel vertical lines in a circular region to produce unequal fifths, declaring them to be fair shares.', 'fraction', 'partitioning', 'algorithmic halving', 'student', 'kindergarten and the first three grades', 'JRME_Pothier_1983_Partitioning', 'Yvonne Pothier and Daiyo Sawada', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '311, 312', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Pothier_1983_Partitioning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37524, 'Because of a rigid reliance on always making a median cut first, children conclude that partitioning a shape into an odd number of equal parts is impossible.', 'When asked to make an odd number of parts like three or five, a child responds, ''I can''t get it because it''s not even'' or ''It can''t be done.''', 'fraction', 'partitioning', 'odd number of parts', 'student', 'kindergarten and the first three grades', 'JRME_Pothier_1983_Partitioning', 'Yvonne Pothier and Daiyo Sawada', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '314, 315', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Pothier_1983_Partitioning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37525, 'Students inappropriately generalize whole number operations to fraction computations by independently adding, subtracting, or multiplying numerators and denominators, or by multiplying a whole number by both parts of a fraction.', 'For 6 x 4/7, students incorrectly chose 24/42. For 3/4 - 1/6, students answered 2/2. For 5/6 + 3/4, students selected 8/10.', 'fraction', 'computation', 'algorithms', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Cai_1995_Cognitive', 'Jinfa Cai', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'primary', '58', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Cai_1995_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37526, 'Students incorrectly interpret a relational proposition in a word problem as an assignment proposition, resulting in a reversed algebraic relationship.', 'When translating ''John has 5 more marbles than Pete,'' students selected the incorrect number sentence ''John''s marbles + 5 = Pete''s marbles''.', 'algebraic', 'word problem translation', 'relational propositions', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Cai_1995_Cognitive', 'Jinfa Cai', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'primary', '61, 62', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Cai_1995_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37527, 'Students directly round a decimal or fractional remainder from a division calculation up or down without appropriately interpreting the result in the real-world context of the problem.', 'To find how many 24-person buses are needed for 296 people, a student computes 12.3 and incorrectly writes ''The remainder is less than 5, so I just take the .3 away'' and answers 12.', 'whole_number', 'division', 'interpreting remainders', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Cai_1995_Cognitive', 'Jinfa Cai', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'primary', '68, 69, 70', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Cai_1995_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37528, 'Students attempt to estimate the area of an irregular shape by counting the number of units around the boundary of the shape, confusing area with perimeter.', 'When estimating the area of an island on a grid, a student explicitly stated they obtained their estimate by counting the number of squares around the island.', 'measurement', 'area', 'area vs perimeter', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Cai_1995_Cognitive', 'Jinfa Cai', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '71', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Cai_1995_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37529, 'Students fail to conserve the problem''s global conditions, treating different constraints as applying to different, independent sets rather than a single consistent set.', 'When finding a total number of blocks that leaves a remainder of 1 when divided by 2, 3, or 4, a student partitioned one set into groups of 2 with 1 left, a different set into groups of 3 with 1 left, yielding multiple answers that do not simultaneously satisfy all conditions.', 'whole_number', 'number theory', 'common multiples', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Cai_1995_Cognitive', 'Jinfa Cai', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '78, 79', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Cai_1995_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37530, 'When faced with an open-ended problem, students resort to unjustified symbol manipulation by extracting given numbers from the text and combining them using random operations irrelevant to the context.', 'In solving a word problem about grouping blocks into 2s, 3s, and 4s with a remainder of 1, a student simply added all the numbers presented in the text together.', 'whole_number', 'problem solving', 'open-ended tasks', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Cai_1995_Cognitive', 'Jinfa Cai', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '78, 79, 80, 86', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Cai_1995_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37531, 'Students misapply the average concept or formula by improperly incorporating the target average into the sum, or dividing by an incorrect number of terms.', 'To find the hats sold in week 4 to average 7 hats total across four weeks, a student added the hats sold in weeks 1, 2, and 3 plus the average (9+3+6+7), and then divided the sum by 4, answering 6.', 'statistics', 'average', 'missing value problems', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Cai_1995_Cognitive', 'Jinfa Cai', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'primary', '85, 86', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Cai_1995_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37532, 'Students employing a trial-and-error strategy prematurely halt their trials without successfully meeting the stated constraints, violating the stopping rule.', 'A student guesses a number for week 4, checks the average, realizes it does not equal the target average of 7, but stops there and provides the guess as the answer instead of continuing the process.', 'statistics', 'average', 'trial and error', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Cai_1995_Cognitive', 'Jinfa Cai', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '85, 86', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Cai_1995_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37533, 'Students assume that tossing two coins results in three equally likely outcomes (two heads, two tails, or one of each) and incorrectly assign a probability of 1/3 or equal chance to each.', 'Well I''ve drawn the name of what you can get, there is an equal chance to land on two heads, two tails or um, or a one H and one Tail because there are 3 options it can land on, 2H, 2T or 1H 1T.', 'probability', 'theoretical probability', 'sample space for two coins', 'student', 'Grade 4', 'JRME_English_2016_Development', 'Lyn D. English and Jane M. Watson', '2016', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '32, 35, 41, 48, 49', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_English_2016_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37534, 'Students process compound events by treating the components independently without combining them into a joint sample space, resulting in isolated probability models for each single coin.', 'I did 1H and 1T and 1/2 and the same for Coin 2 and then when they join together I put HT and then 1/2 and 2/2 because there''s like, when a HH go together it''s like 2 wholes, 2 halves, and that makes a whole.', 'probability', 'compound events', 'probability models', 'student', 'Grade 4', 'JRME_English_2016_Development', 'Lyn D. English and Jane M. Watson', '2016', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '48, 49, 50', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_English_2016_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37535, 'Students judge an occurrence or categorization as probable based on how ''typical'' it seems, sometimes resulting in the belief that a conjunction of events is more probable than a single event.', 'The probability of a man''s being black and being unemployed is judged to be higher than the probability of his being black.', 'probability', 'probability judgments', 'representativeness', 'other', '', 'JRME_Garfield_1988_Difficulties', 'Joan Garfield and Andrew Ahlgren', '1988', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '52', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Garfield_1988_Difficulties.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37536, 'Students mistakenly believe that small samples are as reliable or representative of a population as large samples.', 'A preference for Brand X over Brand Y in four out of five people would typically be believed to be clearly indicative of a general preference - although the probability of getting such an extreme sample of 5 just by chance is 3/8.', 'statistics', 'sampling', 'law of small numbers', 'other', '', 'JRME_Garfield_1988_Difficulties', 'Joan Garfield and Andrew Ahlgren', '1988', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '52', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Garfield_1988_Difficulties.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37537, 'Students employ an outcome orientation where they evaluate probabilities by making deterministic yes-or-no decisions about single events rather than viewing them through a frequency model.', 'When asked what the outcome would be of selecting six marbles from a population that was 5/6 black and 1/6 white, most students thought it more likely that all six would be black than that one would be white.', 'probability', 'probability models', 'outcome orientation', 'other', '', 'JRME_Garfield_1988_Difficulties', 'Joan Garfield and Andrew Ahlgren', '1988', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '56', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Garfield_1988_Difficulties.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37538, 'Students believe that a fluctuation in a random series will actively tend to be balanced by an opposite fluctuation.', '', 'probability', 'random sequences', 'gambler''s fallacy', 'other', '', 'JRME_Garfield_1988_Difficulties', 'Joan Garfield and Andrew Ahlgren', '1988', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '55', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Garfield_1988_Difficulties.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37539, 'Students treat the mean as a simple computational rule rather than a conceptual act, failing to understand when and how to weight data.', 'College students asked to combine two grade point averages that were based on different numbers of courses into a single average were unable to do the task correctly.', 'statistics', 'descriptive statistics', 'mean', 'other', '', 'JRME_Garfield_1988_Difficulties', 'Joan Garfield and Andrew Ahlgren', '1988', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '53', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Garfield_1988_Difficulties.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37540, 'People fail to identify correlations unless they already expect them, and if they do expect a correlation, they tend to falsely perceive one even when it does not exist.', '', 'statistics', 'correlation', 'illusory correlation', 'other', '', 'JRME_Garfield_1988_Difficulties', 'Joan Garfield and Andrew Ahlgren', '1988', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '52', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Garfield_1988_Difficulties.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37541, 'Students judge a categorization or event as probable based on how easily instances of it can be brought to mind.', '', 'probability', 'probability judgments', 'availability', 'other', '', 'JRME_Garfield_1988_Difficulties', 'Joan Garfield and Andrew Ahlgren', '1988', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '52', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Garfield_1988_Difficulties.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37542, 'A student constructs systematic but mathematically incorrect procedural rules to convert fractions to decimals, without being troubled by the contradictions these rules create.', 'To convert 3/2 to .5, the student added the numerator 3 and denominator 2 and prefixed a decimal point, and applied the same rule to convert 2/3 to .5 without noticing the contradiction.', 'fraction', 'converting fractions to decimals', 'invented algorithms', 'other', '', 'JRME_Noddings_1990_Chapter', 'Nel Noddings', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '13', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Noddings_1990_Chapter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37543, 'PSTs conceive of all digits in a multidigit number in terms of only ones, treating the digits as concatenated strings rather than distinct place value reference units.', 'Claire explained regrouping in 527 - 135 by stating ''You change the 5 to the number below it, which is 4. And you mark a 1, which makes this 12'', failing to relate the digits to hundreds or tens.', 'whole_number', 'multidigit whole numbers', 'place value', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JRME_Thanheiser_2009_Preservice', 'Eva Thanheiser', '2009', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '269, 270', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Thanheiser_2009_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37544, 'PSTs conceive of at least one digit in a multidigit number in terms of an incorrect unit type, struggling to correctly relate the values of digits to one another during regrouping.', 'Katie knew she took 1 hundred from the hundreds place in 527 - 135, but viewed the regrouped 1 in the tens place as just 10, unable to explain how the value stayed the same.', 'whole_number', 'multidigit subtraction', 'regrouping', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JRME_Thanheiser_2009_Preservice', 'Eva Thanheiser', '2009', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '267, 268, 269', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Thanheiser_2009_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37545, 'PSTs overgeneralize the standard base-ten subtraction algorithm to calculate elapsed time, failing to recognize that an hour must be regrouped as 60 minutes rather than 100.', 'When presented with a student''s work subtracting 2:53 from 7:08 that regrouped 1 hour into 100 minutes, 9 of 15 PSTs initially thought the child''s base-ten application was correct.', 'measurement', 'elapsed time', 'regrouping', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JRME_Thanheiser_2009_Preservice', 'Eva Thanheiser', '2009', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '259, 272, 273', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Thanheiser_2009_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37546, 'PSTs incorrectly generalize the relationships between powers of ten, asserting that 100 times 100 equals 1000.', 'When discussing the relationships among unit types, 40 percent of the PSTs stated at least once that 100 x 100 = 1000.', 'whole_number', 'powers of ten', 'multiplicative relationships', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JRME_Thanheiser_2009_Preservice', 'Eva Thanheiser', '2009', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '274, 275', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Thanheiser_2009_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37547, 'Students inappropriately alter the standard division-of-fractions algorithm by inverting the dividend instead of the divisor, or by inverting both.', 'When asked to compute 1/4 / 4, a student incorrectly writes 1/4 / 4 = 1/4 x 4 = 1.', 'fraction', 'division', 'algorithms', 'PST', 'prospective elementary teachers', 'JRME_Tirosh_2000_Enhancing', 'Dina Tirosh', '2000', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '7, 9', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Tirosh_2000_Enhancing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37548, 'Students interpret division solely through a primitive, partitive model, leading to the incorrect intuitive beliefs that the divisor must be a whole number, the dividend must be greater than the divisor, and division always makes numbers smaller.', 'For a measurement division problem asking how many 1/4 kg packages can be made from 4 kg, students choose 1/4 x 4 because they believe division makes smaller and multiplication makes bigger.', 'fraction', 'division', 'intuitive models', 'PST', 'prospective elementary teachers', 'JRME_Tirosh_2000_Enhancing', 'Dina Tirosh', '2000', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '7, 11, 12, 19, 20', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Tirosh_2000_Enhancing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37549, 'Students misapply formal knowledge by assuming that the operation of division is commutative.', 'A student might argue that 1 / 1/2 = 1/2 because 1 / 1/2 = 1/2 / 1 = 1/2.', 'fraction', 'division', 'properties of operations', 'PST', 'prospective elementary teachers', 'JRME_Tirosh_2000_Enhancing', 'Dina Tirosh', '2000', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '7', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Tirosh_2000_Enhancing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37550, 'Teachers and student teachers incorrectly translate the formal definition of associativity to the specific operation defined.', 'For the operation a * b = |a + b|, refuting the associativity by showing that |a + b| + |c| != |a| + |b + c| instead of ||a + b| + c| != |a + |b + c||.', 'algebraic', 'binary operations', 'associativity', 'mixed', 'secondary', 'JRME_Zaslavsky_1996_Inhibiting', 'Orit Zaslavsky, Irit Peled', '1996', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '73', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Zaslavsky_1996_Inhibiting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37551, 'Teachers and student teachers incorrectly overgeneralize the associative property to hold for two distinct operations.', 'Defining the operation as a * b = a · b, and checking associativity by showing that (a · b) + c != a · (b + c), instead of (a · b) · c = a · (b · c).', 'algebraic', 'binary operations', 'associativity', 'mixed', 'secondary', 'JRME_Zaslavsky_1996_Inhibiting', 'Orit Zaslavsky, Irit Peled', '1996', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '73', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Zaslavsky_1996_Inhibiting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37552, 'When checking the commutative property, subjects confuse the operation sign with the number sign when changing the order of the elements.', 'For the operation of subtraction, checking commutativity by showing that -2 - 3 = -3 - 2, instead of showing that -2 - 3 != 3 - (-2).', 'algebraic', 'binary operations', 'commutativity', 'mixed', 'secondary', 'JRME_Zaslavsky_1996_Inhibiting', 'Orit Zaslavsky, Irit Peled', '1996', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '73', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Zaslavsky_1996_Inhibiting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37553, 'Subjects incorrectly verify that a defined operation is commutative by checking only one special instance where the property holds.', 'For the noncommutative operation a * b = a^b, verifying that it is commutative by showing that 2^4 = 4^2.', 'algebraic', 'binary operations', 'logical inferences', 'mixed', 'secondary', 'JRME_Zaslavsky_1996_Inhibiting', 'Orit Zaslavsky, Irit Peled', '1996', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '73', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Zaslavsky_1996_Inhibiting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37554, 'Teachers and student teachers incorrectly hold a wrong belief that any binary operation is either both commutative and associative or both noncommutative and nonassociative.', '', 'algebraic', 'binary operations', 'properties of operations', 'mixed', 'secondary', 'JRME_Zaslavsky_1996_Inhibiting', 'Orit Zaslavsky, Irit Peled', '1996', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '68, 76', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Zaslavsky_1996_Inhibiting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37555, 'Subjects construct an ill-defined or inappropriate operation, such as a unary operation, when asked to generate a binary operation.', 'Constructing a unary operation, a* = a^2 - 1, then checking its so-called commutative property by using an inappropriate expression as follows: a * b = (a + b)^2 - 1.', 'algebraic', 'binary operations', 'unary versus binary operations', 'mixed', 'secondary', 'JRME_Zaslavsky_1996_Inhibiting', 'Orit Zaslavsky, Irit Peled', '1996', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '73', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Zaslavsky_1996_Inhibiting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37556, 'Preservice teachers explicitly believe that in a division problem the quotient must always be less than the dividend, an overgeneralization rooted in the primitive partitive model of whole numbers.', 'When asked to evaluate the statement ''10 ÷ .65 is greater than 10'' without computing, subjects incorrectly claimed the quotient must be less than 10 because ''division always makes smaller.''', 'decimal', 'division', 'quotient size', 'PST', 'sophomores or juniors', 'JRME_Tirosh_1990_Evoking', 'Dina Tirosh and Anna O. Graeber', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '98, 99, 100, 102, 104', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Tirosh_1990_Evoking.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37557, 'Preservice teachers distrust their correct computational results if those results violate their belief that division makes smaller, often incorrectly adjusting procedures like ''moving the decimal'' to ensure a smaller quotient.', 'A subject calculated 10 ÷ 0.5 as 20 by changing it to 100 ÷ 5, but then questioned the result, stating ''the answer to the original problem is smaller'' because she was confused by the decimal point shift.', 'decimal', 'division', 'division algorithm', 'PST', 'sophomores or juniors', 'JRME_Tirosh_1990_Evoking', 'Dina Tirosh and Anna O. Graeber', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '103, 104, 106', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Tirosh_1990_Evoking.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37558, 'When solving linear equations, students may misapply the balancing rule by incorrectly subtracting a constant from both sides to eliminate a negative term.', 'To solve the equation 8t - 9 = t + 12, a student says ''Take nine off both sides'' and incorrectly replaces the 12 with a 3, leading to the erroneous equation 7t = 3.', 'algebraic', 'linear equations', 'balancing equations', 'student', 'secondary, 12-year-old', 'JRME_Pirie_1997_Chapter', 'Susan Pirie', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '91, 92', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Pirie_1997_Chapter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37559, 'When expanding binomials, students may incorrectly combine the inner and outer terms by adding their absolute values instead of paying attention to their respective signs.', 'When expanding (x - 2)(x + 3), a student initially wrote x^2 + 5x - 6 because she added the 3 and the 2 instead of subtracting.', 'algebraic', 'polynomial expansion', 'combining like terms', 'student', 'secondary, 12-year-old', 'JRME_Pirie_1997_Chapter', 'Susan Pirie', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'passing', '93, 94', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Pirie_1997_Chapter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37560, 'The student imagines motion as entailing distance traveled but not simultaneously entailing an amount of time, viewing time as something to be determined only after the fact.', 'Ann claimed it took Rabbit no time to run and pointed to the timer showing 0 seconds, stating it took too little time for the computer to record it.', 'proportional', 'speed and motion', 'covariation of distance and time', 'mixed', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Thompson_1996_Talking', 'Alba G. Thompson, Patrick W. Thompson', '1996', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '5, 7, 8', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Thompson_1996_Talking.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37561, 'The teacher encapsulates his deep understandings of rate within the language of numbers and operations, making his instruction calculational and insensitive to the student''s conceptual difficulties.', 'Bill characterized speed to Ann as ''distance divided by time'' and spoke of dividing a distance of 200 feet up into 5.2 seconds, relying on the formula rather than the proportional correspondence of segments.', 'proportional', 'rate and proportionality', 'calculational orientation', 'mixed', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Thompson_1996_Talking', 'Alba G. Thompson, Patrick W. Thompson', '1996', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'primary', '5, 15, 18, 20', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Thompson_1996_Talking.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37562, 'When solving problems with rational numbers, students face a conceptual hurdle in deciding what represents the ''whole'' or point of origin, leading to confusion when naming fractional parts of fractions.', 'When asked to solve 2/3 of 3/4 by drawing a grid, a student may express confusion about whether the answer is 6/9 or 6/12 depending on what they identify as the whole.', 'fraction', 'multiplication of fractions', 'identifying the whole', 'mixed', 'first grade through undergraduate', 'JRME_Confrey_1995_Splitting', 'Jere Confrey and Erick Smith', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '76', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Confrey_1995_Splitting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37563, 'Traditional curriculum approaches that immediately introduce the base ''e'' for continuous exponential situations obscure the underlying multiplicative unit, causing students to rely on rote algorithmic manipulation rather than conceptual understanding.', 'In a radioactive decay problem, finding the time for a 1/1000 reduction using A = A0e^(kt) involves rote procedures to find k, rather than intuitively recognizing 2^10 is approximately 1000 using 1/2 as a base.', 'algebraic', 'exponential functions', 'continuous growth and decay', 'mixed', 'first grade through undergraduate', 'JRME_Confrey_1995_Splitting', 'Jere Confrey and Erick Smith', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '83', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Confrey_1995_Splitting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37564, 'Students experience a direct confrontation between concepts of rate in exponential functions, often confusing a constant multiplicative rate with a varying, increasing additive rate when observing data.', 'A compound interest rate of 5% is seen as ''holding the rate constant,'' yet when looking at the data, students conclude the function is changing at a varying rate that gets faster as the balance grows.', 'algebraic', 'exponential functions', 'rate of change', 'mixed', 'first grade through undergraduate', 'JRME_Confrey_1995_Splitting', 'Jere Confrey and Erick Smith', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '78', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Confrey_1995_Splitting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37565, 'Students inappropriately define area strictly as the ''inside'' and perimeter as the ''outside'' of an object, leading to confusion when applying these concepts to surfaces like walls.', 'When asked if area or perimeter is needed to determine the amount of paint or wallpaper for a room, students stated perimeter because ''Walls don''t have area because they go around.''', 'measurement', 'area and perimeter', 'definitions and applications', 'student', 'fifth-grade', 'JRME_Pesek_2000_Interference', 'Dolores D. Pesek and David Kirshner', '2000', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '535, 538', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Pesek_2000_Interference.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37566, 'Students apply the concept of two-dimensional area to one-dimensional or three-dimensional real-world contexts.', 'Students mistakenly claimed that area is needed to measure ''the lengths of boards,'' ''how much liquid,'' ''the thickness of concrete,'' and the ''height of a pole.''', 'measurement', 'area', 'dimensionality', 'student', 'fifth-grade', 'JRME_Pesek_2000_Interference', 'Dolores D. Pesek and David Kirshner', '2000', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '536', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Pesek_2000_Interference.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37567, 'Students attempt to calculate area and perimeter by haphazardly guessing operations (adding or multiplying) to apply to the given numbers, rather than grounding their calculations in the spatial properties of the figure.', 'For the area of a 2-by-2 shape, a student recommended ''2 times 2'' but justified it incorrectly by saying ''Because there''s four sides.'' Another student described perimeter calculation as ''sometimes they will multiply it... or they will add it and then they will add the two and then they will multiply the other ones with it.''', 'measurement', 'area and perimeter', 'calculation strategies', 'student', 'fifth-grade', 'JRME_Pesek_2000_Interference', 'Dolores D. Pesek and David Kirshner', '2000', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '536', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Pesek_2000_Interference.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37568, 'Students invent illogical explanations for the components of measurement formulas based on superficial features of the shapes rather than mathematical relationships.', 'When asked to explain the ''2'' in the perimeter formula P = 2(l + w), students responded ''the length is 2 and the width is 2'' or ''because it has 2 different sides''. For the area of a triangle formula, a student reasoned ''there''s three sides on a triangle [therefore] ... there''s three numbers.''', 'measurement', 'area and perimeter', 'formulas', 'student', 'fifth-grade', 'JRME_Pesek_2000_Interference', 'Dolores D. Pesek and David Kirshner', '2000', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '537', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Pesek_2000_Interference.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37569, 'Students are unable to mentally remove partition marks to recognize lower-term equivalent fractions on a number line.', 'When given a number line showing an unreduced fraction, students could not choose the correct reduced fraction name, hesitating to ''take out points'' or ignore extra partition lines.', 'fraction', 'number line representation', 'unpartitioning', 'student', 'fourth and fifth graders', 'JRME_Bright_1988_Identifying', 'George W. Bright, Merlyn J. Behr, Thomas R. Post and Ipke Wachsmuth', '1988', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '217, 225, 227, 228, 229', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bright_1988_Identifying.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37570, 'Students incorrectly use the entire visible number line segment, rather than the intended segment from 0 to 1, as the reference unit.', 'When working with a number line labeled from 0 to 2, students'' incorrect responses were consistent with incorrectly using the interval from 0 to 2 as the single unit.', 'fraction', 'number line representation', 'unit identification', 'student', 'fourth and fifth graders', 'JRME_Bright_1988_Identifying', 'George W. Bright, Merlyn J. Behr, Thomas R. Post and Ipke Wachsmuth', '1988', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '221, 225', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bright_1988_Identifying.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37571, 'Students incorrectly apply additive or isolated multiplicative rules, creating syntactic errors, rather than proportionally coordinating equivalent fractions on a number line.', 'When asked to find an equivalent fraction for 5/3 in twelfths on a number line, a student reasoned ''6 + 6 = 12 so 8 + 8 = 16'' or another reasoned ''3 x 2 = 6 so 8 x 2 = 16'' and incorrectly marked 16/3.', 'fraction', 'equivalent fractions', 'symbolic algorithms', 'student', 'fourth and fifth graders', 'JRME_Bright_1988_Identifying', 'George W. Bright, Merlyn J. Behr, Thomas R. Post and Ipke Wachsmuth', '1988', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '219, 223', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bright_1988_Identifying.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37572, 'Students count the tick marks on a number line rather than counting the continuous intervals between the marks.', '', 'fraction', 'number line representation', 'intervals vs. marks', 'student', 'fourth and fifth graders', 'JRME_Bright_1988_Identifying', 'George W. Bright, Merlyn J. Behr, Thomas R. Post and Ipke Wachsmuth', '1988', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '221', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bright_1988_Identifying.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37573, 'Students rigidly mislabel fractions on a number line by assuming denominators must increase consecutively from left to right.', 'Students mislabeled tick marks on a number line as 1/2, 1/3, and 1/4 consecutively from left to right between 0 and 1.', 'fraction', 'fraction order', 'number line labeling', 'student', 'fourth and fifth graders', 'JRME_Bright_1988_Identifying', 'George W. Bright, Merlyn J. Behr, Thomas R. Post and Ipke Wachsmuth', '1988', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '228', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bright_1988_Identifying.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37574, 'Students claim that a number resulting from the product of two primes is itself a prime number, believing that two primes produce a third of the same kind.', 'A student stated ''Both 151 and 157 are prime numbers, and 2 prime numbers multiplied together are going to give another prime number.''', 'whole_number', 'prime numbers', 'prime factorization', 'PST', 'elementary (K-7) level', 'JRME_Zazkis_2004_Understanding', 'Rina Zazkis and Peter Liljedahl', '2004', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'primary', '175', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Zazkis_2004_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37575, 'Students believe that if a number is composite, it must be divisible by a small prime, leading them to falsely conclude large numbers are prime after testing only a few small primes.', 'A student calculated 151 x 157 = 23707, found its square root, but then only checked divisibility for familiar small primes before concluding 23707 was prime.', 'whole_number', 'prime numbers', 'divisibility testing', 'PST', 'elementary (K-7) level', 'JRME_Zazkis_2004_Understanding', 'Rina Zazkis and Peter Liljedahl', '2004', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '175, 182', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Zazkis_2004_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37576, 'Students believe that any number represented as a product of variables or expressions cannot be prime, ignoring the possibility of a trivial factorization where one factor is 1.', 'Students argued that m(2k + 1) cannot be prime because it has factors 1, m, 2k + 1, and m(2k + 1), failing to consider that m could equal 1.', 'algebraic', 'prime numbers', 'trivial factorization', 'PST', 'elementary (K-7) level', 'JRME_Zazkis_2004_Understanding', 'Rina Zazkis and Peter Liljedahl', '2004', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'primary', '176, 177, 183', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Zazkis_2004_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37577, 'Students overgeneralize divisibility rules, such as claiming a number is prime if the sum of its digits is a prime number.', 'A student argued that 23707 is prime because the sum of its digits is 19, which is a prime number.', 'whole_number', 'prime numbers', 'divisibility rules', 'PST', 'elementary (K-7) level', 'JRME_Zazkis_2004_Understanding', 'Rina Zazkis and Peter Liljedahl', '2004', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'secondary', '175, 176', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Zazkis_2004_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37578, 'Students confuse the properties of being prime and being odd, incorrectly inferring that odd numbers are prime by mistaking a necessary condition for a sufficient one.', 'A student reasoned that F (151 x 157) is prime because ''The product of two odd numbers is always odd. Therefore making F odd too.''', 'whole_number', 'prime numbers', 'parity (odd/even)', 'PST', 'elementary (K-7) level', 'JRME_Zazkis_2004_Understanding', 'Rina Zazkis and Peter Liljedahl', '2004', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'secondary', '176, 182', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Zazkis_2004_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37579, 'Students inappropriately apply whole-number additive schemas when reasoning about rational numbers, such as judging fraction sizes by the absolute number of missing pieces or asserting that no numbers exist between consecutive decimals.', 'When asked to compare 2/3 and 3/4, a student reasoned, ''They are both the same size because they both have one piece missing.'' Another claimed there is no number between decimal 3 and decimal 4.', 'rational', 'comparison and ordering', 'magnitude and density', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'JRME_Moss_1999_Developing', 'Joan Moss and Robbie Case', '1999', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '136, 137, 141, 142', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Moss_1999_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37580, 'When translating between rational number representations, students treat fractions and decimals as disconnected syntactic systems, relying on superficial matching of digits rather than proportional equivalence.', 'When asked what 1/8 is as a decimal, a student replied, ''I think it is decimal eight... Because 1/8 is probably the same as decimal eight.''', 'rational', 'multiple representations', 'fractions to decimals', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'JRME_Moss_1999_Developing', 'Joan Moss and Robbie Case', '1999', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '139', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Moss_1999_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37581, 'When using pre-partitioned area models, students ignore the relationship between the target fraction and the whole, instead simply counting and shading the absolute number of pieces indicated by the fraction''s numerator.', 'When asked to shade 3/4 of a pizza predivided into 8ths, a student shaded 3 sections and explained, ''It says 3/4, so you need to shade in three parts.''', 'fraction', 'representations', 'area model', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'JRME_Moss_1999_Developing', 'Joan Moss and Robbie Case', '1999', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '137, 138', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Moss_1999_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37582, 'Students misapply whole-number arithmetic rules or rote syntactic patterns to nonstandard fractional computations without considering semantic proportionality.', 'When asked if 7 is 3/4 of 10, a student replied, ''Yes, 7 is 3/4 of 10 because 3 plus 4 equals 7.''', 'fraction', 'nonstandard computation', 'proportional reasoning', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'JRME_Moss_1999_Developing', 'Joan Moss and Robbie Case', '1999', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '135', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Moss_1999_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37583, 'When drawing or interpreting fractions, the student understands the shaded region to be the ''amount taken,'' causing them to incorrectly attend to the fractional complement--the amount ''left''.', 'For the fraction 7/12, the student drew a shape, partitioned it into 12 pieces, shaded 7, and stated ''seven pieces are gone,'' incorrectly determining that 7/12 was smaller than 1/2 because they focused on the 5 pieces left.', 'fraction', 'fractional representations', 'area models and shading conventions', 'student', 'adult', 'JRME_Lewis_2014_Difference', 'Katherine E. Lewis', '2014', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '365, 368, 369, 370', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lewis_2014_Difference.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37584, 'The student interprets a fractional representation based on the part composed of fewer pieces, focusing on the nonshaded or missing pieces if they comprise the smaller part.', 'When shown an area model representing 5/6 (with 5 shaded pieces and 1 nonshaded piece), the student interpreted the fraction as 1/6 because ''There''s like, all, except one shaded.''', 'fraction', 'fractional representations', 'interpreting area models', 'student', 'adult', 'JRME_Lewis_2014_Difference', 'Katherine E. Lewis', '2014', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '366, 371, 372, 373', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lewis_2014_Difference.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37585, 'The student represents and understands the fraction 1/2 not as a fractional quantity (one out of two total pieces), but as the act of partitioning or the balance between parts, omitting any shading.', 'When asked to draw 1/2, students drew shapes partitioned down the middle but omitted standard shading, explaining it using a chopping gesture or stating ''it''s cut in half, and there is two.''', 'fraction', 'unit fractions', 'meaning of one-half', 'student', 'adult', 'JRME_Lewis_2014_Difference', 'Katherine E. Lewis', '2014', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '365, 366, 377, 378', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lewis_2014_Difference.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37586, 'The student judges the magnitude of a fraction based solely on the denominator using an inverse relationship.', 'The student incorrectly argued that 1/2 was larger than 4/5 because halves are larger than fifths.', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'denominator magnitude', 'student', 'adult', 'JRME_Lewis_2014_Difference', 'Katherine E. Lewis', '2014', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '364, 394', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lewis_2014_Difference.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37587, 'The student compares fractions by attending to the total number of pieces in the whole rather than the size of the piece.', 'The student would argue that 1/4 is larger than 1/3 because 1/4 has more total pieces.', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'number of pieces versus piece size', 'student', 'adult', 'JRME_Lewis_2014_Difference', 'Katherine E. Lewis', '2014', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '366, 396', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lewis_2014_Difference.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37588, 'The student modifies symbols and representations based on superficial aspects without attending to whether the modification alters the fractional quantity involved.', 'The student crossed out the 4 in 1/4 and rewrote the expression 1/2 + 1/4 as 1/2 + 1/2, ultimately getting 2/2.', 'fraction', 'fraction operations', 'addition', 'student', 'adult', 'JRME_Lewis_2014_Difference', 'Katherine E. Lewis', '2014', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '364, 394', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lewis_2014_Difference.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37589, 'When transitioning to double-digit multipliers, prospective teachers initially attempt to compute the product by multiplying the tens by the tens and the ones by the ones, failing to account for the cross-products.', 'To mentally compute 23 x 23, a student calculated 20 x 20 + 3 x 3, obtaining 409.', 'whole_number', 'multidigit multiplication', 'partial products', 'PST', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Whitacre_2016_Prospective', 'Ian Whitacre and Susan D. Nickerson', '2016', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '285, 286, 287', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Whitacre_2016_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37590, 'Students do not distinguish between positive and negative numbers, treating all numbers as positive and ignoring the negative signs entirely.', 'One student counted backward, ''Five, four, three, two, one''. He ordered the first set of integers by absolute value, and explained, ''Zero, one, two. Two, three, then four, five, six, seven, eight.''', 'integer', 'negative numbers', 'order and values', 'student', 'first grade', 'JRME_Bofferding_2014_Negative', 'Laura Bofferding', '2014', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '222', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bofferding_2014_Negative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37591, 'Students order negative numbers correctly on a number line but determine which numbers are greatest or least as if they had whole number (absolute) values.', 'A student ordered the integer cards correctly (e.g., -8, -4, -2, 1, 5, 7) but then said that ''eight'' was the greatest and ''one'' was the least.', 'integer', 'integer values', 'absolute value', 'student', 'first grade', 'JRME_Bofferding_2014_Negative', 'Laura Bofferding', '2014', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '222', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bofferding_2014_Negative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37592, 'Students inconsistently treat negative numbers as equivalent to their positive counterparts or zero, resulting in conflicted value judgments.', 'When comparing -7 and -2, a student said that -7 is greater because ''Seven is greater than two'' and then added, ''But they''re both zero.''', 'integer', 'integer values', 'zero equivalence', 'student', 'first grade', 'JRME_Bofferding_2014_Negative', 'Laura Bofferding', '2014', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '223', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bofferding_2014_Negative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37593, 'Students understand negative numbers are less than zero, but mistakenly assume that negative integers with larger absolute values are greater than those with smaller absolute values.', 'When describing why -6 is greater than -2, one student explained, ''They''re both in the negative part, but this one [-6] is a bit higher in the negative part.''', 'integer', 'integer values', 'magnitude versus direction', 'student', 'first grade', 'JRME_Bofferding_2014_Negative', 'Laura Bofferding', '2014', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '223', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bofferding_2014_Negative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37594, 'Students rely exclusively on the binary meaning of the minus sign and interpret a negative integer as an incomplete subtraction problem.', 'Students might rely on its binary, subtraction meaning and interpret -5 as 5 - 5.', 'integer', 'negative numbers', 'meaning of the minus sign', 'student', 'first grade', 'JRME_Bofferding_2014_Negative', 'Laura Bofferding', '2014', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '197, 204', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bofferding_2014_Negative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37595, 'Students focus only on the direction of movement and ignore magnitude comparatives like ''more'' or ''less'', or treat locations as fixed distinct zones.', 'When asked to move the cat, which was sitting just above the middle point of the staircase, less high, he moved the cat up the stairs; otherwise it would have ended up in his ''less low'' section.', 'integer', 'directed magnitude', 'more/less in a direction', 'student', 'first grade', 'JRME_Bofferding_2014_Negative', 'Laura Bofferding', '2014', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '225', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bofferding_2014_Negative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37596, 'Students confuse the length of a decimal fraction with its value, believing that longer decimal fractions are either necessarily larger or necessarily smaller.', 'A student might believe that .355 is more than .5 because 355 is more than 5, or conversely, that 0.1 is larger than 1.5.', 'decimal', 'magnitude', 'comparing and ordering', 'student', 'Year 7 and 8 (ages 11 and 12)', 'JRME_Irwin_2001_Using', 'Kathryn C. Irwin', '2001', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '401, 402, 405, 406', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Irwin_2001_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37597, 'Students treat whole numbers and decimal fractions as distinct units separated by a ''decorative dot'', applying mathematical operations symmetrically to both sides without regard to place value.', 'A student adds 2.5 + 1 and gets 3.6 by applying the addition to both sides of the decimal point.', 'decimal', 'addition', 'role of the decimal point', 'student', 'Year 7 and 8 (ages 11 and 12)', 'JRME_Irwin_2001_Using', 'Kathryn C. Irwin', '2001', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '402, 405', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Irwin_2001_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37598, 'Students incorrectly assume a mirrored symmetry in place value column names around the decimal point, inventing a column for ''oneths'' immediately to the right of the point.', 'When discussing place value, a student refers to the columns after the decimal point as ''oneths, tenths, hundredths'' or explicitly states ''That''s tenths, not oneths.''', 'decimal', 'place value', 'column names', 'student', 'Year 7 and 8 (ages 11 and 12)', 'JRME_Irwin_2001_Using', 'Kathryn C. Irwin', '2001', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '402, 411, 412', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Irwin_2001_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37599, 'Students incorrectly translate common fractions to decimals by treating the fraction''s components directly as decimal digits.', 'A student believes that 1/4 can be written as 0.4, or that 93 1/4 is written as 93.04 in decimals.', 'decimal', 'representations', 'fractions to decimals', 'student', 'Year 7 and 8 (ages 11 and 12)', 'JRME_Irwin_2001_Using', 'Kathryn C. Irwin', '2001', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '402, 405, 406, 411', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Irwin_2001_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37600, 'Students incorrectly believe that the infinite repeating decimal 0.999... is strictly less than one rather than equal to one.', 'When asked to vote whether 0.999... is less than one or equal to one, the majority of students voted that the fraction is less than one.', 'decimal', 'infinite decimals', 'equivalence', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Toom_1993_Russian', 'Andrei Toom', '1993', '', 'uncoded', 'primary', '133', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Toom_1993_Russian.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37601, 'Students inappropriately treat algebraic variables as direct labels or names for people rather than as representations of quantitative parameters like time or rate.', 'Given a problem where Tom and Dick can do a job in 2 hours, a student wrote the equation ''T + D = 2'' and explicitly stated that the letters meant ''just Tom, Dick and Harry'', not parameters.', 'algebraic', 'variables', 'word problem translation', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Toom_1993_Russian', 'Andrei Toom', '1993', '', 'uncoded', 'primary', '134', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Toom_1993_Russian.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37602, 'Unskilled mental calculators unnecessarily rely on inefficient mental analogues of standard pencil-and-paper algorithms, failing to exploit basic number properties like the identity principle.', 'When asked to mentally multiply 20 x 30, a student visualized the vertical written algorithm and proceeded digit by digit (0x0, 0x3, 2x0, 2x3) instead of immediately recognizing the product as 600.', 'whole_number', 'multiplication', 'mental calculation strategies', 'student', 'Grade 11 and 12', 'JRME_Hope_1987_Characteristics', 'John A. Hope and James M. Sherrill', '1987', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '104, 105', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hope_1987_Characteristics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37603, 'Unskilled calculators fragment addition tasks during mental multiplication by using a digit-by-digit, right-to-left process, which overburdens short-term memory and prevents the retrieval of obvious sums.', 'In adding 2500 and 500, a student retrieved the digits ''5, 0, 0'' and ''2, 5, 0, 0'' and attempted to add them right to left, losing track of the place values and answering ''2, 7, 5, 0''.', 'whole_number', 'addition', 'mental calculation', 'student', 'Grade 11 and 12', 'JRME_Hope_1987_Characteristics', 'John A. Hope and James M. Sherrill', '1987', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '105', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hope_1987_Characteristics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37604, 'Students use a rounded decimal approximation for a fraction in calculations and are perturbed when the result is not exact, sparking a debate on when it is appropriate to use fractions versus decimals.', 'Trevor multiplied 5.33333 by 4.5 and was surprised to get 23.99999 instead of exactly 24, realizing that his decimal representation for 5 1/3 was not exact.', 'fraction', 'fraction representations', 'repeating decimals', 'student', '6th- and 7th-grade', 'JRME_Johanning_2008_Learning', 'Debra I. Johanning', '2008', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '292, 293', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Johanning_2008_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37605, 'Students hesitate to apply known operations or functional relationships, such as division to find a missing side or squaring a scale factor to find area change, when the values involved are fractions rather than whole numbers.', 'Students struggled to determine the change in area for a scale factor of 2.5, failing to recognize they could square it just as they had done with whole number scale factors.', 'fraction', 'operations and scale factors', 'extending whole-number concepts', 'student', '6th- and 7th-grade', 'JRME_Johanning_2008_Learning', 'Debra I. Johanning', '2008', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '294, 295', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Johanning_2008_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37606, 'Students are unsure whether ratios can be represented, renamed, and divided in the exact same ways as fractions to determine equivalence and similarity.', 'When the teacher introduced the a/b representation for a 4 to 1 ratio, students debated whether it was a fraction, with one stating, ''It is like ... its kind of like fractions but not really.''', 'ratio', 'ratio representations', 'relationship to fractions', 'student', '6th- and 7th-grade', 'JRME_Johanning_2008_Learning', 'Debra I. Johanning', '2008', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '301, 302, 303', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Johanning_2008_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37607, 'Students lack understanding of place value, interpreting the digits in a multi-digit number serially by their face value rather than their base-10 value.', 'When asked to show the checkers represented by the 2 in 23, Bonny counts out 2 checkers instead of 20.', 'whole_number', 'place value', 'base-10 representations', 'in_service', 'K-6', 'JRME_Hill_2008_Unpacking', 'Heather C. Hill, Deborah Loewenberg Ball and Stephen G. Schilling', '2008', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '380, 400', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hill_2008_Unpacking.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37608, 'Students make regrouping errors in multi-digit addition by carrying an incorrect amount to the next column.', 'When adding 38, 49, and 65, a student carries a 1 instead of a 2 to the tens column.', 'whole_number', 'multi-digit addition', 'carrying/regrouping', 'in_service', 'K-6', 'JRME_Hill_2008_Unpacking', 'Heather C. Hill, Deborah Loewenberg Ball and Stephen G. Schilling', '2008', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '380, 400', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hill_2008_Unpacking.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37609, 'Students incorrectly order decimals by applying whole-number rules and ignoring the decimal point.', 'Students might correctly order .60, 2.53, 3.14, and .45 only because they ignore the decimal point and order the sequences of digits (45, 60, 253, 314) like whole numbers.', 'decimal', 'ordering decimals', '', 'in_service', 'K-6', 'JRME_Hill_2008_Unpacking', 'Heather C. Hill, Deborah Loewenberg Ball and Stephen G. Schilling', '2008', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '380, 381, 400', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hill_2008_Unpacking.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37610, 'Students perform a ''buggy'' subtraction algorithm by always subtracting the smaller digit from the larger digit in each column, regardless of whether it is the minuend or subtrahend.', 'To solve 51 - 18, Jill subtracts 1 from 8 to get 7 in the ones place, instead of trading tens for ones.', 'whole_number', 'multi-digit subtraction', 'regrouping', 'in_service', 'K-6', 'JRME_Hill_2008_Unpacking', 'Heather C. Hill, Deborah Loewenberg Ball and Stephen G. Schilling', '2008', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '381, 400', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hill_2008_Unpacking.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37611, 'Students struggle to accept the formalized, abstract definitions of geometric objects, instead attempting to interpret them using physical, real-world properties like size and thickness.', 'A student joked that if a point were ''wider'' there could be several perpendicular lines through it, and asked, ''If a dot has no size, then it... isn''t anything.''', 'geometric', 'points and lines', 'abstract definitions vs physical representations', 'in_service', 'high school', 'JRME_Gregg_1995_Tensions', 'Jeff Gregg', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '449', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Gregg_1995_Tensions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37612, 'Students and teachers interactively reduce the complex mathematical practices of explaining and justifying in proofs to simply naming a formal rule, procedure, or theorem.', 'When asked for the thought process of how to prove two angles congruent, a student simply responded with ''substitution property'' rather than providing a logical sequence.', 'geometric', 'proof', 'justifying steps', 'in_service', 'high school', 'JRME_Gregg_1995_Tensions', 'Jeff Gregg', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '451', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Gregg_1995_Tensions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37613, 'When asked to convert a decimal to a fraction, a student incorrectly uses the digit before the decimal as the numerator and the digit after the decimal as the denominator.', 'Jenna answered ''9 over 3'' when asked to convert 9.3 to a fraction.', 'decimal', 'decimal to fraction conversion', '', 'in_service', 'high school', 'JRME_Gregg_1995_Tensions', 'Jeff Gregg', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'passing', '457', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Gregg_1995_Tensions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37614, 'In the school mathematics tradition, correct mathematical answers that deviate from the specific procedural form mandated by the textbook are evaluated as incorrect, leading to arbitrary judgments of right and wrong.', 'A student correctly converted .2 to 1/5, but the teacher marked it incorrect because the expected textbook procedure generated the unsimplified fraction 2/10.', 'rational', 'decimal to fraction conversion', 'equivalent fractions', 'in_service', 'high school', 'JRME_Gregg_1995_Tensions', 'Jeff Gregg', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '458', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Gregg_1995_Tensions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37615, 'Students compare decimal fractions by treating the decimal portion as a whole number, concluding that the decimal with more digits is larger.', 'A student might conclude that 3.214 is greater than 3.8 because 214 as a whole number is larger than 8.', 'decimal', 'decimal comparison', 'whole number rule', 'student', 'fourth to sixth grade', 'JRME_Resnick_1989_Conceptual', 'Lauren B. Resnick, Pearla Nesher, Francois Leonard, Maria Magone, Susan Omanson and Irit Peled', '1989', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '9, 11, 20, 24', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Resnick_1989_Conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37616, 'Students compare decimals by assuming that longer decimals represent smaller parts (like thousandths vs. hundredths) but fail to coordinate this with the number of parts, thus concluding that a decimal with more places is always smaller.', 'A student might judge 1.35 as smaller than 1.2 because 1.35 has more decimal places, reasoning that hundredths are smaller than tenths.', 'decimal', 'decimal comparison', 'fraction rule', 'student', 'fourth to sixth grade', 'JRME_Resnick_1989_Conceptual', 'Lauren B. Resnick, Pearla Nesher, Francois Leonard, Maria Magone, Susan Omanson and Irit Peled', '1989', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '9, 13, 21, 25', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Resnick_1989_Conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37617, 'Students apply a specialized whole number rule where they assume any decimal with a zero immediately following the decimal point is smaller than a decimal with a nonzero digit in that position.', 'Given 3.214, 3.09, and 3.8, a student chooses 3.09 as the smallest because of the zero, but then incorrectly orders the rest as 3.8 < 3.214.', 'decimal', 'decimal comparison', 'zero rule', 'student', 'fourth to sixth grade', 'JRME_Resnick_1989_Conceptual', 'Lauren B. Resnick, Pearla Nesher, Francois Leonard, Maria Magone, Susan Omanson and Irit Peled', '1989', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '9, 13, 24', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Resnick_1989_Conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37618, 'When translating ordinary fractions to decimal notation, students encode only the numerator into the decimal and completely ignore the denominator.', 'A student translates 3/4 as .3 or .003, and 3/100 as .3.', 'decimal', 'fraction-to-decimal translation', 'numerator encoding', 'student', 'fourth to sixth grade', 'JRME_Resnick_1989_Conceptual', 'Lauren B. Resnick, Pearla Nesher, Francois Leonard, Maria Magone, Susan Omanson and Irit Peled', '1989', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '23', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Resnick_1989_Conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37619, 'When translating ordinary fractions to decimal notation, students encode only the denominator into the decimal and ignore the numerator.', 'A student translates 1/3 as .3, and 3/4 as .04 or .4.', 'decimal', 'fraction-to-decimal translation', 'denominator encoding', 'student', 'fourth to sixth grade', 'JRME_Resnick_1989_Conceptual', 'Lauren B. Resnick, Pearla Nesher, Francois Leonard, Maria Magone, Susan Omanson and Irit Peled', '1989', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '23', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Resnick_1989_Conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37620, 'Students perform a syntactic translation by keeping both the numerator and the denominator and placing a decimal point somewhere between them.', 'A student translates 3/4 as 3.4 or .34.', 'decimal', 'fraction-to-decimal translation', 'syntactic translation', 'student', 'fourth to sixth grade', 'JRME_Resnick_1989_Conceptual', 'Lauren B. Resnick, Pearla Nesher, Francois Leonard, Maria Magone, Susan Omanson and Irit Peled', '1989', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '23', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Resnick_1989_Conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37621, 'Students expect the rate of change in a two-variable linear function to be constant regardless of the combination of inputs, experiencing conflict when uncoordinated changes in the independent variables produce varying differences in the dependent variable.', 'Aya noticed that matching pairs like (1 day, 1 km) and (2 days, 2 km) yielded a constant difference, but an unmatched pair like (2 days, 4 km) yielded a different difference, leading her to incorrectly claim ''there is no constant connection''.', 'algebraic', 'multivariable functions', 'rate of change', 'student', 'seventh-grade', 'JRME_Yerushalmy_1997_Designing', 'Michal Yerushalmy', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Yerushalmy_1997_Designing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37622, 'When presented with a 3D orthogonal coordinate system drawn on a 2D surface, students may interpret the oblique axis as a negative direction on a standard 2D Cartesian plane.', 'Looking at a 3D sketch, Vera interpreted the third axis drawn diagonally down-and-left as indicating a negative value, stating ''it means that this one is negative. He should have drawn it this way''.', 'geometric', 'graphing', '3D coordinate systems', 'student', 'seventh-grade', 'JRME_Yerushalmy_1997_Designing', 'Michal Yerushalmy', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Yerushalmy_1997_Designing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37623, 'To avoid the complexity of 3D graphing, a student may inappropriately merge two independent variables onto a single axis by assuming a constant proportional relationship between them, effectively restricting the domain.', 'Adi placed both ''days'' and ''kilometers'' on the x-axis using different colors, inherently dividing the total kilometers evenly among the days, assuming a fixed average distance per day.', 'algebraic', 'multivariable functions', 'independent variables', 'student', 'seventh-grade', 'JRME_Yerushalmy_1997_Designing', 'Michal Yerushalmy', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Yerushalmy_1997_Designing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37624, 'When solving ratio and proportion problems without physical or pictorial referents, a student might erroneously apply an additive rather than a multiplicative strategy.', 'To find how many candies 9 quarters can buy (given 12 quarters buy 8 candies), a student concluded the answer is 5 because 8 is 4 less than 12, so 5 is 4 less than 9.', 'proportional', 'missing value', 'additive error', 'student', 'fifth-grade', 'JRME_Lo_1997_Developing', 'Jane-Jane Lo and Tad Watanabe', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '223, 224', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lo_1997_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37625, 'The student struggles to shift smoothly between quotitive and partitive division when finding an equivalent ratio unit, attempting to use quotitive division for both quantities rather than coordinating the number of groups.', 'After successfully grouping 12 quarters into 4 groups of 3 (quotitive division), the student attempts to put 28 candies into groups of 3 (quotitive) rather than putting them into 4 groups (partitive).', 'whole_number', 'division', 'partitive and quotitive division', 'student', 'fifth-grade', 'JRME_Lo_1997_Developing', 'Jane-Jane Lo and Tad Watanabe', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '228', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lo_1997_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37626, 'The student relies on a ''multiplication as repeated addition'' model that breaks down when the multiplier is larger than 10, preventing them from meaningfully applying multiplication to curtail a build-up sequence.', 'When asked how far a helicopter traveling 16 miles per 10 minutes goes in 2 hours (120 minutes), the student attempts to multiply 16 x 120 and gets 1920, later crossing out the zero without knowing why, instead of multiplying 16 by 12.', 'whole_number', 'multiplication and division', 'meaning of operations', 'student', 'fifth-grade', 'JRME_Lo_1997_Developing', 'Jane-Jane Lo and Tad Watanabe', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '229, 230', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lo_1997_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37627, 'When asked to cover a rectangular area with smaller rectangular units, the student focuses inappropriately on linear perimeter measurements and outlines, failing to correctly model 2D spatial area.', 'To cover a 30x40 rectangle with 2x2 squares, the student incorrectly added 30 and 40 to get 70, then divided by 2 to get 35. When drawing, the student mapped the outline of the shape first and then placed a ''cross'' in the middle.', 'measurement', 'area', 'covering with units', 'student', 'fifth-grade', 'JRME_Lo_1997_Developing', 'Jane-Jane Lo and Tad Watanabe', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '231, 232', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lo_1997_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37628, 'Students base their balance predictions on weight alone, assuming the side of the fulcrum with the greater weight will go down and ignoring distance.', 'For complex proportions, the weights on either side of the fulcrum are summed.', 'proportional', 'balance puzzle', 'torque', 'student', '7th, 9th, and 11th graders', 'JRME_Linn_1983_Aptitude', 'Marcia C. Linn and Steven Pulos', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '35', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Linn_1983_Aptitude.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37629, 'Students base their balance predictions on distance alone, completely ignoring weight or assuming it is equal.', '', 'proportional', 'balance puzzle', 'torque', 'student', '7th, 9th, and 11th graders', 'JRME_Linn_1983_Aptitude', 'Marcia C. Linn and Steven Pulos', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '35', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Linn_1983_Aptitude.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37630, 'Students incorrectly assume the beam balances when weights and distances are symmetric, placing greater weight and greater distance on the same side.', 'Heavier weights are placed further from the fulcrum than lighter weights.', 'proportional', 'balance puzzle', 'symmetry', 'student', '7th, 9th, and 11th graders', 'JRME_Linn_1983_Aptitude', 'Marcia C. Linn and Steven Pulos', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '35', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Linn_1983_Aptitude.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37631, 'Students use an additive rather than multiplicative strategy, assuming the shorter distance requires more weight but calculating it by adding the difference in distance to the weight.', 'If a 5-gram weight is hung at a distance of 6, an additive responder choosing a weight for a distance 4 would choose a 7-gram weight, adding one gram for each unit closer to the beam.', 'proportional', 'balance puzzle', 'additive reasoning', 'student', '7th, 9th, and 11th graders', 'JRME_Linn_1983_Aptitude', 'Marcia C. Linn and Steven Pulos', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '35', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Linn_1983_Aptitude.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37632, 'Students view a limit as a boundary that the function cannot cross or exceed, either locally or globally.', 'When evaluating a limit, a student asserts that ''if you come close, like within 10, then sure, it can assume values both larger and smaller than the limit. But if you get close like within a tenth, then it should be approaching the number,'' viewing the limit as a strict local bound.', 'calculus', 'limit', 'limit as bound', 'student', 'second-semester calculus', 'JRME_Szydlik_2000_Mathematical', 'Jennifer Earles Szydlik', '2000', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '269, 270, 271', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Szydlik_2000_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37633, 'Students believe that a function can get infinitely close to its limit but can never actually equal or reach the limiting value.', 'A student states, ''You''re getting so close to that number, but you''re never actually going to reach it. So that is your limit because you''re never actually going to reach it.''', 'calculus', 'limit', 'limit as unreachable', 'student', 'second-semester calculus', 'JRME_Szydlik_2000_Mathematical', 'Jennifer Earles Szydlik', '2000', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '269, 270, 276', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Szydlik_2000_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37634, 'Students believe that the real number system contains gaps or that there is an infinitely small distance between 0.999... and 1, rather than recognizing them as equal.', 'When asked what is between 0.999... and 1, a student claimed that ''you can''t really answer because 0.999... goes on forever and never finishes.''', 'decimal', 'real number system', 'density and infinity', 'student', 'second-semester calculus', 'JRME_Szydlik_2000_Mathematical', 'Jennifer Earles Szydlik', '2000', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '265, 266', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Szydlik_2000_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37635, 'Students inappropriately use buggy subtraction procedures, relying on blind application of rules like automatically crossing out a zero and replacing it with a nine.', 'When solving 3004 - 286, a student immediately replaced the 0 in the minuend numeral with 9 without properly executing a regrouping action.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'regrouping across zeros', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'JRME_Thompson_1992_Notations', 'Patrick W. Thompson', '1992', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '136', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Thompson_1992_Notations.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37636, 'Students treat the decimal point merely as a separator between two independent whole numbers when adding or subtracting decimals.', 'A student calculated 7.31 - 6.4 as 1.27, or attempted to calculate it as ''four minus one... three minus six'', struggling because the digits didn''t form matching whole-number pairs on both sides of the decimal.', 'decimal', 'addition and subtraction', 'alignment', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'JRME_Thompson_1992_Notations', 'Patrick W. Thompson', '1992', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '135, 139', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Thompson_1992_Notations.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37637, 'Students believe a decimal number with more digits is larger because they interpret the digits after the decimal point as a whole number.', 'A student claimed 7.89 is bigger than 7.9 because ''eight nine is bigger than nine.''', 'decimal', 'ordering', 'comparing magnitudes', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'JRME_Thompson_1992_Notations', 'Patrick W. Thompson', '1992', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '140', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Thompson_1992_Notations.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37638, 'Students perform operations on decimal numerals by ignoring the decimal point, executing whole-number algorithms, and arbitrarily inserting the decimal point at the end of the calculation.', 'A student explained their addition method by saying, ''I just thought about it as doing it without the decimal and then I just added the decimal at the end.''', 'decimal', 'addition and subtraction', 'algorithms', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'JRME_Thompson_1992_Notations', 'Patrick W. Thompson', '1992', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '140, 141', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Thompson_1992_Notations.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37639, 'Students fixate on using learned standard algorithms, such as finding exact common denominators, rather than employing flexible estimation strategies, often leading to frustration and errors.', 'Students attempted to mentally calculate common denominators for fractions, apologizing for the imprecision of their estimates and stating they could do better with a calculator or paper.', 'fraction', 'computational estimation', 'common denominators', 'student', 'undergraduate students', 'JRME_Hanson_2000_Computational', 'Sheri A. Hanson and Thomas P. Hogan', '2000', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '493, 494', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hanson_2000_Computational.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37640, 'When estimating fraction addition or subtraction, students may avoid finding common denominators by incorrectly adding or subtracting directly across the numerators and denominators.', 'To estimate 12/31 - 5/8, a student subtracted the numerators (12-5) and denominators (31-8) to get 7/23.', 'fraction', 'computational estimation', 'addition and subtraction', 'student', 'undergraduate students', 'JRME_Hanson_2000_Computational', 'Sheri A. Hanson and Thomas P. Hogan', '2000', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '494, 499', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hanson_2000_Computational.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37641, 'Students inappropriately apply memorized procedural rules from one fractional operation to another, such as taking the reciprocal when multiplying instead of dividing.', 'When estimating the multiplication of two fractions, a student frequently used the reciprocal of the second fraction.', 'fraction', 'multiplication', 'algorithms', 'student', 'undergraduate students', 'JRME_Hanson_2000_Computational', 'Sheri A. Hanson and Thomas P. Hogan', '2000', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '494', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hanson_2000_Computational.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37642, 'Students incorrectly assume the validity of the converse of a conditional rule.', 'When given the rule ''Whenever there is a number below the line, there is a letter above the line,'' and a card showing a letter, students indicate that only a number could be below the tape.', 'discrete', 'conditional reasoning', 'converse', 'student', 'grade 8, 10, and 12', 'JRME_Jansson_1978_Comparison', 'Lars C. Jansson', '1978', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '181', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Jansson_1978_Comparison.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37643, 'Students incorrectly reason from the inverse of a conditional rule.', 'When presented with a card that negates the antecedent, students fallaciously omit the necessary consequent from their choices.', 'discrete', 'conditional reasoning', 'inverse', 'student', 'grade 8, 10, and 12', 'JRME_Jansson_1978_Comparison', 'Lars C. Jansson', '1978', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '182', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Jansson_1978_Comparison.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37644, 'Students incorrectly interpret a one-way conditional implication as a biconditional statement.', '', 'discrete', 'conditional reasoning', 'biconditional interpretation', 'student', 'grade 8, 10, and 12', 'JRME_Jansson_1978_Comparison', 'Lars C. Jansson', '1978', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '186', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Jansson_1978_Comparison.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37645, 'Students may reject the equivalence of different forms of formal notation, such as fractions and decimals, because translating to a new form obscures structural features of the original problem.', 'Students correctly calculated 3 divided by 6 as .50, but rejected .50 as an appropriate representation for 3/6 because it obscured the number of pieces the item was divided into.', 'rational', 'representational equivalence', 'fractions and decimals', 'student', '6th-grade', 'JRME_Brenner_1999_Cross-national', 'Mary E. Brenner, Sally Herman, Hsiu-Zu Ho, Jules M. Zimmer', '1999', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Brenner_1999_Cross-national.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37646, 'Students may broadly reject algebraic notation, such as the use of variables or parentheses, as an appropriate way to represent arithmetic word problems.', 'Students marked both correct and incorrect representational subitems using algebraic notation as wrong, indicating a rejection of the notation itself.', 'algebraic', 'algebraic notation', 'variables and parentheses', 'student', '6th-grade', 'JRME_Brenner_1999_Cross-national', 'Mary E. Brenner, Sally Herman, Hsiu-Zu Ho, Jules M. Zimmer', '1999', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Brenner_1999_Cross-national.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37647, 'Children incorrectly believe that the relative strength of the taste of two samples from the same mixture depends on the relative volumes of the samples, reasoning that different sizes leave different amounts of room for ingredients.', 'The 7-oz. glass has room for more water and less orange stuff, but the 4-oz. glass has less water and more orange stuff.', 'ratio', 'intensive quantities', 'constancy of taste', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Harel_1994_Invariance', 'Guershon Harel, Merlyn Behr, Richard Lesh and Thomas Post', '1994', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '333, 334, 335, 336', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Harel_1994_Invariance.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37648, 'Children believe that constancy of taste applies only to ''ingredients free'' homogeneous liquids like water or Coke, and that liquids composed of multiple mixed ingredients will vary in taste depending on the sample volume.', 'When asked about Coke, a child stated it would taste the same because ''It doesn''t have water and orange stuff. It just comes from the same thing.''', 'ratio', 'intensive quantities', 'uniformity of liquids', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Harel_1994_Invariance', 'Guershon Harel, Merlyn Behr, Richard Lesh and Thomas Post', '1994', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '337, 338, 341', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Harel_1994_Invariance.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37649, 'Children abandon their intuitive sense of taste constancy when presented with numerical data, feeling compelled to perform calculations that subsequently dictate their incorrect conclusions.', 'A student incorrectly divided the ingredients'' ounces by the glass sizes and concluded they wouldn''t taste the same, stating she would only think they tasted the same if she didn''t know the specific amounts.', 'ratio', 'ratio and proportion', 'problem solving context', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Harel_1994_Invariance', 'Guershon Harel, Merlyn Behr, Richard Lesh and Thomas Post', '1994', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '339, 340, 341', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Harel_1994_Invariance.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37650, 'When determining the measure of interior angles of a regular hexagon, students inappropriately rely on a previous visual decomposition into six equilateral triangles, incorrectly summing all triangle angles (6 x 180 = 1080) without subtracting the 360 degrees at the central vertex.', 'A student reasons that ''6 times 180 will give us the angles inside the hexagon'' because they remember the six triangles that formed a hexagon in a previous problem.', 'geometric', 'angles', 'interior angles of polygons', 'student', 'fifth-grade', 'JRME_Civil_2002_Chapter', 'Marta Civil', '2002', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'primary', '56, 57, 58', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Civil_2002_Chapter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37651, 'In identifying the basic repeating unit of a semiregular tessellation, students may incorrectly select a single regular polygon instead of the necessary combination of shapes that tile the plane.', 'For a tessellation made of hexagons and triangles, a student circled a single hexagon as the basic repeating tile.', 'geometric', 'tessellations', 'repeating tiles', 'student', 'fifth-grade', 'JRME_Civil_2002_Chapter', 'Marta Civil', '2002', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '54', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Civil_2002_Chapter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37652, 'Students mistakenly believe estimation should involve exact computation followed by rounding the final answer, failing to recognize that computational estimation fundamentally involves computing with approximate numbers.', 'A ninth grader preferred the compute-then-round method because it ''gives an exact amount and an estimated amount.''', 'whole_number', 'computational estimation', 'role of approximate numbers', 'student', 'Grades 3, 5, 7, and 9', 'JRME_Sowder_1989_Development', 'Judith T. Sowder and Margariete M. Wheeler', '1989', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '138, 139, 143, 144', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Sowder_1989_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37653, 'Students believe that estimation problems must yield a single right answer and refuse to accept multiple valid estimates or alternative valid estimation processes.', 'When evaluating two different acceptable rounding processes, students concluded ''Neither one used the right numbers,'' or insisted that only the process yielding the closer result to the exact computation was correct.', 'whole_number', 'computational estimation', 'multiple outcomes', 'student', 'Grades 3, 5, 7, and 9', 'JRME_Sowder_1989_Development', 'Judith T. Sowder and Margariete M. Wheeler', '1989', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '139, 140, 144', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Sowder_1989_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37654, 'Students apply school-taught rounding rules inflexibly and algorithmically, rejecting valid approximations that deviate from standard rounding rules.', 'Some students objected to rounding 267 to 250 rather than 300, arguing: ''You''re always taught to go up if it''s past five.''', 'whole_number', 'rounding', 'algorithmic rules', 'student', 'Grades 3, 5, 7, and 9', 'JRME_Sowder_1989_Development', 'Judith T. Sowder and Margariete M. Wheeler', '1989', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '144, 145', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Sowder_1989_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37655, 'When subtracting multi-digit numbers, a student may systematically subtract the smaller digit from the larger digit in each column, regardless of whether it is the minuend or subtrahend.', 'When calculating 32 - 23, a student explains that the difference is 11 because ''3 take away 2 is 1, and 2 take away 3 is 1.''', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'multi-digit subtraction', 'student', 'second-grade', 'JRME_Wood_1993_Chapter', 'Terry Wood, Paul Cobb, and Erna Yackel', '1993', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'primary', '64, 65', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Wood_1993_Chapter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37656, 'Students believe that an improper fraction cannot be represented using a single whole, arguing that it inherently requires multiple separate objects to exist.', 'When discussing the fraction 5/4, a student claims ''It''s possible with two things, but not with one.''', 'fraction', 'improper fractions', 'referent whole', 'student', 'second-grade', 'JRME_Wood_1993_Chapter', 'Terry Wood, Paul Cobb, and Erna Yackel', '1993', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '62', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Wood_1993_Chapter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37657, 'A student rigidly applies fraction notation rules to a context involving multiple wholes, asserting that the denominator cannot change even when the total number of parts in the new defined whole has increased.', 'When determining what fraction of two 12-slice pizzas is eaten by 7 students who each eat two slices, a student rejects the answer 14/24, incorrectly insisting ''You can''t change the 12. It''s 14/12.''', 'fraction', 'fractional representation', 'referent whole', 'student', 'fifth grade', 'JRME_Davis_1990_Chapter', 'Robert B. Davis and Carolyn A. Maher', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'primary', '73', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Davis_1990_Chapter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37658, 'A student misapplies an algorithmic procedure to a word problem, generating an incorrect answer that contradicts her own correct concrete representation, and struggles to reconcile the two.', 'After correctly using blocks to find that half of a third is one-sixth, the student attempts to show it with numbers by incorrectly applying the ''invert and multiply'' division algorithm (1/3 divided by 1/2 = 2/3), becoming confused when it doesn''t yield 1/6.', 'fraction', 'multiplication and division', 'algorithmic solution procedure', 'student', 'fifth grade', 'JRME_Davis_1990_Chapter', 'Robert B. Davis and Carolyn A. Maher', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'primary', '75, 76', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Davis_1990_Chapter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37659, 'Students lack the understanding that 100 represents the ''total'' or base of all percents, leading to uncertainty in contexts where percents must sum to a whole.', 'When asked what percent represents the total, one student suggested ''110 or 200%'' and chose the largest pie chart sector for a missing percent because ''they would probably [have] a lot left over.''', 'percent', 'meaning of percent', 'base of 100', 'student', 'grades 5, 7, 9, and 11', 'JRME_Lembke_1994_Development', 'Linda O. Lembke and Barbara J. Reys', '1994', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '244', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lembke_1994_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37660, 'Students regard percent as an absolute amount or quantity rather than recognizing its conceptual nature as a ratio.', 'One student said percents are used on the news ''because that is how much there is.''', 'percent', 'concept of percent', 'percent as ratio', 'student', 'grades 5, 7, 9, and 11', 'JRME_Lembke_1994_Development', 'Linda O. Lembke and Barbara J. Reys', '1994', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '243', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lembke_1994_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37661, 'Students blindly apply school-taught percent equation rules without relating them to the problem situation, leading to the generation of unreasonable answers.', 'In trying to determine 21% of 400, a 9th grader used a calculator to solve 21% x n = 400, yielding n = 1904.76, before reconsidering and using a different equation.', 'percent', 'percent applications', 'equations', 'student', 'grades 5, 7, 9, and 11', 'JRME_Lembke_1994_Development', 'Linda O. Lembke and Barbara J. Reys', '1994', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '248, 251, 256', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lembke_1994_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37662, 'Students who lack a fully developed part-whole scheme may fail to disembed a fractional part from the whole, merely pointing to a discrete location on a partitioned shape rather than representing the quantitative size of the fractional part.', 'When asked to make 5/8 of a bar partitioned into eight pieces, a student drew an arrow pointing to the fifth segment and wrote ''Answer: The place where I drew an arrow.''', 'fraction', 'fractional parts', 'disembedding', 'student', '5th- and 6th-grade', 'JRME_Norton_2018_Brief', 'Anderson Norton, Jesse L. M. Wilkins, and Cong ze Xu', '2018', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '211, 213', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Norton_2018_Brief.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37663, 'Students who have not yet constructed a splitting operation may incorrectly iterate the given whole rather than producing a piece that could be iterated to reproduce the given whole.', 'When told an unpartitioned stick was 3 times as long as another stick and asked to draw the other stick, a student drew a new stick that was three iterations of the original stick.', 'fraction', 'splitting operation', 'partitioning and iterating', 'student', '5th- and 6th-grade', 'JRME_Norton_2018_Brief', 'Anderson Norton, Jesse L. M. Wilkins, and Cong ze Xu', '2018', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '217, 218', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Norton_2018_Brief.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37664, 'Students incorrectly apply whole number ordering rules to fractions, judging a fraction with a larger denominator as greater because its integer is larger.', 'A student states ''One fifth is less ... because five is less than nine.''', 'fraction', 'fraction order', 'whole number dominance', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'JRME_Post_1985_Order', 'Thomas R. Post, Ipke Wachsmuth, Richard Lesh and Merlyn J. Behr', '1985', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '23, 24', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Post_1985_Order.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37665, 'Students experience linguistic interference when ordering fractions, confusing ''more'' (number of parts) with ''greater'' or ''bigger'' (size of parts).', 'When asked which of two fractions is less, a student might be unsure whether the question refers to the size of each subdivision or the number of subdivisions.', 'fraction', 'fraction order', 'linguistic interference', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'JRME_Post_1985_Order', 'Thomas R. Post, Ipke Wachsmuth, Richard Lesh and Merlyn J. Behr', '1985', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '24, 33, 34', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Post_1985_Order.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37666, 'Students invent a rule where they multiply the numerator and denominator, assuming the larger the product, the smaller the fraction.', 'A student orders 2/3 and 2/6 by reasoning ''Two thirds takes six pieces to cover the whole ... two sixths takes twelve pieces to cover the whole, so [2/6] has got to be smaller.''', 'fraction', 'fraction order', 'nonunit fractions', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'JRME_Post_1985_Order', 'Thomas R. Post, Ipke Wachsmuth, Richard Lesh and Merlyn J. Behr', '1985', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '25', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Post_1985_Order.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37667, 'Students lack thought flexibility to coordinate translations, meaning they can compare physical fraction pieces but cannot translate that physical judgment into a formal mathematical symbol statement.', 'A student visually recognizes ''One orange is greater than one purple'' but when prompted to use fractions states ''One fifth equals one orange'' instead of establishing the order relation.', 'fraction', 'fraction order', 'translation between modes', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'JRME_Post_1985_Order', 'Thomas R. Post, Ipke Wachsmuth, Richard Lesh and Merlyn J. Behr', '1985', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '23, 24', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Post_1985_Order.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37668, 'Students'' perception of partition lines in a fraction embodiment dominates their perception of area equivalence, preventing them from recognizing equivalent fractions in a single display.', 'A student is confused by a paper-folding display showing 3 parts with 2 shaded and 6 parts with 4 shaded simultaneously, unable to conclude that 2/3 covers the same amount as 4/6.', 'fraction', 'equivalent fractions', 'embodiment transformations', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'JRME_Post_1985_Order', 'Thomas R. Post, Ipke Wachsmuth, Richard Lesh and Merlyn J. Behr', '1985', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '26, 27', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Post_1985_Order.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37669, 'The student applies a buggy subtraction algorithm by reversing the order of the numbers in a column, always subtracting the smaller digit from the larger digit to avoid borrowing.', 'When calculating 703 minus 245, Talli switched the order of the numbers, subtracting 3 from 5 to get 2, 0 from 4 to get 4, and 2 from 7 to get 5, resulting in the incorrect answer 542.', 'whole_number', 'multi-digit subtraction', 'standard algorithm', 'student', '11th graders', 'JRME_BenYehuda_2005_Doing', 'Miriam Ben-Yehuda, Ilana Lavy, Liora Linchevski, Anna Sfard', '2005', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '209, 234, 235, 247', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_BenYehuda_2005_Doing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37670, 'The student incorrectly applies a false distribution law for multi-digit multiplication, multiplying the parts improperly instead of computing all partial products.', 'Mira attempts to perform multiplication by applying the false distribution law (a + b)(c + d) = ac + bd.', 'whole_number', 'multi-digit multiplication', 'distributive property', 'student', '11th graders', 'JRME_BenYehuda_2005_Doing', 'Miriam Ben-Yehuda, Ilana Lavy, Liora Linchevski, Anna Sfard', '2005', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '209, 228, 229, 247', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_BenYehuda_2005_Doing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37671, 'The student treats literate arithmetic procedures as rigid, authoritative rituals devoid of real-world context, failing to connect symbolic operations to situational meanings.', 'Talli could not tell a story or describe a real-life situation that would lead to the operation 24 times 9, stating ''I can''t explain it. It is multiplication.''', 'whole_number', 'multiplication', 'real-world contexts', 'student', '11th graders', 'JRME_BenYehuda_2005_Doing', 'Miriam Ben-Yehuda, Ilana Lavy, Liora Linchevski, Anna Sfard', '2005', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '214, 216, 238, 239', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_BenYehuda_2005_Doing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37672, 'Teachers constrained to reasoning with two levels of units struggle to shift referent units and coordinate parts of parts in drawn models, sometimes inappropriately discarding units they deem irrelevant.', 'When analyzing 2/3 divided by 1/4, a teacher discarded one third of the whole and treated the remaining two thirds as a new whole, which prevented her from maintaining the original whole as the referent unit for the one fourth.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'referent units and drawn models', 'in_service', 'middle-grades', 'JRME_Izsak_2012_Measuring', 'Andrew Izsák, Erik Jacobson, Zandra de Araujo, and Chandra Hawley Orrill', '2012', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '411, 413, 414, 415', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Izsak_2012_Measuring.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37673, 'Even when teachers can form three-level unit structures and conceptualize division as repeated subtraction, they may struggle to identify the appropriate referent unit for the quotient in complex fraction division problems.', 'In a whole-class discussion about why 2/3 divided by 1/4 equals 8/3, a teacher incorrectly assigned ''1'' as the referent unit for all quantities, stating ''8/3 of 1''.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'referent units for quotients', 'in_service', 'middle-grades', 'JRME_Izsak_2012_Measuring', 'Andrew Izsák, Erik Jacobson, Zandra de Araujo, and Chandra Hawley Orrill', '2012', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '418, 419', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Izsak_2012_Measuring.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37674, 'Teachers may rely on procedural numeric computations to guide their construction and interpretation of a drawn model rather than reasoning conceptually from the quantitative relationships depicted.', 'When attempting to model 2/3 divided by 3/4, a teacher calculated the incorrect answer 8/6 algorithmically and drew a grid shading 6 of 8 pieces to match her erroneous calculation.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'connecting drawn models to numeric methods', 'in_service', 'middle-grades', 'JRME_Izsak_2012_Measuring', 'Andrew Izsák, Erik Jacobson, Zandra de Araujo, and Chandra Hawley Orrill', '2012', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '412, 413', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Izsak_2012_Measuring.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37675, 'Students believe that only ''units of measure one'' can be partitioned, viewing composite units (like 2/3 of a cookie) merely as the result of a prior partitioning rather than as a unit that can be partitioned itself.', 'When asked to partition two thirds of a cookie into fourths, a student shaded two parts of a three-part circle and said, ''I have two (pieces) but I need four (pieces)... It''s already cut up. It''s not just one piece. I don''t think I can do this.''', 'fraction', 'partitioning', 'composite units', 'student', 'fifth-grade', 'JRME_Mack_2001_Building', 'Nancy K. Mack', '2001', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '276, 277, 285', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mack_2001_Building.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37676, 'Students view the individual pieces resulting from partitioning as independent whole-number counting units rather than as fractional portions of a referent whole.', 'To find 1/4 of 1/2 of a cookie, a student splits the half into 4 parts, and reasons ''if this [one half] is four [pieces], this whole thing is eight [pieces], and I gave her one part out of eight,'' treating the pieces as counting units.', 'fraction', 'multiplication', 'interpretation of parts', 'student', 'fifth-grade', 'JRME_Mack_2001_Building', 'Nancy K. Mack', '2001', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '276, 278, 280', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mack_2001_Building.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37677, 'When finding a fraction of a fraction, students rely strictly on the number of parts (e.g., 2/3 is simply ''2 out of 3 pieces'') and ignore remainder pieces if the grouping doesn''t come out evenly, failing to repartition the unit.', 'When finding 2/3 of 9/10, Lisa grouped pieces into threes and took two from each group, stating, ''If it doesn''t come out even, I pretend (the remaining) pieces are not there.''', 'fraction', 'multiplication', 'grouping pieces', 'student', 'fifth-grade', 'JRME_Mack_2001_Building', 'Nancy K. Mack', '2001', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '288, 289', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mack_2001_Building.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37678, 'The student produces an ambiguous representation for equal sharing that isolates the remainder without relating its fractional size to the whole.', 'Pho used 5 linking cubes to represent sharing 1 piece of paper among 4 children, taking away two and two, leaving one, without specifying its fractional size.', 'fraction', 'equal sharing', 'partitioning continuous quantities', 'student', 'first-grade', 'JRME_Empson_2003_Low-performing', 'Susan B. Empson', '2003', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '320, 321', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Empson_2003_Low-performing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37679, 'The student struggles to conceptually partition a discrete object that represents a continuous unit, believing the remainder cannot be shared.', 'Pho dealt 4 apples to each horse but said ''There''s one more left, but I can''t give them this'' and rejected another student''s idea to cut it in half because ''four and four is eight.''', 'fraction', 'equal sharing', 'halving a remainder', 'student', 'first-grade', 'JRME_Empson_2003_Low-performing', 'Susan B. Empson', '2003', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '322, 323', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Empson_2003_Low-performing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37680, 'The student fails to differentiate between the count of whole objects and fractional objects when quantifying a total share.', 'After splitting a ''pretend'' apple (linking cube) in half, Patrick initially counts the total share for one horse as ''five'' (four whole apples and one half apple).', 'fraction', 'quantifying fractional shares', 'mixed numbers', 'student', 'first-grade', 'JRME_Empson_2003_Low-performing', 'Susan B. Empson', '2003', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '324', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Empson_2003_Low-performing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37681, 'The student believes that partitioning a remainder among a large number of people will result in pieces too small to be viable, creating a barrier to solving the problem.', 'When asked to share 2 left-over pancakes among 6 children, Patrick says ''we can''t split ''em in half... ''Cause then each gets teeny-weeny-tiny pieces.''', 'fraction', 'equal sharing', 'partitioning a remainder', 'student', 'first-grade', 'JRME_Empson_2003_Low-performing', 'Susan B. Empson', '2003', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '325, 326', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Empson_2003_Low-performing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37682, 'The student misidentifies a fractional representation by counting irrelevant features like internal lines rather than examining the size and number of the equal regions.', 'Pho incorrectly stated a circle partitioned into thirds but with extra internal lines stood for a fourth ''because there''s four lines here, four lines here, and four lines here.''', 'fraction', 'fraction representations', 'area models', 'student', 'first-grade', 'JRME_Empson_2003_Low-performing', 'Susan B. Empson', '2003', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'secondary', '327', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Empson_2003_Low-performing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37683, 'The student incorrectly generalizes the informal principle that ''more people sharing means a smaller share'' to situations involving equivalent ratios.', 'When comparing 2 children sharing 3 chocolate bars vs. 6 children sharing 9 bars, Patrick said the table of six would get less because there were more children.', 'proportional', 'fraction equivalence', 'comparison of ratios', 'student', 'first-grade', 'JRME_Empson_2003_Low-performing', 'Susan B. Empson', '2003', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '316', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Empson_2003_Low-performing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37684, 'Students make errors in symbolic manipulation when attempting to solve a linear equation with fractions, incorrectly adding a term instead of subtracting it.', 'Gal and Roni wrote x + 450 = x + x/5, rewrote terms as fractions, and mistakenly added an x to the left side to get 2x/1 + 450/1 = x/5.', 'algebraic', 'linear equations', 'symbolic manipulation', 'student', 'Grades 7 to 9', 'JRME_Yerushalmy_2006_Slower', 'Michal Yerushalmy', '2006', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'primary', '371, 372, 373', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Yerushalmy_2006_Slower.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37685, 'Students construct an incorrect algebraic expression from a word problem by misplacing operations, which produces a graph that contradicts their expected numerical values.', 'Gal and Roni typed the mistaken expression (10 - x) * 2 * x instead of (10 - x * 2) * x for the Number Game problem.', 'algebraic', 'formulating expressions', 'word problems', 'student', 'Grades 7 to 9', 'JRME_Yerushalmy_2006_Slower', 'Michal Yerushalmy', '2006', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '369, 370, 371', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Yerushalmy_2006_Slower.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37686, 'Students struggle to formulate and manipulate algebraic inequalities or equations to model the relationship between two changing quantities, often relying on poorly remembered rules of thumb.', 'For the Age problem, Inbar and Mika wrote incorrect models like 2 + (x + 24.5 * 1.5) > x + 2, and after manipulating incorrectly, arrived at 24 = x without understanding what it meant.', 'algebraic', 'formulating equations', 'word problems', 'student', 'Grades 7 to 9', 'JRME_Yerushalmy_2006_Slower', 'Michal Yerushalmy', '2006', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'primary', '373, 374, 383, 385', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Yerushalmy_2006_Slower.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37687, 'A student believes that graphing two linear functions to find their intersection is useless for an age comparison problem because the two aging processes represent ages that can never be equal.', 'Inbar argues against graphing the father''s and son''s ages to find a solution, stating, ''But father and son can''t be the same age!''', 'algebraic', 'function representation', 'intersecting graphs', 'student', 'Grades 7 to 9', 'JRME_Yerushalmy_2006_Slower', 'Michal Yerushalmy', '2006', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'secondary', '373, 374, 375', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Yerushalmy_2006_Slower.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37688, 'When confronted with multiplicative word problems, students may fail to impose an appropriate equal-sized-group mathematical structure, instead simply adding the given numbers together or counting irrelevant features.', 'In response to Problem 1 (2 tables, 4 children at each), Michelle put out three blue and five red cubes in two groups of four, said there were three boys and five girls, and calculated ''3 + 5 = 8.''', 'whole_number', 'multiplication', 'word problems', 'student', 'Grades 2 and 3', 'JRME_Mulligan_1997_Young', 'Joanne T. Mulligan and Michael C. Mitchelmore', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '315, 316, 317', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mulligan_1997_Young.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37689, 'Students misinterpret the language of quotition division problems by associating the stated numbers with incorrect physical features, fundamentally changing the mathematical structure they model.', 'Children often interpreted the phrase ''There are 2 children at each table'' as ''The children are at 2 tables.''', 'whole_number', 'division', 'quotition', 'student', 'Grades 2 and 3', 'JRME_Mulligan_1997_Young', 'Joanne T. Mulligan and Michael C. Mitchelmore', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '323', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mulligan_1997_Young.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37690, 'Students rely on superficial linguistic cues rather than an understanding of the semantic structure to select a calculation strategy.', 'Lisa responded to a comparison problem by saying, ''times as many ... that''s multiply ... three fours.''', 'whole_number', 'multiplication', 'multiplicative comparison', 'student', 'Grades 2 and 3', 'JRME_Mulligan_1997_Young', 'Joanne T. Mulligan and Michael C. Mitchelmore', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '320', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mulligan_1997_Young.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37691, 'Children applying written subtraction algorithms manipulate symbols without preserving the meaning of the quantities or place value, leading them to accept absurd answers such as remainders larger than the minuend.', 'When subtracting 35 from 200 using a vertical written arrangement, a child incorrectly computed 235 and initially accepted this result without recognizing the logical contradiction.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'written algorithms', 'student', 'third graders', 'JRME_Carraher_1987_Written', 'Terezinha Nunes Carraher, David William Carraher and Analucia Dias Schliemann', '1987', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '94, 95', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Carraher_1987_Written.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37692, 'Children are confused by the explicit representation of zeroes in written subtraction algorithms, often failing to borrow correctly and questioning the mathematical value of the zero digit itself.', 'When subtracting 5 from 0 in written form, children often hesitated as to whether the result was 5 or 0, and asked questions like ''Is this zero a number?''', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'zeroes', 'student', 'third graders', 'JRME_Carraher_1987_Written', 'Terezinha Nunes Carraher, David William Carraher and Analucia Dias Schliemann', '1987', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '94', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Carraher_1987_Written.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37693, 'Students apply an incorrect algorithm to add fractions, typically by adding the numerators together and the denominators together.', 'Ted mentally computed the sum of 5/6 and 4/7 as ''nine thirteenths'' by adding the top numbers and bottom numbers.', 'fraction', 'fraction addition', 'algorithms', 'student', 'fourth and fifth grade', 'JRME_Behr_1985_Construct', 'Merlyn J. Behr, Ipke Wachsmuth and Thomas R. Post', '1985', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '125, 128', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Behr_1985_Construct.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37694, 'Students attempt to estimate the sum of two fractions using a reference point but misjudge the sizes of the fractions due to a lack of understanding of fraction equivalence and order.', 'Jessie incorrectly reasoned that 5/6 is ''less than a half'', leading to an incorrect estimation of the sum.', 'fraction', 'fraction size', 'order and equivalence', 'student', 'fourth and fifth grade', 'JRME_Behr_1985_Construct', 'Merlyn J. Behr, Ipke Wachsmuth and Thomas R. Post', '1985', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '124, 125', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Behr_1985_Construct.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37695, 'Students make a gross estimate of fraction sizes based on flawed rules, such as incorrectly generalizing that the sum of two proper fractions will always be less than 1.', 'Ted understood that fractions with a denominator greater than the numerator are less than 1, but incorrectly generalized that the sum of two such fractions would automatically be less than 1.', 'fraction', 'fraction addition', 'estimation', 'student', 'fourth and fifth grade', 'JRME_Behr_1985_Construct', 'Merlyn J. Behr, Ipke Wachsmuth and Thomas R. Post', '1985', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '125, 128', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Behr_1985_Construct.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37696, 'When modeling metric area units, students view squares solely in terms of linear dimensions and draw them as isolated models rather than recognizing how linear dimensions relate to shared area.', 'Students built a square meter by forming the perimeter first, and modeled smaller metric units by drawing isolated squares inside one another rather than sharing a corner to show proportional relationships.', 'measurement', 'area and linear measurement', 'metric units', 'mixed', 'middle schools', 'JRME_Henningsen_1997_Mathematical', 'Marjorie Henningsen and Mary Kay Stein', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '543, 544', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Henningsen_1997_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37697, 'Students are unable to distinguish between basic angle types, which hinders their ability to systematically compare, record, and discover geometric properties.', '', 'geometric', 'angles', 'comparing and classifying', 'mixed', 'middle schools', 'JRME_Henningsen_1997_Mathematical', 'Marjorie Henningsen and Mary Kay Stein', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '545', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Henningsen_1997_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37698, 'There exist numbers on the real number line that are infinitely close to each other.', 'The student identifies ''3.999999 repeating forever'' and ''4'' as two different numbers that are infinitely close to one another.', 'decimal', 'real number line', 'infinitely close numbers', 'student', 'sophomore', 'JRME_Ely_2010_Nonstandard', 'Robert Ely', '2010', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '127, 128, 129', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Ely_2010_Nonstandard.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37699, 'Infinitesimal numbers and distances exist and can be represented using infinite decimal expansions with extra digits placed at the end.', 'The student wrote 0.000...1 (point zero repeating infinitely with a 1 after it) to represent an infinitely small number located between 0.999... and 1.', 'decimal', 'infinitesimal numbers', 'decimal notation', 'student', 'sophomore', 'JRME_Ely_2010_Nonstandard', 'Robert Ely', '2010', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '129, 130, 131', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Ely_2010_Nonstandard.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37700, 'Infinitesimal numbers can be operated upon using standard arithmetic, with squaring producing smaller infinitesimals and reciprocation producing varying sizes of infinite numbers.', 'The student reasoned that 1 divided by 0.000...1 produces a number that gets ''bigger and bigger'', while 1 divided by 0.000...01 is a ''bigger infinity than that''.', 'calculus', 'operations on infinitesimals', 'infinite numbers', 'student', 'sophomore', 'JRME_Ely_2010_Nonstandard', 'Robert Ely', '2010', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '133, 134, 135, 136', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Ely_2010_Nonstandard.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37701, 'The repeating decimal 0.999... does not equal 1, because accepting their equality would violate the internal consistency of the student''s nonstandard infinitesimal framework.', 'The student rejected the algebraic proof that 0.999... equals 1, arguing that ''The only reason to have 0.9 repeating is to show that it''s not 1!''', 'decimal', 'repeating decimals', 'equality to 1', 'student', 'sophomore', 'JRME_Ely_2010_Nonstandard', 'Robert Ely', '2010', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '137, 138', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Ely_2010_Nonstandard.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37702, 'Students erroneously equate the mathematical concept of average with the mode, believing the typical value is simply the one that occurs most frequently, rather than representing the entire data set.', 'On the Potato Chips Problem, a student placed most prices at $1.38 to make it typical, and only raised a few others to make it realistic.', 'statistics', 'average', 'mode', 'student', 'fourth through eighth graders', 'JRME_Mokros_1995_Children''s', 'Jan Mokros and Susan Jo Russell', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '27, 28', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mokros_1995_Children''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37703, 'Students blindly apply the averaging algorithm without checking if the result is reasonable or mathematically possible for the given context.', 'On the Elevator Problem, a student divided a total weight of 1580 pounds by 2 groups instead of 10 individuals, getting an absurd average weight of 790 pounds per person, and concluded ''I can live with that.''', 'statistics', 'average', 'mean algorithm', 'student', 'fourth through eighth graders', 'JRME_Mokros_1995_Children''s', 'Jan Mokros and Susan Jo Russell', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '29', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mokros_1995_Children''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37704, 'Students misapply mathematical operations to create ''meaningless algorithms'' for generating data points that supposedly average to a target number.', 'To get an average of $1.38, one student created pairs of numbers where the cents summed to 38, such as $1.08 and $1.30.', 'statistics', 'average', 'data construction', 'student', 'fourth through eighth graders', 'JRME_Mokros_1995_Children''s', 'Jan Mokros and Susan Jo Russell', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '29', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mokros_1995_Children''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37705, 'Students falsely believe that an exact average cannot be calculated or evaluated without knowing the precise individual values of every piece of data.', 'A student correctly calculated the total weight for 10 people but balked at finding the average, stating you couldn''t know it exactly because ''you don''t know exactly how much each woman or each male... weighs.''', 'statistics', 'average', 'exactness', 'student', 'fourth through eighth graders', 'JRME_Mokros_1995_Children''s', 'Jan Mokros and Susan Jo Russell', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '30, 31', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mokros_1995_Children''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37706, 'Students mistakenly believe that an average requires the sum of all data values below the mean to equal the sum of all data values above the mean.', 'Billy constructed a data set where the sum of values below $1.50 equaled the sum of values above $1.50, failing to realize this produced an actual mean of $.72.', 'statistics', 'average', 'balance point', 'student', 'fourth through eighth graders', 'JRME_Mokros_1995_Children''s', 'Jan Mokros and Susan Jo Russell', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '33, 34', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mokros_1995_Children''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37707, 'Students view the average strictly as a midpoint and are unable to break the ''symmetry barrier,'' mistakenly assuming a data distribution must be perfectly symmetrical around the mean.', 'When asked to adjust a distribution without using the target average as a data point, students either said it couldn''t be done or struggled to compensate nonsymmetrically.', 'statistics', 'average', 'symmetry', 'student', 'fourth through eighth graders', 'JRME_Mokros_1995_Children''s', 'Jan Mokros and Susan Jo Russell', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '31, 32', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mokros_1995_Children''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37708, 'Students reject negative numbers as legitimate quantities because they view numbers strictly through a magnitude-based or cardinal lens, struggling to conceive of a quantity that is less than nothing.', 'Negative numbers aren''t really numbers because we don''t really count with them in school. And there''s no negative 1 cube.', 'integer', 'negative numbers', 'meaning of negative numbers', 'student', 'Grades 1-4', 'JRME_Bishop_2014_Obstacles', 'Jessica Pierson Bishop, Lisa L. Lamb, Randolph A. Philipp, Ian Whitacre, Bonnie P. Schappelle and Melinda L. Lewis', '2014', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '44', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bishop_2014_Obstacles.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37709, 'Students believe it is impossible to subtract a larger number from a smaller number, viewing subtraction strictly as removing or separating objects from a concrete set.', 'Three minus five is zero because you have 3 and you can''t take away 5, so take away the 3 and it leaves you with zero.', 'integer', 'subtraction', 'subtrahend greater than minuend', 'student', 'Grades 1-4', 'JRME_Bishop_2014_Obstacles', 'Jessica Pierson Bishop, Lisa L. Lamb, Randolph A. Philipp, Ian Whitacre, Bonnie P. Schappelle and Melinda L. Lewis', '2014', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '45', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bishop_2014_Obstacles.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37710, 'Students overgeneralize their experiences with whole numbers, assuming that addition must always result in a larger number and subtraction must always result in a smaller number.', 'In response to the problem 6 + c = 4, a student said, ''What''s that plus for? Isn''t it supposed to be a minus?''', 'integer', 'addition and subtraction', 'direction of effect', 'student', 'Grades 1-4', 'JRME_Bishop_2014_Obstacles', 'Jessica Pierson Bishop, Lisa L. Lamb, Randolph A. Philipp, Ian Whitacre, Bonnie P. Schappelle and Melinda L. Lewis', '2014', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '45', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bishop_2014_Obstacles.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37711, 'Students interpret the hour on an analog clock based on strict precise alignment or by treating the interval as a discrete container, incorrectly stating the prior hour if the hand is not exactly on the dot.', 'When asked to identify 7:00 with the hour hand directly on the 7, a student stated ''Six o''clock'' because the hour hand was ''not precise'' and therefore ''you go backwards.''', 'measurement', 'telling time', 'interpreting analog clock hands', 'student', 'Grades 2 and 4', 'JRME_Earnest_2017_Clock', 'Darrell Earnest', '2017', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '194, 205, 206, 216', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Earnest_2017_Clock.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37712, 'Students treat the hour and minute hands as independent, incorrectly believing the hour hand points exactly at the hour numeral until the minute hand reaches the half-hour mark, at which point it jumps to the midpoint.', 'To show 8:30 as the result of an elapsed time task, a student accurately moved the minute hand to the 6 but placed the hour hand directly on the 8.', 'measurement', 'elapsed time', 'hour-minute proportional relations', 'student', 'Grades 2 and 4', 'JRME_Earnest_2017_Clock', 'Darrell Earnest', '2017', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '207, 208, 209, 210, 216', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Earnest_2017_Clock.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37713, 'Students apply a rigid procedural rule to elapsed time in digital notation by mapping words like ''half'' directly to ''30'' in the final answer, failing to compose the elapsed minutes with the starting minutes.', 'When asked to add an hour and a half to 5:30, a student wrote 6:30, reasoning that ''half an hour is 30 minutes, so I just put a 30 there.''', 'measurement', 'elapsed time', 'composing digital hour and minute units', 'student', 'Grades 2 and 4', 'JRME_Earnest_2017_Clock', 'Darrell Earnest', '2017', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '211, 212, 213, 214', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Earnest_2017_Clock.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37714, 'Students confuse the natural language meaning of a term with its mathematical meaning, or confuse a word with its antonym.', 'If the word ''similar'' is taken to mean ''closely resembling but not identical'', a child might reject the correct response because it has the identical shape of the original figure.', 'geometric', 'geometric figures', 'vocabulary', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Marshall_1983_Sex', 'Sandra P. Marshall', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '329', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Marshall_1983_Sex.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37715, 'Students make translation errors when working with ratios and decimals, such as inverting the ratio or ignoring decimal placement.', 'A child inverts the ratio of 350:14 and ignores decimal placement.', 'ratio', 'ratios and decimals', 'translation', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Marshall_1983_Sex', 'Sandra P. Marshall', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '329, 332', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Marshall_1983_Sex.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37716, 'Students misinterpret units of scale on a graph or geometric figure.', '', 'measurement', 'graphs and figures', 'scale', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Marshall_1983_Sex', 'Sandra P. Marshall', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '332', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Marshall_1983_Sex.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37717, 'Students demonstrate errors in skill or algorithm mastery, such as choosing an incorrect arithmetic operation or using incorrect decimal alignment.', '', 'decimal', 'arithmetic operations', 'algorithms', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Marshall_1983_Sex', 'Sandra P. Marshall', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '332', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Marshall_1983_Sex.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37718, 'Students exhibit negative transfer by combining numbers based on familiar patterns with little regard to the specific context of the problem.', 'A child forms a habit of subtracting when two quantities of money are presented and incorrectly applies this to a different problem structure.', 'whole_number', 'word problems', 'operations', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Marshall_1983_Sex', 'Sandra P. Marshall', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '332, 333', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Marshall_1983_Sex.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37719, 'Students do not persevere to the end of a computation or fail to repeatedly perform a required algorithm, settling for an intermediate value.', 'Calculating the distance between the first two points to be 12 and the midpoint as 6, but failing to repeat this procedure to find the next point.', 'whole_number', 'multistep problems', 'algorithms', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Marshall_1983_Sex', 'Sandra P. Marshall', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '333', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Marshall_1983_Sex.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37720, 'Students incorrectly associate an arithmetic operation with certain key words in a word problem without understanding the full context.', 'Using the key word ''of'' to trigger fraction multiplication, or ''how many'' to trigger addition.', 'fraction', 'word problems', 'key words', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Marshall_1983_Sex', 'Sandra P. Marshall', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '333', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Marshall_1983_Sex.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37721, 'Students experience interference from related concepts or expected representations, such as confusing perimeter with area or misaligning horizontal calculations.', 'Using a formula for area when perimeter is appropriate, or experiencing interference with expected vertical alignment when a problem is presented horizontally.', 'measurement', 'area and perimeter', 'formulas', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Marshall_1983_Sex', 'Sandra P. Marshall', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '333', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Marshall_1983_Sex.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37722, 'Students guess answers according to irrelevant rules or fixed patterns when in doubt, such as choosing the smallest value, the largest value, or the first/last option.', '', 'other', 'multiple-choice tests', 'guessing strategies', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Marshall_1983_Sex', 'Sandra P. Marshall', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '333, 334', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Marshall_1983_Sex.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37723, 'Teachers believe that mathematical proofs are fallible constructs and that a counterexample or contradictory evidence might be found to render a proven statement invalid.', 'Six teachers responded that it might be possible to find a counterexample to a proof, stating ''Somebody could finally come up with a counterexample that proved it wrong.''', 'other', 'proof and justification', 'generality and fallibility', 'in_service', 'secondary school (Grades 9-12)', 'JRME_Knuth_2002_Secondary', 'Eric J. Knuth', '2002', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '387, 388', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Knuth_2002_Secondary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37724, 'Teachers accept empirical evidence, such as testing a few specific cases or using a visual demonstration, as a mathematically valid formal proof.', 'Five teachers rated an empirically based argument, which tore up the angles of a triangle to form a straight line, as a valid proof.', 'geometric', 'proof and justification', 'empirical evidence', 'in_service', 'secondary school (Grades 9-12)', 'JRME_Knuth_2002_Secondary', 'Eric J. Knuth', '2002', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '391, 392', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Knuth_2002_Secondary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37725, 'Teachers evaluate an algebraic argument as a valid proof based solely on the correctness of the symbolic manipulations, failing to recognize that the argument illogically proves the converse.', 'Ten teachers considered an argument to be a proof because the algebraic steps were correct, even though the argument started by assuming the conclusion (proving the converse).', 'algebraic', 'proof and justification', 'proving the converse', 'in_service', 'secondary school (Grades 9-12)', 'JRME_Knuth_2002_Secondary', 'Eric J. Knuth', '2002', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '392, 393', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Knuth_2002_Secondary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37726, 'Teachers accept a proof of a specific, particular case as a valid proof for the general case.', 'Four teachers rated a proof showing that 756 is divisible by 3 because the sum of its digits is divisible by 3 as a valid proof for the general divisibility rule for any whole number.', 'whole_number', 'proof and justification', 'particular vs. general cases', 'in_service', 'secondary school (Grades 9-12)', 'JRME_Knuth_2002_Secondary', 'Eric J. Knuth', '2002', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '394', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Knuth_2002_Secondary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37727, 'Students attempt to determine the sign of an integer multiplication product by misapplying a rule from integer addition, assigning a negative value because the negative sign is on the larger factor.', 'A pair of students solved 3 x -4 by determining the magnitude (3 x 4 = 12) and then assigning the product a negative value ''because the minus was on the bigger number.''', 'integer', 'integer multiplication', 'sign rules', 'mixed', 'seventh-grade', 'JRME_Davis_2003_Understanding', 'Brent Davis and Elaine Simmt', '2003', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '158, 159', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Davis_2003_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37728, 'Students misunderstand the requirements of a mathematical explanation in an exploratory classroom setting, viewing the task as an exercise in rule-generation rather than the interpretation of concepts.', 'Three pairs of students saw the task of explaining 3 x -4 purely in terms of generation of rules, ''playing the wrong game'' instead of engaging in conceptual interpretation.', 'other', 'mathematical explanation', 'argumentation vs rule-generation', 'mixed', 'seventh-grade', 'JRME_Davis_2003_Understanding', 'Brent Davis and Elaine Simmt', '2003', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'secondary', '163', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Davis_2003_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37729, 'Students create overly restrictive, overly broad, or procedurally focused descriptions when attempting to mathematically define geometric concepts, lacking an understanding of essentiality and precision.', 'All the possible series of points equidistant from a single point.', 'geometric', 'definitions', 'circle', 'student', 'secondary school', 'JRME_Borasi_1994_Capitalizing', 'Raffaella Borasi', '1994', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '175, 176, 177, 178', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Borasi_1994_Capitalizing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37730, 'When finding the equation of a circle passing through three given points, a student inappropriately sets up an analytic system using only two of the points, resulting in an unresolvable equation in two variables.', 'Setting up (7 - h)^2 + (0 - k)^2 = r^2 and (5 - h)^2 + (4 - k)^2 = r^2 and solving to get 2k = h - 2.', 'geometric', 'analytic geometry', 'circle through points', 'student', 'secondary school', 'JRME_Borasi_1994_Capitalizing', 'Raffaella Borasi', '1994', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '178, 179', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Borasi_1994_Capitalizing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37731, 'Students are presented with and initially attempt to verify an intentionally incorrect theorem stating that the sum of interior angles of a polygon is simply 180 degrees times the number of sides.', 'In any polygon the sum of the interior angles is 180 degrees times the number of sides.', 'geometric', 'polygons', 'sum of interior angles', 'student', 'secondary school', 'JRME_Borasi_1994_Capitalizing', 'Raffaella Borasi', '1994', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '180, 181, 182', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Borasi_1994_Capitalizing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37732, 'The extension of exponentiation rules yields contradictory results for zero to the power of zero, creating cognitive dissonance about the certainty of mathematical rules.', 'Using one pattern (2^0=1, 1^0=1), 0^0 should be 1. Using another pattern (0^2=0, 0^1=0), 0^0 should be 0.', 'algebraic', 'exponentiation', 'zero to the power of zero', 'student', 'secondary school', 'JRME_Borasi_1994_Capitalizing', 'Raffaella Borasi', '1994', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '183, 184', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Borasi_1994_Capitalizing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37733, 'Students erroneously use an additive strategy, assuming a constant additive difference between pairs of items rather than a multiplicative relationship.', 'Incorrectly reasoning that a difference of two between the first pair of items meant that there should also be a difference of two between the second pair of items.', 'proportional', 'proportional reasoning', 'additive strategy', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Lawton_1993_Contextual', 'Carol A. Lawton', '1993', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '463, 464', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lawton_1993_Contextual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37734, 'Students assume that two similar items in a mixture problem share a one-to-one correspondence and fail to recognize the need to convert units.', '', 'proportional', 'mixture problems', 'unit conversion', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Lawton_1993_Contextual', 'Carol A. Lawton', '1993', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '460', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lawton_1993_Contextual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37735, 'Students use reversal reasoning in mixture problems, falsely assuming that swapping the item quantities yields the correct proportional amount.', 'If four cups of the first item were mixed with six cups of the second, then six cups of the first item must be mixed with four cups of the second.', 'proportional', 'mixture problems', 'reversal reasoning', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Lawton_1993_Contextual', 'Carol A. Lawton', '1993', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '465', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lawton_1993_Contextual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37736, 'Students harbor the misconception that two items in a mixture must always add up to a constant total.', '', 'proportional', 'mixture problems', 'constant total', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Lawton_1993_Contextual', 'Carol A. Lawton', '1993', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '465', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lawton_1993_Contextual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37737, 'Prospective teachers believe that all functions are equations or can always be represented by formulas or algebraic expressions.', 'Bob stated that he thinks all functions can be written in terms of equations, such as trigonometric equations.', 'algebraic', 'functions', 'arbitrariness and representation', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JRME_Even_1993_Subject-matter', 'Ruhama Even', '1993', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '104, 105', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Even_1993_Subject-matter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37738, 'Prospective teachers expect the graphs of functions to be continuous, smooth, or nice, mistakenly rejecting valid functions that have discontinuities or sharp points.', 'Brian rejected a piecewise valid function because it was discontinuous and did not look pretty, while Tracy rejected a function because it had sharp corners or discontinuities.', 'algebraic', 'functions', 'graphical representation', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JRME_Even_1993_Subject-matter', 'Ruhama Even', '1993', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '105, 106', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Even_1993_Subject-matter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37739, 'Prospective teachers believe that functions must have specific names or familiar shapes, leading them to conclude that only a finite number of functions can pass through two or three given points.', 'When asked how many functions pass through two given points, Brian guessed just four because he could only infer based on the two familiar functions he knew.', 'algebraic', 'functions', 'arbitrary relations', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JRME_Even_1993_Subject-matter', 'Ruhama Even', '1993', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '107, 108', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Even_1993_Subject-matter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37740, 'Prospective teachers exhibit compartmentalized knowledge regarding the univalence requirement, inconsistently applying the vertical line test and sometimes accepting familiar shapes like circles as functions despite failing the test.', 'Brian stated that a circle is a function but acknowledged it does not pass the vertical line test, demonstrating conflicting schemes.', 'algebraic', 'functions', 'univalence', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JRME_Even_1993_Subject-matter', 'Ruhama Even', '1993', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '109, 110, 111', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Even_1993_Subject-matter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37741, 'Students incorrectly extend the 50% probability or 1:2 ratio of a single coin toss outcome to any compound event, asserting that any specific outcome of multiple coin tosses always has a 50% chance of occurring.', 'When asked the probability of getting all tails if 100 or 100,000 quarters are flipped, a student maintained the answer is 50%.', 'probability', 'compound events', 'coin tossing', 'student', 'grades 5, 7, 9, and 11', 'JRME_Rubel_2007_Middle', 'Laurie H. Rubel', '2007', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '541, 542, 543', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Rubel_2007_Middle.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37742, 'Students exhibit the negative recency effect, believing that a sequence of independent coin tosses must ''even out,'' so an outcome that has occurred several times in a row will likely be followed by the opposite outcome.', 'A student predicted that after four consecutive heads, the fifth toss would be tails because ''the probability has to even out, to equal out.''', 'probability', 'independent events', 'coin tossing', 'student', 'grades 5, 7, 9, and 11', 'JRME_Rubel_2007_Middle', 'Laurie H. Rubel', '2007', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '544, 545', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Rubel_2007_Middle.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37743, 'Students exhibit the positive recency effect, assuming that a repeated outcome in a series of independent random trials constitutes a pattern that will continue.', 'A student claimed that heads would be the most likely outcome of a fifth toss after four consecutive heads because ''it''s constant'' and ''you can look for patterns''.', 'probability', 'independent events', 'coin tossing', 'student', 'grades 5, 7, 9, and 11', 'JRME_Rubel_2007_Middle', 'Laurie H. Rubel', '2007', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '545', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Rubel_2007_Middle.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37744, 'Students resolve cognitive conflicts between their probabilistic intuitions and mathematical rules by compartmentalizing their knowledge, attributing mathematically normative answers to the classroom and heuristic-driven answers to the ''real world.''', 'A student stated that ''strictly mathematically'' all coin toss sequences are equally likely, but in a ''real-world scenario,'' an alternating sequence like HTHTHT is more likely.', 'probability', 'independent events', 'mathematical epistemology', 'student', 'grades 5, 7, 9, and 11', 'JRME_Rubel_2007_Middle', 'Laurie H. Rubel', '2007', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'secondary', '551, 552, 553', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Rubel_2007_Middle.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37745, 'A child struggles to solve a fraction subtraction problem when presented symbolically, but correctly solves the exact same mathematical problem when it is situated in a relevant real-world context.', 'A child incorrectly solved 4 - 1/8 represented symbolically, then correctly solved the problem situated in the context of eating 1/8 of one of four cookies.', 'fraction', 'subtraction', 'context versus symbolic representation', 'PST', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Philipp_2007_Effects_a', 'Randolph A. Philipp, Rebecca Ambrose, Lisa L. C. Lamb, Judith T. Sowder, Bonnie P. Schappelle, Larry Sowder, Eva Thanheiser, Jennifer Chauvot', '2007', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '460', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Philipp_2007_Effects_a.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37746, 'Children correctly execute the standard algorithm for multidigit subtraction but demonstrate a lack of understanding of the underlying place-value concepts required for the procedure.', 'Children correctly applied the standard algorithm for multidigit subtraction but demonstrated a lack of understanding of place-value concepts when probed with questions by the PSTs.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'standard algorithm and place value', 'PST', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Philipp_2007_Effects_a', 'Randolph A. Philipp, Rebecca Ambrose, Lisa L. C. Lamb, Judith T. Sowder, Bonnie P. Schappelle, Larry Sowder, Eva Thanheiser, Jennifer Chauvot', '2007', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '466', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Philipp_2007_Effects_a.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37747, 'Prospective elementary school teachers fail to recognize the equivalence of simple fractions or identify the larger of two fractions sharing the same denominator.', '16% of the PSTs did not identify 1/2 and 3/6 as equal, and 10% failed to identify 3/7 as larger than 2/7 on a pretest.', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison and equivalence', '', 'PST', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Philipp_2007_Effects_a', 'Randolph A. Philipp, Rebecca Ambrose, Lisa L. C. Lamb, Judith T. Sowder, Bonnie P. Schappelle, Larry Sowder, Eva Thanheiser, Jennifer Chauvot', '2007', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '469', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Philipp_2007_Effects_a.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37748, 'Students mistakenly believe that the value of a fraction or rate remains the same whenever both the numerator and denominator decrease or increase, failing to recognize that such changes can be proportional or nonproportional.', 'When asked what happens to a rate if the numerator and denominator both decrease or increase, the most frequent answer given by students was that the fraction or rate remains the same.', 'proportional', 'qualitative reasoning', 'directional reasoning', 'student', 'seventh- and eighth-grade', 'JRME_Heller_1990_Qualitative', 'Patricia M. Heller, Thomas R. Post, Merlyn Behr, Richard Lesh', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '395, 398', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Heller_1990_Qualitative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37749, 'Students view context-free fraction exercises and proportion-related word problems as distinct tasks requiring different reasoning processes, failing to apply known rational number skills to structurally identical word problems.', '', 'proportional', 'problem solving', 'structural similarity', 'student', 'seventh- and eighth-grade', 'JRME_Heller_1990_Qualitative', 'Patricia M. Heller, Thomas R. Post, Merlyn Behr, Richard Lesh', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '400', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Heller_1990_Qualitative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37750, 'The prospective teacher lacks conceptual knowledge of geometric formulas, such as the derivation of pi, and instead relies on rote memory aids and arbitrary rules to teach students.', 'Think about it. Area has an r sound in it. A-r-e-a and radius.', 'geometric', 'area and circumference of circles', 'pi', 'PST', 'grades K-8', 'JRME_Eisenhart_1993_Conceptual', 'Margaret Eisenhart, Hilda Borko, Robert Underhill, Catherine Brown, Doug Jones and Patricia Agard', '1993', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '18', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Eisenhart_1993_Conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37751, 'The prospective teacher procedurally memorizes visual diagrams for operations without understanding the conceptual link between the diagram and the algorithm.', 'She drew a rectangle for 2.35 x .7, shaded intersections, but then decided what place value each product had and couldn''t complete the explanation.', 'decimal', 'multiplication', 'area model', 'PST', 'grades K-8', 'JRME_Eisenhart_1993_Conceptual', 'Margaret Eisenhart, Hilda Borko, Robert Underhill, Catherine Brown, Doug Jones and Patricia Agard', '1993', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '16, 17', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Eisenhart_1993_Conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37752, 'In attempting to provide a concrete conceptual example for division of fractions, the prospective teacher inadvertently generates a scenario that models multiplication of fractions.', 'What portion of a wall would you be able to paint if 1/4 of the wall were painted and you had enough paint to cover 1/2 of the remaining area?', 'fraction', 'division', 'concrete models', 'PST', 'grades K-8', 'JRME_Eisenhart_1993_Conceptual', 'Margaret Eisenhart, Hilda Borko, Robert Underhill, Catherine Brown, Doug Jones and Patricia Agard', '1993', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '23', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Eisenhart_1993_Conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37753, 'Prospective teachers perceive methods course demonstrations designed to connect conceptual and procedural knowledge as just another set of rote routines and formulas to memorize.', 'Student teachers asked for more lists and concrete things to refer to because they wanted exact procedures for three-digit multiplication across all four developmental levels.', 'other', 'teaching methods', 'developmental levels', 'PST', 'grades K-8', 'JRME_Eisenhart_1993_Conceptual', 'Margaret Eisenhart, Hilda Borko, Robert Underhill, Catherine Brown, Doug Jones and Patricia Agard', '1993', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '27, 28', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Eisenhart_1993_Conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37754, 'Students fail to recognize geometric figures such as squares when they are presented in a nonstandard orientation.', '', 'geometric', 'squares', 'figure recognition', 'PST', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Mayberry_1983_Van', 'Joanne Mayberry', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '64, 65', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mayberry_1983_Van.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37755, 'Students draw a figure but fail to recognize its necessary properties, such as assuming a right triangle does not have to have a longest side or a largest angle.', 'Even though they drew a right triangle, 12 students did not think that such a triangle had to have a longest side and 7 students did not think it required a largest angle.', 'geometric', 'right triangles', 'properties', 'PST', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Mayberry_1983_Van', 'Joanne Mayberry', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '65', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mayberry_1983_Van.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37756, 'Students incorrectly assume that angle measures scale proportionally with side lengths in similar figures.', 'Five students said that if triangles A and B were similar with A''s sides half the length of B''s sides, then A''s angles were half as large as B''s.', 'geometric', 'similar triangles', 'angle measures', 'PST', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Mayberry_1983_Van', 'Joanne Mayberry', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '65', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mayberry_1983_Van.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37757, 'Students answer generalized class inclusion and relationship questions by drawing specific figures and deriving answers from the visual appearance of their own drawings.', 'Given that triangles ABC and DEF were similar or congruent, students drew specific figures and answered whether certain angles or sides were equal depending on their own diagram.', 'geometric', 'similarity and congruence', 'class inclusions', 'PST', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Mayberry_1983_Van', 'Joanne Mayberry', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '65', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mayberry_1983_Van.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37758, 'Students do not perceive a geometric proof as a logical chain connecting given information to a conclusion, often believing the proof terminates at an intermediate statement.', 'When given a chain of ordered statements a -> b -> c -> d, students stated that the second or third statement in the chain had been proved, missing the final conclusion.', 'geometric', 'proofs', 'logical chaining', 'PST', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Mayberry_1983_Van', 'Joanne Mayberry', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '66', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mayberry_1983_Van.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37759, 'A student attempts to solve equivalent fraction problems using superficial numerical patterns or inappropriate visual algorithms rather than operational partitioning schemes.', 'To solve 2/6 = /3, Alan guessed 4 for the numerator, arguing that ''3 and 3 is 6, plus 2 more of 6 equals 12''.', 'fraction', 'equivalent fractions', 'symbolic algorithms', 'student', 'fourth grade', 'JRME_Hunting_1983_Alan', 'Robert P. Hunting', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '193, 194', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hunting_1983_Alan.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37760, 'A student treats the denominator of a fraction as a direct indicator of absolute magnitude or quantity, failing to recognize the inverse relationship between the denominator and the unit fraction size.', 'Alan concluded that a 1/8-full packet of M&Ms contained more than a 1/4-full packet because ''there''s eight in here, and there''s four in each of those''.', 'fraction', 'fraction magnitude', 'unit fractions', 'student', 'fourth grade', 'JRME_Hunting_1983_Alan', 'Robert P. Hunting', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '194', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hunting_1983_Alan.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37761, 'A student misapplies whole-number partitioning strategies to find fractions of a set, grouping objects according to the numerator rather than first determining the fractional unit size based on the denominator.', 'When asked to find 3/5 of 10 Easter eggs, Alan made three equal groups of 3 eggs, leaving 1 egg over, because he did not know the meaning of the fraction 1/5.', 'fraction', 'fractions of a set', 'partitioning', 'student', 'fourth grade', 'JRME_Hunting_1983_Alan', 'Robert P. Hunting', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '192, 193', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hunting_1983_Alan.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37762, 'When applying the standard vertical decomposition algorithm, students may mechanically borrow a ''1'' to place next to the minuend without realizing they have merely recreated the original problem.', 'Michael (9+) wrote 18 - 9 vertically, put a little 1 by the 8, and said, ''I can''t take 9 from 8 but if I put a little 1 it makes it easier because now it''s 9 from 18.''', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'decomposition algorithm', 'student', 'aged 7 to 12', 'JRME_Gray_1994_Duality', 'Eddie M. Gray and David O. Tall', '1994', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '130', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Gray_1994_Duality.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37763, 'Less successful students fixate on procedural counting-back as the natural reverse of addition, creating an enormous cognitive burden and frequent errors when the subtrahend is large, rather than flexibly using a count-up strategy.', 'To calculate 19 - 17, a below-average student is likely to laboriously count back 17 from 19, a procedure that usually ends in failure.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'counting strategies', 'student', 'aged 7 to 12', 'JRME_Gray_1994_Duality', 'Eddie M. Gray and David O. Tall', '1994', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '125, 129, 130', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Gray_1994_Duality.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37764, 'In attempting to derive facts without a flexible proceptual understanding, students may rely on isolated known combinations while struggling with simpler subtractions embedded within the same problem space.', 'To solve 8 + 6, a student knew 8 + 2 = 10 but had trouble taking 2 from 6, so he split 8 into 4 and 4, added 6 and 4 to make 10, and added the remaining 4 to make 14.', 'whole_number', 'addition', 'derived facts', 'student', 'aged 7 to 12', 'JRME_Gray_1994_Duality', 'Eddie M. Gray and David O. Tall', '1994', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '135', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Gray_1994_Duality.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37765, 'Students focus exclusively on the substitutive property of the equals sign and ignore its basic relational meaning, making substitutions using mathematically false statements without noticing the logical absurdities.', 'Students made substitutions using false statements like 15 + 28 = 44 without realizing they were false until presented with blatant absurdities like 77 = 11 + 33.', 'algebraic', 'equality', 'substitutive vs. basic relational meanings', 'student', 'Year 5 and Year 8', 'JRME_Jones_2012_Substituting', 'Ian Jones and Dave Pratt', '2012', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '16, 17', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Jones_2012_Substituting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37766, 'Students view the equals sign primarily as a place indicator for a result, focusing on computation and creating statements with a single numeral on one side rather than observing structural or substitutive properties.', 'When asked to generate puzzles, Colin and Imogen generated statements that focused mostly on computation (e.g., 45 add 45 is equal to...) and ensuring a single numeral was on one side of the equals sign.', 'algebraic', 'equality', 'place-indicator view', 'student', 'Year 5 and Year 8', 'JRME_Jones_2012_Substituting', 'Ian Jones and Dave Pratt', '2012', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '22', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Jones_2012_Substituting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37767, 'When required to construct equations, students struggle to coordinate the basic relational meaning of equality with its substitutive utility, creating true statements that are mathematically superfluous or fail to match the terms needed for substitution.', 'Derek and John created statements like 85 = 80 + 5 that were numerically equivalent to the target expression but could not actually be used to make a substitution because the specific numerals did not match the terms present.', 'algebraic', 'equality', 'equivalence and substitution', 'student', 'Year 5 and Year 8', 'JRME_Jones_2012_Substituting', 'Ian Jones and Dave Pratt', '2012', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '20, 21, 22, 25', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Jones_2012_Substituting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37768, 'The student relies on a part-whole fractional scheme and whole-number procedures, failing to conceptualize nonunit fractions partitively as iterations of a unit fraction.', 'To produce 2/3 from a 6/6 bar, the student pulled three parts and then pulled two of those, thinking it would be 2/3.', 'fraction', 'nonunit fractions', 'partitive vs part-whole schemes', 'student', 'Grade 6', 'JRME_Norton_2008_Zpc', 'Anderson Norton and Beatriz S. D''Ambrosio', '2008', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '227', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Norton_2008_Zpc.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37769, 'The student ignores the constraint of preserving the whole when creating equivalent fractions, focusing only on the numerosity of parts.', 'The student drew a 6/9 bar much bigger than a 2/3 bar because ''there are nine parts in that one and only three in the other one.''', 'fraction', 'equivalent fractions', 'preserving the whole', 'student', 'Grade 6', 'JRME_Norton_2008_Zpc', 'Anderson Norton and Beatriz S. D''Ambrosio', '2008', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '227', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Norton_2008_Zpc.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37770, 'The student misinterprets improper fractions and mixed numbers by failing to coordinate sizes of wholes, treating fractional pieces as units of one.', 'The student agreed that lining up a 3/3 bar with a 1/3 bar represented 3 and 1/3, not recognizing that the 3/3 bar is just one whole and that the result was smaller than 10/3.', 'fraction', 'improper fractions', 'mixed numbers', 'student', 'Grade 6', 'JRME_Norton_2008_Zpc', 'Anderson Norton and Beatriz S. D''Ambrosio', '2008', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '233, 234', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Norton_2008_Zpc.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37771, 'The student invents procedural schemes that confuse the unique roles of the numerator and denominator.', 'To produce 16/5, the student partitioned the whole bar into 16 parts and pulled 5 parts.', 'fraction', 'improper fractions', 'numerator and denominator roles', 'student', 'Grade 6', 'JRME_Norton_2008_Zpc', 'Anderson Norton and Beatriz S. D''Ambrosio', '2008', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '235, 236', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Norton_2008_Zpc.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37772, 'Students believe that the instruction to solve an equation means to find a single numerical answer, failing to recognize or correctly interpret equations with no solution or infinite solutions.', 'A student incorrectly concludes that x = 0 is the solution to the identity 4(x - 1) - x = 3x - 4, or gets confused when solving 4(x - 1) - x = 3x + 3 results in a false statement like -4 = 3.', 'algebraic', 'equations', 'solving linear equations', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JRME_McCrory_2012_Knowledge', 'Raven McCrory, Robert Floden, Joan Ferrini-Mundy, Mark D. Reckase, and Sharon L. Senk', '2012', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '593, 603', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_McCrory_2012_Knowledge.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37773, 'Students interpret algebraic letters as labels for objects or abbreviated words rather than as generalized numbers or specific unknowns.', 'The sentence ''There are six times as many students as professors'' is written by students as the incorrect equation 6S = P.', 'algebraic', 'variables and equations', 'modeling word problems', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JRME_McCrory_2012_Knowledge', 'Raven McCrory, Robert Floden, Joan Ferrini-Mundy, Mark D. Reckase, and Sharon L. Senk', '2012', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '603', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_McCrory_2012_Knowledge.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37774, 'Students operate on algebraic expressions as if they are equations, ignoring basic rules of arithmetic to perform procedures like eliminating denominators.', 'When given the rational expression (x-4)/(x+3) - 9/(x-2), a student incorrectly multiplies the entire expression by the common denominator (x+3)(x-2) to clear the fractions.', 'algebraic', 'algebraic expressions', 'rational expressions', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JRME_McCrory_2012_Knowledge', 'Raven McCrory, Robert Floden, Joan Ferrini-Mundy, Mark D. Reckase, and Sharon L. Senk', '2012', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '607', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_McCrory_2012_Knowledge.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37775, 'Students incorrectly generalize that calculating slope from function tables involves simply computing differences between successive y-values, without accounting for the corresponding changes in x-values.', '', 'algebraic', 'linear functions', 'slope', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JRME_McCrory_2012_Knowledge', 'Raven McCrory, Robert Floden, Joan Ferrini-Mundy, Mark D. Reckase, and Sharon L. Senk', '2012', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '606', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_McCrory_2012_Knowledge.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37776, 'Students incorrectly attempt to isolate a variable in an equation involving subtraction by subtracting the constant from both sides, rather than adding its additive inverse.', 'To make zero in the equation m - 12 = 5, students suggested ''subtract twelve''.', 'algebraic', 'linear equations', 'inverse operations', 'other', 'fifth-grade, seventh-grade', 'JRME_Leatham_2015_Conceptualizing', 'Keith R. Leatham, Blake E. Peterson, Shari L. Stockero and Laura R. Van Zoest', '2015', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '110, 115', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Leatham_2015_Conceptualizing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37777, 'When adding a constant to its additive inverse, students ignore the negative sign and incorrectly add the absolute values.', 'When asked what minus twelve plus twelve is, a student responded, ''It''s 24. You''re supposed to add twelve.''', 'algebraic', 'linear equations', 'additive inverses', 'other', 'fifth-grade, seventh-grade', 'JRME_Leatham_2015_Conceptualizing', 'Keith R. Leatham, Blake E. Peterson, Shari L. Stockero and Laura R. Van Zoest', '2015', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '111, 116', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Leatham_2015_Conceptualizing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37778, 'Students blur the unit when modeling fraction multiplication, confusing pieces of a single whole with pieces of multiple wholes.', 'A student explained 1/4 times 3 as ''like one fourth of a pie and then somebody brings two more... three pieces of pie that came out of four pieces of pie''.', 'fraction', 'fraction multiplication', 'unit and equipartitioning', 'other', 'fifth-grade, seventh-grade', 'JRME_Leatham_2015_Conceptualizing', 'Keith R. Leatham, Blake E. Peterson, Shari L. Stockero and Laura R. Van Zoest', '2015', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '106, 112, 113, 118', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Leatham_2015_Conceptualizing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37779, 'Students compare fractions using fractional terms but fail to account for differences in the size of the whole units.', 'You''d get the same amount, because they''re both one-half', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'unit size', 'student', 'fourth, sixth, and eighth grades', 'JRME_Armstrong_1995_Students''', 'Barbara E. Armstrong and Carol Novillis Larson', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '9, 10', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Armstrong_1995_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37780, 'Students evaluate area by directly comparing a single physical dimension, such as the length or width of the shaded parts, rather than considering the part-whole area relationships.', 'Orange, because it''s wider and this one''s a lot thinner.', 'fraction', 'area comparison', 'perceptual features', 'student', 'fourth, sixth, and eighth grades', 'JRME_Armstrong_1995_Students''', 'Barbara E. Armstrong and Carol Novillis Larson', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '3, 11, 12', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Armstrong_1995_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37781, 'Students compare the shaded areas of rectangles by simply counting the number of shaded partitions or unshaded partitions without considering the size of the partitions or the whole.', 'Same, because there''s seven colored in on this one, and there''s seven colored in on this one.', 'fraction', 'area comparison', 'counting pieces', 'student', 'fourth, sixth, and eighth grades', 'JRME_Armstrong_1995_Students''', 'Barbara E. Armstrong and Carol Novillis Larson', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '11, 12', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Armstrong_1995_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37782, 'Students blindly perform straightforward arithmetic operations with the numbers given in word problems, suspending real-world knowledge and failing to consider realistic constraints.', 'For a problem asking how many 1 m planks can be sawed from four 2.5 m planks, a student simply calculates 4 x 2.5 and answers ''10 planks'', ignoring that a 0.5 m piece remains from each individual plank.', 'measurement', 'word problems', 'realistic mathematical modeling', 'student', 'fifth- and sixth-grade', 'JRME_Verschaffel_1997_Teaching', 'Lieven Verschaffel and Erik De Corte', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '578, 579, 581, 586', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Verschaffel_1997_Teaching.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37783, 'When confronted with the unrealistic nature of their straightforward arithmetic solutions, students stubbornly invent far-fetched contextual conditions to justify their answers rather than critically revising their mathematical model.', 'After incorrectly answering that 300 soldiers could transport ''38 crates'' (which require 8 men each), a student justified the remaining 4 men carrying the 38th crate by suggesting they ''might be extremely strong soldiers.''', 'whole_number', 'word problems', 'division with remainder', 'student', 'fifth- and sixth-grade', 'JRME_Verschaffel_1997_Teaching', 'Lieven Verschaffel and Erik De Corte', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '590, 595', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Verschaffel_1997_Teaching.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37784, 'Students hold the persistent misconception that multiplication always results in a larger number and division always results in a smaller number, causing them to choose the incorrect operation when problems involve numbers less than one.', '', 'rational', 'multiplication and division', 'operations with decimals', 'student', '10 to 20 years', 'JRME_Bell_1989_Children''s', 'Alan Bell, Brian Greer, Lindsay Grimison and Clare Mangan', '1989', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '434, 436, 438', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bell_1989_Children''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37785, 'Students possess strong numerical preferences to divide the larger number by the smaller number and to divide by an integer, leading them to reverse operations or ignore decimal points to satisfy this preference.', '', 'rational', 'division', 'decimal division', 'student', '10 to 20 years', 'JRME_Bell_1989_Children''s', 'Alan Bell, Brian Greer, Lindsay Grimison and Clare Mangan', '1989', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '434, 438, 440', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bell_1989_Children''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37786, 'Students have a weak grasp of the numerator and denominator roles of the two quantities in a rate, causing them to confuse the two and inappropriately reverse the quantities when resisting division by a larger number.', '4 grams per penny and 4 pence per gram are the same, just swapped around', 'proportional', 'rate', 'intensive quantities', 'student', '10 to 20 years', 'JRME_Bell_1989_Children''s', 'Alan Bell, Brian Greer, Lindsay Grimison and Clare Mangan', '1989', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '436, 438, 443', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bell_1989_Children''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37787, 'Students apply incorrect procedural rules during multidigit computation and fail to validate their nonsensical answers using simple estimation.', 'When asked to multiply 19 by 20, seventh-grade students produce strange procedures resulting in invalid answers like 3800 or 218.', 'whole_number', 'multidigit multiplication', 'procedural rules', 'other', '', 'JRME_Gagne_1983_Some', 'Robert M. Gagne', '1983', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '12, 13, 15, 17', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Gagne_1983_Some.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37788, 'Prospective teachers rely rigidly on partitive models of division and are unable to flexibly generate quotitive models when problem constraints, such as division by fractions or rounding up, require them.', 'When asked to write a word problem for 51 divided by 4 with an answer of 13, a subject wrote an inappropriate partitive problem: ''If you have 51 marbles and you wanted to make 4 groups of them, how many would be in each group, if you rounded up to the nearest whole number?''', 'whole_number', 'division', 'partitive and quotitive models', 'PST', 'prospective elementary teachers', 'JRME_Simon_1993_Prospective', 'Martin A. Simon', '1993', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '239, 247', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Simon_1993_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37789, 'Prospective teachers view long division as a disconnected set of procedural rules, explaining the multiply and subtract steps solely as ways to get the next number rather than representing quantitative actions like making equal groups.', 'When asked why 12 is multiplied by 5 to get 60 in long division, a subject explained, ''The 60 is important because you need it to continue the division. It is not.... You need it to continue going on.''', 'whole_number', 'division', 'long division algorithm', 'PST', 'prospective elementary teachers', 'JRME_Simon_1993_Prospective', 'Martin A. Simon', '1993', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '243, 244, 248', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Simon_1993_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37790, 'Prospective teachers fail to understand the units associated with remainders and fractional quotients, frequently misinterpreting the fractional part of a quotient as a quantity of the original dividend rather than a fraction of the divisor''s unit.', 'After dividing 35 cups of flour by 3/8 of a cup, resulting in 93 1/3, subjects mistakenly claimed there was 1/3 of a cup of flour left over, rather than recognizing 1/3 referred to a fraction of a cookie.', 'fraction', 'division', 'units and remainders', 'PST', 'prospective elementary teachers', 'JRME_Simon_1993_Prospective', 'Martin A. Simon', '1993', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '243, 245, 249', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Simon_1993_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37791, 'Students incorrectly generalize that all problem conditions and visual cues are consistent with and relevant to the intended solution, causing them to struggle with tasks that require ignoring perceptual distracters.', 'When asked to shade 2/3 of a continuous model (like a rectangle) that is already divided into 5 equal parts, students struggle to ignore the existing 5 divisions and restructure the image into 3 parts.', 'fraction', 'rational number concepts', 'perceptual distracter tasks', 'student', 'fourth graders', 'JRME_Cramer_1989_Cognitive', 'Kathleen A. Cramer, Thomas R. Post and Merlyn J. Behr', '1989', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '103, 104', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Cramer_1989_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37792, 'A preservice teacher incorrectly assumes that multiplying fractions always produces a larger answer and dividing fractions always produces a smaller answer, indicating a lack of understanding of fractions as quantities.', 'Data collected during the mathematics methods course indicated that she viewed the multiplication of fractions as producing larger answers and division of fractions as producing smaller answers.', 'fraction', 'multiplication and division', 'relative effects of operations', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JRME_Vacc_1999_Elementary', 'Nancy Nesbitt Vacc and George W. Bright', '1999', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '106', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Vacc_1999_Elementary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37793, 'Students learn the parts of a fraction symbol as isolated counting procedures rather than understanding the fraction as a single quantity representing a proportional relationship.', 'A student stated, ''The top number is the numerator and that''s the one that we chose stuff to put in, and the bottom number is the denominator and that shows all the things that are together.''', 'fraction', 'fractional representation', 'numerator and denominator', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JRME_Vacc_1999_Elementary', 'Nancy Nesbitt Vacc and George W. Bright', '1999', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '102', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Vacc_1999_Elementary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37794, 'Students treat derived fact strategies as rote procedural rules, indiscriminately applying verbal rules without attending to the relative size of the addends.', 'For combinations like 7 + 6, where the first addend is larger, students used the rote rule ''Double the first addend and add 1''.', 'whole_number', 'addition', 'derived facts strategies', 'student', 'second-grade', 'JRME_Steinberg_1985_Instruction', 'Ruth M. Steinberg', '1985', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '348, 349', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Steinberg_1985_Instruction.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37795, 'Students physically model the transformation of one addition problem into a compensation or ''doubles'' problem but fail to recognize that the two combinations yield the same sum.', 'A student transforms 8 + 6 to 7 + 7 using manipulatives, but writes two different answers for the two problems or calculates each sum separately.', 'whole_number', 'addition', 'compensation', 'student', 'second-grade', 'JRME_Steinberg_1985_Instruction', 'Ruth M. Steinberg', '1985', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '349', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Steinberg_1985_Instruction.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37796, 'When asked to map inverse operations, students formulate mathematically incorrect subtraction equations by improperly ordering the numbers from a related addition family.', 'Given the addition fact 5 + 4 = 9, a student attempts to write a matching subtraction sentence and writes 4 - 5 = 1.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'inverse relationship to addition', 'student', 'second-grade', 'JRME_Steinberg_1985_Instruction', 'Ruth M. Steinberg', '1985', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '349', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Steinberg_1985_Instruction.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37797, 'The student teacher confuses representations for multiplication of fractions with those for division of fractions, demonstrating a rote rather than conceptual understanding of the topic.', 'When attempting to visually explain 3/4 divided by 1/2 to her class using a drawing of a wall, the teacher inadvertently modeled 1/2 of 3/4 (multiplication) rather than division.', 'fraction', 'division of fractions', 'instructional representations', 'PST', 'seniors in an elementary teacher education program', 'JRME_Borko_1992_Learning', 'Hilda Borko, Margaret Eisenhart, Catherine A. Brown, Robert G. Underhill, Doug Jones and Patricia C. Agard', '1992', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '197, 198', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Borko_1992_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37798, 'Preservice teachers hold the unrealistic expectation that concrete or semiconcrete visual representations and real-world stories can be used to directly derive or explain the invert-and-multiply algorithm, rather than just verifying its solutions.', 'A student teacher suggested that if students could visualize dividing pizzas into 12ths, ''they would see why you flip the second number.''', 'fraction', 'division of fractions', 'invert-and-multiply algorithm', 'PST', 'seniors in an elementary teacher education program', 'JRME_Borko_1992_Learning', 'Hilda Borko, Margaret Eisenhart, Catherine A. Brown, Robert G. Underhill, Doug Jones and Patricia C. Agard', '1992', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '209, 214, 215', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Borko_1992_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37799, 'Preservice teachers incorrectly believe that multiplication always results in a larger number and division always results in a smaller number, leading them to inappropriately select division for word problems involving decimal operators less than 1.', 'When given a multiplication problem with a decimal operator less than 1, interviewees claimed division was the appropriate operation because ''division always makes smaller and multiplication always makes larger.''', 'decimal', 'multiplication and division word problems', 'choice of operation', 'PST', 'early elementary education majors', 'JRME_Graeber_1989_Preservice', 'Anna O. Graeber, Dina Tirosh and Roseanne Glover', '1989', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '98', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Graeber_1989_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37800, 'Preservice teachers incorrectly overgeneralize the procedure for finding a unit fraction of a quantity to pure decimals, erroneously dividing the quantity by the decimal to find a decimal part of a number.', 'To find .75 of 15, preservice teachers wrote an expression to divide 15 by .75, reasoning that ''To find a [pure] decimal part of a number, you ought to divide the number by the decimal.''', 'decimal', 'multiplication', 'decimal operands', 'PST', 'early elementary education majors', 'JRME_Graeber_1989_Preservice', 'Anna O. Graeber, Dina Tirosh and Roseanne Glover', '1989', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '98, 99', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Graeber_1989_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37801, 'Preservice teachers believe that in division, the larger number must always be divided by the smaller number, causing them to reverse the roles of the divisor and dividend when the divisor is larger.', 'For problems where the whole-number divisor was larger than the dividend, interviewees reversed the roles, claiming ''it is impossible to divide a smaller number by a bigger number.''', 'whole_number', 'division', 'roles of divisor and dividend', 'PST', 'early elementary education majors', 'JRME_Graeber_1989_Preservice', 'Anna O. Graeber, Dina Tirosh and Roseanne Glover', '1989', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '99, 100', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Graeber_1989_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37802, 'Preservice teachers hold the faulty rule that the divisor must be a whole number, sometimes deliberately interchanging the divisor and dividend to avoid dividing by a decimal.', 'One interviewee initially wrote 5 / 3.25 but interchanged the divisor and dividend to 3.25 / 5 when confronted with the decimal divisor, attempting to avoid a decimal divisor.', 'decimal', 'division', 'decimal divisors', 'PST', 'early elementary education majors', 'JRME_Graeber_1989_Preservice', 'Anna O. Graeber, Dina Tirosh and Roseanne Glover', '1989', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '95, 100', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Graeber_1989_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37803, 'Preservice teachers confuse different division notations or overgeneralize the commutative property, incorrectly assuming that reversing the dividend and divisor yields the same result.', 'An interviewee stated, ''I think 15 / 5 and 5 / 15 will have the same solution.''', 'whole_number', 'division', 'notation and commutativity', 'PST', 'early elementary education majors', 'JRME_Graeber_1989_Preservice', 'Anna O. Graeber, Dina Tirosh and Roseanne Glover', '1989', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '100', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Graeber_1989_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37804, 'When finding a fraction of a grouped quantity, preservice teachers may lack the cognitive ability to compose and decompose composite units, reverting instead to memorized symbolic algorithms applied only to singleton units.', 'To find 3/4 of 8 bundles of 4 sticks, a student transforms the quantity to 32 singleton sticks by symbolic multiplication (8x4), symbolically applies the fraction to get 24, and then divides to find the bundles, without ever operating on the bundles themselves as units.', 'fraction', 'rational number operator', 'composition and decomposition of conceptual units', 'PST', 'undergraduate college seniors', 'JRME_Behr_1997_Conceptual', 'Merlyn J. Behr, Helen A. Khoury, Guershon Harel, Thomas Post, Richard Lesh', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '60', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Behr_1997_Conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37805, 'Students conceive angle measures strictly as labels or pointers for geometric objects rather than as a measurable quantity representing a multiplicative relationship between an arc and a circle''s radius.', 'Zac conceived angle measures as labels for geometric objects, explaining that a line is 180 degrees and a circle is 360 degrees, and estimated openness instead of measuring.', 'measurement', 'angle measure', 'meaning of angle measure', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Moore_2014_Quantitative', 'Kevin C. Moore', '2014', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '115', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Moore_2014_Quantitative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37806, 'Students rely on nonquantitative features, such as the physical shape or continuous motion of an object in a situation, to explain the shape or curvature of a graph rather than reasoning about the covariational relationship between quantities.', 'Zac justified the smooth curvature of his graph by referencing the continuous rotation and circular shape of the fan, rather than analyzing amounts or rates of change.', 'algebraic', 'graphing functions', 'covariational reasoning', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Moore_2014_Quantitative', 'Kevin C. Moore', '2014', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '117, 118, 130', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Moore_2014_Quantitative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37807, 'When creating a formula for circular motion with a novel starting position, students may formulate an expression that does not accurately reflect the vertical shift relative to the chosen reference point, resulting in an inconsistency with their own emergent graph.', 'Zac''s initial formula for a Ferris wheel ride starting at the bottom evaluated to -36 at an input of zero, contradicting his graph which correctly showed a starting vertical distance of zero feet from the ground.', 'algebraic', 'trigonometric functions', 'modeling circular motion', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_Moore_2014_Quantitative', 'Kevin C. Moore', '2014', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '126', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Moore_2014_Quantitative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37808, 'Children do not understand that partitioned quantities must exhaust the given whole, instead demonstrating idiosyncratic sharing notions where they might ignore leftover material or expect more to be provided.', 'When making skipping ropes from string so that it was ''all used up and each doll gets an even share,'' some children made two cuts, resulting in leftover material.', 'fraction', 'partitioning', 'sharing quantities', 'student', 'preschool children', 'JRME_Hunting_1988_Fraction', 'Robert P. Hunting and Christopher F. Sharpley', '1988', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '177, 178', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hunting_1988_Fraction.pdf')).
