research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37811, 'A student struggles to understand why a fractional stick composed of multiple small parts is measured as an equivalent, simpler fraction by the software.', 'Josh creates a 4/18 stick and is surprised when the computer measures it as 2/9.', 'fraction', 'equivalent fractions', 'composite units', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Norton_2008_Josh''s', 'Anderson Norton', '2008', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '414, 415, 416', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Norton_2008_Josh''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37812, 'A student identifies fractions solely by the total number of pieces rather than relative size to the whole, demonstrating difficulty with fractional language.', 'When asked what remains if 2/7 is removed from a 7/7 bar, Josh answers ''five pieces'' and guesses ''five sevenths'', ''fifty sevenths'', or ''seven fifths''.', 'fraction', 'fractional language', 'part-whole relations', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Norton_2008_Josh''s', 'Anderson Norton', '2008', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'secondary', '412, 419', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Norton_2008_Josh''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37813, 'Students successfully execute the division computation for a story problem but fail to engage in sense-making, leaving the raw computational result as their final answer without interpreting the remainder in the context of the real-world situation.', 'Some students simply provided division computational work and gave the computational result of 31 1/3 as the answer, without any interpretation.', 'whole_number', 'division', 'interpreting remainders', 'student', 'fifth and sixth graders', 'JRME_Cai_1995_Solution', 'Jinfa Cai and Edward A. Silver', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '494, 495', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Cai_1995_Solution.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37814, 'Rather than reasoning about the physical constraints of a story situation, students inappropriately apply rote computational rounding rules to deal with remainders.', 'After calculating a result of 31.33 for the number of buses needed, a student wrote, ''3 is less than 5, so I just take the .33 away.''', 'whole_number', 'division', 'rounding rules', 'student', 'fifth and sixth graders', 'JRME_Cai_1995_Solution', 'Jinfa Cai and Edward A. Silver', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '494', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Cai_1995_Solution.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37815, 'Students compare decimals by treating the fractional part as a whole number, concluding that a longer decimal string is a larger number.', 'A student selected .42 as larger than .5 and explained that ''forty-two is bigger than five.''', 'decimal', 'decimal comparison', 'whole number rule', 'student', 'Grades 4, 5, and 6', 'JRME_Wearne_1988_Cognitive', 'Diana Wearne and James Hiebert', '1988', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '378', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Wearne_1988_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37816, 'Students perform ragged decimal addition by blindly applying syntactic rules or whole number arithmetic, ignoring the positional values of the digits.', 'A student wrote .8 as the answer to 5 + .3 and explained that ''five plus three is eight, and the point stays right in front of it.''', 'decimal', 'addition', 'ragged decimals', 'student', 'Grades 4, 5, and 6', 'JRME_Wearne_1988_Cognitive', 'Diana Wearne and James Hiebert', '1988', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '378, 380', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Wearne_1988_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37817, 'Students rely exclusively on procedural division to determine divisibility, failing to use prime factorization or conceptual properties of multiples.', 'When asked if 3^3 x 5^2 x 7 is divisible by 7, a student multiplies out the entire number to get 4725 and then divides it by 7 to check for a whole number quotient.', 'whole_number', 'divisibility', 'prime factorization', 'PST', 'preservice elementary school teachers', 'JRME_Zazkis_1996_Divisibility', 'Rina Zazkis and Stephen Campbell', '1996', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '546, 547, 548', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Zazkis_1996_Divisibility.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37818, 'Students incorrectly conclude a number is prime, or not divisible by a specific divisor, by misapplying divisibility rules for 3 and 9 to other numbers like 23.', 'A student claims 391 is not divisible by 23, and is likely prime, because the sum of its digits is 13, and 13 is not divisible by 23.', 'whole_number', 'divisibility', 'divisibility rules', 'PST', 'preservice elementary school teachers', 'JRME_Zazkis_1996_Divisibility', 'Rina Zazkis and Stephen Campbell', '1996', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '556, 557, 558', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Zazkis_1996_Divisibility.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37819, 'Students inappropriately generalize last-digit divisibility rules to numbers for which they do not apply, such as 7 or 9.', 'A student concludes that 1575 is not divisible by 7 because she looks at the last digit (5), and claims it is not divisible by 9 because she looks at the last two digits (75).', 'whole_number', 'divisibility', 'divisibility rules', 'PST', 'preservice elementary school teachers', 'JRME_Zazkis_1996_Divisibility', 'Rina Zazkis and Stephen Campbell', '1996', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '558', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Zazkis_1996_Divisibility.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37820, 'Students incorrectly assume that prime factorization is distributive over addition, believing that the prime factorization of a sum equals the sum of the prime factorizations of its parts.', 'A student suggests that the prime factorization of 391 could be found by finding the prime factorizations of 300, 80, and 11 independently.', 'whole_number', 'prime factorization', 'distributivity', 'PST', 'preservice elementary school teachers', 'JRME_Zazkis_1996_Divisibility', 'Rina Zazkis and Stephen Campbell', '1996', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '559, 560', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Zazkis_1996_Divisibility.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37821, 'Students believe that fractions are defined by the visual arrangement of identical parts rather than understanding that equal partitioning produces a specific unit of quantity relative to the whole.', 'Micki claims she would rather eat one of the pieces of Anne''s diagonally cut cookie than Sam''s vertically cut cookie because ''it is bigger,'' despite agreeing both original cookies and their respective halves are identical.', 'fraction', 'fraction as a quantity', 'fraction as an arrangement', 'student', 'fourth- and fifth-grade', 'JRME_Simon_2004_Explicating', 'Martin A. Simon, Ron Tzur, Karen Heinz, Margaret Kinzel', '2004', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '315, 316', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Simon_2004_Explicating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37822, 'When attempting to partition a length, the student does not initially anticipate that there is a unique, specific size for the part that will exactly reproduce the whole when iterated.', 'When asked to cut a stick into 5 equal parts, Micki iterates a small stick 5 times, sees it is too short, and then iteratively adjusts the size of the small stick rather than anticipating the exact length needed.', 'fraction', 'partitioning', 'iterating composite units', 'student', 'fourth- and fifth-grade', 'JRME_Simon_2004_Explicating', 'Martin A. Simon, Ron Tzur, Karen Heinz, Margaret Kinzel', '2004', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '317, 318, 319', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Simon_2004_Explicating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37823, 'Students overgeneralize whole-number properties to fractions, incorrectly assuming that a fraction with a larger denominator is larger.', 'Students initially thought one-eighth was larger than one-third because eight is bigger than three.', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'unit fractions', 'mixed', 'elementary/middle school', 'JRME_Gutstein_1997_Culturally', 'Eric Gutstein, Pauline Lipman, Patricia Hernandez, Rebeca de los Reyes', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'passing', '719, 720', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Gutstein_1997_Culturally.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37824, 'Students experience difficulty with the concepts of scale and linear measurement on maps, compounded by an unfamiliar English idiom.', 'Students'' measurements of distance varied because cities were marked with dots inside small circles, and they did not understand what ''as the crow flies'' meant.', 'measurement', 'scale and distance', 'map representations', 'mixed', 'elementary/middle school', 'JRME_Gutstein_1997_Culturally', 'Eric Gutstein, Pauline Lipman, Patricia Hernandez, Rebeca de los Reyes', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'passing', '723', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Gutstein_1997_Culturally.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37825, 'Students view fractions strictly as part-whole relationships, believing it is impossible to have an improper fraction because one cannot take more parts out of a whole than exist.', 'When asked to draw nine sevenths of a bar, a student said ''you can''t take nine out of something that''s seven'' and partitioned the given bar unevenly to make nine parts instead of iterating a seventh.', 'fraction', 'improper fractions', 'iterative fraction scheme', 'student', 'middle and high school', 'JRME_Hackenberg_2015_Relationships', 'Amy J. Hackenberg and Mi Yeon Lee', '2015', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '211, 212', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hackenberg_2015_Relationships.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37826, 'Students rely on specific numerical examples to write equations and remain tied to those values, treating equations as arithmetic operations on specific numbers rather than generalizing structural relationships between unknowns.', 'A student wrote x * 5 = 15 using a specific example value, and only changed the 15 to the variable y when explicitly prompted by the interviewer.', 'algebraic', 'equation writing', 'multiplicative relationships', 'student', 'middle and high school', 'JRME_Hackenberg_2015_Relationships', 'Amy J. Hackenberg and Mi Yeon Lee', '2015', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '217, 218', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hackenberg_2015_Relationships.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37827, 'Students can determine a smaller unknown quantity from a larger one using division in a concrete numerical example but lack the reversibility to represent this structural relationship as an algebraic equation involving unknowns.', 'A student correctly divided 15 by 5 to find a smaller length of 3, but when asked to write a general equation to find the smaller unknown from the larger one, he could not produce y / 5 = x.', 'algebraic', 'equation writing', 'reversibility and inverse operations', 'student', 'middle and high school', 'JRME_Hackenberg_2015_Relationships', 'Amy J. Hackenberg and Mi Yeon Lee', '2015', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '217, 218', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hackenberg_2015_Relationships.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37828, 'Students view fractional relationships additively rather than multiplicatively, using subtraction to relate unknowns instead of recognizing fractions as multipliers.', 'When told Sam''s stack is two fifths the height of Theo''s, a student wrote T - 3/5 = S, indicating she viewed making 2/5 of a quantity as taking away 3/5 of it.', 'algebraic', 'equation writing', 'fractions as multipliers', 'student', 'middle and high school', 'JRME_Hackenberg_2015_Relationships', 'Amy J. Hackenberg and Mi Yeon Lee', '2015', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '222, 223', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hackenberg_2015_Relationships.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37829, 'Students who have recently learned to compare unit fractions via a hands-on partitioning activity may revert to whole-number reasoning when presented with the same task outside the activity context.', 'The teacher writes two fractions on the board, ''1/7'' and ''1/5,'' and asks which one is larger. To the teacher''s surprise, most of the students claim that 1/7 is larger because 7 is larger than 5.', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'unit fractions', 'student', 'fourth- and fifth-grade', 'JRME_Simon_2016_Participatory', 'Martin A. Simon, Nicora Placa, and Arnon Avitzur', '2016', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '64', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Simon_2016_Participatory.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37830, 'When asked to iterate a non-unit fraction, a student may incorrectly multiply both the numerator and the denominator by the multiplier if they do not actively conceive of the non-unit fraction as an iteration of a unit fraction.', 'When asked to repeat a bar that is two fifths of a unit long four times, Kylie multiplied both the numerator and denominator by four, resulting in eight twentieths.', 'fraction', 'fraction iteration', 'non-unit fractions', 'student', 'fourth- and fifth-grade', 'JRME_Simon_2016_Participatory', 'Martin A. Simon, Nicora Placa, and Arnon Avitzur', '2016', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '69, 70', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Simon_2016_Participatory.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37831, 'A student may fall back on an additive comparison between two quantities instead of using a multiplicative ratio if the task constraints prevent them from mentally iterating a unit fraction.', 'When asked to compare her steps (42) to Max''s steps (21), Kylie stated ''Yours is bigger, Twenty-one steps bigger'', treating the relationship additively rather than using the 2:1 ratio she used in a prior task.', 'ratio', 'proportional reasoning', 'multiplicative versus additive comparison', 'student', 'fourth- and fifth-grade', 'JRME_Simon_2016_Participatory', 'Martin A. Simon, Nicora Placa, and Arnon Avitzur', '2016', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '71', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Simon_2016_Participatory.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37832, 'A student may be unable to calculate a fraction of a whole number if the task is posed abstractly, lacking a cue to the bar-partitioning activity through which they originally developed the concept.', 'When asked ''What is five fourteenths of 322?'', Kylie responded ''Nope'' when asked if she had any idea how to do it, despite successfully doing similar calculations in prior sessions.', 'fraction', 'fraction of a whole number', 'anticipatory stage limitations', 'student', 'fourth- and fifth-grade', 'JRME_Simon_2016_Participatory', 'Martin A. Simon, Nicora Placa, and Arnon Avitzur', '2016', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '88, 89', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Simon_2016_Participatory.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37833, 'When adding fractions with like denominators, a student incorrectly produces a sum where the denominator has been combined (e.g., added or multiplied) rather than retaining the common denominator.', 'When asked to solve 1/5 + 1/5, a student answered ''One tenth.''', 'fraction', 'addition', 'like denominators', 'in_service', 'eighth-grade', 'JRME_Davis_1997_Listening', 'Brent Davis', '1997', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'passing', '357, 358', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Davis_1997_Listening.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37834, 'Students imagine a multidimensional object varying in size along one dimension until it vanishes, resulting in a collapsed object of reduced dimension, and inappropriately generalize this topological limit to other properties like slope.', 'Shawna described the secant line''s base and height getting smaller until ''you can go no more'' at which point the secant magically becomes the tangent line and its slope.', 'calculus', 'definition of the derivative', 'secant and tangent lines', 'student', 'freshmen', 'JRME_Oehrtman_2009_Collapsing', 'Michael Oehrtman', '2009', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '409, 410, 411', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Oehrtman_2009_Collapsing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37835, 'Students argue that an unbounded solid has finite volume due to a physical limitation, reasoning that a small physical object will eventually get stuck in the narrowing passage and plug it up.', 'When explaining Torricelli''s trumpet, a student wrote that a single water molecule sliding down the conical cup will eventually get stuck where the sides are too close, allowing the rest of the volume to fill up.', 'calculus', 'improper integrals', 'solids of revolution', 'student', 'freshmen', 'JRME_Oehrtman_2009_Collapsing', 'Michael Oehrtman', '2009', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '418, 419', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Oehrtman_2009_Collapsing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37836, 'Students treat infinity as a number or a ''really big number'' that can be manipulated in arithmetic calculations, such as dividing a finite number by infinity to yield a value extremely close to zero.', 'Jared explained the limit comparison test by stating that a finite number over infinity yields ''some number extremely close to zero'' and Louis argued that 20 is ''just about nothing when compared to infinity.''', 'calculus', 'limits', 'limit comparison test', 'student', 'freshmen', 'JRME_Oehrtman_2009_Collapsing', 'Michael Oehrtman', '2009', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '417, 418', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Oehrtman_2009_Collapsing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37837, 'Students improperly apply a limit process to the fundamental theorem of calculus, imagining a volume collapsing into its boundary''s surface area as its thickness goes to zero.', 'When asked why the derivative of the volume of a sphere is its surface area, students described concentric spherical shells where the ''last shell'' gets thinner and becomes the ''last sphere''s surface area.''', 'calculus', 'fundamental theorem of calculus', 'definite integrals', 'student', 'freshmen', 'JRME_Oehrtman_2009_Collapsing', 'Michael Oehrtman', '2009', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '413', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Oehrtman_2009_Collapsing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37838, 'Students reject the equality of 0.999... and 1 by viewing the repeating decimal as a sequence of approximations with an infinitely small error, rather than acknowledging an exact limit.', 'Students disagreed that 0.999... = 1 by describing the difference between the partial sums and 1 as ''irrelevant'' or ''negligible differences'' and ''infinitely small errors'' that ''don''t matter''.', 'decimal', 'infinite series', 'repeating decimals', 'student', 'freshmen', 'JRME_Oehrtman_2009_Collapsing', 'Michael Oehrtman', '2009', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '414, 415', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Oehrtman_2009_Collapsing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37839, 'Students may compute sums by ignoring addend order to make counting easier without actually understanding or believing in the commutative principle.', 'Shown 6 + 4 = 10, the student concludes that 4 + 6 would not add up to 10.', 'whole_number', 'addition', 'commutativity', 'mixed', 'preschool, primary, junior high', 'JRME_Baroody_1990_Chapter', 'Arthur J. Baroody and Herbert P. Ginsburg', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '55', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Baroody_1990_Chapter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37840, 'A child overgeneralizes counting patterns by inventing new terms based on perceived rules rather than the standard sequence.', 'When asked what comes after 29, the child answers ''tenty'' because ten follows nine and the twenties end in ''ty''.', 'whole_number', 'counting', 'number words', 'mixed', 'preschool, primary, junior high', 'JRME_Baroody_1990_Chapter', 'Arthur J. Baroody and Herbert P. Ginsburg', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '55, 56', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Baroody_1990_Chapter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37841, 'Students blindly follow measurement and formula procedures without applying common sense or recognizing the physical constraints of the task.', 'After cutting a 25 cm^2 square into different shapes, a college student accepts different calculated areas (like 24.5) due to measurement errors without realizing the pieces must all conserve the original area.', 'measurement', 'area', 'conservation of area', 'mixed', 'preschool, primary, junior high', 'JRME_Baroody_1990_Chapter', 'Arthur J. Baroody and Herbert P. Ginsburg', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '61', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Baroody_1990_Chapter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37842, 'Students systematically subtract the smaller digit from the larger digit in a column, regardless of which is the minuend and which is the subtrahend.', 'When solving 52 - 24, the student subtracts 2 from 4 to get 2, and 2 from 5 to get 3, resulting in 32, and is untroubled if the difference is larger than the starting amount.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'borrowing algorithm', 'mixed', 'preschool, primary, junior high', 'JRME_Baroody_1990_Chapter', 'Arthur J. Baroody and Herbert P. Ginsburg', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'primary', '61, 62', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Baroody_1990_Chapter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37843, 'Students execute an incomplete borrowing procedure where they correctly add 10 to the ones column but fail to reduce the tens column.', 'For 40 - 12, the student changes 0 to 10 and subtracts 2 to get 8, but fails to reduce the 4 in the tens place, resulting in an incorrect answer of 38.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'borrowing algorithm', 'mixed', 'preschool, primary, junior high', 'JRME_Baroody_1990_Chapter', 'Arthur J. Baroody and Herbert P. Ginsburg', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'primary', '61, 62', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Baroody_1990_Chapter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37844, 'Students believe there is only one correct, school-taught method to solve a problem and rigidly refuse to use informal knowledge or common sense when the formal algorithm fails.', 'When asked to convert 3 feet 6 inches to feet, the student becomes flustered using a formal proportion algorithm and refuses to use her informal knowledge that 6 inches is half a foot.', 'measurement', 'unit conversion', 'beliefs about mathematics', 'mixed', 'preschool, primary, junior high', 'JRME_Baroody_1990_Chapter', 'Arthur J. Baroody and Herbert P. Ginsburg', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'uncoded', 'primary', '63', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Baroody_1990_Chapter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37845, 'Students interpret areas partitioned into unequal parts as discrete quantities, simply counting the shaded parts and total parts without accommodating for the different sizes of the continuous area parts.', 'On an Unequal Area problem with one small gray part out of five unequal parts total, a student writes 1/5, treating the grayed area as a discrete quantity.', 'fraction', 'part-whole relations', 'unequal area models', 'student', 'Grades 4, 5, and 6', 'JRME_Saxe_2005_Representing', 'Geoffrey B. Saxe, Edd V. Taylor, Clifton McIntosh, and Maryl Gearhart', '2005', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'primary', '141, 144, 146, 154', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Saxe_2005_Representing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37846, 'Students acquire standard fraction notation independently of the conceptual referent, using conventional notation to represent relationships that are not normative part-whole relations.', '57% of the students used conventional notation without part-whole reference on Unequal Area problems.', 'fraction', 'fraction notation', 'conventional vs. unconventional notation', 'student', 'Grades 4, 5, and 6', 'JRME_Saxe_2005_Representing', 'Geoffrey B. Saxe, Edd V. Taylor, Clifton McIntosh, and Maryl Gearhart', '2005', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '147, 148', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Saxe_2005_Representing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37847, 'When finding a whole amount from a discrete nonunit fraction, the student is perturbed by multiple numerosities and incorrectly interprets the fractional parts as literal whole number values.', 'For a moment, Michael interpreted ''two tenths'' as ''two 10s'' and got his answer of 20 cents.', 'fraction', 'fractions of discrete wholes', 'nonunit fractions', 'student', 'third grade', 'JRME_SaenzLudlow_1994_Michael''s', 'Adalira Saenz-Ludlow', '1994', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '67, 68, 69', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_SaenzLudlow_1994_Michael''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37848, 'When dividing a smaller unit fraction by a larger unit fraction, the student inappropriately applies a natural-number division strategy, concluding the answer is zero because the larger segmenting unit cannot fit into the smaller unit.', 'Zero, because you cannot fit one-third into one-twelfth.', 'fraction', 'division', 'division of fractions', 'student', 'third grade', 'JRME_SaenzLudlow_1994_Michael''s', 'Adalira Saenz-Ludlow', '1994', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '80, 81', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_SaenzLudlow_1994_Michael''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37849, 'Students draw partly correct models that miss essential details or misrepresent the relationship of a quantity to the generator.', 'In the Furniture Problem, the representation of the chair was accurate, but the cost of the table did not show how it was related to the generator, resulting in an incorrect arithmetic equation.', 'algebraic', 'word problems', 'model representation', 'mixed', 'Primary 5', 'JRME_Ng_2009_Model', 'Swee Fong Ng and Kerry Lee', '2009', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '305, 306, 307', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Ng_2009_Model.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37850, 'Students change the generator or base of comparison midway through constructing a comparative model.', 'In the Furniture Problem, instead of comparing the cost of the table to the cost of the iron (the generator), 26% of solutions erroneously compared the cost of the table with the cost of the chair.', 'algebraic', 'word problems', 'base of comparison', 'mixed', 'Primary 5', 'JRME_Ng_2009_Model', 'Swee Fong Ng and Kerry Lee', '2009', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '307', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Ng_2009_Model.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37851, 'Students correctly represent and compute intermediate values but stop prematurely, failing to answer the specific question asked in the problem.', 'In the Furniture Problem, children stopped after successfully finding the cost of the iron, failing to proceed to find the cost of the chair as requested.', 'algebraic', 'word problems', 'goal monitoring', 'mixed', 'Primary 5', 'JRME_Ng_2009_Model', 'Swee Fong Ng and Kerry Lee', '2009', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '307, 308', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Ng_2009_Model.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37852, 'Students represent a fractional relationship additively instead of multiplicatively due to a lack of conceptual knowledge of fractions.', 'For the Water Problem, students represented the volume of C (1/4 of B) by additively attaching a 1/4 rectangle to three rectangles representing B, misinterpreting it as 1/4 more than B.', 'fraction', 'fractional relationships', 'multiplicative reasoning', 'mixed', 'Primary 5', 'JRME_Ng_2009_Model', 'Swee Fong Ng and Kerry Lee', '2009', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '308', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Ng_2009_Model.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37853, 'Students misinterpret the role of rectangles in a model, treating rectangles representing specific unit rates as unknown units that sum to the total.', 'In the Book Problem, students divided the total cost ($528) by the total number of book rectangles, instead of grouping the cost of one math book and four science books into a $44 set.', 'algebraic', 'word problems', 'proportional reasoning', 'mixed', 'Primary 5', 'JRME_Ng_2009_Model', 'Swee Fong Ng and Kerry Lee', '2009', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '308, 309', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Ng_2009_Model.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37854, 'Students ignore the base of comparison in comparative phrases, treating the given difference as an absolute value for the new quantity.', 'When given ''Sunshine Primary School has 89 more pupils'', students treated 89 as the actual enrollment for Sunshine Primary because they ignored Dunearn Primary as the base for comparison.', 'whole_number', 'comparative phrases', 'more than/less than', 'mixed', 'Primary 5', 'JRME_Ng_2009_Model', 'Swee Fong Ng and Kerry Lee', '2009', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '309', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Ng_2009_Model.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37855, 'Students view estimation as a rigid, isolated procedure to be followed rather than a flexible process for ''honing in'' on the exact answer.', 'A student might mentally calculate the exact answer to a computation but then round that exact answer to produce their estimate, believing they must strictly apply the estimation rule they were taught to be ''right''.', 'whole_number', 'estimation', 'nature of estimation', 'student', 'fourth, fifth, and sixth graders', 'JRME_Schoen_1981_Instruction', 'Harold L. Schoen, Charles D. Friesen, Joscelyn A. Jarrett and Tonya D. Urbatsch', '1981', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '176', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Schoen_1981_Instruction.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37856, 'Children believe that counting after dealing out discrete items is logically essential to establish a fair share, failing to recognize that the systematic dealing procedure intrinsically ensures equality.', 'When asked if a boy in a video knew the dolls had a fair share before counting, a child replied ''No... Because he hadn''t counted them.''', 'whole_number', 'sharing', 'dealing and counting', 'student', 'second-grade', 'JRME_Davis_1990_Cognitive', 'Gary Ernest Davis and Anne Pitkethly', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '146, 149, 150, 152, 153', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Davis_1990_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37857, 'Students inappropriately apply whole-number ordering strategies or rote computational procedures (such as cross multiplication) to compare fractions with the same numerator, failing to recognize the inverse relationship between the denominator and the size of the fractional parts.', 'When asked which is larger, 4/15 or 4/10, a student responded ''4/15'' and reasoned ''Because 10 is less than 15.'' Another student reasoned, ''If you take 4 from 15 it''s 11; if you take 4 from 10 that''s 6. 6 is way smaller than 11.''', 'fraction', 'ordering', 'same numerator', 'student', 'fourth and fifth graders', 'JRME_Cramer_2002_Initial', 'Kathleen A. Cramer, Thomas R. Post, and Robert C. delMas', '2002', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '129, 130, 131', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Cramer_2002_Initial.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37858, 'When comparing two fractions that are each one piece away from the whole, students misapply an additive rule, incorrectly claiming the fractions are equal because the absolute difference between the numerator and denominator is the same.', 'When comparing 4/5 and 11/12, a student incorrectly concluded they are equal, explaining ''They are equal because 5 is one more than 4 and 12 is 1 more than 11.''', 'fraction', 'ordering', 'residual strategy', 'student', 'fourth and fifth graders', 'JRME_Cramer_2002_Initial', 'Kathleen A. Cramer, Thomas R. Post, and Robert C. delMas', '2002', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '130, 132, 133', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Cramer_2002_Initial.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37859, 'Rather than using number sense and the relative sizes of fractions to construct a reasonable estimate for a sum or difference, students blindly attempt to execute standard algorithms, frequently making whole-number additive errors in the process.', 'When asked to estimate the location of 11/12 - 4/6 on a number line, a student attempted to calculate an exact answer with a faulty algorithm: ''I took 11 - 4 = 7. I added 12 and 6 and got 18 so I got 7/18 and I thought 11/18 is between 1/2 and 1.''', 'fraction', 'estimation', 'addition and subtraction', 'student', 'fourth and fifth graders', 'JRME_Cramer_2002_Initial', 'Kathleen A. Cramer, Thomas R. Post, and Robert C. delMas', '2002', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '134, 135, 136, 137', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Cramer_2002_Initial.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37860, 'Students instantiate variables with single, fixed numerical values to resolve a word problem, failing to treat the quantities as variables that can represent a range of possible solutions.', 'When given a problem stating Tom is 4 inches taller than Maria and Maria is 6 inches shorter than Leslie, Kevin instantiated Maria''s height as exactly 4 feet 6 inches and calculated the others, rather than leaving the heights as variable.', 'algebraic', 'variables', 'additive functions', 'student', 'third-grade', 'JRME_Carraher_2005_Treating', 'David Carraher, Analúcia D. Schliemann and Bárbara Brizuela', '2005', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'productive', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Carraher_2005_Treating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37861, 'Students believe that a variable letter cannot represent a real-world quantity because real-world objects possess one specific, unvarying measure, conflicting with the mathematical definition of a variable.', 'Jennifer argued that ''x'' could not be used to represent Alan''s height because ''Everybody in the world has a height,'' and therefore it must have a specific number, not ''any'' number.', 'algebraic', 'variables', 'real-world contexts', 'student', 'third-grade', 'JRME_Carraher_2005_Treating', 'David Carraher, Analúcia D. Schliemann and Bárbara Brizuela', '2005', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'productive', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Carraher_2005_Treating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37862, 'Students fill out function tables by finding sequential numerical patterns within a single column rather than determining the functional rule that maps the input column to the output column.', 'To find the price for 3 boxes of cookies, students counted by 3s down the price column (3, 6, 9, 12...) instead of multiplying the number of boxes in the input column by 3.', 'algebraic', 'function tables', 'functional relationships', 'student', 'third-grade', 'JRME_Carraher_2005_Treating', 'David Carraher, Analúcia D. Schliemann and Bárbara Brizuela', '2005', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education. Monograph', 'productive', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Carraher_2005_Treating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37863, 'Students using a partitive fraction scheme view a unit fraction strictly as a part of a specific whole, causing them to incorrectly redefine the reference whole when a part is iterated to produce a quantity larger than the original whole.', 'When iterating a 1/8 piece nine times to produce a 9/8 stick, students named the result ''nine ninths'' or ''a whole'', shifting their reference whole to the newly created 9-part stick.', 'fraction', 'improper fractions', 'reference whole', 'student', 'fourth graders', 'JRME_Tzur_1999_Integrated', 'Ron Tzur', '1999', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Tzur_1999_Integrated.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37864, 'Students believe that changing a variable symbol implies changing its referent or the numerical value it represents.', 'When asked to supply a missing value in a function table where column C was changed to column A, a student gave a different value for A because ''just because the letter changed''.', 'algebraic', 'variables', 'literal symbols', 'student', 'Grades 5-12', 'JRME_Wagner_1981_Conservation', 'Sigrid Wagner', '1981', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '114, 115, 116', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Wagner_1981_Conservation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37865, 'Students assume that the linear ordering of the alphabet corresponds to the linear ordering of the number system, causing them to assign larger numerical values to letters that appear later in the alphabet.', 'When comparing identical equations with variables W and N, a student stated W would be larger ''because N comes first in the alphabet.''', 'algebraic', 'variables', 'alphabetic ordering', 'student', 'Grades 5-12', 'JRME_Wagner_1981_Conservation', 'Sigrid Wagner', '1981', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '114, 115, 116', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Wagner_1981_Conservation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37866, 'Students view equations solely as a signal to execute a computational procedure rather than as a statement of relationship, preventing them from using structural equivalence to compare expressions.', 'When asked whether W or N would be larger in two structurally identical equations, a student replied, ''You can''t tell without solving the equations.''', 'algebraic', 'equations', 'meaning of equation', 'student', 'Grades 5-12', 'JRME_Wagner_1981_Conservation', 'Sigrid Wagner', '1981', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '114, 116', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Wagner_1981_Conservation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37867, 'Students rely on key words in word problems to dictate the arithmetic operation, applying multiplication instead of division when they encounter the word ''times'', particularly in multiplicative compare problems with an unknown referent.', 'Rita justified using multiplication for a multiplicative compare referent problem (which required division) by stating, ''Well, it says ''3 times'' in the problem. We have to multiply!''', 'whole_number', 'multiplicative comparison word problems', 'inconsistent language', 'student', 'fifth-grade', 'JRME_Xin_2008_Effect', 'Yan Ping Xin', '2008', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '546', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Xin_2008_Effect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37868, 'Students choose arithmetic operations based on the size of the given numbers rather than the problem''s underlying semantic structure, avoiding operations that would produce answers they perceive as too large.', 'Sara applied division to a rate-times-a-quantity problem with the numbers 258 and 12 because she felt the product (3,096) would be ''too large'', instead producing an answer of 21 with a remainder of 6.', 'whole_number', 'word problems', 'choice of operation', 'student', 'fifth-grade', 'JRME_Xin_2008_Effect', 'Yan Ping Xin', '2008', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '545', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Xin_2008_Effect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37869, 'Students understand that a fraction symbol means to subdivide a figure but fail to recognize that the subdivisions must be equal in size.', 'A student sketched a circle with lines going through the center that divided sections into unequal parts, indicating the portions were meant to be of different sizes.', 'fraction', 'fraction representation', 'equal parts', 'student', 'sixth grade', 'JRME_Peck_1981_Conceptual', 'Donald M. Peck and Stanley M. Jencks', '1981', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '342', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Peck_1981_Conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37870, 'Students compare the sizes of two fractions by counting the number of unshaded pieces left over in their sketches rather than the relative area of the shaded portions.', 'A student sketched 2/3 and 3/4 and claimed that these fractions were equal because ''there are the same number of pieces left over.''', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'leftover pieces', 'student', 'sixth grade', 'JRME_Peck_1981_Conceptual', 'Donald M. Peck and Stanley M. Jencks', '1981', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '343', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Peck_1981_Conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37871, 'Students misapply and jumble portions of previously learned procedural rules when adding fractions, such as attempting to cross-cancel across addition signs.', 'To add 2/3 + 1/4, a student divided the 2 and 4, added the 3 and 2 to get 5, and left the 1s alone, resulting in an answer of 1/5.', 'fraction', 'fraction addition', 'procedural rules', 'student', 'sixth grade', 'JRME_Peck_1981_Conceptual', 'Donald M. Peck and Stanley M. Jencks', '1981', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '342, 343', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Peck_1981_Conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37872, 'Students incorrectly justify the flawed ''add numerators and add denominators'' rule by superficially combining their sketches.', 'A student claimed that 2/5 + 1/3 = 3/8 and justified it by drawing two separate sketches and combining the counts of shaded and total pieces.', 'fraction', 'fraction addition', 'visual models', 'student', 'sixth grade', 'JRME_Peck_1981_Conceptual', 'Donald M. Peck and Stanley M. Jencks', '1981', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '343', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Peck_1981_Conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37873, 'Students rely exclusively on the size of the denominator to compare fractions, assuming that more pieces automatically means a smaller fraction regardless of the numerator.', 'When comparing 2/5 and 3/7, a student reasoned that 3/7 is smaller because ''When you use sevenths, there are more pieces, so they are smaller.''', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'denominator dominance', 'student', 'sixth grade', 'JRME_Peck_1981_Conceptual', 'Donald M. Peck and Stanley M. Jencks', '1981', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '343', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Peck_1981_Conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37874, 'Pupils conceive of decimal numbers as integers with a decimal point or as pairs of integers separated by a decimal point, leading them to misapply integer properties such as asserting no number exists between consecutive decimals.', 'Answering that it is not possible to find a real number between 2.746 and 2.747, or incorrectly calculating (2.4)^2 = 4.16.', 'decimal', 'properties of decimals', 'density and operations', 'student', '7th-grade', 'JRME_Balacheff_1990_Towards', 'Nicolas Balacheff', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'secondary', '261, 262', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Balacheff_1990_Towards.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37875, 'Pupils hold the conception that the sum of the angles of a triangle depends on its shape and size, specifically believing that larger triangles have a larger sum of angles.', 'When asked to predict the sum of the angles of three contrasting triangles, pupils made predictions ranging from 160 degrees to 770 degrees.', 'geometric', 'triangles', 'sum of interior angles', 'student', '7th-grade', 'JRME_Balacheff_1990_Towards', 'Nicolas Balacheff', '1990', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '265, 266, 268, 269', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Balacheff_1990_Towards.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37876, 'Students commit a reversal error on inconsistent language compare word problems by directly translating the relational term into an arithmetic operation rather than using the required opposite operation.', 'When given a problem stating a quantity is ''2 times as many'' as the unknown quantity, a student incorrectly multiplies 120 by 2 instead of dividing.', 'whole_number', 'compare word problems', 'inconsistent language', 'student', 'sixth- and seventh-grade', 'JRME_Pape_2004_Middle', 'Stephen J. Pape', '2004', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '189, 190, 192, 196, 218', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Pape_2004_Middle.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37877, 'Students perform erroneous mathematical computations due to misrepresenting units or omitting computational steps indicated in the text.', 'A student multiplies a quantity by 7 when the given amount was already designated for 1 week.', 'whole_number', 'arithmetic word problems', 'units of measure', 'student', 'sixth- and seventh-grade', 'JRME_Pape_2004_Middle', 'Stephen J. Pape', '2004', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '196', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Pape_2004_Middle.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37878, 'Students overgeneralize whole-number concepts to proper fractions, mistakenly interpreting the numerator as the number of wholes and the denominator as the number of parts in each whole.', 'When asked the meaning of the symbolic representation 1/3, a student explained it meant ''three pieces or one pizza that''s cut into three pieces,'' or interpreted 3/5 as ''three whole pumpkin pies with five pieces in each pie.''', 'fraction', 'fraction symbols', 'meaning of numerator and denominator', 'student', 'third-grade and fourth-grade', 'JRME_Mack_1995_Confounding', 'Nancy K. Mack', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '430, 431, 432', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mack_1995_Confounding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37879, 'When solving symbolic subtraction problems involving a whole number and a fraction, students inappropriately treat the whole number as a fractional quantity, acting as if the whole number is the numerator of a fraction with the same denominator as the fraction being subtracted.', 'For the problem 1 - 4/5, a student read it as ''one minus four fifths'' but interpreted the ''1'' as ''one fifth'' and got an answer of three fifths by calculating ''four... take one away''.', 'fraction', 'subtraction', 'subtracting a fraction from a whole number', 'student', 'third-grade and fourth-grade', 'JRME_Mack_1995_Confounding', 'Nancy K. Mack', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '433, 434, 435', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mack_1995_Confounding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37880, 'Students do not understand that mathematical symbols require shared communication, leading them to inappropriately use whole numbers to represent fractional quantities in equations as long as they can mentally interpret the quantities correctly.', 'A student wrote the number sentence ''2/4 + 1/4 = 3'' and explained that the ''3'' stood for ''three fourths of a pizza,'' insisting that ''five eighths as five or I can write it as five eighths. It doesn''t matter.''', 'fraction', 'symbolic representation', 'shared communication', 'student', 'third-grade and fourth-grade', 'JRME_Mack_1995_Confounding', 'Nancy K. Mack', '1995', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '436, 437', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Mack_1995_Confounding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37881, 'When attempting to find a total that includes hidden items, students in a transitional counting stage may successfully count visible items first but then fail to keep track of the number of subsequent counts needed for the hidden items, resulting in an incorrect total.', 'When told there were 7 pigs hidden in a shed and 4 visible outside, a developing counter would count the visible pigs first and then try to count the hidden ones by pointing over the shed, but lose track of their counts and report an incorrect total of 8 or 9.', 'whole_number', 'counting', 'hidden items', 'student', 'preschool', 'JRME_Pepper_1998_Preschoolers''', 'Kristine L. Pepper and Robert P. Hunting', '1998', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '174', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Pepper_1998_Preschoolers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37882, 'When distributing items into hidden or opaque containers, a student''s over-reliance on counting skills to pre-measure shares can interfere with the successful use of systematic dealing procedures, leading to confusion if a counting error leaves a remainder.', 'A student attempted to pre-count groups of coins for each money box instead of systematically dealing them one by one. After a counting error left two remaining coins, he could not determine where to place them and tried unsuccessfully to look inside the boxes.', 'whole_number', 'sharing', 'dealing vs counting strategies', 'student', 'preschool', 'JRME_Pepper_1998_Preschoolers''', 'Kristine L. Pepper and Robert P. Hunting', '1998', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '180', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Pepper_1998_Preschoolers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37883, 'Students overgeneralize from whole numbers that addition always makes a quantity larger and subtraction always makes a quantity smaller, leading them to believe that counterintuitive integer open number sentences have no solution.', 'A second-grade student claimed that 6 + [] = 4 cannot be solved because 6 is the starting amount and adding more cannot result in a smaller sum of 4.', 'integer', 'integer addition and subtraction', 'counterintuitive problems', 'student', 'Grades 2, 4, 7, and 11', 'JRME_Lamb_2018_Cross-sectional', 'Lisa L. Lamb, Jessica Pierson Bishop, Randolph A. Philipp, Ian Whitacre and Bonnie P. Schappelle', '2018', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '576, 586, 600, 607, 608', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lamb_2018_Cross-sectional.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37884, 'Students confound numerical value with absolute magnitude when comparing negative numbers, concluding that subtraction problems resulting in a negative number with a larger absolute value are mathematically impossible.', 'When asked to solve -2 - [] = -8, 54% of students without prior integer instruction claimed it had no answer because they believed -8 is greater than -2 and subtraction should make the result smaller.', 'integer', 'integer subtraction', 'negative-side problems', 'student', 'Grades 2, 4, 7, and 11', 'JRME_Lamb_2018_Cross-sectional', 'Lisa L. Lamb, Jessica Pierson Bishop, Randolph A. Philipp, Ian Whitacre and Bonnie P. Schappelle', '2018', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '601, 602', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lamb_2018_Cross-sectional.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37885, 'The student conceptualizes speed strictly as a distance measuring unit (a speed-length) and understands time additively as the accumulation of these units, failing to see speed as a proportional relationship between distance and time.', 'When asked how long it would take to travel 100 feet at a speed of 0.1 ft/sec, Ann couldn''t answer because her strategy relied on practically measuring the 100 feet with the speed-length, which felt impossible with such a small unit.', 'proportional', 'speed and rate', 'speed as distance', 'mixed', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Thompson_1994_Talking', 'Patrick W. Thompson and Alba G. Thompson', '1994', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '287, 288, 289', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Thompson_1994_Talking.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37886, 'The teacher encapsulates sophisticated conceptual meanings of rate and proportionality within the language of arithmetic operations, mistakenly assuming that the student''s successful use of division implies a conceptual understanding of rate.', 'Bill assumed that because Ann correctly used division to find time (100 / 20), she understood speed conceptually as a rate, missing that she was merely using a ''fitting'' strategy to see how many speed-lengths physically fit into the total distance.', 'proportional', 'teaching rates', 'calculational language', 'mixed', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Thompson_1994_Talking', 'Patrick W. Thompson and Alba G. Thompson', '1994', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '289, 299, 300, 301', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Thompson_1994_Talking.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37887, 'The student views the equality of time intervals merely as a matter of physical drawing accuracy rather than recognizing the logical necessity of proportional correspondence between partitions of distance and time.', 'When drawing tick marks to represent seconds on a time line, Ann haphazardly drew unequal segments and stated they couldn''t be perfect unless accurately measured with a ruler by a scientist.', 'proportional', 'covariation', 'partitioning', 'mixed', 'sixth-grade', 'JRME_Thompson_1994_Talking', 'Patrick W. Thompson and Alba G. Thompson', '1994', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '294, 295', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Thompson_1994_Talking.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37888, 'Students confuse similar-sounding mathematical terms, incorrectly believing an intercepted arc refers to lines crossing or hitting each other.', 'A student thought ''intercept'' meant ''goin'' through it or hitting it'' and crossed his hands in an X to represent the lines intersecting.', 'geometric', 'circles and angles', 'intercepted arcs', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_WilliamsPierce_2017_What', 'Caroline (Caro) Williams-Pierce, Elizabeth L. Pier, Candace Walkington, Rebecca Boncoddo, Virginia Clinton, Martha W. Alibali and Mitchell J. Nathan', '2017', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '249, 250', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_WilliamsPierce_2017_What.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37889, 'Students attempt to prove the general triangle inequality conjecture by inappropriately citing the Pythagorean theorem formula and using static rather than transformational reasoning.', 'A participant stated that Mary''s conjecture is true ''because if you look at the formula a squared plus b squared equals c squared'' and formed a nondynamic triangle with her hands.', 'geometric', 'triangle inequality conjecture', 'transformational proofs', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_WilliamsPierce_2017_What', 'Caroline (Caro) Williams-Pierce, Elizabeth L. Pier, Candace Walkington, Rebecca Boncoddo, Virginia Clinton, Martha W. Alibali and Mitchell J. Nathan', '2017', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '253, 254, 255', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_WilliamsPierce_2017_What.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37890, 'Students who initially form valid proofs may adopt invalid ones when instructed to incorporate abstract physical actions that they struggle to connect meaningfully to the mathematical concepts.', 'When told that an alternating tapping action was related to a gear parity conjecture, nearly 18% of participants shifted from a valid to an invalid proof.', 'discrete', 'parity in gear systems', 'directed actions and proof', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'JRME_WilliamsPierce_2017_What', 'Caroline (Caro) Williams-Pierce, Elizabeth L. Pier, Candace Walkington, Rebecca Boncoddo, Virginia Clinton, Martha W. Alibali and Mitchell J. Nathan', '2017', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '256, 257', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_WilliamsPierce_2017_What.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37891, 'Children misinterpret the keyword ''more'' in compare subtraction problems as a cue to perform an additive operation, leading to the use of an incorrect strategy.', '', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'compare word problems', 'student', 'Grades 1 through 4', 'JRME_Adetula_1989_Solutions', 'Lawal O. Adetula', '1989', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '495, 496', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Adetula_1989_Solutions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37892, 'Students confuse the slope of a distance-time graph with speed, erroneously applying interpretations from a more recently learned speed-time graph.', 'Students become confused about the slope of a distance-time graph, and the teacher has to show them that the slope of a speed versus time graph is not speed.', 'algebraic', 'distance-time graphs', 'slope', 'in_service', 'Grades 6-12', 'JRME_Hohensee_2016_Teachers''', 'Charles Hohensee', '2016', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '24', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hohensee_2016_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37893, 'Students inappropriately apply the concept of a multiplicative growth or decay factor, learned from exponential functions, to previously learned linear functions.', 'The concept of multiplying the growth factor or decay factor [from exponential functions] sometimes confuses them.', 'algebraic', 'linear functions', 'constant rate of change', 'in_service', 'Grades 6-12', 'JRME_Hohensee_2016_Teachers''', 'Charles Hohensee', '2016', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '24', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hohensee_2016_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37894, 'Students confuse the rules for adding and subtracting integers with the rules for multiplying and dividing them after learning the latter operations.', 'When we go back to adding and subtracting, they''ll get themselves confused with the rules of the two.', 'integer', 'integer operations', 'addition and subtraction', 'in_service', 'Grades 6-12', 'JRME_Hohensee_2016_Teachers''', 'Charles Hohensee', '2016', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '24', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hohensee_2016_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37895, 'Students mix up the concepts of unit rates and complex fractions, applying procedures for complex fractions incorrectly to previously learned unit rates.', 'Learn a new concept and go back and apply wrong concepts to something they learned previously. We''re talking about unit rates, go talk about complex fractions, mix up the concepts.', 'ratio', 'unit rates', 'complex fractions', 'in_service', 'Grades 6-12', 'JRME_Hohensee_2016_Teachers''', 'Charles Hohensee', '2016', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '23, 25', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hohensee_2016_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37896, 'Students unnecessarily express metric conversions in scientific notation even when not prompted to, due to backward transfer from recently learning scientific notation.', 'Students might put all of their metric stuff into scientific notation when not asked for it.', 'measurement', 'metric conversions', 'scientific notation', 'in_service', 'Grades 6-12', 'JRME_Hohensee_2016_Teachers''', 'Charles Hohensee', '2016', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '23, 25', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Hohensee_2016_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37897, 'A student incorrectly claims that the area of a rectangle always increases as its perimeter increases, and the majority of U.S. teachers erroneously believe the student''s claim is valid.', 'A student provides the example of a 4 x 4 square changing to a 4 x 8 rectangle: perimeter increases from 16 to 24, while area increases from 16 to 32. Over 90% of U.S. teachers were inclined to believe that the student''s claim was valid.', 'geometric', 'area and perimeter', 'relationship between area and perimeter', 'in_service', 'elementary', 'JRME_Howe_1999_Knowing', 'Roger Howe', '1999', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Howe_1999_Knowing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37898, 'A student incorrectly multiplies multi-digit numbers by failing to shift the partial products to the left, and many teachers lack the place value understanding needed to explain the error conceptually.', 'The student correctly forms the partial products of 123 with the digits of 645 but aligns them directly underneath each other without shifting. Only about 40% of U.S. teachers could explain the reason for the correct alignment based on place value.', 'whole_number', 'multiplication', 'multi-digit algorithm and place value', 'in_service', 'elementary', 'JRME_Howe_1999_Knowing', 'Roger Howe', '1999', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Howe_1999_Knowing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37899, 'Teachers are unable to correctly perform the division of fractions or conceptualize a real-world scenario that models the operation.', 'Well under half of the American teachers computed 1 3/4 divided by 1/2 correctly, and only one created a technically acceptable, though pedagogically questionable, story problem.', 'fraction', 'division', 'contextual modeling', 'in_service', 'elementary', 'JRME_Howe_1999_Knowing', 'Roger Howe', '1999', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Howe_1999_Knowing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37900, 'A student incorrectly creates an array for a set number of items by drawing two unequal columns instead of arranging them in equal rows.', 'Jeremy drew one column of 14 dots and another of 10, incorrectly believing that as long as the total was 24, it satisfied the requirement for equal rows.', 'whole_number', 'multiplication', 'arrays and equal groups', 'in_service', 'third-grade', 'JRME_Bray_2011_Collective', 'Wendy S. Bray', '2011', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '22, 23', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bray_2011_Collective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37901, 'A student draws a model representing a division problem with remainders by creating equal groups that sum to a different total than the dividend, and pairs it with a mismatched number sentence.', 'For a problem with 23 candles and 3 in each row, Andre drew 3 columns of 8, totaling 24, and wrote the number sentence 24 / 8 = 3.', 'whole_number', 'division', 'remainders and models', 'in_service', 'third-grade', 'JRME_Bray_2011_Collective', 'Wendy S. Bray', '2011', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '26, 27', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bray_2011_Collective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37902, 'A student executes the standard multidigit multiplication algorithm without maintaining the proper place values of the partial products.', 'A student calculates 645 x 123 by stacking the partial products directly under each other without shifting to account for place value.', 'whole_number', 'multiplication', 'standard algorithm and place value', 'in_service', 'third-grade', 'JRME_Bray_2011_Collective', 'Wendy S. Bray', '2011', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '13, 18, 19, 20', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bray_2011_Collective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37903, 'A student inappropriately applies repeated division by 2 based on additive composition instead of multiplicative composition to solve a division problem.', 'To solve 144 / 8, the student divided by 2 four times, reasoning incorrectly that ''2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 8''.', 'whole_number', 'division', 'nonstandard strategy', 'in_service', 'third-grade', 'JRME_Bray_2011_Collective', 'Wendy S. Bray', '2011', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '13, 20, 21', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Bray_2011_Collective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37904, 'Students with phonological-processing deficits fail to use spoken language as a transitive bridge to connect different mathematical representations, treating each physical model, spoken word, and written symbol as an isolated concept.', 'The student could not spontaneously connect an index-card model of ''five halves'' with the written symbol 5/2, and she misnamed 8/8 as ''eight over eight'' rather than ''eight eighths'', failing to perceive the fractional relationship between the numerator and denominator.', 'fraction', 'multiple representations', 'concept flexibility', 'student', 'fifth grade', 'JRME_Montis_2000_Language', 'Kristine K. Montis', '2000', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '549, 550, 551, 552', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Montis_2000_Language.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37905, 'Students miscopy written numbers due to incorrect internal subvocalization of the symbols rather than due to visual perception errors or visual reversals.', 'The student copied the number 368 but wrote a 9 instead of a 6 because she had incorrectly verbalized the digit to herself, even though she could visually distinguish a 6 from a 9 when explicitly asked.', 'whole_number', 'symbolic representation', 'copying numbers', 'student', 'fifth grade', 'JRME_Montis_2000_Language', 'Kristine K. Montis', '2000', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '547, 548', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Montis_2000_Language.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37906, 'Students apply a whole-number ordering rule to denominators when comparing fractions, failing to incorporate the inverse relation between numerator and denominator.', 'One third is less than one fourth "because three is less than four."', 'fraction', 'fraction order', 'same numerators', 'student', 'fourth grade', 'JRME_Behr_1984_Order', 'Merlyn J. Behr, Ipke Wachsmuth, Thomas R. Post and Richard Lesh', '1984', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '328, 333, 337', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Behr_1984_Order.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37907, 'Students incorrectly compare the sizes of fractional parts when the denominators are the same by inverting the relation between numerator and denominator.', 'Nine thirteenths is less than four thirteenths because "four pieces are so big, nine pieces would have to be smaller to fit the whole."', 'fraction', 'fraction order', 'same denominators', 'student', 'fourth grade', 'JRME_Behr_1984_Order', 'Merlyn J. Behr, Ipke Wachsmuth, Thomas R. Post and Richard Lesh', '1984', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '330', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Behr_1984_Order.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37908, 'Students erroneously evaluate fraction equivalence by adding a constant to both the numerator and the denominator.', 'Three fourths equals seven eighths because "three plus four equals seven, and four plus four equals eight."', 'fraction', 'fraction equivalence', 'different numerators and denominators', 'student', 'fourth grade', 'JRME_Behr_1984_Order', 'Merlyn J. Behr, Ipke Wachsmuth, Thomas R. Post and Richard Lesh', '1984', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '331, 336', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Behr_1984_Order.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37909, 'Students make use of one correct ratio in a proportion but fail to apply it correctly across the entire equivalent fraction relationship.', 'Asked to solve 6/4 = [ ]/8, the child writes 3 in the box because "three into six twice; two times four equals eight."', 'fraction', 'fraction equivalence', 'missing value', 'student', 'fourth grade', 'JRME_Behr_1984_Order', 'Merlyn J. Behr, Ipke Wachsmuth, Thomas R. Post and Richard Lesh', '1984', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '332', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Behr_1984_Order.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37910, 'Students make separate comparisons of the numerators and the denominators using the ordering of whole numbers to judge fraction inequalities.', 'Three fifths is less than six tenths because "3 is less than 6, and 5 is less than 10."', 'fraction', 'fraction order', 'different numerators and denominators', 'student', 'fourth grade', 'JRME_Behr_1984_Order', 'Merlyn J. Behr, Ipke Wachsmuth, Thomas R. Post and Richard Lesh', '1984', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '332, 337', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Behr_1984_Order.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37911, 'Students incorrectly apply a formula for the second difference of a quadratic function table derived for a height increment of 1 to a table with a height increment of 5, resulting in an overgeneralization that fails to mathematically account for the varying height increments.', 'Daeshim calculated the second difference as 2 * 5^2 * 15 = 750, incorrectly using a prior generalization that was only valid when the difference between successive height values was 1.', 'algebraic', 'quadratic functions', 'second differences', 'student', 'eighth-grade', 'JRME_Ellis_2011_Generalizing-promoting', 'Amy B. Ellis', '2011', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '326, 327, 344, 345', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Ellis_2011_Generalizing-promoting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37912, 'Students incorrectly assume that a generalized relationship found in a specific quadratic table, such as the second difference divided by 2 equaling the parameter ''a'', holds true universally for all quadratic tables regardless of the independent variable''s increment.', 'Bianca hypothesized that ''DiRoG over 2 equals a'' or ''DiRoG over 2 equals DiL'', which is only mathematically valid when the height value increases by 1, failing to recognize its limited domain.', 'algebraic', 'quadratic functions', 'parameter a and second differences', 'student', 'eighth-grade', 'JRME_Ellis_2011_Generalizing-promoting', 'Amy B. Ellis', '2011', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '330, 331, 344', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Ellis_2011_Generalizing-promoting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37913, 'Students use a deterministic approach to one-stage probability experiments, imposing a degree of certainty where none exists by incorrectly believing only one outcome is possible, often driven by subjective preferences.', 'When asked what colors could come up on a red-white spinner, a student only listed ''Red'' because ''it''s my favorite color'', failing to acknowledge the full sample space.', 'probability', 'sample space', 'one-stage experiments', 'student', '3rd-grade', 'JRME_Jones_1999_Students''', 'Graham A. Jones, Cynthia W. Langrall, Carol A. Thornton, A. Timothy Mogill', '1999', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Jones_1999_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37914, 'Students automatically assume that any probability situation with exactly two possible outcomes must have equally likely outcomes (a 50-50 chance), regardless of the actual quantitative distribution.', 'When comparing 1 red and 4 green chips in a gumball machine, a student incorrectly asserted that ''They are the same; it''s 50-50'' simply because there were two colors.', 'probability', 'probability of an event', 'equally likely outcomes', 'student', '3rd-grade', 'JRME_Jones_1999_Students''', 'Graham A. Jones, Cynthia W. Langrall, Carol A. Thornton, A. Timothy Mogill', '1999', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Jones_1999_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37915, 'In conditional probability scenarios involving nonreplacement, students fail to recognize that the total number of outcomes (the whole) has decreased, and they continue calculating probabilities based on the original sample space.', 'After drawing two red bears from an initial set of nine, a student recognized that there were still two yellow bears remaining but failed to account for the total dropping to seven, claiming the probability had not changed.', 'probability', 'conditional probability', 'without-replacement tasks', 'student', '3rd-grade', 'JRME_Jones_1999_Students''', 'Graham A. Jones, Cynthia W. Langrall, Carol A. Thornton, A. Timothy Mogill', '1999', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Jones_1999_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37916, 'Students view fractions strictly as pieces of a shape or a situation rather than as numbers that can be placed on a number line.', 'When asked to put the fraction 2/3 on a number line, a student said, ''You can''t put it on a number line, because it''s two pieces out of three pieces, it''s not a number.''', 'fraction', 'fractions as numbers', 'number line representation', 'mixed', 'Grades 2-5', 'JRME_Lewis_2017_Lesson', 'Catherine Lewis and Rebecca Perry', '2017', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '266', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lewis_2017_Lesson.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37917, 'Students incorrectly add fractions with different denominators by summing the numerators and summing the denominators, failing to understand they are adding units of different sizes.', 'Students add 1/3 + 1/5 and get 2/8, without realizing they are adding two different things (thirds and fifths), sort of like adding apples and hammers.', 'fraction', 'fraction addition', 'unlike denominators', 'mixed', 'Grades 2-5', 'JRME_Lewis_2017_Lesson', 'Catherine Lewis and Rebecca Perry', '2017', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '266', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lewis_2017_Lesson.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37918, 'Students judge the size of a fraction by incorrectly comparing only the numerators, only the denominators, or the difference between the numerator and denominator.', 'A student may think 4/9 is bigger than 3/4 because 4 is bigger than 3, or that 3/5 is the same size as 5/7 because the difference between the top and the bottom in both fractions is 2.', 'fraction', 'fraction magnitude', 'fraction comparison', 'mixed', 'Grades 2-5', 'JRME_Lewis_2017_Lesson', 'Catherine Lewis and Rebecca Perry', '2017', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '267', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lewis_2017_Lesson.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37919, 'Students erroneously judge two identical fractions as representing the exact same quantity without considering the size of the original reference whole, a misconception exacerbated by textbooks that present fractions without contexts.', 'Students end up putting ''equal'' when asked to compare ''half'' and ''half'', without realizing that half of a small cookie is not the same as half of a large cookie.', 'fraction', 'fraction of a whole', 'reference whole', 'mixed', 'Grades 2-5', 'JRME_Lewis_2017_Lesson', 'Catherine Lewis and Rebecca Perry', '2017', 'Journal for Research in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '266, 286', 'pdfs/JRME/JRME_Lewis_2017_Lesson.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37920, 'Students inappropriately model a two-stage random experiment by treating a probability tree as a simple choice tree to count total outcomes, implicitly assuming all outcomes are equally probable even when they are not.', 'To find the probability of drawing a 10-euro banknote from one of two randomly chosen wallets with different banknote distributions, a student conceptually ''emptied'' both wallets to count 5 ten-euro notes out of 14 total notes, yielding an incorrect probability of 5/14.', 'probability', 'Probability calculation', 'Weighted trees and equal probability', 'mixed', 'Grades 9 and 10 (age 14 and 15)', 'ZDM_Kuzniak_2016_Understanding', 'Alain Kuzniak, Assia Nechache, J. P. Drouhard', '2016', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '867, 868', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Kuzniak_2016_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37921, 'Students incorrectly evaluate the magnitude of fractions by relying on the magnitudes of their natural number components, struggling to recognize that the overall magnitude scales inversely with the denominator.', 'Students find it difficult to correctly compare inconsistent fraction pairs, such as 5/6 and 8/19, because the two greater natural number components (8 and 19) are found in the lesser fraction (8/19).', 'fraction', 'Fraction comparison', 'Magnitude', 'student', '5th, 6th, and 7th grade', 'ZDM_Gomez_2015_Effect', 'David Maximiliano Gomez, Abelino Jimenez, Roberto Bobadilla, Cristian Reyes, Pablo Dartnell', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '803, 804', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Gomez_2015_Effect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37922, 'Students lack cognitive control when translating a generalized verbal procedure for a geometric pattern into a specific visual representation, resulting in incorrectly constructed drawings.', 'When asked to represent the fifth triangular number (T5), a student successfully explains a generic procedure verbally but draws a figure where the three center columns have four ''rounds'' each, rather than descending properly (4, 3, 2, 1).', 'algebraic', 'Pattern generalization', 'Triangular numbers', 'student', '7th grade', 'ZDM_Hitt_2016_Arithmetic-algebraic', 'Fernando Hitt, Mireille Saboya, Carlos Cortes Zavala', '2016', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '780', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Hitt_2016_Arithmetic-algebraic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37923, 'Students experience an ephemeral consensus regarding generalized algebraic formulas, abandoning the algebraic representations they co-constructed in favor of primitive drawing strategies or incorrectly remembered formulas after a period of time.', 'Forty-five days after constructing a valid algebraic formula to calculate triangular numbers during class consensus, some students reverted to drawing dots to find the answer, while another student incorrectly wrote a remembered formula without verifying it.', 'algebraic', 'Algebraic generalization', 'Formula retention', 'student', '7th grade', 'ZDM_Hitt_2016_Arithmetic-algebraic', 'Fernando Hitt, Mireille Saboya, Carlos Cortes Zavala', '2016', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '788', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Hitt_2016_Arithmetic-algebraic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37924, 'When solving linear equations, students confuse the inverse principle used to isolate the unknown with the balance principle used to maintain equality across the equation, mistakenly applying opposite operations to both sides.', 'To solve ? + 4 = 7, a student reasoned that 4 had to be subtracted from the left side to find ?, and therefore incorrectly added 4 to the right side.', 'algebraic', 'Linear equations', 'Balance and inverse principles', 'student', 'Years 2 to 6', 'ZDM_Cooper_2008_Effect', 'Tom J. Cooper, Elizabeth Warren', '2008', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '32, 33', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Cooper_2008_Effect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37925, 'Students struggle to generalize pattern rules directly from the visual geometry of growing patterns, failing to notice structural commonalities without relying on numerical tables of values.', '', 'algebraic', 'Growing patterns', 'Visual analysis', 'student', 'Years 2 to 6', 'ZDM_Cooper_2008_Effect', 'Tom J. Cooper, Elizabeth Warren', '2008', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '28, 30', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Cooper_2008_Effect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37926, 'When writing equations to represent the inverse change (backtracking) of a function, students struggle to write the reverse equation left-to-right and fail to apply the correct order of operations.', 'For a function that multiplies by 3 then adds 4, students struggled to formulate the inverse as (25 - 4) / 3 = 7, and some incorrectly used arithmetic symbols instead of words like x3 + 4.', 'algebraic', 'Functions and change', 'Inverse equations', 'student', 'Years 2 to 6', 'ZDM_Cooper_2008_Effect', 'Tom J. Cooper, Elizabeth Warren', '2008', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '30', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Cooper_2008_Effect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37927, 'Students experience confusion because the subtraction compensation principle requires applying the same change to both numbers, which directly opposes the recently learned addition compensation principle requiring opposite changes.', '', 'algebraic', 'Compensation principles', 'Addition and subtraction', 'student', 'Years 2 to 6', 'ZDM_Cooper_2008_Effect', 'Tom J. Cooper, Elizabeth Warren', '2008', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '33', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Cooper_2008_Effect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37928, 'Prospective teachers erroneously believe that 0.999... is not exactly equal to 1, often viewing it as a decimal that runs against 1, is approximately equal, or cannot be converted to an integer.', 'Student teachers justify that 0.999... != 1 by stating ''0.99999... is a decimal number which cannot be converted into an integer, but 1 is an integer'' or ''We round up to the next integer. Formally correct, you should write 0.99999... is approx 1.''', 'calculus', 'Infinity and limits', 'Equality of 0.999... and 1', 'PST', 'secondary level', 'ZDM_Buchholtz_2013_Future', 'Nils Buchholtz, Frederick K. S. Leung, Lin Ding, Gabriele Kaiser, Kyungmee Park, Bjorn Schwarz', '2013', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '114, 115', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Buchholtz_2013_Future.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37929, 'Prospective teachers rely on school-influenced functional prototypes and superficial characteristics—such as requiring a function to be defined on all real numbers, be bijective, or have a continuous graph—to erroneously reject valid functions.', 'When asked if f(n) = sqrt(n) for n in N is a valid function, some rejected it by stating ''This is a radical-function, which assigns every x-value to two y-values'' or ''Functions are defined on R... THIS is a sequence.''', 'algebraic', 'Concept of function', 'Domain and characteristics of radical functions', 'PST', 'secondary level', 'ZDM_Buchholtz_2013_Future', 'Nils Buchholtz, Frederick K. S. Leung, Lin Ding, Gabriele Kaiser, Kyungmee Park, Bjorn Schwarz', '2013', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '116, 117, 118', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Buchholtz_2013_Future.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37930, 'Individuals hold the misconception that a finite number sequence can only be continued in one specific way governed by a single algebraic expression.', 'Assuming that for the sequence 3, 6, 9, 12..., the expression must be T(n) = 3n and therefore the next term must be exactly 15 with no other possible answer.', 'algebraic', 'Number sequences', 'Algebraic generalization', 'PST', 'secondary level', 'ZDM_Buchholtz_2013_Future', 'Nils Buchholtz, Frederick K. S. Leung, Lin Ding, Gabriele Kaiser, Kyungmee Park, Bjorn Schwarz', '2013', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '111, 112', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Buchholtz_2013_Future.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37931, 'Future teachers misunderstand the formal mathematical necessity of extending rational numbers to real numbers, believing it is merely to fill gaps on the number line with irrational numbers rather than to solve certain algebraic equations.', 'Selecting the distractor option that irrational numbers are introduced because an irrational number exists between any two rational numbers, instead of the need to solve equations like x^2 = a.', 'rational', 'Number systems', 'Extension to real numbers', 'PST', 'secondary level', 'ZDM_Buchholtz_2013_Future', 'Nils Buchholtz, Frederick K. S. Leung, Lin Ding, Gabriele Kaiser, Kyungmee Park, Bjorn Schwarz', '2013', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '111, 112', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Buchholtz_2013_Future.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37932, 'Students view the equal sign operationally as a computational command to calculate a result, rather than relationally as a symbol of quantitative equivalence or sameness.', 'Given the missing number sentence 8 + 4 = [ ] + 5, students commonly suggest 12 (calculating 8 + 4) or 17 (adding all the numbers) as the answer.', 'algebraic', 'Equality', 'Meaning of the equal sign', 'PST', 'elementary school', 'ZDM_Crespo_2011_Imagining', 'Sandra Crespo, Joy Ann Oslund, Amy Noelle Parks', '2011', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '122, 123, 124', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Crespo_2011_Imagining.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37933, 'Students using highly automated instructional software may interpret underlying mathematical properties, such as the consistent size of units, as arbitrary features of the computer interface rather than logically necessary structural properties of the mathematical model.', 'A student working with an automated interface discovers that meters cannot be resized and recites ''the meters are always the same'' as an interface rule she has detected, rather than understanding it as an instrumental function for solving the problem.', 'algebraic', 'Algebraic modeling', 'Consistent measures and variables', 'student', 'Grade 4 and Grade 9', 'ZDM_Chase_2015_Reverse-scaffolding', 'Kiera Chase, Dor Abrahamson', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '1205, 1206', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Chase_2015_Reverse-scaffolding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37934, 'During multidigit subtraction, students incorrectly subtract the smaller digit from the larger digit within a column regardless of which is the minuend or subtrahend.', 'When solving 346 - 157, a student computes the difference as 211 by subtracting the smaller numbers from the larger numbers in each column (6 - 7 becomes 7 - 6 = 1, 4 - 5 becomes 5 - 4 = 1).', 'whole_number', 'Multidigit subtraction', 'Regrouping and columns', 'student', 'kindergarten, Grade 1, Grade 2', 'ZDM_Fuson_2009_Cross-cultural', 'Karen C. Fuson, Yeping Li', '2009', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '804, 805', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Fuson_2009_Cross-cultural.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37935, 'English-speaking children fail to conceptualize teen numbers as composed of a ten and some ones due to irregular English number words, leading them to rely on less efficient counting strategies rather than make-a-ten methods.', 'When finding a single-digit total over ten, such as 9 + 5, a student might write down 1 in the tens place and then count fingers (eleven, twelve, thirteen, fourteen) to find out they need to write 4 in the ones place.', 'whole_number', 'Single-digit addition and subtraction', 'Teen numbers and base-ten structure', 'student', 'kindergarten, Grade 1, Grade 2', 'ZDM_Fuson_2009_Cross-cultural', 'Karen C. Fuson, Yeping Li', '2009', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '795, 796, 800, 801', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Fuson_2009_Cross-cultural.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37936, 'Students erroneously believe that mathematical equations must strictly format the operations on the left side and a single answer on the right side.', '', 'algebraic', 'Equations', 'Equals sign', 'student', 'kindergarten, Grade 1, Grade 2', 'ZDM_Fuson_2009_Cross-cultural', 'Karen C. Fuson, Yeping Li', '2009', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '800', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Fuson_2009_Cross-cultural.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37937, 'Students mistakenly believe that ungrouping (borrowing) changes the total value of the top multidigit number because they view subtraction as isolated vertical operations on single digits.', '', 'whole_number', 'Multidigit subtraction', 'Ungrouping and conservation of value', 'student', 'kindergarten, Grade 1, Grade 2', 'ZDM_Fuson_2009_Cross-cultural', 'Karen C. Fuson, Yeping Li', '2009', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '804, 805', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Fuson_2009_Cross-cultural.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37938, 'Students erroneously assume that force is directly proportional to velocity, believing that moving at a constant speed requires a constant net force and results in a constant non-zero acceleration.', 'Stating in a written response that if an object''s speed does not change, ''the forces acting on the object are constant as is the acceleration.''', 'proportional', 'Direct proportionality', 'Rates and equations', 'PST', 'freshman', 'ZDM_Tasar_2010_What', 'Mehmet Fatih Tasar', '2010', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '474, 475', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Tasar_2010_What.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37939, 'Students incorrectly interpret a decreasing rate of change as a decrease in the overall quantity, assuming that a decreasing acceleration means the object is decelerating or losing speed.', 'Believing that if an object is pushed with a continuously decreasing force (decreasing acceleration), its velocity will decrease, rather than recognizing it is still gaining speed but at a slower rate.', 'calculus', 'Rate of change', 'Acceleration and velocity', 'PST', 'freshman', 'ZDM_Tasar_2010_What', 'Mehmet Fatih Tasar', '2010', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '476, 477, 478', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Tasar_2010_What.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37940, 'Students inappropriately overgeneralize the algorithm for multiplication to the addition of fractions, separately adding numerators and denominators without finding a common denominator.', 'Calculating 1/2 + 1/4 as 2/6 instead of the correct 3/4.', 'fraction', 'Addition of fractions', 'Common denominators', 'student', 'grade 6', 'ZDM_Hoch_2018_Design', 'Stefan Hoch, Frank Reinhold, Bernhard Werner, Jurgen Richter-Gebert, Kristina Reiss', '2018', 'ZDM', 'deficit', 'secondary', '840', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Hoch_2018_Design.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37941, 'Students overgeneralize properties of natural numbers to rational numbers, incorrectly assuming that multiplication will always result in a larger product.', 'Believing the statement ''Multiplication always enlarges'' when working with fractions.', 'fraction', 'Multiplication of fractions', 'Magnitude of products', 'student', 'grade 6', 'ZDM_Hoch_2018_Design', 'Stefan Hoch, Frank Reinhold, Bernhard Werner, Jurgen Richter-Gebert, Kristina Reiss', '2018', 'ZDM', 'deficit', 'secondary', '840', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Hoch_2018_Design.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37942, 'Students incorrectly anticipate that addition and multiplication always result in a larger number, and that subtraction and division always produce a smaller number, generalizing from natural number behavior.', 'Students incorrectly judged operations like 6 : _ = 14 or _ x 4 = 1 as not possible because they violated their intuition that division makes numbers smaller and multiplication makes them larger.', 'rational', 'Arithmetic operations', 'Effect of operations', 'student', 'fifth and sixth graders', 'ZDM_Christou_2015_Natural', 'Konstantinos P. Christou', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '748, 749, 751, 755', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Christou_2015_Natural.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37943, 'When reasoning about unknown or missing numbers, students assume these symbols represent natural numbers and try to substitute them exclusively with natural numbers.', 'Students performed poorly on congruent tasks with missing rational numbers (e.g., 6 x _ = 11) compared to natural numbers (e.g., 7 x _ = 21) because they attempted to substitute the missing symbol only with natural numbers.', 'rational', 'Missing numbers', 'Variable substitution', 'student', 'fifth and sixth graders', 'ZDM_Christou_2015_Natural', 'Konstantinos P. Christou', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '749, 750, 752, 755', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Christou_2015_Natural.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37944, 'Students misapply natural number properties when ordering rational numbers, such as believing a larger denominator makes a fraction larger or more digits make a decimal larger.', 'Almost half of the students ordered unit fractions using the rule ''the bigger the denominator the bigger the fraction'', and 36% ordered decimals following ''the longer the number, the larger''.', 'rational', 'Ordering numbers', 'Fractions and decimals', 'student', 'fifth and sixth graders', 'ZDM_Christou_2015_Natural', 'Konstantinos P. Christou', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '753', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Christou_2015_Natural.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37945, 'Students believe that rational numbers have a discrete structure like natural numbers, asserting that there are no numbers or only a finite amount of numbers between two pseudosuccessive fractions or decimals.', 'When asked how many numbers are between 0.005 and 0.006, the majority of students responded that there is no other number in between.', 'rational', 'Density of rational numbers', 'Discreteness', 'student', 'fifth and sixth graders', 'ZDM_Christou_2015_Natural', 'Konstantinos P. Christou', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '748, 752, 754', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Christou_2015_Natural.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37946, 'Students harbor the misconception that continuity of a real-valued function necessarily implies differentiability.', 'Assuming that a function must be differentiable everywhere it is continuous, which is challenged by Weierstrass'' continuous-everywhere-but-differentiable-nowhere function.', 'calculus', 'Derivatives and continuity', 'Differentiability', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Sriraman_2017_Mathematical', 'Bharath Sriraman, Benjamin Dickman', '2017', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'secondary', '137', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Sriraman_2017_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37947, 'Students misinterpret the rule for cancellation in the simplification of fractions, applying it inappropriately to individual digits.', 'Simplifying 19/95 to 1/5 by incorrectly canceling the 9s.', 'fraction', 'Fraction simplification', 'Cancellation', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Sriraman_2017_Mathematical', 'Bharath Sriraman, Benjamin Dickman', '2017', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '140', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Sriraman_2017_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37948, 'Students assume that a whole number''s partial products representation uniquely identifies its factors, drawing from intuition about unique prime factorization.', 'Discovering that the number 1008 can be decomposed into partial products corresponding to three different factorizations (21x48, 84x12, and 42x24).', 'whole_number', 'Multiplication', 'Partial products and factorization', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Sriraman_2017_Mathematical', 'Bharath Sriraman, Benjamin Dickman', '2017', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '140, 141', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Sriraman_2017_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37949, 'Students attempt to apply Euler''s formula to pathological solids where it does not hold.', 'Applying Euler''s formula to solids with curved surfaces, polyhedra with holes, or solids made of multiple polyhedra sharing points, lines, or faces.', 'geometric', 'Polyhedra', 'Euler''s formula', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Sriraman_2017_Mathematical', 'Bharath Sriraman, Benjamin Dickman', '2017', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '141', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Sriraman_2017_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37950, 'Students mistakenly assume that a four-set Venn diagram showing all possible regions of overlap can be drawn using four circles.', 'Attempting to draw a 4-Venn diagram with four circles, failing to represent the intersection of sets A and C, or B and D.', 'whole_number', 'Least common multiple (LCM)', 'Venn diagrams', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Sriraman_2017_Mathematical', 'Bharath Sriraman, Benjamin Dickman', '2017', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '142, 143', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Sriraman_2017_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37951, 'Students incorrectly translate the relational term in an additive comparison word problem directly into an arithmetic operation, leading to errors in inconsistent-language problems where the term conflicts with the necessary operation.', 'In an inconsistent-language problem like ''John has 8 more marbles than does Mary. If John has 15 marbles, how many marbles does Mary have?'', students struggle because the phrase ''more than'' conflicts with the required subtraction operation.', 'whole_number', 'Word problems', 'Additive comparison', 'mixed', 'first grade', 'ZDM_Huang_2018_Developing', 'Rongjin Huang, Qinqiong Zhang, Yu-ping Chang, Dovie Kimmins', '2018', 'ZDM', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Huang_2018_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37952, 'Students fail to recognize the conceptual equivalence between the terms ''how many more'', ''how many less'', and ''the difference'' when comparing two quantities.', 'Students could not solve a post-test problem asking for ''the difference'' between 2 and 5 pieces of chocolate, stating they did not understand what the difference means, even though they were capable of solving it if asked ''how many more''.', 'whole_number', 'Word problems', 'Additive comparison', 'mixed', 'first grade', 'ZDM_Huang_2018_Developing', 'Rongjin Huang, Qinqiong Zhang, Yu-ping Chang, Dovie Kimmins', '2018', 'ZDM', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Huang_2018_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37953, 'When asked to name a share of a collection relationally, students answer with a simple count of the items in the share instead of describing its size relative to the whole collection.', 'Students regarded ''how many coins did each person get?'' and ''how much of the whole collection did each person get?'' as identical, answering both with a simple count of the items.', 'fraction', 'Equipartitioning collections', 'Relational naming', 'student', '2nd through 4th graders', 'ZDM_Confrey_2015_Design', 'Jere Confrey, Alan Maloney', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '924, 925', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Confrey_2015_Design.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37954, 'When comparing the whole collection to a single fair share, students use additive language focusing on difference rather than multiplicative language.', 'For more than two persons sharing, students tended to describe the collection in terms of how many more objects were in the whole collection than in the fair share.', 'ratio', 'Multiplicative comparison', 'Reassembly', 'student', '2nd through 4th graders', 'ZDM_Confrey_2015_Design', 'Jere Confrey, Alan Maloney', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '925', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Confrey_2015_Design.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37955, 'When equipartitioning a single whole, students fail to apply necessary criteria such as creating equal-sized pieces, obtaining the correct number of shares, and exhausting the whole.', 'Students drew 6 parallel lines on a rectangle to share for 6 people, resulting in 7 parts, or removed equal-sized pieces without using the entire whole because ''at home four children would never be allowed to eat an entire cake''.', 'geometric', 'Equipartitioning single wholes', 'Equipartitioning criteria', 'student', '2nd through 4th graders', 'ZDM_Confrey_2015_Design', 'Jere Confrey, Alan Maloney', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'primary', '925, 926', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Confrey_2015_Design.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37956, 'Students believe that a circle cannot be fairly partitioned for an odd number of people, struggling to conceptualize radial cuts.', 'Many students claimed it was impossible to share a circle for an odd number of people, while a few drew a ''peace symbol'' or ''Mercedes Benz logo'' to achieve a 3-split.', 'geometric', 'Equipartitioning single wholes', 'Sharing circles for odd numbers', 'student', '2nd through 4th graders', 'ZDM_Confrey_2015_Design', 'Jere Confrey, Alan Maloney', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '926', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Confrey_2015_Design.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37957, 'Students evaluate mathematical propositions using the logic of everyday ''open general statements,'' assuming rules are generally true but might admit exceptions under modifying conditions, rather than viewing them as universally valid ''closed'' theorems.', 'A pupil reasoned that 5! is divisible by 3 because it includes a factor of 3, but then explicitly checked that the subsequent multiplications by 2 and 1 wouldn''t ''change'' or disturb the divisibility, showing he viewed the algebraic rule as potentially vulnerable to exceptions.', 'other', 'Mathematical proof', 'General validity', 'student', 'secondary and tertiary levels, Yr8, Yr9', 'ZDM_Jahnke_2008_Theorems', 'Hans Niels Jahnke', '2008', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'primary', '363, 369', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Jahnke_2008_Theorems.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37958, 'Students conflate a mathematical implication with its logical converse because the strict distinction between necessary and sufficient conditions loses its decisiveness when reasoning is based on open general statements.', '', 'other', 'Mathematical proof', 'Logical implication and converse', 'student', 'secondary and tertiary levels, Yr8, Yr9', 'ZDM_Jahnke_2008_Theorems', 'Hans Niels Jahnke', '2008', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'primary', '368', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Jahnke_2008_Theorems.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37959, 'Students erroneously conclude that a function''s limit at infinity does not exist by uncritically interpreting the apparent oscillation shown on a graphing calculator''s viewing window.', 'Inferring that the limit of x + ln x + 10 sin x does not exist as x approaches infinity because the curve oscillates visually on the calculator screen.', 'calculus', 'Limits', 'Infinity', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Trouche_2010_Handheld', 'Luc Trouche, Paul Drijvers', '2010', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'secondary', '672', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Trouche_2010_Handheld.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37960, 'Students lack awareness of the limitations of the viewing window or pixel effects, leading them to copy strange or distorted graphical representations directly onto paper as if they were mathematically accurate.', 'Copying a distorted, disjointed graph of a rational function from a calculator screen directly onto paper without compensating for pixel limitations.', 'algebraic', 'Functions and graphs', 'Rational functions and asymptotes', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Trouche_2010_Handheld', 'Luc Trouche, Paul Drijvers', '2010', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '671', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Trouche_2010_Handheld.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37961, 'Students misinterpret the reference unit of a fraction in a story problem, confusing a fraction of a specific standard unit with a fraction of the total available amount.', 'Rex determined Kenzie could hike 2 miles instead of 8 because he thought ''drinks 1/2 cup of water'' meant drinking 1/2 of her total 4 cups of water every mile.', 'fraction', 'Word problems', 'Reference units', 'mixed', 'fourth and fifth graders', 'ZDM_Jacobs_2016_Responding', 'Victoria R. Jacobs, Susan B. Empson', '2016', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '190, 191', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Jacobs_2016_Responding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37962, 'Students equate differing units when reasoning about rates, ignoring the specific fractional relationship given in the problem.', 'Ethan initially answered 4 miles for a problem asking how many miles one could hike with 4 cups if drinking 2/3 cup per mile, incorrectly equating 1 cup of water with 1 mile hiked.', 'fraction', 'Rate problems', 'Relationships between quantities', 'mixed', 'fourth and fifth graders', 'ZDM_Jacobs_2016_Responding', 'Victoria R. Jacobs, Susan B. Empson', '2016', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '193', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Jacobs_2016_Responding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37963, 'Students confuse the operations of dividing by a whole number and dividing by a unit fraction.', 'When determining 1/2 of 2/4, Gabby was unsure whether she was dividing by 2 or dividing by 1/2.', 'fraction', 'Division of fractions', 'Division by a whole number vs fraction', 'mixed', 'fourth and fifth graders', 'ZDM_Jacobs_2016_Responding', 'Victoria R. Jacobs, Susan B. Empson', '2016', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '195', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Jacobs_2016_Responding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37964, 'Students naively apply additive forms of reasoning to multiplicative or proportional systems, struggling to see that fractions with different internal additive differences can represent equivalent quantities.', 'Students looking at the sequence 1/2, 2/4, 3/6 find it difficult to accept they represent equivalent quantities because they focus on the different internal differences between the numerator and denominator (1, 2, and 3 respectively).', 'proportional', 'Rational numbers and proportion', 'Equivalent fractions', 'student', 'Grade 4–6', 'ZDM_Abrahamson_2014_Coordinating', 'Dor Abrahamson, Rosa G. Lee, Andrea G. Negrete, José F. Gutiérrez', '2014', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '81', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Abrahamson_2014_Coordinating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37965, 'When attempting to maintain a continuous proportional ratio between two cursors, students initially believe the physical vertical distance between their hands should remain constant as they move them.', 'A student states, ''I think it''s just, like, you stay the same distance apart,'' employing a fixed-interval strategy to maintain a 1:2 ratio.', 'proportional', 'Ratio', 'Continuous variation', 'student', 'Grade 4–6', 'ZDM_Abrahamson_2014_Coordinating', 'Dor Abrahamson, Rosa G. Lee, Andrea G. Negrete, José F. Gutiérrez', '2014', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '85, 86', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Abrahamson_2014_Coordinating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37966, 'A student interprets a contextualized proportional reasoning problem strictly as a routine mathematical calculation requiring exact decimal precision, rather than applying real-world constraints to round to a sensible whole number.', 'When adjusting an apple cake recipe for 10 people, a student calculates 6.666 eggs and refuses to round to 7 eggs, arguing that ''This comes from a recipe book! It must be exact.''', 'proportional', 'Proportional reasoning', 'Contextualized word problems', 'student', '15-year-old', 'ZDM_Gorgorio_2005_Cultural', 'Nuria Gorgorio, Nuria Planas', '2005', 'ZDM', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '66', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Gorgorio_2005_Cultural.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37967, 'Students interpret the term ''restriction'' literally in the context of an equation, believing it refers to all numbers that make the two sides unequal (i.e., non-solutions), rather than specific values where the algebraic expressions are mathematically undefined.', 'A student defines equivalent expressions as an equation where values of x exist that make both sides equal, suggests that x + 2 and x^2 could be equivalent, and considers all non-solution values to be ''restrictions''.', 'algebraic', 'Equivalence of expressions', 'Domain restrictions', 'mixed', '10th-grade', 'ZDM_Kieran_2013_Design', 'Carolyn Kieran, Andre Boileau, Denis Tanguay, Paul Drijvers', '2013', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '1051', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Kieran_2013_Design.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37968, 'Students incorrectly assume that if one side of an equation carries a domain restriction, that same restriction automatically applies to the distinct expression on the other side of the equation.', 'When equating a rational expression undefined at -2 with a polynomial, a student argues, ''If one side has a restriction at negative two, doesn''t the other side automatically have a restriction at negative two also?''', 'algebraic', 'Equations', 'Domain restrictions', 'mixed', '10th-grade', 'ZDM_Kieran_2013_Design', 'Carolyn Kieran, Andre Boileau, Denis Tanguay, Paul Drijvers', '2013', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '1050, 1051', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Kieran_2013_Design.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37969, 'Students fail to coordinate domain restrictions with the transitivity of equivalence, assuming equivalence transfers seamlessly across multiple expressions without tracking the union of their undefined values.', 'A student concludes that since expression 1 is equivalent to expressions 3 and 4, their equivalence relationship transfers without acknowledging that the rational expression 4 carries a domain restriction (x cannot equal -2) that the polynomial expression 1 does not.', 'algebraic', 'Equivalence of expressions', 'Transitivity and domain', 'mixed', '10th-grade', 'ZDM_Kieran_2013_Design', 'Carolyn Kieran, Andre Boileau, Denis Tanguay, Paul Drijvers', '2013', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '1051', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Kieran_2013_Design.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37970, 'Young students hold the initially productive belief that fair shares of a single whole must have the exact same size and shape, failing to recognize that equal parts need not be congruent.', 'When children encounter two identical rectangles—one split in half vertically and the other split in half diagonally—they may struggle to recognize the pieces are equal in size because they lack the same shape.', 'geometric', 'Equipartitioning single wholes', 'Congruence and equal area', 'student', 'grades two to five', 'ZDM_Confrey_2014_Learning', 'Jere Confrey, Alan P. Maloney, Andrew K. Corley', '2014', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '721, 722, 727', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Confrey_2014_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37971, 'When fairly sharing a collection, students can easily name the share using an absolute count of objects but struggle to name the share relationally compared to the whole collection.', 'When sharing 12 coins among 3 people, students can state the fair share is 4 coins, but struggle to describe the whole collection as three times as many as a single share.', 'fraction', 'Equipartitioning collections', 'Relational naming', 'student', 'grades two to five', 'ZDM_Confrey_2014_Learning', 'Jere Confrey, Alan P. Maloney, Andrew K. Corley', '2014', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '728', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Confrey_2014_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37972, 'When equipartitioning a continuous whole, students fail to coordinate all necessary criteria, often creating an incorrect number of shares, unequal-sized pieces, or failing to exhaust the entire whole.', 'When drawing shares for 4 persons on a rectangular cake, students might draw pieces that are the same size but do not cover the whole rectangle, or they might draw four unequal-sized pieces.', 'geometric', 'Equipartitioning single wholes', 'Criteria for equipartitioning', 'student', 'grades two to five', 'ZDM_Confrey_2014_Learning', 'Jere Confrey, Alan P. Maloney, Andrew K. Corley', '2014', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '729', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Confrey_2014_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37973, 'Students apply a minus sign located in front of a bracket only to the first number inside the bracket, rather than to the entire algebraic expression.', '3 - (5 + 2) = 3 - 5 + 2', 'algebraic', 'Transforming simple number terms with brackets', 'Distributive law', 'in_service', 'lower secondary', 'ZDM_Pankow_2016_Early', 'Lena Pankow, Gabriele Kaiser, Andreas Busse, Johannes Konig, Sigrid Blomeke, Jessica Hoth, Martina Dohrmann', '2016', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '61', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Pankow_2016_Early.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37974, 'Students separately add the numerators and the denominators when computing the sum of two fractions.', '2/7 + 3/7 = 5/14', 'fraction', 'Addition of fractions', 'Addition rules', 'in_service', 'lower secondary', 'ZDM_Pankow_2016_Early', 'Lena Pankow, Gabriele Kaiser, Andreas Busse, Johannes Konig, Sigrid Blomeke, Jessica Hoth, Martina Dohrmann', '2016', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '61', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Pankow_2016_Early.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37975, 'Students incorrectly associate the evaluation of cosine at zero degrees with the multiplication of zero, erroneously concluding that the value is zero.', 'cos 0 = 0', 'other', 'Trigonometry', 'Trigonometric values to specific angles', 'in_service', 'lower secondary', 'ZDM_Pankow_2016_Early', 'Lena Pankow, Gabriele Kaiser, Andreas Busse, Johannes Konig, Sigrid Blomeke, Jessica Hoth, Martina Dohrmann', '2016', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '61, 62', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Pankow_2016_Early.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37976, 'Students fail to account for the negative algebraic sign from the original equation when applying the pq-formula, or incorrectly omit the plus/minus sign in front of the root.', 'For x^2 + 8x - 4 = 0, a student calculates the expression under the root as 16 - 4 instead of 16 - (-4).', 'algebraic', 'Quadratic equations', 'pq-formula', 'in_service', 'lower secondary', 'ZDM_Pankow_2016_Early', 'Lena Pankow, Gabriele Kaiser, Andreas Busse, Johannes Konig, Sigrid Blomeke, Jessica Hoth, Martina Dohrmann', '2016', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '62', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Pankow_2016_Early.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37977, 'Students incorrectly adjust a factored polynomial function by adding or subtracting a constant to fit a specific point, failing to realize this shifts the entire curve and changes the previously established zeros.', 'Substituting x = 3 into a tentative function f(x) = x(x - 2)(x + 4) gives 21 instead of the desired 3, so the student writes f(x) = x(x - 2)(x + 4) - 18.', 'algebraic', 'Polynomial functions', 'Factored form and zeros', 'mixed', '11- and 12-year-olds', 'ZDM_Watson_2011_Qualities', 'Anne Watson, Helen Chick', '2011', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '288', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Watson_2011_Qualities.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37978, 'Students erroneously assume a direct relationship between the digits of a fraction''s denominator and its decimal representation.', 'Stating that one-eighth is ''0.8'' or ''nought point eight'', often following an assumed pattern based on knowing one-third is 0.33.', 'rational', 'Fraction-decimal equivalence', 'Reciprocals', 'mixed', '11- and 12-year-olds', 'ZDM_Watson_2011_Qualities', 'Anne Watson, Helen Chick', '2011', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '291, 292', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Watson_2011_Qualities.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37979, 'Pre-service teachers struggle to apply the measurement interpretation of division to fractions, incorrectly assuming that a larger fraction divisor cannot fit into a smaller fraction dividend.', 'When asked ''how many 1/2''s are in 1/3?'', many participants incorrectly gave an answer of ''none'' or ''0''.', 'fraction', 'Fraction division', 'Measurement interpretation of division', 'PST', 'middle school', 'ZDM_Li_2008_Knowledge', 'Yeping Li, Gerald Kulm', '2008', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '840', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Li_2008_Knowledge.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37980, 'Pre-service teachers rigidly adhere to the ''invert and multiply'' algorithm, failing to conceptually recognize or validate alternative correct methods for dividing fractions.', 'When asked to evaluate a student''s suggestion to compute fraction division by directly dividing numerators and denominators ((a/b) / (c/d) = (a/c)/(b/d)), only 2 out of 46 teachers recognized it as correct, while the majority insisted it must be ''flip and multiply'' or ''KFC''.', 'fraction', 'Fraction division', 'Alternative algorithms and conceptual justification', 'PST', 'middle school', 'ZDM_Li_2008_Knowledge', 'Yeping Li, Gerald Kulm', '2008', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '840, 841', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Li_2008_Knowledge.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37981, 'Pre-service teachers struggle to evaluate the relative size of division expressions conceptually without first calculating the exact procedural outcome.', 'Many participants were confused about which approach to use when asked to tell whether 9/11 / 2/3 is greater than or less than 9/11 / 3/4 without evaluating the expressions.', 'fraction', 'Fraction division', 'Relative size and estimation', 'PST', 'middle school', 'ZDM_Li_2008_Knowledge', 'Yeping Li, Gerald Kulm', '2008', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '840', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Li_2008_Knowledge.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37982, 'Individuals fail to aggregate distinct, equiprobable individual outcomes into broader events, leading them to mistakenly judge non-equivalent grouped events as equally probable.', 'When considering a game with three coin tosses, students judge the event of one boy winning all three coins as equally probable to the event of each boy winning at least one coin, failing to recognize the latter is composed of more individual outcomes.', 'probability', 'Sample space', 'Aggregation of outcomes', 'student', 'Grade 6', 'ZDM_Nunes_2014_Cognitive', 'Terezinha Nunes, Peter Bryant, Deborah Evans, Laura Gottardis, Maria-Emmanouela Terlektsi', '2014', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '438, 441', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Nunes_2014_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37983, 'Students create incorrect written representations of decimals by directly combining different units, using the decimal point to separate them without regard for formal decimal place value.', 'When adding 30 agorot to 7.70, a student writes ''7.100'' to represent 7 NIS and 100 agorot.', 'decimal', 'Decimal representation', 'Place value and units', 'student', 'fifth grade', 'ZDM_Broza_2015_Contingent', 'Orit Broza, Yifat Ben-David Kolikant', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '1099', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Broza_2015_Contingent.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37984, 'When subtracting a decimal from a whole number, students subtract the whole numbers but append the decimal part unchanged, failing to recognize the need to borrow or convert units.', 'When calculating 20 minus 7.70, a student answers 13.30, having subtracted 7 from 20 but failing to convert a whole unit to hundredths to subtract the 70 agorot.', 'decimal', 'Subtraction', 'Borrowing across the decimal point', 'student', 'fifth grade', 'ZDM_Broza_2015_Contingent', 'Orit Broza, Yifat Ben-David Kolikant', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '1101, 1102', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Broza_2015_Contingent.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37985, 'Students confuse the order of minuend and subtrahend during oral calculation steps, incorrectly stating they are subtracting the larger number from the smaller one.', 'A student states ''eight minus twenty'' when attempting to subtract 8 from 20.', 'whole_number', 'Subtraction', 'Minuend and subtrahend order', 'student', 'fifth grade', 'ZDM_Broza_2015_Contingent', 'Orit Broza, Yifat Ben-David Kolikant', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '1100', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Broza_2015_Contingent.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37986, 'Low-achieving students exhibit fragile memory, causing them to abandon or misapply previously constructed subtraction strategies and regress to earlier difficulties.', 'A student successfully uses a ''common denominator'' conversion strategy to solve 50 - 6.40, but fails to use it and cannot solve similar tasks in subsequent lessons.', 'other', 'Strategy retention', 'Fragile memory', 'student', 'fifth grade', 'ZDM_Broza_2015_Contingent', 'Orit Broza, Yifat Ben-David Kolikant', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '1097, 1098, 1103', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Broza_2015_Contingent.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37987, 'Students conceptualize the limit of an infinite process as retaining the properties of its individual terms, believing that a continually decreasing quantity results in an infinitesimal non-zero object.', 'Believing that the infinite decimal 0.999... is a limiting process which never quite reaches 1.', 'calculus', 'Limits', 'Infinity and infinitesimals', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Tall_2009_Dynamic', 'David O. Tall', '2009', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '483', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Tall_2009_Dynamic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37988, 'Students select a derivative graph based on its overall visual similarity to the original function''s graph rather than analyzing the relationship between the function and its changing slope.', 'When asked to identify the derivative of a graph, choosing a slope graph that looks similar to the original graph.', 'calculus', 'Derivatives', 'Graphical representations', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Tall_2009_Dynamic', 'David O. Tall', '2009', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '486', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Tall_2009_Dynamic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37989, 'Students experience confusion due to the conflicting use of Leibniz notation, where dy/dx is taught strictly as a limit rather than a fraction, but is later treated as a fraction to solve differential equations.', 'Being taught that the dx in an integral is not the same as the dx in dy/dx, yet later solving dy/dx = -x/y by cross-multiplying to get ydy = -xdx.', 'calculus', 'Differentials', 'Leibniz notation', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Tall_2009_Dynamic', 'David O. Tall', '2009', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '485', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Tall_2009_Dynamic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37990, 'Students incorrectly judge the likelihood of a sample statistic deviating from the population parameter by relying on its similarity to the population parameter, failing to take into account the effect of sample size on variance.', 'When given the hospital problem, students incorrectly state that a large hospital and a small hospital are equally likely to record days where more than 60% of babies born are boys, reasoning that both have the same underlying 50% chance for a baby boy.', 'probability', 'Law of large numbers', 'Sample size and variance', 'student', 'university', 'ZDM_Lem_2015_Intuitiveness', 'Stephanie Lem', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '783, 784', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Lem_2015_Intuitiveness.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37991, 'When faced with ratio and proportional problems involving nonintegral multiples, students return to addition-based methods rather than using a multiplicative strategy.', 'For example, to solve the ratio 6:4, Taiwanese students calculated that 6 - 4 = 2 and judged that the ratio equaled other ratios in which the former term was larger than the latter term by 2, such as 8:6.', 'ratio', 'Ratio and proportion', 'Nonintegral multiples', 'mixed', 'primary and secondary', 'ZDM_Lin_2018_Effects', 'Fou-Lai Lin, Ting-Ying Wang, Yu-Ping Chang', '2018', 'ZDM', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Lin_2018_Effects.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37992, 'Students lack mathematical literacy in real-world contexts, becoming distracted by superfluous information and failing to appropriately estimate or use evidence-based explanations.', 'Students may have encountered triangles with the angles of 30, 60, and 90 degrees or with the lengths of sides proportional to 1 : 2 : sqrt(3), but they had never paid attention to the approximate value of sqrt(3).', 'other', 'Mathematical literacy', 'Real-world modeling and estimation', 'mixed', 'primary and secondary', 'ZDM_Lin_2018_Effects', 'Fou-Lai Lin, Ting-Ying Wang, Yu-Ping Chang', '2018', 'ZDM', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Lin_2018_Effects.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37993, 'Preservice primary teachers struggle to understand advanced mathematical concepts such as using algebraic variables, and display a weak judgment of students'' understanding and competence.', 'Only 42% of the preservice teachers could understand n as an arbitrary number to compare the size of 2n and n + 2 with proper reasoning.', 'algebraic', 'Mathematical content knowledge and pedagogical content knowledge', 'Arbitrary numbers and diagnosing student difficulties', 'mixed', 'primary and secondary', 'ZDM_Lin_2018_Effects', 'Fou-Lai Lin, Ting-Ying Wang, Yu-Ping Chang', '2018', 'ZDM', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Lin_2018_Effects.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37994, 'Children interpret the equal sign as a command to compute the total rather than a relational symbol indicating mathematical equivalence.', 'When given an equation with operations on both sides such as 5 + 3 + 7 = 5 + __, children incorrectly solve it by adding all the numbers or by adding only the numbers before the equal sign.', 'algebraic', 'Mathematical equivalence', 'The equal sign', 'other', '', 'ZDM_RittleJohnson_2017_Promoting', 'Bethany Rittle-Johnson, Abbey M. Loehr, Kelley Durkin', '2017', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_RittleJohnson_2017_Promoting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37995, 'Students hold intuitive, finitist views about infinity and limits, often treating limits procedurally or dynamically rather than formally.', 'Believing that a limit may be approached but not reached, or that it is a bound that cannot be crossed.', 'calculus', 'Limits', 'Infinity', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Thomas_2015_Inhibiting', 'Michael O. J. Thomas', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '868, 869', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Thomas_2015_Inhibiting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37996, 'Students intuitively generalize rules and operations from natural numbers to rational numbers, leading to errors in anticipation of numerical operation outcomes.', 'Assuming multiplication yields larger numbers and division smaller ones, causing difficulty with tasks like 8 x _ = 3 and 2 / _ = 5.', 'rational', 'Rational number operations', 'Multiplication and division', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Thomas_2015_Inhibiting', 'Michael O. J. Thomas', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '870, 871', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Thomas_2015_Inhibiting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37997, 'Students'' intuitive spatial reasoning about the value or position of a function graph interferes with their ability to analyze the function''s changing gradient.', 'Struggling to reason about gradient when the change in gradient and the change in the function''s value are incongruent, such as when the gradient is increasing but the function value is decreasing.', 'calculus', 'Derivatives', 'Gradients and graphs', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Thomas_2015_Inhibiting', 'Michael O. J. Thomas', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '874', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Thomas_2015_Inhibiting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37998, 'When estimating and comparing the number of items in visual arrays, individuals erroneously base their judgments on salient continuous visual cues—such as the total area (convex hull) or the average size of the items—rather than the actual numerosity.', 'Participants performed significantly below chance on incongruent trials with larger set sizes, demonstrating that they systematically chose the array with the larger convex hull even when it contained fewer dots.', 'whole_number', 'Numerosity comparison', 'Approximate Number System (ANS)', 'student', '7-9 years', 'ZDM_Clayton_2015_Inhibition', 'Sarah Clayton, Camilla Gilmore', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '762, 766, 767', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Clayton_2015_Inhibition.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(37999, 'Students inappropriately employ inefficient additive strategies to interpret a pie chart, repeatedly estimating and summing sectors instead of capitalizing on the chart''s nature as a proportional representation.', 'A student visually measured the ''games'' sector, mentally overlaid it around the pie chart to count by tens, and added the segments to reach the total, rather than recognizing a segment as a quarter and multiplying by four.', 'proportional', 'Data displays', 'Pie charts and proportional reasoning', 'student', 'grade 7', 'ZDM_Diezmann_2009_Role', 'Carmel Mary Diezmann, Tom Lowrie', '2009', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '656, 657, 658', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Diezmann_2009_Role.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38000, 'Students struggle to recognize a sector as a specific proportion of a circle, such as a quarter, if it is presented in an atypical orientation rather than with standard horizontal and vertical radii.', 'A student found the task difficult because the ''clothes'' sector was not oriented as an ''outright quarter'' with vertical and horizontal boundary lines.', 'geometric', 'Circles and sectors', 'Orientation', 'student', 'grade 7', 'ZDM_Diezmann_2009_Role', 'Carmel Mary Diezmann, Tom Lowrie', '2009', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '659', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Diezmann_2009_Role.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38001, 'Students lack understanding of the conventions of pie charts as proportional graphics, believing a protractor is always necessary or that data must be presented in a bar graph to be understandable.', 'A student stated it was hard to read the pie graph without a protractor and suggested it would be easier if the data were put into a ''standard graph'' like a bar graph with a scale beside it.', 'statistics', 'Data displays', 'Pie chart conventions', 'student', 'grade 7', 'ZDM_Diezmann_2009_Role', 'Carmel Mary Diezmann, Tom Lowrie', '2009', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '659', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Diezmann_2009_Role.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38002, 'Students may possess the necessary prior knowledge, such as identifying a sector as a quarter and knowing its monetary value, but fail to apply this proportional knowledge to solve for the whole, resorting to guessing instead.', 'A student correctly identified the ''clothes'' sector as a quarter worth $30, but still guessed the total as $150 and did not use the quarter to check her guess.', 'proportional', 'Part-whole relationships', 'Application of prior knowledge', 'student', 'grade 7', 'ZDM_Diezmann_2009_Role', 'Carmel Mary Diezmann, Tom Lowrie', '2009', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '657', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Diezmann_2009_Role.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38003, 'Students rely on a direct translation strategy, mistakenly assuming that specific keywords always represent the same mathematical operations regardless of the problem''s semantic context.', 'In the problem ''Peter has three discs. He has two discs more than Joe has. How many discs does Joe have?'', students mistakenly use addition instead of subtraction because of the keyword ''more''.', 'other', 'Arithmetic word problems', 'Inconsistent language', 'student', 'third and sixth grades', 'ZDM_Mevarech_2010_Effects', 'Zemira R. Mevarech, Shirlei Terkieltaub, Tova Vinberger, Vered Nevet', '2010', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '195, 196', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Mevarech_2010_Effects.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38004, 'Students mistakenly read algebraic expressions and equations linearly from left to right like words in a sentence, failing to recognize their two-dimensional syntactic organization governed by the order of operations.', 'For an expression such as 7(x - 3)(x - 2), students apply the ''little shower'' diagram linearly and multiply the 7 by both binomial factors, failing to recognize there are three distinct factors and two operations.', 'algebraic', 'Algebraic expressions', 'Syntactic structure', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Duval_2014_Commentary', 'Raymond Duval', '2014', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '163, 164', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Duval_2014_Commentary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38005, 'There is a widespread, misleading assumption that geometric understanding naturally grows from concrete perceptual experiences, masking the fact that true mathematical visualization requires dimensional deconstruction which often contradicts immediate perception.', 'Students rely entirely on the perceptual recognition of whole 2D or 3D shapes without breaking them down into configurations of lower-dimensional figural units (like intersecting 1D lines or 0D points) that dictate their geometric properties.', 'geometric', 'Visualization', 'Dimensional deconstruction', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Duval_2014_Commentary', 'Raymond Duval', '2014', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '165, 166, 167', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Duval_2014_Commentary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38006, 'Students interpret the visual representation of partitioned circles as whole, distinct shapes to be counted, applying natural number addition instead of viewing them as fractional parts of a whole.', 'When the teacher asks for the sum of a shaded third of a circle and a shaded quarter of a circle, a student says ''one plus one'' equals ''two''.', 'fraction', 'Fractions', 'Addition of fractions', 'student', 'kindergarten and Year 4', 'ZDM_Schutte_2014_Language-related', 'Marcus Schutte', '2014', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '929, 930', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Schutte_2014_Language-related.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38007, 'Children frame the task of creating a ''row'' or ''order'' using everyday spatial orientations of ''in front'' and ''behind'' rather than a combinatorial framework of generating different sequential permutations.', 'When asked to find different orders for toy animals to walk across a platform, the children focus on spatial positioning without connecting it to finding all possible sequential combinations.', 'combinatorial', 'Permutations', 'Ordering', 'student', 'kindergarten and Year 4', 'ZDM_Schutte_2014_Language-related', 'Marcus Schutte', '2014', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '935', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Schutte_2014_Language-related.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38008, 'Students struggle to accept and reason within the ''absurd world'' of a proof by contradiction, as assuming a false hypothesis makes them question whether standard mathematical rules still apply.', 'Maria doubts whether 0/b = 0 is valid in an ''absurd world'' where ab = 0 but a and b are non-zero, feeling she has lost control over what is true.', 'algebraic', 'Indirect proof', 'Theory of reference', 'student', 'grades 11, 13, and university', 'ZDM_Antonini_2008_Indirect', 'Samuele Antonini, Maria Alessandra Mariotti', '2008', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '406', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Antonini_2008_Indirect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38009, 'Students experience a cognitive logical gap and feel they must make an act of faith to accept that proving the secondary statement successfully validates the principal statement.', 'Fabio understands and accepts the proof that ''if n is odd then n^2 is odd'', but feels a logical jump and remains unsatisfied when linking it back to prove ''if n^2 is even then n is even''.', 'other', 'Indirect proof', 'Meta-theorem', 'student', 'grades 11, 13, and university', 'ZDM_Antonini_2008_Indirect', 'Samuele Antonini, Maria Alessandra Mariotti', '2008', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '407, 408', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Antonini_2008_Indirect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38010, 'During indirect argumentation, instead of reaching a contradiction, students may inappropriately modify or deform a geometric figure to maintain the validity of standard theorems.', 'When assuming the angle between two bisectors is a right angle, Paolo and Riccardo deform the triangle into a quadrilateral to preserve the 180-degree triangle angle sum, rather than rejecting the premise.', 'geometric', 'Indirect argumentation', 'Reference theory application', 'student', 'grades 11, 13, and university', 'ZDM_Antonini_2008_Indirect', 'Samuele Antonini, Maria Alessandra Mariotti', '2008', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '409, 410', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Antonini_2008_Indirect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38011, 'Students assume that the point of origin on the number line is 1 rather than 0 because zero and negative integers do not reflect quantities in the material world.', '', 'integer', 'Number line', 'Origin and zero', 'student', 'fourth and fifth grade', 'ZDM_Saxe_2013_Towards', 'Geoffrey B. Saxe, Ronli Diakow, Maryl Gearhart', '2013', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '344', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Saxe_2013_Towards.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38012, 'Students incorrectly believe that negative integers with a greater absolute value have a greater numerical value.', 'Assuming that -5 is greater than -4.', 'integer', 'Negative integers', 'Magnitude and order', 'student', 'fourth and fifth grade', 'ZDM_Saxe_2013_Towards', 'Geoffrey B. Saxe, Ronli Diakow, Maryl Gearhart', '2013', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '344', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Saxe_2013_Towards.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38013, 'Students treat the numerals in fraction representations as whole numbers, applying whole-number schemes instead of conceptualizing the multiplicative relations between quantities.', '', 'fraction', 'Fractions', 'Notation and algorithms', 'student', 'fourth and fifth grade', 'ZDM_Saxe_2013_Towards', 'Geoffrey B. Saxe, Ronli Diakow, Maryl Gearhart', '2013', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '344', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Saxe_2013_Towards.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38014, 'Teachers initially hold the restrictive conception that calculator use undermines mathematical rigor, hinders the development of calculation skills, and masks underlying concepts for operations like percentages.', 'Teachers opposed students using the percent key on the calculator for a discount problem, believing students must explicitly write out the multiplication step to mathematically understand the concept.', 'percent', 'Percentage computation', 'Calculator operations', 'in_service', 'grades 5-6', 'ZDM_Ponte_2012_Teacher', 'João Pedro da Ponte, Célia Mercê', '', '', 'productive', 'primary', '7, 8, 12, 13', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Ponte_2012_Teacher.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38015, 'A student erroneously calculates a percent discount by subtracting the percentage value as a whole number instead of computing the proportional amount.', 'To find a 40% discount on 89€, the student Francisco writes ''89 - 40% = 49€'', treating the percentage as an absolute currency value.', 'percent', 'Discounts', '', 'in_service', 'grades 5-6', 'ZDM_Ponte_2012_Teacher', 'João Pedro da Ponte, Célia Mercê', '', '', 'productive', 'secondary', '7', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Ponte_2012_Teacher.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38016, 'Students experience semiotic conflicts when attempting to apply combinatorial definitions learned through a selection model to new situations requiring a partition model.', 'Struggling to correctly determine the number of ways to distribute four differently colored cars to three siblings.', 'combinatorial', 'Combinatorial problems', 'Selection vs. partition models', 'student', 'ultimo curso de la licenciatura de matematicas, Bachillerato (16-17 anos)', 'ZDM_Godino_2009_Un', 'Juan D. Godino, Carmen Batanero, Vicenc Font', '2009', 'ZDM. The International Journal on Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Godino_2009_Un.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38017, 'Students lack sufficient foundational understanding of prerequisite concepts, such as functions and average rate of change, hindering their ability to grasp the semiotically complex definition of the derivative.', '', 'calculus', 'Derivative', 'Limit of average rates of change', 'student', 'ultimo curso de la licenciatura de matematicas, Bachillerato (16-17 anos)', 'ZDM_Godino_2009_Un', 'Juan D. Godino, Carmen Batanero, Vicenc Font', '2009', 'ZDM. The International Journal on Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Godino_2009_Un.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38018, 'When transposing a formula, students may apply operations in an incorrect sequence, such as subtracting a term from both sides before correctly isolating the variable using division or multiplication.', 'A student who thinks that the first step to transpose an equation is to subtract a variable from both sides will see on the CAS that it does not give them the desired isolated variable.', 'algebraic', 'Formula transposition', 'Sequence of operations', 'student', 'secondary school', 'ZDM_Bowers_1997_Opportunities', 'David Bowers', '1997', 'ZDM', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Bowers_1997_Opportunities.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38019, 'Students become confused and may doubt their own correct work when the algebraic output of a computer algebra system looks structurally different from their expected handwritten answer, failing to recognize equivalence.', 'Pupils can become confused when the algebraic output of a CAS is unexpected, as their paper answers appear different from the computer''s.', 'algebraic', 'Algebraic expressions', 'Equivalence', 'student', 'secondary school', 'ZDM_Bowers_1997_Opportunities', 'David Bowers', '1997', 'ZDM', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Bowers_1997_Opportunities.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38020, 'Due to the limited display precision of standard calculators, students struggle to distinguish between rational numbers with long recurring decimal cycles and irrational numbers, sometimes incorrectly assuming long decimals are irrational.', 'Students might not realize that a fraction has a long recurring cycle and might assume it is irrational because they cannot see the pattern on a standard 10-digit calculator.', 'rational', 'Decimals and fractions', 'Rational vs. irrational numbers', 'student', 'secondary school', 'ZDM_Bowers_1997_Opportunities', 'David Bowers', '1997', 'ZDM', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Bowers_1997_Opportunities.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38021, 'Students interpret a graph representing a function''s changing area as a photographic or literal geometric image of the situation rather than an abstract mapping of variables.', 'When asked to match an area function to an expanding triangle, students chose a graph that visually resembled the shape of the triangle and reasoned that ''The area of the graph is just like the area F(x)''.', 'calculus', 'Functions and graphs', 'Graph interpretation', 'student', '10th grade students of age 15-16', 'ZDM_Hoffkamp_2011_Use', 'Andrea Hoffkamp', '2011', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '361, 362', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Hoffkamp_2011_Use.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38022, 'Students struggle to differentiate between the accumulation of a quantity (stock) and its rate of change, conflating the two when describing the shape of a graph.', 'A student looking at the area function of a triangle insists the graph ''definitely increases'' and denies any decrease, failing to recognize that while the total area (stock) increases, the rate at which area is added (change) decreases.', 'calculus', 'Functions and calculus', 'Stock and change', 'student', '10th grade students of age 15-16', 'ZDM_Hoffkamp_2011_Use', 'Andrea Hoffkamp', '2011', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '367', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Hoffkamp_2011_Use.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38023, 'Students believe that the mathematical concept of ''slope'' is strictly limited to straight lines and cannot be applied to curves or non-linear functions.', 'A student asserts, ''the function does not have a slope, because, if it would have one, it would be a line'', when trying to describe the curvature of an area graph.', 'calculus', 'Rate of change', 'Slope of non-linear functions', 'student', '10th grade students of age 15-16', 'ZDM_Hoffkamp_2011_Use', 'Andrea Hoffkamp', '2011', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'primary', '368', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Hoffkamp_2011_Use.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38024, 'Students perceive variables as changing in discrete jumps rather than continuously, failing to recognize that real numbers are continuous between any two visible or sampled values.', 'A student claims the area of a rectangle ''is always the same'' between endpoints or says ''the value 25 is never reached'', because the visible discrete decimal readout on the software jumps from 25.085 to another value without displaying exactly 25.', 'calculus', 'Continuity', 'Continuous vs. discrete change', 'student', '10th grade students of age 15-16', 'ZDM_Hoffkamp_2011_Use', 'Andrea Hoffkamp', '2011', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'primary', '368, 369', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Hoffkamp_2011_Use.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38025, 'Students lack the intuition that they must continuously refine decimal approximations to find an exact maximum value, inappropriately settling for integer approximations.', 'When searching for the maximum area of a rectangle, students test integer x-values, find an area of 36 at P(6,6), check an arbitrary decimal P(5.2,7.1), and incorrectly conclude the maximum is 36 instead of refining their search to find the true maximum of 37.5 at P(5,7.5).', 'calculus', 'Optimization', 'Approximation', 'student', '10th grade students of age 15-16', 'ZDM_Hoffkamp_2011_Use', 'Andrea Hoffkamp', '2011', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '369', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Hoffkamp_2011_Use.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38026, 'Students inappropriately rely on superficial cue words like ''more than'' to automatically choose addition without understanding the underlying relational structure of the word problem.', '', 'whole_number', 'Word problems', 'Comparison problems', 'in_service', 'primary school', 'ZDM_Kaur_2018_Why', 'Berinderjeet Kaur', '2018', 'ZDM', 'deficit', 'passing', '', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Kaur_2018_Why.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38027, 'When solving complex relational word problems, students make transformation errors (such as omitting values of change or failing to integrate them into a coherent model) and translation errors (such as incorrectly translating models into number sentences based on random cues).', '', 'whole_number', 'Word problems', 'Multi-step and before-after problems', 'in_service', 'primary school', 'ZDM_Kaur_2018_Why', 'Berinderjeet Kaur', '2018', 'ZDM', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Kaur_2018_Why.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38028, 'Students treat linear functions purely as recursive sequences by focusing only on the difference between successive output values, failing to construct a direct input-output relationship.', 'When predicting seats for 100 tables, students using the ''keep adding two'' recursive method struggled and guessed 200 or 101, unable to directly compute the output from the input.', 'algebraic', 'Linear functions', 'Generalization and closed-form expressions', 'student', 'third grade', 'ZDM_Carraher_2008_Early', 'David W. Carraher, Mara V. Martinez, Analucia D. Schliemann', '2008', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'primary', '8, 11, 14, 15, 18', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Carraher_2008_Early.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38029, 'When trying to find a rule relating input to output, students focus on an additive difference between the two columns instead of recognizing a multiplicative relationship.', 'A student noticed that for 1 table you add 3 to get 4, and for 2 tables you add 4 to get 6, forming an auxiliary sequence of additive increments instead of a multiplier.', 'algebraic', 'Linear functions', 'Input-output relations', 'student', 'third grade', 'ZDM_Carraher_2008_Early', 'David W. Carraher, Mara V. Martinez, Analucia D. Schliemann', '2008', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '14, 15', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Carraher_2008_Early.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38030, 'Students create numerically correct general rules that are inconsistent with the semantic referents of the problem, performing operations on values with mismatched units.', 'A student correctly predicted seats by adding 3 tables + 3 tables + 2 people = 8 people, mixing the units of tables and people in the arithmetic.', 'algebraic', 'Mathematical modeling', 'Referent transformation and units', 'student', 'third grade', 'ZDM_Carraher_2008_Early', 'David W. Carraher, Mara V. Martinez, Analucia D. Schliemann', '2008', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '15, 16', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Carraher_2008_Early.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38031, 'A student incorrectly applies the distributive law by failing to distribute a negative sign to all terms inside a parenthesis, caused by mentally partitioning the equation into separate chunks at the subtraction sign.', 'When solving 0.6(x-5) - 2/3(11-2x) = 1+x, the student dictates the term - 2/3 * 2x instead of + 2/3 * 2x, incorrectly subtracting the second part from the first without applying the negative sign to the negative 2x.', 'algebraic', 'Equation solving', 'Distributive law and negative signs', 'student', 'grammar school level, 11th grade', 'ZDM_Kaune_2006_Reflection', 'Christa Kaune', '2006', 'ZDM', 'deficit', 'primary', '351, 352', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Kaune_2006_Reflection.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38032, 'Students carelessly manipulate algebraic terms to force a calculation to proceed smoothly, ignoring underlying basic rules such as the domain of a root function.', 'When given the expression sqrt(sqrt(18)-4.5) * sqrt(sqrt(18)+4.5), a fictitious student calculates it to sqrt(-2.25) and concludes the answer is -1.5, ignoring that sqrt(18)-4.5 is negative and undefined under a real square root.', 'algebraic', 'Simplification of terms', 'Domain of root functions', 'student', 'grammar school level, 11th grade', 'ZDM_Kaune_2006_Reflection', 'Christa Kaune', '2006', 'ZDM', 'deficit', 'primary', '353, 354', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Kaune_2006_Reflection.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38033, 'A student believes that fundamental mathematical terms (like volume) are pre-existing, tangible entities that cannot be defined or deduced, rather than abstract conceptual constructs.', 'A student argues that ''volume'' is a fundamental term that ''is like a Lego brick, it does exist'' and therefore cannot be a definition, conflating the general concept of volume with the specific definable formula for the volume of a rotational body.', 'other', 'Mathematical epistemology', 'Definitions and fundamental terms', 'student', 'grammar school level, 11th grade', 'ZDM_Kaune_2006_Reflection', 'Christa Kaune', '2006', 'ZDM', 'deficit', 'secondary', '358', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Kaune_2006_Reflection.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38034, 'Students mistakenly use additive operations instead of multiplicative ratios when comparing the measures of two objects, failing to interpret measurement as a ratio between a unit quantity and the object being measured.', 'A student who correctly claimed a larger box could contain 900 unit cubes incorrectly stated that the larger box was 899 times larger than the small unit cube.', 'measurement', 'Volume and area comparison', 'Multiplicative versus additive reasoning', 'student', 'grades 2 through 5', 'ZDM_Barrett_2011_Children''s', 'Jeffrey E. Barrett, Craig Cullen, Julie Sarama, Douglas H. Clements, David Klanderman, Amanda L. Miller, Chepina Rumsey', '2011', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '645, 648', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Barrett_2011_Children''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38035, 'Students struggle to abstract and identify length units consistently across different representations, relying on counting visually perceptible units rather than utilizing numerical labels and intervals.', 'When given a ruler with a portion of the numbers and tick marks obscured by a ribbon, some students struggled to deduce the length because they could not directly count the visually perceptible units.', 'measurement', 'Length', 'Unit iteration and rulers', 'student', 'grades 2 through 5', 'ZDM_Barrett_2011_Children''s', 'Jeffrey E. Barrett, Craig Cullen, Julie Sarama, Douglas H. Clements, David Klanderman, Amanda L. Miller, Chepina Rumsey', '2011', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '641', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Barrett_2011_Children''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38036, 'When obtaining inconsistent measurements using different tools, students blame the physical tools rather than recognizing a flaw in their own measurement scheme.', 'Two students attributed the inconsistency of their measures with different tools to a poor fit between these tools instead of changing their own scheme.', 'measurement', 'Length', 'Measurement consistency', 'student', 'grades 2 through 5', 'ZDM_Barrett_2011_Children''s', 'Jeffrey E. Barrett, Craig Cullen, Julie Sarama, Douglas H. Clements, David Klanderman, Amanda L. Miller, Chepina Rumsey', '2011', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '641', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Barrett_2011_Children''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38037, 'Students interpret the mathematical concept of limit as a physical boundary, restriction, or maximum amount that cannot be crossed, based on their everyday language intuition of the word.', 'When asked what they think of when they hear ''limit,'' students responded with phrases like ''How high/fast/far something can go'' or ''Limits always stop people from fulfilling their dreams.''', 'calculus', 'Limit of a sequence', 'Limit definition', 'PST', 'undergraduate students who are training to become elementary teachers', 'ZDM_Keene_2014_Sequence', 'Karen Allen Keene, William Hall, Alina Duca', '2014', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '565, 566', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Keene_2014_Sequence.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38038, 'Students apply an asymptotic intuition to limit scenarios, incorrectly assuming that a limit is always a value that a sequence or function approaches but can never actually reach.', 'A student states, ''The graph is approaching the line x = 2 but is never reached'' when describing a mathematical limit.', 'calculus', 'Limit of a sequence', 'Asymptotic behavior', 'PST', 'undergraduate students who are training to become elementary teachers', 'ZDM_Keene_2014_Sequence', 'Karen Allen Keene, William Hall, Alina Duca', '2014', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '566, 570', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Keene_2014_Sequence.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38039, 'Students mechanistically rely on standard algebraic procedures like ''plugging in'' values to find a limit, avoiding conceptual engagement with infinite processes.', 'When asked how to solve a limit problem, a student suggests, ''Plug 2 in for x and if that doesn''t work, try to simplify.''', 'calculus', 'Limit evaluation', 'Procedural vs conceptual understanding', 'PST', 'undergraduate students who are training to become elementary teachers', 'ZDM_Keene_2014_Sequence', 'Karen Allen Keene, William Hall, Alina Duca', '2014', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '566', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Keene_2014_Sequence.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38040, 'Teachers hold an exclusive, partitional concept image of quadrilaterals rather than an inclusive, hierarchical one, preventing them from recognizing subset relationships among shapes.', 'When asked if it was possible for a parallelogram to have congruent diagonals, teachers answered incorrectly because their concept image of a parallelogram excluded rectangles as a special type.', 'geometric', 'Quadrilaterals', 'Classification and definitions', 'in_service', 'elementary and middle school', 'ZDM_Ng_2012_Using', 'Dicky Ng', '2012', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '406, 409', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Ng_2012_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38041, 'Students inappropriately persist in using visual diagrams to solve complex division of fraction problems, which constrains their mathematical thinking because the diagrams become too complicated to draw and interpret.', 'Students attempt to draw a diagram to visualize 1/2 / 1/3 but are unable to do so effectively, instead of shifting to proportional reasoning.', 'fraction', 'Division of fractions', 'Algorithm justification', 'mixed', 'grades 1-6', 'ZDM_Huang_2016_Implementing', 'Rongjin Huang, Zikun Gong, Xue Han', '2016', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '432, 434, 435', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Huang_2016_Implementing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38042, 'Students assume that arithmetic operations behave uniformly, believing that addition always results in a larger number and subtraction always results in a smaller number, which interferes with learning negative numbers.', '', 'integer', 'Negative numbers', 'Addition and subtraction', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Tall_2007_Developing', 'David O. Tall', '2007', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '150', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Tall_2007_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38043, 'Students inappropriately treat algebraic symbols as physical objects or labels for things rather than variables, which causes failure when interpreting expressions involving subtraction.', 'Treating 2a + 3b + 4a as 2 apples, 3 bananas, and 4 apples, which allows simplification to 6a + 3b, but fails to give meaning to 6a - 3b since one cannot have 6 apples minus 3 bananas.', 'algebraic', 'Algebraic expressions', 'Variables and unlike terms', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Tall_2007_Developing', 'David O. Tall', '2007', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '152', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Tall_2007_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38044, 'Treating an equation conceptually as a physical balance causes difficulties when extending algebraic operations to include negative terms.', '', 'algebraic', 'Equations', 'Negative terms', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Tall_2007_Developing', 'David O. Tall', '2007', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '152', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Tall_2007_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38045, 'Students routinize procedures without conceptual flexibility, leading them to rely on complex, familiar algorithms even when a problem''s format allows for a simpler deductive solution.', 'Given the equation (x - 3)(x - 2) = 0, students attempt to multiply out the brackets and use the quadratic formula rather than recognizing that if one bracket is 0, the product is 0.', 'algebraic', 'Quadratic equations', 'Factored form', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Tall_2007_Developing', 'David O. Tall', '2007', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '151', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Tall_2007_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38046, 'Children incorrectly apply an additive model to solve multiplicative missing-value word problems, computing constant differences instead of multiplicative ratios.', 'For the problem ''A car travels 6 miles in 2 min. How far will it travel in 4 min?'', children incorrectly answer 8 miles instead of 12 miles by reasoning that the difference between 2 and 4 is 2, so 6 + 2 = 8.', 'proportional', 'Missing-value word problems', 'Multiplicative reasoning', 'student', 'fifth and sixth grade', 'ZDM_Degrande_2018_Open', 'Tine Degrande, Jo Van Hoof, Lieven Verschaffel, Wim Van Dooren', '2018', 'ZDM', 'mixed', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Degrande_2018_Open.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38047, 'Children erroneously apply a multiplicative model to solve missing-value word problems that have an additive underlying mathematical model.', 'When given the problem: ''Ellen and Kim run equally fast but Ellen started later. When Ellen has run 4 laps, Kim has run 8 laps. When Ellen has run 12 laps, how many has Kim run?'', children erroneously model it multiplicatively as ''4 x 3 = 12, so 8 x 3 = 24 laps''.', 'proportional', 'Missing-value word problems', 'Additive reasoning', 'student', 'fifth and sixth grade', 'ZDM_Degrande_2018_Open', 'Tine Degrande, Jo Van Hoof, Lieven Verschaffel, Wim Van Dooren', '2018', 'ZDM', 'mixed', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Degrande_2018_Open.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38048, 'Young children struggle with multiplicative reasoning in proportional tasks featuring discrete quantities because they overextend numerical counting routines to the discrete units.', '', 'ratio', 'Multiplicative reasoning', 'Discrete vs. continuous quantities', 'student', 'fifth and sixth grade', 'ZDM_Degrande_2018_Open', 'Tine Degrande, Jo Van Hoof, Lieven Verschaffel, Wim Van Dooren', '2018', 'ZDM', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Degrande_2018_Open.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38049, 'Students regress to an elementary counting-all action when visually decomposing tens into ones for subtraction with regrouping, which prevents them from transitioning to higher-level numerical processing.', 'When solving 41 - 16, a student decomposes a tens stick into 10 circles, but then counts all 11 circles starting from 1 to obtain the total instead of using a counting-on strategy.', 'whole_number', 'Subtraction with regrouping', 'Place value splitting', 'student', 'second- and third-grade', 'ZDM_Rivera_2014_From', 'F. D. Rivera', '2014', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '65, 66', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Rivera_2014_From.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38050, 'Students inappropriately apply linear proportional reasoning to the area of a circle, assuming that doubling the radius also doubles the area.', 'Students struggled with evaluating ''what if'' statements based on non-proportional functions, such as incorrectly agreeing that ''if one doubles the radius of a cylinder then diameter and surface area of the circle is doubled as well''.', 'geometric', 'Area', 'Scaling and proportions', 'student', '9th classes of secondary level', 'ZDM_Kittel_2005_Computer', 'Andreas Kittel, Astrid Beckmann, Volker Hole, Silke Ladel', '2005', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '382', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Kittel_2005_Computer.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38051, 'Students struggle with the financial notion of interest, improperly calculating the interest amount by directly multiplying a monthly interest rate by the number of installments.', 'In a task calculating the cost of a mountain bike paid in installments, students incorrectly quoted the interest rate as 0.7% x 6 or 0.7% x 12 instead of applying the rate correctly.', 'percent', 'Financial mathematics', 'Interest and installments', 'student', '9th classes of secondary level', 'ZDM_Kittel_2005_Computer', 'Andreas Kittel, Astrid Beckmann, Volker Hole, Silke Ladel', '2005', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '382, 383', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Kittel_2005_Computer.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38052, 'Students lack the specific vocabulary and conceptual understanding to distinguish between the lateral surface area (nappe) and the total surface area of a 3D figure.', 'When tasked with finding the radius that minimizes sheet steel for a tin, students were uncertain whether to examine the nappe or the surface, with one guessing, ''Now, how much sheet steel is needed? That''s the nappe.''', 'measurement', 'Surface area of a cylinder', 'Lateral vs total area', 'student', '9th classes of secondary level', 'ZDM_Kittel_2005_Computer', 'Andreas Kittel, Astrid Beckmann, Volker Hole, Silke Ladel', '2005', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '387', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Kittel_2005_Computer.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38053, 'Students do not pay sufficient attention to the algebraic features of equations, sometimes confusing the equation for a line with the equation for a parabola.', 'Some students mistook the linear equation y = 4x + 2 as an upwards opened parabola.', 'algebraic', 'Functions and graphs', 'Linear vs quadratic equations', 'student', '9th classes of secondary level', 'ZDM_Kittel_2005_Computer', 'Andreas Kittel, Astrid Beckmann, Volker Hole, Silke Ladel', '2005', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '387', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Kittel_2005_Computer.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38054, 'Students blindly follow and misapply standard multiplication algorithms taught by teachers without understanding place value alignment.', 'When multiplying 245 by 37, a student multiplies 3 x 5 to get 15 but incorrectly places the 5 underneath the second 1 of 1715 because they are just blindly following steps modeled by the teacher.', 'whole_number', 'Multiplication algorithm', 'Place value alignment', 'student', 'primary school', 'ZDM_Sembiring_2008_Reforming', 'Robert K. Sembiring, Sutarto Hadi, Maarten Dolk', '2008', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '929, 930', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Sembiring_2008_Reforming.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38055, 'Students attempt to add fractions by incorrectly adding the numerators together and the denominators together.', 'Students add the fractions 1/4 and 2/5 simply by adding the numerators and adding the denominators, getting the wrong answer of 3/9.', 'fraction', 'Addition of fractions', 'Unlike denominators', 'student', 'primary school', 'ZDM_Sembiring_2008_Reforming', 'Robert K. Sembiring, Sutarto Hadi, Maarten Dolk', '2008', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '932', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Sembiring_2008_Reforming.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38056, 'Students evaluate decimal numbers as if they were whole numbers, leading them to believe that longer decimals represent larger values.', 'Students consider 0.8 to be smaller than 0.14 because they treat the decimal digits as whole numbers where 14 is greater than 8.', 'decimal', 'Decimal magnitude', 'Comparing decimals', 'student', 'primary school', 'ZDM_Sembiring_2008_Reforming', 'Robert K. Sembiring, Sutarto Hadi, Maarten Dolk', '2008', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '932', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Sembiring_2008_Reforming.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38057, 'Students reject a mathematically valid but unfamiliar procedure for solving a problem because they strictly adhere to the standard algorithm, demonstrating a rule-oriented attitude rather than conceptual flexibility.', 'Students judge ''Yoshiko''s method'' for dividing fractions (dividing numerators and denominators, e.g., 8/15 / 2/5 = (8/2)/(15/5) = 4/3) as an incorrect procedure simply because it differs from the standard algorithm they know.', 'fraction', 'Division of fractions', 'Alternative algorithms', 'mixed', 'elementary and lower secondary school', 'ZDM_Hino_2007_Toward', 'Keiko Hino', '2007', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '506', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Hino_2007_Toward.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38058, 'Students make systematic errors in the counting sequence, particularly around decade transitions, by incorrectly overgeneralizing linguistic patterns.', 'A student might count ''18...19...10-teen...11-teen'' or ''8...9...10...10-one...10-two''.', 'whole_number', 'Counting', 'Decade transitions and teen numbers', 'student', 'Kindergarten through Grade 3', 'ZDM_Ginsburg_2016_Research-inspired', 'Herbert P. Ginsburg, Young-Sun Lee, Sandra Pappas', '2016', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '1008', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Ginsburg_2016_Research-inspired.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38059, 'Students incorrectly translate spoken numbers into written numerals by writing the digits exactly as they sound, ignoring place value rules.', 'Writing ''203'' for ''twenty-three''.', 'whole_number', 'Writing numbers', 'Place value', 'student', 'Kindergarten through Grade 3', 'ZDM_Ginsburg_2016_Research-inspired', 'Herbert P. Ginsburg, Young-Sun Lee, Sandra Pappas', '2016', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '1009', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Ginsburg_2016_Research-inspired.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38060, 'Students misapply the standard algorithm for addition by failing to carry the tens digit, simply appending the partial sums together.', 'A student concludes that the answer to 99 + 1 is 910 because of the failure to ''carry'' the 1.', 'whole_number', 'Written computation', 'Addition algorithm', 'student', 'Kindergarten through Grade 3', 'ZDM_Ginsburg_2016_Research-inspired', 'Herbert P. Ginsburg, Young-Sun Lee, Sandra Pappas', '2016', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '1009, 1010', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Ginsburg_2016_Research-inspired.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38061, 'When solving subtractions where the subtrahend is very close to the minuend, children fail to inhibit the inclination to perform direct subtraction and therefore miss out on more efficient subtraction-by-addition strategies.', 'For the problem 81 - 79, children hardly use the subtraction by addition strategy spontaneously (79 + 2 = 81), instead trying to calculate the explicitly shown direct subtraction.', 'whole_number', 'Subtraction', 'Calculation strategies', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Dooren_2015_Inhibitory', 'Wim Van Dooren, Matthew Inglis', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '714', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Dooren_2015_Inhibitory.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38062, 'Learners rely on intuitive tendencies regarding representativeness, incorrectly believing that a random-looking sequence of numbers has a higher probability of occurring than a sequential one.', 'When asked who is more likely to win the lottery, learners incorrectly say Anne (who picked 5, 16, 27, 31, 33, 39) is more likely to win than John (who picked 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6) because her numbers look more representative.', 'probability', 'Probability', 'Representativeness heuristic', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Dooren_2015_Inhibitory', 'Wim Van Dooren, Matthew Inglis', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '717', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Dooren_2015_Inhibitory.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38063, 'Students apply the intuitive ''same A-same B'' rule based on superficial external task features, incorrectly assuming that reciprocal changes to a shape''s dimensions leave its perimeter unchanged.', 'The majority of 11th-graders believed that when the vertical sides of a rectangle are shortened by 20% and the horizontal sides are prolonged by 20%, the perimeter stayed the same.', 'geometric', 'Perimeter', 'Intuitive rules', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Dooren_2015_Inhibitory', 'Wim Van Dooren, Matthew Inglis', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '717', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Dooren_2015_Inhibitory.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38064, 'Students fail to inhibit automatic, Type 1 syntactic matching processes when translating word problems into algebra, mistakenly reproducing the order of words in their equations.', 'When asked to write an equation for ''There are six times as many students as professors at this university'', the typical incorrect response is ''6S = P'', repeating the order of concepts in the sentence.', 'algebraic', 'Algebraic equations', 'Word problem translation', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Dooren_2015_Inhibitory', 'Wim Van Dooren, Matthew Inglis', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '717, 718', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Dooren_2015_Inhibitory.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38065, 'Students incorrectly apply the discreteness of natural numbers to the dense domain of rational numbers, assuming there are no numbers or only finitely many numbers between two decimals.', 'Students commonly make the mistake of thinking that there are no (or finitely many) numbers between two pseudo-successive numbers such as 1.2 and 1.3.', 'rational', 'Rational numbers', 'Density', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Dooren_2015_Inhibitory', 'Wim Van Dooren, Matthew Inglis', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '719', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Dooren_2015_Inhibitory.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38066, 'During the process of conceptual change, learners form synthetic models that attempt to assimilate new information about rational numbers without fully restructuring their prior incompatible knowledge.', 'Learners might state that there are infinitely many fractions between two decimal numbers, while simultaneously refusing to accept that there can be decimal numbers between fractions.', 'rational', 'Rational numbers', 'Fractions and decimals', 'other', '', 'ZDM_Dooren_2015_Inhibitory', 'Wim Van Dooren, Matthew Inglis', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'secondary', '719', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Dooren_2015_Inhibitory.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38067, 'Children restrict their definition of a triangle to a prototypical isosceles or equilateral shape with a horizontal base, failing to recognize right-angled or non-prototypical triangles as valid triangles.', 'When shown a right-angled triangle, some students called it a ''half-triangle, because if you put two of them together you get a real triangle.''', 'geometric', 'Properties of shapes', 'Defining triangles', 'mixed', 'primary and middle years', 'ZDM_Clarke_2011_Building', 'Doug Clarke, Barbara Clarke, Anne Roche', '2011', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '910', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Clarke_2011_Building.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38068, 'Students use additive ''gap thinking'' to compare fractions, assuming fractions are equivalent if the absolute difference between their numerator and denominator is the same.', 'Some students claim that 5/6 and 7/8 are equivalent because they both require one ''bit'' to make a whole, failing to consider the actual size of the parts.', 'fraction', 'Fraction comparison', 'Gap thinking', 'mixed', 'primary and middle years', 'ZDM_Clarke_2011_Building', 'Doug Clarke, Barbara Clarke, Anne Roche', '2011', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '910', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Clarke_2011_Building.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38069, 'When comparing proper non-unit fractions, a teacher incorrectly relies on a learned rule about numerators without considering the denominators.', 'A teacher selects an incorrect answer by applying the rule ''the bigger the numerator, the greater the portion'' and providing no consideration for the nature of the denominators.', 'fraction', 'Fraction comparison', 'Role of numerator and denominator', 'in_service', 'primary school', 'ZDM_Cole_2012_Assessing', 'Yaa Cole', '2012', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '421', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Cole_2012_Assessing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38070, 'Teachers dismiss valid, non-standard student-generated subtraction algorithms because they find them confusing or not aligned with official textbook formulas.', 'When asked to evaluate alternative subtraction methods, a teacher says ''this is too confusing for children'' and relies on ''the same formula'' from the textbook instead of recognizing the mathematical validity of the strategies.', 'whole_number', 'Subtraction', 'Alternative algorithms', 'in_service', 'primary school', 'ZDM_Cole_2012_Assessing', 'Yaa Cole', '2012', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '422, 423', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Cole_2012_Assessing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38071, 'Participants erroneously rely on the number-of-cases heuristic, dividing the number of prizes by the number of remaining alternatives to judge posterior probabilities as equal, while ignoring the dependency of the host''s behavior on the initial choice.', 'Judging that both staying and switching have the same posterior winning probabilities of 0.50 after the host opens a door revealing a mock prize.', 'probability', 'Monty Hall dilemma', 'Posterior probabilities', 'student', 'third graders of primary school, second graders of secondary school, and first year university', 'ZDM_Saenen_2015_Inhibitory', 'Lore Saenen, Mieke Heyvaert, Wim Van Dooren, Patrick Onghena', '2015', 'ZDM Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '838, 839', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Saenen_2015_Inhibitory.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38072, 'Pupils attempt to solve a system of linear equations by inappropriately dividing one equation by another, generating a meaningless equation which they then try to interpret by comparing coefficients.', 'When trying to find adequate multipliers, a group divided the first equation by the initial one, made a sign mistake yielding ''3x = 23x'', and erroneously concluded the invariant was x or 2x by comparing the coefficients.', 'algebraic', 'Systems of linear equations', 'Linear combinations', 'mixed', 'grade 9', 'ZDM_Artigue_1997_Pupils''', 'Michèle Artigue, Jean-Baptiste Lagrange', '1997', 'ZDM', 'uncoded', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Artigue_1997_Pupils''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38073, 'Pupils experience ''pseudo-transparency phenomena'', where they are distracted from the mathematical task by the software''s commands and fail to recognize the underlying mathematical structure.', 'Pupils failed to notice that linear combinations allowed them to obtain equations with one unknown, instead focusing merely on the fact that the ''Simplify'' and ''Develop'' commands yielded the same result.', 'algebraic', 'Systems of linear equations', 'CAS commands and algebraic equivalence', 'mixed', 'grade 9', 'ZDM_Artigue_1997_Pupils''', 'Michèle Artigue, Jean-Baptiste Lagrange', '1997', 'ZDM', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Artigue_1997_Pupils''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38074, 'Pupils are unable to determine if two algebraically equivalent expressions are the same when the software''s output format differs from their handwritten calculations.', 'When using the software to ''double check'' their work, pupils could not decide whether the software''s output and their pencil-and-paper result denoted the same mathematical expression.', 'algebraic', 'Algebraic expressions', 'Equivalence of expressions', 'mixed', 'grade 9', 'ZDM_Artigue_1997_Pupils''', 'Michèle Artigue, Jean-Baptiste Lagrange', '1997', 'ZDM', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/ZDM/ZDM_Artigue_1997_Pupils''.pdf')).
