research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38859, 'Students rely on a dynamic, continuity-based intuition of functions, believing that continuous functions do not have gaps and must be able to be drawn without lifting a pencil, which conflicts with modern formal definitions in analysis.', 'Students believe that continuous functions ''may be drawn freely without lifting the pencil from the paper'', leading them to see modern formal definitions as artificial and proofs as unnecessary.', 'calculus', 'Continuity', 'Concept image of continuous functions', 'other', '', 'ESM_Jahnke_2001_Cantors', 'HANS NIELS JAHNKE', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'mixed', 'primary', '193, 194', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Jahnke_2001_Cantors.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38860, 'Students incorrectly read the slope (coefficient directeur) of a line from its graph by calculating the ratio as X/Y instead of Y/X based on a direction vector, and struggle with assigning the correct sign.', 'Virginie incorrectly reads the slope as X/Y where X and Y are coordinates of a direction vector, and misreads the sign of X.', 'algebraic', 'Linear functions', 'Slope / Coefficient directeur', 'student', 'Terminale B', 'ESM_Dagher_1996_Apprentissage', 'ANTOINE DAGHER', '1996', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '382', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Dagher_1996_Apprentissage.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38861, 'Students rely on vague visual heuristics when interpreting the coefficients of a quadratic function, such as believing that coefficient ''b'' simply ''leads the vertex'' or incorrectly assuming it represents the y-intercept, before establishing a rigorous algebraic-graphical link.', 'Cecile initially states that ''b mene le sommet'' (b leads the vertex) and later guesses that ''c c''est l''ordonnee a l''origine'' without a formal understanding of why.', 'algebraic', 'Quadratic functions', 'Graphical interpretation of coefficients', 'student', 'Terminale B', 'ESM_Dagher_1996_Apprentissage', 'ANTOINE DAGHER', '1996', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '384, 385', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Dagher_1996_Apprentissage.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38862, 'Students lack basic algebraic mastery and make severe structural errors when solving exponential inequalities, such as incorrectly dropping the base ''e'' to treat the exponent directly as a linear inequality.', 'Virginie incorrectly simplifies the inequality ''e^(-3/4x+1/2) > 1'' directly to ''(-3/4)x + 1/2 > 0'' by arbitrarily dropping the base ''e''.', 'algebraic', 'Exponential inequalities', 'Algebraic manipulation', 'student', 'Terminale B', 'ESM_Dagher_1996_Apprentissage', 'ANTOINE DAGHER', '1996', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'passing', '377, 378', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Dagher_1996_Apprentissage.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38863, 'Students overgeneralize from their experience with whole numbers that addition always makes a quantity larger and subtraction always makes a quantity smaller.', 'When given 5 + [ ] = 3, Violet stated: ''There''s no way to do that. ... Because you have to do 5 minus something equals 2. If you add to that, say 5 plus 2, that would be 7 not 3.''', 'integer', 'Integer addition and subtraction', 'Directionality of operations', 'student', '2nd-grade', 'ESM_Bishop_2014_Using', 'Jessica Pierson Bishop & Lisa L. Lamb & Randolph A. Philipp & Ian Whitacre & Bonnie P. Schappelle', '2014', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '47, 49, 50, 52', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bishop_2014_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38864, 'Students with a strictly cardinal view of numbers interpret zero as absolute ''nothing'', making the existence of negative numbers paradoxical since they would represent an amount less than nothing.', 'Zero is nothing, but negative is more nothing.', 'integer', 'Negative numbers', 'Cardinality and magnitude', 'student', '2nd-grade', 'ESM_Bishop_2014_Using', 'Jessica Pierson Bishop & Lisa L. Lamb & Randolph A. Philipp & Ian Whitacre & Bonnie P. Schappelle', '2014', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '42, 43', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bishop_2014_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38865, 'Students reject the numerical status of negative numbers because they cannot be modeled as a physical, countable set of objects.', 'Negative numbers aren''t really numbers. ... We don''t really count them [negative numbers] in school, and there''s no negative 1 cube and stuff.', 'integer', 'Definition of negative numbers', 'Numerical status and objects', 'student', '2nd-grade', 'ESM_Bishop_2014_Using', 'Jessica Pierson Bishop & Lisa L. Lamb & Randolph A. Philipp & Ian Whitacre & Bonnie P. Schappelle', '2014', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '48, 49', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bishop_2014_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38866, 'Students believe that adding or subtracting a negative number is an impossible operation because they lack a conceptual meaning for a negative change value.', 'You can''t add negative 2 unless they''re negative numbers. ... Because that''s a negative 2 [pointing to -2 in 6 + -2], and you can''t add negative numbers.', 'integer', 'Integer addition and subtraction', 'Negative change values (double signs)', 'student', '2nd-grade', 'ESM_Bishop_2014_Using', 'Jessica Pierson Bishop & Lisa L. Lamb & Randolph A. Philipp & Ian Whitacre & Bonnie P. Schappelle', '2014', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '47, 48, 50, 51', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bishop_2014_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38867, 'Teachers change between mathematical representations automatically and without making explicit connections, incorrectly assuming that the conversions are as obvious to the learners as they are to themselves.', 'In a classroom scenario, a student asks how to see the addition of 1/4 and 2/3 in a rectangle representation, but the teacher ignores the request and introduces a completely new pizza representation without connecting the two.', 'fraction', 'Multiple representations', 'Conversions of representations', 'mixed', 'secondary school', 'ESM_Dreher_2015_Teachers''', 'Anika Dreher & Sebastian Kuntze', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '92, 96, 97', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Dreher_2015_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38868, 'Students incorrectly claim that two fractions are equal in size if they both lack exactly one piece from the whole, focusing on the absolute number of missing pieces rather than the proportional size of the pieces.', 'A student claims that ''The fractions 5/6 and 7/8 are of equal size! There is always one piece of the whole missing.''', 'fraction', 'Fraction comparison', 'Missing pieces', 'mixed', 'secondary school', 'ESM_Dreher_2015_Teachers''', 'Anika Dreher & Sebastian Kuntze', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '98', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Dreher_2015_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38869, 'Pre-service teachers hold the misconception that fraction addition and fraction multiplication cannot be shown or explained using the same type of pictorial representation.', 'A pre-service teacher evaluates a vignette by incorrectly claiming, ''You cannot show both operations in the same representation,'' revealing a lack of specific content knowledge about rectangle models.', 'fraction', 'Operations with fractions', 'Pictorial representations', 'mixed', 'secondary school', 'ESM_Dreher_2015_Teachers''', 'Anika Dreher & Sebastian Kuntze', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '106, 107', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Dreher_2015_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38870, 'Students over-rely on inefficient, one-by-one counting strategies when using an array to solve multiplication problems, which frequently leads to losing track and arriving at incorrect answers.', 'When calculating 7x9, a student ignores the groupings of 5s and 25s entirely, instead attempting to count every single dot one by one from 25 upwards, eventually losing track around 45.', 'whole_number', 'Multiplication calculations', 'Calculation strategies', 'student', 'Year 4 and Year 6', 'ESM_Barmby_2009_Array', 'Patrick Barmby, Tony Harries, Steve Higgins & Jennifer Suggate', '2009', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '232, 233, 234', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Barmby_2009_Array.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38871, 'Students fail to connect the two numbers being multiplied with the two-dimensional row and column structure of an array, highlighting a lack of understanding of the binary nature of multiplication.', 'When asked to represent 11x6 on the array, two Year 4 students who knew the answer was 66 via rote memorization were entirely unable to determine how to position the array''s right-angle to show the calculation visually.', 'whole_number', 'Multiplication concepts', 'Binary nature of multiplication and arrays', 'student', 'Year 4 and Year 6', 'ESM_Barmby_2009_Array', 'Patrick Barmby, Tony Harries, Steve Higgins & Jennifer Suggate', '2009', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '233, 234, 236', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Barmby_2009_Array.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38872, 'Students view the limit concept as a potentially infinite process, mistakenly believing that any property common to all terms of a sequence holds for the limit and assuming that gradual causes must always have gradual effects.', 'Students believe that a change of 0.001 in Δt will not produce a big change in its effect, leading them to view the derivative simply as Δy/Δx for a small Δx without taking the limit.', 'calculus', 'Limit and derivative', 'Definition of the derivative', 'student', 'First year college students', 'ESM_Kidron_2008_Abstraction', 'Ivy Kidron', '2008', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '200, 201, 206, 207', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Kidron_2008_Abstraction.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38873, 'Students incorrectly attribute the structural error in Euler''s numerical method solely to the computer''s round-off error or the accumulating effect of iterations, rather than recognizing that the algorithm mathematically replaces the exact derivative limit with a finite approximation.', 'A student states that MatLab computes only 15 digits after the decimal point and attributes the completely divergent graph behavior strictly to this computational round-off effect.', 'calculus', 'Numerical methods', 'Euler''s method error analysis', 'student', 'First year college students', 'ESM_Kidron_2008_Abstraction', 'Ivy Kidron', '2008', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '205, 206, 207, 208', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Kidron_2008_Abstraction.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38874, 'Students focus on the syntactic surface structure of algebraic equations, grouping them based on superficial visual features rather than underlying relational deep structure.', 'A student groups different types of equations together simply because they equal zero or have ''numbers on one side and algebra on the one side'', rather than recognizing their linear or quadratic structures.', 'algebraic', 'Algebraic equations', 'Classification and relational structure', 'student', 'grade 10 and grade 12', 'ESM_English_1996_Analogical', 'LYN D. ENGLISH AND PATRICK V. SHARRY', '1996', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '140, 152, 153, 154', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_English_1996_Analogical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38875, 'Students hold misunderstandings about algebraic solution processes, arbitrarily applying operations like ''getting an equation to square root'' without understanding the quadratic formula or equation structure.', 'A student attempts to solve or classify a complex algebraic equation by stating ''you could get them all to equal zero'' and struggling to know ''whether you could get it to square root''.', 'algebraic', 'Solving equations', 'Quadratic and non-arithmetical linear equations', 'student', 'grade 10 and grade 12', 'ESM_English_1996_Analogical', 'LYN D. ENGLISH AND PATRICK V. SHARRY', '1996', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '149, 150, 153', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_English_1996_Analogical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38876, 'Students erroneously define an irrational number simply as a decimal containing an infinity of digits, missing the crucial property of non-periodicity.', 'A student explains that an irrational number is a decimal with an infinity of digits because ''the result is not exact'', associating infinity with an inability to reach exactitude.', 'decimal', 'Irrational numbers', 'Decimal representation', 'mixed', 'grades 9 and 10 and prospective teachers', 'ESM_Fischbein_1995_Concept', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN, RUTH JEHIAM AND DORIT COHEN', '1995', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '36', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1995_Concept.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38877, 'Students intuitively associate irrational numbers with negative numbers or any number that is not a whole number because they deviate from the original prototype of wholeness.', 'When asked to define an irrational number, twenty-eight percent of 9th graders gave answers like ''..which is not a whole number''.', 'rational', 'Irrational numbers', 'Intuitive prototypes', 'mixed', 'grades 9 and 10 and prospective teachers', 'ESM_Fischbein_1995_Concept', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN, RUTH JEHIAM AND DORIT COHEN', '1995', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '36', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1995_Concept.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38878, 'Students incorrectly believe that the set of rational numbers and the set of irrational numbers have the same amount of elements, often assuming an alternating one-to-one correspondence.', 'Students justify that both sets have the same amount of elements by stating, ''Between two rationals, there is one irrational'' or ''For each rational, there is an irrational number''.', 'rational', 'Set cardinality', 'Rational vs. irrational numbers', 'mixed', 'grades 9 and 10 and prospective teachers', 'ESM_Fischbein_1995_Concept', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN, RUTH JEHIAM AND DORIT COHEN', '1995', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '38', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1995_Concept.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38879, 'Students reject the concept of incommensurability, erroneously believing it is always possible to find a common unit of measure for any two segments by indefinitely decreasing the unit''s size.', 'When asked about finding a common unit for a square''s side and diagonal, students claim ''we may decrease the length of the unit as much as we want in order to obtain a common unit''.', 'measurement', 'Incommensurability', 'Common unit of measure', 'mixed', 'grades 9 and 10 and prospective teachers', 'ESM_Fischbein_1995_Concept', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN, RUTH JEHIAM AND DORIT COHEN', '1995', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '39, 40', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1995_Concept.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38880, 'Students incorrectly use an additive or constant difference procedure instead of a multiplicative proportional relationship to compare rates.', 'When comparing 6 pieces of gum for 12 cents and 8 pieces for 15 cents, a student computes 6 + 2 = 8 and 12 + 2 = 14, concluding Richard''s was cheaper.', 'proportional', 'Proportional reasoning', 'Rate comparison', 'student', 'sixth graders and eighth graders', 'ESM_Karplus_1983_Early', 'ROBERT KARPLUS, STEVEN PULOS, and ELIZABETH K. STAGE', '1983', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '225, 226, 229, 230', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Karplus_1983_Early.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38881, 'Students confuse proportions with cross-multiplication by mistakenly multiplying the two variables within each relationship instead of dividing them.', 'To compare 6 pieces of gum for 12 cents against 8 pieces for 15 cents, a student incorrectly multiplies 12 x 6 = 72 and 15 x 8 = 120, then concludes Richard''s was cheaper.', 'proportional', 'Proportional reasoning', 'Cross-multiplication', 'student', 'sixth graders and eighth graders', 'ESM_Karplus_1983_Early', 'ROBERT KARPLUS, STEVEN PULOS, and ELIZABETH K. STAGE', '1983', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '225, 226, 229', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Karplus_1983_Early.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38882, 'Children hypothesize that the presence of a comma or period in a written numeral intrinsically signifies its magnitude, using punctuation as a direct proxy for ''bigger'' or ''lower'' numbers rather than understanding its role in place value.', 'I think the period means that there needs to be kind of like lower numbers, then the comma needs bigger numbers to put the comma in.', 'whole_number', 'Numerical writing system', 'Punctuation marks and value', 'student', 'Kindergarten and first grade', 'ESM_Brizuela_2008_Roles', 'Barbara M. Brizuela & Gabrielle A. Cayton', '2008', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '220', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Brizuela_2008_Roles.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38883, 'Children view punctuation marks in numerals as categorical labels that simply designate the ''type'' of number, such as indicating that a number represents a price.', 'Periods are for prices.', 'other', 'Numerical writing system', 'Punctuation marks as categorical markers', 'student', 'Kindergarten and first grade', 'ESM_Brizuela_2008_Roles', 'Barbara M. Brizuela & Gabrielle A. Cayton', '2008', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '219, 220', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Brizuela_2008_Roles.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38884, 'Children confuse the monetary value term ''cent'' with the physical object ''coin'', leading them to incorrectly count the number of coins rather than their cumulative value.', 'When asked ''How many cents is this?'', children treated groups of three pennies and three nickels as each being equal to three cents, because there were three coins in each group.', 'other', 'Money', 'Coin values and vocabulary', 'student', 'preschool through second grade', 'ESM_Brenner_1998_Meaning', 'MARY E. BRENNER', '1998', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'mixed', 'primary', '133, 144', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Brenner_1998_Meaning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38885, 'Children apply their everyday understanding of realistic prices to school word problems, leading them to reject the mathematical premises of problems that feature artificially low prices.', 'When shown a textbook picture pricing apple juice at three cents, a student argued that the price must be just for the cup itself or for a tiny sample, because real juice costs ''dollar something''.', 'other', 'Money word problems', 'Real-world context friction', 'student', 'preschool through second grade', 'ESM_Brenner_1998_Meaning', 'MARY E. BRENNER', '1998', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '146, 147', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Brenner_1998_Meaning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38886, 'School tasks expect children to identify exact change combinations for purchases, whereas children''s everyday shopping script relies on the practical strategy of simply providing a denomination larger than the price.', 'On a test asking which coins to use to pay for a 3-cent sticker, children indicated a quarter, which covered the cost in real life but was marked wrong according to school expectations.', 'other', 'Purchasing and change', 'Exact change vs. sufficiency', 'student', 'preschool through second grade', 'ESM_Brenner_1998_Meaning', 'MARY E. BRENNER', '1998', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '145, 147, 148', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Brenner_1998_Meaning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38887, 'The school curriculum introduces money from the smallest unit (pennies) to the largest to teach base-10 addition, conflicting with children''s top-down everyday knowledge anchored in dollars and quarters.', 'Kindergarten children learned little from school money lessons because the curriculum focused heavily on pennies, which children viewed as ''junk'' and practically useless for real shopping.', 'whole_number', 'Place value and addition', 'Base-10 structure via money', 'student', 'preschool through second grade', 'ESM_Brenner_1998_Meaning', 'MARY E. BRENNER', '1998', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'secondary', '143, 144, 145, 149', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Brenner_1998_Meaning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38888, 'Students compartmentalize the process of solving an algebraic equation from the conceptual understanding of what an equation and its solution actually are.', 'A student correctly solves the equation 7x-3=13x+15 but is unable to subsequently tell whether x=10 is a solution to the very same equation.', 'algebraic', 'Algebraic equations', 'Meaning of an equation and solution', 'in_service', 'upper secondary', 'ESM_Jankvist_2015_Framework', 'Uffe Thomas Jankvist & Mogens Niss', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '275', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Jankvist_2015_Framework.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38889, 'Students view formal mathematical proof as an inconsequential ritual, leading them to rely on empirical evidence or specific numerical examples rather than accepting a general logical verification.', 'After seeing a general proof that n^3 - n is divisible by 6, students asked to prove that 59^3 - 59 is divisible by 6 prefer to calculate it with numbers or re-apply the algebraic steps to 59 rather than invoking the already proven general theorem.', 'discrete', 'Mathematical proof', 'Empirical proof schemes', 'in_service', 'upper secondary', 'ESM_Jankvist_2015_Framework', 'Uffe Thomas Jankvist & Mogens Niss', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'secondary', '276, 277', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Jankvist_2015_Framework.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38890, 'During the mathematization phase of modeling, students incorrectly enforce the use of mathematical functions from their repertoire of familiarity rather than selecting functions appropriate to the actual extra-mathematical situation.', 'When a modeling task about wave energy requires transformed trigonometric functions, students might try to fit the data using familiar linear functions even though the properties are inadequate.', 'algebraic', 'Mathematical modelling', 'Mathematization', 'in_service', 'upper secondary', 'ESM_Jankvist_2015_Framework', 'Uffe Thomas Jankvist & Mogens Niss', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '277, 278', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Jankvist_2015_Framework.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38891, 'Learners refuse to recognize a valid geometric shape if it is presented in a non-standard orientation, confusing its defining properties with its specific spatial alignment.', 'Children will refuse to consider a specific figure to be a right-angled triangle because of its orientation.', 'geometric', 'Triangles', 'Orientation', 'student', '', 'ESM_Bishop_nodate_What', 'Alan J. Bishop', '', 'Studies in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '149', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bishop_nodate_What.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38892, 'Students conflate the specific visual drawing of a figure with the general geometric concept it is meant to represent.', 'Once the general triangle is drawn, it can become, in the child''s mind, a specific triangle, leading to a confusion of form and content.', 'geometric', 'Geometric figures', 'Representation and abstraction', 'student', '', 'ESM_Bishop_nodate_What', 'Alan J. Bishop', '', 'Studies in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'primary', '149', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bishop_nodate_What.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38893, 'Students exhibit geometrical rigidity where their conceptions become trapped by a diagram, leaving them unable to perceive a line or component in an alternative role.', 'A student is unable to use the line AD as a ''side'' of a sub-triangle because they only perceive it as the ''height'' of the larger triangle ABC.', 'geometric', 'Triangles', 'Altitudes and sides', 'student', '', 'ESM_Bishop_nodate_What', 'Alan J. Bishop', '', 'Studies in Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '151', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bishop_nodate_What.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38894, 'Children hold naive assumptions about scaling large-scale spatial phenomena to representations, resulting in flawed models of objects like the Earth and gravity.', 'Children drew a sphere set in a vertical gravity field, with all the accompanying beliefs about objects above the earth falling onto it while others were ''falling off the earth''.', 'geometric', '3D Space / Spheres', 'Scaling and spatial conceptualization', 'student', '', 'ESM_Bishop_nodate_What', 'Alan J. Bishop', '', 'Studies in Mathematics Education', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '149', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bishop_nodate_What.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38895, 'When unable to generate an appropriate visual schema or conceptualize the spatial mathematical structure, students resort to superficial and inappropriate calculations combining the isolated numbers present in the problem text.', 'A student attempts to solve the painted cube problem by simply dividing 27 (the number of small cubes) by 4 (the four different types of painted cubes) because they are ''just looking at the numbers''.', 'geometric', '3D geometry', 'Painted cube problem', 'student', 'grade 10', 'ESM_Campbell_1995_Visual', 'K. JENNIFER CAMPBELL, KEVIN F. COLLIS AND JANE M. WATSON', '1995', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '185', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Campbell_1995_Visual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38896, 'Students rely heavily on concrete, everyday reasoning about a problem''s real-world context, leading to misleading elaborations that distract them from correctly applying the necessary mathematical relationships.', 'In a problem involving linear relationships of blood alcohol levels, a student incorrectly concludes that two drivers must lose alcohol at the exact same rate because ''if people get drunk they can''t just do something to make them different''.', 'algebraic', 'Linear modeling', 'Word problems and real-world contexts', 'student', 'grade 10', 'ESM_Campbell_1995_Visual', 'K. JENNIFER CAMPBELL, KEVIN F. COLLIS AND JANE M. WATSON', '1995', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'mixed', 'primary', '189, 190', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Campbell_1995_Visual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38897, 'Preservice teachers hold absolutist views of mathematics, believing it consists solely of rules and procedures that must be transmitted by the teacher, creating friction and resistance to inquiry-based methods.', 'Preservice teachers express a desire to ''be told the basics by the teacher'' and question why they should learn how children operate when they already ''remember the rules''.', 'other', 'Epistemology of mathematics', 'Teaching and learning practices', 'PST', 'primary and early years', 'ESM_Klein_2012_How', 'Mary Klein', '2012', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '28, 29, 31, 32', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Klein_2012_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38898, 'When comparing fractions close to one, the student incorrectly assumes that a larger denominator inherently makes the fraction smaller, misapplying the rule that more parts in a whole means smaller pieces.', 'A student initially thinks 99/100 is smaller than 15/16 because she cannot get past the core idea that ''the more parts in the whole, the smaller the parts''.', 'fraction', 'Fraction comparison', 'Comparing fractions to the whole', 'PST', 'primary and early years', 'ESM_Klein_2012_How', 'Mary Klein', '2012', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '35', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Klein_2012_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38899, 'A student creates an idiosyncratic graphical representation for a combinatorics problem that only works for the first trial and cannot be generalized to subsequent trials.', 'When asked to show combinations of a coin thrown twice or three times, the illustration of the first pupil could only be applied to the situation when the coin was thrown for the first time.', 'probability', 'Combinations', 'Tree diagrams', 'student', '11 to 12 years of age, 12 to 13 years, 8 to 9 years', 'ESM_Abele_1978_Usage', 'A. ABELE', '1978', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'secondary', '12, 13', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Abele_1978_Usage.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38900, 'When transitioning from a physical number line model to formal operator notation, students invent a logical but mathematically contradictory use of directional arrows.', 'Students drew green arrows pointing left alongside standard boxes, which contradicted the established rules that green numbers must be to the left of red numbers without directional arrows.', 'integer', 'Integral numbers as phases and operators', 'Graphical representations', 'student', '11 to 12 years of age, 12 to 13 years, 8 to 9 years', 'ESM_Abele_1978_Usage', 'A. ABELE', '1978', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'secondary', '14, 15', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Abele_1978_Usage.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38901, 'Students over-rely on sampling representativeness, believing a sample tells everything about a population, or over-rely on sampling variability, believing a sample tells nothing useful.', '', 'statistics', 'Statistical inference', 'Representativeness and variability', 'other', '', 'ESM_BenZvi_2015_Learning', 'Dani Ben-Zvi & Arthur Bakker & Katie Makar', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '291', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_BenZvi_2015_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38902, 'Students prefer biased or purposive sampling methods, such as voluntary samples, because they distrust random sampling as a process capable of producing fair samples.', 'roughly 60% of the students indicated a preference for selecting the first 60 children in a line over drawing 60 student names from a hat.', 'statistics', 'Sampling methods', 'Random vs. biased sampling', 'other', '', 'ESM_BenZvi_2015_Learning', 'Dani Ben-Zvi & Arthur Bakker & Katie Makar', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '293, 294, 295', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_BenZvi_2015_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38903, 'Students accept random sampling in games of chance but fail to grasp its validity or rationale in the context of opinion surveys, assuming it cannot account for covariant traits.', 'A random sample in the context of drawing marbles, for example, is considered acceptable, whereas a random sample in the context of an opinion survey is not.', 'statistics', 'Random sampling', 'Contextual influence', 'other', '', 'ESM_BenZvi_2015_Learning', 'Dani Ben-Zvi & Arthur Bakker & Katie Makar', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '295', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_BenZvi_2015_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38904, 'Individuals hold the intuition that even small random samples are highly representative of the population and thus expect two small samples to be more similar to each other than sampling theory predicts.', '', 'statistics', 'Sampling variability', 'Law of Small Numbers', 'other', '', 'ESM_BenZvi_2015_Learning', 'Dani Ben-Zvi & Arthur Bakker & Katie Makar', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '295', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_BenZvi_2015_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38905, 'Students confuse the frequency distributions of individual samples with the theoretical sampling distribution, erroneously believing that sampling distributions should look like the population or that large samples have more variability.', '', 'statistics', 'Sampling distribution', 'Sample vs. sampling distribution', 'other', '', 'ESM_BenZvi_2015_Learning', 'Dani Ben-Zvi & Arthur Bakker & Katie Makar', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '295, 296', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_BenZvi_2015_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38906, 'Students believe that a sample is only representative or ''good'' if its size constitutes a large percentage of the overall population.', '', 'statistics', 'Sample size', 'Population fraction', 'other', '', 'ESM_BenZvi_2015_Learning', 'Dani Ben-Zvi & Arthur Bakker & Katie Makar', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '296', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_BenZvi_2015_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38907, 'Students execute computational procedures fluently but fail to recognize or exploit the underlying structural inverse relationships that would simplify the problem.', 'When given the problem (274 + 274 + 274 + 274 + 274) / 5, some children who are good at arithmetic calculate the sum and then divide, remaining entirely blind to the inverse structure.', 'whole_number', 'Inverse operations', 'Repeated addition and division', 'other', '', 'ESM_Greer_2012_Inversion', 'Brian Greer', '2012', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '429, 430', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Greer_2012_Inversion.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38908, 'Students possess firmly entrenched implicit models, such as the ''take away'' model for subtraction, which creates conceptual obstacles when extending operations to negative numbers.', 'The entrenched ''take away'' model leads to the belief that a calculation like 3-8 is an impossibility or absurd because one cannot take more from a number than it contains.', 'integer', 'Subtraction', 'Negative numbers', 'other', '', 'ESM_Greer_2012_Inversion', 'Brian Greer', '2012', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '432', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Greer_2012_Inversion.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38909, 'Teachers erroneously believe that a mathematically reasonable answer must be exactly correct, failing to distinguish between number sense bounds and exact calculations.', 'If we said 5 - 2 = 3, 4 will not be a reasonable answer. Any answer less than 3 will not be reasonable. The reasonable answer is just 3.', 'whole_number', 'Reasonableness of answers', 'Exactness vs approximation', 'in_service', 'eighth-grade', 'ESM_Alajmi_2007_Reasonable', 'AMAL ALAJMI AND ROBERT REYS', '2007', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '82, 83', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Alajmi_2007_Reasonable.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38910, 'Teachers evaluate the reasonableness of a student''s answer based on the percentage of procedural steps executed correctly, rather than assessing the mathematical magnitude or real-world practicality of the final result.', 'If a student was adding two fractions, 3/5 + 1/3, if he got the common factor of 15 and did something like 3/15 + 1/15 = 4/15, for me the answer is reasonable because he worked correctly half of the problem.', 'fraction', 'Reasonableness of answers', 'Procedural completeness', 'in_service', 'eighth-grade', 'ESM_Alajmi_2007_Reasonable', 'AMAL ALAJMI AND ROBERT REYS', '2007', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '83, 84', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Alajmi_2007_Reasonable.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38911, 'Students mechanically apply the decimal counting rule for multiplication to place the decimal point without estimating the magnitude, leading to incorrect placement when trailing zeros are dropped.', 'For 534.6 x 0.545, students placed the decimal point 4 digits from the right to get 29.1357, failing to notice the dropped zero from 6x5=30 or that the answer should be around half of 534.', 'decimal', 'Decimal multiplication', 'Placing the decimal point', 'in_service', 'eighth-grade', 'ESM_Alajmi_2007_Reasonable', 'AMAL ALAJMI AND ROBERT REYS', '2007', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '88, 89', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Alajmi_2007_Reasonable.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38912, 'A student incorrectly identifies a three-dimensional tetrahedron as a ''triangle'' with four ''sides'', conflating the terminology for 2D sides and 3D faces.', 'When asked if all triangles have three sides, Kayla says false and draws a tetrahedron, which the teacher immediately dismisses by saying, ''No. That''s not a triangle. It''s not flat.''', 'geometric', 'Shapes', '2D and 3D attributes', 'PST', '', 'ESM_Harkness_2009_Social', 'Shelly Sheats Harkness', '2009', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'secondary', '243, 244', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Harkness_2009_Social.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38913, 'A student overgeneralizes a rule for comparing fractions, claiming that the fraction with the lowest ''bottom number'' is always the greatest, which is only true for unit fractions or fractions with common numerators.', 'During a game of ''Capture'', Sam states that he looked at the ''bottom number'' and the one with the lowest bottom number was the greatest, prompting the teacher to say his method will not work.', 'fraction', 'Fraction comparison', 'Denominators', 'PST', '', 'ESM_Harkness_2009_Social', 'Shelly Sheats Harkness', '2009', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'secondary', '256', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Harkness_2009_Social.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38914, 'Students view arithmetical operations and mathematical definitions as pre-existing entities in a ''world of numbers'' rather than human-made arbitrary definitions, causing them to incorrectly classify defining formulas as laws or facts.', 'When presented with the defining formula a^m = a * a * ... * a (m times), many students identified it as a ''law'' or a ''fact about numbers'' rather than a ''definition''.', 'algebraic', 'Exponentiation', 'Mathematical definitions', 'student', 'undergraduate level', 'ESM_Vinner_1977_Concept', 'Shlomo Vinner', '1977', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'uncoded', 'primary', '18, 19, 21, 22, 25', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Vinner_1977_Concept.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38915, 'Students inappropriately generalize the addition rule for similar terms to multiplication, multiplying the coefficients but merely copying the variable.', 'A student performs the multiplication 3x * 6x by multiplying the coefficients 3 and 6 but just copying x, resulting in 18x, stating ''in multiplication I multiply numbers and write x''.', 'algebraic', 'Simplifying algebraic expressions', 'Multiplication of monomials', 'student', 'Grade 7 and Grade 8', 'ESM_Demby_1997_Algebraic', 'AGNIESZKA DEMBY', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '55, 56', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Demby_1997_Algebraic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38916, 'Students invent temporary, inconsistent ''quasi-rules'' based on superficial visual attractors in an expression, changing these rules haphazardly across similar problems.', 'A student sees +3 and -3x in an expression and is tempted to ''cancel'' them without understanding the algebraic structure of the expression.', 'algebraic', 'Simplifying algebraic expressions', 'Quasi-rules and attractors', 'student', 'Grade 7 and Grade 8', 'ESM_Demby_1997_Algebraic', 'AGNIESZKA DEMBY', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '56', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Demby_1997_Algebraic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38917, 'Students inappropriately evaluate expressions by applying an exponent to both the coefficient and the variable of a polynomial term during addition or subtraction.', 'A student incorrectly simplifies 2x^2 - x - 5x^2 as 4x - x - 25x = -22x, explaining ''we must first compute the powers here''.', 'algebraic', 'Simplifying algebraic expressions', 'Order of operations with powers', 'student', 'Grade 7 and Grade 8', 'ESM_Demby_1997_Algebraic', 'AGNIESZKA DEMBY', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '55', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Demby_1997_Algebraic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38918, 'Students incorrectly overgeneralize the distributivity law, applying it to powers of binomials.', 'A student writes (8 - 2x)^2 = 8^2 - (2x)^2 and justifies it by stating it is ''by distributivity law''.', 'algebraic', 'Simplifying algebraic expressions', 'Binomial expansion', 'student', 'Grade 7 and Grade 8', 'ESM_Demby_1997_Algebraic', 'AGNIESZKA DEMBY', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '55, 63', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Demby_1997_Algebraic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38919, 'A teacher incorrectly evaluates a student''s mathematically valid division algorithm as wrong simply because it reflects a culturally different method from the student''s home country rather than the host country''s standard.', 'David solves division problems with decimals using the Ecuadorian method of balancing zeros to remove decimals, which his Spanish teacher marks wrong, stating ''In Ecuador you do it wrong''.', 'decimal', 'Long division', 'Standard algorithms', 'mixed', 'elementary and secondary', 'ESM_Gorgorio_2009_Social', 'Nuria Gorgorio & Guida de Abreu', '2009', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '70, 71', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Gorgorio_2009_Social.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38920, 'Teachers erroneously attribute immigrant students'' mathematical difficulties to inherent cognitive deficits, lack of ability, or lack of motivation, ignoring the mediating role of cultural differences and language barriers.', 'A teacher characterizes an immigrant student strictly by their perceived deficits, noting they have ''language problems'' and are ''behind their grade level,'' while viewing diversity primarily as an individual cognitive issue.', 'other', 'Mathematical ability and learning', '', 'mixed', 'elementary and secondary', 'ESM_Gorgorio_2009_Social', 'Nuria Gorgorio & Guida de Abreu', '2009', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '67, 68, 69', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Gorgorio_2009_Social.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38921, 'A student applies a borrowing algorithm procedurally without conceptual understanding of place value, resulting in nonsensical answers when subtracting a larger number from a smaller one.', 'When asked to solve 235 minus 341, the student subtracted 1 from 5, then inappropriately borrowed from the hundreds to solve 3 minus 4, ultimately arriving at 884.', 'whole_number', 'Subtraction', 'Borrowing and regrouping', 'student', 'upper high school level', 'ESM_Reynolds_1995_Addressing', 'Theodora H. Reynolds', '1995', '', 'uncoded', 'primary', '13, 14', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Reynolds_1995_Addressing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38922, 'A student abandons a correct, sense-making visual representation of a fraction problem in favor of an incorrect formal algorithm, falsely concluding that multiplication and division of fractions are the same operation.', 'After correctly illustrating that half of 1/3 is 1/6, the student tried using numbers: 1/3 divided by 1/2 = 2/3. Since this didn''t match her drawing, she tried 1/2 x 1/3 = 1/6 and concluded division and multiplication are the same.', 'fraction', 'Multiplication and division', 'Algorithm application', 'student', 'upper high school level', 'ESM_Reynolds_1995_Addressing', 'Theodora H. Reynolds', '1995', '', 'uncoded', 'primary', '14, 15', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Reynolds_1995_Addressing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38923, 'A teacher fails to understand a student''s valid, non-standard mathematical reasoning regarding units and forces the student to adopt the teacher''s procedural method, invalidating the student''s sense-making.', 'A student correctly solved a fraction problem using 24 total pizza slices as the unit, getting 14/24. The teacher interrupted, expecting the unit to be 12 slices, and directed the students to the answer 14/12ths.', 'fraction', 'Fractional units', 'Identifying the whole', 'student', 'upper high school level', 'ESM_Reynolds_1995_Addressing', 'Theodora H. Reynolds', '1995', '', 'productive', 'primary', '12, 13', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Reynolds_1995_Addressing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38924, 'A student experiences a temporary cognitive conflict when mental arithmetic patterns break down, generating a ''false image'' before reflecting and correcting the error.', 'When asked ''What is 31 take away 30?'', the student paused, initially guessed ''One?'', and then after a prompt, corrected his previous sequence by saying ''Two... I''ve got it now.''', 'whole_number', 'Subtraction', 'Mental arithmetic', 'student', 'upper high school level', 'ESM_Reynolds_1995_Addressing', 'Theodora H. Reynolds', '1995', '', 'productive', 'secondary', '16', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Reynolds_1995_Addressing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38925, 'A student taught through behavioristic methods invents idiosyncratic and mathematically incorrect rules to achieve correct answers on an answer sheet.', 'Benny concluded that 3/2 and 2/3 both equal .5.', 'rational', 'Fractions and decimals', 'Equivalence', 'student', 'upper high school level', 'ESM_Reynolds_1995_Addressing', 'Theodora H. Reynolds', '1995', '', 'productive', 'passing', '21, 22', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Reynolds_1995_Addressing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38926, 'Students misread decimal numbers in word problems, erroneously interpreting the decimal part as a different whole-number unit of measure or a fraction.', 'An average speed of 11.9 miles per hour was read as ''11 miles 9 minutes per hour''; and ''pork chops weighting 1.07 lb'' was read as ''1 lb 7 ounces''.', 'decimal', 'Decimal place value', 'Reading decimals in context', 'student', 'aged 12-16, fourth year secondary class of 15 year old pupils', 'ESM_Bell_1981_Choice', 'ALAN BELL, MALCOLM SWAN AND GLENDA TAYLOR', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '405, 411', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bell_1981_Choice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38927, 'Students hold the misconception that multiplication always results in a larger number and division always results in a smaller number, causing them to choose the wrong operation when working with decimal operands less than one.', 'When asked the cost of filling a 0.22 gallon can at £1.20 a gallon, pupils chose division (1.20/0.22) because the cost ''needed to be smaller than the cost of a gallon led them to think that division was necessary.''', 'decimal', 'Choice of operation', 'Multiplication and division by numbers less than one', 'student', 'aged 12-16, fourth year secondary class of 15 year old pupils', 'ESM_Bell_1981_Choice', 'ALAN BELL, MALCOLM SWAN AND GLENDA TAYLOR', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '405', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bell_1981_Choice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38928, 'Students erroneously assume the smaller number must always be divided into the larger, and frequently confuse the directionality of the a / b division symbol.', 'Students confused the a / b symbolism with the a)b format, reading a / b as ''how many a''s go into b?''.', 'decimal', 'Division', 'Symbolism and non-commutativity', 'student', 'aged 12-16, fourth year secondary class of 15 year old pupils', 'ESM_Bell_1981_Choice', 'ALAN BELL, MALCOLM SWAN AND GLENDA TAYLOR', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '406, 413', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bell_1981_Choice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38929, 'Students are reluctant to multiply two numbers together if they are associated with different units of measure.', 'Pupils were reluctant to multiply 1.07 by 0.86 because ''the first is measured in lbs and the latter in GBP''.', 'measurement', 'Rates and multiplication', 'Dimensional units', 'student', 'aged 12-16, fourth year secondary class of 15 year old pupils', 'ESM_Bell_1981_Choice', 'ALAN BELL, MALCOLM SWAN AND GLENDA TAYLOR', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '406', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bell_1981_Choice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38930, 'When using the strategy of substituting simpler numbers to understand a problem''s structure, students incorrectly believe that changing the numbers might change the mathematical operation required.', 'Pupils solved a problem when simpler numbers were inserted but assumed that substituting different numbers could change the operation to be performed.', 'decimal', 'Choice of operation', 'Problem structure invariance', 'student', 'aged 12-16, fourth year secondary class of 15 year old pupils', 'ESM_Bell_1981_Choice', 'ALAN BELL, MALCOLM SWAN AND GLENDA TAYLOR', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '407, 415', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bell_1981_Choice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38931, 'The student translates words directly into mathematical symbols without understanding the underlying functional notation, treating the word ''of'' simply as a parenthesis.', 'When asked to deduce the formula for doubling the area of a circle, Ralf writes ''A(r = r^2\\pi'' and later suggests writing ''r(2A)'' instead of A(2r).', 'algebraic', 'Function notation', 'Interpreting symbols', 'student', 'grades five to twelve', 'ESM_Jungwirth_1991_Interaction', 'Helga Jungwirth', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '275, 276', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Jungwirth_1991_Interaction.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38932, 'The student rigidly adheres to a previously memorized formula and fails to mathematically interpret newly calculated variables to answer the question at hand.', 'After the teacher calculates the decay factor q = 0.8706 for a geometric sequence, Wilma is unable to determine the rate of price decrease, instead reverting to her original formula''s parameter p and suggesting ''p divided by a hundred''.', 'algebraic', 'Geometric sequences', 'Interpreting parameters', 'student', 'grades five to twelve', 'ESM_Jungwirth_1991_Interaction', 'Helga Jungwirth', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '277, 278', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Jungwirth_1991_Interaction.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38933, 'Students are unable to read and comprehend mathematical terminology in word problems, leading them to misunderstand what the question is asking.', '', 'algebraic', 'Linear equation word problems', 'Reading and comprehension', 'student', 'senior high school first year', 'ESM_Adu_2015_Students''', 'E. Adu, C. K. Assuah, & S. K. Asiedu-Addo', '2015', 'African Journal of Educational Studies in Mathematics and Sciences', 'deficit', 'primary', '26', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Adu_2015_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38934, 'Students fail to translate a textual word problem into an appropriate mathematical or algebraic expression.', 'For the problem ''Seven times a number is 36 less than 10 times the number'', a student wrote the incorrect inequality ''7 x x < 36 x 10''.', 'algebraic', 'Linear equation word problems', 'Transformation and formulating equations', 'student', 'senior high school first year', 'ESM_Adu_2015_Students''', 'E. Adu, C. K. Assuah, & S. K. Asiedu-Addo', '2015', 'African Journal of Educational Studies in Mathematics and Sciences', 'deficit', 'primary', '24, 25, 26', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Adu_2015_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38935, 'Students make computational mistakes when performing algebraic operations to solve linear equations, such as mishandling positive and negative signs.', '', 'algebraic', 'Linear equations', 'Computation and algebraic operations', 'student', 'senior high school first year', 'ESM_Adu_2015_Students''', 'E. Adu, C. K. Assuah, & S. K. Asiedu-Addo', '2015', 'African Journal of Educational Studies in Mathematics and Sciences', 'deficit', 'primary', '27', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Adu_2015_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38936, 'Students fail to express the final answer in an acceptable form or fail to substitute the calculated variable value back into the equation to answer the original question.', '', 'algebraic', 'Linear equation word problems', 'Encoding the final answer', 'student', 'senior high school first year', 'ESM_Adu_2015_Students''', 'E. Adu, C. K. Assuah, & S. K. Asiedu-Addo', '2015', 'African Journal of Educational Studies in Mathematics and Sciences', 'deficit', 'primary', '25, 27', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Adu_2015_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38937, 'Novice learners correctly identify a complete set of possible outcomes (a sample set) but incorrectly assume that these outcomes are equiprobable.', 'A student claims the probability of having two boys and a girl in a three-child family is 1/4 because it is one of four possible options (3 boys, 3 girls, 1 girl/2 boys, 1 boy/2 girls).', 'probability', 'Sample space', 'Equiprobability', 'PST', 'high school', 'ESM_Chernoff_2011_From', 'Egan J Chernoff & Rina Zazkis', '2011', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '15, 18, 19', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Chernoff_2011_From.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38938, 'When correcting a student''s probabilistic error, tutors forcefully impose the conventional sample space by demanding the student list all outcomes, failing to recognize that the student has already exhaustively listed a valid sample set.', 'A tutor tells a student who answers 1/4 to ''list out all 8 possibilities,'' disregarding the student''s initial reasoning and creating a pedagogical impasse.', 'probability', 'Sample space', 'Pedagogical approaches', 'PST', 'high school', 'ESM_Chernoff_2011_From', 'Egan J Chernoff & Rina Zazkis', '2011', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '21, 22, 28', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Chernoff_2011_From.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38939, 'When solving the problem of points, prospective teachers generate a sample set of ten possible future game-ending sequences and incorrectly assume these outcomes are equally likely.', 'Participants determined there were 6 possible ways for Fermat to win and 4 for Pascal, incorrectly concluding the pot should be split 60% to 40%.', 'probability', 'Problem of points', 'Equiprobability of future outcomes', 'PST', 'high school', 'ESM_Chernoff_2011_From', 'Egan J Chernoff & Rina Zazkis', '2011', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '26, 29, 30', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Chernoff_2011_From.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38940, 'Students incorrectly use the midrange strategy (averaging the extreme values) when estimating an average or total, rather than accounting for the full distribution of values.', 'Several students explained their ''average box'' as the mean of the emptiest and the fullest box of the grid.', 'statistics', 'Measures of center', 'Average and midrange', 'student', 'grades 7 and 8 (12-14-years old)', 'ESM_Bakker_2006_Historical', 'Arthur Bakker and Koeno P. E. Gravemeijer', '2006', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '154, 162', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bakker_2006_Historical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38941, 'Students mistake the median for the midrange when estimating its location within a graphical representation of a distribution.', 'When students in grade 8... had to estimate the median in a sketch of a right-skewed distribution, they initially mistook the median for the midrange.', 'statistics', 'Median', 'Graphical representation', 'student', 'grades 7 and 8 (12-14-years old)', 'ESM_Bakker_2006_Historical', 'Arthur Bakker and Koeno P. E. Gravemeijer', '2006', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '163, 164', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bakker_2006_Historical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38942, 'Students struggle to understand the mean as an abstract entity representing a population, incorrectly interpreting non-integer decimal averages as literal fragments of discrete objects.', 'When asked what it means that an average family consists of 2.5 people, many students thought ''the point 5 was a child.''', 'statistics', 'Mean', 'Continuous vs discrete values', 'student', 'grades 7 and 8 (12-14-years old)', 'ESM_Bakker_2006_Historical', 'Arthur Bakker and Koeno P. E. Gravemeijer', '2006', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '155, 165', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bakker_2006_Historical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38943, 'Students inappropriately use an additive strategy instead of a multiplicative one when solving ratio or enlargement problems where it is not applicable.', 'When asked to enlarge a letter K based on given units, students attempt to build it up additively instead of using a scalar multiplier, because ''little K''s cannot be added together to make a larger K''.', 'ratio', 'Ratio and enlargement', 'Additive strategy', 'student', '2nd, 3rd and 4th year children (14 year olds)', 'ESM_Kuchemann_1981_Cognitive', 'DIETMAR KÜCHEMANN', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '304', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Kuchemann_1981_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38944, 'Students interpret letters in algebraic expressions as objects or names of objects rather than representing numbers or unknown values.', '39 per cent of 14 year olds interpreted the algebraic expression 4c + 3b as ''4 cakes and 3 buns''.', 'algebraic', 'Algebraic variables', 'Meaning of letters', 'student', '2nd, 3rd and 4th year children (14 year olds)', 'ESM_Kuchemann_1981_Cognitive', 'DIETMAR KÜCHEMANN', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '307, 314', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Kuchemann_1981_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38945, 'Students interpret graphs as direct physical pictures of the situations they model, rather than as mathematical representations mapping abstract variables.', 'Children believed that the racing track corresponding to a speed-distance graph with nine dips in speed had nine physical bends rather than being triangular in shape.', 'algebraic', 'Kinematic graphs', 'Graph as a picture', 'student', '2nd, 3rd and 4th year children (14 year olds)', 'ESM_Kuchemann_1981_Cognitive', 'DIETMAR KÜCHEMANN', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '306, 307', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Kuchemann_1981_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38946, 'Students incorrectly add fractions by adding their numerators together and their denominators together when the fractions are presented abstractly.', 'Students tend to give the answer 5/16 when asked to add 3/8 and 2/8 unless the problem is framed in a realistic context.', 'fraction', 'Fraction addition', 'Adding numerators and denominators', 'student', '2nd, 3rd and 4th year children (14 year olds)', 'ESM_Kuchemann_1981_Cognitive', 'DIETMAR KÜCHEMANN', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'passing', '312', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Kuchemann_1981_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38947, 'Students incorrectly perform integer subtraction by simply taking the smaller absolute number from the larger one, ignoring the signs and operational order.', 'In subtractions like +6 - +8, instead of obtaining a negative result, students just take the smaller number from the larger.', 'integer', 'Integer subtraction', 'Sign ignoring', 'student', '2nd, 3rd and 4th year children (14 year olds)', 'ESM_Kuchemann_1981_Cognitive', 'DIETMAR KÜCHEMANN', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'passing', '312', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Kuchemann_1981_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38948, 'Young children compare the absolute number of payoff elements in two sets rather than integrating both dimensions to calculate the proportional relationship.', 'A child selects a set with 6 blue and 12 yellow beads over a set with 1 blue and 5 yellow beads (when yellow is the payoff color) simply by reasoning ''here there are more yellow units'', ignoring the ratios.', 'probability', 'Probability comparison', 'Absolute vs proportional reasoning', 'student', '4 to 11 years old', 'ESM_Falk_1980_Potential', 'RUMA FALK, RAPHAEL FALK AND IRIS LEVIN', '1980', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '182, 193, 194, 195', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Falk_1980_Potential.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38949, 'Children compute the difference between the number of payoff and non-payoff elements instead of their ratio to make a probability comparison.', 'A child utilizes an ''additive'' integration of dimensions, choosing the set with the larger difference in favor of the payoff color, rather than using proportional computation.', 'probability', 'Probability comparison', 'Difference rule vs ratio', 'student', '4 to 11 years old', 'ESM_Falk_1980_Potential', 'RUMA FALK, RAPHAEL FALK AND IRIS LEVIN', '1980', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '198', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Falk_1980_Potential.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38950, 'Children base their probabilistic choices on irrelevant, aesthetic, or superstitious features rather than mathematical probabilities.', 'A child consistently chooses the left roulette simply because she is left-handed, or demands to change the prize after three successive failures due to ''magic'' thinking.', 'probability', 'Decision making under uncertainty', 'Irrelevant criteria of choice', 'student', '4 to 11 years old', 'ESM_Falk_1980_Potential', 'RUMA FALK, RAPHAEL FALK AND IRIS LEVIN', '1980', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'uncoded', 'passing', '196, 197', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Falk_1980_Potential.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38951, 'Children fail to recognize the blindness of chance, erroneously believing that random operations can be wittingly directed or improved with age, strength, or practice.', 'A child claims that a big girl would win more prizes in a random lottery because ''she is smarter, she has got more brains'', or that playing with open eyes is easier because ''you can see where it reaches''.', 'probability', 'Chance', 'Chance versus determinism', 'student', '4 to 11 years old', 'ESM_Falk_1980_Potential', 'RUMA FALK, RAPHAEL FALK AND IRIS LEVIN', '1980', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '201, 202, 203', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Falk_1980_Potential.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38952, 'Students view data as a general pointer to an entire real-world event rather than a model that abstracts specific attributes.', 'When interpreting a graph of favorite colors, a student focuses on extraneous details of the surveying event, stating ''My shirt is blue. We know everyone''s name. We learned English and Chinese colors.''', 'statistics', 'Data representation', 'Nature of data', 'student', 'grades K-5, eighth grade, and high school seniors', 'ESM_Konold_2015_Data', 'Clifford Konold & Traci Higgins & Susan Jo Russell & Khalimahtul Khalil', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'uncoded', 'primary', '309, 310', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Konold_2015_Data.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38953, 'Students focus on identifying and reading off the attribute values of specific individuals rather than perceiving properties of the data set as a whole.', 'A student prefers a table of values over a scatterplot because it is easier to find a specific person''s exact plaque level without being confused by missing divisions.', 'statistics', 'Data analysis', 'Individual vs. Aggregate', 'student', 'grades K-5, eighth grade, and high school seniors', 'ESM_Konold_2015_Data', 'Clifford Konold & Traci Higgins & Susan Jo Russell & Khalimahtul Khalil', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'uncoded', 'primary', '310, 311, 312, 313', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Konold_2015_Data.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38954, 'Students confuse the numeric value of a case with the frequency of cases with that value, particularly when both are integers.', 'When looking at a case-value plot where a stack of 5 ''x''s represents a single family with 5 members, a peer incorrectly interprets it as 5 separate families.', 'statistics', 'Graph interpretation', 'Frequencies vs. Values', 'student', 'grades K-5, eighth grade, and high school seniors', 'ESM_Konold_2015_Data', 'Clifford Konold & Traci Higgins & Susan Jo Russell & Khalimahtul Khalil', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '316, 317', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Konold_2015_Data.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38955, 'Students interpret the statistical term ''average'' as an adjective characterizing a single typical individual or subset, rather than a noun representing a measure of the entire group.', 'When asked for the average height of the kids in the room, a student responds, ''It''s me. I think I am average,'' while another rejects a calculated average distance of 169 cm because ''I didn''t get 169 as one of my distances. It''s a lie!''', 'statistics', 'Measures of center', 'Average', 'student', 'grades K-5, eighth grade, and high school seniors', 'ESM_Konold_2015_Data', 'Clifford Konold & Traci Higgins & Susan Jo Russell & Khalimahtul Khalil', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '320, 321', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Konold_2015_Data.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38956, 'Students struggle with the dual nature of quotients, exhibiting conflict over whether division should produce a quotient and a remainder or a single decimal quotient.', 'For the division of 6 by 5, the class is unable to decide between the two proposed answers: ''1 remainder 1'' and ''1 point 2''.', 'rational', 'Division', 'Quotient representation', 'student', 'fifth graders', 'ESM_Fluckiger_2005_Macro-situation', 'ANNICK FLUCKIGER', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '67', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fluckiger_2005_Macro-situation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38957, 'Students process long division by incorrectly applying a digit-by-digit Euclidean division, failing to carry remainders to the next place value and mistakenly inserting multiple decimal points into the quotient.', 'When dividing 8645834 by 2, a student divides 5 by 2 to yield ''2.5'' in the middle of the quotient, and similarly divides 3 by 2 to yield ''1.5'', failing to abide by the place notation system.', 'decimal', 'Division algorithm', 'Partial quotients and remainders', 'student', 'fifth graders', 'ESM_Fluckiger_2005_Macro-situation', 'ANNICK FLUCKIGER', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '67, 68', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fluckiger_2005_Macro-situation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38958, 'Students incorrectly break down both the dividend and divisor into additions to isolate simplified quotients, misapplying the distributivity of multiplication.', 'A student erroneously simplifies 346 / 123 into 300 / 100 = 3; 40 / 20 = 2; 6 / 3 = 2, giving a result of 322.', 'whole_number', 'Division algorithm', 'Distributivity', 'student', 'fifth graders', 'ESM_Fluckiger_2005_Macro-situation', 'ANNICK FLUCKIGER', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '75, 76', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fluckiger_2005_Macro-situation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38959, 'Students overgeneralize a previous solution involving repeating decimals, incorrectly assuming that any difficult division problem will result in an infinite decimal.', 'When solving 147097 / 7, a student proposes, ''You have to put a decimal point and then nines to infinity, as LI did for a hundred divided by three.''', 'decimal', 'Division', 'Infinite repeating decimals', 'student', 'fifth graders', 'ESM_Fluckiger_2005_Macro-situation', 'ANNICK FLUCKIGER', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '76, 77', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fluckiger_2005_Macro-situation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38960, 'Students use natural number rules to order fractions, choosing the incorrect inequality sign because they reason based solely on the magnitude of the denominators.', 'When such pupils will have to complete an inequality like 1/8 ... 1/5 with the correct ordering sign (<), this dominance of meaning will manifest itself in the choice of the ''>''-sign, because - as they reason - ''8 is greater than 5''.', 'fraction', 'Fraction comparison', 'Ordering', 'student', 'young children', 'ESM_Streefland_1978_Some', 'Leen Streefland', '1978', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '51', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Streefland_1978_Some.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38961, 'Children face difficulties determining a fractional part of a discrete quantity due to the over-emphasis on the cardinal aspect of natural numbers in early education.', 'Coen faces some difficulties when he has to determine a fractional part of a discrete quantity. ... Taking this fact into account he must be misled, as soon as a question concerning fractions starts with: ''How many...''', 'fraction', 'Fractions of discrete quantities', '', 'student', 'young children', 'ESM_Streefland_1978_Some', 'Leen Streefland', '1978', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '63', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Streefland_1978_Some.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38962, 'Young children struggle to technically execute the subdivision of a continuous area while simultaneously maintaining the equivalence of the parts and the complete exhaustion of the whole.', 'He draws one wedge of pancake and apologizes: ''I am not sure they will all fit.'' ... He decides to subdivide the rest of the pancake in approximately equal wedges, so the final result is: 12 pieces [instead of 7].', 'fraction', 'Subdivision of area', 'Equivalence and exhaustion', 'student', 'young children', 'ESM_Streefland_1978_Some', 'Leen Streefland', '1978', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '55, 56', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Streefland_1978_Some.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38963, 'A child believes that fractions cannot be applied to discrete, countable quantities because they are perceived as indivisible.', 'When told they were away for ''three weeks and a half'', the boy replied, ''Huh, that''s impossible. One week does not have a half. Look here, a week has seven days and seven does not have a half''.', 'fraction', 'Fractions of discrete quantities', 'Continuous vs discrete', 'student', 'young children', 'ESM_Streefland_1978_Some', 'Leen Streefland', '1978', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'secondary', '69', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Streefland_1978_Some.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38964, 'Students interpret the decimal portion of a number as a whole number, judging decimals as larger if they appear larger when read ignoring the decimal point.', 'Students think 0.45 may be judged greater than 0.6.', 'decimal', 'Decimal comparison', 'Magnitude', 'mixed', 'Year 5 and Year 7', 'ESM_Stacey_2001_Effect', 'Kaye Stacey, Sue Helme, Shona Archer and Caroline Condon', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '212, 214', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Stacey_2001_Effect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38965, 'Students struggle to correctly enumerate the volume of multibase blocks, often simply counting visible faces rather than comprehending the 3D volume relationships that represent base ten values.', 'For a 2 x 2 x 2 cube with no hidden, interior minis nearly half the children, when asked to state the number of minis, simply counted visible squares.', 'measurement', 'Volume enumeration', 'Base ten blocks', 'mixed', 'Year 5 and Year 7', 'ESM_Stacey_2001_Effect', 'Kaye Stacey, Sue Helme, Shona Archer and Caroline Condon', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '208, 215', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Stacey_2001_Effect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38966, 'Students fail to recognize the continuous nature of decimals, such as number density, when their understanding is bound to discrete volume-based models.', 'Following instruction with MAB, students'' performance improved on discrete-context tasks but did not improve on continuous-representation tasks, such as finding a number between two decimals.', 'decimal', 'Number density', 'Continuous vs discrete', 'mixed', 'Year 5 and Year 7', 'ESM_Stacey_2001_Effect', 'Kaye Stacey, Sue Helme, Shona Archer and Caroline Condon', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '206, 216', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Stacey_2001_Effect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38967, 'When base-ten blocks are repurposed for decimals, students experience confusion remembering the new names and values of the components due to prior associations with whole numbers.', 'A Year 5 child in the MAB group was confused to such an extent that he named minis ''ones'' and the large blocks ''thousandths.''', 'decimal', 'Decimal models', 'Component naming and values', 'mixed', 'Year 5 and Year 7', 'ESM_Stacey_2001_Effect', 'Kaye Stacey, Sue Helme, Shona Archer and Caroline Condon', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '207, 214', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Stacey_2001_Effect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38968, 'Students may erroneously conflate the physical properties of a place value model''s framework with the numerical values it is meant to represent.', 'Children may erroneously learn that the rod holding the tenths pieces has the value of one tenth, rather than the much shorter pieces that it holds.', 'decimal', 'Place value representations', 'Physical models', 'mixed', 'Year 5 and Year 7', 'ESM_Stacey_2001_Effect', 'Kaye Stacey, Sue Helme, Shona Archer and Caroline Condon', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '210, 211', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Stacey_2001_Effect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38969, 'Students rely on ritualistic, syntactic manipulation of digits to arrive at a target answer, ignoring the place value or logical origin of the numbers.', 'To explain how 25 + 36 equals 61, Mina suggested taking the 1 from the 11 (the sum of 5 and 6) and placing it next to the 6 from the 36.', 'whole_number', 'Addition', 'Place value', 'student', 'third grade', 'ESM_HeydMetzuyanim_2016_Between', 'Einat Heyd-Metzuyanim & Mellony Graven', '2016', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '362, 363', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_HeydMetzuyanim_2016_Between.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38970, 'Students fail to objectify base-ten structures, relying instead on counting by ones to add ten to a two-digit number.', 'When asked to add 10 to 92, Mina counted on her fingers from 92 to 102 instead of recognizing the base-ten pattern.', 'whole_number', 'Addition', 'Adding 10', 'student', 'third grade', 'ESM_HeydMetzuyanim_2016_Between', 'Einat Heyd-Metzuyanim & Mellony Graven', '2016', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'secondary', '356, 357', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_HeydMetzuyanim_2016_Between.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38971, 'Students participate in ritual instruction by chorusing answers based on the teacher''s tone and contextual cues rather than mathematical reasoning, leading to collective factual errors.', 'The class chorused ''8000'' as the number that comes before 9000, and changed their chorused response to whether a number was ''the same'' from ''no'' to ''yes'' simply because the teacher repeated the question.', 'whole_number', 'Number sequence', 'Identifying preceding numbers', 'student', 'third grade', 'ESM_HeydMetzuyanim_2016_Between', 'Einat Heyd-Metzuyanim & Mellony Graven', '2016', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '364, 365', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_HeydMetzuyanim_2016_Between.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38972, 'Pupils incorrectly apply natural number arithmetic rules to operations with rational numbers, such as adding numerators and denominators separately.', 'Pupils assume that 2/3 + 3/5 = 5/8 because they add the numerators (2+3=5) and denominators (3+5=8) separately.', 'fraction', 'Fraction addition', 'Natural number bias', 'PST', 'elementary school', 'ESM_Depaepe_2018_Stimulating', 'Fien Depaepe & Patrick Van Roy & Joke Torbeyns & Thilo Kleickmann & Wim Van Dooren & Lieven Verschaffel', '2018', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '197, 198, 204', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Depaepe_2018_Stimulating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38973, 'Pupils rely on their prior knowledge of natural numbers to compare rational numbers, incorrectly assuming that fractions with larger numerical components are larger overall.', 'A pupil incorrectly assumes that 14/57 > 1/3 since 14 > 1 and 57 > 3.', 'fraction', 'Fraction comparison', 'Natural number bias', 'PST', 'elementary school', 'ESM_Depaepe_2018_Stimulating', 'Fien Depaepe & Patrick Van Roy & Joke Torbeyns & Thilo Kleickmann & Wim Van Dooren & Lieven Verschaffel', '2018', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '198', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Depaepe_2018_Stimulating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38974, 'Pre-service teachers struggle with the conceptual meaning behind rational number operations, often inventing incorrect story problems or applying wrong operations.', 'When asked to invent a story problem for 34.99 x 0.30, a pre-service teacher incorrectly wrote ''Divide EUR 34.99 across 30 children''.', 'rational', 'Operations with rational numbers', 'Conceptual meaning of operations', 'PST', 'elementary school', 'ESM_Depaepe_2018_Stimulating', 'Fien Depaepe & Patrick Van Roy & Joke Torbeyns & Thilo Kleickmann & Wim Van Dooren & Lieven Verschaffel', '2018', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '198, 199, 209, 210', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Depaepe_2018_Stimulating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38975, 'Students face epistemological obstacles when the growth of a semiotic system necessitates the negation of existing rules, such as having to unlearn that subtracting a larger number from a smaller one is impossible.', 'For a young child mastering elementary calculation, the task 3-4 is impossible. But later it has a determinate answer: 3 - 4 = -1.', 'integer', 'Subtraction', 'Negative numbers', 'other', '', 'ESM_Ernest_2006_Semiotic', 'PAUL ERNEST', '2006', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '76', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Ernest_2006_Semiotic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38976, 'Students form the rule that multiplication always increases magnitude, which acts as an epistemological obstacle when the number domain is expanded to include fractions and zero.', 'In early multiplication tasks children learn implicitly or explicitly that ''multiplying always makes bigger'', a rule that is contradicted when the domain is expanded to include fractional and decimal numbers.', 'whole_number', 'Multiplication', 'Effect on magnitude', 'other', '', 'ESM_Ernest_2006_Semiotic', 'PAUL ERNEST', '2006', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '76', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Ernest_2006_Semiotic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38977, 'Children interpret ''half'' as generically meaning ''a little bit'' or a smaller number, leading them to erroneously conclude that a number like ''seven and a half'' is smaller than ''seven''.', 'Monique explained that when you are seven and a half, ''you''re not seven but you''re a little bit seven.'' She also explained that seven and a half is less than seven.', 'fraction', 'Magnitude of fractions', 'Meaning of half', 'student', 'Kindergarten and first grade', 'ESM_Brizuela_2005_Young', 'BARBARA M. BRIZUELA', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '297, 298, 299, 300', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Brizuela_2005_Young.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38978, 'Children use a plus sign as a ''dummy number'' or placeholder for ''half'' in their written notations because they know a fraction must be represented by something, even if they don''t know the conventional symbol.', 'When asked to make a notation for six and a half, Sheila wrote ''6+'', but explicitly stated that six and a half is less than six, showing the plus sign was not meant to denote addition.', 'fraction', 'Notation for fractions', 'Invented symbols', 'student', 'Kindergarten and first grade', 'ESM_Brizuela_2005_Young', 'BARBARA M. BRIZUELA', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '292, 293', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Brizuela_2005_Young.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38979, 'Children physically cross out or strike a line through a written whole number to represent a half, attempting to symbolically capture the physical action of halving the quantity.', 'Taylor crossed out the numeral seven to show ''seven and a half'', visually representing a seven cut in half rather than seven whole units plus an additional half unit.', 'fraction', 'Notation for fractions', 'Partitioning numerals', 'student', 'Kindergarten and first grade', 'ESM_Brizuela_2005_Young', 'BARBARA M. BRIZUELA', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '293, 294', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Brizuela_2005_Young.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38980, 'Children incorrectly overgeneralize partial features of conventional fraction notation by appending a line and the number 2 beneath any whole number when they hear the word ''half''.', 'Lucas wrote 7/2 to mean ''seven and a half'', explaining ''I know to put a 2 cause one and a half has a 2.''', 'fraction', 'Notation for fractions', 'Overgeneralization of denominator', 'student', 'Kindergarten and first grade', 'ESM_Brizuela_2005_Young', 'BARBARA M. BRIZUELA', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '291, 292', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Brizuela_2005_Young.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38981, 'Children can correctly order mixed numbers abstractly but fail to map this continuous magnitude onto a timeline of age, believing two different ages can occur simultaneously.', 'Haley stated that seven and a half is more than seven, but when asked about her birthday, she said that on the very same day she would be both seven and seven and a half.', 'fraction', 'Measurement of time', 'Continuous magnitude and timelines', 'student', 'Kindergarten and first grade', 'ESM_Brizuela_2005_Young', 'BARBARA M. BRIZUELA', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'secondary', '298', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Brizuela_2005_Young.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38982, 'Students initially declare a disjunction false if one of its components is false, either failing to distinguish the truth-value of the part from the whole or interpreting the statement pragmatically as a claim of uncertainty.', 'Students decide the statement ''The integer 15 is even or 15 is odd'' is false simply because ''15 is even'' is false.', 'discrete', 'Mathematical logic', 'Disjunctions', 'student', 'undergraduate calculus 3 students', 'ESM_Dawkins_2017_Guiding', 'Paul Christian Dawkins & John Paul Cook', '2017', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '247, 248, 249', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Dawkins_2017_Guiding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38983, 'Students reject true mathematical disjunctions because they contain extraneous or unnecessary conditions, applying conversational pragmatics instead of formal logic.', 'Students state that ''Given any two real numbers x and y, x <= y or y <= x'' is false under the assumption that x != y, because the equality condition is extraneous.', 'discrete', 'Mathematical logic', 'Disjunctions', 'student', 'undergraduate calculus 3 students', 'ESM_Dawkins_2017_Guiding', 'Paul Christian Dawkins & John Paul Cook', '2017', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '248', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Dawkins_2017_Guiding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38984, 'Students inappropriately rephrase given disjunctions by substituting semantic relationships between properties, which can inadvertently change the logical meaning or render the statement a tautology.', 'A student substitutes the property ''is not acute'' with ''is obtuse'', or replaces ''is equilateral'' based on the warrant that ''equilateral triangles are acute'', leading them to incorrectly rephrase a geometric disjunction.', 'geometric', 'Mathematical logic', 'Disjunctions', 'student', 'undergraduate calculus 3 students', 'ESM_Dawkins_2017_Guiding', 'Paul Christian Dawkins & John Paul Cook', '2017', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '252', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Dawkins_2017_Guiding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38985, 'Students adopt an exclusive interpretation of ''or'', declaring a disjunction false if both of its component parts are true.', 'Students reject a disjunction where both conditions hold because using ''and'' seems more pragmatically appropriate.', 'discrete', 'Mathematical logic', 'Disjunctions', 'student', 'undergraduate calculus 3 students', 'ESM_Dawkins_2017_Guiding', 'Paul Christian Dawkins & John Paul Cook', '2017', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '252', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Dawkins_2017_Guiding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38986, 'Students misinterpret the magnitude of decimal numbers by processing the digits to the right of the decimal point as if they were whole numbers or by being confused by different numbers of digits.', 'Students believe that 1.20 > 1.2, or that 1.02 > 1.1 because 2 > 1, and struggle to identify which number is closest to 1.003 when given options with varying numbers of decimal places.', 'decimal', 'Decimal magnitude and comparison', 'Place value', 'student', '6th, 7th, 8th, 9th and 10th classes (ages 13-17)', 'ESM_Ekenstam_1977_Children''s', 'Adolf Af Ekenstam', '1977', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '317, 318, 321, 322, 327', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Ekenstam_1977_Children''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38987, 'Students view fractions strictly as concrete operations (e.g., sharing objects) rather than as quantitative values, leading to an inability to equate fractions with decimals or place them on a number line.', 'Students associate 1/3 strictly with ''one third of a cake'' but fail to mark the point corresponding to 1/2 or 3/4 on a number line ranging from 0 to 1.', 'fraction', 'Fraction magnitude and representation', 'Number lines and decimal equivalence', 'student', '6th, 7th, 8th, 9th and 10th classes (ages 13-17)', 'ESM_Ekenstam_1977_Children''s', 'Adolf Af Ekenstam', '1977', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '317, 325, 327', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Ekenstam_1977_Children''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38988, 'Students mechanically perform algorithmic procedures for solving equations without conceptual understanding, failing to notice absurd results or understand the purpose of checking their work.', 'After performing a solving procedure, children might not notice that the result is absurd. If they have learned to test the result it can happen that they do not know whether this is needed because it is not sure that the result is the solution... or merely in order to see whether they didn''t commit some computational mistakes.', 'algebraic', 'Solving equations', 'Algorithmic procedures', 'student', '', 'ESM_Surany_1977_Have', 'János Surány', '1977', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '37, 38', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Surany_1977_Have.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38989, 'Students erroneously apply the ''median'' of two fractions operation (adding numerators together and denominators together) instead of the standard addition algorithm.', 'sometimes pupils apply this operation instead of addition.', 'fraction', 'Fraction addition', 'Median of fractions', 'student', '', 'ESM_Surany_1977_Have', 'János Surány', '1977', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '36, 39', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Surany_1977_Have.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38990, 'Students misunderstand the use of parentheses, treating them as rigid algebraic rules rather than conventions for the order of operations.', '', 'algebraic', 'Order of operations', 'Parentheses', 'student', '', 'ESM_Surany_1977_Have', 'János Surány', '1977', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '36', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Surany_1977_Have.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38991, 'Preservice teachers confuse volume with area when presented with 3-dimensional shapes, identifying the empty space inside or the edges as the area.', '...five of the students revealed that they confused volume with area by indicating either the empty space inside or the edges, saying that the area could be calculated by measuring the height, width and length.', 'measurement', 'Area and volume concepts', '3-dimensional shapes', 'PST', 'first-year teacher education students', 'ESM_Baturo_1996_Student', 'ANNETTE BATURO and ROD NASON', '1996', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '251, 257', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Baturo_1996_Student.pdf')).
