research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38993, 'Students mistakenly believe that there are 100 square centimeters in a square meter because there are 100 centimeters in a meter.', '...all of the students but two said that it was more than 1 square meter because there''s 100 cm in a meter.', 'measurement', 'Area conversions', 'Square units', 'PST', 'first-year teacher education students', 'ESM_Baturo_1996_Student', 'ANNETTE BATURO and ROD NASON', '1996', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '253, 257', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Baturo_1996_Student.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38994, 'Students literally interpret the notation for square units, reading terms like ''6 m^2'' as ''6 meters squared'' instead of ''6 square meters'', which can lead to confusion about the shape''s dimensions.', 'All of the other students read it literally as ''6 meters squared''.', 'measurement', 'Area notation', 'Reading square units', 'PST', 'first-year teacher education students', 'ESM_Baturo_1996_Student', 'ANNETTE BATURO and ROD NASON', '1996', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '259, 267', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Baturo_1996_Student.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38995, 'Students equate the existence of area strictly with the act of measuring it, believing that non-polygonal shapes or unmeasured entities lack an area.', 'Another student selected only the polygons because they had ''four sides''. These three students appeared to associate the notion of area with the measurement of area (i.e., area does not exist until it is measured).', 'measurement', 'Definition of area', 'Existence of area vs measurement', 'PST', 'first-year teacher education students', 'ESM_Baturo_1996_Student', 'ANNETTE BATURO and ROD NASON', '1996', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '251', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Baturo_1996_Student.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38996, 'Students attempt to calculate the area of a triangle by mechanically halving the base and multiplying by the height without any conceptual understanding of the triangle''s relationship to an enclosing rectangle.', 'Three students halved the base and then multiplied by the perpendicular height, indicating that they had not had any experiences that related triangles and rectangles.', 'geometric', 'Area of a triangle', 'Formula derivation', 'PST', 'first-year teacher education students', 'ESM_Baturo_1996_Student', 'ANNETTE BATURO and ROD NASON', '1996', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '256', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Baturo_1996_Student.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38997, 'Students inappropriately restrict the domain of a valid general algebraic formula to avoid dealing with intermediate decimal calculations during division.', 'G3-1 stated that her general formula for calculating triangular numbers, (position + 1) ÷ 2 × position, only functions for odd triangular numbers, seemingly to avoid decimal results from dividing an odd number by 2.', 'algebraic', 'Algebraic formulas', 'Domain restriction', 'student', 'first grade of secondary school and third grade of secondary school', 'ESM_Hitt_2017_Rupture', 'Fernando Hitt & Mireille Saboya & Carlos Cortés Zavala & GRUTEAM', '2017', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '111, 112', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hitt_2017_Rupture.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38998, 'Students demonstrate fragile knowledge retention of self-derived algebraic formulas over time and fail to verify their processes, leading to the use of incorrect expressions.', 'G4-1, the most advanced pupil, used an incorrect formula when calculating the 11th triangular number 45 days after the experiment, having forgotten the correct formula he previously generated.', 'algebraic', 'Algebraic formulas', 'Knowledge retention', 'student', 'first grade of secondary school and third grade of secondary school', 'ESM_Hitt_2017_Rupture', 'Fernando Hitt & Mireille Saboya & Carlos Cortés Zavala & GRUTEAM', '2017', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '109, 110, 113', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hitt_2017_Rupture.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(38999, 'Students incorrectly hypothesize that pressing the multiply and equals keys in sequence on a calculator doubles the input number rather than squaring it.', 'When asked what happens when pressing ''2, x, ='', children assume it is doubled, and subsequently expect ''1, x, ='' to produce 2 instead of 1.', 'whole_number', 'Calculator operations', 'Squaring vs. doubling', 'student', '10-year-old children', 'ESM_Fielker_1987_Calculator', 'DAVID S. FIELKER', '1987', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '419', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fielker_1987_Calculator.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39000, 'Students struggle to recognize the relationship between certain decimals and their fraction equivalents when exploring reciprocals on a calculator.', 'When putting large numbers into a ''divide-equals'' function and getting answers like 0.01, children fail to recognize the output as a fraction (one-hundredth) until prompted.', 'decimal', 'Decimals and fractions', 'Reciprocals', 'student', '10-year-old children', 'ESM_Fielker_1987_Calculator', 'DAVID S. FIELKER', '1987', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '426, 427', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fielker_1987_Calculator.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39001, 'When asked to find how many times a factor goes into a number, students rely on additive or multiplicative trial-and-error strategies instead of utilizing division.', 'To find how many twos go into 52, a student reasons additively: ''Cos two twenty fives are 50, and another one makes 52, and if you share out the other 2 you get 26,'' rather than dividing 52 by 2.', 'whole_number', 'Factors', 'Division vs addition', 'student', '10-year-old children', 'ESM_Fielker_1987_Calculator', 'DAVID S. FIELKER', '1987', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'secondary', '432, 433', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fielker_1987_Calculator.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39002, 'Students amalgamate the concepts of area, perimeter, and shape, believing that area is intrinsically tied to the shape''s form or that increasing a shape''s perimeter necessarily increases its area.', 'When asked to modify a surface to decrease its area and increase its perimeter, some students drew a similar shape inside the original (decreasing both) and then attempted to ''add perimeter'' by doubling the inner border.', 'measurement', 'Area and Perimeter', 'Dissociation of magnitudes', 'student', 'CM1 (9-10 ans) et un CM2 (10-11 ans)', 'ESM_Douady_1989_Un', 'Regine Douady and Marie-Jeanne Perrin-Glorian', '1989', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '387, 394, 395, 405, 406, 407', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Douady_1989_Un.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39003, 'Students erroneously extend area formulas to situations where they are invalid, such as multiplying all the dimensions of a shape together.', 'To calculate the area of a triangle with dimensions a, b, and c, students proposed multiplying the three dimensions (a x b x c) or calculating (a + b + c) x 3.', 'measurement', 'Area formulas', 'Triangles and parallelograms', 'student', 'CM1 (9-10 ans) et un CM2 (10-11 ans)', 'ESM_Douady_1989_Un', 'Regine Douady and Marie-Jeanne Perrin-Glorian', '1989', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '395, 409, 410, 411', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Douady_1989_Un.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39004, 'Students hold contradictory convictions regarding the conservation of area and lengths during the continuous deformation of a shape, such as tilting a parallelogram.', 'Students believe that ''straightening'' a tilted parallelogram into a rectangle conserves its area but changes its heights, or that a parallelogram with 1 cm sides has an area of 1 cm² regardless of its tilt.', 'measurement', 'Area conservation', 'Deformation of figures', 'student', 'CM1 (9-10 ans) et un CM2 (10-11 ans)', 'ESM_Douady_1989_Un', 'Regine Douady and Marie-Jeanne Perrin-Glorian', '1989', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'uncoded', 'primary', '410, 412, 413, 414, 421', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Douady_1989_Un.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39005, 'Students believe that expressing the area of a surface in a specific unit (like cm²) strictly requires the ability to physically pave the surface with shapes of that exact unit.', 'Students encounter difficulties expressing the area of a triangle in cm² because they cannot physically pave the triangle with square tiles.', 'measurement', 'Area measurement', 'Paving and units', 'student', 'CM1 (9-10 ans) et un CM2 (10-11 ans)', 'ESM_Douady_1989_Un', 'Regine Douady and Marie-Jeanne Perrin-Glorian', '1989', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '394, 418', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Douady_1989_Un.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39006, 'Children perceive the continuous variation of an area during deformation in a discontinuous manner, believing the area remains constant until it nears an extreme limit.', 'When tilting a parallelogram, students initially think the area does not change, but then suddenly perceive it as becoming very small only when it is close to being completely flattened.', 'geometric', 'Area variation', 'Continuity', 'student', 'CM1 (9-10 ans) et un CM2 (10-11 ans)', 'ESM_Douady_1989_Un', 'Regine Douady and Marie-Jeanne Perrin-Glorian', '1989', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '394, 413', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Douady_1989_Un.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39007, 'Children suffer from a ''loss of whole'' when quantifying shares from multiple partitioned objects, determining the denominator by counting the total number of pieces across all objects rather than the number of pieces making up one whole unit.', 'If two objects were each partitioned into four pieces, the total number of pieces would be eight, leading the child to incorrectly name the fraction ''eighths''.', 'fraction', 'Naming and quantifying fractions', 'Identifying the whole', 'student', 'Year Three / third grade students (aged between 7.9 and 8.3 years)', 'ESM_Charles_2000_Young', 'KATHY CHARLES and ROD NASON', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '214, 215', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Charles_2000_Young.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39008, 'Children inappropriately convert a continuous region model task into a discrete set model task after distributing pieces, identifying a share as one part out of the total separate elements distributed rather than a fraction of the original whole object.', 'After Claudia distributed four half-pizzas to four people, she incorrectly quantified each person''s share as 1/4 because there were four separate pieces in total on the plates.', 'fraction', 'Quantifying fractions', 'Region vs. set models', 'student', 'Year Three / third grade students (aged between 7.9 and 8.3 years)', 'ESM_Charles_2000_Young', 'KATHY CHARLES and ROD NASON', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '204, 205', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Charles_2000_Young.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39009, 'Children draw on real-world social practices of food sharing to partition circular objects into many unequal pieces, attempting to balance visual area instead of creating mathematically equal fractional parts.', 'Ben cut a pizza into many unequal pieces and shared them as evenly by area as he could, making formal quantification of the shares impossible.', 'fraction', 'Partitioning', 'Equal shares vs. visual area', 'student', 'Year Three / third grade students (aged between 7.9 and 8.3 years)', 'ESM_Charles_2000_Young', 'KATHY CHARLES and ROD NASON', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '206, 215', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Charles_2000_Young.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39010, 'When quantifying a share composed of multiple pieces, children confuse the number of pieces in that specific share with the denominator for the whole object.', 'Ben cut a pizza into eighths and dealt 4 pieces to each person, but incorrectly quantified each person''s share as 1/4 instead of 4/8, confusing the 4 pieces in the share with the number of pieces in the whole.', 'fraction', 'Naming and quantifying fractions', 'Numerator vs. denominator confusion', 'student', 'Year Three / third grade students (aged between 7.9 and 8.3 years)', 'ESM_Charles_2000_Young', 'KATHY CHARLES and ROD NASON', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '204', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Charles_2000_Young.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39011, 'Children successfully partition an object into the correct number of pieces to share evenly, but are unable to recognize that the number of people dictates the fraction name, preventing them from quantifying the shares.', 'Candice partitioned a pizza into three equal portions for three people, but was unable to accurately quantify each share because she did not link the number of people to the fraction name ''thirds''.', 'fraction', 'Quantifying fractions', 'Mapping people to fraction name', 'student', 'Year Three / third grade students (aged between 7.9 and 8.3 years)', 'ESM_Charles_2000_Young', 'KATHY CHARLES and ROD NASON', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '200, 201', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Charles_2000_Young.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39012, 'Students rely on faulty intuitive assumptions regarding geometric scaling, erroneously believing that proportionally enlarging the radius of a massive sphere requires a vastly larger circumference increase than it actually does.', 'Most people... experience a compelling intuitive sense that ''a lot'' of extra string is needed [to place a string on 1.8 meter poles around the Earth].', 'geometric', 'Circumference', 'Scaling and intuition', 'other', '', 'ESM_Arcavi_2003_Role', 'ABRAHAM ARCAVI', '2003', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '221, 222', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Arcavi_2003_Role.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39013, 'When interpreting computer-generated graphs, students often base their observations on visually salient software artifacts rather than the underlying mathematical properties of the functions.', 'Students ''noticed'' the way the software draws the lines as ''starting'' from the bottom of the screen, or talked about the degree of jaggedness of the lines.', 'algebraic', 'Graphing linear functions', 'Interpreting graphical software', 'other', '', 'ESM_Arcavi_2003_Role', 'ABRAHAM ARCAVI', '2003', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '230, 231, 232', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Arcavi_2003_Role.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39014, 'When confronted with assessment tasks that closely resemble textbook exercises, students view the tasks as requiring only recalled facts or algorithms (Imitative Reasoning) rather than conceptual, mathematically founded reasoning.', 'Camilla directly applies differentiation rules to find f''(x) for a polynomial, stating ''its only the rules that one has learned in the head that you use,'' without any conceptual argumentation.', 'calculus', 'Derivatives', 'Differentiation rules', 'student', '16 or 17 years of age', 'ESM_Boesen_2010_Relation', 'Jesper Boesen, Johan Lithner, Torulf Palm', '2010', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '93, 103, 104', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Boesen_2010_Relation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39015, 'Students attempt to solve unfamiliar tasks by arbitrarily selecting a known algorithm that shares superficial features with the task, without understanding the intrinsic mathematical appropriateness of the algorithm.', 'Beatrice notices a 90-degree angle in a triangle problem and blindly sets up proportional equations for similar triangles, stating ''I just took the sides BA divided by the same side... but I don''t really understand.''', 'geometric', 'Problem solving', 'Algorithm selection', 'student', '16 or 17 years of age', 'ESM_Boesen_2010_Relation', 'Jesper Boesen, Johan Lithner, Torulf Palm', '2010', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '94, 101', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Boesen_2010_Relation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39016, 'Students confuse a trigonometric function with its argument (the angle) and fail to distinguish between the inverse of a trigonometric function and its algebraic reciprocal.', 'Diana does not distinguish between the angle v and sin v in calculations, nor between arcsine (sin^-1 v) and the reciprocal (sin v)^-1.', 'other', 'Trigonometry', 'Sine and arcsine', 'student', '16 or 17 years of age', 'ESM_Boesen_2010_Relation', 'Jesper Boesen, Johan Lithner, Torulf Palm', '2010', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '101, 102', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Boesen_2010_Relation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39017, 'Students ignore the direction of change when calculating secant slopes, failing to assign a negative sign to a decrease, and misapply differentiation rules like using the power rule for exponential functions.', 'Carl incorrectly differentiates 34 * 0.95^t as 34 * 0.95^-t and calculates a secant slope as 2/4 = 0.5 without initially accounting for the negative change in y.', 'calculus', 'Derivatives and slopes', 'Exponential functions and decreasing functions', 'student', '16 or 17 years of age', 'ESM_Boesen_2010_Relation', 'Jesper Boesen, Johan Lithner, Torulf Palm', '2010', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'mixed', 'secondary', '95, 96', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Boesen_2010_Relation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39018, 'Students rely on everyday meanings of ''in proportion'', judging similarity based on superficial visual resemblance or single attributes rather than mathematical multiplicative relationships.', 'Toby picked answers (a) and (c) for the I task, believing the Is were in proportion just because they were the same letter and the same amount was added to each part.', 'proportional', 'Meaning of proportion', 'Everyday vs mathematical definitions', 'student', '13-year-old pupils', 'ESM_Hoyles_1992_Pedagogy', 'CELIA HOYLES AND RICHARD NOSS', '1992', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '38, 39, 40', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hoyles_1992_Pedagogy.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39019, 'Students hold the situated abstraction that multiplication always results in a larger number, acting surprised when multiplying by a decimal less than one reduces the outcome.', 'When trying to reach a target of 100 from a starting value of 13, Jenny initially did not use decimals between 0 and 1 to reduce numbers until she faced 117 and tried 0.2.', 'decimal', 'Multiplication', 'Multiplying by a decimal', 'student', '13-year-old pupils', 'ESM_Hoyles_1992_Pedagogy', 'CELIA HOYLES AND RICHARD NOSS', '1992', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '41, 42, 43', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hoyles_1992_Pedagogy.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39020, 'When asked to construct a proportional enlargement of a shape, students use an additive strategy by adding a constant amount to each side length instead of using a multiplicative scale factor.', 'Toby and David added 8 to the roof length to get the new roof length (since the original roof was 8 more than the original vertical side), fixing on this additive strategy.', 'proportional', 'Enlargement', 'Additive strategies', 'student', '13-year-old pupils', 'ESM_Hoyles_1992_Pedagogy', 'CELIA HOYLES AND RICHARD NOSS', '1992', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '47, 48, 52', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hoyles_1992_Pedagogy.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39021, 'Students treat computer drawing tasks perceptually rather than analytically, closing visual gaps iteratively rather than recognizing the necessity of a multiplicative relationship.', 'When an additive strategy left a gap at the base of the house, Toby and David guessed the bottom line length as 106.95 to visually close it, avoiding mathematical analysis.', 'proportional', 'Proportional figures', 'Iterative visual adjustment', 'student', '13-year-old pupils', 'ESM_Hoyles_1992_Pedagogy', 'CELIA HOYLES AND RICHARD NOSS', '1992', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '47, 48, 52', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hoyles_1992_Pedagogy.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39022, 'Students make apparent mathematical mistakes because they are interpreting the mathematical situation through a competing, non-mathematical discursive practice, such as story-telling or home practices.', '', 'other', 'Early mathematics', 'Relational terms', 'other', '', 'ESM_Pimm_1991_Review', 'David Pimm', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '394', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Pimm_1991_Review.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39023, 'Students project personal and emotional family dynamics onto mathematical objects when asked to provide a mathematical explanation, violating the expected disembodied mathematical discourse.', '''My favourite fraction is 4/5. This is my favourite fraction because it gives me a lot of things to remember. Because there are five people in my family, and only 4 of them are living in my house. My mom is the fifth person. She''s the one that is gone.''', 'fraction', 'Fractions', 'Meaning of fractions', 'other', '', 'ESM_Pimm_1991_Review', 'David Pimm', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'secondary', '397', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Pimm_1991_Review.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39024, 'A student justifies an algebraic rule about odd and even numbers using non-mathematical family and social signifiers, relating ''odd'' to ''bastard'' through a bilingual homophonic association (pair/impair).', '''Answer B, because their [sic] is the ''odd'' wich [sic] is the ''bastard'' and an ''even'' plus the ''bastard'' you will still have a bastard. The ''odd'' is stronger than the ''even''.''', 'algebraic', 'Odd and even numbers', 'Addition rules', 'other', '', 'ESM_Pimm_1991_Review', 'David Pimm', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'secondary', '398', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Pimm_1991_Review.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39025, 'Students attempt to directly assign the outcomes of a probability generator, such as a die, to final destinations in a multi-stage problem, instead of properly mapping them to the intermediate path choices.', 'A student proposes coding the final destinations directly to the numbers on the die ({1,2} for the soccer field, {3,4} for the playground) instead of linking the numbers to the right and left path choices at each crossing.', 'probability', 'Mathematical modeling', 'Multi-stage probability', 'in_service', 'sixth grade', 'ESM_Bromme_1994_Interactive', 'RAINER BROMME AND HEINZ STEINBRING', '1994', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '230, 231', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bromme_1994_Interactive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39026, 'Students focus entirely on the formal, symbolic calculation of probabilities or tabulation of raw outcomes, losing sight of the real-world context or objective meaning of the task.', 'Students suggest tables which list all the coin tosses separately without considering that the real concern is to list the destinations attained by the coin tosses.', 'probability', 'Probability outcomes', 'Symbol vs. Object level', 'in_service', 'sixth grade', 'ESM_Bromme_1994_Interactive', 'RAINER BROMME AND HEINZ STEINBRING', '1994', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '240, 241', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bromme_1994_Interactive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39027, 'Students incorrectly use the equality symbol instead of the membership symbol when writing solution sets, or fail to distinguish a set from its elements.', 'Writing x = {5, -5} for the solution set of x^2 - 25 = 0.', 'algebraic', 'Solution sets', 'Notation', 'student', 'grade 6, college students', 'ESM_Byers_1984_Content', 'VICTOR BYERS AND STANLEY ERLWANGER', '1984', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '262', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Byers_1984_Content.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39028, 'When solving rational inequalities, students struggle to use formal symbolic syllogisms and instead rely on intuitive leaps that bypass formal mathematical justification.', 'When asked to solve 2/(1+x) > 0, students simply write x > -1 without showing the formal logical chain of multiplying by (1+x)^2.', 'algebraic', 'Inequalities', 'Formal proof and syllogisms', 'student', 'grade 6, college students', 'ESM_Byers_1984_Content', 'VICTOR BYERS AND STANLEY ERLWANGER', '1984', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '267', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Byers_1984_Content.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39029, 'Students fail to recognize the identity of mathematical forms under various disguises, preventing them from applying known techniques like factoring to structurally similar expressions.', 'A student can easily solve 6x^2 - 5x + 1 = 0 by factoring, but fails to recognize the exact same form in 6sin^2(theta) - 5sin(theta) + 1 = 0.', 'algebraic', 'Factoring', 'Form recognition', 'student', 'grade 6, college students', 'ESM_Byers_1984_Content', 'VICTOR BYERS AND STANLEY ERLWANGER', '1984', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '268, 269, 270', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Byers_1984_Content.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39030, 'Students develop a ''relativity theory'' of mathematical content, believing that different algorithms or representational forms legitimately yield different answers, rather than understanding that content remains invariant regardless of form.', 'Benny adds 2 + .3 to get .5, but adds 2 + 3/10 to get 2 3/10, explaining that ''They are the same but different answers'' depending on the method.', 'rational', 'Equivalence and operations', 'Fractions and decimals', 'student', 'grade 6, college students', 'ESM_Byers_1984_Content', 'VICTOR BYERS AND STANLEY ERLWANGER', '1984', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '273, 274', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Byers_1984_Content.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39031, 'Students misinterpret algebraic expressions in computer algebra systems due to subtle semiotic differences between software representation and standard paper-and-pencil notations.', 'Entering 3+3/3+3/5 and getting an unexpected display, adding parentheses to get 3+(3/3)+(3/5) which yields the same display, and blaming the software for not drawing a long enough fraction line.', 'algebraic', 'Algebraic expressions', 'Computer algebra systems (CAS) syntax', 'mixed', '14 to 18 years old (college and lycee)', 'ESM_Artigue_1997_Le', 'MICHELE ARTIGUE', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '149, 150, 151', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Artigue_1997_Le.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39032, 'Students interpret tasks exclusively through the lens of the software''s specific outputs and algorithms rather than broader mathematical concepts, attempting to find idiosyncratic rules governing the software''s behavior.', 'When asked to factor X^n - 1, students focus on DERIVE''s rational factorization outputs (which use advanced, non-standard algorithms) and try to find arbitrary patterns in those specific complex outputs rather than recognizing standard algebraic roots.', 'algebraic', 'Polynomial factorization', 'Roots and factors', 'mixed', '14 to 18 years old (college and lycee)', 'ESM_Artigue_1997_Le', 'MICHELE ARTIGUE', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '152, 153, 154', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Artigue_1997_Le.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39033, 'Students rely on superficial perceptual analogies and arbitrary software operations to solve algebraic problems, bypassing conceptual understanding of algebraic equivalence.', 'Dividing the equation (6x - 15y = 24) by (2x - 5y = 8) using DERIVE, getting ''3=3'', and incorrectly assuming the operation ''division'' produces the multiplier 3 needed to transform the equations, then applying this rule blindly.', 'algebraic', 'Systems of linear equations', 'Equivalent equations', 'mixed', '14 to 18 years old (college and lycee)', 'ESM_Artigue_1997_Le', 'MICHELE ARTIGUE', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '156, 157', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Artigue_1997_Le.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39034, 'Students focus exclusively on achieving a visual or perceptual match on a graphing screen rather than analyzing the algebraic properties of the functions, leading them to ignore contradictions in derivatives.', 'Students graph f(x) = 4exp(-15x^2), calculate its derivative to check if f''(0)=0, and upon seeing a distorted derivative graph, randomly adjust parameters to fix the visual curve instead of analyzing the algebraic expression 120x*exp(-15x^2).', 'calculus', 'Graphing functions and derivatives', 'Visual vs algebraic analysis', 'mixed', '14 to 18 years old (college and lycee)', 'ESM_Artigue_1997_Le', 'MICHELE ARTIGUE', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '157, 158, 161', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Artigue_1997_Le.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39035, 'Students erroneously interpret the probability term ''certain'' as meaning ''unlikely'', ''possible'', or ''a particular thing'', rather than a 100% chance of occurrence, largely due to interference from its translation in Chichewa.', 'A student gave the meaning of certain as ''Mtunduwina'' (a particular tribe), writing ''Mtunduwina wa anthu unabwera kuchokera ku South Africa'' (a particular tribe of people came from South Africa).', 'probability', 'Probability vocabulary', 'Meaning of certain', 'student', 'first year of secondary school', 'ESM_Kazima_2006_Malawian', 'MERCY KAZIMA', '2006', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Kazima_2006_Malawian.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39036, 'Students confuse the probability term ''likely'' with the verb ''like'' (meaning to love, want, or be similar) or improperly equate it to ''certainty'' (always happens).', 'A student provided the meaning ''Kukonda'' (liking/loving), with the example ''Ndimakonda nzanga Chalosi'' (I love my friend Charles).', 'probability', 'Probability vocabulary', 'Meaning of likely', 'student', 'first year of secondary school', 'ESM_Kazima_2006_Malawian', 'MERCY KAZIMA', '2006', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Kazima_2006_Malawian.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39037, 'Students interpret ''unlikely'' as meaning ''unlike'' (not similar or not liking) or conflate it directly with ''impossible''.', 'A student gave the meaning ''Zosazikonda'' (not liking/loving), with the example ''Sindimazikonda kudya nyemba tsiku ndi tsiku'' (I do not like eating beans everyday).', 'probability', 'Probability vocabulary', 'Meaning of unlikely', 'student', 'first year of secondary school', 'ESM_Kazima_2006_Malawian', 'MERCY KAZIMA', '2006', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Kazima_2006_Malawian.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39038, 'Students rely on the ordinary everyday use of ''impossible'' to describe events that are merely very unlikely, difficult, or forbidden, rather than events with exactly a 0% mathematical probability.', 'Students gave examples such as ''impossible to fight in school'' (something not allowed) or ''impossible to do maths homework by myself'' (something difficult).', 'probability', 'Probability vocabulary', 'Meaning of impossible', 'student', 'first year of secondary school', 'ESM_Kazima_2006_Malawian', 'MERCY KAZIMA', '2006', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Kazima_2006_Malawian.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39039, 'Students fail to apply school-learned fraction comparison algorithms to contextual problems, instead reverting to inappropriate additive strategies like summing the numerators or raw scores.', 'Most of the students simply added up the penalty scores and did not take account of denominators as they had in the abstract question.', 'fraction', 'Fraction comparison', 'Contextual interference', 'student', 'Year 8', 'ESM_Boaler_1993_Encouraging', 'JO BOALER', '1993', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '360, 364, 366', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Boaler_1993_Encouraging.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39040, 'Students attempt to apply learned algorithms for comparing abstract fractions but execute them incorrectly because they have memorized the procedure without conceptual understanding.', 'When comparing 3/10 and 6/15, a student incorrectly extracted numbers to write 4/3 and 2/2, failing to remember the proper algorithmic method.', 'fraction', 'Fraction algorithms', 'Mechanical proficiency', 'student', 'Year 8', 'ESM_Boaler_1993_Encouraging', 'JO BOALER', '1993', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '363, 364, 366, 369', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Boaler_1993_Encouraging.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39041, 'Students hold the treasured intuition that gradual causes always have gradual effects, incorrectly assuming that a very small change in the step size of a numerical method will only produce a similarly small change in the result.', 'A student believes that if Euler''s method with Δt=0.017 gives a poor solution, changing it to Δt=0.016 will not produce a big improvement, only affecting some digits after the decimal point.', 'calculus', 'Numerical methods', 'Euler''s method', 'student', 'first year college students', 'ESM_Kidron_2011_Tacit', 'Ivy Kidron', '2011', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '113, 116', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Kidron_2011_Tacit.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39042, 'Students view the limit of a derivative as a potentially infinite, monotonic process of getting closer, incorrectly expecting that smaller values of Δx will always steadily improve the approximation without ever reaching the actual entity.', 'A student expects that the smaller Δx is, the better Δy/Δx reaches its real meaning in a monotonic way, and struggles to understand how smaller and smaller Δt could result in a wild non-monotonic behavior.', 'calculus', 'Definition of the derivative', 'Limit concept', 'student', 'first year college students', 'ESM_Kidron_2011_Tacit', 'Ivy Kidron', '2011', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '117, 118', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Kidron_2011_Tacit.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39043, 'Students hold a tacit pictorial model of the infinitely small differential ''dt'', treating and drawing it as a concrete, finite segment ''Δt'' that can be manipulated algebraically.', 'Despite correctly stating that substituting Δt for dt is an error, a student visually draws the infinitely small dt and multiplies dy/dt by dt to obtain dy.', 'calculus', 'Differentials', 'Infinitely small quantities', 'student', 'first year college students', 'ESM_Kidron_2011_Tacit', 'Ivy Kidron', '2011', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'mixed', 'primary', '118, 121, 122', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Kidron_2011_Tacit.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39044, 'Students erroneously believe that the elements of a set must possess an explicit common property, rejecting sets formed of arbitrary elements.', 'When asked to explain the concept of a set, a student states, ''A collection of elements having a common property.''', 'discrete', 'Set theory', 'Definition of a set', 'mixed', 'Grade 8, Grade 10, college students (preservice teachers)', 'ESM_Fischbein_1999_Mathematical', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN and MADLEN BALTSAN', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '8, 9', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1999_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39045, 'Students reject the concept of a singleton, believing that a set must be composed of more than one element.', 'When presented with a single element and asked if it constitutes a set, a student argues, ''At least two elements should be in a set.''', 'discrete', 'Set theory', 'Singletons', 'mixed', 'Grade 8, Grade 10, college students (preservice teachers)', 'ESM_Fischbein_1999_Mathematical', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN and MADLEN BALTSAN', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '9, 10, 11', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1999_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39046, 'Students reject the concept of an empty set, considering a collection with no objects to be a logical contradiction.', 'When asked if the intersection points of two parallel lines can define a set, a student answers, ''It cannot be a set because there are no elements.''', 'discrete', 'Set theory', 'Empty set', 'mixed', 'Grade 8, Grade 10, college students (preservice teachers)', 'ESM_Fischbein_1999_Mathematical', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN and MADLEN BALTSAN', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '11, 12', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1999_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39047, 'Students mistakenly define two equal sets as sets having the same number of elements rather than sets composed of the exact same elements.', 'When asked to explain the notion of two equal sets, a student writes, ''Two equal sets are two sets having the same number of elements.''', 'discrete', 'Set theory', 'Equality of sets', 'mixed', 'Grade 8, Grade 10, college students (preservice teachers)', 'ESM_Fischbein_1999_Mathematical', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN and MADLEN BALTSAN', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '14, 15', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1999_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39048, 'Students count repeated elements as distinct elements within a set, contradicting the mathematical rule that identical elements are only counted once.', 'When asked how many elements are in the set {4, 4, 4, ...}, students argue that there is an infinity of elements because the number 4 repeats endlessly.', 'discrete', 'Set theory', 'Repeated elements', 'mixed', 'Grade 8, Grade 10, college students (preservice teachers)', 'ESM_Fischbein_1999_Mathematical', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN and MADLEN BALTSAN', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '15, 16', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1999_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39049, 'Students assume the operator in a multiplication problem must be a whole number and that multiplication always makes the result bigger, constrained by the tacit repeated addition model.', 'Students struggle to choose the correct operation for ''1 kilo of detergent makes 15 kilos of soap. How much soap can be made from 0.75 kilos of detergent?'' because 0.75 acts as the operator.', 'decimal', 'Multiplication', 'Decimal multipliers', 'mixed', 'Grade 8, Grade 10, college students (preservice teachers)', 'ESM_Fischbein_1999_Mathematical', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN and MADLEN BALTSAN', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '18', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1999_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39050, 'Students bypass conceptual understanding of word problems by simply searching for any numbers in the text, including irrelevant ones like exercise numbers, and combining them using a random arithmetic operation.', 'In one exercise, the text referred to a previous problem, No. 282, and students blindly included the number 282 in their calculations, with one student noting ''One can always take a chance''.', 'whole_number', 'Word problems', 'Choice of operation', 'student', 'intermediate level (forms 4-6, aged 10-12)', 'ESM_Hedren_1985_Hand-held', 'ROLF HEDRÉN', '1985', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '176', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hedren_1985_Hand-held.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39051, 'Students struggle to interpret and appropriately round repeating decimals generated by calculator division, assuming all displayed digits must be part of the final answer even when an integer is contextually required.', 'When a division like 5000 ÷ 9 resulted in 555.5555 on the display, students became confused and found it difficult to round off to an integer.', 'decimal', 'Calculator use', 'Rounding division results', 'student', 'intermediate level (forms 4-6, aged 10-12)', 'ESM_Hedren_1985_Hand-held', 'ROLF HEDRÉN', '1985', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '176', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hedren_1985_Hand-held.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39052, 'Applying standard ''correct to 1 significant figure'' rounding rules to multiplication yields unacceptably high percentage errors when the numbers have small leading digits.', 'Applying exactly the same rule to 149 x 249 gives 20000 with an error of 46%, and 149 x 149 being estimated as 10000 gives an error of 122% of the estimate.', 'whole_number', 'Computational estimation', 'Multiplication and rounding', 'student', 'adults', 'ESM_Edwards_1984_Computational', 'A. EDWARDS', '1984', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '62, 68', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Edwards_1984_Computational.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39053, 'Estimating division quotients by rounding the divisor to one significant figure causes massive proportional errors.', '3887 divided by 1590 gives an estimate of 1, with an error of 59% of the true value.', 'whole_number', 'Computational estimation', 'Division', 'student', 'adults', 'ESM_Edwards_1984_Computational', 'A. EDWARDS', '1984', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '69, 70', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Edwards_1984_Computational.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39054, 'Students believe that the area or quantity of a half varies depending on the physical orientation of the cut, or they focus on the number of pieces rather than the proportional size of the fraction.', 'A student suggested that depending on how a pizza was cut, either side-to-side or top-to-bottom, one amount was more than the other.', 'fraction', 'Comparing fractions', 'Conservation of area', 'in_service', 'K-4', 'ESM_Crespo_2006_Elementary', 'SANDRA CRESPO', '2006', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '33, 34', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Crespo_2006_Elementary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39055, 'Students write complex number sentences with mixed operations but evaluate them strictly from left to right, ignoring standard order of operations.', 'A student wrote ''30 x 2 - 10 / 5'' to equal 10, calculating it from left to right: 30 x 2 is 60, minus 10 is 50, divided by 5 is 10.', 'algebraic', 'Order of operations', 'Mixed operations', 'in_service', 'K-4', 'ESM_Crespo_2006_Elementary', 'SANDRA CRESPO', '2006', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '43, 44', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Crespo_2006_Elementary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39056, 'Teachers memorize the univalence requirement for functions but simultaneously hold contradictory beliefs that circles and ellipses are functions.', 'A teacher correctly defines a function using univalence but later states, ''Uh, a circle is a function, but a circle doesn''t pass the line test.''', 'algebraic', 'Functions', 'Univalence requirement', 'mixed', 'secondary school', 'ESM_Even_1995_Subject-matter', 'RUHAMA EVEN AND DINA TIROSH', '1995', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '7, 8', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Even_1995_Subject-matter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39057, 'Teachers incorrectly evaluate the undefined mathematical operation (-8)^(1/3) as -2, failing to recognize that extending rational exponents to negative bases violates the requirement that definitions cannot depend on equivalent representatives.', 'Teachers argued (-8)^(1/3) = -2 and struggled to refute a fictitious student''s claim that (-8)^(1/3) = (-8)^(2/6) = sqrt6((-8)^2) = 2, sometimes partially accepting both answers.', 'algebraic', 'Undefined mathematical operations', 'Rational exponents with negative bases', 'mixed', 'secondary school', 'ESM_Even_1995_Subject-matter', 'RUHAMA EVEN AND DINA TIROSH', '1995', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '8, 9', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Even_1995_Subject-matter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39058, 'Teachers rely on a rule-based argument for division by zero, viewing mathematics as an arbitrary set of unexplainable rules to be memorized rather than providing a logical mathematical explanation.', 'When a student asks why they cannot divide by zero, a teacher responds, ''In mathematics we have rules... These rules often do not seem reasonable... There is no reason and there is no point at all in looking for explanations.''', 'algebraic', 'Undefined mathematical operations', 'Division by zero', 'mixed', 'secondary school', 'ESM_Even_1995_Subject-matter', 'RUHAMA EVEN AND DINA TIROSH', '1995', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '9, 10', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Even_1995_Subject-matter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39059, 'Students and some teachers wrongly assume that the slope of a linear function varies in direct proportion to the angle between the line and the x-axis.', 'A student concludes that since the line y=x (at a 45-degree angle) has a slope of 1, a steeper line with roughly twice the angle must have a slope of about 2.', 'algebraic', 'Linear functions', 'Slope and angle relationship', 'mixed', 'secondary school', 'ESM_Even_1995_Subject-matter', 'RUHAMA EVEN AND DINA TIROSH', '1995', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '13, 14, 15', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Even_1995_Subject-matter.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39060, 'Students confuse the fractional place value in a non-standard base with its decimal counterpart, incorrectly interpreting 1/3 as 0.3.', 'When converting 0.2 in base three, a student reasons that 0.2_three = 2 x 0.3 = 0.6.', 'fraction', 'Place value in non-standard bases', 'Assigning place values', 'PST', 'junior and senior years', 'ESM_Khoury_1994_Fractions', 'HELEN A. KHOURY AND RINA ZAZKIS', '1994', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '195, 196', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Khoury_1994_Fractions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39061, 'Students assume that the base of a number system dictates the size of the unit whole, interpreting a base-fraction as a part-whole relationship relative to the base itself.', 'A student interprets 0.2 in base three as meaning ''two out of three'' and compares it to 0.2 in base five, which is viewed as ''two out of five''.', 'fraction', 'Meaning of base representations', 'Unit whole', 'PST', 'junior and senior years', 'ESM_Khoury_1994_Fractions', 'HELEN A. KHOURY AND RINA ZAZKIS', '1994', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '196, 201', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Khoury_1994_Fractions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39062, 'Students overgeneralize the decimal representation of ''one-half'', universally applying the symbol ''0.5'' to other bases regardless of whether the digit 5 is valid or meaningful in that base.', 'A student writes ''one-half'' in base three as 0.5, and then calculates its value as 5 x 1/3 = 5/3.', 'fraction', 'Symbolic representation of fractions', 'Overgeneralization of decimal symbols', 'PST', 'junior and senior years', 'ESM_Khoury_1994_Fractions', 'HELEN A. KHOURY AND RINA ZAZKIS', '1994', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '199, 200', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Khoury_1994_Fractions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39063, 'Students believe that fractions lack invariance, assuming that the numerical value or quantity of a fraction changes when represented in a different base system.', 'A student claims that one-half in base three is less than one-half in base five because ''in base three you have less numbers''.', 'fraction', 'Invariance of fractions', 'Base dependence', 'PST', 'junior and senior years', 'ESM_Khoury_1994_Fractions', 'HELEN A. KHOURY AND RINA ZAZKIS', '1994', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '199, 202', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Khoury_1994_Fractions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39064, 'Students believe they must routinely apply algebraic factoring techniques before or instead of direct substitution when evaluating limits of rational functions, regardless of whether a 0/0 indetermination actually exists.', 'Student S1 substitutes 1 into the expression (x-1)/(x^2+x) to get 0/2, but disregards this valid result to immediately attempt factoring, stating ''The next step is to factor.''', 'calculus', 'Limits of functions', 'Rational expressions', 'student', 'college level', 'ESM_Hardy_2009_Students''', 'Nadia Hardy', '2009', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '350, 351', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hardy_2009_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39065, 'Students incorrectly classify limit problems and choose mathematical techniques based purely on the superficial algebraic appearance of the polynomials, such as whether they belong to familiar factoring categories.', 'When given a rational expression with polynomials that are not easily factorable trinomials or binomials, a student erroneously assumes it must be a limit to infinity or requires dividing by x, simply because the typical factoring norm does not visually apply.', 'calculus', 'Limits of functions', 'Problem classification', 'student', 'college level', 'ESM_Hardy_2009_Students''', 'Nadia Hardy', '2009', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '352, 353, 354', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hardy_2009_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39066, 'Students fail to differentiate between zero divided by a number and a number divided by zero, concluding that both operations yield ''nothing'' and are therefore conceptually identical.', 'Even though they gave an answer of ''zero'' for 0 / 8 and an answer of ''no answer'', or ''undefined'', for 8 / 0, students concluded that both of these responses meant ''nothing'', and therefore the two questions were the same.', 'whole_number', 'Division', 'Division by zero', 'in_service', 'cross-grade', 'ESM_Davis_2013_Profound', 'Brent Davis & Moshe Renert', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '247', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Davis_2013_Profound.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39067, 'Students compare two multi-digit numbers digit-by-digit from left to right instead of perceiving and comparing the numbers as whole global entities.', 'When trying to form a number larger than 423, a pupil played the digits 5, 4, 3 sequentially instead of treating 423 as a complete magnitude, demonstrating an inability to form a number on a term-to-term correspondence.', 'whole_number', 'Number comparison', 'Place value and multi-digit numbers', 'student', 'ages 6 to 10 (1st, 3rd, and 4th year)', 'ESM_Bednarz_1982_Understanding', 'NADINE BEDNARZ AND BERNADETTE JANVIER', '1982', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '44, 53', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bednarz_1982_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39068, 'Students interpret the words ''hundreds'', ''tens'', and ''ones'' merely as indicating the sequential order for writing digits from left to right, rather than representing hierarchical quantitative groupings.', 'In order to form 445 using written tags, a student might take 4 tens, 5 units and look for 4 hundreds; or a child who has 5 hundreds and 1 unit looks for 0 tens to form 501.', 'whole_number', 'Place value', 'Hundreds, tens, and ones', 'student', 'ages 6 to 10 (1st, 3rd, and 4th year)', 'ESM_Bednarz_1982_Understanding', 'NADINE BEDNARZ AND BERNADETTE JANVIER', '1982', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '46, 47, 53', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bednarz_1982_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39069, 'Students struggle to simultaneously handle two different levels of groupings, such as a large grouping containing smaller sub-groupings, and often conflate the levels when performing subtraction.', 'When given a subtraction problem involving candies in bags (which contain rolls), one child confused the groupings, subtracting 10 for the bag and then attempting to subtract 70 to account for the individual rolls.', 'whole_number', 'Subtraction', 'Multi-level groupings', 'student', 'ages 6 to 10 (1st, 3rd, and 4th year)', 'ESM_Bednarz_1982_Understanding', 'NADINE BEDNARZ AND BERNADETTE JANVIER', '1982', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '48, 49, 53, 54', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bednarz_1982_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39070, 'Students view borrowing in subtraction merely as a pictorial or verbal procedure of crossing out a digit and adding a ''1'' to the next, without understanding the concept of exchanging hierarchical groupings.', 'When asked what the ''one'' means when borrowing in 234 - 178, students give no meaning to the idea, seeing it only as ''1'', ''one bar'', or ''one ten''.', 'whole_number', 'Subtraction algorithm', 'Borrowing and regrouping', 'student', 'ages 6 to 10 (1st, 3rd, and 4th year)', 'ESM_Bednarz_1982_Understanding', 'NADINE BEDNARZ AND BERNADETTE JANVIER', '1982', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '50, 54', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bednarz_1982_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39071, 'Students initially focus on the surface features of word problems, such as the story context or the starting sentence, rather than the underlying relational mathematical structure when attempting to match or categorize them.', 'Students paired an addition and a subtraction problem simply because ''They both start off, Bill has 52 marbles and Jan has 29 marbles'' or because ''They are both about marbles.''', 'whole_number', 'Word problems', 'Recognition of problem structures', 'student', 'fifth-grade', 'ESM_English_1997_Development', 'LYN D. ENGLISH', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '186, 196, 198', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_English_1997_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39072, 'Students inadvertently create addition problems or basic equivalent set problems when attempting to construct a Cartesian product multiplication problem, struggling to coordinate the two dimensions.', 'When asked to create a problem for 4x3, a student intending to make a Cartesian product problem instead writes, ''Kelly has 4 pairs of shorts and 3 T-shirts. How many pieces of clothing does she have?''', 'whole_number', 'Multiplication word problems', 'Cartesian product vs. Addition', 'student', 'fifth-grade', 'ESM_English_1997_Development', 'LYN D. ENGLISH', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '207', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_English_1997_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39073, 'Students erroneously believe that a division operation is impossible if the dividend is smaller than the divisor, assuming that division must result in whole numbers and that ''turning it around'' is invalid.', 'When presented with the expression 3 / 4, a student says ''You can''t do that... you can''t turn divided by around,'' and another argues that if 3 cakes are shared among 4 people, ''One person won''t get any.''', 'fraction', 'Division', 'Quotient construct / Dividend smaller than divisor', 'student', 'fifth-grade', 'ESM_English_1997_Development', 'LYN D. ENGLISH', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '208', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_English_1997_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39074, 'Students inappropriately apply additive reasoning to a proportional reasoning task, calculating a constant difference instead of a constant ratio between quantities.', 'A student calculates the difference between the number of children and adults at capacity (20 - 15 = 5) and subtracts this constant difference from the 12 children on the elevator to arrive at 7 adults.', 'proportional', 'Proportional reasoning', 'Additive vs. multiplicative strategies', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'ESM_Che_2012_Problem', 'Megan Che & Elaine Wiegert & Karen Threlkeld', '2012', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '316, 319', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Che_2012_Problem.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39075, 'Students blindly apply a standard cross-multiplication procedure using the numbers provided in the text without considering the real-world context or whether the resulting answer is mathematically viable for the specific problem.', 'A student sets up the proportion 20/15 = 12/x, cross-multiplies 20x = 180, and solves for x = 9, failing to realize that if the elevator is already more than half full with 12 children, fewer than half of the 15 adults (7.5) could still fit.', 'proportional', 'Proportional reasoning', 'Algorithmic procedures', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'ESM_Che_2012_Problem', 'Megan Che & Elaine Wiegert & Karen Threlkeld', '2012', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '315, 316, 319, 320', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Che_2012_Problem.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39076, 'Students recognize that additive reasoning or a 1:1 ratio is inappropriate, but assign a simpler, incorrect in-viable ratio (such as 2:1) instead of using the actual quantities given in the problem.', 'A student assumes a 2:1 children-to-adult ratio, asserting that one child is 50 lbs and one adult is 100 lbs, and works appropriately with those invented weights to solve the problem.', 'proportional', 'Proportional reasoning', 'Ratios', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'ESM_Che_2012_Problem', 'Megan Che & Elaine Wiegert & Karen Threlkeld', '2012', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '317, 321', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Che_2012_Problem.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39077, 'Students falsely generalize that multiplication always results in a larger number and division always results in a smaller number, leading to incorrect operation choices when problems involve decimals less than one.', 'In the ''Mini'' problem (converting 5.5 gallons to litres where 1 litre is 0.22 gallons), a lot of pupils incorrectly chose multiplication because they knew the answer should be a larger number.', 'decimal', 'Choice of operation', 'Decimals less than one', 'student', '12- and 13-year-olds', 'ESM_Bell_1984_Choice', 'ALAN BELL, EFRAIM FISCHBEIN, AND BRIAN GREER', '1984', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '129, 134, 144, 145', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bell_1984_Choice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39078, 'Students hold the misconception that division must always involve dividing a larger number by a smaller number, causing them to reverse the operands when a problem naturally requires dividing a smaller number by a larger one.', 'When given the calculation 8.7 / 59.1 to write a story for, students reversed the numbers, such as writing a story that required dividing 59.1 wheels into 8.7 boxes.', 'decimal', 'Division', 'Order of operands', 'student', '12- and 13-year-olds', 'ESM_Bell_1984_Choice', 'ALAN BELL, EFRAIM FISCHBEIN, AND BRIAN GREER', '1984', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '129, 134, 139, 141', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bell_1984_Choice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39079, 'Students display profound uncertainty and confusion regarding the language and standard notation of division, failing to distinguish between ''divided by'' and ''divided into''.', 'When asked to represent GBP22 divided among 25 men, three pupils wrote 22)25, 25)22, and 25 / 22, but all read their written expressions aloud identically as ''25 divided by 22.''', 'whole_number', 'Division notation', 'Language and symbols', 'student', '12- and 13-year-olds', 'ESM_Bell_1984_Choice', 'ALAN BELL, EFRAIM FISCHBEIN, AND BRIAN GREER', '1984', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '142', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bell_1984_Choice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39080, 'Students lack an understanding of the invariance of operational structure across changes in number size, believing that substituting decimals into a familiar context changes the mathematical operation required.', 'Students did not perceive any conflict in using multiplication to work out the cost for 2 gallons of petrol, but switching to division to work out the cost for 0.53 gallons.', 'decimal', 'Choice of operation', 'Invariance of operation', 'student', '12- and 13-year-olds', 'ESM_Bell_1984_Choice', 'ALAN BELL, EFRAIM FISCHBEIN, AND BRIAN GREER', '1984', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '142, 146', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bell_1984_Choice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39081, 'When an expected division operation yields an answer that intuitively seems to be the wrong magnitude, students will abandon multiplicative reasoning and incorrectly use subtraction to reach an acceptable magnitude.', 'To find how much meat priced at GBP2.56 per pound can be bought for GBP2, students realized the amount should be just under a pound, saw that 2.56 / 2 gave an answer of the wrong magnitude, and chose to subtract GBP2 from GBP2.56 instead.', 'decimal', 'Choice of operation', 'Substitution of subtraction for division', 'student', '12- and 13-year-olds', 'ESM_Bell_1984_Choice', 'ALAN BELL, EFRAIM FISCHBEIN, AND BRIAN GREER', '1984', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '145', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bell_1984_Choice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39082, 'Students struggle to draw three-dimensional objects on a two-dimensional surface without distortion because they attempt to preserve all characteristic properties, such as 90-degree angles and parallel lines.', 'Jeroen made many attempts to draw a cube... ''It''s just not a cube, is it?'', he sighed. It is, of course, impossible to make a two-dimensional drawing of a spatial object such as a cube, leaving all its properties intact.', 'geometric', 'Spatial figures and 3D representations', 'Drawing cubes and perspective', 'student', '12 to 16 year olds (seventh and eighth graders)', 'ESM_Brink_1993_Different', 'JAN VAN DEN BRINK', '1993', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '36, 37, 38, 44', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Brink_1993_Different.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39083, 'Students erroneously assume that a straight line on a flat map represents the shortest route and true direction, failing to account for the spherical nature of the earth and the role of great circles.', 'According to the Imam''s book, from Havana you should pray due east; but the great circle, on the other hand, indicated north-east.', 'geometric', 'Spherical geometry', 'Great circles and distance', 'student', '12 to 16 year olds (seventh and eighth graders)', 'ESM_Brink_1993_Different', 'JAN VAN DEN BRINK', '1993', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '47, 48, 50, 51', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Brink_1993_Different.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39084, 'Students apply the left-to-right reading order of normal algebraic language when using a calculator, mistakenly pressing the function key before the numerical argument.', 'When children are asked to figure, for example, sin 30 on the calculator, they mistakenly first press the sin-key and then the number 30, which corresponds to the direction of reading.', 'algebraic', 'Calculator functions', 'Order of operations and syntax', 'student', '12 to 16 year olds (seventh and eighth graders)', 'ESM_Brink_1993_Different', 'JAN VAN DEN BRINK', '1993', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '60', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Brink_1993_Different.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39085, 'Students incorrectly assume that an arithmetic expression entered into any calculator will yield the identical result, failing to recognize the differing built-in logic systems of different machines.', 'The sum 70 + 1 x 447 = is answered by one machine with LELIE (31737), while another machine gives the answer as LIS (517).', 'whole_number', 'Order of operations', 'Calculator logic', 'student', '12 to 16 year olds (seventh and eighth graders)', 'ESM_Brink_1993_Different', 'JAN VAN DEN BRINK', '1993', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'secondary', '53, 54', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Brink_1993_Different.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39086, 'Students incorrectly use additive or absolute thinking in proportional situations that require multiplicative or relative thinking.', 'In a recipe requiring 4 cups of sugar and 10 cups of flour, if sugar is increased to 6 cups, students reason that because sugar increased by 2 cups, flour should also increase by 2 cups.', 'proportional', 'Proportional situations', 'Scaling', 'mixed', 'middle years (Years 5 to 9)', 'ESM_Hilton_2016_Promoting', 'Annette Hilton, Geoff Hilton, Shelley Dole, & Merrilyn Goos', '2016', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '194, 196, 201', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hilton_2016_Promoting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39087, 'Students incorrectly over-rely on multiplicative strategies in situations that are additive and non-proportional.', 'In a race where Jack starts first and is always 2 laps ahead of Sue, students incorrectly use a multiplicative strategy to predict their relative positions, rather than an additive one.', 'proportional', 'Non-proportional situations', 'Additive relationships', 'mixed', 'middle years (Years 5 to 9)', 'ESM_Hilton_2016_Promoting', 'Annette Hilton, Geoff Hilton, Shelley Dole, & Merrilyn Goos', '2016', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '194, 201', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hilton_2016_Promoting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39088, 'Students use absolute thinking instead of considering scale or relative relationships when interpreting data or scaling diagrams.', 'When evaluating the position of 37 on a number line from 33 to 53, students justify its placement by saying it is 4 cm along the line because 33 + 4 = 37, ignoring the scale.', 'proportional', 'Scale', 'Relative vs absolute thinking', 'mixed', 'middle years (Years 5 to 9)', 'ESM_Hilton_2016_Promoting', 'Annette Hilton, Geoff Hilton, Shelley Dole, & Merrilyn Goos', '2016', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '201, 204', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hilton_2016_Promoting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39089, 'Students use ''gap thinking'', focusing on the absolute difference between the numerator and denominator rather than the proportional ratio or size of the parts, essentially treating the fraction as two independent whole numbers.', 'For the pair 3/4 and 7/9, a student chooses 3/4 because it has a ''smaller gap'' or claims 5/6 and 7/8 are the ''same because each has one left''.', 'fraction', 'Fraction comparison', 'Gap thinking', 'student', 'grade 6', 'ESM_Clarke_2009_Students''', 'Doug M. Clarke & Anne Roche', '2009', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '128, 132, 133', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Clarke_2009_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39090, 'Students inappropriately apply whole number generalizations to fractions, asserting that a fraction with numerically larger components is always larger, or conversely that smaller digits always yield a larger fraction.', 'When comparing 4/7 and 4/5, students choose 4/7 as larger because of ''larger numbers'', demonstrating a form of whole number dominance.', 'fraction', 'Fraction comparison', 'Whole number dominance', 'student', 'grade 6', 'ESM_Clarke_2009_Students''', 'Doug M. Clarke & Anne Roche', '2009', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '133, 134', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Clarke_2009_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39091, 'Students struggle to conceptualize the magnitude of improper fractions, sometimes treating them as equivalent to proper fractions that share the same digits, or arbitrarily flipping them to make sense of the values.', 'When asked to compare 2/4 and 4/2, 25.3% of incorrect explanations claimed they were ''the same'', presumably because both fractions contain the digits 2 and 4.', 'fraction', 'Fraction comparison', 'Improper fractions', 'student', 'grade 6', 'ESM_Clarke_2009_Students''', 'Doug M. Clarke & Anne Roche', '2009', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '133', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Clarke_2009_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39092, 'Students erroneously endorse more conditional inferences that lead to negative conclusions than those that lead to affirmative conclusions, struggling with the logical step of double negation.', 'The inference ‘if A then 3; not-3; therefore not-A’ is made more often than the inference ‘if not-A then 3; not-3; therefore A’ despite both being valid modus tollens inferences.', 'discrete', 'Conditional inference', 'Negative conclusion effect', 'student', 'undergraduates', 'ESM_Inglis_2008_Conditional', 'Matthew Inglis, Adrian Simpson', '2008', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '191, 195, 199', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Inglis_2008_Conditional.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39093, 'Students improperly endorse more conditional inferences from affirmative premises than from negative premises, particularly failing to recognize implicitly negated premises as relevant to the conditional rule.', 'The inference ‘if not-A then 3; A; therefore not-3’ is made more often than the inference ‘if A then 3; R; therefore not-3’, because ''R'' is less obviously related to the conditional than ''A''.', 'discrete', 'Conditional inference', 'Affirmative premise effect', 'student', 'undergraduates', 'ESM_Inglis_2008_Conditional', 'Matthew Inglis, Adrian Simpson', '2008', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '192, 196, 200, 201', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Inglis_2008_Conditional.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39094, 'Students frequently draw invalid logical inferences, such as Denial of the Antecedent and Affirmation of the Consequent, effectively treating ''if-then'' conditional statements as biconditionals.', 'When given the rule ''if our opponents win, interest rates will rise'', people may make the invalid DA inference that ''if they win, interest rates will not rise''.', 'discrete', 'Conditional inference', 'Invalid inferences', 'student', 'undergraduates', 'ESM_Inglis_2008_Conditional', 'Matthew Inglis, Adrian Simpson', '2008', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '189, 190, 195', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Inglis_2008_Conditional.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39095, 'Students partially simplify fractions, leaving them in an incomplete form rather than reducing them to their lowest terms.', 'When asked to simplify 42/60, some students provided intermediate equivalent fractions like 21/30 or 14/20 instead of the fully simplified form.', 'fraction', 'Simplifying fractions', '', 'student', 'classe de troisième', 'ESM_DHondt_1978_Quelques', 'M. Vandewiele et W. D''Hondt', '1978', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '443', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_DHondt_1978_Quelques.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39096, 'Students confuse arithmetic operation symbols with set theory symbols, leading to errors in finding the Cartesian product.', 'When evaluating A X B where A and B are sets, many students failed because they mistakenly applied arithmetic multiplication instead of the Cartesian product.', 'discrete', 'Set theory', 'Cartesian product', 'student', 'classe de troisième', 'ESM_DHondt_1978_Quelques', 'M. Vandewiele et W. D''Hondt', '1978', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '444', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_DHondt_1978_Quelques.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39097, 'Students struggle to correctly determine the order of negative integers, partly due to linguistic phrasing issues.', 'When asked for the greatest relative integer satisfying a < -6, 75% of students failed or abstained from answering.', 'integer', 'Negative numbers', 'Inequalities and ordering', 'student', 'classe de troisième', 'ESM_DHondt_1978_Quelques', 'M. Vandewiele et W. D''Hondt', '1978', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '444', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_DHondt_1978_Quelques.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39098, 'Students confuse the definitions and visual properties of parallelograms and trapezoids.', 'When asked to place a point D to complete a parallelogram ABCD, some students drew a trapezoid instead.', 'geometric', 'Quadrilaterals', 'Parallelograms and trapezoids', 'student', 'classe de troisième', 'ESM_DHondt_1978_Quelques', 'M. Vandewiele et W. D''Hondt', '1978', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '444', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_DHondt_1978_Quelques.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39099, 'Students lack mastery of the concept of absolute value, leading to failures when evaluating functions involving it.', 'Students struggled to calculate h(-6) for the function h(x) = x + |x| due to difficulties handling the absolute value.', 'algebraic', 'Functions', 'Absolute value', 'student', 'classe de troisième', 'ESM_DHondt_1978_Quelques', 'M. Vandewiele et W. D''Hondt', '1978', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '444', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_DHondt_1978_Quelques.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39100, 'Students inappropriately mix the algorithm for prime factorization with the Euclidean algorithm of division when trying to factorize a prime number.', 'When trying to find a prime factorization, a student writes ''7=2x3+1 (no, 7 is prime!)''.', 'integer', 'Prime factorization', 'Euclidean algorithm', 'student', 'university and PhD students', 'ESM_Furinghetti_2009_Every', 'Fulvia Furinghetti & Francesca Morselli', '2009', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '79, 80', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Furinghetti_2009_Every.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39101, 'Students incorrectly translate the definition of coprime numbers into symbolic form by using the least common multiple (LCM) instead of the greatest common factor.', 'A student formally translates the definition of coprime numbers as ''Least Common Multiple (p,q) = 1''.', 'integer', 'Coprime numbers', 'Formal symbolic translation', 'student', 'university and PhD students', 'ESM_Furinghetti_2009_Every', 'Fulvia Furinghetti & Francesca Morselli', '2009', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '82, 83', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Furinghetti_2009_Every.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39102, 'Students hold the belief that mathematical proof requires only linear, automatic sequential procedures, and experience debilitating panic when a cognitive link or deeper manipulation of representations is required.', 'After correctly establishing the prime factorization of two numbers and their sum, a student gives up and writes ''Help! I cannot do it, I still do not see anything. The deepest darkness.''', 'algebraic', 'Mathematical proof', 'Strategic knowledge', 'student', 'university and PhD students', 'ESM_Furinghetti_2009_Every', 'Fulvia Furinghetti & Francesca Morselli', '2009', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '80, 81', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Furinghetti_2009_Every.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39103, 'Students prematurely dismiss valid intuitive reasoning or intermediate heuristic representations as entirely unsound, believing that only strictly formal symbolic arguments are acceptable in proof.', 'A student discovers that the fraction (p+q)/p cannot be simplified, but Abandons the approach stating ''intuitively this fraction cannot be simplified, but this is only an unsound argument''.', 'algebraic', 'Mathematical proof', 'Intuitive arguments', 'student', 'university and PhD students', 'ESM_Furinghetti_2009_Every', 'Fulvia Furinghetti & Francesca Morselli', '2009', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '84', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Furinghetti_2009_Every.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39104, 'Students mistakenly believe that multiplication always results in a larger number and division always results in a smaller number, applying rules from natural numbers to all rational numbers.', 'When asked to find the cost of 0.75 litres of juice if one litre costs 5 shekels, students intuitively tend to answer ''By division'', considering that ''division makes smaller''.', 'rational', 'Operations with decimals/fractions', 'Choosing the correct operation', 'mixed', 'grades 5 to 11 and college students', 'ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '15, 16, 19', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39105, 'Students erroneously judge the size of an angle based on the visual length of its drawn arms, overriding the formal definition of opposite angles.', 'When shown two intersecting lines where the lines on one side are longer, many subjects claim that the opposite angles with longer ''arms'' are bigger.', 'geometric', 'Angles', 'Opposite angles', 'mixed', 'grades 5 to 11 and college students', 'ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '16', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39106, 'Students intuitively assume that two shapes with visually different contours cannot have equivalent areas, even when they can be proven equal by translation.', 'When asked to prove that two areas formed between parallel curves are equivalent, students intuitively react that the two areas are not equal and that ''there is nothing to prove''.', 'geometric', 'Area', 'Equivalence of areas', 'mixed', 'grades 5 to 11 and college students', 'ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '20, 21', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39107, 'Students reject rectangles, squares, or rhombuses as parallelograms because their visual structure differs from the prototypical oblique parallelogram shape.', 'In grade 11, only 76% of the students identified the rhombus as a parallelogram, and only 85% identified squares and rectangles as such, relying on visual structure rather than formal definitions.', 'geometric', 'Quadrilaterals', 'Parallelograms', 'mixed', 'grades 5 to 11 and college students', 'ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '25', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39108, 'Students exhibit representativeness bias in probability, believing that an ordered sequence of numbers is less likely to occur randomly than a scrambled sequence.', 'Students consider that a lotto choice of 39, 1, 17, 33, 8, 27 has a greater chance of winning than 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 because it seems to better represent randomness.', 'probability', 'Randomness', 'Representativeness bias', 'mixed', 'grades 5 to 11 and college students', 'ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '26, 44', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39109, 'Students apply the schema that ''the whole is bigger than a part of it'' to infinite sets, incorrectly concluding that the set of natural numbers is larger than the set of even numbers.', 'Most students intuitively answer that the set of natural numbers is ''bigger'' than the set of even numbers, struggling to accept that a set of elements may be equivalent to a subset of it.', 'discrete', 'Set theory', 'Actual infinity and equivalence', 'mixed', 'grades 5 to 11 and college students', 'ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '26, 35, 43, 44', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39110, 'Students find the borrowing procedure in subtraction counter-intuitive, sometimes reversing the operation when subtracting a larger digit from a smaller one.', 'When solving 1702 - 1368, children sometimes reverse the operation in the units column (8-2) and write 6 because subtracting 8 from 2 intuitively does not work.', 'whole_number', 'Subtraction algorithm', 'Borrowing across zero', 'mixed', 'grades 5 to 11 and college students', 'ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '28', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39111, 'Students view an infinite repeating decimal as a potential infinity that only ''tends to'' the equivalent fraction rather than actually equaling it.', 'When asked if 0.3333... is equal to 1/3 or tends to 1/3, students respond that it tends to 1/3 but never reaches it because infinity can never be reached.', 'rational', 'Infinite decimals', 'Equivalence to fractions', 'mixed', 'grades 5 to 11 and college students', 'ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '32, 33', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39112, 'Students over-apply the schema of proportion in probability, erroneously claiming that events with equivalent ratios have equal likelihoods regardless of sample size.', 'Subjects justify that the likelihood of getting at least 2 heads in 3 tosses is equal to getting at least 200 heads in 300 tosses by affirming that 200/300 = 2/3, ignoring the law of large numbers.', 'probability', 'Law of large numbers', 'Sample size', 'mixed', 'grades 5 to 11 and college students', 'ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '45', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39113, 'Students adhere to a strict chronological ''time axis'' causality schema in probability, failing to update the probability of an earlier event based on the known outcome of a subsequent event.', 'When told a second drawn marble is known to be white, students still claim the probability that the first drawn marble was white is equal to it being black, ignoring the new condition.', 'probability', 'Conditional probability', 'Time axis causality', 'mixed', 'grades 5 to 11 and college students', 'ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions', 'EFRAIM FISCHBEIN', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '45, 46', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Fischbein_1999_Intuitions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39114, 'Students view functions statically as actions or algebraic expressions to evaluate by plugging in numbers, rather than as a dynamic mental process that transforms inputs to outputs.', 'A function is an equation in which a variable is manipulated so that an answer is calculated using numbers in place of that variable.', 'algebraic', 'Functions', 'Action vs. process conception', 'PST', 'sophomore and junior math majors', 'ESM_Breidenbach_1992_Development', 'DANIEL BREIDENBACH, ED DUBINSKY, JULIE HAWKS AND DEVILYNA NICHOLS', '1992', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '251, 252', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Breidenbach_1992_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39115, 'Students believe that an explicit formula, discernible pattern, or causal relationship is required for a relation to be considered a function.', 'There''s really no pattern. I don''t see any pattern but you definitely have an input and for that specific x value you have a y.', 'algebraic', 'Functions', 'Arbitrary mappings vs. explicit formulas', 'PST', 'sophomore and junior math majors', 'ESM_Breidenbach_1992_Development', 'DANIEL BREIDENBACH, ED DUBINSKY, JULIE HAWKS AND DEVILYNA NICHOLS', '1992', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '261, 262, 270', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Breidenbach_1992_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39116, 'Students confuse the requirement for a function to be single-valued with the property of being one-to-one, erroneously believing a function cannot map two different inputs to the same output.', 'It''s a boolean expression here, so you''d, you''d either get true or false and so you could get true more than once or false more than once.', 'algebraic', 'Functions', 'Single-valued vs. one-to-one', 'PST', 'sophomore and junior math majors', 'ESM_Breidenbach_1992_Development', 'DANIEL BREIDENBACH, ED DUBINSKY, JULIE HAWKS AND DEVILYNA NICHOLS', '1992', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '272, 273', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Breidenbach_1992_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39117, 'Students mistakenly believe that linear models have universal applicability, inappropriately applying proportional reasoning to calculate the area or volume of similar figures by assuming that if lengths are multiplied by a factor, the area is multiplied by that same factor rather than its square.', 'When asked how long it takes to manure a 600m square piece of land if a 200m square takes 8 hours, a student applies a linear multiplier of 3 and incorrectly answers 24 hours instead of the correct 72 hours.', 'proportional', 'Proportional reasoning', 'Area of similar figures', 'student', 'seventh-graders and tenth-graders', 'ESM_Bock_1998_Predominance', 'DIRK DE BOCK, LIEVEN VERSCHAFFEL and DIRK JANSSENS', '1998', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '65, 66, 68, 71, 73', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bock_1998_Predominance.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39118, 'Students incorrectly apply non-proportional reasoning to situations where a linear proportional model is actually appropriate, often as an over-correction after discovering the non-linear nature of other geometric problems.', '', 'proportional', 'Proportional reasoning', 'Appropriate use of linear models', 'student', 'seventh-graders and tenth-graders', 'ESM_Bock_1998_Predominance', 'DIRK DE BOCK, LIEVEN VERSCHAFFEL and DIRK JANSSENS', '1998', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '79', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bock_1998_Predominance.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39119, 'When subtracting multi-digit numbers, the student uses an idiosyncratic, syntactic routine without objectifying numbers or understanding base-ten structure, resulting in remote, associative guesses.', 'When asked to calculate 5000 minus 2, the student answered 448, then 4048, then 4058, before guessing ''three thousand!'' and ''three million!''.', 'whole_number', 'Subtraction', 'Multi-digit subtraction', 'student', '7th grade', 'ESM_HeydMetzuyanim_2013_Co-construction', 'Einat Heyd-Metzuyanim', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '349, 350', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_HeydMetzuyanim_2013_Co-construction.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39120, 'The student attempts to solve word problems by syntactically combining all given digits using addition or subtraction, rather than interpreting the semantic structure or using multiplication.', 'In a problem involving 4 shirts and 6 pants, the student suggested subtracting 4, 6, and 2 from the prices: ''All the numbers (you) do minus.''', 'whole_number', 'Word problems', 'Choosing operations', 'student', '7th grade', 'ESM_HeydMetzuyanim_2013_Co-construction', 'Einat Heyd-Metzuyanim', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '350, 351, 352', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_HeydMetzuyanim_2013_Co-construction.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39121, 'The student seeks to learn arithmetic with negative numbers as a purely syntactic rule of attaching plus or minus signs, ignoring and rejecting the teacher''s conceptual model using a number line.', 'After the teacher explains adding (+4) and (-1) using right and left arrows on a number line, the student replies, ''So, no, this, I don''t have problem with this. Just minus or plus?''', 'integer', 'Addition', 'Directed numbers', 'student', '7th grade', 'ESM_HeydMetzuyanim_2013_Co-construction', 'Einat Heyd-Metzuyanim', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'secondary', '356, 357', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_HeydMetzuyanim_2013_Co-construction.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39122, 'Students struggle to shift from an analytical to an algebraic point of view, failing to treat complex mathematical processes like functions, series, or polynomials as plain elements of a set (vectors).', 'When asked to exhibit a basis for a 2-dimensional vector space consisting of Fibonacci series, many students answered with real numbers (u0, u1) rather than actual series, missing the contradiction.', 'algebraic', 'Vector spaces', 'Basis and elements of a vector space', 'student', 'first year of a French science university', 'ESM_Dorier_1995_Meta', 'JEAN-LUC DORIER', '1995', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '178, 179', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Dorier_1995_Meta.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39123, 'Students remain overly ''answer-oriented'' and fail to anticipate the generalizing power of new algebraic tools, defaulting instead to familiar, more cumbersome direct methods to solve problems.', 'When asked to give an interpolating polynomial satisfying four conditions, students ignored the newly learned sophisticated interpolation formula and instead solved a system of 4 linear equations in 4 unknowns.', 'algebraic', 'Polynomial interpolation', 'Linear systems vs. general methods', 'student', 'first year of a French science university', 'ESM_Dorier_1995_Meta', 'JEAN-LUC DORIER', '1995', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '183, 194, 195', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Dorier_1995_Meta.pdf')).
