research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39162, 'Students view fractions merely as a pairing of two whole numbers and inappropriately apply whole-number arithmetic rules to fraction operations and comparisons.', 'Children adding fractions tend to ''add tops and bottoms'' separately, or judge that 4/8 is larger than 2/4 by focusing on the magnitudes of the individual numbers rather than the ratio they form.', 'fraction', 'Fraction addition and comparison', 'Whole number bias', 'student', '1st Yr (Age 12) to 4th Yr (Age 15)', 'ESM_Booth_1981_Child-methods', 'LESLEY R. BOOTH', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '34', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Booth_1981_Child-methods.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39163, 'Students attempt to solve ratio and proportion problems using an intuitive method of successive addition rather than setting up an equivalent multiplicative fraction or ratio.', 'To determine food for a 25 cm eel based on a 15 cm eel getting 9 cm of food, a student calculates that 10 cm is two-thirds of 15, so they add two-thirds of 9 (which is 6) to the base amount of 9.', 'ratio', 'Proportions', 'Additive vs. multiplicative reasoning', 'student', '1st Yr (Age 12) to 4th Yr (Age 15)', 'ESM_Booth_1981_Child-methods', 'LESLEY R. BOOTH', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '34', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Booth_1981_Child-methods.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39164, 'Students mistakenly believe that multiplication must always result in a product larger than either of its factors.', 'When shown the correct equation 15 x .6 = 9, many students find it strange or wrong, stating ''the answer should be bigger than 9'' or ''the answer should be higher.''', 'decimal', 'Multiplication', 'Products with decimals less than one', 'student', 'fourth and fifth graders', 'ESM_Graeber_1990_Insights', 'ANNA O. GRAEBER AND DINA TIROSH', '1990', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '573', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Graeber_1990_Insights.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39165, 'Students choose division instead of multiplication for word problems with a decimal operator less than one because they know the answer must be smaller than the operand.', 'When asked how to find the amount of chocolate needed for .75 of a 3-ounce recipe, a student incorrectly chooses division, explaining ''Because you will need less than 3 ounces of chocolate.''', 'decimal', 'Word problems', 'Choosing the operation', 'student', 'fourth and fifth graders', 'ESM_Graeber_1990_Insights', 'ANNA O. GRAEBER AND DINA TIROSH', '1990', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '574, 575', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Graeber_1990_Insights.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39166, 'Students firmly believe it is mathematically impossible to divide a smaller number by a larger number, often reversing the divisor and dividend to compensate.', 'When asked to write an expression for equally sharing 5 pounds of trail mix among 15 friends, students incorrectly suggest ''15 / 5'', with one stating ''5 divided by 15 is impossible.''', 'whole_number', 'Division', 'Divisor greater than dividend', 'student', 'fourth and fifth graders', 'ESM_Graeber_1990_Insights', 'ANNA O. GRAEBER AND DINA TIROSH', '1990', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '580, 581, 583', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Graeber_1990_Insights.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39167, 'Students rely on a limited conceptual image of functions, rejecting piecewise relationships because they believe a function must be defined by a single algebraic formula.', 'Students refuse to recognize a functional relationship defined by two different algebraic expressions over two intervals as a function, describing it instead as ''a function provided by two formulas''.', 'algebraic', 'Functions', 'Piecewise functions', 'other', '', 'ESM_Cantoral_2003_Mathematics', 'RICARDO CANTORAL and ROSA M. FARFAN', '2003', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '260', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Cantoral_2003_Mathematics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39168, 'Students experience unacknowledged cognitive conflict by believing that an infinite repeating decimal like 0.999... is strictly less than one, while simultaneously calculating the sum of its equivalent geometric series as exactly one.', 'When asked to compare 0.999... and 1, students answer that 0.999... < 1 because ''writing never stops'', but when asked to sum the series 9/10 + 9/100 + 9/1000 + ..., they activate a calculation procedure and answer 1.', 'calculus', 'Infinite series and limits', 'Decimal expansions', 'other', '', 'ESM_Cantoral_2003_Mathematics', 'RICARDO CANTORAL and ROSA M. FARFAN', '2003', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '260, 261', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Cantoral_2003_Mathematics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39169, 'Pre-service teachers fail to distinguish between unit fractions and other types of fractions, inappropriately using proper or improper fractions when required to write questions specifically about unit fractions.', 'P15: Align 1/4, 6/3, -7, -2/3 and 4/3 and show them on the numerical axis.', 'fraction', 'Fraction definitions', 'Unit fractions', 'PST', '3rd grade', 'ESM_Yilmaz_2018_Investigation', 'Nurbanu Yilmaz, Ilhan Karatas', '2018', 'International Journal of Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '4, 8', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Yilmaz_2018_Investigation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39170, 'Pre-service teachers erroneously represent multiple unit fractions with different denominators on the same number line using equally spaced tick marks, ignoring the continuous and proportional nature of the numerical axis.', 'P17 identified such points on the same numerical axis as if there were unit fractions although they were not unit fractions and asked students to write numerical form of those fractions based on their positions on the numerical axis and align them in a descending order.', 'fraction', 'Number line representation', 'Unit fractions', 'PST', '3rd grade', 'ESM_Yilmaz_2018_Investigation', 'Nurbanu Yilmaz, Ilhan Karatas', '2018', 'International Journal of Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '4, 5, 8', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Yilmaz_2018_Investigation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39171, 'Students wrongly assume that words indicating an increase or higher position necessarily imply mathematical addition, failing to reverse operations when the initial state is unknown.', 'When given ''1800 fewer fans this week than last. 12000 this week.... How many last week?'', students struggle because they fail to resist the cue word ''fewer'' and reverse the operation.', 'integer', 'Directional quantities', 'Reversal of operations', 'student', 'secondary school class aged 11 and 12 years; 13-14 years old', 'ESM_Bell_1993_Some', 'ALAN BELL', '1993', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '116, 118, 119', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bell_1993_Some.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39172, 'Students incorrectly believe that horizontal objects must have horizontal images and vertical objects must have vertical images, or vice versa, under geometric reflection.', '', 'geometric', 'Reflection geometry', 'Orientation of objects and images', 'student', 'secondary school class aged 11 and 12 years; 13-14 years old', 'ESM_Bell_1993_Some', 'ALAN BELL', '1993', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '131', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bell_1993_Some.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39173, 'Students inappropriately map the geometric process of reflection to familiar pairs of linguistic opposites, deducing an image''s orientation based on verbal rules rather than geometric mapping.', 'A pupil producing a response said, ''this one is on the left and points up so that one must be on the right and point down''.', 'geometric', 'Reflection geometry', 'Spatial reasoning and language', 'student', 'secondary school class aged 11 and 12 years; 13-14 years old', 'ESM_Bell_1993_Some', 'ALAN BELL', '1993', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '131', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bell_1993_Some.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39174, 'Students mistakenly view reflection as moving a physical mirror along a line rather than as a mathematical folding operation, leading them to believe there can be multiple correct images.', 'Pupils justified their conclusion that there could be more than one correct image by showing how the mirror could be moved—still standing on the same line—to produce a movement of the image.', 'geometric', 'Reflection geometry', 'Mirror images vs. folding', 'student', 'secondary school class aged 11 and 12 years; 13-14 years old', 'ESM_Bell_1993_Some', 'ALAN BELL', '1993', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '131, 132', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bell_1993_Some.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39175, 'Students over-generalize the concept of symmetry, believing a shape has a line of symmetry if it can simply be split into two equal parts, ignoring whether those parts map onto each other via reflection.', 'Pupils placed erroneous lines of symmetry on the letter N or as a diagonal of a rectangle, justifying it by stating ''It is the same on both sides''.', 'geometric', 'Lines of symmetry', 'Congruence and orientation', 'student', 'secondary school class aged 11 and 12 years; 13-14 years old', 'ESM_Bell_1993_Some', 'ALAN BELL', '1993', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '132', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Bell_1993_Some.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39176, 'Students struggle to interpret the mean conceptually, viewing data locally rather than globally, and compare a single value to a preconceived anchor value rather than considering the entire distribution.', 'When discussing firm grades, S5 insists 7 is a good grade regardless of the data, while S4 compares it to the school anchor value of 5, rather than the distribution''s actual average.', 'statistics', 'Mean', 'Global vs. local interpretation', 'student', 'masters level', 'ESM_Hahn_2014_Linking', 'Corinne Hahn', '2014', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '245', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hahn_2014_Linking.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39177, 'Students mechanically calculate statistical measures as a school exercise without connecting them to the situational context, rendering them unable to use the results to answer pragmatic questions.', 'S9 calculates the exact average as 6.76 but when asked if that is a good result for the business, he cannot answer, demonstrating technical rationality disconnected from practical use.', 'statistics', 'Data Analysis', 'Contextual application', 'student', 'masters level', 'ESM_Hahn_2014_Linking', 'Corinne Hahn', '2014', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '246, 247', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hahn_2014_Linking.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39178, 'Students struggle to reverse their operational thinking to find a whole given a fractional part, often resorting to meaningless arithmetic combinations.', 'When asked to find the original number of cakes if 4 pieces represent 2/7 of the total, one student calculated ''0.2 * 4 = 2 * 40 = 80''.', 'fraction', 'Fractions as operators', 'Finding the whole', 'student', '7th year of school', 'ESM_Hasemann_1981_Difficulties', 'KLAUS HASEMANN', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '76', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hasemann_1981_Difficulties.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39179, 'Students erroneously multiply instead of dividing when faced with division word problems or computations involving fractions.', 'To calculate 63 divided by 9/5, a pupil incorrectly multiplied 63 by 9 to get 567, then divided by 5, or multiplied 63 by 9/5 to get 113.40.', 'fraction', 'Division', 'Choosing the operation', 'student', '7th year of school', 'ESM_Hasemann_1981_Difficulties', 'KLAUS HASEMANN', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '77, 78', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hasemann_1981_Difficulties.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39180, 'Students can mechanically execute a computational rule for multiplying fractions but fail entirely to interpret or apply the same operation when presented in a visual diagram.', 'Many students could correctly calculate 1/6 * 3/4, but when asked to shade 1/6 of a 3/4 dotted region in a circle diagram, they failed to recognize it as a multiplication exercise or execute it.', 'fraction', 'Multiplication', 'Diagrammatic representation', 'student', '7th year of school', 'ESM_Hasemann_1981_Difficulties', 'KLAUS HASEMANN', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '78', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hasemann_1981_Difficulties.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39181, 'Students incorrectly add fractions by applying a flawed algorithm where they add the numerators together and the denominators together, or by inappropriately transferring the rule for multiplication.', 'When adding 1/4 and 1/6, students wrote 2/10, calculating (1+1)/(4+6), or applied multiplication rules to get 1/18.', 'fraction', 'Addition', 'Unlike denominators', 'student', '7th year of school', 'ESM_Hasemann_1981_Difficulties', 'KLAUS HASEMANN', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '78, 79', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hasemann_1981_Difficulties.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39182, 'Teachers and non-teaching adults incorrectly assume that arithmetic word problems conforming to the intuitive ''taking away'' model of subtraction are always easier for students, failing to recognize when mental simulation makes those problems more cognitively costly.', 'Participants wrongly judged that students would find it easier to calculate 42 minus 39 (a difficult mental simulation within the intuitive scope) than finding the missing addend 39 plus what equals 42 (an easy simulation outside the intuitive scope).', 'whole_number', 'Arithmetic word problems', 'Problem difficulty and subtraction', 'mixed', 'second-grade and elementary', 'ESM_Gvozdic_2018_When', 'Katarina Gvozdic & Emmanuel Sander', '2018', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '161, 162, 168, 171, 172', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Gvozdic_2018_When.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39183, 'When using area models to add fractions, students may incorrectly add the number of shaded parts to get the numerator and the total number of pieces to get the denominator.', 'For 1/2 + 1/4, students drew two rectangles, dividing one into halves and one into quarters. They counted the shaded parts and total parts, arriving at incorrect answers of 2/6 or 3/8.', 'fraction', 'Fraction addition', 'Area models', 'mixed', '', 'ESM_Ahmed_2004_How', 'AFZAL AHMED, ALISON CLARK-JEAVONS and ADRIAN OLDKNOW', '2004', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'passing', '315', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Ahmed_2004_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39184, 'Prospective teachers fail to constrain the remainder in whole number division, inappropriately arriving at decimal answers or allowing remainders larger than the divisor.', 'Arriving at the solution 0.5 when asked about the remainder of 21 divided by 2, or concluding the quotient is 9 and the remainder 3 for 21 divided by 2 (because 2x9 = 18 and 18+3 = 21).', 'whole_number', 'Whole number division', 'Remainders and quotients', 'other', '', 'ESM_Tsamir_2002_Learning', 'Pessia Tsamir', '2002', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '274', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tsamir_2002_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39185, 'Prospective teachers mix informal and formal mathematical terminology when discussing divisibility, inventing imprecise terms to confirm meanings based on word structure or partitive/quotitive views of division.', 'Inventing words like ''timesing'' and saying ''You''re timesing it by 6, it''s a multiple of 6'', or stating that a number ''can be divided evenly''.', 'whole_number', 'Divisibility', 'Mathematical terminology', 'other', '', 'ESM_Tsamir_2002_Learning', 'Pessia Tsamir', '2002', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '275, 276', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tsamir_2002_Learning.pdf')).
