research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39207, 'Students memorize the procedures for solving an equation by rote but lack the conceptual understanding that solving an equation means finding the value that makes both sides equal.', 'A student can do ''miracles'' with procedures but cannot answer what the meaning of solving an equation is.', 'algebraic', 'equations', 'solving equations', 'in_service', 'junior secondary', 'ESM_Stouraitis_2017_Contradictions', 'Konstantinos Stouraitis & Despina Potari & Jeppe Skott', '2017', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'passing', '211', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Stouraitis_2017_Contradictions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39208, 'Students perceive algebraic expressions purely as a call to execute a series of operations rather than recognizing their underlying mathematical structure.', 'Students see the expressions 3(a+b+c) and 3a+3b+3c only in terms of addition and multiplication operations, missing the overarching structure of product and sum.', 'algebraic', 'algebraic expressions', 'structure vs. process', 'in_service', 'junior secondary', 'ESM_Stouraitis_2017_Contradictions', 'Konstantinos Stouraitis & Despina Potari & Jeppe Skott', '2017', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'passing', '211', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Stouraitis_2017_Contradictions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39209, 'Students mechanically delete exponents when simplifying algebraic fractions instead of conceptually connecting the simplification to division and power properties.', 'Students mechanically delete exponents when simplifying expressions with algebraic terms in the numerator and denominator.', 'algebraic', 'algebraic fractions', 'exponents', 'in_service', 'junior secondary', 'ESM_Stouraitis_2017_Contradictions', 'Konstantinos Stouraitis & Despina Potari & Jeppe Skott', '2017', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'passing', '211', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Stouraitis_2017_Contradictions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39210, 'Students mistakenly believe that when all terms in an algebraic fraction are cancelled out, the result is zero rather than one.', 'Concluding that a complex fraction where the numerator and denominator share identical factors after expansion cancels out to equal 0 instead of 1.', 'algebraic', 'algebraic fractions', 'cancellation', 'in_service', 'seventh grade, eighth grade, and ninth grade', 'ESM_Zodik_2008_Characteristics', 'Iris Zodik & Orit Zaslavsky', '2008', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '173', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Zodik_2008_Characteristics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39211, 'Students erroneously distribute square root operations over addition, or extract factors incorrectly, assuming linear properties for non-linear radical operations.', 'Believing that sqrt(50) = 2 * sqrt(25) = 10, or assuming that sqrt(4^2 + 3^2) equals sqrt(4^2) + sqrt(3^2).', 'algebraic', 'square roots', 'properties of radicals', 'in_service', 'seventh grade, eighth grade, and ninth grade', 'ESM_Zodik_2008_Characteristics', 'Iris Zodik & Orit Zaslavsky', '2008', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '173', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Zodik_2008_Characteristics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39212, 'Students incorrectly assume that corresponding and alternate angles only exist when the two lines intersected by a transversal are parallel.', 'Failing to identify corresponding or alternate angles if the base lines are visibly non-parallel.', 'geometric', 'angles', 'transversal lines', 'in_service', 'seventh grade, eighth grade, and ninth grade', 'ESM_Zodik_2008_Characteristics', 'Iris Zodik & Orit Zaslavsky', '2008', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '173, 174', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Zodik_2008_Characteristics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39213, 'Students believe that the parity of a number is inherently determined by its last digit, blindly applying this rule even when the number is represented in a non-decimal base.', 'Claiming that 34_five is even because it ends in a 4, or 121_three is odd because it ends in a 1, without converting the numbers to base ten first.', 'whole_number', 'base-10 and other base systems', 'parity', 'PST', 'preservice elementary', 'ESM_Zazkis_1998_Odds', 'RINA ZAZKIS', '1998', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '82', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Zazkis_1998_Odds.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39214, 'Students confuse an even exponent with an even factor, incorrectly assuming that any number raised to an even power must result in an even number.', 'Concluding that 3^100 is an even number simply because the exponent, 100, is an even number.', 'whole_number', 'exponentiation', 'parity', 'PST', 'preservice elementary', 'ESM_Zazkis_1998_Odds', 'RINA ZAZKIS', '1998', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '83, 84', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Zazkis_1998_Odds.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39215, 'Students mistakenly believe that a perfect square must be an even number, possibly confounding the everyday meaning of ''even'' (perfect, without rough ends) with its mathematical definition.', 'Stating that the product of 3, 17, and 19 cannot be a perfect square because odd numbers ''can''t be whole numbers when they''re square rooted'', whereas the product of 2, 12, and 16 has a ''good chance'' of being a perfect square because they are even.', 'whole_number', 'number theory', 'perfect squares and parity', 'PST', 'preservice elementary', 'ESM_Zazkis_1998_Odds', 'RINA ZAZKIS', '1998', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '79, 84', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Zazkis_1998_Odds.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39216, 'When prompted to abstract a general arithmetical rule from a set of subtraction problems, students struggle to conceptualize the structural pattern and instead retreat to executing isolated calculations for single concrete examples.', 'While trying to explain why some answers fall below 10 and others do not, Felix attempts a general explanation but ultimately falls back to pointing at the specific digits in the task 12 - 4 = 8, noting that taking 4 from 2 means it ''cannot be simply 10 or so.''', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'transition of the 10', 'student', 'second-grade', 'ESM_Steinbring_1997_Epistemological', 'HEINZ STEINBRING', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '61, 62, 70, 71, 81, 82', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Steinbring_1997_Epistemological.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39217, 'Pre-service teachers struggle to identify and coordinate the whole when posing fraction problems, sometimes mistakenly creating scenarios where fractional parts of a single whole sum to more than one.', 'A student posed the problem: ''Mum bought a pizza. She cut it into 8 pieces. Of the pizza, 1/2 was eaten by Dad and 3/4 by Lucy.'', failing to realize 1/2 + 3/4 exceeds one whole pizza.', 'fraction', 'problem posing', 'the whole', 'PST', 'primary school', 'ESM_Ticha_2013_Developing', 'Marie Tichá & Alena Hošpesová', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '138, 139', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Ticha_2013_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39218, 'Pre-service teachers fail to distinguish between different subconstructs of fractions, incorrectly viewing problems where a fraction acts as a measure and where it acts as an operator as mathematically identical.', 'Concluding that the problems ''Petr got 3/4 of a cake. He ate 1/2 of the cake'' and ''There were 3/4 kg biscuits in the box. Eva ate 1/2 of them'' are identical problems, viewing the wording differences as a ''fruitless play with words.''', 'fraction', 'fraction interpretations', 'measure vs operator', 'PST', 'primary school', 'ESM_Ticha_2013_Developing', 'Marie Tichá & Alena Hošpesová', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '138', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Ticha_2013_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39219, 'Students reject the equality 0.999... = 1, viewing 0.999... as an infinite sequence of growing approximations that never reaches its limit, leaving a minute, infinitesimal difference.', 'Ewa states that the difference between 0.999... and 1 is ''like the asymptote of a hyperbola: they will never touch each other'', while Jack insists ''what we add is getting smaller and smaller''.', 'calculus', 'limits', 'repeating decimals', 'student', '17 year old', 'ESM_Sierpinska_1987_Humanities', 'ANNA SIERPIŃSKA', '1987', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '378, 379, 380, 381', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sierpinska_1987_Humanities.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39220, 'Students argue that infinite division is impossible because it must ultimately result in an indivisible smallest quantity (an ''atom''), preventing the completion of an infinite sequence of additions in finite time.', 'When Tom proposes cutting a match into infinitely small pieces and adding them back together, Jack objects, stating ''There are no infinitely small quantities'' and ''Everything is finished in this world of ours'', referring to the atom as indivisible.', 'calculus', 'infinite series', 'infinite divisibility', 'student', '17 year old', 'ESM_Sierpinska_1987_Humanities', 'ANNA SIERPIŃSKA', '1987', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '381, 382', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sierpinska_1987_Humanities.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39221, 'Students incorrectly assume that algebraic rules for finite numbers apply directly to infinity, leading them to conclude that infinity divided by infinity equals one.', 'Thomas implicitly justifies 0.999... = 1 by stating ''It is infinity divided by infinity, isn''t it? I was always told that if you divide the same number by the same number then you get one''.', 'calculus', 'infinity', 'operations with infinity', 'student', '17 year old', 'ESM_Sierpinska_1987_Humanities', 'ANNA SIERPIŃSKA', '1987', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '379, 394', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sierpinska_1987_Humanities.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39222, 'Students hold a primitive conception of infinity as something exclusively unlimited and endless, leading to a mental conflict when confronted with a sequence that is infinite yet bounded.', 'Thomas notes the contradiction in Jack''s reasoning, showing the struggle to reconcile ''infinite'' with ''bounded'' by pointing out ''first you say that it is always possible to put one more nine, and then you say that there is a limit to it''.', 'calculus', 'infinity', 'bounded infinite sequences', 'student', '17 year old', 'ESM_Sierpinska_1987_Humanities', 'ANNA SIERPIŃSKA', '1987', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '383, 394, 395', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sierpinska_1987_Humanities.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39223, 'Students unthinkingly apply the computational rule for a simple mean to calculate an overall mean from two subgroup means, ignoring the different sample sizes (weights) of the subgroups.', 'Calculating the overall GPA as 3.5 by simply averaging a 3.2 GPA (from two semesters) and a 3.8 GPA (from three semesters).', 'statistics', 'arithmetic mean', 'weighted mean', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'ESM_Pollatsek_1981_Concept', 'A. POLLATSEK, S. LIMA AND A. D. WELL', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '193, 194, 196, 197', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Pollatsek_1981_Concept.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39224, 'Students erroneously assume that any random sample must average exactly to the known population mean, leading them to calculate expected but unknown sample scores by balancing the known sample scores against the population mean.', 'Finding that four known SAT scores in a sample of five average to 450, a student estimates the fifth score must be 350 so that all five average out to the population mean of 400.', 'statistics', 'sampling', 'estimating unknown scores', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'ESM_Pollatsek_1981_Concept', 'A. POLLATSEK, S. LIMA AND A. D. WELL', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '196, 198, 199', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Pollatsek_1981_Concept.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39225, 'Students misapply the computational algorithm by adding the two given subgroup means and dividing by the total number of items, yielding a mathematically illogical result.', 'Adding the GPAs 3.2 and 3.8 and then dividing by 5 (the total number of semesters) to get an overall GPA of 1.4.', 'statistics', 'arithmetic mean', 'computational algorithm', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'ESM_Pollatsek_1981_Concept', 'A. POLLATSEK, S. LIMA AND A. D. WELL', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'passing', '193, 194', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Pollatsek_1981_Concept.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39226, 'Prospective teachers treat fractions as a number of discrete pieces rather than as continuous quantities, often attempting to separate the numerator and denominator into separate whole-number entities when posing story problems.', 'When attempting to pose a problem for 2/3 * 1/2, stating ''there are 2 cakes'' and ''cut the cake into thirds'', which separates the fraction into parts rather than referring directly to the fraction as a quantity. Alternatively, creating a problem using only whole numbers to avoid the fractions entirely.', 'rational', 'fractions', 'fractions as quantities', 'PST', 'primary', 'ESM_Xie_2017_Examining', 'Jinxia Xie & Joanna O. Masingila', '2017', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '110, 111, 114', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Xie_2017_Examining.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39227, 'Prospective teachers misunderstand the semantic meaning of arithmetic operations with fractions, confusing what the fractions refer to and failing to recognize when their drawn model or procedural calculation contradicts the intended mathematical operation.', 'Interpreting the expression 3/4 - 1/2 as ''taking 3/4 away from half of an apple'', which leads them to draw a half apple, split it into fourths, and take 3 of those fourths away (effectively calculating 1/2 - (3/4 * 1/2) = 1/8).', 'rational', 'arithmetic operations', 'subtraction of fractions', 'PST', 'primary', 'ESM_Xie_2017_Examining', 'Jinxia Xie & Joanna O. Masingila', '2017', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '111, 112', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Xie_2017_Examining.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39228, 'Prospective teachers ignore or misidentify the reference unit when operating with fractions, mistakenly treating a fraction of a remaining or partial amount as if it shares the same reference unit as the original whole.', 'Posing a problem where a person puts 1/2 of a cake in the freezer, and of the remaining half, eats 1/4 of it. The student incorrectly solves for the remainder by subtracting 1/2 - 1/4 = 1/4, ignoring that the unit of 1/4 in the story is ''half of the cake'', not the whole cake.', 'rational', 'fractions', 'concept of unit', 'PST', 'primary', 'ESM_Xie_2017_Examining', 'Jinxia Xie & Joanna O. Masingila', '2017', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '112, 114', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Xie_2017_Examining.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39229, 'Teacher believes student-generated common-denominator fraction division algorithm is incorrect.', 'Student correctly computes 3/5 div 7/10 as 6/10 div 7/10 = 6/7; teacher says the algorithm was wrong.', 'fraction', 'division', 'alternative algorithms', 'in_service', 'seventh grade', 'ESM_Manouchehri_2000_Implementing', 'AZITA MANOUCHEHRI and TERRY GOODMAN', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '17, 18', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Manouchehri_2000_Implementing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39230, 'Students assume visually alike shapes are similar without verifying proportional enlargement.', 'Squares and rectangles similar; same people only bigger.', 'geometric', 'similarity', 'proportionality', 'in_service', 'seventh grade', 'ESM_Manouchehri_2000_Implementing', 'AZITA MANOUCHEHRI and TERRY GOODMAN', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'secondary', '24, 25', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Manouchehri_2000_Implementing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39231, 'Students believe that an infinite repeating decimal approaches but never exactly equals its fractional equivalent.', 'A student asserts that 0.333... is ''basically a third'' and ''doesn''t quite get to the third'', and that adding 0.333... and 0.666... results in ''nearly one''.', 'rational', 'infinite decimals', 'fraction equivalence', 'student', 'aged 10 to 12', 'ESM_Rowland_1995_Hedges', 'TIM ROWLAND', '1995', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '348, 349', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Rowland_1995_Hedges.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39232, 'Students misinterpret the syntax of compounded algebraic signs by focusing on perceptual shapes rather than structural meanings, leading them to incorrectly apply operations to isolated parts of a rational expression.', 'A student attempts to subtract 17 from the expression (x+17)/2 by subtracting 17 from the numerator alone, thinking it will leave x/2.', 'algebraic', 'algebraic equations', 'rational expressions', 'student', 'Grade 8', 'ESM_Radford_2007_Syntax', 'LUIS RADFORD AND LUIS PUIG', '2007', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '154, 155, 156, 157, 158', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Radford_2007_Syntax.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39233, 'When solving complex ratio problems, students often abandon formal multiplicative methods and fall back on informal additive ''building up'' strategies, which become cumbersome and lead to errors when fractions are involved.', 'When scaling an onion soup recipe from 8 people to 6 people, instead of multiplying by 3/4, students use repeated halving to find quantities for 4 and 2 people, but struggle to add the resulting fractions with different denominators.', 'proportional', 'ratio and proportion', 'recipe scaling', 'student', 'primary school', 'ESM_Streefland_1985_Search', 'LEEN STREEFLAND', '1985', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '88, 89, 90, 91', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Streefland_1985_Search.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39234, 'Children use absolute strategies instead of relative strategies when comparing probabilities, focusing on the absolute number of favorable outcomes rather than their proportional relationship to the whole.', 'When drawing a winning ball from two different urns, children may initially compare the absolute numbers of the winning color rather than the numerical ratios.', 'probability', 'comparing probabilities', 'absolute vs relative comparison', 'student', 'primary school', 'ESM_Streefland_1985_Search', 'LEEN STREEFLAND', '1985', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '82, 83', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Streefland_1985_Search.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39235, 'Students initially interpret the equal symbol as a command to perform an arithmetical operation or a prompt to write the answer, rather than a relational symbol indicating quantitative sameness between two sides.', 'When asked what the equal sign means in the sentence 246 + 14 = [ ] + 246, a student states ''The equal sign is there so you can put the answer by the equal sign.''', 'whole_number', 'equality', 'equal symbol', 'student', 'third-grade', 'ESM_Saenzludlow_1998_Third', 'ADALIRA SÁENZ-LUDLOW and CATHERINE WALGAMUTH', '1998', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '168, 169, 172, 173', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Saenzludlow_1998_Third.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39236, 'Students project the meaning of the horizontal line used in vertical addition onto the equal symbol, using it as a separator in a continuous sequence of operations rather than an indicator of equivalence.', 'A student conceptualizes the sequence of operations for 246 + 14 = 260 + 246 as a continuous addition 246 + 14 = 260 + 246 = 506, adding all numbers together.', 'whole_number', 'equations', 'notation', 'student', 'third-grade', 'ESM_Saenzludlow_1998_Third', 'ADALIRA SÁENZ-LUDLOW and CATHERINE WALGAMUTH', '1998', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '166, 176, 177, 178', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Saenzludlow_1998_Third.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39237, 'Students believe that an equality is valid if the exact same numbers and operations are repeated syntactically on both sides, focusing on nominal sameness rather than quantitative equivalence.', 'In the open sentence 246 + 14 = [ ] + 246, a student suggests putting 246 in the box to create visual repetition, concluding that ''two hundred forty-six plus fourteen equals two hundred forty-six plus two hundred forty-six.''', 'whole_number', 'equality', 'nominal sameness', 'student', 'third-grade', 'ESM_Saenzludlow_1998_Third', 'ADALIRA SÁENZ-LUDLOW and CATHERINE WALGAMUTH', '1998', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '171, 172, 175, 176', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Saenzludlow_1998_Third.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39238, 'When scaling an object up by a linear factor, young students may inconsistently apply the scaling factor to only one dimension or assume different scaling factors for length and width, failing to grasp that the 2D area increases by the square of the linear scale factor.', 'When told a giant''s foot is four times the length of the teacher''s, a student suggested the width should be only two or three times as wide instead of four.', 'proportional', 'linear enlargement', 'area scaling', 'student', 'third-graders (8-9 years old)', 'ESM_Streefland_1984_Search', 'LEEN STREEFLAND', '1984', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '328, 341, 342', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Streefland_1984_Search.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39239, 'Students conflate a mathematical model, such as a sector diagram or pie chart, with the physical object it represents, believing that a diagram representing a physically larger quantity must necessarily be drawn physically larger.', 'When asked to draw a sector diagram for a recipe that is four times larger, a student protests that the teacher''s drawn circle is ''too big'' for the blackboard, identifying the diagram''s physical size with the actual cake''s size.', 'proportional', 'modeling ratio', 'sector diagrams', 'student', 'third-graders (8-9 years old)', 'ESM_Streefland_1984_Search', 'LEEN STREEFLAND', '1984', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '344, 345, 346', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Streefland_1984_Search.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39240, 'Students inappropriately apply the divisibility rule for primes to composite numbers, assuming that if a composite number like 4 divides a squared integer, it must also divide the integer itself.', 'Writing ''4 | p^2 => 4 | p'' in the proof for SQRT 4, mirroring the valid step ''2 | p^2 => 2 | p'' from the SQRT 2 proof.', 'integer', 'divisibility', 'prime and composite factors', 'PST', 'undergraduate', 'ESM_Movshovitzhadar_1990_Preservice', 'NITSA MOVSHOVITZ-HADAR AND RINA HADASS', '1990', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '269, 270, 273, 274, 280', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Movshovitzhadar_1990_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39241, 'Students exhibit knowledge fragility by accepting the absurd conclusion of a fallacious indirect proof, failing to notice that the derived conclusion contradicts obvious arithmetic facts.', 'Completing the fallacious proof that SQRT 4 is irrational without mentioning that SQRT 4 equals 2.', 'rational', 'indirect proof', 'evaluating conclusions', 'PST', 'undergraduate', 'ESM_Movshovitzhadar_1990_Preservice', 'NITSA MOVSHOVITZ-HADAR AND RINA HADASS', '1990', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '273, 278, 279', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Movshovitzhadar_1990_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39242, 'Students inappropriately use additive reasoning to find a missing length in similar rectangles, focusing on the difference between given quantities rather than recognizing the multiplicative scale factor.', 'In finding the missing side of a 10cm wide rectangle similar to a 4x6cm rectangle, Sally explained that the side would be 12 cm long because 4+2=6.', 'proportional', 'similar rectangles', 'missing value problems', 'PST', 'elementary and secondary', 'ESM_Son_2013_How', 'Ji-Won Son', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '50, 52, 58', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Son_2013_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39243, 'Preservice teachers misdiagnose students'' conceptual additive reasoning errors as mere procedural failures to set up a ratio, leading them to rely on procedural show-and-tell interventions.', 'A preservice teacher diagnoses the error by saying ''Sally did not calculate the ratio of corresponding sides... This is incorrect not setting up a ratio,'' focusing on the missing formula rather than Sally''s conceptual misunderstanding of similarity.', 'proportional', 'pedagogical content knowledge', 'interpreting student errors', 'PST', 'elementary and secondary', 'ESM_Son_2013_How', 'Ji-Won Son', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '58, 59, 61, 64', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Son_2013_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39244, 'Students view the function symbol ''f'' not as a mathematical object or process, but merely as a label, an abbreviation for the word ''function'', or a sign indicating the start of a new problem.', 'Kasia states that ''f'' means ''the beginning of a new thought or a new task'' because tasks often begin with ''f(x) = ...''.', 'algebraic', 'functions', 'functional notation', 'student', 'secondary school', 'ESM_Sajka_2003_Secondary', 'MIROSLAWA SAJKA', '2003', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '238, 246, 247', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sajka_2003_Secondary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39245, 'Students associate the notation ''f(number)'' exclusively with the zero of a function, failing to understand it as evaluating the function at a specific argument.', 'When evaluating ''f(3)'', Kasia immediately thinks of a ''zero of the function'' because she had only encountered that notation when testing for zeros in class.', 'algebraic', 'functions', 'function evaluation', 'student', 'secondary school', 'ESM_Sajka_2003_Secondary', 'MIROSLAWA SAJKA', '2003', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '236, 237, 247', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sajka_2003_Secondary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39246, 'Students conflate the concept of a function with the algebraic formula used to define it, treating the notation ''f(x)'' and the expression interchangeably as ''the formula of a function''.', 'Kasia calls ''f(x)'' the ''formula of a function'' and treats ''f(x+y) = (x^2 - 2x + 3) + (x^2 + 5)'' as an equation representing a single formula.', 'algebraic', 'functions', 'formulas vs functions', 'student', 'secondary school', 'ESM_Sajka_2003_Secondary', 'MIROSLAWA SAJKA', '2003', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '237, 238, 239, 247, 251', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sajka_2003_Secondary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39247, 'Students interpret ''f(x)'', ''f(y)'', and ''f(x+y)'' as representing three distinct functions rather than the same function evaluated at different arguments, due to the different variable letters.', 'Kasia assigns f(x) = 2x+1 and f(y) = y+5, insisting they cannot be the same function because one has ''x'' and the other has ''y''.', 'algebraic', 'functions', 'variables as arguments', 'student', 'secondary school', 'ESM_Sajka_2003_Secondary', 'MIROSLAWA SAJKA', '2003', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '244, 245, 248', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sajka_2003_Secondary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39248, 'Students erroneously treat a functional equation as a standard numerical identity, applying properties like the distributive law to the function name as if it were a variable.', 'Kasia perceives an iconic similarity between ''f(x+y) = f(x) + f(y)'' and the distributive law ''3(2+1) = 3*2 + 3*1'', concluding that the equation merely needs to be verified.', 'algebraic', 'functions', 'functional equations', 'student', 'secondary school', 'ESM_Sajka_2003_Secondary', 'MIROSLAWA SAJKA', '2003', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '242, 249, 250', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sajka_2003_Secondary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39249, 'Students rigidly interpret the letter ''y'' within function notation, such as ''f(y)'', strictly as the ordinate of a point on a graph rather than as an independent argument.', 'Kasia assumes that ''y'' always represents the ordinate, and extends this to believe that ''b'' in ''f(b)'' must also be the ordinate of some hypothetical point on a drawing.', 'algebraic', 'functions', 'variables and graphs', 'student', 'secondary school', 'ESM_Sajka_2003_Secondary', 'MIROSLAWA SAJKA', '2003', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '240, 248', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sajka_2003_Secondary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39250, 'When adding square roots, students erroneously add the radicands or ignore the radical symbol entirely to perform whole number addition, treating the radical as an object without numerical meaning.', 'Calculating √2 + √2 = √4, or calculating 2 + 2 = 4 and then possibly reattaching the square root notation to form the answer.', 'other', 'irrational numbers', 'adding square roots', 'in_service', 'junior high school', 'ESM_Yang_2015_Exploring', 'Kai-Lin Yang & Hui-Yu Hsu & Fou-Lai Lin & Jian-Cheng Chen & Ying-Hao Cheng', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '27, 28', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Yang_2015_Exploring.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39251, 'Learners (in this case, participating teachers) hold a rigid, calculation-based perspective on ratio, believing that ratios can only be formed and compared when exact numerical values are provided, missing the qualitative essence of ratio.', 'Asserting that exact numbers are always needed to make comparisons, such as needing to know ''one is 4/5 and another is 1/5'' to evaluate which class performed better.', 'rational', 'ratio and proportion', 'qualitative comparisons', 'in_service', 'junior high school', 'ESM_Yang_2015_Exploring', 'Kai-Lin Yang & Hui-Yu Hsu & Fou-Lai Lin & Jian-Cheng Chen & Ying-Hao Cheng', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '26', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Yang_2015_Exploring.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39252, 'Students erroneously believe that empirical evidence or successfully generating examples that fit a pattern repeatedly is sufficient to constitute a formal mathematical proof.', 'A student asserts that a formula for generating Pascal''s Triangle rows based on powers of 11 is ''proved'' simply because ''It comes out all right every time.''', 'other', 'proof', 'empirical vs deductive proof', 'student', 'second phase of secondary education (approx. 16 years old)', 'ESM_Schalkwijk_2000_Learning', 'LOUIS VAN SCHALKWIJK, THEO BERGEN and ARNOUD VAN ROOIJ', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '298, 300, 301, 304, 305, 307', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Schalkwijk_2000_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39253, 'Students believe that values in a problem-solving procedure must be exclusively derived from operations on the given data, resisting the algebraic concept of assigning arbitrary values to an independent variable.', 'Gastón argues against choosing a number at random, insisting on using two separate calculation formulas because ''you have to say that you chose a number at random'' which he feels violates the norm to justify mathematical answers.', 'algebraic', 'algebraic procedures', 'independent variables', 'student', 'grade 7', 'ESM_Sadovsky_2005_Adidactic', 'PATRICIA SADOVSKY and CARMEN SESSA', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '101, 102, 103, 104', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sadovsky_2005_Adidactic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39254, 'Students incorrectly assume that different correct procedures for the same mathematical problem can yield different numbers of total valid solutions.', 'The class accepts two different procedures as correct but believes one procedure generates 201 solutions while the other valid procedures generate only 41 solutions.', 'algebraic', 'solution sets', 'multiple procedures', 'student', 'grade 7', 'ESM_Sadovsky_2005_Adidactic', 'PATRICIA SADOVSKY and CARMEN SESSA', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '105, 106, 107', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sadovsky_2005_Adidactic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39255, 'Students assume the total number of solutions to a two-variable equation equals the number of values in the independent variable''s domain, failing to account for whether the mapping is injective (one-to-one).', 'Students concluded there were 201 solutions because the independent variable (number of 10C coins) could take 201 values, not realizing that many of these values produced duplicate or invalid solution pairs for the 50C coins.', 'algebraic', 'functions and equations', 'injective mappings', 'student', 'grade 7', 'ESM_Sadovsky_2005_Adidactic', 'PATRICIA SADOVSKY and CARMEN SESSA', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '106', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sadovsky_2005_Adidactic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39256, 'Students inappropriately apply standard academic graphing conventions (such as reading the horizontal axis as strictly increasing from left to right) to idiosyncratic workplace graphs, resulting in contradictory interpretations of physical phenomena.', 'A student reading a machine-generated thermal stability graph from left to right concluded that the sample ''cooled down'' over time, even though she knew the sample was being constantly heated, because the horizontal axis was defined as an inverse function of temperature (10000/(Temp+273)).', 'algebraic', 'graphing', 'interpreting scales and axes', 'mixed', 'College', 'ESM_Williams_2007_Black', 'Julian Williams and Geoff Wake', '2007', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '328, 329', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Williams_2007_Black.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39257, 'Prospective teachers misunderstand the arithmetic magnitude of large numbers, treating the growth of place values as linear rather than exponential. While they know the mechanics of place value, they lack a true sense of scale.', 'When asked to place 1,000 on a number line bounded by 0 and 1,000,000, students routinely place it much too far to the right, typically between 10,000 and 100,000, failing to recognize that 1,000 is merely 1/1000th of the line.', 'whole_number', 'place value', 'magnitude and number lines', 'other', 'university', 'ESM_Zazkis_2011_Significance', 'Rina Zazkis & Dov Zazkis', '2011', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '252, 253', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Zazkis_2011_Significance.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39258, 'Students mistakenly believe that dividing a non-zero number by zero results in ''infinity,'' confusing the undefined arithmetic operation of division by zero with the limits of rational functions in calculus.', 'When discussing division by zero, a prospective teacher suggests the answer is ''infinity'', recalling the calculus concept of a limit where the denominator approaches zero, and misapplying it to the arithmetic division by absolute zero.', 'whole_number', 'division', 'division by zero', 'other', 'university', 'ESM_Zazkis_2011_Significance', 'Rina Zazkis & Dov Zazkis', '2011', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '255, 256', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Zazkis_2011_Significance.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39259, 'Students become confused by the mathematical notation for inverses, assuming that because a -1 exponent means the reciprocal for numbers, it should also mean the reciprocal for functions, viewing the notation as contradictory.', 'A student correctly states that 3 to the negative 1 is 1/3, but then questions why f^(-1) means the inverse function rather than 1/f, asking if mathematicians ''ran out of symbols.''', 'algebraic', 'functions', 'inverse functions', 'other', 'university', 'ESM_Zazkis_2011_Significance', 'Rina Zazkis & Dov Zazkis', '2011', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '256, 257', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Zazkis_2011_Significance.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39260, 'Students hold an atomistic view of the continuous real line, believing that magnifying the line would reveal discrete, disconnected points rather than a continuous structure.', 'Stating that magnifying a line x times would reveal ''un groupe de points distincts et désordonnés, eux-mêmes formés d''une infinité de points'' or drawing discrete dots.', 'geometric', 'the real line', 'continuity vs discreteness', 'student', '17-18 years old and first year university students', 'ESM_Robinet_1986_Les', 'J. ROBINET', '1986', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '382, 383, 384, 385', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Robinet_1986_Les.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39261, 'Students incorrectly believe that an infinite decimal expansion only approaches but never reaches its exact value, implying that a real number exists strictly between the fraction and its infinite decimal equivalent.', 'A student claims there is a real number between 1/3 and 0.333... because ''0,333 même si on développe les 3 à l''infini, ne pourra jamais atteindre la valeur exacte représentée par 1/3.''', 'rational', 'infinite decimal expansions', 'equality of representations', 'student', '17-18 years old and first year university students', 'ESM_Robinet_1986_Les', 'J. ROBINET', '1986', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '374, 375', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Robinet_1986_Les.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39262, 'Students confuse the mathematical properties of numbers with their written decimal representations, believing that a number is only ''decimal'' if it has a finite string of digits after a comma, or that reals are simply a superset of written decimals.', 'Arguing that there are more decimals than rationals because ''un décimal implique une virgule et des nombres ensuite... tandis que pour les rationnels on peut ruser, on peut écrire 2 comme par exemple 4/2 ou 16/8.''', 'decimal', 'number systems', 'decimal vs rational definitions', 'student', '17-18 years old and first year university students', 'ESM_Robinet_1986_Les', 'J. ROBINET', '1986', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '376, 377, 378', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Robinet_1986_Les.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39263, 'Students conceptualize the set of real numbers merely as a finite collection of known subsets (N, Z, Q, D) and perhaps a few specific irrationals, lacking the concept of irrational completeness.', 'Defining the real numbers strictly as ''L''ensemble des nombres réels R comprend les ensembles N (entiers naturels), Z (entiers relatifs, Z+, Z-), Q (ensemble des quotients).''', 'other', 'real numbers', 'set of real numbers', 'student', '17-18 years old and first year university students', 'ESM_Robinet_1986_Les', 'J. ROBINET', '1986', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '365, 366, 367', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Robinet_1986_Les.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39264, 'Students overgeneralize planar geometry experience to spheres, struggling to conceive great circles as simplest lines or expecting great circles to be parallel.', '12-13 year old: most simple spherical line was a quarter of a great circle because it always falls off from the middle of the ball.', 'geometric', 'spherical geometry', 'great circles and parallel lines', 'mixed', '10 to 22 years of age', 'ESM_Lenart_1993_Alternative', 'ISTVÁN LÉNÁRT', '1993', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '284, 285', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Lenart_1993_Alternative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39265, 'Students apply prior geography rigidly, believing there is only one pair of opposite poles and one equator on a sphere.', 'Thinking that only the North/South Poles and Earths Equator can act as poles and polars.', 'geometric', 'spherical geometry', 'poles and polars', 'mixed', '10 to 22 years of age', 'ESM_Lenart_1993_Alternative', 'ISTVÁN LÉNÁRT', '1993', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '287', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Lenart_1993_Alternative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39266, 'Students expect sum of angles in spherical triangle to be 180 degrees.', 'After applying plane geometry, class unanimously said 180 before measuring spherical triangle angles.', 'geometric', 'spherical geometry', 'sum of angles in a triangle', 'mixed', '10 to 22 years of age', 'ESM_Lenart_1993_Alternative', 'ISTVÁN LÉNÁRT', '1993', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '290, 309', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Lenart_1993_Alternative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39267, 'Students treat visual models like the number line not as sense-making tools, but as formalized procedures to be performed to satisfy the teacher''s requirement to ''show your work.''', 'Zane and Jack correctly computed 5/6 x 4 using the standard algorithm, then constructed an incorrect number line representation, eventually adjusting their drawing to match their numerical answer rather than using the drawing to find the answer.', 'fraction', 'multiplication', 'number line representation', 'mixed', 'fifth grade', 'ESM_McCloskey_2017_Theorizing', 'Andrea McCloskey & Gwendolyn Lloyd & Courtney Lynch', '2017', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_McCloskey_2017_Theorizing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39268, 'Students rely on memorized mnemonic tricks provided by outside influences for performing fraction division, bypassing mathematical reasoning and sense-making.', 'A student suggests using ''KFC: Keep, Flip, Change'' to solve 2/3 divided by 1/6, which the class adopts without being able to explain why the algorithm works.', 'fraction', 'division', 'algorithms', 'mixed', 'fifth grade', 'ESM_McCloskey_2017_Theorizing', 'Andrea McCloskey & Gwendolyn Lloyd & Courtney Lynch', '2017', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_McCloskey_2017_Theorizing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39269, 'Students do not distinguish very large finite sets from infinite sets.', 'Grains of sand in a desert or distance to distant star marked infinite.', 'other', 'infinity', 'distinguishing finite and infinite sets', 'PST', 'university', 'ESM_Kolar_2012_Analysis', 'Vida Manfreda Kolar, Tatjana Hodnik Cadez', '2012', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '401, 402', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Kolar_2012_Analysis.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39270, 'Students apply finite physical reality to geometric infinity.', 'Number of points on a segment corresponds to its length; cannot draw point for smallest triangle.', 'geometric', 'infinity', 'infinitely close and infinitely many points', 'PST', 'university', 'ESM_Kolar_2012_Analysis', 'Vida Manfreda Kolar, Tatjana Hodnik Cadez', '2012', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '401, 405, 407', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Kolar_2012_Analysis.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39271, 'Students count only numbers with same decimal precision as endpoints.', 'Only a few numbers between 0.9 and 1.5 counted.', 'decimal', 'density of rational numbers', 'bounded intervals', 'PST', 'university', 'ESM_Kolar_2012_Analysis', 'Vida Manfreda Kolar, Tatjana Hodnik Cadez', '2012', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '407', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Kolar_2012_Analysis.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39272, 'When trying to construct a midpoint using a historical physical tool (a rope), students apply a partial contemporary abstract conception (equidistance) but omit the necessary alignment constraint, failing to coordinate the physical tool with the formal definition.', 'Placing a midpoint I at an equal distance from the segment''s endpoints O and E, but not aligned with them, because they try to apply the contemporary definition of ''middle'' without considering the physical tautness of the rope.', 'geometric', 'geometric construction', 'midpoint', 'student', 'aged 11-12', 'ESM_Vittori_2018_Analyzing', 'Thomas de Vittori', '2018', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'secondary', '132', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Vittori_2018_Analyzing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39273, 'Students conceive of fraction equivalence as a matter of degree, asserting that some equivalent fractions are ''less equivalent'' than others based on their numerical distance from the reduced fraction.', 'Leanne states that two 8ths and three 12ths ''aren''t as equivalent'' and three 12ths and seven 28ths are ''hardly equivalent at all''.', 'fraction', 'equivalence', 'degrees of equivalence', 'student', '8 year olds and 12 year olds', 'ESM_Pirie_1992_Creating', 'SUSAN PIRIE AND THOMAS KIEREN', '1992', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '525', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Pirie_1992_Creating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39274, 'When transitioning from continuous to discrete fraction models, students may fail to map their continuous fraction knowledge and instead rely on primitive counting strategies disconnected from the fractional whole.', 'June successfully counts three eggs for a quarter of a dozen but fails to conceptualize this as ''one fourth'', asking ''What''s one fourth?''.', 'fraction', 'continuous vs discrete models', 'fractions of a set', 'student', '8 year olds and 12 year olds', 'ESM_Pirie_1992_Creating', 'SUSAN PIRIE AND THOMAS KIEREN', '1992', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '519, 520, 522', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Pirie_1992_Creating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39275, 'Students invent non-standard fractional language by extending the logical linguistic pattern of previous fractions rather than adopting conventional terminology.', 'Rebecca calls a fraction ''a thirty-twoth'' instead of ''a thirty-second''.', 'fraction', 'fraction names', 'terminology', 'student', '8 year olds and 12 year olds', 'ESM_Pirie_1992_Creating', 'SUSAN PIRIE AND THOMAS KIEREN', '1992', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'secondary', '518, 519', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Pirie_1992_Creating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39276, 'Students erroneously assume that the linear enlargement of a two-dimensional shape only scales in one direction rather than in both dimensions.', 'When drawing a giant''s shoe sole that is four times the length of a human''s, students initially assumed it would only be two soles wide instead of four.', 'proportional', 'linear enlargement', 'area scaling', 'student', '10 to 14 year-olds, third grade', 'ESM_Streefland_1993_Design', 'L. STREEFLAND', '1993', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '116, 117', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Streefland_1993_Design.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39277, 'Students experience a conflict between absolute and relative reasoning when comparing proportional densities, initially struggling to reconcile the absolute number of items with their relative concentration.', 'When comparing two ginger cakes of different sizes, students must settle a conflict between the absolute number of ginger chunks (e.g., 60 vs 120) and the relative amount of ginger per bite.', 'proportional', 'density', 'absolute vs relative comparison', 'student', '10 to 14 year-olds, third grade', 'ESM_Streefland_1993_Design', 'L. STREEFLAND', '1993', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'secondary', '126', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Streefland_1993_Design.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39278, 'Teachers and students struggle to resolve the contradiction that arises when extending operations of rational exponents to negative bases, erroneously expecting standard power theorems to remain consistent across equivalent fraction representations.', 'One student suggests that (-8)^(1/3) = -2 because the cube root of -8 is -2, while another argues it equals 2 because (-8)^(1/3) = (-8)^(2/6) = the sixth root of (-8)^2 = 2.', 'algebraic', 'rational exponents', 'negative bases', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'ESM_Tirosh_1997_Define', 'DINA TIROSH, RUHAMA EVEN', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'mixed', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tirosh_1997_Define.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39279, 'Educated adults hold persistent intuitions derived from natural numbers that addition and multiplication always make numbers bigger, while subtraction and division make numbers smaller, which interferes with their reasoning about rational numbers.', 'Incorrectly judging as false, or taking significantly longer to correctly identify as true, an incongruent statement like ''5/x can be bigger than 5''.', 'rational', 'arithmetical operations', 'effect of operations', 'other', 'educated adults', 'ESM_Vamvakoussi_2013_Educated', 'Xenia Vamvakoussi & Wim Van Dooren & Lieven Verschaffel', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '323, 325, 326, 327', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Vamvakoussi_2013_Educated.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39280, 'Students evaluate and simplify algebraic expressions based on visual or syntactic similarities, attempting to cross out identical numbers or variables that are not legitimate factors.', 'A student wonders if they can cross out the ''4''s in the expression 1/4 - x/4 to get 1-x, simply because the numbers look similar and there is a minus sign between them.', 'algebraic', 'fractional expressions', 'simplifying expressions', 'mixed', '9th or 10th school year', 'ESM_Ruede_2013_How', 'Christian Rüede', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '399', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Ruede_2013_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39281, 'Students perform arbitrary operations just to change the appearance of an algebraic expression, focusing on removing visual features like brackets without a mathematically valid goal.', 'A student tries to remove brackets from an expression by arbitrarily multiplying unrelated numerators and denominators across fractions, such as multiplying 1/4 by x/(4x+1).', 'algebraic', 'fractional expressions', 'equation solving', 'mixed', '9th or 10th school year', 'ESM_Ruede_2013_How', 'Christian Rüede', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '399, 400', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Ruede_2013_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39282, 'Students attempt to cancel factors in fractional expressions by only looking at the coefficients factored out in front of brackets, completely ignoring the content inside the brackets.', 'A student factors (6x^3 + 9x^2) / (4x + 6) to have x^2 and 3 on top, and 2 on the bottom, but concludes they can''t cancel each other out because they only look at the terms in front of the brackets and not the identical (2x+3) factors inside.', 'algebraic', 'fractional expressions', 'factoring and cancelling', 'mixed', '9th or 10th school year', 'ESM_Ruede_2013_How', 'Christian Rüede', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '398', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Ruede_2013_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39283, 'Students and schools view mathematically valid, informal home-based algorithms as incorrect, illegitimate, or cheating simply because they differ from standard school-sanctioned procedures.', 'A student rejects their mother''s valid alternative method for computing multi-digit division, telling her ''Mom, yours is not a right way'' or ''Mom, you''re wrong,'' while another considers finger multiplication to be ''cheating''.', 'whole_number', 'multiplication and division', 'informal algorithms', 'mixed', 'elementary school-aged', 'ESM_Takeuchi_2018_Power', 'Miwa Aoki Takeuchi', '2018', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '45, 46, 47, 48', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Takeuchi_2018_Power.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39284, 'Students interpret mathematical entities purely as computational processes to be performed rather than as static, self-contained objects, which limits their conceptual understanding.', 'Many 13-year-olds are unable to represent the division problem ''7 divided by 4'' as a fraction because they view division strictly as an active process rather than a static entity or number.', 'fraction', 'rational numbers', 'division', 'other', '', 'ESM_Sfard_1991_Dual', 'ANNA SFARD', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '11, 32', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sfard_1991_Dual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39285, 'Unable to conceptually reify an invisible mathematical object, students may adopt a debased, quasi-structural approach where they erroneously equate the abstract concept entirely with one of its specific visual or symbolic representations.', 'Beginners may identify the abstract concept of a function exclusively with its formula or graph, or imagine only linear mappings when the notion of function is mentioned.', 'algebraic', 'functions', 'representations', 'other', '', 'ESM_Sfard_1991_Dual', 'ANNA SFARD', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '21', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sfard_1991_Dual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39286, 'When comparing two data sets of unequal sizes, students inappropriately use absolute strategies, such as calculating total scores or subtracting an absolute number of participants to equalize the groups, rather than applying proportional reasoning or averages.', 'To make a ''fair'' comparison between a class of 36 and a class of 21, a student suggests to ''take off the same amount'' from the larger class and re-do the test, or simply calculates and compares the absolute totals without adjusting for the different group sizes.', 'statistics', 'data analysis', 'comparing distributions', 'student', 'grades 3 to 9', 'ESM_Watson_1999_Beginning', 'JANE M. WATSON and JONATHAN B. MORITZ', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '155, 156, 157, 161, 162', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Watson_1999_Beginning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39287, 'Students interpret arithmetic expressions like ''3 + 5'' strictly as commands to perform an operation, rather than as names or representations for a number, causing confusion with open equations.', 'Treating ''3 + 5'' solely as a command to add, making algebraic-style equations like ''[] - 7 = 4'' impossible to decipher.', 'algebraic', 'expressions and equations', 'meaning of operations', 'other', '', 'ESM_Whitney_1987_Coming', 'HASSLER WHITNEY', '1987', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'passing', '236', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Whitney_1987_Coming.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39288, 'When asked to translate a proportional relationship word problem into an equation, students mistakenly write the equation reflecting the literal word order rather than the mathematical relationship.', 'Writing 6S = P for the statement ''There are six times as many students as professors at this university'' (where S is students and P is professors).', 'algebraic', 'algebraic translation', 'word problems', 'other', '', 'ESM_Whitney_1987_Coming', 'HASSLER WHITNEY', '1987', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'passing', '237, 238', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Whitney_1987_Coming.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39289, 'Students haphazardly guess which arithmetic operation to use based on the numbers presented in a word problem, entirely disregarding the semantic meaning of the problem.', 'Adding or multiplying 10 and 6 when asked how far it is ''all the way around'' a 10 by 6 rectangular garden.', 'other', 'word problems', 'guessing operations', 'other', '', 'ESM_Whitney_1987_Coming', 'HASSLER WHITNEY', '1987', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'passing', '231', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Whitney_1987_Coming.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39290, 'Students hold a dynamic concept image of a limit, believing that a sequence approaches but never actually reaches its target, leading them to incorrectly reject that an infinite decimal like 0.999... is equal to 1.', 'Students state that 0.999... is ''just less than one'' because the process of getting closer to one goes on forever without ever being completed.', 'calculus', 'limits', 'limits of sequences', 'student', 'first year university and A-level', 'ESM_Tall_1981_Concept', 'DAVID TALL AND SHLOMO VINNER', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'mixed', 'primary', '158, 159', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tall_1981_Concept.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39291, 'Students believe that a continuous function must have a graph with ''no gaps'', be ''all in one piece'', or be given by a ''single formula'', causing them to incorrectly classify piecewise or rational functions.', 'Students classified the discontinuous function f(x) = 1/x (x ≠ 0) as continuous because ''the function is given by a single formula'', and classified a continuous piecewise function as discontinuous because ''it is not given by a single formula'' or has a ''sudden change in gradient''.', 'calculus', 'continuous functions', 'graphical representation and formulas', 'student', 'first year university and A-level', 'ESM_Tall_1981_Concept', 'DAVID TALL AND SHLOMO VINNER', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'mixed', 'primary', '166, 167', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tall_1981_Concept.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39292, 'Students incorrectly generalize the dynamic concept of limits to composite functions, assuming that if f(x) approaches b and g(y) approaches c, then g(f(x)) must approach c.', 'When asked if ''Suppose as x -> a then f(x) -> b and as y -> b then g(y) -> c then it follows that as x -> a, then g(f(x)) -> c'', twenty-one out of twenty-two students asserted it was true, relying on their ''getting close to'' concept image.', 'calculus', 'limits', 'composite functions', 'student', 'first year university and A-level', 'ESM_Tall_1981_Concept', 'DAVID TALL AND SHLOMO VINNER', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'mixed', 'secondary', '163, 164', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tall_1981_Concept.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39293, 'Students incorrectly believe that the reliability of a sample depends proportionally on the size of the population, assuming that a small sample from a very large population will likely consist entirely of extreme values.', 'A student argues that a sample of 30 is fine for a population of 40, but for a population of a million, a sample of 30 could easily be composed entirely of extreme values that ''completely sort of skew your data.''', 'statistics', 'sampling', 'sample size and population size', 'student', 'Year 11 (age 15-16)', 'ESM_Pfannkuch_2015_What', 'Maxine Pfannkuch & Pip Arnold & Chris J. Wild', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '353', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Pfannkuch_2015_What.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39294, 'Prior to instruction, students view a sample as a physical ''product sample'' or merely a part of a whole, focusing on the sheer number of data values rather than understanding it as a representative subset for statistical inference.', 'Describing a sample ''like those shopping stores that give you out free samples but you only get a little bit and it''s kind of a sample.''', 'statistics', 'sampling', 'concept of sample and population', 'student', 'Year 11 (age 15-16)', 'ESM_Pfannkuch_2015_What', 'Maxine Pfannkuch & Pip Arnold & Chris J. Wild', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '352', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Pfannkuch_2015_What.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39295, 'Students develop a purely procedural understanding of slope, treating it strictly as a geometric ''rise over run'' calculation rather than as a rate of change representing the relationship between two quantities. They mechanically write slopes as unsimplified fractions and struggle to interpret the numerical value in context.', 'When asked for the slope of a line where the rate is 4 people per desk, students write the slope as the fraction ''4/1'', explaining ''it moved up 4 and 1 to the right,'' but later struggle to interpret what the slope means in the context of the problem.', 'algebraic', 'linear functions', 'slope as rate of change', 'student', 'ninth grade', 'ESM_Zahner_2015_Rise', 'William Zahner', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '26, 27, 32', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Zahner_2015_Rise.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39296, 'When generalizing linear patterns, students often use an erroneous ''direct proportion'' method, determining the nth element simply as the nth multiple of the constant difference between consecutive terms, even for sequences of non-multiples.', 'Applying a direct proportion approach to sequences like 2, 5, 8, 11, mistakenly assuming a far term can be found by multiplying its position by the constant difference of 3.', 'algebraic', 'patterns and sequences', 'linear patterns', 'PST', 'preservice elementary', 'ESM_Zazkis_2002_Generalization', 'RINA ZAZKIS and PETER LILJEDAHL', '2002', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'passing', '380, 381', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Zazkis_2002_Generalization.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39297, 'Students exhibit a fixation on recursive, additive reasoning (finding the next term by adding the constant difference to the previous term), which prevents them from shifting their attention to the general, multiplicative structure of the pattern.', 'To find a large number in a sequence with a constant difference of 8, a student states they would have to ''count up by 8''s'' or ''subtract 8 until the last number... was discovered'', recognizing it is ''too tedious'' but failing to see the underlying multiplicative relationship.', 'algebraic', 'patterns and sequences', 'generalization', 'PST', 'preservice elementary', 'ESM_Zazkis_2002_Generalization', 'RINA ZAZKIS and PETER LILJEDAHL', '2002', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '381, 389', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Zazkis_2002_Generalization.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39298, 'Preservice teachers falsely evaluate a mathematically valid student-invented strategy using partial differences and negative numbers as incorrect or not generalizable due to their rigid adherence to traditional algorithms.', 'Several preservice teachers mistakenly considered Tommy''s strategy of subtracting by partial differences to be incorrect, asserting that getting the right answer was merely ''just luck''.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'student-invented strategies', 'PST', 'elementary', 'ESM_Son_2016_Moving', 'Ji-Won Son', '2016', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '113, 114, 123', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Son_2016_Moving.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39299, 'Students incorrectly subtract the smaller digit from the larger digit in a given column, regardless of whether it is in the minuend or subtrahend, to avoid regrouping or negative numbers.', 'In solving 62 minus 25, Sally puts 3 in the ones place because 5 is bigger than 2, effectively executing the larger minus smaller rule.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'multi-digit subtraction', 'PST', 'elementary', 'ESM_Son_2016_Moving', 'Ji-Won Son', '2016', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '109, 121, 126', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Son_2016_Moving.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39300, 'Students hold a closed view of ''math tasks'', expecting all necessary information to be provided and definite goals to be stated, leading them to view assumption-building in open modeling tasks as arbitrary ''guesswork'' rather than a mathematical process.', 'When working on the Roadkill Rabbits problem, students expressed frustration that they ''don''t know anything'', concluding that ''you''ll just have to assume, assume, assume... we''ll just have to guess'' instead of formulating mathematical relationships.', 'other', 'mathematical modeling', 'assumption building', 'student', 'first-semester university', 'ESM_Viirman_2018_Negotiating', 'Olov Viirman & Elena Nardi', '2018', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Viirman_2018_Negotiating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39301, 'Students misinterpret conditional mathematical assumptions in models by evaluating them as absolute empirical or biological statements, rejecting mathematically simplifying assumptions if they contradict real-world biological reality.', 'Students rejected the mathematical premise evaluating ''what happens if there are zero rabbits'' because ''there''s not any absence yet'', and applied the biological concept of ''carrying capacity'' to an assumption explicitly defining unlimited exponential growth.', 'algebraic', 'difference equations', 'modeling population dynamics', 'student', 'first-semester university', 'ESM_Viirman_2018_Negotiating', 'Olov Viirman & Elena Nardi', '2018', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Viirman_2018_Negotiating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39302, 'Students use part-whole relation for infinite sets, concluding whole is always bigger than part, rejecting bijective equivalence.', 'Naturals have more elements than evens because they contain odd and even.', 'other', 'actual infinity', 'comparing infinite sets', 'student', 'university', 'ESM_Moreno_1991_Conceptual', 'LUIS E. MORENO A. AND GUILLERMINA WALDEGG', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '219, 221, 224', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Moreno_1991_Conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39303, 'Students use geometric characteristics (length) to determine numerosity of infinite point sets, ignoring 1-1 correspondence.', 'CD has more points than AB because CD is longer, despite acknowledging correspondence.', 'geometric', 'infinite sets of points', 'segments', 'student', 'university', 'ESM_Moreno_1991_Conceptual', 'LUIS E. MORENO A. AND GUILLERMINA WALDEGG', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '225', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Moreno_1991_Conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39304, 'Students believe infinite sets cannot be compared; all infinite sets equal or incomparable.', 'As the sets are infinite, no comparison can be established.', 'other', 'actual infinity', 'comparing infinite sets', 'student', 'university', 'ESM_Moreno_1991_Conceptual', 'LUIS E. MORENO A. AND GUILLERMINA WALDEGG', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '224', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Moreno_1991_Conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39305, 'Learners incorrectly refer to the entire linear equation ''mx + b'' as the slope of the line, confusing the equation of the line with the specific parameter that determines its slope.', 'When asked what slope is, stating ''It''s mx plus b that''s the slope of the line.''', 'algebraic', 'linear functions', 'slope and linear equations', 'in_service', 'elementary school teachers', 'ESM_Walter_2007_Teachers''', 'JANET G. WALTER AND HOPE GERSON', '2007', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'passing', '214, 215', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Walter_2007_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39306, 'When observing a constant additive pattern in the dependent variable (e.g., adding a constant to y for each step of x), learners erroneously assume the explicit equation should involve multiplying the dependent variable (y) by that constant, rather than the independent variable (x).', 'Arguing that the term ''3x'' in the equation y = 3x + 5 does not make sense, and that multiplying y by three makes more sense because successive values of y are obtained by repeatedly adding three.', 'algebraic', 'linear functions', 'relating tables to equations', 'in_service', 'elementary school teachers', 'ESM_Walter_2007_Teachers''', 'JANET G. WALTER AND HOPE GERSON', '2007', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '213, 214', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Walter_2007_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39307, 'Students exhibit a negative recency effect, preferring the non-predominant event to balance the sequence because they confuse probability with representativeness and misapply the law of large numbers to small samples.', 'After a sequence of face, pile, pile, a student thinks the fourth toss has a higher chance of being face because the coin already fell twice on pile and only once on face.', 'probability', 'independence of random events', 'negative recency effect', 'student', '4ème and 2nde', 'ESM_Maury_1985_Influence', 'SYLVETTE MAURY', '1985', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '285, 294, 295, 296', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Maury_1985_Influence.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39308, 'Students attempt to explain the outcome of a random event using deterministic physical factors, thereby evacuating the random nature of the situation and avoiding uncertainty.', 'Stating that the outcome of a coin toss depends on the force with which it is thrown.', 'probability', 'randomness', 'deterministic thinking', 'student', '4ème and 2nde', 'ESM_Maury_1985_Influence', 'SYLVETTE MAURY', '1985', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '295, 298', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Maury_1985_Influence.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39309, 'Students incorrectly estimate the probability of a future independent event based strictly on its relative frequency of occurrence in a very short, immediately preceding sequence.', 'Concluding that the coin has a two-thirds chance of landing on pile and one-third chance of landing on face for the fourth toss, because the previous sequence was face, pile, pile.', 'probability', 'probability vs relative frequency', 'small sample size', 'student', '4ème and 2nde', 'ESM_Maury_1985_Influence', 'SYLVETTE MAURY', '1985', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '296', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Maury_1985_Influence.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39310, 'Students rely on superficial visual features or specific shapes to judge the equivalence of rational number representations, failing to grasp the underlying multiplicative relations.', 'Grouping cards showing the numbers 1/2 and 3/5 together and explaining ''they''re the same, if you draw a circle.''', 'rational', 'rational number representations', 'part-whole perspective', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'ESM_Moseley_2005_Students''', 'BRYAN MOSELEY', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '40, 44, 51, 59, 60', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Moseley_2005_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39311, 'Students fall back on inappropriate additive or non-multiplicative operations, such as subtraction, when attempting to understand ratio and operator concepts.', 'Attempting to use non-multiplicative operations such as subtraction to relate cards during the sorting task.', 'rational', 'ratio and operator perspectives', 'multiplicative relations', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'ESM_Moseley_2005_Students''', 'BRYAN MOSELEY', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '45, 61', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Moseley_2005_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39312, 'Students conceive of limits as asymptotes, believing that a sequence approaches its limit but can never equal or surpass it, leading them to reject limits for constant or certain oscillating sequences.', 'Brian states a constant sequence (an = 1) has no limit because it is a straight line and is ''approaching a number but not reaching it.''', 'calculus', 'limit of a sequence', 'limits as asymptotes', 'student', 'college', 'ESM_Roh_2008_Students''', 'Kyeong Hah Roh', '2008', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '223, 224, 228, 230', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Roh_2008_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39313, 'Students conceptualize limits as cluster points, incorrectly believing a sequence converges to any value that infinitely many terms get close to or equal to, leading to the assumption of multiple limits.', 'Erica asserts that an oscillating divergent sequence (0, 1, 0, 1...) has two limits, 0 and 1, because ''no matter what n you plug in, you would have some huge [number of points] around here.''', 'calculus', 'limit of a sequence', 'limits as cluster points', 'student', 'college', 'ESM_Roh_2008_Students''', 'Kyeong Hah Roh', '2008', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '225, 226, 227, 231', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Roh_2008_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39314, 'Students incorrectly believe that having infinitely many sequence terms inside an epsilon-strip implies having only finitely many terms outside it, failing to realize the complement of an infinite set can also be infinite.', 'Elena states, ''there is infinitely many points on the inside the graph, then it kind of says there have to be finitely many points outside, you know.''', 'calculus', 'limit of a sequence', 'epsilon-N definition', 'student', 'college', 'ESM_Roh_2008_Students''', 'Kyeong Hah Roh', '2008', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '221, 222', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Roh_2008_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39315, 'Students assume that any function should be linear, interpreting the values of a function as strictly proportional to the argument.', 'When asked to find g(10) after seeing that g(5) is 20, Gur concludes that ''10 must have 40'' by incorrectly applying a simple proportion.', 'algebraic', 'functions', 'linearity', 'student', 'first grade, grade 7', 'ESM_Sfard_2001_There', 'ANNA SFARD', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '17, 37', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sfard_2001_There.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39316, 'Students treat mathematical symbols like tables and formulas as self-sustained, independent objects rather than unified representations of the same underlying abstract mathematical entity.', 'Gur wonders why his partner is finding a formula to calculate a function value, treating the formula 5x-5 and the table as completely unrelated items without object mediation.', 'algebraic', 'functions', 'multiple representations', 'student', 'first grade, grade 7', 'ESM_Sfard_2001_There', 'ANNA SFARD', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '37, 45', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sfard_2001_There.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39317, 'Students believe there is a ''biggest number'' because they use the word ''number'' in an unobjectified way, treating number-words as a finite set of discrete things rather than an infinite sequence.', 'Noa claims ''two millions'' is the biggest number and doesn''t initially see ''million and one'' as a contradictory larger number because she views it merely as a concatenation of words.', 'whole_number', 'infinity', 'number sequence', 'student', 'first grade, grade 7', 'ESM_Sfard_2001_There', 'ANNA SFARD', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '19, 46, 47', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sfard_2001_There.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39318, 'Students mistakenly apply whole number reasoning to unit fractions, assuming that a larger denominator means a larger fraction, or that to divide a whole into more parts, one must use larger pieces.', 'Asserting that 1/8 is larger than 1/6 because 8 is larger than 6, or independently deciding to make a piece longer when attempting to partition a whole into 7 parts after successfully using a shorter piece for 6 parts.', 'fraction', 'unit fractions', 'inverse relationship', 'student', 'third grade', 'ESM_Tzur_2007_Fine', 'Ron Tzur', '2007', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '273, 276, 277, 283', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tzur_2007_Fine.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39319, 'Students erroneously believe that the product of two prime numbers results in a prime number, exhibiting a ''tendency towards closure'' where an operation on elements of a set yields an element of the same set.', 'Concluding that 437 is a prime number explicitly because it is calculated as 19 multiplied by 23, stating ''two prime numbers multiplied by each other are prime.''', 'whole_number', 'number theory', 'prime and composite numbers', 'PST', 'prospective elementary', 'ESM_Zazkis_2008_What', 'Rina Zazkis & Egan J. Chernoff', '2008', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '198, 199, 200', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Zazkis_2008_What.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39320, 'Students attempt to determine if a number is prime by checking its divisibility only against a limited set of ''small primes'' (e.g., 2, 3, and 5) which they view as the fundamental ''building blocks'' of numbers, rather than checking larger prime factors.', 'Testing a large number like 437 for divisibility by 2, 3, 5, and 7, and when these fail, concluding or leaning toward the assumption that the number is prime, treating primes like 19 or 23 as categorically different from ''building block'' primes.', 'whole_number', 'number theory', 'prime testing / divisibility', 'PST', 'prospective elementary', 'ESM_Zazkis_2008_What', 'Rina Zazkis & Egan J. Chernoff', '2008', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '198, 199, 200', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Zazkis_2008_What.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39321, 'Students incorrectly compare the magnitude of fractions by calculating the difference between the denominator and the numerator, concluding that the fraction with the larger difference is the smaller fraction.', 'Comparing 2/7 and 3/7 by subtracting the numerator from the denominator (7-2=5, 7-3=4) and concluding 2/7 is smaller because 5 is greater than 4. Also applying this successfully to 4/9 and 5/7 (9-4=5, 7-5=2, so 5/7 is larger).', 'fraction', 'fractions', 'ordering and comparing', 'PST', 'prospective elementary', 'ESM_Zazkis_2008_What', 'Rina Zazkis & Egan J. Chernoff', '2008', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '202, 203, 204', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Zazkis_2008_What.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39322, 'Students inappropriately change the required operation from division to multiplication when dealing with a divisor less than 1, violating the conservation of operations because the problem context remains structurally identical.', 'Calculating 116 times 0.8 instead of dividing, which gives 92.8 yen for 1 liter, a value illogically smaller than the given price for 0.8 liters.', 'decimal', 'division', 'choice of operation', 'student', '5th grade', 'ESM_Okazaki_2005_Characteristics', 'MASAKAZU OKAZAKI and MASATAKA KOYAMA', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '223, 234, 235', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Okazaki_2005_Characteristics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39323, 'Constrained by the implicit model that division must make the answer smaller, students misinterpret a quotient that is larger than the dividend as representing a smaller unit of measure rather than the standard unit.', 'Arguing that the result of 116 ÷ 0.8 (which is 145) cannot be the price of 1 liter because it is larger than 116, and therefore claiming 145 must be the price of 0.1 liters.', 'decimal', 'division', 'interpreting quotients', 'student', '5th grade', 'ESM_Okazaki_2005_Characteristics', 'MASAKAZU OKAZAKI and MASATAKA KOYAMA', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '235, 236, 242', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Okazaki_2005_Characteristics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39324, 'Students find division by a decimal less than 1 conceptually meaningless because they cannot map it to a concrete, operational partitive sharing model.', 'Stating ''I don''t know how we get 145 when we divide by 0.8'' because they could understand a concrete division like dividing by 2 to get half, but lacked a model for dividing by 0.8.', 'decimal', 'division', 'dividing by a decimal less than 1', 'student', '5th grade', 'ESM_Okazaki_2005_Characteristics', 'MASAKAZU OKAZAKI and MASATAKA KOYAMA', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '237, 238, 239, 240, 243', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Okazaki_2005_Characteristics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39325, 'Students erroneously base their judgments of an iteration sequence''s convergence or oscillation solely on its computer-generated graphical representation, concluding a sequence is ''finally constant'' or non-oscillating because of screen resolution limits, ignoring its formal mathematical properties.', 'Concluding that the iteration sequence for f(x) = -0.6x + 2 with a1 = 1 does not oscillate because the computer image shows a ''stagnating sequence'' that appears constant after a few terms, even though the sequence mathematically oscillates as it converges.', 'other', 'iteration sequences', 'convergence and oscillation', 'mixed', '11th graders', 'ESM_Weigand_1991_Iteration', 'HANS-GEORG WEIGAND', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '426, 427, 434', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Weigand_1991_Iteration.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39326, 'Students assumed the software operated using standard mathematical logic, failing to understand its internal algorithmic constraints when simplifying trigonometric expressions.', 'Attempting to simplify sin(x) + sin(2x) to sin(3x/2)cos(x/2) but receiving unhelpful expressions because the software''s simplification algorithms operated on the entire expression differently.', 'algebraic', 'trigonometric simplification', '', 'student', 'enseignement secondaire', 'ESM_Lagrange_2000_L''integration', 'JEAN-BAPTISTE LAGRANGE', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '4, 5', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Lagrange_2000_L''integration.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39327, 'Students reasoned about the textual form (syntax) of computer algebra output rather than the mathematical structure (semantics) when attempting to generalize rules.', 'Trying to find a general rule for the derivative of a polynomial times a trigonometric function by looking at the syntax of DERIVE outputs.', 'calculus', 'differentiation', 'generalizing rules', 'student', 'enseignement secondaire', 'ESM_Lagrange_2000_L''integration', 'JEAN-BAPTISTE LAGRANGE', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '7', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Lagrange_2000_L''integration.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39328, 'Students misinterpreted the exact rewritten differential expression from the calculator as the actual evaluated derivative.', 'Transcribing the calculator''s output d(f(a*x), x) as f''(ax) using prime notation and thinking it was the correct derivative.', 'calculus', 'differentiation', 'chain rule', 'student', 'enseignement secondaire', 'ESM_Lagrange_2000_L''integration', 'JEAN-BAPTISTE LAGRANGE', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '8', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Lagrange_2000_L''integration.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39329, 'Students believed that the product of a function tending to zero and a function tending to infinity must necessarily have a limit of zero.', '', 'calculus', 'limits', 'indeterminate forms', 'student', 'enseignement secondaire', 'ESM_Lagrange_2000_L''integration', 'JEAN-BAPTISTE LAGRANGE', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'secondary', '9', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Lagrange_2000_L''integration.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39330, 'Students invented an erroneous symbolic differentiation rule for exponential functions by overgeneralizing the power rule for polynomials.', 'Differentiating f(x) = 3^x as f''(x) = x * 3^(x-1), leading to f''(4) = 4 * 3^3.', 'calculus', 'differentiation', 'exponential functions', 'student', 'enseignement secondaire', 'ESM_Lagrange_2000_L''integration', 'JEAN-BAPTISTE LAGRANGE', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '12', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Lagrange_2000_L''integration.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39331, 'Students struggled to generalize polynomial factorizations because the software provided complete irreducible factorizations that obscured the partial patterns they were expecting.', 'Students conjectured a factorization in two factors for even n, but the software contradicted this by providing three factors for n=9, preventing them from progressing.', 'algebraic', 'polynomial factorization', '', 'student', 'enseignement secondaire', 'ESM_Lagrange_2000_L''integration', 'JEAN-BAPTISTE LAGRANGE', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'mixed', 'primary', '18, 19, 20', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Lagrange_2000_L''integration.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39332, 'Students make logical assumptions from everyday language, confusing perimeter with area outside boundary.', 'Student calculated area outside figure for perimeter; explained basketball perimeter shooter shoots from outside border.', 'measurement', 'perimeter', 'everyday language interference', 'student', 'high school', 'ESM_Lochhead_1992_Knocking', 'JACK LOCHHEAD', '1992', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'secondary', '551', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Lochhead_1992_Knocking.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39333, 'Students troubled by extensions violating original definitions (negative/fractional exponents).', 'Multiplying base -1/2 times is difficult to reconcile with the repeated-multiplication definition.', 'algebraic', 'exponents', 'negative and fractional exponents', 'student', 'high school', 'ESM_Lochhead_1992_Knocking', 'JACK LOCHHEAD', '1992', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'passing', '551', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Lochhead_1992_Knocking.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39334, 'Students divide both sides of an algebraic equation by a variable expression without considering that the expression might be zero, thereby losing a valid solution.', 'Simplifying the equation xy = y^2 to x = y by dividing both sides by y, losing the valid solution where y = 0.', 'algebraic', 'equations', 'cancelling variables', 'student', '9th grade', 'ESM_Zehavi_1985_Qualitative', 'NURIT ZEHAVI, MAXIM BRUCKHEIMER AND RUTH BEN-ZVI', '1985', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '37, 38', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Zehavi_1985_Qualitative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39335, 'Students believe that substituting a fraction into a linear algebraic expression will always yield a fractional result, making it impossible to obtain an even integer.', 'Claiming that it is impossible to obtain an even number when substituting a fraction into the expression 3x + 1.', 'algebraic', 'fractions', 'operations resulting in integers', 'student', '9th grade', 'ESM_Zehavi_1985_Qualitative', 'NURIT ZEHAVI, MAXIM BRUCKHEIMER AND RUTH BEN-ZVI', '1985', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'passing', '33', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Zehavi_1985_Qualitative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39336, 'Students may believe that two expressions that are syntactically equivalent via expanding or factoring must also be numerically equivalent for all values of x, failing to account for domain restrictions that apply to the original rational expression.', 'Andrew initially concluded that Expressions 3 and 5 would ''always be equivalent'' and ''always equal each other'' for any x, missing the fact that Expression 5 is undefined at x = -2 because he relied purely on their expanded and factored polynomial forms.', 'algebraic', 'algebraic equivalence', 'rational expressions', 'student', '10th grade', 'ESM_Solares_2013_Articulating', 'Armando Solares & Carolyn Kieran', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '127, 130, 131', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Solares_2013_Articulating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39337, 'Students expect that syntactic equivalence tests and manipulations on CAS tools will inherently recognize and display domain restrictions, leading to confusion when the calculator simplifies an expression to a form that eliminates the restriction.', 'Andrew expected the calculator to show ''Undefined'' or display the restriction when expanding Expression 5, assuming the CAS would recognize that the original form was undefined at x = -2.', 'algebraic', 'algebraic equivalence', 'CAS tool use', 'student', '10th grade', 'ESM_Solares_2013_Articulating', 'Armando Solares & Carolyn Kieran', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '132, 133, 135, 136', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Solares_2013_Articulating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39338, 'Students assume that if two expressions are not generally equivalent, they cannot yield the same result for any value of x, conflating the lack of general equivalence with the absence of specific equation solutions.', 'Andrew assumed Expressions 2 and 3 were never equal because their expanded forms differed, asserting they ''weren''t equal to each other no matter what''.', 'algebraic', 'equations', 'non-equivalent expressions', 'student', '10th grade', 'ESM_Solares_2013_Articulating', 'Armando Solares & Carolyn Kieran', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '138, 139', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Solares_2013_Articulating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39339, 'When asked to estimate, students inappropriately attempt to mentally apply standard written exact computation algorithms rather than using estimation strategies.', 'A student mentally calculated 12/13 + 7/8 using the algorithm ({ad + bc}/bd) to get 193/104 instead of estimating each fraction to 1.', 'fraction', 'estimation', 'fraction addition', 'student', '5th and 8th grade', 'ESM_Reys_1991_Estimation', 'BARBARA J. REYS, ROBERT E. REYS, AND ALFINIO FLORES PEÑAFIEL', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '364, 367, 368, 369', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Reys_1991_Estimation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39340, 'Students focus procedurally on the placement of the decimal point rather than the conceptual magnitude of the decimal number when making estimates.', 'For estimating 486 x 0.24, instead of thinking of 0.24 as about 1/4, a student focuses on the procedural rule, stating ''I have to remember to separate two numbers to place the decimal point.''', 'decimal', 'estimation', 'decimal multiplication', 'student', '5th and 8th grade', 'ESM_Reys_1991_Estimation', 'BARBARA J. REYS, ROBERT E. REYS, AND ALFINIO FLORES PEÑAFIEL', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '369', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Reys_1991_Estimation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39341, 'Students fail to recognize unreasonable estimates or order-of-magnitude errors because they do not reflect conceptually on their produced answers.', 'A student estimated 30% of 54,215 as 2000, failing to recognize the order-of-magnitude error even though they knew 30% is more than 1/4.', 'percent', 'estimation', 'reasonableness of results', 'student', '5th and 8th grade', 'ESM_Reys_1991_Estimation', 'BARBARA J. REYS, ROBERT E. REYS, AND ALFINIO FLORES PEÑAFIEL', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '364, 367', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Reys_1991_Estimation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39342, 'Young students perform numerical comparison tasks as socially oriented rituals rather than explorations geared toward an objective mathematical outcome.', 'A toddler touches blocks as if counting, but without 1-to-1 correspondence, treating the physical act of counting as the task.', 'whole_number', 'counting and comparing sets', 'early numerical thinking', 'student', 'early childhood to seventh grade', 'ESM_Lavie_2018_Routines', 'Irit Lavie, Aya Steiner, Anna Sfard', '2018', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Lavie_2018_Routines.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39343, 'Students fail to apply learned procedures in novel out-of-school task situations due to situativity and insufficient de-ritualization.', 'Fifth-graders try to describe a point on a number line over the phone by measuring with pinky fingers instead of using fraction-naming routines.', 'fraction', 'number line locations', 'transfer and situativity', 'student', 'early childhood to seventh grade', 'ESM_Lavie_2018_Routines', 'Irit Lavie, Aya Steiner, Anna Sfard', '2018', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Lavie_2018_Routines.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39344, 'Students hold an additive conception of sample, viewing a sample merely as a subset of a population and multiple samples as multiple subsets, which leads them to confound the number of individuals in a sample with the number of samples drawn.', 'Student M expects a simulation drawing 1,000 samples to produce a result of ''25 to 30%'' because the population fraction is 30%, misinterpreting the simulation''s output as a percentage of people sampled rather than a percentage of samples.', 'statistics', 'sampling', 'sampling distributions', 'student', '11th- and 12th-grade', 'ESM_Saldanha_2002_Conceptions', 'LUIS SALDANHA and PATRICK THOMPSON', '2002', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '262, 263, 264, 265', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Saldanha_2002_Conceptions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39345, 'Students judge a sample''s representativeness by comparing it exclusively to the underlying population proportion, failing to imagine a distribution of sample statistics to determine its relative unusualness.', 'Students understand that a sample statistic''s value varies, but base their judgment of its unusualness strictly on how it compares to the population parameter per se, rather than comparing it to a clustering of similar sample statistics.', 'statistics', 'statistical inference', 'sampling variability', 'student', '11th- and 12th-grade', 'ESM_Saldanha_2002_Conceptions', 'LUIS SALDANHA and PATRICK THOMPSON', '2002', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '264, 265', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Saldanha_2002_Conceptions.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39346, 'Forgetting to place the decimal point when performing decimal multiplication, despite correctly executing whole-number multiplication.', 'Student multiplies 60 by .20 and gets 1200, then self-corrects by reasoning about magnitude.', 'decimal', 'decimal multiplication', 'percent discount', 'PST', 'elementary', 'ESM_Lee_2016_Pre-service', 'Ji-Eun Lee & Kyoung-Tae Kim', '2016', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'secondary', '368, 369, 376', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Lee_2016_Pre-service.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39347, 'PSTs made algorithmically based mistakes by misapplying basic operational rules for the invert and multiply algorithm.', 'Multiplying without inverting (3/5 x 1/20 = 3/100), inverting the dividend instead of the divisor, or inverting both.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'invert and multiply algorithm', 'PST', 'elementary', 'ESM_Lee_2017_Pre-service', 'Mi Yeon Lee', '2017', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '339', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Lee_2017_Pre-service.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39348, 'PSTs made mistakes based on formal knowledge of fraction operations by inappropriately selecting multiplication instead of division.', 'Creating an expression 3/5 x 1/20 instead of 3/5 div 1/20.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'operation selection', 'PST', 'elementary', 'ESM_Lee_2017_Pre-service', 'Mi Yeon Lee', '2017', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '339', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Lee_2017_Pre-service.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39349, 'PSTs failed to transfer given information into appropriate mathematical ideas or symbolic representation.', 'Verbalizing erroneous reasoning or providing depictions which did not make sense as solutions.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'word problems', 'PST', 'elementary', 'ESM_Lee_2017_Pre-service', 'Mi Yeon Lee', '2017', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '339', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Lee_2017_Pre-service.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39350, 'PSTs provided inappropriate representations by using incorrect referent units or misusing partitioning and iterating.', 'Treating the 3/5 bar as the whole and partitioning into 20 parts, so dividend and divisor had different referent units.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'referent units and length models', 'PST', 'elementary', 'ESM_Lee_2017_Pre-service', 'Mi Yeon Lee', '2017', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '341, 342', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Lee_2017_Pre-service.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39351, 'Students mistakenly apply the limit definition''s counting process to an epsilon-strip with a width of zero, treating a straight line as a valid epsilon-strip to evaluate convergence.', 'Brian determined the limit of the sequence 1/n is 0, but rejected the formal definitions because he imagined a 0-strip on the x-axis and found that no sequence points would be covered by it.', 'calculus', 'limit of a sequence', 'epsilon-N definition', 'student', 'college', 'ESM_Roh_2010_Empirical', 'Kyeong Hah Roh', '2010', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '270, 271', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Roh_2010_Empirical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39352, 'Students evaluate the limit of a sequence by analyzing only a single, specifically sized epsilon-strip, failing to recognize the mathematical requirement that the condition must hold for ''any'' arbitrary epsilon.', 'Emily placed a 0.2-strip centered at y=0.1 over the sequence (-1)^n/n and incorrectly concluded that 0.1 could be the limit because infinitely many points were inside this specific strip.', 'calculus', 'limit of a sequence', 'arbitrariness of epsilon', 'student', 'college', 'ESM_Roh_2010_Empirical', 'Kyeong Hah Roh', '2010', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '272, 273', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Roh_2010_Empirical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39353, 'Students acknowledge that epsilon-strips can be arbitrary in size, but fail to realize that this arbitrariness inherently implies that one must consider strips whose widths decrease toward zero.', 'Beth generalized that for any epsilon-strip centered at y=0, there would be infinitely many terms of 1/n inside it, but she insisted the definition needed to be revised to explicitly state that the strips must get ''smaller and smaller''.', 'calculus', 'limit of a sequence', 'arbitrariness of epsilon', 'student', 'college', 'ESM_Roh_2010_Empirical', 'Kyeong Hah Roh', '2010', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '273, 274', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Roh_2010_Empirical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39354, 'Students incorrectly apply additive operations rather than multiplicative ones when comparing ratios, assuming that adding the same amount to both the numerator and denominator maintains the proportion.', 'Louise argues that a ratio of (2, 1) is equal to (3, 2) ''Because if I added a glass of water and of juice, it would give the same answer as B''.', 'proportional', 'ratio comparison', 'additive vs multiplicative strategies', 'student', '', 'ESM_Noelting_1980_Development_a', 'GERALD NOELTING', '1980', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '339', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Noelting_1980_Development_a.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39355, 'Students center their attention on only one dimension of a ratio (e.g., only the juice or only the water), ignoring the reciprocal influence of the other term on the total mixture.', 'When comparing equal ratios of (2, 2) and (3, 3), a student chooses B ''Because there is more orange here'', focusing solely on the absolute amount of juice.', 'proportional', 'ratio comparison', 'centration on a single term', 'student', '', 'ESM_Noelting_1980_Development_a', 'GERALD NOELTING', '1980', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '335, 336, 337, 339, 351', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Noelting_1980_Development_a.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39356, 'Young children fail to separate subsets of the same kind that belong to different groups, treating all the juice across both mixtures as one set and all the water as another.', 'When comparing mixture A to mixture B, a child points to the glasses of orange juice in both A and B simultaneously, unable to isolate the relationship within a single mixture.', 'proportional', 'ratio concept', 'identifying sets and subsets', 'student', '', 'ESM_Noelting_1980_Development_a', 'GERALD NOELTING', '1980', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '334, 335', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Noelting_1980_Development_a.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39357, 'When determining the range of values that make up a sequentially ordered composite unit (e.g., the 14th ten), students incorrectly assume the range begins with the order number (e.g., 141 to 150) rather than recognizing the correct interval (131 to 140).', 'When asked to which subscribers the 14th box corresponds, a student incorrectly states ''It is from 141 to 150'' instead of 131 to 140.', 'whole_number', 'place value', 'composite units', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'ESM_Triantafillou_2014_Revisiting', 'Chrissavgi Triantafillou & Despina Potari', '2014', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '347, 348, 350, 352', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Triantafillou_2014_Revisiting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39358, 'Students struggle to perceive algebraic expressions like 2N as a particular but non-specific number, instead confusing it with ''any'' number, leading to flawed algebraic proofs.', 'When asked to prove ''The sum of any two even numbers is even'', a student writes ''Let 2N be any even number. Then 2N + 2N = 4N'', failing to realize they used the same particular even number twice rather than two independent even numbers.', 'algebraic', 'variables and generality', 'algebraic proofs', 'student', 'secondary and university', 'ESM_Mason_1984_Generic', 'JOHN MASON AND DAVID PIMM', '1984', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '280, 283', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Mason_1984_Generic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39359, 'Students misinterpret mathematical quantifiers such as ''any'' or ''some'' by relying on their everyday conversational meanings, leading to invalid proof strategies or false evaluations of statements.', 'Given the task ''For any matrix A in W, show that A^2 = A'', a student picks one specific matrix, derives the result, and explains ''Well it said show it for any, so I just picked one.''', 'algebraic', 'quantifiers and proof', 'universal quantification', 'student', 'secondary and university', 'ESM_Mason_1984_Generic', 'JOHN MASON AND DAVID PIMM', '1984', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '281, 282', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Mason_1984_Generic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39360, 'Students view counterexamples as isolated, artificial anomalies rather than generic representatives of a class that disprove a general statement, leading them to subconsciously exclude the counterexample from the theorem''s scope.', 'When presented with the function x -> |x| as a counterexample to a claim about differentiability, students concentrate on the specific function rather than seeing it as representing a whole class of non-differentiable continuous functions.', 'calculus', 'proof and counterexamples', 'role of counterexamples', 'student', 'secondary and university', 'ESM_Mason_1984_Generic', 'JOHN MASON AND DAVID PIMM', '1984', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '285, 286', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Mason_1984_Generic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39361, 'Learners may view continuous probability distributions, like the normal distribution for human height, as arbitrary ''mathematical tricks'' or forced functions rather than patterns emerging from underlying probabilistic rules.', 'Lynn questions whether the normal curve is ''just a mathematical trick... maybe some mathematician somewhere just concocted this crazy function... and decided to say that height fit it.''', 'statistics', 'distributions', 'normal distribution', 'other', 'age fourteen to sixty-four', 'ESM_Wilensky_1997_What', 'URI WILENSKY', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '185', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Wilensky_1997_What.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39362, 'Learners confuse continuous and discrete probability models, inappropriately asserting that in a continuous distribution, the exact mean value can simply be ''ignored'' because it is just one out of infinitely many data points.', 'Stating that the number of women who are exactly 5''5" (the mean) shouldn''t matter ''because they''re just one data point and so can be ignored... because there are infinitely many data points''.', 'statistics', 'distributions', 'continuous vs discrete', 'other', 'age fourteen to sixty-four', 'ESM_Wilensky_1997_What', 'URI WILENSKY', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'secondary', '184', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Wilensky_1997_What.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39363, 'When modeling probability distributions, learners mistakenly believe that unequal step sizes (magnitude of change) with equal probabilities will produce an asymmetrical or skewed distribution.', 'Alan programs computational rabbits to jump 2 units right or 1 unit left with equal probability, expecting the resulting distribution graph to ''skew to the right''.', 'statistics', 'distributions', 'skewness and symmetry', 'other', 'age fourteen to sixty-four', 'ESM_Wilensky_1997_What', 'URI WILENSKY', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '190', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Wilensky_1997_What.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39364, 'Students erroneously assume that a decimal number with more digits after the decimal point is larger in magnitude, overgeneralizing whole number properties to decimals.', 'Asserting that 0.021 is higher than 0.87 because ''it has more digits behind the decimal point''.', 'rational', 'decimals', 'ordering and comparing', 'mixed', 'Year 9 and first year undergraduate (pre-service teachers)', 'ESM_Walshaw_2012_Affective', 'Margaret Walshaw & Tony Brown', '2012', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'secondary', '190, 191', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Walshaw_2012_Affective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39365, 'Students struggle to comprehend multiplication by one and zero because it presents a conceptual discontinuity between the singular and the plural, making standard concretizations seem trivial or nonsensical.', 'A child may wonder why the absence of anything should be interpreted as something like ''no boxes, with 3 cakes in each''.', 'whole_number', 'multiplication', 'multiplication by zero and one', 'other', 'elementary', 'ESM_Semadeni_1984_Principle', 'ZBIGNIEW SEMADENI', '1984', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'uncoded', 'primary', '382, 383, 384', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Semadeni_1984_Principle.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39366, 'When faced with subtracting a negative number, students tend to drop one of the operations and erroneously perform a standard subtraction of a positive number.', 'Students have the tendency to drop one of the two operations, interpreting ''subtracting -2'' simply as ''subtracting 2''.', 'integer', 'subtraction', 'negative numbers', 'other', 'elementary', 'ESM_Semadeni_1984_Principle', 'ZBIGNIEW SEMADENI', '1984', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '390, 391', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Semadeni_1984_Principle.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39367, 'Students misinterpret the operation of dividing by a fraction, confounding ''dividing by half'' with ''dividing into halves'' (which actually means dividing by two).', 'The phrase ''dividing by half'' is often interpreted as ''divided into halves'', i.e., dividing by two.', 'fraction', 'division', 'language of division', 'other', 'elementary', 'ESM_Semadeni_1984_Principle', 'ZBIGNIEW SEMADENI', '1984', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '390', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Semadeni_1984_Principle.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39368, 'Students incorrectly treat the entire visible portion of a number line or its arrows as the complete unit of 1, and inappropriately apply whole number counting schemes by counting tick marks instead of the continuous intervals.', 'When asked to place ''1'' on a number line that shows a longer interval, students might place ''1'' at the very end of the visible line, or they might determine a fraction''s value by counting the number of tick marks instead of spaces.', 'fraction', 'number line representation', 'identifying the unit', 'student', 'fourth and fifth grade', 'ESM_TuncPekkan_2015_Analysis', 'Zelha Tunç-Pekkan', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '426, 437', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_TuncPekkan_2015_Analysis.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39369, 'Students assume a real function is periodic if its graph appears visually periodic or results from a repetitive drawing process, even if it is not mathematically periodic.', 'Students identify a non-periodic graph as periodic because they perceive that ''the line always repeats itself'' or ''the figure remains the same figure,'' focusing on the repetitive drawing process rather than checking if the y-values obey a periodic pattern.', 'algebraic', 'periodicity', 'periodic functions', 'mixed', 'grades 3 to 12', 'ESM_Shama_1998_Understanding', 'GILLI SHAMA', '1998', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '264, 265, 271', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Shama_1998_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39370, 'Students believe that a period must strictly begin on the visual left edge of a given representation, rejecting valid periods that are located elsewhere.', 'A student claims that a correctly marked non-edge period of a series is not a period ''since the small rectangle doesn''t start [the representation].''', 'algebraic', 'periodicity', 'period identification', 'mixed', 'grades 3 to 12', 'ESM_Shama_1998_Understanding', 'GILLI SHAMA', '1998', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '267, 268, 269, 271', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Shama_1998_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39371, 'Students believe that the edges of a period must have identical values or characteristics, treating the period of a real function as a closed segment rather than a half-open one.', 'A sixth-grade student argues that a marked section is not a period because ''if the sun is shining in the beginning it should also shine at the end,'' insisting on identical starting and ending states.', 'algebraic', 'periodicity', 'period edges', 'mixed', 'grades 3 to 12', 'ESM_Shama_1998_Understanding', 'GILLI SHAMA', '1998', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '269, 270, 273', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Shama_1998_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39372, 'Students misclassify a function as globally periodic if it exhibits periodic behavior only to the right (or left) of a certain starting point.', 'A ninth-grade student suggests a graph that only becomes periodic to the right of the y-axis as an example of a periodic function.', 'algebraic', 'periodicity', 'periodic functions', 'mixed', 'grades 3 to 12', 'ESM_Shama_1998_Understanding', 'GILLI SHAMA', '1998', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '263, 271', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Shama_1998_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39373, 'Students define and identify a phenomenon as periodic merely if it contains a repeating part, even when that repetition is interrupted by arbitrary, non-repeating events.', 'A student considers a TV-station broadcast periodic because most programs repeat weekly, ignoring the fact that 3 or 4 programs change arbitrarily from week to week.', 'algebraic', 'periodicity', 'periodic phenomena', 'mixed', 'grades 3 to 12', 'ESM_Shama_1998_Understanding', 'GILLI SHAMA', '1998', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '263, 264, 271', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Shama_1998_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39374, 'Students ignore the percent sign and treat the percent discount strictly as a whole number, resulting in them erroneously dividing the original price by the percent discount.', 'To find a 20% discount on $69 and a 25% discount on $75, a student proposes dividing 69 by 20 and 75 by 25.', 'percent', 'percent discounts', 'division', 'student', 'sixth grade', 'ESM_Shreyar_2010_Thinking', 'Sam Shreyar & Betina Zolkower & Silvia Pérez', '2010', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'mixed', 'primary', '33, 36, 37', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Shreyar_2010_Thinking.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39375, 'Students attempt to calculate a final discounted price by directly subtracting the percent value from the original price as if the percent were an absolute monetary amount.', 'A student attempts to find the sought percent discounts by simply subtracting 20 from 69 and subtracting 25 from 75.', 'percent', 'percent discounts', 'relational meaning', 'student', 'sixth grade', 'ESM_Shreyar_2010_Thinking', 'Sam Shreyar & Betina Zolkower & Silvia Pérez', '2010', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '33, 42, 43', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Shreyar_2010_Thinking.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39376, 'Students strictly taught the standard long division algorithm make frequent procedural errors, such as omitting zeroes in the quotient or leaving remainders that are too large.', 'When executing long division, students might omit zeroes in the quotient, leave remainders larger than the divisor, fail to bring down the next digit, or record partial quotients of 10 or more.', 'whole_number', 'division', 'long division algorithm', 'student', 'third, fourth, and fifth grades', 'ESM_Treffers_1987_Integrated', 'A. TREFFERS', '1987', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '140', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Treffers_1987_Integrated.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39377, 'Because traditional instruction treats word problems as applications after-the-fact, students fail to recognize when to apply formal multiplication and division algorithms to context problems.', 'More than one-third of children cannot make optimal use of column arithmetic procedures for simple word problems, instead falling back on circuitous informal additive methods because they cannot identify the required operation.', 'whole_number', 'word problems', 'operation sense', 'student', 'third, fourth, and fifth grades', 'ESM_Treffers_1987_Integrated', 'A. TREFFERS', '1987', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '132, 133, 140', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Treffers_1987_Integrated.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39378, 'Students incorrectly add fractions by summing the numerators together and the denominators together, failing to recognize that added fractions must refer to the same unit element.', 'A student suggests that 1/2 + 1/3 = 2/5, erroneously treating fraction addition as analogous to combining test scores of ''one out of two'' and ''one out of three''.', 'fraction', 'fraction addition', 'unitization', 'mixed', 'adult', 'ESM_Swanson_2014_Making', 'David Swanson & Julian Williams', '2014', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '202, 203, 204', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Swanson_2014_Making.pdf')).
