research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39420, 'Students and teachers use ''chance'' as a magical, universal reverse-concept to casually explain away discrepancies between theoretical probability and empirical outcomes, avoiding a deeper mathematical analysis of experimental conditions such as trial independence.', 'When an urn game''s empirical results yield a gain despite Pascal''s theoretical prediction of a loss, the teacher and students dismiss the contradiction by simply claiming they had ''luck'' or that it was ''chance'', rather than questioning if the students'' draws were truly independent.', 'probability', 'chance', 'theoretical versus empirical probability', 'student', 'fifth grade', 'ESM_Steinbring_1991_Concept', 'HEINZ STEINBRING', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '509, 511, 512, 514, 515, 518', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Steinbring_1991_Concept.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39421, 'Students erroneously apply the factorization procedure to a quadratic equation that does not equal zero (e.g., set equal to a non-zero constant), incorrectly setting each factor equal to that constant based on superficial similarities to the canonical zero-product form.', 'Solving x^2 - 5x + 6 = 2 by factoring to (x - 2)(x - 3) = 2, then concluding x - 2 = 2 and x - 3 = 2, resulting in x = 4 and x = 5.', 'algebraic', 'quadratic equations', 'factorization', 'student', 'multiple grades including primary, high school, and college', 'ESM_Vinner_1997_Pseudo-conceptual', 'SHLOMO VINNER', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '117, 118', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Vinner_1997_Pseudo-conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39422, 'In word problems, students pseudo-analytically apply the arithmetic operation typically associated with a single cue word (e.g., subtracting when seeing ''less'') while completely ignoring the semantic structure of the text.', 'Reading that a milkman brought 7 bottles, which was 4 ''less'' than Sunday, and calculating 7 - 4 = 3 instead of 7 + 4 = 11.', 'whole_number', 'word problems', 'cue words', 'student', 'multiple grades including primary, high school, and college', 'ESM_Vinner_1997_Pseudo-conceptual', 'SHLOMO VINNER', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '125', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Vinner_1997_Pseudo-conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39423, 'Students misinterpret the algebraic expression -x^-1 + x as -(-x) + x due to a fuzzy visual memory, incorrectly conflating the multiplicative inverse with the additive inverse because both involve minus signs, and erroneously simplifying to 2x.', 'Selecting the multiple-choice distracter 2x for the expression -x^-1 + x.', 'algebraic', 'simplification', 'negative signs and inverses', 'student', 'multiple grades including primary, high school, and college', 'ESM_Vinner_1997_Pseudo-conceptual', 'SHLOMO VINNER', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '118, 119', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Vinner_1997_Pseudo-conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39424, 'Students pseudo-analytically assume that questions asking to find a ''number greater than x by y'' simply require calculating the difference (x - y), relying on fuzzy memories of similar problems rather than conceptual understanding.', 'When asked on a computer ''Which number is greater than 4 by 7?'', a 9-year-old pupil calculates the difference and answers ''3''.', 'whole_number', 'addition/subtraction', 'word problems', 'student', 'multiple grades including primary, high school, and college', 'ESM_Vinner_1997_Pseudo-conceptual', 'SHLOMO VINNER', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '119, 120', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Vinner_1997_Pseudo-conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39425, 'Students confuse geometrically distinct entities, such as a segment and its length, relying on uncontrolled associations without applying a conceptual control mechanism.', 'When asked ''What is the distance between two points?'', a student answers ''A segment'', and even after the teacher corrects them by saying ''it is the length of the segment'', the student insists ''But this is exactly what I have said.''', 'geometric', 'analytical geometry', 'distance', 'student', 'multiple grades including primary, high school, and college', 'ESM_Vinner_1997_Pseudo-conceptual', 'SHLOMO VINNER', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'passing', '107, 108', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Vinner_1997_Pseudo-conceptual.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39426, 'Students hold the intuitive conviction that the terms of a convergent sequence approach the limit but must never actually reach it.', 'Students hesitate to identify a constant sequence (like an = 1) or a sequence that eventually equals 1 as converging to 1 because the terms reach the value rather than merely approaching it.', 'calculus', 'limit of a sequence', 'approaching vs. reaching', 'student', 'secondary school and university', 'ESM_Przenioslo_2005_Introducing', 'MALGORZATA PRZENIOSLO', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '72, 73, 77, 87', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Przenioslo_2005_Introducing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39427, 'Students intuitively associate convergence with monotonicity, believing a sequence must be monotonically increasing or decreasing, at least from a certain point, to have a limit.', 'Students place a non-monotonic sequence like an = 1 + (-1/2)^n into a separate category of ''subsequences'' rather than recognizing it as a standard convergent sequence.', 'calculus', 'limit of a sequence', 'monotonicity', 'student', 'secondary school and university', 'ESM_Przenioslo_2005_Introducing', 'MALGORZATA PRZENIOSLO', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '73, 86, 88', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Przenioslo_2005_Introducing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39428, 'Students believe that a sequence can have a limit at a specific natural number n0, erroneously transferring the concept of right and left-hand limits from continuous functions.', 'Students think that the limit of a sequence at a natural number n0 exists if the discrete points (n, an) get closer and closer to (n0, an0) from both sides.', 'calculus', 'limit of a sequence', 'limit at a point', 'student', 'secondary school and university', 'ESM_Przenioslo_2005_Introducing', 'MALGORZATA PRZENIOSLO', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '74', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Przenioslo_2005_Introducing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39429, 'Students misinterpret the formal definition''s allowance for a ''finite number of initial terms'' to deviate, assuming this only applies to a small number (like hundreds) and not astronomically large finite numbers.', 'Students think that a sequence where the first billion terms act differently cannot be convergent because a billion is ''too many'' terms to ignore.', 'calculus', 'limit of a sequence', 'finite initial terms', 'student', 'secondary school and university', 'ESM_Przenioslo_2005_Introducing', 'MALGORZATA PRZENIOSLO', '2005', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '73, 78', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Przenioslo_2005_Introducing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39430, 'Students conceive of a fraction merely as an arrangement of identical parts within a whole, rather than as a specific quantity relative to the whole, leading them to believe that differently shaped halves of the same whole might not be equal in size.', 'When evaluating a rectangular half and a triangular half of a square, students assert that one half is larger than the other rather than recognizing they must have the same area because they are both half of the same whole.', 'fraction', 'fractional equivalence', 'area models', 'other', '', 'ESM_Simon_2017_Explicating', 'Martin A. Simon', '2017', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'uncoded', 'primary', '120, 124, 128, 132', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Simon_2017_Explicating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39431, 'Students believe division problems without integral solutions are either unsolvable or must be divided backward to yield an integer.', 'When solving 9y-7=0, students might state ''you can''t divide 7 by 9'' or they solve it by incorrectly dividing 9 by 7.', 'algebraic', 'linear equations', 'division step', 'student', 'ninth-grade', 'ESM_Peck_2016_Reinventing', 'Frederick Peck & Michael Matassa', '2016', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '245, 246', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Peck_2016_Reinventing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39432, 'Because they do not recognize the direction of division inherent in partitive problems, students divide the numbers both ways and choose the quotient that is an integer or ''looks easier''.', 'A student divided 7 by 4 and 4 by 7, then chose the incorrect answer (1.75) because it ''looks easier'' and rejected 0.57142 because ''you can''t split a pizza into that''.', 'fraction', 'partitive division', 'direction of division', 'student', 'ninth-grade', 'ESM_Peck_2016_Reinventing', 'Frederick Peck & Michael Matassa', '2016', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '255, 256, 263, 264', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Peck_2016_Reinventing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39433, 'In fair-sharing problems, students incorrectly write the reciprocal of the required fraction because they perceive the numerator and denominator as separate extensive quantities rather than a single intensive unit rate.', 'A student wrote 5/4 to represent 4 sandwiches shared among 5 people, justifying it by pointing to the numerals 5 and 4 in the problem text.', 'fraction', 'fair sharing', 'intensive quantities', 'student', 'ninth-grade', 'ESM_Peck_2016_Reinventing', 'Frederick Peck & Michael Matassa', '2016', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '254, 259, 260', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Peck_2016_Reinventing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39434, 'Additive approach: judge estimates by absolute error rather than proportional reasoning.', 'Estimates of 25 for 20 dots and 55 for 50 dots equally good; missing by 1 is same for 10 as for 1000.', 'proportional', 'proportional reasoning', 'relative vs absolute error', 'student', 'third grade', 'ESM_Markovits_1997_Relative', 'ZVIA MARKOVITS and RINA HERSHKOWITZ', '1997', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '31, 41, 42, 43, 44', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Markovits_1997_Relative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39435, 'Students intuitively interpret the term ''average'' using colloquial meanings (such as ''normal'') or other measures of central tendency (like ''most'' or ''middle''), failing to apply the arithmetic mean.', 'Explaining that an average of 3 hours of TV per day means that 3 hours is ''more popular'' or ''what the most students had,'' relying on the mode.', 'statistics', 'measures of central tendency', 'average (mean, median, mode)', 'student', 'Grades 3, 6, and 9', 'ESM_Watson_2007_Role', 'JANE M. WATSON', '2007', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '23, 24, 33, 34', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Watson_2007_Role.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39436, 'Students interpret the decimal value of an average literally when referring to discrete objects (like children), incorrectly believing an average cannot be a non-integer.', 'Claiming that an average of 2.3 children is impossible ''because you can''t really have 3/4 of a child unless they have lost like 2 legs''.', 'statistics', 'arithmetic mean', 'interpreting non-integer averages', 'student', 'Grades 3, 6, and 9', 'ESM_Watson_2007_Role', 'JANE M. WATSON', '2007', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '29, 37', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Watson_2007_Role.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39437, 'When finding an overall average from two subgroups of unequal sizes with different subgroup averages, students erroneously calculate the simple mean of the two averages (e.g., adding them and dividing by two) rather than computing a weighted mean.', 'Calculating the average of 8 hours (for 25 students) and 4 hours (for 75 students) by adding 8 and 4 to get 12, then dividing by 2 to get 6 hours.', 'statistics', 'arithmetic mean', 'weighted mean', 'student', 'Grades 3, 6, and 9', 'ESM_Watson_2007_Role', 'JANE M. WATSON', '2007', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '30, 36, 37, 38', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Watson_2007_Role.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39438, 'Students compare decimal values as if they are whole numbers, assuming that a decimal with more digits or a larger integer value is greater.', 'A student argues that the value .231 is larger than .31 because they are comparing the decimal values as if they are whole numbers.', 'decimal', 'decimal magnitude', 'comparison', 'student', 'fifth grade', 'ESM_Moskal_2000_Making', 'BARBARA M. MOSKAL and MARIA E. MAGONE', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '316', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Moskal_2000_Making.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39439, 'Students incorrectly assume that the farther a digit is from the decimal point, the larger the value of that number, applying whole number distance rules to decimals.', 'A student argues that .02 is larger then .2, since in the former example the digit 2 is farther from the decimal than is the digit 2 in the later example.', 'decimal', 'decimal magnitude', 'place value', 'student', 'fifth grade', 'ESM_Moskal_2000_Making', 'BARBARA M. MOSKAL and MARIA E. MAGONE', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '316', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Moskal_2000_Making.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39440, 'Students incorrectly label decimal place values by reflecting the whole number column names across the decimal point instead of the ones position.', 'Labeling the digits in .023 such that 3 is in the oneths, 2 is in the tenths and 0 is in the hundredths.', 'decimal', 'place value', 'labels', 'student', 'fifth grade', 'ESM_Moskal_2000_Making', 'BARBARA M. MOSKAL and MARIA E. MAGONE', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '317', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Moskal_2000_Making.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39441, 'Students inappropriately apply whole number rules to decimal zeros, concluding that leading zeros have no effect while trailing zeros increase the magnitude to that of a whole number.', 'Afwandi explains that .008000 is the greatest value because ''The zeros in front don''t mean anything, but it has zero''s in the back and that means that it is (8,000)-eight thousand''.', 'decimal', 'decimal magnitude', 'role of zero', 'student', 'fifth grade', 'ESM_Moskal_2000_Making', 'BARBARA M. MOSKAL and MARIA E. MAGONE', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '323, 324', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Moskal_2000_Making.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39442, 'Students treat a decimal value as if it were a fraction by replacing the decimal point with a fraction bar, sometimes inserting a 1 as a placeholder if there is no whole number.', 'Anthony asserts that ''.8 is 1/8 of a whole'', incorrectly converting the decimal by changing the decimal point to a fraction bar.', 'decimal', 'fraction-decimal equivalence', 'conversion', 'student', 'fifth grade', 'ESM_Moskal_2000_Making', 'BARBARA M. MOSKAL and MARIA E. MAGONE', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '317, 318, 324, 325', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Moskal_2000_Making.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39443, 'The student believes it is impossible to determine fractional relationships or solve fraction tasks if the exact numerical magnitude of the whole is unknown or unspecified.', 'When asked what part of the money is left over after giving away parts of a ''certain amount of money'', Ann replies, ''I don''t know because I don''t know the amount of money that you have in your pocket.''', 'fraction', 'fractional relationships', 'unspecified wholes', 'student', 'third graders', 'ESM_Saenzludlow_1995_Ann''s', 'ADALIRA SÁENZ-LUDLOW', '1995', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '124, 125', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Saenzludlow_1995_Ann''s.pdf')).
