research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39446, 'Students perceive the limit as a global property of continuous functions, expecting the graph to be drawn without lifting a pencil from the ends of the domain up to the point of the limit.', 'When asked to complete a graph to show a limit at infinity, students assume points not represented on the graph behave similarly to the visible portion, incorrectly stating that the graph ''constantly waves''.', 'calculus', 'limit of a function', 'graphical representations', 'student', 'university', 'ESM_Przenioslo_2004_Images', 'MALGORZATA PRZENIOSLO', '2004', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '119, 120', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Przenioslo_2004_Images.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39447, 'Students believe that the terms of a sequence must approach but never reach the limit, causing them to invent different types of infinity to explain how 0.999... and 1 can both be limits of the same sequence.', 'A student explained that 0.999... and 1 are different limits because sequence terms reach 0.999... in ''aleph-null'' infinity, but they approach and do not reach 1 in a larger ''continuum type'' infinity.', 'calculus', 'limit of a sequence', 'repeating decimals', 'student', 'university', 'ESM_Przenioslo_2004_Images', 'MALGORZATA PRZENIOSLO', '2004', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '123, 124', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Przenioslo_2004_Images.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39448, 'Students schematically identify limits by visual matching, assuming that if a graph''s shape roughly resembles a familiar elementary function, it must share that function''s limit properties.', 'Interpreting a given graph that waves as y=sin(x) merely based on its approximate shape, without knowing the actual function formula.', 'calculus', 'limit of a function', 'graphical representations', 'student', 'university', 'ESM_Przenioslo_2004_Images', 'MALGORZATA PRZENIOSLO', '2004', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '128', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Przenioslo_2004_Images.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39449, 'Students believe that a limit exists at a point if and only if the function is defined at that point, extending the property of continuous functions to mistakenly assert that the limit always equals the function''s value.', 'Stating ''the limit of a function at a point is equal to the value of the function at this point'' and applying it to isolated points or discontinuous piecewise functions.', 'calculus', 'limit of a function', 'value at a point', 'student', 'university', 'ESM_Przenioslo_2004_Images', 'MALGORZATA PRZENIOSLO', '2004', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '125, 128', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Przenioslo_2004_Images.pdf')).
