research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39467, 'When solving partitive division problems, students relying on advanced formal algorithms or known products often experience a clash between computation and situation, blindly manipulating numbers and rejecting correct facts because they cannot relate the multiplicative structure to the sharing context.', 'A sixth grader attempting to share 28 marbles among 4 boys tried the division algorithm, rejected the answer 0.14, tried a multiplication algorithm resulting in 112, and ultimately rejected the known fact ''7x4=28'' because she could not understand how it applied to the situation.', 'whole_number', 'division', 'partitive division and algorithms', 'student', 'grade 2-6', 'ESM_Neuman_1999_Early', 'DAGMAR NEUMAN', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '117, 118', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Neuman_1999_Early.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39468, 'Students eagerly using compressed computational strategies, such as repeated addition, for partitive division problems inadvertently transform the problem into a quotitive scenario and fail to map their numerical chunks back to the original sharing situation.', 'A third grader solving ''28 marbles, 7 boys'' grouped the marbles as ''seven and seven... twenty-eight'', arriving at 4, but when asked why he chose seven, he incorrectly explained he was giving 7 marbles to one boy, realizing there were not enough marbles for all boys.', 'whole_number', 'division', 'partitive vs quotitive reasoning', 'student', 'grade 2-6', 'ESM_Neuman_1999_Early', 'DAGMAR NEUMAN', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '114, 115', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Neuman_1999_Early.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39469, 'Children inappropriately apply whole number strategies and concepts to fraction symbols, viewing fractions as two unrelated whole numbers or assuming fractions with larger denominators are larger.', 'Believing that fractions with larger denominators were larger than those with smaller denominators.', 'fraction', 'fraction symbols and magnitude', 'whole number interference', 'student', '', 'ESM_Pitkethly_1996_Review', 'ANNE PITKETHLY AND ROBERT HUNTING', '1996', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '10, 11, 12, 23', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Pitkethly_1996_Review.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39470, 'An overreliance on continuous part-whole area models leads students to rigidly view fractions as pieces of a specific shape, preventing them from adapting to discrete contexts or recognizing fractions as numbers.', 'Children find it impossible to adapt a circle-based part-whole model to a situation where they must share three circles into four equal parts.', 'fraction', 'part-whole concept', 'continuous vs discrete models', 'student', '', 'ESM_Pitkethly_1996_Review', 'ANNE PITKETHLY AND ROBERT HUNTING', '1996', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '11', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Pitkethly_1996_Review.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39471, 'Instruction overly reliant on continuous quantity approaches promotes limiting stereotypes, such as the belief that a fraction must always be less than a whole.', 'Understanding a fraction merely as a label attached to a visual or manipulatory context, rather than realizing that different entities can be unified within a single numerical construct.', 'fraction', 'fraction concept', 'stereotypes', 'student', '', 'ESM_Pitkethly_1996_Review', 'ANNE PITKETHLY AND ROBERT HUNTING', '1996', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '12, 13', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Pitkethly_1996_Review.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39472, 'Students hold idiosyncratic notions of samples derived from everyday consumer experiences, failing to distinguish between sampling from homogeneous entities and heterogeneous populations, leading to an underestimation of required sample size.', 'Describing a sample as ''an item which is used just for testing purposes'' like trying 1-2 pieces of cheese at a supermarket.', 'statistics', 'samples and sampling', 'sample size and homogeneity', 'student', 'Grade 6', 'ESM_MeletiouMavrotheris_2015_Developing', 'Maria Meletiou-Mavrotheris & Efi Paparistodemou', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '387, 391, 393, 394, 395', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_MeletiouMavrotheris_2015_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39473, 'Students distrust simple random sampling because they have no control over the sample composition, instead preferring stratified or self-selected sampling due to a preoccupation with colloquial notions of fairness.', 'Evaluating self-selected sampling as ''fairer because all children are given the opportunity to participate,'' and preferring stratified selection of an equal number of boys and girls for ''fairness'' rather than representativeness.', 'statistics', 'sampling methods', 'simple random sampling vs stratification', 'student', 'Grade 6', 'ESM_MeletiouMavrotheris_2015_Developing', 'Maria Meletiou-Mavrotheris & Efi Paparistodemou', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '386, 392, 393, 396, 398', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_MeletiouMavrotheris_2015_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39474, 'Students over-rely on sampling variability, concluding that random samples might yield extreme outcomes and provide no useful information because ''anything could happen,'' ignoring the stabilizing effect of large sample sizes.', 'Arguing that tossing a fair coin 100 times could reasonably result in 90 heads, or getting only 1s and 2s on 100 die rolls is ''quite possible'' because ''it''s random.''', 'probability', 'sampling variability', 'law of large numbers', 'student', 'Grade 6', 'ESM_MeletiouMavrotheris_2015_Developing', 'Maria Meletiou-Mavrotheris & Efi Paparistodemou', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '387, 392, 393, 397, 398', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_MeletiouMavrotheris_2015_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39475, 'When presented with multiple surveys, students ignore the quality of the sampling methods used, instead aggregating all results indiscriminately or relying on personal experiences to draw conclusions.', 'Aggregating the results of all five surveys regardless of their quality, or refusing to use the data at all to draw a conclusion.', 'statistics', 'data inference', 'evaluating survey quality', 'student', 'Grade 6', 'ESM_MeletiouMavrotheris_2015_Developing', 'Maria Meletiou-Mavrotheris & Efi Paparistodemou', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '392, 393, 396', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_MeletiouMavrotheris_2015_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39476, 'Students erroneously assume that a linear pattern with a constant additive difference implies a direct multiplicative relationship, concluding that the nth term is simply the common difference multiplied by n.', 'To find the number of matches for a 1000-rung ladder (where the rule is 3n+2), a student multiplies 1000 by the constant difference 3 to get 3000.', 'algebraic', 'linear generalisation', 'constant difference', 'student', 'Years 4, 5, 6, 7, and 8', 'ESM_Stacey_1989_Finding', 'KAYE STACEY', '1989', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '150, 151, 160', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Stacey_1989_Finding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39477, 'Students inappropriately treat an entire spatial or numerical term as a complete proportional unit, calculating a far generalisation by taking a direct multiple of a near generalisation.', 'To find the number of matches for a 20-rung ladder, a student calculates the matches for a 10-rung ladder (32) and multiplies it by 2 to erroneously arrive at 64.', 'algebraic', 'linear generalisation', 'direct proportion', 'student', 'Years 4, 5, 6, 7, and 8', 'ESM_Stacey_1989_Finding', 'KAYE STACEY', '1989', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '150, 151, 153, 160', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Stacey_1989_Finding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39478, 'Students apply conflicting criteria, such as ''part-whole'' inclusion or ''single infinity'', to compare infinite sets, leading to contradictory conclusions about the equivalence of the same sets depending on how they are represented visually or numerically.', 'When presented horizontally, students argue the set of natural numbers is larger than the set of numbers bigger than 2 because it includes two extra elements. Yet, when shown a geometric matching, they conclude the sets are equal.', 'other', 'infinity', 'comparing infinite sets', 'student', 'secondary school students (16 to 18 years old)', 'ESM_Tsamir_2001_When', 'PESSIA TSAMIR', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '290, 298, 299, 300', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tsamir_2001_When.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39479, 'Learners inappropriately apply the discrete structure of natural numbers to rational numbers, failing to understand that there are infinitely many rational numbers between any two given rational numbers.', 'Stating that there is only one number between 3/8 and 5/8, or struggling to find and write a number between 3.49 and 3.50.', 'rational', 'structure of numbers', 'density and discreteness', 'student', '4th to 12th grade', 'ESM_Hoof_2015_Inappropriately', 'Jo Van Hoof & Lieven Verschaffel & Wim Van Dooren', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '40, 44', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hoof_2015_Inappropriately.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39480, 'Learners wrongly assume that if the natural numbers in the symbolic representation of a rational number are larger, the magnitude of the rational number is also larger.', 'Incorrectly assuming that 0.65 is larger than 0.8 because 65 is larger than 8, or that 3/9 is larger than 2/5.', 'rational', 'comparing quantities', 'size and magnitude', 'student', '4th to 12th grade', 'ESM_Hoof_2015_Inappropriately', 'Jo Van Hoof & Lieven Verschaffel & Wim Van Dooren', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '40, 44', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hoof_2015_Inappropriately.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39481, 'Learners wrongly assume that addition and multiplication always result in a larger number, and division and subtraction always result in a smaller number, applying natural number rules to rational numbers.', 'Assuming that 50 x 3/2 is smaller than 50 or that 72 x 0.99 is bigger than 72 without calculating the exact answer.', 'rational', 'arithmetic operations', 'effect of operations', 'student', '4th to 12th grade', 'ESM_Hoof_2015_Inappropriately', 'Jo Van Hoof & Lieven Verschaffel & Wim Van Dooren', '2015', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '40, 41, 44', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Hoof_2015_Inappropriately.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39482, 'Students intuitively believe that a longer line segment contains proportionally more points than a shorter one, a view that contradicts Cantorian cardinal infinity but aligns productively with the concept of infinite measuring numbers.', 'When asked to imagine the number of points on a line segment and on another segment twice as long, an eight-year-old boy estimates ''fifty'' for the first and ''a hundred'' for the second.', 'measurement', 'infinity', 'points on a line', 'student', 'eight years', 'ESM_Tall_1980_Notion', 'DAVID TALL', '1980', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '272, 273, 281, 282', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tall_1980_Notion.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39483, 'Students'' natural experiences dictate that ''the whole is greater than the part'', leading them to reject that an infinite set can have the same cardinal number as its proper subset.', 'Students believe the whole set of natural numbers is greater than the part consisting of even numbers, conflicting with the formal one-to-one correspondence between them.', 'other', 'cardinal numbers', 'infinite sets', 'student', '', 'ESM_Tall_2001_Natural', 'DAVID TALL', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '204', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tall_2001_Natural.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39484, 'Students view limits as a potentially infinite process of getting closer and closer without ever reaching the target, and conceptualize variables tending to zero as infinitesimally small quantities rather than seeing the limit as a fixed value.', 'Students think of the difference between 0.999... and 1 as an extremely small but non-zero quantity, or describe a limit as ''nearly there, but not quite''.', 'calculus', 'limits', 'infinitesimals', 'student', '', 'ESM_Tall_2001_Natural', 'DAVID TALL', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '219, 220, 233, 234', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tall_2001_Natural.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39485, 'Students experience a ''lack of closure'' with limit expressions because they sense them only as incomplete processes rather than manipulable mathematical objects (procepts).', 'Students view an infinite sum or a limit formula solely as an invitation to calculate a potentially infinite process, causing uneasiness because there is no finite algorithm to compute the exact value.', 'calculus', 'limits', 'procepts', 'student', '', 'ESM_Tall_2001_Natural', 'DAVID TALL', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '233', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tall_2001_Natural.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39486, 'When prompted to perform the reverse of a learned mathematical process, a student may incorrectly apply the forward process because they have not yet abstracted the reverse concept.', 'When given a 1/6 unit bar and asked to make 1/3, the student partitions the 1/6 bar in half, creating 1/12 instead of iterating it to create 1/3.', 'fraction', 'recursive partitioning', 'reversibility', 'student', 'grade 4', 'ESM_Simon_2016_Categorizing', 'Martin A. Simon & Melike Kara & Nicora Placa & Hakan Sandir', '2016', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '148, 149', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Simon_2016_Categorizing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39487, 'Students conceptualize the limit of a sequence or infinite decimal dynamically as a process that approaches but never reaches a target, producing an arbitrarily small entity that is not zero.', 'Students consider the infinite decimal 0.999... to be a quantity that is just less than 1, or as close as possible to 1 without equaling it.', 'calculus', 'limits', 'infinitesimals and sequences', 'other', '', 'ESM_Tall_2014_Cognitive', 'David Tall & Mikhail Katz', '2014', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '100, 117', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tall_2014_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39488, 'When the multiplicative relationship between two ratios is not an integer, students may fall back on an additive strategy, erroneously assuming the difference between quantities is preserved rather than their ratio.', 'To find the missing height (h) of a 15 cm wide rectangle similar to a 6x8 cm rectangle, a student calculates 15 - 8 = 7, and then adds 7 to 6 to get h = 13 cm.', 'proportional', 'similar rectangles', 'non-integral relations', 'student', 'grade six', 'ESM_Singh_2000_Understanding', 'PARMJIT SINGH', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '287, 288', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Singh_2000_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39489, 'A student relies strictly on a taught procedural ''unit method'' without conceptual understanding, leading them to use additive iterations when forced to abandon the algorithm or when confronted with non-integer unit rates.', 'When asked to solve how many chairs 25 cans of paint will cover if 15 cans paint 18 chairs (without using the school unit method), the student iteratively adds 18 five times, erroneously arriving at 810 chairs.', 'proportional', 'missing value problems', 'unit method', 'student', 'grade six', 'ESM_Singh_2000_Understanding', 'PARMJIT SINGH', '2000', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '285, 286, 288', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Singh_2000_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39490, 'Students conceptualize an infinite series as an infinite unending process of addition, leading them to erroneously apply properties of finite sums, such as the associative property, to infinite series.', 'Accepting an argument that groups terms of an infinite series differently to produce an alternate sum of 1, ignoring the formal sequence of partial sums.', 'calculus', 'infinite series', 'sequence of partial sums', 'student', 'graduate', 'ESM_MartinezPlanell_2012_Students''', 'Rafael Martínez-Planell & Ana Carmen Gonzalez & Gladys DiCristina & Vanessa Acevedo', '2012', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '235, 238, 239, 241, 242', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_MartinezPlanell_2012_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39491, 'Students view the sum of a series and the limit of its sequence of partial sums as unrelated or distinct concepts, retrieving the formal definition merely as a memorized fact that is easily discarded.', 'Victor stated that finding the limit of partial sums to infinity ''should not be the same thing as when I find the series of the sequence'' when explaining his confusion.', 'calculus', 'infinite series', 'limit of partial sums', 'student', 'graduate', 'ESM_MartinezPlanell_2012_Students''', 'Rafael Martínez-Planell & Ana Carmen Gonzalez & Gladys DiCristina & Vanessa Acevedo', '2012', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '242', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_MartinezPlanell_2012_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39492, 'Multiplication conceptualized as repeated addition implies multiplier must be integer and product larger than multiplicand, preventing commutativity with decimal multipliers.', 'Fewer difficulties with 0.6F for 38 pencils (0.6 x 38) than 43F for 0.7 kg (43 x 0.7).', 'decimal', 'multiplication word problems', 'commutativity and repeated addition', 'student', 'primary cycle', 'ESM_Levain_1992_La', 'JEAN-PIERRE LEVAIN', '1992', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '142, 157', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Levain_1992_La.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39493, 'Implicit division models imposing divisor as integer and dividend larger than divisor.', 'Partition division: divisor represents number of objects, implying integer.', 'decimal', 'division word problems', 'primitive models of division', 'student', 'primary cycle', 'ESM_Levain_1992_La', 'JEAN-PIERRE LEVAIN', '1992', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '142', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Levain_1992_La.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39494, 'Students generate systematic procedural errors or bugs when executing arithmetic cognitive processes such as place value subtraction.', '', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'place value', 'other', 'elementary', 'ESM_Levin_1981_Estimation', 'JAMES A. LEVIN', '1981', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'passing', '432', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Levin_1981_Estimation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39495, 'Children interpret the digits in a multi-digit numeral strictly by their individual face values, ignoring positional value and sometimes treating the numeral as an additive assembly of those face values.', 'To determine whether 298 or 511 is larger, a child chooses 298, reasoning ''there''s eight and nine and those are more than all the others.''', 'whole_number', 'multi-digit numerals', 'place value', 'student', '6 year-olds', 'ESM_Sinclair_1993_Understanding', 'ANNE SINCLAIR AND NORA SCHEUER', '1993', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'mixed', 'primary', '209, 214, 215', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sinclair_1993_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39496, 'Children believe that a written numeral represents a quantity through a written counting sequence, writing out all numbers up to the total cardinality instead of using a single multi-digit numeral.', 'When asked to write down how many chips were in a heap of 17, a child wrote down the whole series ''123...1617'', insisting that every number in the sequence must correspond to a chip.', 'whole_number', 'numeration', 'cardinality representation', 'student', '6 year-olds', 'ESM_Sinclair_1993_Understanding', 'ANNE SINCLAIR AND NORA SCHEUER', '1993', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '204, 213', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sinclair_1993_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39497, 'Children assign a global meaning to a bi-digit numeral but believe its individual digits are indivisible and cannot be meaningfully mapped to subsets of the represented quantity.', 'A child refused the suggestion that the ''1'' in the units place of 11 might represent one chip and the ''1'' in the tens place might represent ten chips, stating ''both ones are for all.''', 'whole_number', 'place value', 'part-whole relationships', 'student', '6 year-olds', 'ESM_Sinclair_1993_Understanding', 'ANNE SINCLAIR AND NORA SCHEUER', '1993', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '213, 214, 215', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sinclair_1993_Understanding.pdf')).
