research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39504, 'Prospective teachers believe that the set of rational numbers is finite while the set of irrational numbers is infinite.', 'Probability of picking a rational is 0 because we have an infinite number of irrationals between 0 and 1, but we only have a finite number of rationals.', 'rational', 'cardinality and density', 'infinite sets', 'PST', 'secondary', 'ESM_Sirotic_2007_Irrational', 'NATASA SIROTIC AND RINA ZAZKIS', '2007', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '58, 65, 66, 67', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sirotic_2007_Irrational.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39505, 'Prospective teachers believe that because both the rational and irrational sets are infinite, they must have equal cardinality or be equally rich.', 'You can''t have one infinity greater than other infinity. So both sets are equally rich!', 'rational', 'cardinality', 'infinite sets', 'PST', 'secondary', 'ESM_Sirotic_2007_Irrational', 'NATASA SIROTIC AND RINA ZAZKIS', '2007', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '60, 69', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sirotic_2007_Irrational.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39506, 'Prospective teachers believe that because irrational numbers are highly dense and outnumber rationals, there exist closest, consecutive irrational numbers with no rational number between them.', 'In one case there will be an irrational number that''s right beside another one... So there can''t be anything between those two.', 'rational', 'density', 'real number line', 'PST', 'secondary', 'ESM_Sirotic_2007_Irrational', 'NATASA SIROTIC AND RINA ZAZKIS', '2007', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '62, 64, 68, 69', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sirotic_2007_Irrational.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39507, 'Prospective teachers assume the set of irrational numbers is closed under addition and multiplication, relying heavily on non-terminating decimal representations that cast irrationals as unending processes rather than encapsulated objects.', 'Because the decimal representations of irrational numbers cannot be terminated, the sum of such numbers will be a decimal that cannot be terminated.', 'other', 'irrational numbers', 'operations', 'PST', 'secondary', 'ESM_Sirotic_2007_Irrational', 'NATASA SIROTIC AND RINA ZAZKIS', '2007', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '70, 71, 72, 73', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Sirotic_2007_Irrational.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39508, 'Prospective teachers erroneously believe that if all the sides of a given polygon (such as a hexagon) are equal, then all its angles must also be equal, applying the intuitive rule ''same A-same B''.', 'When asked to validate the statement ''In ANY hexagon if all sides are equal, then its angles are also equal'', a prospective teacher confidently draws an incorrect geometric proof concluding the angles must be 120 degrees.', 'geometric', 'polygons', 'sides and angles', 'PST', 'secondary', 'ESM_Tsamir_2007_When', 'PESSIA TSAMIR', '2007', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '261, 262, 263, 264, 266, 267, 268', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tsamir_2007_When.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39509, 'Students process algebraic expressions sequentially from left to right as they do in natural language, violating algebraic precedence rules or inappropriately combining unlike terms.', 'A child may read the expression 2 + 3a from left to right as 2 + 3 giving 5, and consider the full expression to be the same as 5a, or read ab as a + b.', 'algebraic', 'algebraic expressions', 'parsing and precedence', 'student', '11/12 and 12/13 year olds', 'ESM_Tall_1991_Encouraging', 'DAVID TALL AND MICHAEL THOMAS', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '125, 126, 135, 143, 144', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tall_1991_Encouraging.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39510, 'Students hold an unfulfilled and erroneous expectation that algebraic expressions should yield a single numerical answer, causing discomfort when they cannot carry out the process to close the expression.', 'Interpreting 3 + 2a as an invitation to calculate an answer, similar to 3 + 2, and experiencing discomfort when unable to perform the calculation because ''a'' is unknown.', 'algebraic', 'algebraic expressions', 'unclosed expressions', 'student', '11/12 and 12/13 year olds', 'ESM_Tall_1991_Encouraging', 'DAVID TALL AND MICHAEL THOMAS', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '126, 135, 145', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tall_1991_Encouraging.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39511, 'Students view algebraic expressions solely as a process to be carried out rather than an encapsulated product or object, leading them to see equivalent expressions with different computational steps as completely different entities.', 'To a child who thinks only in terms of process, the symbols 3(a + b) and 3a + 3b are quite different because the first requires addition before multiplication, while the second requires multiplication before addition.', 'algebraic', 'algebraic expressions', 'equivalence', 'student', '11/12 and 12/13 year olds', 'ESM_Tall_1991_Encouraging', 'DAVID TALL AND MICHAEL THOMAS', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '126, 135, 144, 145', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tall_1991_Encouraging.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39512, 'Students fail to conserve the structure of an equation under a change of variable, believing that changing the letter requires resolving the equation from scratch or that different letters imply different answers.', 'After solving 2p - 1 = 5 to find p = 3, students are asked to solve 2s - 1 = 5 and begin recalculating rather than recognizing it is the exact same equation structure.', 'algebraic', 'equations', 'variables', 'student', '11/12 and 12/13 year olds', 'ESM_Tall_1991_Encouraging', 'DAVID TALL AND MICHAEL THOMAS', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '140, 141, 144', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tall_1991_Encouraging.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39513, 'Students erroneously attribute properties of finite sets to infinite ones, applying multiple conflicting methods like ''inclusion'' or ''intervals'' to compare infinite sets, or globally asserting that all infinite sets are equal.', 'When asked to compare the set of natural numbers and the set of positive even numbers, students might use ''inclusion'' to incorrectly conclude they have an unequal number of elements, or state that because both are infinite, they are inherently equal.', 'other', 'infinity', 'comparing infinite sets', 'PST', 'prospective secondary school mathematics teachers', 'ESM_Tsamir_1999_Transition', 'PESSIA TSAMIR', '1999', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '209, 210, 211, 226, 228, 229, 230', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tsamir_1999_Transition.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39514, 'Prospective teachers struggle with the concept of density, believing that between two rational numbers there are only a finite number of other numbers, often restricted to equivalent fractions of a single generated midpoint.', 'When asked to find numbers between 3/5 and 4/5, a student identifies a midpoint of 3.5/5 (or 7/10) and then erroneously claims that the only other numbers between them are fractions equivalent to 7/10 (like 14/20 or 21/30).', 'rational', 'rational numbers', 'density', 'PST', 'first year university', 'ESM_Zachariades_2013_Reflective', 'Theodossios Zachariades & Constantinos Christou & Demetra Pitta-Pantazi', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '17', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Zachariades_2013_Reflective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39515, 'Prospective teachers base their conceptions of real numbers almost entirely on their experiences with natural numbers, incorrectly concluding that real numbers must be integers or rejecting infinite decimals as valid rational numbers.', 'Stating that 0.7777... is not a real number ''because real numbers should be integer numbers,'' or believing an infinite repeating decimal cannot be a fraction because ''we cannot be sure where it ends.''', 'other', 'real numbers', 'definitions and properties', 'PST', 'first year university', 'ESM_Zachariades_2013_Reflective', 'Theodossios Zachariades & Constantinos Christou & Demetra Pitta-Pantazi', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '17, 18', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Zachariades_2013_Reflective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39516, 'When determining if two geometric figures are similar (of the same form), students erroneously use additive comparisons instead of multiplicative ratios, such as assuming that adding a constant length to all sides preserves the form.', 'Asserting that a 3cm x 5cm rectangle and a 5cm x 7cm rectangle are of the ''same form'' because the larger is obtained by adding 2cm to both sides, or because the difference between their length and width is the same (5-3 = 7-5).', 'geometric', 'similarity', 'proportional reasoning', 'other', '', 'ESM_Winslw_2013_Study', 'Carl Winsløw & Yves Matheron & Alain Mercier', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '279, 280', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Winslw_2013_Study.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39517, 'When generalizing operations to negative numbers, students incorrectly conclude that the sum and difference of two negative numbers can yield the same result, failing to correctly execute subtraction with negative integers.', 'Finding that both −7 + (−2) and −7 − (−2) yield −9, leading to cognitive conflict because the sum and difference of two numbers should not logically be the same.', 'integer', 'integers', 'addition and subtraction of negative numbers', 'other', '', 'ESM_Winslw_2013_Study', 'Carl Winsløw & Yves Matheron & Alain Mercier', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'uncoded', 'passing', '278', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Winslw_2013_Study.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39518, 'Students use superficial solution strategies that focus solely on the numbers given in the task and mindless calculations, failing to analyze the situation or evaluate the answer''s appropriateness in reality.', 'Answering that 7.5 buses are needed for a school trip and stating in the interview, ''I did not think about buses, it was just 7.5.''', 'other', 'word problem solving', 'realistic considerations', 'student', '5th grade', 'ESM_Palm_2008_Impact', 'Torulf Palm', '2008', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '50, 51', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Palm_2008_Impact.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39519, 'Students believe that mathematical school tasks always have a single numerical solution attainable purely from the given data, leading them to intentionally ignore real-world constraints like fatigue.', 'A student calculated a 10,000m running time by multiplying a runner''s 100m pace by 100, acknowledging the runner would get tired but stating ''they don''t want to know how long time it will take, they want to know if I can calculate 10x100.''', 'other', 'word problem solving', 'beliefs about school mathematics', 'student', '5th grade', 'ESM_Palm_2008_Impact', 'Torulf Palm', '2008', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '51, 52, 54', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Palm_2008_Impact.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39520, 'Students believe that pictures in word problems are merely decorative or subordinated to the text, causing them to ignore critical visual information that indicates a non-linear relationship.', 'Students failed to notice that the depth of water in a bowl would not rise at a constant speed because they thought the text provided sufficient information and the picture of the non-cylindrical bowl was just for decoration.', 'proportional', 'rate of change', 'interpreting visual information', 'student', '5th grade', 'ESM_Palm_2008_Impact', 'Torulf Palm', '2008', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '53, 54', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Palm_2008_Impact.pdf')).
