research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39523, 'Students judge the relative size of decimal numbers by interpreting the fractional part as a whole number, leading to incorrect comparisons based on the magnitude of the string of digits.', 'A student judges 0.45 to be greater than 0.6 because 45 is larger than 6, or judges 0.4502 as smaller than 0.45 for similar whole-number pattern reasons.', 'decimal', 'decimal numeration', 'comparing decimals', 'student', 'Year 5 and Year 7', 'ESM_Stacey_2001_Effect_a', 'KAYE STACEY, SUE HELME, SHONA ARCHER and CAROLINE CONDON', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '212, 213, 214', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Stacey_2001_Effect_a.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39524, 'Students lack a robust grasp of volume and cannot visualize or enumerate the hidden interior units of a 3D block, instead merely counting the visible exterior faces or squares.', 'When asked to state the number of minis in a large block, a student counted the 6 faces and suggested there were 600 minis, or simply counted the visible squares of a 2x2x2 cube.', 'measurement', 'volume', 'rectangular arrays', 'student', 'Year 5 and Year 7', 'ESM_Stacey_2001_Effect_a', 'KAYE STACEY, SUE HELME, SHONA ARCHER and CAROLINE CONDON', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '208', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Stacey_2001_Effect_a.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39525, 'Students and teachers perceive the various components of base-ten blocks (minis, longs, flats, blocks) as having different dimensions (0, 1, 2, and 3-D) rather than a unified volume relationship, making it difficult to generalize beyond the model.', 'A student teacher describes the large cube and mini as 3-D, but the flat and long as 2-D, showing a shift in attention from length to area to volume depending on the block''s shape.', 'decimal', 'decimal models', 'multibase blocks', 'student', 'Year 5 and Year 7', 'ESM_Stacey_2001_Effect_a', 'KAYE STACEY, SUE HELME, SHONA ARCHER and CAROLINE CONDON', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'secondary', '208, 209, 215', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Stacey_2001_Effect_a.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39526, 'Students view decimals strictly as discrete quantities and fail to grasp the continuous nature of number density, struggling to understand that another decimal can always be found between any two given decimals.', 'Students struggle to choose a number between 0.3 and 0.4 when using MAB, as the volume model does not naturally map to the continuous number line.', 'decimal', 'number density', 'continuous quantities', 'student', 'Year 5 and Year 7', 'ESM_Stacey_2001_Effect_a', 'KAYE STACEY, SUE HELME, SHONA ARCHER and CAROLINE CONDON', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '206, 216', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Stacey_2001_Effect_a.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39527, 'Students conceive angle measures as intrinsic properties of familiar geometric objects or purely calculational formulas, struggling to attribute quantitative meaning to measures outside these familiar contexts.', 'Judy claimed she didn''t know what an angle was outside of formulas, relying on the rule that it is supplementary to 179 degrees when asked to describe an angle measure of 1 degree.', 'measurement', 'angle measure', 'degrees', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'ESM_Moore_2013_Making', 'Kevin C. Moore', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '230, 231, 232, 233, 240', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Moore_2013_Making.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39528, 'Students adopt a calculational orientation when dealing with ratios in angle problems, viewing them merely as numbers or a series of operations to find a missing variable rather than values representing a multiplicative relationship between quantities.', 'Zac set up the proportion 90/80.1 = x/32 simply because ''it gives you three of the four you need,'' but could not provide a quantitative meaning for the ratio 90/80.1.', 'proportional', 'ratio and proportion', 'angle measure calculations', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'ESM_Moore_2013_Making', 'Kevin C. Moore', '2013', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '231, 234', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Moore_2013_Making.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39529, 'Students intuitively focus on the potential infinity of a limiting process rather than its finite limit, erroneously believing that an infinite additive geometric process will never end and that its sum must therefore be infinity.', 'When asked the sum of continuously added halving segments (1m + 1/2m + 1/4m + ...), 51% of surveyed 8th and 9th graders thought the sum would be infinity rather than 2.', 'other', 'limits', 'infinite series', 'other', '', 'ESM_Tall_2001_Infinity', 'David Tall and Dina Tirosh', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '68', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tall_2001_Infinity.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39530, 'When comparing infinite quantities, students apply conflicting intuitive schemes derived from finite experiences, resulting in contradictory ''Finite'' answers (e.g., applying the part-whole principle to claim a proper subset is smaller) or ''Infinite'' answers (claiming all infinite sets are equivalent).', 'Asserting that a proper subset of a given set is not equivalent to the set because ''the whole must be greater than its parts'', or conversely stating that all infinite sets have the same number of elements because they are all ''pure potentiality''.', 'other', 'infinity', 'cardinality of infinite sets', 'other', '', 'ESM_Tall_2001_Infinity', 'David Tall and Dina Tirosh', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Tall_2001_Infinity.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39531, 'Students trained exclusively in standard algorithms lack number sense and estimation skills (innumeracy), accepting absurd numerical statements or generating unreasonable estimates because they treat numbers as abstract cyphers rather than meaningful quantities.', 'When told a 4-story building is 800 meters tall, or asked to evaluate a newspaper clipping stating the US is 1000 times larger than the Netherlands, students accept the figures without noticing the lack of realism.', 'whole_number', 'estimation', 'number sense', 'mixed', 'primary school (3rd to 6th grades) and first year''s students of teacher training colleges', 'ESM_Treffers_1991_Meeting', 'ADRIAN TREFFERS', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'deficit', 'primary', '333, 335, 337, 343, 347', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Treffers_1991_Meeting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39532, 'Children''s natural subtraction strategies involve working from left to right (larger to smaller units) and comfortably dealing with partial negative differences (shortages), conflicting with the standard right-to-left ''borrowing'' algorithm.', 'To calculate 75 - 48, a student subtracts the tens first (70 - 40 = 30) and then, facing 5 - 8, recognizes a shortage of 3, subtracting 3 from 30 to get 27.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'informal strategies', 'mixed', 'primary school (3rd to 6th grades) and first year''s students of teacher training colleges', 'ESM_Treffers_1991_Meeting', 'ADRIAN TREFFERS', '1991', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'secondary', '341, 342, 346', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Treffers_1991_Meeting.pdf')).
