research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39863, 'Students inappropriately assume that continuous quantities or dimensions must be divided into a whole number of squares to be measured or rearranged.', 'Trying to divide a 5m by 1m board into five squares to rearrange them into a larger square, then recursively dividing them into smaller squares, assuming they must fit into whole units.', 'measurement', 'Length and area', 'Continuous measurement', 'PST', 'second or third year of college mathematics, taking courses towards a mathematics teacher certification', 'IJMEST_Peled_1999_Difficulties', 'Irit Peled & Sara Hershkovitz', '1999', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Peled_1999_Difficulties.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39864, 'Students incorrectly assess a fraction''s proximity to 1 by calculating the absolute difference between the denominator and numerator, assuming equal differences imply equal proximity.', 'Subtracting 15 from 16 and 8 from 9 to get 1, and concluding that 15/16 and 8/9 are equally close to 1.', 'fraction', 'Fraction comparison', 'Proximity to 1', 'student', 'seventh grade', 'IJMEST_Butuner_2018_Comparing', 'Suphi Onder Butuner', '2018', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Butuner_2018_Comparing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39865, 'Students mistakenly assume that multiplication always yields a product larger than the initial factor and division always yields a quotient smaller than the dividend, failing to consider operands less than 1.', 'Believing that multiplying a number by a decimal less than 1 must result in a greater number, or that dividing a number by a proper fraction must result in a smaller number.', 'rational', 'Operations with rational numbers', 'Relative effect of operations', 'student', 'seventh grade', 'IJMEST_Butuner_2018_Comparing', 'Suphi Onder Butuner', '2018', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Butuner_2018_Comparing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39866, 'Students erroneously combine two weighted means as if they were simple arithmetic means or improperly compute a missing score to match a given population mean.', 'Calculating the average weight of 4 women (120 lbs) and 6 men (180 lbs) by simply using (120+180)/2 = 150.', 'statistics', 'Mean', 'Weighted mean and expected value', 'mixed', '', 'IJMEST_Batanero_1994_Errors', 'C. Batanero, J. D. Godino, A. Vallecillos, D. R. Green & P. Holmes', '1994', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '531', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Batanero_1994_Errors.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39867, 'Students mistakenly assume that a data set together with the arithmetic mean or variance constitutes a mathematical group, incorrectly applying properties like associativity or identity elements.', 'Believing that adding a new value of zero to a distribution does not alter the value of the mean.', 'statistics', 'Mean and variance', 'Mathematical properties', 'mixed', '', 'IJMEST_Batanero_1994_Errors', 'C. Batanero, J. D. Godino, A. Vallecillos, D. R. Green & P. Holmes', '1994', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '532, 534', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Batanero_1994_Errors.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39868, 'Students incorrectly believe that z-scores always range strictly from -3.0 to +3.0, or conversely, that there is no restriction on maximum values in a finite distribution because the normal curve is asymptotic.', 'Assuming a z-score cannot exceed 3.0, or not recognizing that a finite binomial distribution (e.g., n=10, p=0.5) has a finite maximum z-score (3.16).', 'statistics', 'Standard deviation', 'z-scores', 'mixed', '', 'IJMEST_Batanero_1994_Errors', 'C. Batanero, J. D. Godino, A. Vallecillos, D. R. Green & P. Holmes', '1994', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '534', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Batanero_1994_Errors.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39869, 'When evaluating contingency tables, students inappropriately judge statistical association by only looking at the favorable positive cases (one cell) or by comparing only two cells, ignoring the other marginal frequencies.', 'Concluding a positive association exists between taking a drug and having digestive troubles simply because the cell representing people who took the drug and had troubles contains the highest frequency.', 'statistics', 'Association', 'Contingency tables', 'mixed', '', 'IJMEST_Batanero_1994_Errors', 'C. Batanero, J. D. Godino, A. Vallecillos, D. R. Green & P. Holmes', '1994', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '537', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Batanero_1994_Errors.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39870, 'Applying the representativeness heuristic, students ignore sample size and assume small samples are just as likely as large samples to exhibit extreme deviations from the population proportion.', 'Believing that a small hospital and a large hospital have ''about the same'' chance of having days where over 60% of babies born are boys.', 'probability', 'Sampling', 'Sample size and variability', 'mixed', '', 'IJMEST_Batanero_1994_Errors', 'C. Batanero, J. D. Godino, A. Vallecillos, D. R. Green & P. Holmes', '1994', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '540', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Batanero_1994_Errors.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39871, 'Students misinterpret the level of significance (Type I error probability) by interchanging the condition and conditional event, treating it as the probability that the null hypothesis is true given that it was rejected.', 'Interpreting a 5% significance level to mean ''on average, 5 times out of every 100 times we reject the null hypothesis, we will be wrong.''', 'statistics', 'Hypothesis testing', 'Level of significance', 'mixed', '', 'IJMEST_Batanero_1994_Errors', 'C. Batanero, J. D. Godino, A. Vallecillos, D. R. Green & P. Holmes', '1994', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '542', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Batanero_1994_Errors.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39872, 'Students confuse statistical significance with practical significance, or incorrectly assume that a statistically significant result corroborates the null hypothesis instead of rejecting it.', 'Believing that finding a statistically significant result implies a relationship has important practical significance, regardless of context.', 'statistics', 'Hypothesis testing', 'Interpretation of results', 'mixed', '', 'IJMEST_Batanero_1994_Errors', 'C. Batanero, J. D. Godino, A. Vallecillos, D. R. Green & P. Holmes', '1994', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '543', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Batanero_1994_Errors.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39873, 'Students separate geometric images from conceptual constraints, relying on visual prototypes to mistakenly assume triangles must always be equilateral or have a horizontal base.', 'Describing a triangle by stating ''It is a geometric figure with three equal sides'' or ''Draw a horizontal segment and then like two diagonals.''', 'geometric', 'Triangles', 'Figural concepts', 'student', 'sixth grade', 'IJMEST_Prieto_2014_Designing', 'Nuria Joglar Prieto, Jose Maria Sordo Juanena & Jon R. Star', '2014', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '404, 409', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Prieto_2014_Designing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39874, 'Students over-generalize or omit necessary conditions when defining squares, often assuming orientation properties like horizontal or vertical lines while forgetting to specify equal side lengths.', 'Describing a square as ''It is a figure formed with two vertical and two horizontal lines.''', 'geometric', 'Quadrilaterals', 'Squares', 'student', 'sixth grade', 'IJMEST_Prieto_2014_Designing', 'Nuria Joglar Prieto, Jose Maria Sordo Juanena & Jon R. Star', '2014', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '404, 409', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Prieto_2014_Designing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39875, 'Students assume every quadrilateral is a parallelogram or erroneously believe the rhombus is the only parallelogram without right angles.', 'Drawing a parallelogram when asked to draw a general quadrilateral, or asking ''Isn''t every quadrilateral a parallelogram?''', 'geometric', 'Quadrilaterals', 'Parallelograms', 'student', 'sixth grade', 'IJMEST_Prieto_2014_Designing', 'Nuria Joglar Prieto, Jose Maria Sordo Juanena & Jon R. Star', '2014', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '404, 408, 410', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Prieto_2014_Designing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39876, 'Students use a ''face value strategy'' where they believe the numerical attribute (face value) of an object affects its probability of selection, often substituting this value into the numerator or denominator.', 'When asked for the probability of selecting a card numbered 9 from cards 1, 4, 6, and 9, offering an answer like 9/20, 9/4, or 4/9.', 'probability', 'probability of a single event', 'theoretical probability', 'student', 'age sixteen plus', 'IJMEST_Nissen_1989_Probably', 'Phillip Nissen', '1989', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '510, 511, 513, 516', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Nissen_1989_Probably.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39877, 'Students provide probabilities greater than one, indicating a lack of understanding that probability is bounded between 0 and 1.', 'Giving an answer of 9/4 for the probability of selecting a specific card.', 'probability', 'properties of probability', 'probability bounds', 'student', 'age sixteen plus', 'IJMEST_Nissen_1989_Probably', 'Phillip Nissen', '1989', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '511, 516', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Nissen_1989_Probably.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39878, 'In multiple event probability questions, students persist in using the number of initial objects as the denominator, failing to recognize that the total number of combined possible outcomes must be calculated.', 'Providing answers like 1/4, 2/4, 3/4, or 4/4 when asked for the probability that the sum of two chosen cards is more than 9.', 'probability', 'multiple events', 'sample space', 'student', 'age sixteen plus', 'IJMEST_Nissen_1989_Probably', 'Phillip Nissen', '1989', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '515, 516', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Nissen_1989_Probably.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39879, 'Students conceptualize the arithmetic mean purely as a computational algorithm (''add-them-all up and divide'') rather than as a statistical property, rendering them unable to infer the mean directly from a known fixed total.', 'In a problem asking for the mean area of 4 sectors of a circle, a student calculates the decimal area of each sector, adds them up (getting 3.15 due to rounding), and divides by 4, failing to recognize the fixed total area is pi.', 'statistics', 'mean', 'inference from a fixed total', 'student', 'high school', 'IJMEST_Zazkis_2013_Students''', 'Dov Zazkis', '2013', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '208, 209, 210, 211', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Zazkis_2013_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39880, 'Subjects fail to independently assess variance from the mean, resulting in variance estimates that are incorrectly correlated with their mean estimates.', '', 'statistics', 'variance', 'intuitive estimation', 'student', 'first-year undergraduate', 'IJMEST_Lovie_1976_Teaching', 'Patricia Lovie and A. D. Lovie', '1976', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '30, 37', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Lovie_1976_Teaching.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39881, 'Subjects underestimate the true variance of a dataset because they do not give sufficient weight to extreme or outlying numerical values.', '', 'statistics', 'variance', 'intuitive estimation', 'student', 'first-year undergraduate', 'IJMEST_Lovie_1976_Teaching', 'Patricia Lovie and A. D. Lovie', '1976', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '30, 31', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Lovie_1976_Teaching.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39882, 'Students use an additive comparison rather than a multiplicative one when comparing ratios, often deploying it as a fallback repair when they reach a cognitive impasse.', 'John uses 4 spoonfuls of concentrate and 10 of water; Mary uses 6 and 15. John uses 6 more spoonfuls of water than of concentrate, and Mary uses 9 more; nine is greater than 6, therefore John''s squash has the stronger taste.', 'proportional', 'ratio and proportion', 'comparing mixtures', 'student', '17-18 year old students', 'IJMEST_Mellar_1991_Modelling', 'Harvey G. Mellar', '1991', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'productive', 'primary', '112, 113, 115, 118', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Mellar_1991_Modelling.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39883, 'Students use remainders from division to compare ratios, which can lead to incorrect conclusions based on comparing the absolute size of the remainders.', 'For John, 10/4 is 2 remainder 2. For Mary, 15/6 is 2 remainder 3; 2 remainder 3 is greater than 2 remainder 2, therefore John''s squash has the stronger taste.', 'proportional', 'ratio and proportion', 'division with remainders', 'student', '17-18 year old students', 'IJMEST_Mellar_1991_Modelling', 'Harvey G. Mellar', '1991', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'mixed', 'primary', '112, 113, 118', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Mellar_1991_Modelling.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39884, 'Students form incorrect logical deductions from relational inequalities, such as assuming a smaller dilution ratio implies a stronger mixture when the opposite is true.', 'Mary''s dilution < John''s dilution therefore ''John''s mixture is stronger''.', 'proportional', 'ratio and proportion', 'interpreting dilutions', 'student', '17-18 year old students', 'IJMEST_Mellar_1991_Modelling', 'Harvey G. Mellar', '1991', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '116', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Mellar_1991_Modelling.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39885, 'Students inappropriately over-generalize and apply whole number properties to fractions, interfering with their understanding of fractions as discrete entities and causing errors in comparison and arithmetic.', 'Thinking 3/8 is bigger than 3/5 because 8 is bigger than 5, or adding numerators and denominators directly, such as 1/10 + 3/5 = 4/15.', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison and addition', 'whole number interference', 'other', '', 'IJMEST_Tsai_2017_Towards', 'Tsung-Lung Tsai & Hui-Chuan Li', '2017', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Tsai_2017_Towards.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39886, 'Students struggle with the quotient construct of fractions, preventing them from interpreting a fraction as a division operation, which leads to superficial errors when converting to decimals.', 'Asserting that as a decimal, 1/8 would be 0.8.', 'fraction', 'fraction constructs', 'quotient construct', 'other', '', 'IJMEST_Tsai_2017_Towards', 'Tsung-Lung Tsai & Hui-Chuan Li', '2017', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Tsai_2017_Towards.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39887, 'Students perceive the concept of fraction as limited, describing area models that are not divided into equal parts as valid fractions.', 'S4 describes an area model (a triangle) that is not divided into equal parts as a fraction 1/3.', 'fraction', 'fraction representation', 'area models and equal parts', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'IJMEST_Yilmaz_2018_Using', 'Fatma Gizem Karaoglan Yilmaz, Burçin Gökkurt Özdemir & Zehra Yasar', '2018', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Yilmaz_2018_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39888, 'Students misinterpret the visual representation of mixed fractions in area models, perceiving fully shaded integer models as part of a compound fraction rather than a whole.', 'A student accepted an area model that represents ''1 whole'' divided into four equal parts as 6/4 rather than identifying the separate 1 whole of the mixed fraction 1 2/4.', 'fraction', 'fraction representation', 'mixed and compound fractions', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'IJMEST_Yilmaz_2018_Using', 'Fatma Gizem Karaoglan Yilmaz, Burçin Gökkurt Özdemir & Zehra Yasar', '2018', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Yilmaz_2018_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39889, 'Students make errors when representing fractions on a number line, such as using an area model format on the line or treating the numerator and denominator as separate whole numbers.', 'Students drew area-like shaded rectangles directly onto the number line, or failed to partition the line into equal, congruent segments.', 'fraction', 'fraction representation', 'number line model', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'IJMEST_Yilmaz_2018_Using', 'Fatma Gizem Karaoglan Yilmaz, Burçin Gökkurt Özdemir & Zehra Yasar', '2018', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Yilmaz_2018_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39890, 'Students are confused about the two different ways of reading and pronouncing fractions, making structural placement mistakes when translating from spoken words to written fractions.', 'A student correctly wrote fractions read from top to bottom but made a mistake writing fractions given from bottom to top, writing ''5/2'' for ''two fifths'' (beşte iki).', 'fraction', 'fraction notation', 'reading and writing', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'IJMEST_Yilmaz_2018_Using', 'Fatma Gizem Karaoglan Yilmaz, Burçin Gökkurt Özdemir & Zehra Yasar', '2018', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Yilmaz_2018_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39891, 'Students incorrectly compare and sort fractions by either dividing the denominator by the numerator and picking the smallest result, or by relying on the smallest difference between the two numbers.', 'Instead of dividing the numerator by the denominator, S5 divides the denominator to the numerator and marks the fraction with the smallest result as the smallest fraction. S23 selected 5/6 because the difference (6-5=1) is the smallest.', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'sorting', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'IJMEST_Yilmaz_2018_Using', 'Fatma Gizem Karaoglan Yilmaz, Burçin Gökkurt Özdemir & Zehra Yasar', '2018', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Yilmaz_2018_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39892, 'Students inappropriately over-generalize operations with natural numbers to fractions, incorrectly adding or subtracting the numerators and denominators together separately.', 'A student added all the numbers in the numerator and denominator, writing 3/6 + 1/6 = 4/12 or 8/8 + 5/8 = 13/16.', 'fraction', 'addition and subtraction', 'equal denominators', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'IJMEST_Yilmaz_2018_Using', 'Fatma Gizem Karaoglan Yilmaz, Burçin Gökkurt Özdemir & Zehra Yasar', '2018', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Yilmaz_2018_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39893, 'Students struggle to determine a fractional part of a given quantity, often dividing the problem''s numbers randomly or irrelevantly multiplying the numerator and denominator.', 'When asked to find how much money remains if Sude spends 3/5 of 45 liras, students executed random arithmetic combinations, like adding 45 and 45 or incorrectly dividing 45 by 5 and subtracting random figures.', 'fraction', 'fraction of a quantity', 'word problems', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'IJMEST_Yilmaz_2018_Using', 'Fatma Gizem Karaoglan Yilmaz, Burçin Gökkurt Özdemir & Zehra Yasar', '2018', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Yilmaz_2018_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39894, 'Prospective teachers possess a distorted notion of mathematical proof, often accepting inductive, empirical arguments as absolute proofs and evaluating deductive proofs based on their ritualistic surface features rather than causality or logic.', 'Accepting inductive arguments as proof of mathematical statements, or failing to reject a false-proof verification because they are influenced by the appearance of the argument rather than its correctness.', 'other', 'mathematical proof', 'inductive and deductive arguments', 'mixed', 'pre-service secondary, pre-service elementary, and 4-6 grade teachers', 'IJMEST_Harel_1994_Teacher', 'Guershon Harel', '1994', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '116, 117', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Harel_1994_Teacher.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39895, 'Pre-service and in-service elementary teachers hold beliefs that are incongruent with the multiplicative operation of rational numbers, matching the misconceptions typically identified in children.', 'Believing the implicit rules that ''multiplication makes bigger'' and ''division makes smaller''.', 'rational', 'multiplication and division', 'properties of operations', 'mixed', 'pre-service secondary, pre-service elementary, and 4-6 grade teachers', 'IJMEST_Harel_1994_Teacher', 'Guershon Harel', '1994', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '116', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Harel_1994_Teacher.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39896, 'Students make basic fact errors, identity errors, inappropriate inversions, or zero errors during calculations, reflecting a lapse in basic number facts or conceptual understanding of operations with zero.', 'Calculating 3)86.40 as 28.80, and then incorrectly calculating 28.80 x 2 = 57.62 (Zero error).', 'decimal', 'basic operations', 'multiplication and division', 'PST', 'elementary / primary', 'IJMEST_Hourigan_2013_Challenges', 'Mairéad Hourigan & John O''Donoghue', '2013', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '48, 49', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Hourigan_2013_Challenges.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39897, 'Students demonstrate a lack of attention to the positional nature of the number system, failing to correctly place the decimal point.', 'Dividing 13.2678 by 2.34 without removing the decimal place from the divisor, or evaluating 125 ÷ 1/5 as 6.25, 62.5, or 0.625.', 'decimal', 'decimal division', 'place value', 'PST', 'elementary / primary', 'IJMEST_Hourigan_2013_Challenges', 'Mairéad Hourigan & John O''Donoghue', '2013', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '49', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Hourigan_2013_Challenges.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39898, 'Students utilize flawed, incomplete, or bizarre procedures to perform algorithms.', 'Calculating 125 x 1/5 as 1/125 x 5/1 = 1/25.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'algorithms', 'PST', 'elementary / primary', 'IJMEST_Hourigan_2013_Challenges', 'Mairéad Hourigan & John O''Donoghue', '2013', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '49, 50', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Hourigan_2013_Challenges.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39899, 'Students select and apply an incorrect mathematical operation, such as multiplying by a fraction instead of dividing.', 'When asked to evaluate 125 ÷ 1/5, the student calculates 1/5 of 125, resulting in 25.', 'fraction', 'division', 'operations', 'PST', 'elementary / primary', 'IJMEST_Hourigan_2013_Challenges', 'Mairéad Hourigan & John O''Donoghue', '2013', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '49, 50', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Hourigan_2013_Challenges.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39900, 'Students ignore the priority of operations, often evaluating expressions sequentially from left to right as they are read.', 'Evaluating 15 - 10 ÷ 2 + 6 x 4 by first subtracting 10 from 15, yielding answers like -14.', 'whole_number', 'order of operations', '', 'PST', 'elementary / primary', 'IJMEST_Hourigan_2013_Challenges', 'Mairéad Hourigan & John O''Donoghue', '2013', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '50, 51', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Hourigan_2013_Challenges.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39901, 'Students lack a fundamental understanding of concepts and their relationships, leading to errors in associating mathematical ideas.', 'Stating that a square is not a rectangle because ''a rectangle has a different length and breadth.''', 'geometric', 'quadrilaterals', 'squares and rectangles', 'PST', 'elementary / primary', 'IJMEST_Hourigan_2013_Challenges', 'Mairéad Hourigan & John O''Donoghue', '2013', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '51', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Hourigan_2013_Challenges.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39902, 'Students exhibit an inability to reason through a problem context, leading them to randomly apply numbers from the problem despite showing some procedural abilities.', 'Knowing that 66.66% is 2/3, but then randomly subtracting 66.66 from 86.40.', 'percent', 'percentage profit', 'reasoning', 'PST', 'elementary / primary', 'IJMEST_Hourigan_2013_Challenges', 'Mairéad Hourigan & John O''Donoghue', '2013', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '52', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Hourigan_2013_Challenges.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39903, 'Students equate the direction of motion with the direction of the resultant force acting on an object.', 'When asked to mark the direction of force on a projectile (a ball moving under gravity with no air resistance), students marked the arrow tangential to the path (in the direction of motion) rather than vertically downwards.', 'other', 'Newtonian mechanics', 'force and motion', 'student', 'first-year undergraduates', 'IJMEST_Berry_1989_Mechanics', 'J. S. Berry, M. Savage, J. Williams', '1989', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '289, 290', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Berry_1989_Mechanics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39904, 'Students entertain misleading ideas regarding specific physical concepts such as centrifugal force and weightlessness.', '', 'other', 'Newtonian mechanics', 'circular motion and gravity', 'student', 'first-year undergraduates', 'IJMEST_Berry_1989_Mechanics', 'J. S. Berry, M. Savage, J. Williams', '1989', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '289', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Berry_1989_Mechanics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39905, 'Adults incorrectly calculate the profit by making an interim balance between every two consecutive transactions, resulting in double-counting the middle transactions.', 'Danny bought for NIS100 and sold for NIS110, namely 110 - 100 = +10. He sold for NIS110 and bought for NIS120, namely 110 - 120 = -10. He bought for NIS120 and sold for NIS130, namely 130 - 120 = +10. Hence, +10 + (-10) + (+10) = +10.', 'whole_number', 'real-world problems', 'buying and selling', 'mixed', 'adults', 'IJMEST_Gazit_2012_Way', 'Avikam Gazit & Dorit Patkin', '2012', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '172', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Gazit_2012_Way.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39906, 'Adults incorrectly calculate the profit by finding the difference between the first buy and the second sale, completely ignoring the middle transactions.', 'Calculating the difference between the first buy and the second sale, 130 - 100 = +30, and ignoring the two middle transactions of selling and buying (NIS110 and NIS120).', 'whole_number', 'real-world problems', 'buying and selling', 'mixed', 'adults', 'IJMEST_Gazit_2012_Way', 'Avikam Gazit & Dorit Patkin', '2012', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '172', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Gazit_2012_Way.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39907, 'Adults incorrectly insert data into a valid mathematical model for balancing payments and receipts, mistakenly pairing the wrong values to calculate overall expenses and income.', 'Adding NIS100 to NIS130 in order to calculate the expenses and NIS110 to NIS120 for calculating the income. Thus, their result was: 130 - 130 = 0.', 'whole_number', 'real-world problems', 'data coding', 'mixed', 'adults', 'IJMEST_Gazit_2012_Way', 'Avikam Gazit & Dorit Patkin', '2012', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '173', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Gazit_2012_Way.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39908, 'Students rely on superficial patterns from previous examples, such as the explicit itemization of categories, to identify which combinatorial rule to apply, failing when the problem structure varies slightly.', 'A student failed to recognize a permutation problem involving five different scholarships because it did not specify the respective dollar amounts of the scholarships, unlike the example presented in the lecture.', 'probability', 'combinatorics', 'permutations', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'IJMEST_Lee_1989_Pattern-forming', 'Allen S. Lee', '1989', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'mixed', 'primary', '325', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Lee_1989_Pattern-forming.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39909, 'Students fail to recognize when structurally identical mathematical problems require the same method if the wording differs slightly, breaking their learned pattern.', 'A student was confused by a binomial problem asking the probability that each student will have a seat, but could solve it immediately when reworded to the familiar pattern ''What is the probability that 5 or fewer students will show up?''', 'probability', 'binomial distribution', 'word problems', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'IJMEST_Lee_1989_Pattern-forming', 'Allen S. Lee', '1989', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'mixed', 'primary', '326', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Lee_1989_Pattern-forming.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39910, 'Students inflexibly apply statistical distributions based on literal matches to sampling procedures, failing to recognize when conventions or practical approximations warrant a different model.', 'A student used the hypergeometric distribution instead of the binomial for a sample of nine accounts from a large population, reasoning that drawing accounts is like drawing cards without replacement.', 'statistics', 'probability distributions', 'hypergeometric and binomial', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'IJMEST_Lee_1989_Pattern-forming', 'Allen S. Lee', '1989', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'mixed', 'primary', '326', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Lee_1989_Pattern-forming.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39911, 'Students misinterpret verbal definitions of statistical formulas by literally translating English prepositions into arithmetic operations based on everyday language patterns.', 'A student computed the Poisson mean incorrectly by translating the phrase ''the expected number of successes over the specified time'' into a fraction where the numerator is the successes and the denominator is the time.', 'statistics', 'Poisson distribution', 'mean', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'IJMEST_Lee_1989_Pattern-forming', 'Allen S. Lee', '1989', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'mixed', 'primary', '326', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Lee_1989_Pattern-forming.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39912, 'Students conflate the statistical definition of a success in a probability trial with its everyday positive connotation, leading to confusion when modeling negative events or zero occurrences.', 'Students questioned ''How can it be a success if it doesn''t happen?'' when evaluating zero successes, or ''How can defects be considered a success?'' in a problem about finding defective parts.', 'probability', 'probability distributions', 'defining outcomes', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'IJMEST_Lee_1989_Pattern-forming', 'Allen S. Lee', '1989', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'mixed', 'primary', '326', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Lee_1989_Pattern-forming.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39913, 'Preservice teachers are insensitive to mathematical errors regarding sets in textbooks, specifically confusing sets with sequences by failing to recognize that the order of elements in a set is immaterial.', 'Considering a textbook problem well-posed when it asks for the ''next two numbers'' in a set like {0, 6, 12, 18, ...}, or failing to identify an error when a textbook associates an ordinal number (e.g., ''6th'') with a member of a set.', 'discrete', 'set theory', 'sets vs. sequences', 'PST', 'elementary school', 'IJMEST_Pinker_1981_Assimilation', 'Aron Pinker', '1981', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '94, 95, 99, 100', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Pinker_1981_Assimilation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39914, 'Elementary textbooks often misuse the concept of a set by presenting ill-defined sets where elements are not distinct, explicitly separated by commas, or unambiguously identifiable.', 'Asking a child to compare fractions of black objects across sets where the objects look identical and lack commas, making it impossible to decide whether an element in one set belongs to another.', 'discrete', 'set theory', 'well-defined sets and distinct elements', 'PST', 'elementary school', 'IJMEST_Pinker_1981_Assimilation', 'Aron Pinker', '1981', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '94', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Pinker_1981_Assimilation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39915, 'Students interpret the equal sign as an operational command to find an answer rather than a relational symbol representing quantitative equivalence.', 'Viewing 5 + 6 = 11 as meaning ''add 5 to 6 to get 11'' instead of understanding that the left side equals the right side.', 'algebraic', 'equation solving', 'equal sign', 'student', '8th grade', 'IJMEST_Ngu_2017_Will', 'Bing Hiong Ngu & Huy P. Phan', '2017', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Ngu_2017_Will.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39916, 'Students struggle to flexibly solve equations when the pronumeral is located on the right side or in the second position of an expression.', 'Solving 1 = 2p or 3 + a = 11 poses greater difficulty than when the pronumeral is the first term on the left side.', 'algebraic', 'one-step equations', 'relative position of the pronumeral', 'student', '8th grade', 'IJMEST_Ngu_2017_Will', 'Bing Hiong Ngu & Huy P. Phan', '2017', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Ngu_2017_Will.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39917, 'Students incorrectly apply the balance method by making procedural errors with operations when equations contain negative numbers or negative pronumerals.', 'When applying the balance method to solve 5 - n = 0, students mistakenly write +5 on both sides instead of -5.', 'algebraic', 'one-step equations', 'negative numbers and pronumerals', 'student', '8th grade', 'IJMEST_Ngu_2017_Will', 'Bing Hiong Ngu & Huy P. Phan', '2017', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Ngu_2017_Will.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39918, 'Students fail to recognize the structural similarity between equations involving whole numbers and those involving fractions, decimals, or percentages.', 'Students perform well on n/2 = 7 but poorly on structurally identical equations like x/0.5 = 5 or 10%x = 20.', 'algebraic', 'one-step equations', 'fractions, decimals, and percentages', 'student', '8th grade', 'IJMEST_Ngu_2017_Will', 'Bing Hiong Ngu & Huy P. Phan', '2017', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Ngu_2017_Will.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39919, 'Students construct novel but mathematically incorrect methods for operating on and identifying fractions, such as finding a fraction between two given fractions by picking a numerator between the given numerators and a denominator between the given denominators.', 'When asked for a fraction between 1/2 and 3/4, a student suggests 2/3, reasoning that 2 is between 1 and 3 (the numerators) and 3 is between 2 and 4 (the denominators).', 'fraction', 'fractions', 'density and ordering', 'student', 'pre-school to research', 'IJMEST_Lerman_1983_Problem-solving', 'Stephen Lerman', '1983', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'productive', 'secondary', '64', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Lerman_1983_Problem-solving.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39920, 'Students and teachers mistakenly accept that static diagrams or shapes formed by a finite rearrangement of circle wedges constitute an exact parallelogram, ignoring the necessary dynamic infinite limiting process.', 'Assuming it is acceptable to measure the area of the shape made by arranging 4, 8, 10, or 12 wedges simply by using the exact area of a parallelogram formula.', 'geometric', 'circle area', 'limits', 'other', 'elementary and grade 8', 'IJMEST_Kajander_2017_Understanding', 'Ann Kajander & Miroslav Lovric', '2017', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Kajander_2017_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39921, 'Students incorrectly conclude that the height of the limiting parallelogram formed by circle sectors is not equal to the circle''s radius because they treat the arcs of the finite sectors as straight lines prematurely.', 'A student might argue that the top and the bottom sides will become straight and draw a parallelogram over the finite wedge arrangement, concluding that the height is not equal to r.', 'geometric', 'circle area', 'convergence of circle sectors', 'other', 'elementary and grade 8', 'IJMEST_Kajander_2017_Understanding', 'Ann Kajander & Miroslav Lovric', '2017', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Kajander_2017_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39922, 'Students struggle to conceptually define instantaneous speed without reference to an elapsed time period, often confusing it with average speed or defining it strictly as speed over an infinitesimally small period of time.', 'When asked what a speedometer measures, a student replied ''the number of kilometres which would be covered by the car (at the current rate) after an hour of travelling.'' Another stated, ''I do not believe you can refer to instantaneous speed without an elapsed time period... I see instantaneous speed as the speed over an infinitesimally small period of time.''', 'calculus', 'rate of change', 'instantaneous speed', 'student', 'first year university', 'IJMEST_Campuzano_2018_Use', 'Juan Carlos Ponce Campuzano, Kelly E. Matthews & Peter Adams', '2018', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Campuzano_2018_Use.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39923, 'Prospective teachers possess a ''position-dependent base'' image, believing the base is strictly the part, section, or surface of the object that touches the ground or is at the bottom, disregarding its actual geometric properties.', 'Naming a square prism a ''rectangular prism'' when it is placed with its rectangular face touching the desk.', 'geometric', 'geometric solids', 'base concept', 'PST', 'freshmen, sophomore', 'IJMEST_Horzum_2018_Prospective', 'Tuğba Horzum & Erhan Ertekin', '2018', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Horzum_2018_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39924, 'Prospective teachers display a ''figure-dependent base'' image, interpreting the base as any structure (corner, edge, side, or face) that holds the geometric figure up or keeps it balanced.', 'Pointing to the corner of an inverted triangle and stating it is the base because it is the point the triangle ''stands'' on.', 'geometric', 'geometric solids and figures', 'base concept', 'PST', 'freshmen, sophomore', 'IJMEST_Horzum_2018_Prospective', 'Tuğba Horzum & Erhan Ertekin', '2018', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Horzum_2018_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39925, 'Prospective teachers overgeneralize the two-dimensional base concept to three dimensions, leading to a ''side/edge-dependent base'' image where they believe the base of a 3D object is an edge rather than a face.', 'Stating that the edges of a prism with equal length are its bases, or suggesting that any side where a height descends on a solid is a base.', 'geometric', 'geometric solids', 'base concept', 'PST', 'freshmen, sophomore', 'IJMEST_Horzum_2018_Prospective', 'Tuğba Horzum & Erhan Ertekin', '2018', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Horzum_2018_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39926, 'Prospective teachers hold a ''height-dependent base'' image where they rigidly define the base as the geometric structure perpendicular to the height, leading to confusion when heights vary or in non-right prisms.', 'Arguing that a truncate cone positioned on its side cannot have a base because the ''height'' descends to a line segment, which is not a plane.', 'geometric', 'geometric solids', 'base concept', 'PST', 'freshmen, sophomore', 'IJMEST_Horzum_2018_Prospective', 'Tuğba Horzum & Erhan Ertekin', '2018', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Horzum_2018_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39927, 'Prospective teachers hold a ''naming-dependent base'' image, where they do not define the base geometrically but rather rely solely on rote recall that the base is the part that gives the prism its name.', 'Explaining the base simply as ''the geometric figure which gives the prisms their names'' without describing any geometric attributes of that base.', 'geometric', 'prisms', 'naming conventions', 'PST', 'freshmen, sophomore', 'IJMEST_Horzum_2018_Prospective', 'Tuğba Horzum & Erhan Ertekin', '2018', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Horzum_2018_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39928, 'Children''s tendency to write numbers backward or upside down is often viewed as a mistake, but it actually reflects a correct understanding of the essential counting strokes obscured by the inessential cursive connections of Hindu-Arabic notation.', 'Writing numerals like 1, 2, 3, or 4 backward or upside down.', 'whole_number', 'numeration', 'writing numerals', 'other', '', 'IJMEST_Shutler_2010_Predator', 'Paul M.E. Shutler & Ng Swee Fong', '2010', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'productive', 'secondary', '74', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Shutler_2010_Predator.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39929, 'Classroom explanations of borrowing and carrying are often unsuccessful because Hindu-Arabic notation lacks a single symbol for the base (10). A borrowed ''1'' must be written as ''10'', forcing teachers to explain a simpler concept (regrouping) using a more advanced concept (columns within columns).', 'Writing a ''10'' within a single column to represent a borrowed base unit.', 'whole_number', 'addition and subtraction', 'borrowing and carrying', 'other', '', 'IJMEST_Shutler_2010_Predator', 'Paul M.E. Shutler & Ng Swee Fong', '2010', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '75, 77', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Shutler_2010_Predator.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39930, 'Teachers incorrectly believe that a decrease in the rate of inflation means that the actual prices of goods and services are decreasing, rather than recognizing it as a decreasing rate of increase.', 'Stating that ''yes, the more the inflation rate percentage decreases the more the value of a rand increases. As a result the price of goods and services decreases.''', 'percent', 'percentage change', 'decreasing rate of increase', 'in_service', 'Grades 10–12', 'IJMEST_Bansilal_2017_Teachers''', 'Sarah Bansilal', '2017', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Bansilal_2017_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39931, 'When performing consecutive percentage increases over multiple periods, teachers fail to compound the amounts, instead calculating separate percentage increases based on the original base price.', 'For an item costing R200 with consecutive inflation rates of 9.5% and 5.9%, calculating the 2008 price as R200 + 9.5% of R200, and the 2009 price as R200 + 5.9% of R200.', 'percent', 'compound percentage increase', 'base quantity', 'in_service', 'Grades 10–12', 'IJMEST_Bansilal_2017_Teachers''', 'Sarah Bansilal', '2017', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Bansilal_2017_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39932, 'Teachers equate the inflation rate directly with the percentage decrease in the purchasing power of the currency, ignoring the actual reciprocal mathematical relationship between the two.', 'Explaining that a 5.9% inflation rate means the buying power of money has decreased by exactly 5.9%, instead of calculating the true decrease as 5.6%.', 'percent', 'inflation', 'purchasing power', 'in_service', 'Grades 10–12', 'IJMEST_Bansilal_2017_Teachers''', 'Sarah Bansilal', '2017', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Bansilal_2017_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39933, 'Teachers incorrectly order decimals by applying the rule ''the shorter the decimal places the larger the number'' (or selecting as smaller the number that has more digits).', 'When ordering 0.248, 0.4, 0.63, and 0.85 increasingly, 32% of teachers sequenced them as 0.248, 0.85, 0.63, and 0.4.', 'decimal', 'decimals', 'ordering decimals', 'in_service', 'grade one to grade five', 'IJMEST_Ubuz_2010_Primary', 'Behiye Ubuz & Betul Yayan', '2010', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '787, 796, 797, 800', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Ubuz_2010_Primary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39934, 'Teachers interpret the decimal point as a separator between two independent whole numbers, ignoring its role in place value.', 'When asked to find the nearest number to 0.16, 55% of the teachers selected 0.21 instead of 0.2, likely thinking 16 is closer to 21 than to 2.', 'decimal', 'decimals', 'finding the nearest decimal', 'in_service', 'grade one to grade five', 'IJMEST_Ubuz_2010_Primary', 'Behiye Ubuz & Betul Yayan', '2010', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '788, 796, 797', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Ubuz_2010_Primary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39935, 'Teachers lack understanding of the denseness and denary nature of decimals, ignoring the number of divisions between whole numbers or decimals, or ignoring the left/right calibration.', 'Responding ''3.2'' instead of ''3.4'' when identifying a decimal on a number line where the subunit was five, or answering ''2.3'' instead of ''2.03'' when the calibration is between 2.0 and 2.1.', 'decimal', 'decimals', 'scale reading', 'in_service', 'grade one to grade five', 'IJMEST_Ubuz_2010_Primary', 'Behiye Ubuz & Betul Yayan', '2010', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '788, 795, 797', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Ubuz_2010_Primary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39936, 'Teachers add or subtract decimals starting from the last digit behind the comma regardless of place value, or struggle with borrowing and carrying.', 'Responding ''0.12'' when adding 0.3 and 0.9, or giving ''6.99'' when adding 6.98 to 0.1.', 'decimal', 'addition and subtraction', 'decimals', 'in_service', 'grade one to grade five', 'IJMEST_Ubuz_2010_Primary', 'Behiye Ubuz & Betul Yayan', '2010', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '788, 796, 797', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Ubuz_2010_Primary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39937, 'Students base their predictions on a small, specific subset of data rather than identifying the trend in the data as a whole.', 'Predicting the price of a 0.25 carat ring by calculating the mean price of only the 0.25 carat rings in the dataset, rather than using the overall trend.', 'statistics', 'bivariate data', 'trend', 'student', 'Year 12 (upper secondary)', 'IJMEST_Forster_2007_Technologies', 'P. A. Forster', '2007', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '147, 148', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Forster_2007_Technologies.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39938, 'Students do not intuitively know which distances to average when determining a line of best fit, often assuming diagonal distances instead of vertical residuals.', 'When asked to find the average distance from the line, students did not know whether to use diagonal or vertical distances.', 'statistics', 'bivariate data', 'least squares regression', 'student', 'Year 12 (upper secondary)', 'IJMEST_Forster_2007_Technologies', 'P. A. Forster', '2007', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '153, 154', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Forster_2007_Technologies.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39939, 'Students uncritically accept fitted models without verifying if the features, such as intercepts, make sense in the real-world context of the data.', 'Accepting a linear model for diamond prices that possesses a negative y-intercept, which nonsensically implies a seller would pay a buyer to take a zero-carat ring.', 'statistics', 'mathematical modeling', 'bivariate data', 'student', 'Year 12 (upper secondary)', 'IJMEST_Forster_2007_Technologies', 'P. A. Forster', '2007', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '144, 154, 155, 156', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Forster_2007_Technologies.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39940, 'Students experience confusion over which variable should be assigned as the independent variable and which as the dependent variable.', '', 'statistics', 'bivariate data', 'variables', 'student', 'Year 12 (upper secondary)', 'IJMEST_Forster_2007_Technologies', 'P. A. Forster', '2007', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'passing', '144, 148, 150', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Forster_2007_Technologies.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39941, 'Students expect an even distribution without clustering in small random samples, driven by an everyday belief in ''fairness'' or ''taking turns''.', 'Expecting to get one of each of the ten possible leading digits (0 to 9) exactly once when generating ten random numbers.', 'probability', 'randomness', 'uniformity and clustering', 'student', '', 'IJMEST_Green_1993_Randomnessa', 'D. R. Green', '1993', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '898, 899', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Green_1993_Randomnessa.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39942, 'Students believe in the ''law of small numbers'' or gambler''s fallacy, assuming that short-term random results will strictly compensate to maintain a theoretical average.', 'Believing that in roulette, if black has come up five times in a row, the odds must favour red on the next roll to satisfy the ''law of averages''.', 'probability', 'randomness', 'gambler''s fallacy', 'student', '', 'IJMEST_Green_1993_Randomnessa', 'D. R. Green', '1993', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '900', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Green_1993_Randomnessa.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39943, 'Students violate the conjunction rule by estimating the probability or frequency of the conjunction of two events to be greater than the probability or frequency of at least one of the component events.', 'Providing a higher frequency estimate for the number of men out of 100 who ''both are over 55 years old and have had one or more heart attacks'' than for the number who ''are over 55 years old''.', 'probability', 'conjunction events', 'conjunction fallacy', 'student', 'grades 5 to 11', 'IJMEST_Watson_2002_School', 'Jane M. Watson & Jonathan B. Moritz', '2002', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Watson_2002_School.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39944, 'Students confuse the conditional probability of an event with its inverse conditional probability, assuming the two statements are asking the exact same question or represent complementary events.', 'Estimating that ''the probability that a woman is a school teacher'' is 50%, and that ''the probability that a school teacher is a woman'' is also 50%.', 'probability', 'conditional events', 'inverse conditional', 'student', 'grades 5 to 11', 'IJMEST_Watson_2002_School', 'Jane M. Watson & Jonathan B. Moritz', '2002', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Watson_2002_School.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39945, 'Students inappropriately use their frequency estimate from a previous related question as the new total conditioning reference set, ignoring the explicitly stated base rate in the new question.', 'After estimating that 30 out of 100 men are left-handed, answering that 15 out of 100 left-handed adults are men (using the previously generated 30 as the implicit reference set, causing them to estimate half of it: 15).', 'probability', 'conditional events', 'conditioning reference set', 'student', 'grades 5 to 11', 'IJMEST_Watson_2002_School', 'Jane M. Watson & Jonathan B. Moritz', '2002', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Watson_2002_School.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39946, 'Students make systematic errors during multi-column subtraction, predominantly caused by the ''borrowing'' sub-procedure, particularly when borrowing across zero or incrementing a 9-digit.', 'When subtracting 1997 from 3002, students struggle with ''borrowing across zero'' in the decomposition method or ''incrementing the 9-digit'' in the equal addition method. Another example is ''borrowing from the tens column when it was unnecessary'' (e.g., 37 - 4 = 23).', 'whole_number', 'multi-column subtraction', 'borrowing and regrouping', 'student', 'primary schools', 'IJMEST_Attisha_1984_Microcomputer-aided', 'M. G. Attisha & K. J. Al-Daimi', '1984', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '559, 560, 561, 562', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Attisha_1984_Microcomputer-aided.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39947, 'Students make unsystematic errors (''slips'') and systematic errors due to misusing basic subtraction and addition fact tables.', '', 'whole_number', 'arithmetic operations', 'fact tables', 'student', 'primary schools', 'IJMEST_Attisha_1984_Microcomputer-aided', 'M. G. Attisha & K. J. Al-Daimi', '1984', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '561, 563', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Attisha_1984_Microcomputer-aided.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39948, 'Students confuse the addition of fractions with that of natural numbers by adding both the numerators and the denominators.', 'I have eaten 1/5 of a pizza and then I eat another 1/3. How much have I eaten? 1/5 + 1/3 = 2/8', 'fraction', 'fraction addition', 'algorithms', 'student', '5th and 6th grade', 'IJMEST_Bouta_2013_Cognitive', 'Hara Bouta & Fotini Paraskeva', '2013', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '171, 172', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Bouta_2013_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39949, 'Students fail to understand fraction magnitudes, either misapplying the whole number magnitude to the denominator or repeating memorized rules without understanding.', 'When asked which is bigger between 1/3 and 1/4, a student answers ''1/3 is bigger because 4 is more than 3''.', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'magnitude', 'student', '5th and 6th grade', 'IJMEST_Bouta_2013_Cognitive', 'Hara Bouta & Fotini Paraskeva', '2013', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '173', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Bouta_2013_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39950, 'Students view fractions with different denominators as completely unrelated because they are divided into different numbers of parts.', 'Stating there is no relationship between 1/3 and 1/6 because ''in the case of 1/3 there is something which is divided into 3 equal parts, whereas in the case of 1/6 we divide it into 6 equal parts.''', 'fraction', 'fraction relationships', 'multiples and submultiples', 'student', '5th and 6th grade', 'IJMEST_Bouta_2013_Cognitive', 'Hara Bouta & Fotini Paraskeva', '2013', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '173', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Bouta_2013_Cognitive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39951, 'Students confuse the concepts of certain, impossible, and probable, often assigning deterministic absolute labels to high or low probability events.', 'Naming the low probability of a spinner stopping at green as impossible and the high probability of stopping at red as a certain event.', 'probability', 'probability of an event', 'certainty and impossibility', 'student', 'seventh grade', 'IJMEST_Gurbuz_2010_Effect', 'Ramazan Gürbüz', '2010', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '757, 758', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Gurbuz_2010_Effect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39952, 'Students rely on their everyday understanding of standard dice, leading to errors in determining sample spaces and outcome sums when non-standard or custom dice are used.', 'Assuming the sum of outcomes could be 5, 6, 7, 8, and 9 when tossing custom dice designed as (2222 44) and (3333 55), failing to realize 6 or 8 were impossible to obtain.', 'probability', 'sample space', 'compound events', 'student', 'seventh grade', 'IJMEST_Gurbuz_2010_Effect', 'Ramazan Gürbüz', '2010', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '757, 758, 759', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Gurbuz_2010_Effect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39953, 'Students exhibit an equiprobability bias, assuming that every random outcome within a sample space is equally likely to occur.', 'Thinking that getting a sum of 5, 7, and 9 on the upper faces of custom dice would have an equal numerical probability in each toss.', 'probability', 'probability of an event', 'equiprobability', 'student', 'seventh grade', 'IJMEST_Gurbuz_2010_Effect', 'Ramazan Gürbüz', '2010', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '759', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Gurbuz_2010_Effect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39954, 'Students base probability comparisons on informal visual intuitions, focusing on a single physical dimension rather than calculating the geometric area.', 'Believing the probability of hitting the green section of a dartboard is lowest because it is nearest to the edge, or that hitting blue is hardest because it is at the very center.', 'probability', 'geometric probability', 'probability comparisons', 'student', 'seventh grade', 'IJMEST_Gurbuz_2010_Effect', 'Ramazan Gürbüz', '2010', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '760, 761', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Gurbuz_2010_Effect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39955, 'Students expect that experimental probabilities will perfectly match theoretical distributions, leading to the belief that uniform or symmetrical distributions will invariably occur in trials.', 'Believing that if a spinner with equal black and white sections is turned 40 times, it will stop exactly 20 times on black and 20 times on white.', 'probability', 'experimental probability', 'sample distribution', 'student', 'seventh grade', 'IJMEST_Gurbuz_2010_Effect', 'Ramazan Gürbüz', '2010', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '760', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Gurbuz_2010_Effect.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39956, 'Pre-service teachers struggle to distinguish between find-part and find-percent problems, leading to incorrect calculations of the percentage quantity.', 'Writing ''55/90 × 100'' when asked to find a 55% markup on a $90 dress.', 'percent', 'percentage change problems', 'percentage quantity', 'PST', 'pre-service primary teachers', 'IJMEST_Ngu_2019_Solution', 'Bing Hiong Ngu', '2019', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Ngu_2019_Solution.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39957, 'Pre-service teachers misinterpret the problem situation, failing to differentiate between complementary conditions like absent versus present, or confusing percentage decrease with percentage increase.', 'Calculating the number of absent students (28 × 6% = 1.68 = 2) when the question asks for the number of students present, or calculating a 15% increase (50 + 7.5 = 57.5) instead of a discount.', 'percent', 'percentage change problems', 'problem interpretation', 'PST', 'pre-service primary teachers', 'IJMEST_Ngu_2019_Solution', 'Bing Hiong Ngu', '2019', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Ngu_2019_Solution.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39958, 'Dealing with a percentage greater than 100% causes pre-service teachers to provide an incomplete solution where they only calculate the percentage amount without applying it to the original amount.', 'For a 120% increase on $1.25, calculating 1.25 × 120/100 = 1.50 and providing that as the final new price.', 'percent', 'percentage change problems', 'percentage greater than 100%', 'PST', 'pre-service primary teachers', 'IJMEST_Ngu_2019_Solution', 'Bing Hiong Ngu', '2019', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Ngu_2019_Solution.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39959, 'Pre-service teachers confuse simple percentage change problems with financial mathematics problems and inappropriately apply the simple interest formula.', 'Writing ''I = PRN'' to solve for a 7% pay rise on a $52,000 present salary.', 'percent', 'percentage change problems', 'formula application', 'PST', 'pre-service primary teachers', 'IJMEST_Ngu_2019_Solution', 'Bing Hiong Ngu', '2019', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Ngu_2019_Solution.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39960, 'Students interpret the equals sign as a one-directional signal to compute an answer rather than a relational symbol of equivalence.', 'Viewing an equation like box = 3 + 4 as backwards because the operations appear on the right side.', 'algebraic', 'equations', 'equals sign', 'student', 'college students', 'IJMEST_Bernard_1984_Student', 'JOHN E. BERNARD and GEORGE W. BRIGHT', '1984', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '401', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Bernard_1984_Student.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39961, 'Students fail to conserve equations, believing that changing the letter used for the variable changes the solution of the equation.', 'Stating that the equation 7W + 22 = 109 has a larger solution than 7x + 22 = 109 because W is later in the alphabet.', 'algebraic', 'linear equations', 'variables', 'student', 'college students', 'IJMEST_Bernard_1984_Student', 'JOHN E. BERNARD and GEORGE W. BRIGHT', '1984', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '401', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Bernard_1984_Student.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39962, 'Students incorrectly split a rational equation into two separate linear equations by equating the respective numerators and denominators.', 'For the equation 5/10 = (x-10)/(x+5), the student writes 5 = x-10 and 10 = x+5.', 'algebraic', 'rational equations', 'solving proportions', 'student', 'college students', 'IJMEST_Bernard_1984_Student', 'JOHN E. BERNARD and GEORGE W. BRIGHT', '1984', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '405', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Bernard_1984_Student.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39963, 'Students overgeneralize algebraic and fraction rules by adding numerators together and denominators together when asked to add rational expressions.', 'Changing a/b + c/d to (a+c)/(b+d).', 'fraction', 'fraction addition', 'algebraic fractions', 'student', 'college students', 'IJMEST_Bernard_1984_Student', 'JOHN E. BERNARD and GEORGE W. BRIGHT', '1984', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '406, 407', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Bernard_1984_Student.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39964, 'Students perform arithmetic operations strictly from left to right instead of following the standard order of operations.', 'Evaluating 3 + 4 x 5 as 35 rather than 23.', 'whole_number', 'order of operations', '', 'student', 'college students', 'IJMEST_Bernard_1984_Student', 'JOHN E. BERNARD and GEORGE W. BRIGHT', '1984', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '407', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Bernard_1984_Student.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39965, 'Students formulate a primitive conjecture based on an implicitly limited case and believe their conjecture is entirely false when confronted with a counterexample (a transformed diagram), before realizing the underlying mathematical relationship still holds.', 'Students conjectured that two segments connecting vertices of overlapping equilateral triangles are equal, but upon moving the manipulatives so the triangles no longer overlapped, they concluded the non-overlapping case was a counterexample and ''there seems to be no relationship''.', 'geometric', 'properties of triangles', 'congruence and counterexamples', 'student', 'eighth and ninth graders', 'IJMEST_Komatsu_2014_Rethinking', 'Kotaro Komatsu, Yosuke Tsujiyama & Aruta Sakamaki', '2014', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'productive', 'primary', '1059, 1060, 1061', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Komatsu_2014_Rethinking.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39966, 'Students believe a proven geometric relationship becomes invalid when implicit diagrammatic suppositions are violated, treating the new configuration as a counterexample that refutes the statement.', 'Believing that the relationship PQ = DQ - BP is impossible when the intersecting line is drawn outside the square because segment BP becomes longer than segment DQ, making DQ - BP negative.', 'geometric', 'proof and proving', 'implicit suppositions and counterexamples', 'student', 'eighth and ninth graders', 'IJMEST_Komatsu_2014_Rethinking', 'Kotaro Komatsu, Yosuke Tsujiyama & Aruta Sakamaki', '2014', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'productive', 'primary', '1062, 1063', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Komatsu_2014_Rethinking.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39967, 'Students confuse the binomial coefficient symbol with a fraction by habitually writing a fraction bar between the population size and the sample size.', 'Writing the binomial coefficient as the fraction N/n.', 'combinatorial', 'combinations', 'binomial coefficient notation', 'other', '', 'IJMEST_Luft_1990_Teaching', 'Philip E. Luft', '1990', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'passing', '484', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Luft_1990_Teaching.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39968, 'Students experience conceptual difficulties and confusion because concrete models for negative numbers require formal operational thinking to perform subtraction, causing cognitive friction for children still in the concrete operational stage.', 'When finding the difference between a shift of +5 and a shift of -2, the student must recognize zero as an intermediate point of reference, effectively requiring them to solve a complex ''three-term series'' problem.', 'integer', 'negative numbers', 'subtraction', 'student', 'primary school age to early secondary', 'IJMEST_Galbraith_1974_Negative', 'Mary J. Galbraith', '1974', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '87, 88, 89, 90', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Galbraith_1974_Negative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39969, 'Students are confused by the dual role of signs on a standard number line, where a plus or minus sign is ambiguously used to indicate both a static position and a direction of movement.', 'In the expression (+3) + (+2), the first plus sign indicates a starting position on the line, while the second plus sign indicates a direction of movement (a shift to the right).', 'integer', 'negative numbers', 'number line models', 'student', 'primary school age to early secondary', 'IJMEST_Galbraith_1974_Negative', 'Mary J. Galbraith', '1974', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '85, 87', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Galbraith_1974_Negative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39970, 'Students confuse the intuitive, everyday concept of a dictator with its formal, technical mathematical definition, expecting uniqueness to be an inherent definitional property rather than a theorem to be proved.', 'Students refuse to question the uniqueness of a dictator because ''this lies in the nature of the dictator concept'', failing to separate the explicandum from the explicans.', 'discrete', 'axiomatization', 'definitions', 'student', 'age 17-18 (grades 11 and 12)', 'IJMEST_Steiner_1988_Mathematization', 'Hans-Georg Steiner', '1988', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'productive', 'primary', '205', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Steiner_1988_Mathematization.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39971, 'Students feel uncomfortable and perceive a logical flaw when a concept is defined implicitly by axioms rather than explicitly referenced to external, concrete properties.', 'Students express ''very bad feelings'' and ''concerns about the seemingly unavoidable circularity'' when ''winning coalition'' is defined purely by its set-theoretic properties (axioms) rather than a vote distribution.', 'discrete', 'axiomatics', 'implicit definitions', 'student', 'age 17-18 (grades 11 and 12)', 'IJMEST_Steiner_1988_Mathematization', 'Hans-Georg Steiner', '1988', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'productive', 'primary', '206', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Steiner_1988_Mathematization.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39972, 'Students experience a ''theory crisis'' when their established axiomatic model contradicts a newly introduced, contextually different situation.', 'Applying the voting body axioms to proposals for opening vs. closing a window yields complementary winning coalitions, contradicting the axiom that a complement of a winning coalition cannot be winning.', 'discrete', 'mathematical modelling', 'model generalizability', 'student', 'age 17-18 (grades 11 and 12)', 'IJMEST_Steiner_1988_Mathematization', 'Hans-Georg Steiner', '1988', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'productive', 'primary', '208, 209', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Steiner_1988_Mathematization.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39973, 'Students incorrectly assume that the ratio of a member''s votes to total votes adequately measures their political power in a voting body.', 'Students realize a chairman with 2 votes against three members with 1 vote has twice the power, but a member with 100 votes against three with 99 has nearly identical vote ratio yet the same absolute power.', 'discrete', 'game theory', 'power indices', 'student', 'age 17-18 (grades 11 and 12)', 'IJMEST_Steiner_1988_Mathematization', 'Hans-Georg Steiner', '1988', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'productive', 'primary', '202, 211', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Steiner_1988_Mathematization.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39974, 'Children do not recognize that equal pushes or pulls in opposite directions result in zero displacement, instead suggesting qualitative physical transformations like the truck breaking or widening.', 'Suggesting the truck would ''squash'', ''widen'', or ''break in half'' when two dolls pull it with equal strength from opposite ends.', 'integer', 'operations with integers', 'zero resultant displacement', 'student', '8 1/2 to 15 years', 'IJMEST_Galbraith_1975_Smith', 'Mary J. Galbraith', '1975', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '289, 290', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Galbraith_1975_Smith.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39975, 'Children incorrectly include the starting point in their count when measuring displacement along a number line, an error exacerbated by the starting point being named or addressed with a number.', 'When the truck starts at red 1 and moves 2 sections, counting ''one'' for red 1 and ''two'' for Start, incorrectly concluding the truck moved to Start.', 'integer', 'number line', 'displacement and counting', 'student', '8 1/2 to 15 years', 'IJMEST_Galbraith_1975_Smith', 'Mary J. Galbraith', '1975', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '294', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Galbraith_1975_Smith.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39976, 'Children consider only one aspect of a problem involving multiple interacting systems (positions and displacements), leading to explanations that are inconsistent with all the provided data.', 'When asked what Jones did if the truck started at Start and ended at black 1, and Smith pulled 2 sections, answering that Jones was pulling ''up to 3'' because ''if he started at 2, then he''d have 3''.', 'integer', 'operations with integers', 'reversibility and multiple interacting systems', 'student', '8 1/2 to 15 years', 'IJMEST_Galbraith_1975_Smith', 'Mary J. Galbraith', '1975', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '296', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Galbraith_1975_Smith.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39977, 'Students struggle to integrate the abstract scheme of a mathematical operation, an identity element, and the definition of an inverse as elements cancelling out to yield the identity.', 'Failing to abstract the property that adding 0 or multiplying by 1 leaves a number unchanged into a general property of an operation and an identity element.', 'algebraic', 'group theory', 'inverses and identity elements', 'mixed', 'adults (teachers and college-level students)', 'IJMEST_Leron_2016_What', 'Uri Leron & Lisser Rye Ejersbo', '2016', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'productive', 'primary', '124', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Leron_2016_What.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39978, 'Students find it counterintuitive and difficult to understand cases where a mathematical function or operation acts as its own inverse.', 'Struggling to see that the function y = 1/x applied twice returns the original value x.', 'algebraic', 'group theory', 'self-inverse elements', 'mixed', 'adults (teachers and college-level students)', 'IJMEST_Leron_2016_What', 'Uri Leron & Lisser Rye Ejersbo', '2016', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'productive', 'primary', '126, 129, 131', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Leron_2016_What.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39979, 'Students take the existence of a limit for granted, assuming a sequence must converge simply because it is non-decreasing and bounded, without recognizing that completeness is a necessary condition.', 'Explaining what non-decreasing and bounded mean, and then concluding ''intuitively, it must converge'' without mathematically justifying why the limit exists.', 'calculus', 'limits', 'convergence of sequences', 'student', 'first-year students, undergraduate', 'IJMEST_Berge_2010_Students''', 'Analia Bergé', '2010', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '221, 222, 223', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Berge_2010_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39980, 'Students use extra-mathematical, discrete metaphorical contexts to understand sequences, viewing them as finite and reducing limits to simple maximums.', 'Comparing a sequence to pupils sorted by height or climbing a staircase to a closed door, where the highest point or last step is considered the limit.', 'calculus', 'sequences', 'limits', 'student', 'first-year students, undergraduate', 'IJMEST_Berge_2010_Students''', 'Analia Bergé', '2010', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '222', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Berge_2010_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39981, 'Students believe a non-decreasing, bounded-above sequence necessarily stabilizes and becomes constant after a certain index.', 'Explaining that the sequence arrives at a top point and its image will always be that point, regardless of the value applied.', 'calculus', 'sequences', 'convergence', 'student', 'first-year students, undergraduate', 'IJMEST_Berge_2010_Students''', 'Analia Bergé', '2010', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '222', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Berge_2010_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39982, 'Students hold a natural vision of completeness based on everyday meanings, defining ''complete'' as ''replete'' or having ''no gaps'', rather than possessing an operational mathematical property.', 'Stating that R is complete because ''it contains all the numbers'' or representing it as a continuous line without gaps.', 'calculus', 'real numbers', 'completeness', 'student', 'first-year students, undergraduate', 'IJMEST_Berge_2010_Students''', 'Analia Bergé', '2010', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '224', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Berge_2010_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39983, 'Students struggle to interpret and locate a decimal measurement on a physical ruler, viewing the decimal string as an arbitrary identifier rather than a quantitative length.', 'Trying to locate 12.88 cm on a ruler, guessing it might mean ''12 and 8'' or ''13'', and stating ''It has gone off 12 88! We have to measure again!''', 'measurement', 'linear measurement', 'decimal approximations on a ruler', 'mixed', 'grades 3-7', 'IJMEST_Bungum_2014_Mathematical', 'Berit Bungum, Saeed Manshadi & Dag Atle Lysne', '2014', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '1139, 1140, 1141', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Bungum_2014_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39984, 'A student struggles with the transition from two-dimensional templates to a three-dimensional model, expressing concern that the scaled model ''becomes flat''.', 'When discussing how parts fit together, a student comments, ''They fit on the top, but it becomes flat.''', 'geometric', 'scaling', '2D to 3D transitions', 'mixed', 'grades 3-7', 'IJMEST_Bungum_2014_Mathematical', 'Berit Bungum, Saeed Manshadi & Dag Atle Lysne', '2014', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'productive', 'secondary', '1137, 1142', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Bungum_2014_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39985, 'Pupils struggle to grasp the conceptual and metrical meaning of real numbers and infinite processes, often confusing physical or perceptual limits (manipulation level) with mathematical limits (numeric level) and discrete patterns with continuous processes.', 'Pupils may understand algebraically that no fraction squared equals 2 but cannot grasp the metrical challenge; or they may view a line segment merely as a finite pencil drawing rather than a continuous whole with infinite non-numerable parts.', 'other', 'real numbers', 'infinite processes and limits', 'in_service', 'secondary school', 'IJMEST_CoriatBenarroch_1993_Numbers', 'M. Coriat-Benarroch, P. S. Martinez-Lopez & J. Baena-Ruiz', '1993', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '501, 508, 509', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_CoriatBenarroch_1993_Numbers.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39986, 'Students incorrectly interpret fractions using a part-to-part ratio rather than a part-whole ratio when analyzing partitioned continuous quantities or discrete objects.', 'When shown a rectangle with 1 shaded part and 4 unshaded parts and asked ''What fraction of this rectangle is shaded?'', a student selects 1/4 (the part-to-part ratio) instead of 1/5.', 'fraction', 'fraction representation', 'part-whole interpretation', 'student', '5th and 7th grade', 'IJMEST_Gennaro_1990_Role', 'M. Di Gennaro, V. Picciarelli & M. T. Rienzi', '1990', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '747, 748, 749, 751', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Gennaro_1990_Role.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39987, 'Students ignore base-rate information and rely solely on indicant or diagnostic data when calculating conditional probabilities.', 'In the taxicab problem, providing an intuitive response of 80% based solely on the witness''s accuracy, ignoring the 10% base rate of blue cabs.', 'probability', 'conditional probability', 'base-rate fallacy', 'student', 'grades 3 to 13', 'IJMEST_Watson_2007_Development', 'J. M. Watson and B. A. Kelly', '2007', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '213, 214, 230, 231', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Watson_2007_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39988, 'Students are misled by the temporal sequence of events, believing that a condition can only affect events that happen subsequently rather than retrospectively.', 'When told the second marble drawn from an urn is white, a student ignores the condition and claims the probability the first marble was white is 1/2 or 50%, focusing only on the original state.', 'probability', 'conditional probability', 'temporal sequence', 'student', 'grades 3 to 13', 'IJMEST_Watson_2007_Development', 'J. M. Watson and B. A. Kelly', '2007', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '214, 222', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Watson_2007_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39989, 'Students allow their contextual or social knowledge to imply casual relationships that interfere with their mathematical interpretation of conditional events.', 'Concluding that the probability a schoolteacher is a woman is higher merely because the student observed ''more women teachers than guys'' at their school, ignoring the actual comparative base sizes of women and schoolteachers.', 'probability', 'conditional probability', 'contextual interference', 'student', 'grades 3 to 13', 'IJMEST_Watson_2007_Development', 'J. M. Watson and B. A. Kelly', '2007', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '214, 215, 218, 219', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Watson_2007_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39990, 'Students incorrectly apply the averaging algorithm by first averaging the known data points, then attempting to arbitrarily adjust that intermediate sum and divide again to produce the target average.', 'A student added the hats sold in three weeks (9, 3, 6), divided by 3 to get 6. Realizing the target average was 7, they added 3 to the sum, divided by 3 to get 7, and gave 3 as the final answer.', 'statistics', 'averages', 'finding a missing value', 'student', 'fourth, fifth, and sixth grade', 'IJMEST_Cai_2003_Singaporean', 'Jinfa Cai', '2003', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '722', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Cai_2003_Singaporean.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39991, 'Students correctly calculate the total sum needed to reach the target average but mistakenly submit this total as the final answer instead of subtracting the known values to find the missing data point.', 'Finding that the total number of hats sold over four weeks must be 28 to average 7, and giving 28 as the final answer.', 'statistics', 'averages', 'finding a missing value', 'student', 'fourth, fifth, and sixth grade', 'IJMEST_Cai_2003_Singaporean', 'Jinfa Cai', '2003', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '722', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Cai_2003_Singaporean.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39992, 'Students unsystematically extract all numerical values from a word problem, including extraneous labels like week numbers, and add them together.', 'Adding the hat quantities (9, 3, 6) together with all the week numbers (1, 2, 3, 4) and the target average (7) to get 39 as the answer.', 'whole_number', 'word problems', 'identifying relevant information', 'student', 'fourth, fifth, and sixth grade', 'IJMEST_Cai_2003_Singaporean', 'Jinfa Cai', '2003', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '722', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Cai_2003_Singaporean.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39993, 'Preservice teachers and students base the generation of number sentences on the literal sequence of numbers in a word problem rather than the underlying structural relationships.', 'Writing ''4 x 2 x 3'' because the problem text mentions ''4 strings'', ''2 sets'', and ''3 boxes'' in that exact order, ignoring that it should structurally represent 3 groups of 2 groups of 4.', 'whole_number', 'multiplication', 'word problems', 'PST', 'fourth grade', 'IJMEST_Ding_2018_Transferring', 'Meixia Ding & Kayla Heffernan', '2018', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Ding_2018_Transferring.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39994, 'Preservice teachers and students focus on the meaning of individual isolated quantities rather than the multiplicative interactions between quantities.', 'Explaining the equation 4 x 3 x 2 by separately asking ''what does the 4 mean?'' and ''what''s the 3?'' instead of asking what ''4 x 3'' refers to together as a combined group.', 'whole_number', 'multiplication', 'quantitative interactions', 'PST', 'fourth grade', 'IJMEST_Ding_2018_Transferring', 'Meixia Ding & Kayla Heffernan', '2018', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Ding_2018_Transferring.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39995, 'Teachers and students mistakenly interpret any sequential calculation of three factors or alternative addition strategies as demonstrating the associative property of multiplication.', 'A student calculates 4 x 3 = 12, then adds 12 + 12 = 24, and the teacher incorrectly praises this entirely different mathematical process as showing the associative property.', 'algebraic', 'associative property', 'multiplication', 'PST', 'fourth grade', 'IJMEST_Ding_2018_Transferring', 'Meixia Ding & Kayla Heffernan', '2018', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Ding_2018_Transferring.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39996, 'Students ignore the specified order of events and the multiplicative rule when calculating the probability of a sequence of independent events.', 'When asked for the probability of a coin landing heads on the first three tosses and tails on the next two, students calculate as if the order were immaterial or accumulate the probability incorrectly.', 'probability', 'joint events', 'independent events', 'student', '15-16-year-olds', 'IJMEST_Wood_1976_Mastery', 'R. Wood, M. Brown', '1976', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '301, 302', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Wood_1976_Mastery.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39997, 'Students lack understanding of fundamental probability axioms, specifically the rule that a computed probability cannot exceed one.', 'Choosing an option of 5/2 for the probability of a specific outcome across five coin tosses.', 'probability', 'axioms of probability', 'probability limits', 'student', '15-16-year-olds', 'IJMEST_Wood_1976_Mastery', 'R. Wood, M. Brown', '1976', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '301', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Wood_1976_Mastery.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39998, 'Students confuse the addition and multiplication laws when calculating the probability of compound events.', 'When calculating the probability of drawing two aces from a deck without replacement, selecting an answer choice that implies adding rather than multiplying the probabilities.', 'probability', 'joint events', 'addition and multiplication laws', 'student', '15-16-year-olds', 'IJMEST_Wood_1976_Mastery', 'R. Wood, M. Brown', '1976', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '302', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Wood_1976_Mastery.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(39999, 'Students are unaware of how to use complementary events and the concept of independence across multiple trials.', 'When asked the probability that none of three thrown dice shows a five or six, selecting 1/3 (the correct probability of the outcome for a single throw) rather than cubing the complementary probability 2/3.', 'probability', 'complementary events', 'independence', 'student', '15-16-year-olds', 'IJMEST_Wood_1976_Mastery', 'R. Wood, M. Brown', '1976', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '303, 304', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Wood_1976_Mastery.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40000, 'Students view a variable as a single unknown number that needs to be solved for, rather than understanding it as an entity that can represent multiple values or as a distinct statistical concept.', 'Describing a variable as ''what I have to solve for'' or an ''undefined aspect of an equation,'' and failing to differentiate between variables in mathematics and statistics.', 'statistics', 'variable', 'statistical variable', 'student', 'first-semester undergraduate', 'IJMEST_Cook_2016_Incoming', 'Samuel A. Cook & Timothy Fukawa-Connelly', '2016', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '174', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Cook_2016_Incoming.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40001, 'Students possess a procedural, process-oriented understanding of the mean and median, focusing on the computing algorithm rather than viewing them as representative measures of a data set''s centre.', 'Claiming the mean and median do not describe similar things because they are calculated via dissimilar processes, without recognizing that both attempt to summarize or balance a data set.', 'statistics', 'measures of centre', 'mean and median', 'student', 'first-semester undergraduate', 'IJMEST_Cook_2016_Incoming', 'Samuel A. Cook & Timothy Fukawa-Connelly', '2016', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '174, 175, 181', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Cook_2016_Incoming.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40002, 'Students conflate the sample mean with the values of the individual data points in the sample, mistakenly assuming that a sample with a specific mean must be composed of identical values equal to that mean.', 'When told a sample of four has a mean of 380, a student reasoned ''seeing (the data point) four times within a large set of numbers isn''t unusual,'' implying all four members of the sample had the value 380.', 'statistics', 'sample mean', 'sampling distribution', 'student', 'first-semester undergraduate', 'IJMEST_Cook_2016_Incoming', 'Samuel A. Cook & Timothy Fukawa-Connelly', '2016', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '175, 181', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Cook_2016_Incoming.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40003, 'Students rely on memorized, rule-based approaches for standard deviation, such as the empirical rule, and misapply these rules to sampling distributions because they lack a conceptual understanding of variation and standard error.', 'Using the standard deviation of a population to determine if a single data point of 380 is unusual, and then incorrectly applying the exact same single-point procedure to determine if a sample of four with a mean of 380 is unusual.', 'statistics', 'standard deviation', 'sampling distribution', 'student', 'first-semester undergraduate', 'IJMEST_Cook_2016_Incoming', 'Samuel A. Cook & Timothy Fukawa-Connelly', '2016', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '177, 178, 179, 180', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Cook_2016_Incoming.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40004, 'Students have difficulty associating statistical symbols with their correct concepts, confusing population parameters with sample statistics or incorrectly identifying mathematical symbols as statistical.', 'Calculating a mean and labeling it with mu, then changing it to x-bar, and ultimately deciding they did not know which was correct.', 'statistics', 'notation', 'statistical symbols', 'student', 'first-semester undergraduate', 'IJMEST_Cook_2016_Incoming', 'Samuel A. Cook & Timothy Fukawa-Connelly', '2016', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '176, 177, 180, 181', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Cook_2016_Incoming.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40005, 'When using visual or concrete grouping models to solve fraction division problems, students may misinterpret the remainder by identifying it as a fraction of the original unit rather than as a fraction of the divisor.', 'When dividing 10 yards of fabric by 3/4 of a yard to make pillowcases, a student counts 13 pillowcases and has 1/4 of a yard of fabric left over, incorrectly concluding the final answer is 13 1/4 instead of 13 1/3.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'remainders', 'in_service', 'highest grades of elementary school', 'IJMEST_Klemer_2018_Missing', 'Anat Klemer, Shirley Rapoport & Hanna Lev-Zamir', '2018', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Klemer_2018_Missing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40006, 'Students incorrectly determine the conditional probability of a two-child family having a girl given they have a boy is 1/2, either by assuming the other child must be a boy or a girl equally, or by using a flawed three-outcome sample space.', 'Asserting that there are three outcomes in the sample space—two girls, two boys, one of each sex—all with equal probability, and since ''two girls'' is eliminated, the probability of one of each sex is 1/2.', 'probability', 'conditional probability', 'sample space', 'other', '', 'IJMEST_Reinhardt_1979_Using', 'Howard E. Reinhardt & Don O. Loftsgaarden', '1979', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '244', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Reinhardt_1979_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40007, 'Students incorrectly believe that observing an expected outcome in scenarios like the prisoner''s dilemma changes the conditional probability to 1/2, failing to account for the agent''s strategy.', 'When a dealer exposes a king of diamonds from a hand of two cards, students think the probability that the dealer''s other card is the queen of spades becomes 1/2 instead of 2/3.', 'probability', 'conditional probability', 'prisoner''s dilemma', 'other', '', 'IJMEST_Reinhardt_1979_Using', 'Howard E. Reinhardt & Don O. Loftsgaarden', '1979', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '245', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Reinhardt_1979_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40008, 'Students attempt to solve ill-posed probability problems without realizing that essential information about decision procedures or strategies is missing.', 'Attempting to calculate the probability of a family having a girl given the information that the child playing in the yard is a boy, without knowing the mother''s decision procedure for which child plays in the yard.', 'probability', 'probability modeling', 'ill-posed problems', 'other', '', 'IJMEST_Reinhardt_1979_Using', 'Howard E. Reinhardt & Don O. Loftsgaarden', '1979', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'secondary', '245', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Reinhardt_1979_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40009, 'Students inappropriately over-generalize and impose proportional or linear models, or take simple averages of rates, without reflecting on whether the mathematical model fits the real-world situation.', 'If all edge lengths are doubled, so is the weight: 4.8 + 4.8 = 9.6', 'proportional', 'mathematical modelling', 'implemented anticipation', 'student', 'grades 10–12', 'IJMEST_Jankvist_2019_Upper', 'Uffe Thomas Jankvist & Mogens Niss', '2019', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Jankvist_2019_Upper.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40010, 'Students mistakenly mathematise the quantity or value of a two-dimensional object using its one-dimensional diameter or circumference rather than its area.', 'A pizza costs 10 kroner per diameter. If you choose the one at 40, it is true that you pay 10 kroner more than for the one at 30 kroner. But you also get 10 diameter more pizza', 'measurement', 'area vs. perimeter', 'circle dimensions', 'student', 'grades 10–12', 'IJMEST_Jankvist_2019_Upper', 'Uffe Thomas Jankvist & Mogens Niss', '2019', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Jankvist_2019_Upper.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40011, 'Students mathematically model a three-dimensional solid based on one- or two-dimensional conceptions, focusing incorrectly on the scaling of edges or faces rather than the scaling of volume.', 'Grams per side: 0.4. Since 4 cm is twice as big as the other one, the weight will be twice as big and that is in accordance with grams per side 0.8 g.', 'geometric', 'volume and scaling', 'three-dimensional solids', 'student', 'grades 10–12', 'IJMEST_Jankvist_2019_Upper', 'Uffe Thomas Jankvist & Mogens Niss', '2019', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Jankvist_2019_Upper.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40012, 'Students allow off-target everyday knowledge or reality to interfere with their interpretation and pre-mathematisation of a task, causing them to reject idealized task conditions.', 'Clearly, we take the one of diameter 40 cm! That''s the largest! But only if we are several people. I myself wouldn''t be able to devour a large pizza, so I would end up throwing some of it away', 'other', 'mathematical modelling', 'pre-mathematisation', 'student', 'grades 10–12', 'IJMEST_Jankvist_2019_Upper', 'Uffe Thomas Jankvist & Mogens Niss', '2019', 'International Journal of Mathematical Education in Science and Technology', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJMEST/IJMEST_Jankvist_2019_Upper.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40013, 'Both students and prospective teachers intuitively interpret a part-to-part ratio of 1:2 as representing a ''one-half'' part-to-whole relationship, rather than recognizing it as one-third of the whole.', 'When viewing a pie graph for a 1 male to 2 female ratio in Gar''s pond, students and teachers initially identify it as representing ''one half'' before realizing the males make up 1/3 of the total.', 'proportional', 'ratios', 'part-to-part vs. part-to-whole relationships', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Lee_2005_Facilitating', 'Hollylynne Stohl Lee', '2005', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '231, 238, 239, 242', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Lee_2005_Facilitating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40014, 'Prospective teachers fail to attend to the overarching constraints of a problem when listing possible equivalent ratios, generating solutions that are mathematically proportional but contextually impossible.', 'A prospective teacher lists 7:14 and 8:16 as viable equivalent ratios for 1:2, ignoring the problem''s constraint that there are only 13 male fish available.', 'proportional', 'equivalent ratios', 'problem constraints', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Lee_2005_Facilitating', 'Hollylynne Stohl Lee', '2005', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '233, 234, 235', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Lee_2005_Facilitating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40015, 'Students experience cognitive conflict and struggle with the algebraic notation of translating a function horizontally, specifically exhibiting confusion over whether to add or subtract a negative parameter.', 'Some students struggled with the conflict between adding or subtracting a negative h.', 'algebraic', 'transformations', 'horizontal translations', 'in_service', 'Year 10 / secondary', 'JMTE_Pierce_2013_Teaching', 'Robyn Pierce, Kaye Stacey', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'passing', '336, 337', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Pierce_2013_Teaching.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40016, 'Students attempt to find numerical links between source and image coordinates by guessing spurious arithmetic relationships (like multiplication) rather than recognizing the underlying additive translation, confusing different axes in the process.', 'When asked to find the link between turning points (3, 2) and (-2, -4) and translations of -5 and -6, a student incorrectly conjectures ''If you times them together, you get -6'', multiplying the wrong coordinates.', 'algebraic', 'transformations', 'translations', 'in_service', 'Year 10 / secondary', 'JMTE_Pierce_2013_Teaching', 'Robyn Pierce, Kaye Stacey', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'passing', '339', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Pierce_2013_Teaching.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40017, 'Prospective teachers misinterpret a child''s valid, non-standard informal reasoning for dividing fractions as incorrect because it deviates from the standard algorithm (multiplying by the inverse).', 'A child calculates 6 1/3 divided by 2/3 and correctly reasons through the number of thirds. Christina and other student teachers considered the child''s response as wrong because it was far beyond her mathematical experiences. For her, division of fractions was an algorithmic process.', 'fraction', 'division of fractions', 'informal strategies vs. standard algorithms', 'mixed', 'primary', 'JMTE_Potari_2009_Primary', 'Despina Potari, Barbara Georgiadou-Kabouridis', '2009', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '14', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Potari_2009_Primary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40018, 'A preschool child struggles to accurately count a set of objects, skipping numbers in the counting sequence or reciting odd numbers when asked for the total quantity.', 'When asked to count 6 cubes, a child says ''1, 2, 3, 6, 7, 8''. When asked ''How many cubes are in the box?'', the child points to the objects one by one and says ''3, 5, 7''.', 'whole_number', 'counting', 'one-to-one correspondence', 'mixed', 'primary', 'JMTE_Potari_2009_Primary', 'Despina Potari, Barbara Georgiadou-Kabouridis', '2009', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '15', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Potari_2009_Primary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40019, 'Children indiscriminately add all numbers presented in a word problem without reading or comprehending the mathematical relationship described by the text.', 'Given the problem ''A window is 4 m long and a door is 2 m long. Which one is longer and how much longer is it?'', most children added the two numbers to get the result, although the student teacher read the problem to them many times.', 'whole_number', 'word problems', 'operation sense', 'mixed', 'primary', 'JMTE_Potari_2009_Primary', 'Despina Potari, Barbara Georgiadou-Kabouridis', '2009', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '17', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Potari_2009_Primary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40020, 'Children misunderstand the concept of partitive division, confusing the divisor with the number of items in each group.', 'They did not understand that 10 divided by 5 means to share equally into five parts. They thought that the parts were two.', 'whole_number', 'division', 'equal sharing', 'mixed', 'primary', 'JMTE_Potari_2009_Primary', 'Despina Potari, Barbara Georgiadou-Kabouridis', '2009', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '18', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Potari_2009_Primary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40021, 'Children are able to solve standard arithmetic equations but cannot interpret or solve non-standard equations where the unknown or the operation is on the left side of the equals sign.', 'Children could not reply to the following sentences: ? = 1 + 5 and 6 = 1 + ? although they could solve the sentences 1 + ? = 6 and 1 + 5 = ?.', 'algebraic', 'equations', 'equality', 'mixed', 'primary', 'JMTE_Potari_2009_Primary', 'Despina Potari, Barbara Georgiadou-Kabouridis', '2009', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '19, 20', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Potari_2009_Primary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40022, 'Students bring only a recursive interpretation to a number pattern, observing that the next number increases by a fixed amount from the last, but they fail to link the pattern or the term ''multiple'' to the operation of multiplication.', 'A student correctly identifies the pattern for ''0 3 6 9 12'' as ''goes up by three'', but when solving problems individually, they rely on adding and do not relate the skip counting to multiplication facts.', 'whole_number', 'multiples', 'skip counting vs. multiplication', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_McDuffie_2004_Mathematics', 'Amy Roth McDuffie', '2004', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '45, 46', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_McDuffie_2004_Mathematics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40023, 'A pre-service teacher demonstrates underdeveloped pedagogical content knowledge regarding decimal grid representations, confusing the grids (e.g., stating 100 times 100 is 1000) and incorrectly accepting a student''s flawed representation because she is confused about defining the unit whole.', 'When one student shades 0.075 on a ten-thousandths grid to represent 0.75, and another correctly shades 0.7500, the teacher incorrectly states both are correct due to a vague recollection from a methods course about changing the unit ''one''.', 'decimal', 'representing decimals', 'base-ten grids and unit definition', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_McDuffie_2004_Mathematics', 'Amy Roth McDuffie', '2004', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '50, 51, 52, 53', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_McDuffie_2004_Mathematics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40024, 'A student accurately shades the equivalent of one-tenth on a hundredths grid but mislabels it with the decimal notation for one-hundredth.', 'A student colored in one tenth on the hundredths grid by coloring a row of ten of the one hundredths squares, and then incorrectly labeled it as ''0.01''.', 'decimal', 'decimal notation', 'equivalency', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_McDuffie_2004_Mathematics', 'Amy Roth McDuffie', '2004', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '50', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_McDuffie_2004_Mathematics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40025, 'Teachers may be unclear about the referent unit for a fraction when using drawn models like number lines to represent operations, incorrectly taking the fraction of the wrong quantity.', 'For a problem involving taking 1/8 of a meter from 4/5 of a meter, a teacher might select a diagram representing 4/5 minus 1/8 of 4/5, mistakenly taking the referent unit for 1/8 to be 4/5.', 'fraction', 'fraction operations', 'referent units and drawn models', 'in_service', 'middle-grade', 'JMTE_Jacobson_2015_Knowledge', 'Erik Jacobson, Andrew Izsák', '2015', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'passing', '470, 471', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Jacobson_2015_Knowledge.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40026, 'A student incorrectly adds fractions with unlike denominators by adding the numerators together and the denominators together.', '3/4 + 4/5 = 7/9', 'fraction', 'fraction addition', 'unlike denominators', 'in_service', 'middle school', 'JMTE_An_2004_Pedagogical', 'Shuhua An, Gerald Kulm and Zhonghe Wu', '2004', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '152, 157, 158', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_An_2004_Pedagogical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40027, 'Students order fractions incorrectly by relying on flawed logic, such as looking only at the size of the denominator.', 'Latoya ordering fractions as 1/4, 2/3, 3/8 and Robert ordering as 2/3, 1/4, 3/8.', 'fraction', 'comparing fractions', 'ordering fractions', 'in_service', 'middle school', 'JMTE_An_2004_Pedagogical', 'Shuhua An, Gerald Kulm and Zhonghe Wu', '2004', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '152, 161, 162', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_An_2004_Pedagogical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40028, 'Students fail to conceive of a fraction as a single number, instead separating it into two independent numbers (numerator and denominator).', 'A student separating numerator and denominator into independent parts, failing to see the connection between whole numbers and fractions.', 'fraction', 'concept of fraction', 'fraction as a number', 'in_service', 'middle school', 'JMTE_An_2004_Pedagogical', 'Shuhua An, Gerald Kulm and Zhonghe Wu', '2004', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '157, 160', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_An_2004_Pedagogical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40029, 'Students use incorrect additive or ratio strategies when solving a proportion word problem.', 'Setting up 3/5 = x/40 to find the number of boys, leading to an incorrect calculation of 16 boys, because they used the part-to-part ratio rather than part-to-whole.', 'proportional', 'ratio and proportion', 'part-to-part vs part-to-whole', 'in_service', 'middle school', 'JMTE_An_2004_Pedagogical', 'Shuhua An, Gerald Kulm and Zhonghe Wu', '2004', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '152, 166, 167', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_An_2004_Pedagogical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40030, 'A student correctly applies algebraic procedures to square an absolute value equation and distinguish cases, but fails to check whether the resulting solution satisfies the initial condition for that case.', 'The student finds x = 1/2 under Case 2 where x(x-1) ≥ 0, but fails to observe that 1/2 does not satisfy this inequality.', 'algebraic', 'absolute value equations', 'extraneous solutions', 'in_service', 'Year 10 secondary', 'JMTE_Biza_2007_Using', 'Irene Biza, Elena Nardi, Theodossios Zachariades', '2007', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '303, 304', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Biza_2007_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40031, 'Teachers incorrectly diagnose the source of the student''s error, mistakenly believing that the act of squaring both sides of the equation introduced extraneous solutions, failing to realize this does not apply to equations equal to zero.', '"Raising to the power of 2, we increase the number of solutions, we thus introduce new solutions."', 'algebraic', 'absolute value equations', 'squaring', 'in_service', 'Year 10 secondary', 'JMTE_Biza_2007_Using', 'Irene Biza, Elena Nardi, Theodossios Zachariades', '2007', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '306', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Biza_2007_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40032, 'Teachers attempt to highlight the student''s mathematical flaw by providing mathematically flawed or inappropriate counter-examples due to their own misdiagnosis of the core error.', 'Asking the student whether the equation x = 1 is equivalent to x^2 = 1, or asking "would I be allowed to square |x| + 1 = 0?".', 'algebraic', 'absolute value equations', 'counter-examples', 'in_service', 'Year 10 secondary', 'JMTE_Biza_2007_Using', 'Irene Biza, Elena Nardi, Theodossios Zachariades', '2007', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '306, 307', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Biza_2007_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40033, 'Teachers propose unnecessarily convoluted procedural methods to verify if a solution is acceptable, rather than utilizing direct and simpler methods.', 'Proposing to solve the inequality x(x-1) ≥ 0 and graph the intervals instead of simply substituting x with 1/2 in the inequality or initial equation.', 'algebraic', 'absolute value equations', 'checking solutions', 'in_service', 'Year 10 secondary', 'JMTE_Biza_2007_Using', 'Irene Biza, Elena Nardi, Theodossios Zachariades', '2007', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '306, 307', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Biza_2007_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40034, 'Teachers misinterpret a linear representation of a fraction (a number line with the upper half of the segment shaded) as an area model, erroneously identifying the fraction as 1/8 instead of 1/4 because they evaluate the shaded portion relative to the total area of the diagram rather than its linear distance.', 'A teacher comments: "That struck me, ''that''s one-eighth'' the minute I saw it. It didn''t strike me as a quarter."', 'fraction', 'fraction representations', 'linear vs. area models', 'in_service', 'primary', 'JMTE_Delaney_2008_Mathematical', 'Seán Delaney, Deborah Loewenberg Ball, Heather C. Hill, Stephen G. Schilling, Deborah Zopf', '2008', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '187, 188', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Delaney_2008_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40035, 'Students make procedural errors when adding three two-digit numbers, such as failing to heed place value and failing to note a carry of 2 tens rather than 1 ten.', '', 'whole_number', 'multi-digit addition', 'regrouping and place value', 'in_service', 'primary', 'JMTE_Delaney_2008_Mathematical', 'Seán Delaney, Deborah Loewenberg Ball, Heather C. Hill, Stephen G. Schilling, Deborah Zopf', '2008', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '188', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Delaney_2008_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40036, 'A student incorrectly mathematizes ''removing one-third'' by subtracting the numerical value 1/3 instead of subtracting one-third of the unknown quantity.', 'The first man ate 1/3 which is how come I wrote x - 1/3, which is less than x because of the take away.', 'algebraic', 'algebraic expressions', 'translating word problems', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Chick_2018_Teaching', 'Helen Chick, Kim Beswick', '2018', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '485, 489', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Chick_2018_Teaching.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40037, 'Prospective teachers struggle to fight the pedagogical tendency to ''tell'' students the answer or procedure, opting to direct the student rather than starting from the student''s thinking.', 'In other words I would get him to work from the end of the question back to the start.', 'other', 'pedagogical strategies', 'questioning and telling', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Chick_2018_Teaching', 'Helen Chick, Kim Beswick', '2018', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '486, 488', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Chick_2018_Teaching.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40038, 'A student re-uses the original variable ''x'' to represent a new, modified quantity in subsequent steps of a problem.', 'so then the second man comes along… and he eats 1/3 of the apples. Is that 1/3 * X again?', 'algebraic', 'variables', 'changing quantities', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Chick_2018_Teaching', 'Helen Chick, Kim Beswick', '2018', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '487', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Chick_2018_Teaching.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40039, 'Prospective teachers demonstrate weaknesses in their own algebraic skills, failing to recognize when a student''s expression like ''x - 1/3'' is mathematically incorrect for the context, or struggling to formulate correct algebraic equations themselves.', 'You have represented this as x - 1/3 which is correct. But by not isolating this answer as such, you have said that the second man has also eaten 1/3 of the original amount of apples.', 'algebraic', 'algebraic expressions', 'evaluating student work', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Chick_2018_Teaching', 'Helen Chick, Kim Beswick', '2018', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '489, 490, 491', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Chick_2018_Teaching.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40040, 'Teachers hold a restricted concept image of functions, assuming they must be described by a regular algebraic equation or have continuous graphs, and struggle to apply the criterion of arbitrariness to non-numeric or discrete relationships.', 'When creating a function mapping students to their test scores, a teacher notes: ''I couldn''t really come up with an equation for it, so I didn''t.'' The instructor then clarifies, ''Not everything has to be defined by an equation.''', 'algebraic', 'function', 'definition / arbitrariness', 'mixed', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Steele_2013_Developing', 'Michael D. Steele, Amy F. Hillen, Margaret S. Smith', '2013', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '454, 464', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Steele_2013_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40041, 'Teachers struggle to graphically represent a sudden discrete change in a continuous time context (such as a sudden haircut) without violating the definition of a function, debating how to handle the discontinuous jump to maintain a unique output for the input.', 'When graphing a haircut occurring at a specific month, a teacher argues against closed circles at both heights: ''You would have two different lengths of hair and every second month… there would be two different lengths of hair in that one month. So for that input there''d be two outputs rather than only one.''', 'algebraic', 'function', 'graphical representation / discontinuity', 'mixed', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Steele_2013_Developing', 'Michael D. Steele, Amy F. Hillen, Margaret S. Smith', '2013', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'productive', 'primary', '466, 467', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Steele_2013_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40042, 'Pre-service teachers erroneously apply a linear, one-dimensional model to a two-dimensional area problem, calculating the number of items that can be cut from a larger area by dividing corresponding lengths and widths, but failing to multiply the resulting quotients.', 'These students started by calculating [L/l] and [W/w], but instead of multiplying the numbers, each of which was 10, they concluded that it was possible to get 10 cards.', 'measurement', 'area', 'mathematical modeling / dimensional analysis', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Peled_2007_Role', 'Irit Peled', '2007', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '373, 374, 377', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Peled_2007_Role.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40043, 'Students inappropriately apply a total area division model to a discrete cutting problem, finding the quotient of the total areas without considering the specific dimensional constraints and orientations of the rectangular shapes.', 'Some of the students applied the total area division model, i.e. found the integer part resulting from the division of the cardboard area, S, by the greeting card area, s (85x65 = 5525; 8x6 = 48; 5525/48 = 115).', 'measurement', 'area', 'discrete vs. continuous area / packing', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Peled_2007_Role', 'Irit Peled', '2007', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '372, 373, 377', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Peled_2007_Role.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40044, 'Children make off-by-one errors when using a counting-on procedure for addition because they struggle to simultaneously track the sequence of movement and the sequence that controls the stopping point.', 'common errors such as yielding an answer which is removed by 1', 'whole_number', 'addition', 'counting-on', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Peled_2007_Role', 'Irit Peled', '2007', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'passing', '370, 371', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Peled_2007_Role.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40045, 'Students view the equal sign operationally as a ''do something signal'' or an announcement of the result of an arithmetic operation, rather than relationally as a symbol of mathematical equivalence.', 'When presented with the equation 37 + 54 = [] + 55, a student states ''91 goes in the box, because 37 plus 54 is 91.'' Or when asked to solve 8 + 4 = [] + 5, students often answer 12, 17, or 12 and 17.', 'algebraic', 'equivalence', 'meaning of the equal sign', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Stephens_2006_Equivalence', 'ANA C. STEPHENS', '2006', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '251, 252, 268, 269, 270', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Stephens_2006_Equivalence.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40046, 'Preservice elementary teachers lack awareness of students'' operational misconceptions regarding the equal sign, incorrectly attributing students'' errors on equivalence tasks to simple oversights rather than a flawed underlying concept.', 'When examining a student''s incorrect response to 16 + 15 - 9 = 31 - 9 (''False, because if you minus 9 it won''t still equal 31''), most preservice teachers assumed the student simply ''didn''t see the minus 9'' or made a ''visual overlook'' rather than recognizing it as an equal sign misconception.', 'algebraic', 'student thinking', 'evaluating errors', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Stephens_2006_Equivalence', 'ANA C. STEPHENS', '2006', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '268, 269, 272, 273', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Stephens_2006_Equivalence.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40047, 'Preservice teachers conflate the definitions of factor and multiple, often treating them as interchangeable, and rely on rote procedures (like finding the largest or smallest number that ''goes into'' another) rather than making sense of the mathematical definitions.', 'For me I feel they [the words ''factor'' and ''multiple''] are interchangeable... Multiples are numbers that multiply together to give the number and a factor is a number that goes into the number.', 'whole_number', 'factors and multiples', 'GCF and LCM', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Seaman_2007_Mathematical', 'Carol E. Seaman, Jennifer Earles Szydlik', '2007', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '176', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Seaman_2007_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40048, 'Students struggle with the standard algorithm for dividing decimals, either reversing the divisor and dividend, or incorrectly readjusting the decimal point after division by moving it back to its original erroneous place.', 'After time at the site two students ''moved the decimals'' and then, in the end, moved them back, getting 0.08 as their (incorrect) answer [for 0.4 divided by 0.05].', 'decimal', 'division', 'standard algorithm', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Seaman_2007_Mathematical', 'Carol E. Seaman, Jennifer Earles Szydlik', '2007', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '176, 177', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Seaman_2007_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40049, 'When faced with a word problem containing fractions, students bypass making sense of the context and instead react to isolated key words or the mere presence of fractions to trigger inappropriate procedures like finding common denominators or subtracting.', 'When given a problem where someone ''gives 1/3 of the bag away'', a student immediately states ''First, I''d attempt to get a least common denominator...'' and a majority attempted subtraction because the M&Ms were ''given away.''', 'fraction', 'word problems', 'multiplication vs subtraction', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Seaman_2007_Mathematical', 'Carol E. Seaman, Jennifer Earles Szydlik', '2007', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '177', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Seaman_2007_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40050, 'Students view the equal sign as an operational command to compute an answer rather than a relational symbol indicating balance or equality between two sides of an equation.', '...most of them think the equal sign means ''works out to be'', or ''I get this''... It''s a command that gives you an answer after you have done certain things... You push the equal button and out comes this answer.', 'algebraic', 'equations', 'meaning of the equal sign', 'in_service', 'Year 9 (secondary)', 'JMTE_Walshaw_2010_Mathematics', 'Margaret Walshaw', '2010', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '492, 493', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Walshaw_2010_Mathematics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40051, 'Teachers incorrectly believe that zero is neither even nor odd, reasoning that zero represents ''nothing'' or the ''empty set'' rather than a valid quantity with parity.', 'A teacher claimed that ''0 is nothing so it''s neither even nor odd'', while another stated ''0 is the empty set so it''s neither even nor odd.''', 'whole_number', 'parity', 'parity of zero', 'in_service', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Levenson_2010_Mathematically', 'Esther Levenson, Pessia Tsamir, Dina Tirosh', '2010', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'passing', '356, 357', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Levenson_2010_Mathematically.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40052, 'Teachers conflate the operations and meanings of fractions with those of ratios, misinterpreting the scaling up of a ratio (which increases total quantity) as finding an equivalent fraction (which subdivides a constant whole into smaller parts).', 'Explaining the scaling of the ratio of candies in a jar by stating ''you are really multiplying by 1... the whole is the same but the parts are smaller,'' incorrectly applying fraction subdivision logic to a context where the discrete quantity of candies is actually increasing.', 'ratio', 'equivalent ratios', 'fractions vs. ratios', 'in_service', 'middle grades (grades 6–8)', 'JMTE_Silver_2007_Where', 'Edward A. Silver, Lawrence M. Clark, Hala N. Ghousseini, Charalambos Y. Charalambous, Jenny T. Sealy', '2007', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '268, 269', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Silver_2007_Where.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40053, 'Students inappropriately apply an additive rationale rather than a multiplicative one when solving ratio and proportion problems (often called the addition fallacy).', 'Solving a ratio problem by adding the same amount to both quantities instead of multiplying them by a constant scale factor.', 'ratio', 'proportional reasoning', 'additive vs multiplicative thinking', 'in_service', 'middle grades (grades 6–8)', 'JMTE_Silver_2007_Where', 'Edward A. Silver, Lawrence M. Clark, Hala N. Ghousseini, Charalambos Y. Charalambous, Jenny T. Sealy', '2007', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '270, 271', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Silver_2007_Where.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40054, 'Students and teachers struggle with solving linear inequalities when the variable has a negative coefficient, frequently forgetting to ''flip'' the inequality sign. They also exhibit inflexibility by rigidly moving the variable to the left side, regardless of the equation''s structure.', 'When solving 3 - 5x > 10, a student might divide by -5 but forget to switch the inequality sign, leading to an incorrect solution set (as illustrated in the paper''s Table 1 where S2P2 leads to 1 < x in one step but x < 1 in the erroneous step).', 'algebraic', 'linear inequalities', 'dividing by a negative coefficient', 'in_service', 'middle school and high school', 'JMTE_Yakes_2011_Using', 'Christopher Yakes, Jon R. Star', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '181, 182', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Yakes_2011_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40055, 'Students over-rely on cross-multiplication to solve proportions without understanding why it is mathematically valid, utilizing it as a rote procedure.', 'A teacher noted that ''cross-multiplying is drill and kill though—you almost don''t have to think.''', 'proportional', 'proportions', 'cross-multiplication', 'in_service', 'middle school and high school', 'JMTE_Yakes_2011_Using', 'Christopher Yakes, Jon R. Star', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '182, 183', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Yakes_2011_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40056, 'Students can mechanically use the slope-intercept form to find the equation of a line but lack understanding of what the y-intercept parameter ''b'' represents in the context of the problem.', 'A teacher pointed out that students often wonder, ''What does it really mean that I''m solving for b?''', 'algebraic', 'linear equations', 'slope-intercept form', 'in_service', 'middle school and high school', 'JMTE_Yakes_2011_Using', 'Christopher Yakes, Jon R. Star', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '183', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Yakes_2011_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40057, 'The pedagogical language of ''cancelling out'' or ''cross-cancelling'' common factors in algebraic fractions masks the underlying mathematical operations of factoring and division, leading some to incorrectly associate the action with obtaining zero.', 'During a discussion on simplifying fractions, a teacher remarked that ''cancelling out kind of "sounds like" you should get zero.''', 'algebraic', 'rational expressions', 'simplifying fractions', 'in_service', 'middle school and high school', 'JMTE_Yakes_2011_Using', 'Christopher Yakes, Jon R. Star', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '184', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Yakes_2011_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40058, 'Students attempting to find the Least Common Multiple (LCM) of variable expressions try to misapply the elementary method of generating arithmetic lists of multiples, which breaks down because generating an exhaustive list of varying multiples for algebraic expressions is nearly impossible.', 'Participants tried to write a list for the multiples of 4x^2 (e.g., 4x^2, 8x^2, 12x^2) but quickly realized they would also need to include higher powers of x (4x^3, 4x^4) to capture all multiples, making the listing approach unmanageable.', 'algebraic', 'least common multiple', 'variable expressions', 'in_service', 'middle school and high school', 'JMTE_Yakes_2011_Using', 'Christopher Yakes, Jon R. Star', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '184, 185', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Yakes_2011_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40059, 'Prospective teachers initially view composite shapes (pentominoes) holistically rather than analytically, which allows them to notice patterns but hinders their ability to mathematically explain those patterns using the properties of individual cells.', 'A student observes that symmetrical shapes have a remainder of zero, reasoning that they are ''balanced'' and ''cancel each other out'', but fails to initially explain this structurally using the numerical values of the cells on the hundreds chart.', 'whole_number', 'division and remainders', 'modular arithmetic', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Liljedahl_2007_Interweaving', 'Peter Liljedahl, Egan Chernoff, Rina Zazkis', '2007', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'mixed', 'primary', '242, 243, 244, 245, 246', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Liljedahl_2007_Interweaving.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40060, 'Students incorrectly construct a distance-time graph as a literal picture of the physical situation (e.g., the trajectory of a kicked soccer ball) rather than a mathematical representation of the relationship between distance and time.', 'one student''s ''distance-time graph'' was a picture of someone kicking a ball in the air and the path of the ball as it returned to the ground, rather than a graph of points relating the distance of the ball above the ground to the time that had lapsed', 'algebraic', 'functions and graphing', 'distance-time graphs', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Stockero_2013_Characterizing', 'Shari L. Stockero, Laura R. Van Zoest', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '134, 140', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Stockero_2013_Characterizing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40061, 'A teacher''s inadequate explanation of an asymptote—using only a few numerical examples to show that a function''s values ''keep getting bigger''—fails to highlight the critical formal features of asymptotes, risking the introduction or reinforcement of student misconceptions.', 'The teacher responded by saying, ''...So as I go out, is my square root getting bigger, or will it eventually level off? Like what''s the square root of a hundred?'' ... ''As I go, don''t I keep getting bigger? So I don''t think there will be an asymptote.''', 'algebraic', 'functions and graphing', 'asymptotes', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Stockero_2013_Characterizing', 'Shari L. Stockero, Laura R. Van Zoest', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '140, 141', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Stockero_2013_Characterizing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40062, 'Teachers and students incorrectly define direct and inverse proportional relationships using oversimplified additive rules—assuming direct proportion simply means both variables increase (''up/up'') and inverse proportion means one variable increases while the other decreases (''up/down'')—ignoring the strictly multiplicative nature of the relationship.', 'When a student asks what kind of variation is present, the teacher prompts, ''As one increases, does the other increase or does it go the other way?''', 'proportional', 'proportional relationships', 'direct vs. inverse variation', 'in_service', 'middle school', 'JMTE_Orrill_2015_Tracing', 'Chandra Hawley Orrill, Julie M. Kittleson', '2015', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '290, 291', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Orrill_2015_Tracing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40063, 'Students struggle with the decimal principle when units exceed ten, often incorrectly concatenating digits, juxtaposing them, or dropping digits when attempting to combine quantities.', 'For a sum yielding 3 thousands, 12 hundreds, 1 ten, and 5 ones, students might answer 31215 (juxtaposing), 3215 (eliminating the tens digit of the 12), or 3315 (adding the two digits of 12).', 'whole_number', 'place value', 'unit conversion', 'in_service', 'third grade (8-/9-year-old students)', 'JMTE_Tempier_2016_New', 'Frédérick Tempier', '2016', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '266, 267', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Tempier_2016_New.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40064, 'Teachers overly rely on manipulatives to explain place value, treating ''Number Units'' (like hundreds) merely as physical groupings (like bags) rather than written mathematical equivalences, which hinders students from generalizing and decontextualizing the decimal principle.', 'When a student correctly notes that 12 hundreds is equal to one thousand and two hundreds, the teacher reformulates this purely in terms of manipulatives (''put ten bags in a box'') and fails to write the mathematical conversion on the board (12 hundreds = 1 thousand 2 hundreds).', 'whole_number', 'place value', 'manipulatives vs. written number units', 'in_service', 'third grade (8-/9-year-old students)', 'JMTE_Tempier_2016_New', 'Frédérick Tempier', '2016', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '268, 269', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Tempier_2016_New.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40065, 'When initially learning numbers below one hundred, students treat ''ten'' merely as a position (the tens digit) without making the connection to the corresponding quantity, failing to grasp the concept of a unit.', 'Students do not make the connection between the second digit of the numbers and the corresponding quantity. Ten refers only to a position.', 'whole_number', 'place value', 'concept of unit', 'in_service', 'third grade (8-/9-year-old students)', 'JMTE_Tempier_2016_New', 'Frédérick Tempier', '2016', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '261, 262', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Tempier_2016_New.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40066, 'Trainee teachers misinterpret index notation, commonly multiplying the base by the exponent rather than multiplying the base by itself the indicated number of times.', 'The most common response from the Australian students as the value of 5^5 was 25, suggesting that they were unaware of the meaning of index notation.', 'whole_number', 'exponents', 'index notation', 'mixed', 'primary', 'JMTE_Norton_2018_Primary', 'Stephen Norton, Qinqiong Zhang', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '276', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Norton_2018_Primary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40067, 'Trainee teachers struggle with decimal multiplication and place value, either adding the decimals instead of multiplying them or incorrectly calculating the place value of the product.', 'While some Australian trainee teachers added the decimals to get 0.5, the majority responded with 0.6 mm or 0.6 mm2 suggesting challenges with both the concept of multiplication of decimals and place value.', 'decimal', 'decimal multiplication', 'place value', 'mixed', 'primary', 'JMTE_Norton_2018_Primary', 'Stephen Norton, Qinqiong Zhang', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '276', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Norton_2018_Primary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40068, 'Trainee teachers attempt to solve equations with variables on both sides by inappropriately applying a ''backtracking'' or reversal of operations method, which is only effective when the variable is on one side of the equation.', 'The majority of the trainee teachers tried to ''backtrack'', a reversal of operations method for solving for an unknown in linear equations where the unknown variable is only on one side of the equal sign.', 'algebraic', 'linear equations', 'variables on both sides', 'mixed', 'primary', 'JMTE_Norton_2018_Primary', 'Stephen Norton, Qinqiong Zhang', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '276, 277', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Norton_2018_Primary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40069, 'Prospective teachers incorrectly believe that exponent properties, such as the multiplication and power properties, apply to all numbers without restrictions, failing to recognize conditions like nonzero bases or integer limitations.', 'Ten out of thirteen teachers stated that the multiplication and power properties of exponents work for all numbers, instead of realizing that these properties are limited (e.g., failing to note that 0^0 is undefined).', 'algebraic', 'exponential functions', 'exponent properties', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Davis_2009_Understanding', 'Jon D. Davis', '2009', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '366, 374, 376', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Davis_2009_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40070, 'Prospective teachers struggle to translate exponential data from a table into a recursive equation, often mixing components with closed-form equations, omitting starting values, or erroneously treating the exponential relationship as additive/linear.', 'Writing a recursive equation for an exponential relationship as x_0 = 642; x_n = x_{n-1} + 143.4, treating the pattern as an additive linear relationship instead of multiplicative.', 'algebraic', 'exponential functions', 'recursive equations', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Davis_2009_Understanding', 'Jon D. Davis', '2009', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '374, 375', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Davis_2009_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40071, 'Teachers anticipate that students will erroneously overgeneralize exponent properties, such as applying multiplication rules when bases are different or incorrectly multiplying the bases themselves.', 'Anticipating that students might multiply the bases as well as the exponents (e.g., thinking 3^4 * 2^2 = 6^6) or applying properties to non-matching bases.', 'algebraic', 'exponential functions', 'exponent properties', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Davis_2009_Understanding', 'Jon D. Davis', '2009', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '379, 380', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Davis_2009_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40072, 'When asked to reproduce a whole from a proper fraction, prospective teachers may conceptually conflate fractions as parts out of a whole rather than as relative sizes, leading them to incorrectly partition the given part or inappropriately multiply it.', 'Given a stick that is 4/5 of a whole, a PST partitioned the stick into five parts and appended one more, failing to recognize the stick as comprised of four 1/5s. Given a semicircle that is 5/6 of a piece, a PST multiplied 1/2 by 5/6 to get 5/12.', 'fraction', 'proper fractions', 'reproducing the whole (RPFS)', 'PST', 'pre-K-8', 'JMTE_Lovin_2018_Pre-k-', 'LouAnn H. Lovin, Alexis L. Stevens, John Siegfried, Jesse L. M. Wilkins, Anderson Norton', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '213, 214', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Lovin_2018_Pre-k-.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40073, 'Prospective teachers rely heavily on rote procedures like ''invert and multiply'' for fraction division rather than conceptually coordinating the three levels of fractional units required to make sense of the problem structurally.', 'To find how many 1/8 pizzas fit into a 3/4 pizza, a PST divided 3/4 by 1/8, finding the reciprocal to get 24/4 = 6, rather than understanding 3/4 as three groups of two 1/8s.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'coordinating three levels of units', 'PST', 'pre-K-8', 'JMTE_Lovin_2018_Pre-k-', 'LouAnn H. Lovin, Alexis L. Stevens, John Siegfried, Jesse L. M. Wilkins, Anderson Norton', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '215, 224, 225', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Lovin_2018_Pre-k-.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40074, 'Prospective teachers reflexively convert improper fractions into mixed numbers instead of treating them as valid numbers in their own right, which limits their ability to use an iterative fraction scheme for accurate measurement.', 'When asked to draw a whole candy bar from a bar that is 7/3 as long, a PST changed 7/3 to 2 1/3 and visually split the bar to find ''roughly twice the size,'' resulting in an inaccurate unit of measure instead of partitioning the bar into seven equal 1/3 pieces.', 'fraction', 'improper fractions', 'iterative fraction scheme', 'PST', 'pre-K-8', 'JMTE_Lovin_2018_Pre-k-', 'LouAnn H. Lovin, Alexis L. Stevens, John Siegfried, Jesse L. M. Wilkins, Anderson Norton', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '215, 226, 227', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Lovin_2018_Pre-k-.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40075, 'Prospective teachers over-generalize fraction algorithms, often adding or subtracting across numerators and denominators, or incorrectly maintaining common denominators during multiplication.', 'When multiplying two fractions with a common denominator, such as 2/15 x 7/15, a PST might incorrectly keep the common denominator to get 14/15.', 'fraction', 'fraction arithmetic', 'algorithms', 'PST', 'pre-K-8', 'JMTE_Lovin_2018_Pre-k-', 'LouAnn H. Lovin, Alexis L. Stevens, John Siegfried, Jesse L. M. Wilkins, Anderson Norton', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '207, 208', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Lovin_2018_Pre-k-.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40076, 'Students struggle to carry out a process when converting a fraction to a percentage if the denominator is not easily multiplied to reach 100, reaching an impasse in their planned procedure.', 'When trying to find the percentage for 23/48, a student states: ''I don''t know. First I thought I would try to get it [the 48 in the denominator] as close to 100 as possible so I multiplied it by 2.''', 'percent', 'fraction to percent conversion', 'denominators not factors of 100', 'mixed', 'middle school (6th- and 7th-grade)', 'JMTE_Warshauer_2015_Productive', 'Hiroko Kawaguchi Warshauer', '2015', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'productive', 'secondary', '384, 387, 388', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Warshauer_2015_Productive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40077, 'Students conflate percentage differences with absolute quantity differences, incorrectly assuming that a 10% difference in fullness equates directly to a 10-gallon difference regardless of the container''s total capacity.', 'A student correctly calculated that a rain barrel was 50% full and a jug was 60% full, but concluded that the 10% difference was equivalent to a 10-gallon difference, saying, ''Shade in 10 more squares... its like 80%.''', 'proportional', 'percentages and quantities', 'additive vs proportional difference', 'mixed', 'middle school (6th- and 7th-grade)', 'JMTE_Warshauer_2015_Productive', 'Hiroko Kawaguchi Warshauer', '2015', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '385', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Warshauer_2015_Productive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40078, 'Students struggle to translate a discount word problem into a correct algebraic expression, incorrectly setting up the subtraction in reverse order (e.g., discount minus total).', 'A student wrote the expression ''0.25S - S'' for the sale price. When asked to explain, she said, ''It means that you times it by the percent, which is 0.25, and then you have to subtract it and that''s what you have to pay.''', 'algebraic', 'algebraic expressions', 'order of subtraction', 'mixed', 'middle school (6th- and 7th-grade)', 'JMTE_Warshauer_2015_Productive', 'Hiroko Kawaguchi Warshauer', '2015', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '388', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Warshauer_2015_Productive.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40079, 'Teachers equate computational estimation strictly with the process of rounding numbers, often to the nearest multiple of ten.', 'Rounding 53.5 * 15 to 50 * 15 = 750, or stating that a number less than 5 is rounded to 0 and larger than 5 to 10.', 'whole_number', 'computational estimation', 'rounding', 'in_service', 'elementary and middle school', 'JMTE_Alajmi_2009_Addressing', 'Amal Hussain Alajmi', '2009', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '269, 270', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Alajmi_2009_Addressing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40080, 'Teachers view estimation as simply guessing, or as computing the exact answer first and then rounding the final result.', '"I estimate the final answer. After getting the exact answer, I round it... if I got an answer of 3.4, it will be 3."', 'decimal', 'computational estimation', 'meaning of estimation', 'in_service', 'elementary and middle school', 'JMTE_Alajmi_2009_Addressing', 'Amal Hussain Alajmi', '2009', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '270, 271', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Alajmi_2009_Addressing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40081, 'Teachers abandon estimation to calculate the exact answer using paper-and-pencil standard algorithms, believing mathematics requires exact answers.', '"We taught long division for one whole month and in this item you want me to estimate an answer? That''s impossible… I need to do the long division" for 6123 / 54.', 'whole_number', 'computational estimation', 'standard algorithms', 'in_service', 'elementary and middle school', 'JMTE_Alajmi_2009_Addressing', 'Amal Hussain Alajmi', '2009', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '274, 278', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Alajmi_2009_Addressing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40082, 'Teachers use inappropriate rounding that results in wildly unreasonable estimated answers.', 'Rounding 534 7/9 * 0.495 to 500 * 1 = 500, which more than doubles the answer since 0.495 is less than 0.5.', 'fraction', 'computational estimation', 'reasonableness', 'in_service', 'elementary and middle school', 'JMTE_Alajmi_2009_Addressing', 'Amal Hussain Alajmi', '2009', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '274', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Alajmi_2009_Addressing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40083, 'Teachers inappropriately distribute the denominator across multiplied numerators, fundamentally altering the operation.', 'Estimating (622 * 7)/19 by rounding to (600 * 7)/20, but then incorrectly calculating it as 600/20 * 7/20 = 30 * 1/3 = 10.', 'fraction', 'fraction operations', 'multiplication and division', 'in_service', 'elementary and middle school', 'JMTE_Alajmi_2009_Addressing', 'Amal Hussain Alajmi', '2009', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '273, 274', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Alajmi_2009_Addressing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40084, 'Prospective teachers rely heavily on part-whole reasoning, which prevents them from treating improper fractions as numbers in their own right. Instead, they view improper fractions as something that needs to be ''fixed'' by converting them to mixed numbers.', 'When asked to draw a whole candy bar given a bar that is 5/3 as long, a PST might convert 5/3 to 1 2/3 instead of treating 5/3 as five iterations of a 1/3 unit.', 'fraction', 'improper fractions', 'part-whole vs iterative thinking', 'PST', 'PreK-8', 'JMTE_Stevens_2018_Promoting', 'Alexis L. Stevens, Jesse L. M. Wilkins, LouAnn H. Lovin, John Siegfried, Anderson Norton, Richard Busi', '2018', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Stevens_2018_Promoting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40085, 'Prospective teachers struggle to clearly define the referent whole and confuse ''how many'' questions with ''how much'' questions when working with fractions.', 'When asked how much of a pizza each person would get if 4 pizzas were shared equally among 5 people, almost one-third of the PSTs responded that the answer was 4 pieces of pizza, answering a ''how many'' question (how many individual pieces of pizza) instead of a ''how much'' question (how much of one pizza).', 'fraction', 'meaning of fractions', 'referent whole', 'PST', 'PreK-8', 'JMTE_Stevens_2018_Promoting', 'Alexis L. Stevens, Jesse L. M. Wilkins, LouAnn H. Lovin, John Siegfried, Anderson Norton, Richard Busi', '2018', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Stevens_2018_Promoting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40086, 'An overreliance on halving strategies inhibits the development of more sophisticated partitioning schemes, making it difficult for learners to create fractions with odd denominators.', 'Overemphasis on halving can inhibit a student''s ability to develop partitioning schemes to create fractions that have odd denominators.', 'fraction', 'partitioning', 'halving vs odd denominators', 'PST', 'PreK-8', 'JMTE_Stevens_2018_Promoting', 'Alexis L. Stevens, Jesse L. M. Wilkins, LouAnn H. Lovin, John Siegfried, Anderson Norton, Richard Busi', '2018', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Stevens_2018_Promoting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40087, 'When asked to find the whole given a proper fractional part, prospective teachers mistakenly find the fractional amount of the given piece instead of using the given piece to construct the whole.', 'Given a stick that is 3/7 of a candy bar, a PST partitions the given stick into seven pieces and disembeds three of those pieces, finding 3/7 of the given stick instead of letting the given stick be 3/7 of the whole.', 'fraction', 'reversible partitive fraction scheme (RPFS)', 'reversing operations', 'PST', 'PreK-8', 'JMTE_Stevens_2018_Promoting', 'Alexis L. Stevens, Jesse L. M. Wilkins, LouAnn H. Lovin, John Siegfried, Anderson Norton, Richard Busi', '2018', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Stevens_2018_Promoting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40088, 'Teachers misclassify "Procedures with Connections" tasks as low-level because they focus on the presence of a stated or implied procedure, overlooking the opportunities for developing mathematical connections.', 'Classifying a task that uses a rectangular area model for mental multiplication as low-level because it provides a procedure to follow.', 'other', 'cognitive demands of mathematical tasks', 'procedures with connections', 'in_service', 'secondary (7-12th grade)', 'JMTE_Boston_2013_Connecting', 'Melissa D. Boston', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '20, 26, 28', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Boston_2013_Connecting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40089, 'Teachers overgeneralize that any task containing a real-world context or prompting for an explanation is automatically a high-level task, regardless of the actual cognitive demand.', 'Rating a contextual task that requires applying a well-rehearsed procedure and explaining the process as a high-level task.', 'other', 'cognitive demands of mathematical tasks', 'high-level tasks', 'in_service', 'secondary (7-12th grade)', 'JMTE_Boston_2013_Connecting', 'Melissa D. Boston', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '21, 26', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Boston_2013_Connecting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40090, 'Teachers evaluate the cognitive demand of a task based on its perceived difficulty for students or the presence of visual aids, assuming high-level tasks are simply "hard" and low-level tasks use manipulatives or diagrams.', 'Rating a long division with decimals task as high-level because "this is a difficult skill for my students," or stating a low-level task contains "visual aids."', 'other', 'cognitive demands of mathematical tasks', 'task difficulty and features', 'in_service', 'secondary (7-12th grade)', 'JMTE_Boston_2013_Connecting', 'Melissa D. Boston', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '22, 23, 26', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Boston_2013_Connecting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40091, 'Teachers struggle to conceptualize remainders in division problems, specifically conflating a fractional remainder with a whole-number remainder representing the same quantity.', 'One teacher, when asked by a student if 37 ÷ 5 could be expressed as either 7 remainder 2/5 or 7 remainder 2, was certain that both were correct.', 'whole_number', 'division', 'remainders', 'mixed', 'middle grades', 'JMTE_Sowder_1998_Educating', 'JUDITH SOWDER, BARBARA ARMSTRONG, SUSAN LAMON, MARTIN SIMON, LARRY SOWDER and ALBA THOMPSON', '1998', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '130', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Sowder_1998_Educating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40092, 'Preservice and practicing teachers inappropriately rely on keyword strategies or the misconception that multiplication always results in a larger number and division in a smaller number.', 'Non-conservers of operation often use an erroneous ''multiplication makes bigger, division makes smaller'' guiding principle.', 'decimal', 'word problems', 'operation sense', 'mixed', 'middle grades', 'JMTE_Sowder_1998_Educating', 'JUDITH SOWDER, BARBARA ARMSTRONG, SUSAN LAMON, MARTIN SIMON, LARRY SOWDER and ALBA THOMPSON', '1998', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '133', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Sowder_1998_Educating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40093, 'Prospective teachers use an additive difference strategy (subtracting height from base length) rather than a multiplicative ratio to determine the steepness of an incline.', 'Many explored the additive differences between the height and length of the base as a way of comparing slopes but rejected it because it sometimes led to a negative slope.', 'ratio', 'slope', 'additive vs multiplicative comparison', 'mixed', 'middle grades', 'JMTE_Sowder_1998_Educating', 'JUDITH SOWDER, BARBARA ARMSTRONG, SUSAN LAMON, MARTIN SIMON, LARRY SOWDER and ALBA THOMPSON', '1998', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '140', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Sowder_1998_Educating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40094, 'Teachers over-rely on superficial number patterns to solve perceived proportional situations without analyzing the contextual constraints, leading to absurd conclusions.', 'If one football player weighs 225 pounds, then three players should weigh 675 pounds. ... We see here an example of an over-reliance on patterns to explain everything.', 'proportional', 'proportional reasoning', 'contextual sense-making', 'mixed', 'middle grades', 'JMTE_Sowder_1998_Educating', 'JUDITH SOWDER, BARBARA ARMSTRONG, SUSAN LAMON, MARTIN SIMON, LARRY SOWDER and ALBA THOMPSON', '1998', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '142, 143', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Sowder_1998_Educating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40095, 'Teachers struggle to identify or flexibly shift the reference unit when interpreting complex fraction situations or multiplying/dividing fractions.', 'T: I just changed it all to thirds again. I saw there''s 3/3 and 2/3. PT: Three thirds of? T: Of the whole thing. ... T: The part that''s shaded is 3/5 of the whole thing. But I also knew that it was 3/3. PT: Three thirds of?', 'fraction', 'multiplication of fractions', 'shifting reference unit', 'mixed', 'middle grades', 'JMTE_Sowder_1998_Educating', 'JUDITH SOWDER, BARBARA ARMSTRONG, SUSAN LAMON, MARTIN SIMON, LARRY SOWDER and ALBA THOMPSON', '1998', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '148', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Sowder_1998_Educating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40096, 'Teachers may ''over-hear'' student explanations, erroneously attributing sophisticated generalizations and complete logical arguments to students who have only provided superficial or partial statements, often because the teacher projects their own understanding of the correct solution onto the student.', 'When a student simply states ''Because the number 15 is odd and the number 4 is even, then it''s impossible'', the teacher ''quotes'' the student as having provided a complete deduction: ''...When you have an odd number, and you take off an even [number] you will be left with an odd number, and you would not be able to divide it by 2.''', 'whole_number', 'problem solving', 'interpreting student thinking', 'in_service', 'fourth grade', 'JMTE_Wallach_2005_Hearing', 'TALI WALLACH and RUHAMA EVEN', '2005', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '402, 403, 413, 414', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Wallach_2005_Hearing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40097, 'Teachers fail to recognize or attribute valid mathematical reasoning to students'' correct solutions (''under-hearing'') or completely ignore alternative student strategies (''non-hearing'') when the students'' methods deviate from the teacher''s own anticipated solution path.', 'When students successfully reach a solution by physically building up two groups (7 and 3), the teacher, who expected a strategy of subtracting from the whole, under-hears the reasoning, claiming ''She just said 10 off the top of her head'' and ''It just came out of the blue.'' Furthermore, she completely ignored (non-hearing) a student''s valid suggestion to increase the number to 16, as it conflicted with her strategy of decreasing the number.', 'whole_number', 'problem solving', 'interpreting student thinking', 'in_service', 'fourth grade', 'JMTE_Wallach_2005_Hearing', 'TALI WALLACH and RUHAMA EVEN', '2005', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '404, 405, 408', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Wallach_2005_Hearing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40098, 'When tasked with determining which dimension of a rectangular solid to change to affect its volume the most or least, some participants fail to consider the actual *change* in volume, instead simply reducing the smallest dimension to get the smallest possible total volume.', 'one participant''s solution entailed reducing the width by one unit to ''get the least amount of its volume'' without considering the resulting change from the original volume.', 'measurement', 'volume', 'rate of change / dimensional change', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Osana_2006_Role', 'Helena P. Osana, Guy L. Lacroix, Bradley J. Tucker, Chantal Desrosiers', '2006', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '365', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Osana_2006_Role.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40099, 'When asked to fairly distribute items (e.g., pizzas) among groups of varying sizes, some participants fail to grasp the concept of equal distribution, meaning they distribute the items without ensuring that each individual person receives the same proportional amount.', 'some of the pilot participants would ignore this constraint [fairly distributed] as they struggled with distributing the pizzas among all 24 people... 19% of the participants could not solve the problem nor did they understand the key concept of equal distribution (that each person should receive the same amount of pizza).', 'fraction', 'equal sharing', 'proportional distribution', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Osana_2006_Role', 'Helena P. Osana, Guy L. Lacroix, Bradley J. Tucker, Chantal Desrosiers', '2006', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '365, 366', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Osana_2006_Role.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40100, 'Preservice teachers hold the intuitive misconception that a spinner''s outcomes are biased toward sectors that appear larger and undivided, or toward colors split into multiple smaller pieces, even when the total area is mathematically equivalent.', 'Concluding that an equi-probable spinner is unfair because red/green are larger undivided pieces, or because yellow is split into two parts, giving it two chances instead of one.', 'probability', 'theoretical probability', 'circular spinners', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Baturo_2007_Using', 'Annette Baturo, Tom Cooper, Katherine Doyle, Edlyn Grant', '2007', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '254, 255', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Baturo_2007_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40101, 'Preservice teachers hold a deterministic view of probability, expecting experimental trials to yield results that perfectly match theoretical mathematical laws without natural variation.', 'Being dissatisfied with an aggregated trial result of 252, 236, and 252 for three equal colors, because it did not result in exactly one-third for each color.', 'probability', 'experimental probability', 'validation and trials', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Baturo_2007_Using', 'Annette Baturo, Tom Cooper, Katherine Doyle, Edlyn Grant', '2007', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '255', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Baturo_2007_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40102, 'Prospective teachers inappropriately use fractions and equality to describe a whole quantity that changes over time, modeling a transformation with fraction expressions that equal one while simultaneously trying to express inequality (change) through different colored drawings.', 'A representation uses the equation 1 = 10/10 = 15/15 = 30/30 = 1, where the author notes ''using fractions to describe a whole quantity that changes is problematic''.', 'fraction', 'mathematical modeling', 'representing change and equality', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Ryken_2009_Multiple', 'Amy E. Ryken', '2009', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '353, 354', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Ryken_2009_Multiple.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40103, 'When comparing fractions, students incorrectly assume that a fraction with a smaller denominator is always larger, overgeneralizing the idea of ''bigger pieces'' while ignoring the numerator. Furthermore, when drawing area models to compare fractions, they fail to partition equivalent wholes.', 'When asked ''Which is the greater fraction 5/8 or 1/2?'', a student circles 1/2 and writes ''The smaller numbers the biger,'' and draws a square and a circle of different sizes.', 'fraction', 'comparing fractions', 'role of the denominator / area models', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Ryken_2009_Multiple', 'Amy E. Ryken', '2009', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '356, 357, 358', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Ryken_2009_Multiple.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40104, 'Teachers consider symbolic or numeric notations to be the actual mathematical concepts themselves, while viewing visual or graphical representations merely as secondary, informal objects that assist in the work but reside at the periphery of doing mathematics.', 'A teacher''s comment that ''you want the students to know their algebra first'' led researchers to infer that representation is viewed as an added, often visual and informal format, rather than understanding that the symbolic notation is itself just one type of representation.', 'other', 'multiple representations', 'visual vs symbolic', 'in_service', 'middle school', 'JMTE_Stylianou_2010_Teachers''', 'Despina A. Stylianou', '2010', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '333, 334', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Stylianou_2010_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40105, 'Teachers view the use of multiple mathematical representations as an enrichment activity suitable only for high-performing students, mistakenly believing that exposing lower-performing students to multiple representations would be an inappropriate burden or ''too confusing''.', 'One teacher stated, ''Oftentimes with the more advanced kids, I will have them move onto multiple representations because they finish and they need more work to do'', while another noted that for level one and two (low-performing) students she ''would go with a basic approach''.', 'other', 'multiple representations', 'instructional tracking / equity', 'in_service', 'middle school', 'JMTE_Stylianou_2010_Teachers''', 'Despina A. Stylianou', '2010', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '336, 337', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Stylianou_2010_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40106, 'A student struggles to use number sense to find the difference between a number and 100 in a traditional formal format, but can easily solve the same mathematical problem when situated in a familiar out-of-school context (money/change).', 'A 10-year-old student struggled with 106-100 and 124-100 on a worksheet, but when asked ''suppose you have a hundred six dollars and someone says, give me a hundred dollars, how much would you have left?'', she effortlessly answered ''six dollars.''', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'sense-making / informal knowledge', 'in_service', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Taylor_2012_Supporting', 'Edd V. Taylor', '2012', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '272, 273', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Taylor_2012_Supporting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40107, 'When attempting to design lessons that build on students'' out-of-school practices, teachers initially focus only on the general real-world context of the activities rather than identifying and incorporating the specific mathematics embedded within those practices.', 'A group designed a lesson around the context of setting the table, asking students to calculate the total number of pieces of silverware needed, without realizing that in reality children do not think in terms of total silverware, but just the number of each type (or dealing with variations like a baby brother who just uses a spoon).', 'other', 'pedagogical design', 'connecting formal and informal learning', 'in_service', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Taylor_2012_Supporting', 'Edd V. Taylor', '2012', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '279, 280', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Taylor_2012_Supporting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40108, 'The standard primary school notation for division with remainder violates the transitive property of equality, creating a mathematically nonsensical statement when two different expressions with the same quotient and remainder are equated.', 'Because 17:5 = 3(2) and 11:3 = 3(2), transitivity implies 17:5 = 11:3, which is false.', 'whole_number', 'division with remainder', 'equality and transitivity', 'in_service', 'upper elementary (grades 3–6)', 'JMTE_Cooper_2018_Can', 'Jason Cooper, Ronnie Karsenty', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '244, 246, 247', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Cooper_2018_Can.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40109, 'Teachers believe that a student writing ''remainder zero'' indicates a failure to understand the concept of remainder, viewing ''no remainder'' as conceptually distinct from ''a remainder of zero''.', '"When I taught DWR, I had some children who also wrote the zero in brackets... to me it looked as though they did not understand the concept of remainder."', 'whole_number', 'division with remainder', 'remainder zero', 'in_service', 'upper elementary (grades 3–6)', 'JMTE_Cooper_2018_Can', 'Jason Cooper, Ronnie Karsenty', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '250, 251, 252', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Cooper_2018_Can.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40110, 'Students struggle with decimal long division because the standard whole-number remainder notation obscures the fact that the remainder is a fractional part of the divisor that can be further divided.', 'When moving to decimal division, students "saw only the three [remainder]. They didn''t see the three over four... because with remainder we write 3 in brackets."', 'decimal', 'long division', 'transition to decimals', 'in_service', 'upper elementary (grades 3–6)', 'JMTE_Cooper_2018_Can', 'Jason Cooper, Ronnie Karsenty', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '249, 250', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Cooper_2018_Can.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40111, 'Students view the equal sign as a command to execute a calculation rather than a symbol of equivalence, leading them to blindly operate on the visible numbers in algebra-like problems.', 'In an exercise with an unknown like 3 + ? = 8, students sometimes simply add the numbers to get 11.', 'algebraic', 'equality', 'unknowns', 'in_service', 'upper elementary (grades 3–6)', 'JMTE_Cooper_2018_Can', 'Jason Cooper, Ronnie Karsenty', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'passing', '244', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Cooper_2018_Can.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40112, 'Prospective teachers struggle to define the referent whole for fractions, confusing ''how many'' questions with ''how much'' questions, and frequently answering with a discrete number of pieces rather than the fractional amount of the continuous whole.', 'When asked to share four pizzas equally among five people, the prospective teachers tended to answer ''four pieces'' or how many pieces each person would get instead of answering ''4/5'' or how much pizza each person would get.', 'fraction', 'meaning of fractions', 'referent whole', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Tobias_2013_Prospective', 'Jennifer M. Tobias', '2013', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '86, 95, 100', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Tobias_2013_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40113, 'Prospective teachers have difficulties with fraction language, specifically distinguishing among the phrases ''of a'', ''of one'', ''of the'', and ''of each'' when defining the whole, leading them to give mathematically correct fractional counts that describe the wrong referent unit.', 'When asked to determine what fraction of a pizza each person gets when sharing 4 pizzas among 5 people, some students interpreted ''of a'' to mean ''of all the pizzas'' and answered 4/20, while others interpreted it to mean ''of each pizza'' and answered 1/5.', 'fraction', 'fraction language', 'referent whole', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Tobias_2013_Prospective', 'Jennifer M. Tobias', '2013', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '96, 97, 98', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Tobias_2013_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40114, 'Prospective teachers incorrectly believe that the language defining the whole must be pluralized or greater than one if the fractional amount being described is an improper fraction or mixed number.', 'When describing the sum 1 3/8, a student asked ''Can''t you just write 11/8 of two pizzas?'' because the total amount was more than one pizza, reasoning ''Yeah but why would it be one if you''re eating more than one?''', 'fraction', 'fraction language', 'improper fractions', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Tobias_2013_Prospective', 'Jennifer M. Tobias', '2013', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '98, 99', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Tobias_2013_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40115, 'Students face a discrepancy when dividing fractions using a visual measurement model versus the standard algorithm, confusing the remainder in terms of the original dividend units with the fractional part of the quotient in terms of the divisor unit.', 'Using a visual representation for 2 divided by 3/4, students find 2 groups with 2/4 of a whole left over (2 remainder 2/4), but applying the invert-and-multiply algorithm yields 2 2/3.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'remainders and units', 'PST', 'K-8', 'JMTE_Charalambous_2011_Prospective', 'Charalambos Y. Charalambous, Heather C. Hill, Deborah L. Ball', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'productive', 'primary', '442, 448', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Charalambous_2011_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40116, 'A prospective teacher lacks conceptual understanding of fraction division, relying solely on procedural steps while confusing algorithms, and attempts to use visual representations that simply illustrate final numerical answers rather than modeling the mathematical logic.', 'Vonda tries to explain division by drawing two rectangles for the dividend and a third rectangle equal to 3/4 of the area of the first two for the divisor, later quitting because she is "rusty". In another task, she draws 4 liters poured into 6 containers but artificially labels each share with the final answer of 2/3 instead of the intermediate 4/6.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'representations', 'PST', 'K-8', 'JMTE_Charalambous_2011_Prospective', 'Charalambos Y. Charalambous, Heather C. Hill, Deborah L. Ball', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '454, 455', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Charalambous_2011_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40117, 'A prospective teacher relies entirely on rote memorization of procedures to divide fractions, believing that knowing mathematics simply means knowing and applying formulas, and treating division as an algorithm applicable only to proper fractions.', 'Suzanne states, "I don''t know how [to] visually represent dividing by a fraction... I can''t see it in my brain," and instead draws arrows to "cross-multiply" 2/3 and 1/3.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'algorithms', 'PST', 'K-8', 'JMTE_Charalambous_2011_Prospective', 'Charalambos Y. Charalambous, Heather C. Hill, Deborah L. Ball', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '455, 456, 457', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Charalambous_2011_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40118, 'A prospective teacher incorrectly justifies an inequality chain by claiming one could simply switch the middle two terms of valid inequalities, failing to provide a mathematically sound justification.', 'Kimberley argued that a complex incorrect inequality sequence could still be deduced simply because she "could switch the middle two terms" and it "feels intuitive to us, but it is not right."', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'inequalities', 'PST', 'K-8', 'JMTE_Charalambous_2011_Prospective', 'Charalambos Y. Charalambous, Heather C. Hill, Deborah L. Ball', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '452', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Charalambous_2011_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40119, 'A prospective teacher provides a mathematically valid but highly condensed and incomprehensible explanation that fails to unpack the reasoning, erroneously assuming learners can automatically map intermediate steps onto a visual representation.', 'Nathan draws two rectangles to divide 2 by 3/4, points to the remainder and simply states "that is two-thirds... so two-thirds of three-fourths fit inside these," skipping the explicit logic connecting the different units.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'unpacking explanations', 'PST', 'K-8', 'JMTE_Charalambous_2011_Prospective', 'Charalambos Y. Charalambous, Heather C. Hill, Deborah L. Ball', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '449, 450', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Charalambous_2011_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40120, 'Prospective teachers rely on an additive ''filling in the puzzle'' strategy to construct polynomial rectangles, ignoring the linear dimension tiles on the perimeter and treating the multiplication task as simply fitting areal tiles together.', 'Attempting to fit green (xy) tiles next to purple (x^2) squares without multiplying the respective linear dimension tiles, completely separating the area from the lengths.', 'algebraic', 'polynomial multiplication', 'algebra tiles and area models', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Caglayan_2013_Prospective', 'Günhan Caglayan', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '357, 358, 367, 369', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Caglayan_2013_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40121, 'Prospective teachers construct a polynomial rectangle entirely independent of one variable (x) for a problem involving both x and y, and attempt to mathematically force equivalence by inventing a physical substitution relationship between the distinct variables.', 'Building a completely y-dependent rectangle for (x + 1)(2y + 3), yielding 2y^2 + 9y + 9, and then substituting x = y + 2 into the left-hand side because the physical x-tile happens to look two units longer than the y-tile.', 'algebraic', 'polynomial multiplication', 'variables and equivalence', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Caglayan_2013_Prospective', 'Günhan Caglayan', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '358, 359, 360', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Caglayan_2013_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40122, 'When asked to express the area of same-color tile groups as a product, prospective teachers use pseudo-products indicating repeated addition of area units rather than true multiplication of linear dimensions.', 'Writing ''2 * x^2'' or ''4 * xy'' (coefficient times areal unit) instead of ''(2x) * (x)'' or ''(2x) * (2y)'' (linear quantity times linear quantity).', 'algebraic', 'polynomial multiplication', 'representational quantities and units', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Caglayan_2013_Prospective', 'Günhan Caglayan', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '363, 367, 371', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Caglayan_2013_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40123, 'Learners apply ''gap thinking'' or whole-number reasoning independently to the numerator and denominator, incorrectly concluding that two fractions are equivalent if they are missing the same number of pieces to make a whole, regardless of the pieces'' sizes.', 'Four PTs demonstrated gap thinking by incorrectly concluding that the fractions 8/9 and 12/13 were equivalent because they were both one piece away from 1. These PTs exhibited whole-number reasoning by paying attention to only one quantity (number of pieces) rather than both quantities (number and size of pieces).', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'gap thinking', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Thanheiser_2016_Reflective', 'Eva Thanheiser, Dana Olanoff, Amy Hillen, Ziv Feldman, Jennifer M. Tobias, Rachael M. Welder', '2016', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '127, 131, 133', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Thanheiser_2016_Reflective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40124, 'When finding a common numerator to compare fractions, learners incorrectly apply whole-number reasoning to the denominators, concluding that a fraction is smaller simply because its denominator is a smaller number.', 'These PTs incorrectly wrote that 25/99 < 25/100, presumably because 99 < 100.', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'same numerator', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Thanheiser_2016_Reflective', 'Eva Thanheiser, Dana Olanoff, Amy Hillen, Ziv Feldman, Jennifer M. Tobias, Rachael M. Welder', '2016', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '133', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Thanheiser_2016_Reflective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40125, 'Learners exhibit the belief that all fractions must be less than 1, stating that a proper fraction structure is required for a valid comparison.', 'One PT commented that the fractions in problems #7 (5/6 vs. 6/5) and #10 (24/7 vs. 34/15) could not be compared because ''the [numerator] must be lower than the [denominator].''', 'fraction', 'fraction concept', 'improper fractions', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Thanheiser_2016_Reflective', 'Eva Thanheiser, Dana Olanoff, Amy Hillen, Ziv Feldman, Jennifer M. Tobias, Rachael M. Welder', '2016', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '134', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Thanheiser_2016_Reflective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40126, 'When using a benchmark strategy to compare fractions, learners incorrectly assume that ''closer to the benchmark'' always means ''smaller'' (or larger), failing to coordinate the magnitude of the distance with the directionality relative to the benchmark (especially when both fractions are greater than the benchmark).', 'PTs incorrectly concluded that 8/9 > 12/13 by (a) correctly drawing on BV[1] to identify the distance from 1 as 1/9 and 1/13, (b) applying SNP to state that 1/9 > 1/13, but then (c) incorrectly concluding from this that 8/9 > 12/13... Many PTs argued that one fraction was larger than another because ''it was closer to 1.'' While this reasoning is valid when comparing fractions both <1, it is not when comparing fractions that are both greater than a benchmark, such as 1.', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'benchmark strategies', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Thanheiser_2016_Reflective', 'Eva Thanheiser, Dana Olanoff, Amy Hillen, Ziv Feldman, Jennifer M. Tobias, Rachael M. Welder', '2016', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '133, 142', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Thanheiser_2016_Reflective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40127, 'A student attempting to solve a join-change-unknown problem by counting up loses track of the double count because the method makes too many demands on his working memory.', 'He was keeping track of how many numbers were added by going back and forth between each number sequence: ''first is 18, second is 19, third is 20'' and so forth until he got to ''ninth is 26.'' He lost track of his double count here and stopped.', 'whole_number', 'addition', 'counting on / keeping track', 'in_service', 'fourth grade', 'JMTE_Steinberg_2004_Inquiry', 'RUTH M. STEINBERG, SUSAN B. EMPSON and THOMAS P. CARPENTER', '2004', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '247', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Steinberg_2004_Inquiry.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40128, 'Students incorrectly use the standard subtraction algorithm for problems involving regrouping by consistently subtracting the smaller digit from the larger digit regardless of its position as minuend or subtrahend.', 'For problems involving regrouping, these seven children always subtracted the smaller digit from the larger one, an example of a buggy algorithm when the smaller digit is the minuend.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'standard algorithm / regrouping', 'in_service', 'fourth grade', 'JMTE_Steinberg_2004_Inquiry', 'RUTH M. STEINBERG, SUSAN B. EMPSON and THOMAS P. CARPENTER', '2004', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '248, 249, 253, 254', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Steinberg_2004_Inquiry.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40129, 'A student solves a fraction division/equal sharing problem using an unconventional repeated halving partitioning strategy rather than standard fractional divisions, producing a complex sum of unit fractions.', 'She then partitioned the remaining six cakes in half... halved each of the remaining halves... each sharer had one half, one half of a half, and one seventh of a half.', 'fraction', 'partitioning', 'equal sharing', 'in_service', 'fourth grade', 'JMTE_Steinberg_2004_Inquiry', 'RUTH M. STEINBERG, SUSAN B. EMPSON and THOMAS P. CARPENTER', '2004', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'productive', 'secondary', '254, 255, 256', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Steinberg_2004_Inquiry.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40130, 'Teachers incorrectly assume that both lines on a double number line must share the same scale, becoming confused when equivalent proportional values are placed at the same physical distance from zero but represent different numerical quantities.', 'Woodrow commented, ''they put both of the values [0.75 and 1.25] right at the same distance from zero. That''s kind of maybe, weird for lack of a better word.''', 'proportional', 'double number lines', 'scale and equivalence', 'in_service', 'middle-grade', 'JMTE_Orrill_2012_Making', 'Chandra Hawley Orrill, Rachael Eriksen Brown', '2012', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '390, 391, 399', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Orrill_2012_Making.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40131, 'Teachers rely on standard algorithms like cross-multiplication to find an answer first, and only then use the double number line to illustrate the calculated result, failing to use the representation to actively reason about covariation.', 'Corey interpreted the DNL as illustrating the result of cross multiplication... He extended the number line and marked 9 and 3 3/5 as the new right endpoints.', 'proportional', 'proportional reasoning', 'cross-multiplication vs. representation', 'in_service', 'middle-grade', 'JMTE_Orrill_2012_Making', 'Chandra Hawley Orrill, Rachael Eriksen Brown', '2012', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '388, 389, 392, 399', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Orrill_2012_Making.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40132, 'Teachers invoke additive build-up strategies, repeated subtraction, or estimation rather than multiplicative composed-unit reasoning to find missing values on a double number line.', 'Jacinda and Jamal turned to calculation to explain that each one-fifth segment... would be worth $0.15... then invoked repeated subtraction. Calvin suggested, ''it''s going to be more than point-five but less than three-quarters.''', 'proportional', 'proportional reasoning', 'additive vs. multiplicative reasoning', 'in_service', 'middle-grade', 'JMTE_Orrill_2012_Making', 'Chandra Hawley Orrill, Rachael Eriksen Brown', '2012', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '392, 399', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Orrill_2012_Making.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40133, 'Teachers view the double number line merely as a tool for estimating the relationship between quantities rather than a tool for precisely coordinating and partitioning proportional values.', 'Corey drew lines to ''about 2 1/3'' for batches of cookies, viewing the DNL as showing an approximation rather than using it to reason about the exact relationship.', 'proportional', 'double number lines', 'estimation', 'in_service', 'middle-grade', 'JMTE_Orrill_2012_Making', 'Chandra Hawley Orrill, Rachael Eriksen Brown', '2012', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '396, 399', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Orrill_2012_Making.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40134, 'Prospective teachers write story problems for fraction division that actually represent division by a whole number, demonstrating a procedural rather than conceptual understanding of dividing by a fraction.', 'Jane is making three cakes. She has 2/9 cups of baking soda left and need to divide it evenly among the three cakes. How much baking soda can she put in each cake?', 'fraction', 'division of fractions', 'story problems', 'PST', 'elementary and secondary', 'JMTE_Son_2009_Prospective', 'Ji-Won Son, Sandra Crespo', '2009', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '244, 245', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Son_2009_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40135, 'Prospective teachers mistakenly consider a valid non-traditional fraction division algorithm (dividing numerators and denominators) to be mathematically incorrect or incorrectly conclude it cannot be generalized to all fraction division problems.', 'In one example, the prospective teacher suggested that Dan''s strategy would not work for 2/9 ÷ 5/15.', 'fraction', 'division of fractions', 'alternative algorithms', 'PST', 'elementary and secondary', 'JMTE_Son_2009_Prospective', 'Ji-Won Son, Sandra Crespo', '2009', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '247, 248', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Son_2009_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40136, 'A child incorrectly writes the fraction three-fourths as an addition expression (''three plus four'') instead of using standard fractional notation.', 'When the child writes ''three plus four'' for three-fourths, the interviewer stops questioning and begins teaching the child how to write fractions as numerals.', 'fraction', 'fraction notation', 'symbolic form', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Moyer_2002_Learning', 'Patricia S. Moyer, Elizabeth Milewicz', '2002', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '305', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Moyer_2002_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40137, 'When asked to divide two pizzas equally among five people, a student partitions one pizza into five pieces and the other into six, failing to coordinate the partitions to ensure equal shares for all.', 'A sixth-grader divides one pizza into five pieces and the other pizza into six.', 'fraction', 'partitioning', 'equal sharing', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Moyer_2002_Learning', 'Patricia S. Moyer, Elizabeth Milewicz', '2002', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '305', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Moyer_2002_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40138, 'Learners incorrectly judge a shorter decimal to be larger than a longer decimal, sometimes driven by reciprocal thinking, negative thinking, or denominator-focused thinking.', '0.3 is thought to be bigger than 0.4 by analogy with the fact that 1/3 is larger than 1/4... 0.6 is perceived as smaller than 0, just as -6 is smaller than 0... judge 0.3 to be bigger than 0.426 because they assume that any number of tenths is greater than any number of thousandths.', 'decimal', 'comparing decimals', 'shorter-is-larger', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Stacey_2001_Preservice', 'Kaye Stacey, Sue Helme, Vicki Steinle, Annette Baturo, Kathryn Irwin, Jack Bana', '2001', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '211, 212', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Stacey_2001_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40139, 'Students incorrectly think that a longer decimal is larger than a shorter decimal due to an overgeneralization of whole number properties.', 'The longer the number the bigger the students think it is. Students will have difficulty working in the opposite direction to whole numbers (i.e., the more numbers added to the right of the decimal, the smaller the number is broken into).', 'decimal', 'comparing decimals', 'longer-is-larger', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Stacey_2001_Preservice', 'Kaye Stacey, Sue Helme, Vicki Steinle, Annette Baturo, Kathryn Irwin, Jack Bana', '2001', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '211, 216', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Stacey_2001_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40140, 'Learners struggle to compare decimals with zero, sometimes believing zero is larger because it is in the ones column or it is a whole number, or incorrectly treating decimals as negative numbers below zero.', 'Children are taught that the ones column is larger than the tenths column so assume 0 is bigger than a decimal. 0.22 may be mistaken for a negative number below zero.', 'decimal', 'comparing decimals', 'comparing with zero', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Stacey_2001_Preservice', 'Kaye Stacey, Sue Helme, Vicki Steinle, Annette Baturo, Kathryn Irwin, Jack Bana', '2001', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '215, 217, 218', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Stacey_2001_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40141, 'When subtracting multi-digit numbers, a student incorrectly subtracts the smaller digit from the larger digit in each place value column, regardless of whether the smaller digit is in the minuend or the subtrahend.', 'For 132 - 45, the student writes 113, reasoning ''big number take away small number''.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'renaming/regrouping', 'PST', 'primary', 'JMTE_Norton_2018_Relationship', 'Stephen Norton', '2018', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Norton_2018_Relationship.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40142, 'When multiplying two-digit numbers using a traditional vertical algorithm, a student multiplies the digits in each respective place value directly (e.g., tens by tens and ones by ones) but fails to cross-multiply, and exhibits shallow place-value understanding by recording tens multiplied by tens as tens instead of hundreds.', 'For 27 x 39, the child multiplies 7 by 9 and records 63, then 2 tens by 3 tens and records 6 tens, and subsequently adds 6 and 63 to get 69.', 'whole_number', 'multiplication', 'two-digit multiplication', 'PST', 'primary', 'JMTE_Norton_2018_Relationship', 'Stephen Norton', '2018', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Norton_2018_Relationship.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40143, 'A student confounds the processes of fraction addition and fraction multiplication, seeking a common denominator first, multiplying the numerators, but inappropriately retaining the common denominator instead of multiplying the denominators as well.', 'For 1/2 x 2/3, the student converts to 3/6 x 4/6, multiplies the numerators to get 12, retains the denominator of 6 to write 12/6, and then reduces it to 2/1.', 'fraction', 'fraction multiplication', 'confounding algorithms', 'PST', 'primary', 'JMTE_Norton_2018_Relationship', 'Stephen Norton', '2018', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Norton_2018_Relationship.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40144, 'A student incorrectly adds fractions by treating them as whole numbers, adding the numerators together and adding the denominators together.', 'When calculating 1/3 + 1/2, the student adds the numerators (1+1=2) and denominators (3+2=5) to get 2/5.', 'fraction', 'fraction addition', 'adding numerators and denominators', 'PST', 'primary', 'JMTE_Norton_2018_Relationship', 'Stephen Norton', '2018', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Norton_2018_Relationship.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40145, 'Prospective teachers incorrectly write word problems that represent multiplication by a whole number or division by a whole number when tasked with contextualizing division by a proper fraction.', 'When asked to pose a word problem for ''8 2/3 ÷ 1/4 = ?'', a teacher wrote an equal-group multiplication problem: ''Some apples were shared among 4 people. Each person got 8 2/3 bags. How many bags (of apples) are there in the beginning?'', which actually represents 8 2/3 × 4.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'problem posing', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Lo_2012_Prospective', 'Jane-Jane Lo, Fenqjen Luo', '2012', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '491, 492', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Lo_2012_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40146, 'Students interpret a mathematical graph as a literal, physical picture of a real-world situation rather than a representation of the relationship between variables.', 'When asked to find a situation corresponding to a generic piecewise graph, a pupil answers: ''It may be the path of an excursion during which we had to climb up a hillside, then walk along a flat stretch and then climb down a slope and finally go across another flat stretch before finishing''.', 'algebraic', 'functions', 'interpreting graphs', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Sanchez_2003_Four', 'VICTORIA SÁNCHEZ and SALVADOR LLINARES', '2003', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '13', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Sanchez_2003_Four.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40147, 'Prospective teachers write incorrect story problems for partitive division number sentences with fraction divisors, phrasing questions that reflect taking a fractional part of the dividend rather than dividing by the fraction.', 'For the sentence ''24 oz of water / (1/4) hours = ?'', a teacher wrote ''Amy drinks 24 oz of water in 1 hour. How much does she drink in a 1/4 of an hour?'', representing 1/4 of 24.', 'fraction', 'partitive division', 'translating number sentences to story problems', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Jansen_2016_Examining', 'Amanda Jansen, Charles Hohensee', '2016', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '513', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Jansen_2016_Examining.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40148, 'Prospective teachers hold a rigid conception of partitive division by associating the process of iterating with multiplication or a multi-step operation, rather than recognizing it as a valid process of division.', 'After iterating 24 oz four times to obtain the correct answer 96, a teacher states, ''I keep thinking at it from like a multiplication point of view instead of division, which that''s not right.''', 'fraction', 'partitive division', 'iterating vs. partitioning', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Jansen_2016_Examining', 'Amanda Jansen, Charles Hohensee', '2016', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '514, 515, 516', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Jansen_2016_Examining.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40149, 'Prospective teachers incorrectly apply a measurement meaning of division to a partitive situation with different units, so that they can partition each unit into smaller pieces rather than iterate.', 'To solve 24 oz divided by 1/4 hours, a teacher breaks each 1 oz block into four pieces, stating ''I''m just simply breaking each one of these blocks into equal sized pieces'', effectively subtracting 1/4 oz 96 times.', 'fraction', 'partitive division', 'measurement vs. partitive models', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Jansen_2016_Examining', 'Amanda Jansen, Charles Hohensee', '2016', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '516, 517', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Jansen_2016_Examining.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40150, 'Prospective teachers who use iterating to solve a partitive division problem subsequently try to map their action to division by claiming the act of dividing was partitioning the quotient to get the dividend.', 'A teacher states ''the act of dividing is dividing ninety-six ounces into four parts'' instead of recognizing that iterating 24 four times represents the division process.', 'fraction', 'partitive division', 'partitioning the quotient', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Jansen_2016_Examining', 'Amanda Jansen, Charles Hohensee', '2016', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '516', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Jansen_2016_Examining.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40151, 'A student employs a non-standard constant difference strategy for subtraction (reducing both minuend and subtrahend by the same amount) which deviates from standard decomposition algorithms, acting as a perturbation that the teacher initially struggles to interpret in the moment.', 'To explain the equality 63 - 8 = 60 - 5, Mary states, ''And from the 8, 3 are taken away... We calculate 60 - 5. And it equals 55. As well as 63 - 8 equals 55.''', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'constant difference', 'in_service', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Ticha_2006_Qualified', 'MARIE TICHA and ALENA HOSPESOVA', '2006', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'productive', 'primary', '140, 141, 142', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Ticha_2006_Qualified.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40152, 'Elementary teachers incorrectly consider paper-folding activities as ''not mathematical'' because they do not explicitly involve numbers, preferring instead procedural calculations of colored parts on grids.', 'In Czech schools this activity is unusual because many elementary teachers do not consider it to be ''mathematical'' (i.e., not concerned with number) and prefer calculation of coloured parts of figures drawn on a grid...', 'fraction', 'meaning of fractions', 'geometric vs numeric representations', 'in_service', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Ticha_2006_Qualified', 'MARIE TICHA and ALENA HOSPESOVA', '2006', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '144', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Ticha_2006_Qualified.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40153, 'A student interprets the numerator and denominator of a fraction sequentially as physical commands (number of items and the action of cutting) rather than as a relational part-whole quantity.', 'David answered that 1/2 meant ''1 thing – cut into 2 equal pieces.'' He made it clear that the fraction bar took the place of the words ''cut into.''', 'fraction', 'meaning of fractions', 'part-whole concept', 'in_service', 'K-6', 'JMTE_Schifter_1998_Learning', 'DEBORAH SCHIFTER', '1998', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '55, 56', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Schifter_1998_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40154, 'Students struggle with the changing reference unit in fraction arithmetic, leading them to write equations that violate conventional notation because they dynamically shift the reference unit from the original whole to a new functional unit.', 'A student writes ''1/6 + 4/6 = 5/6 = 1'' because 5/6 of a meter constitutes exactly 1 hair ribbon.', 'fraction', 'division of fractions', 'changing reference unit', 'in_service', 'K-6', 'JMTE_Schifter_1998_Learning', 'DEBORAH SCHIFTER', '1998', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '61, 62, 63', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Schifter_1998_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40155, 'Teachers struggle to coordinate the units of the remainder when solving fraction division problems using different methods, confusing the remainder in terms of the original dividend unit with the remainder in terms of the divisor unit.', 'When solving 4 divided by 3/5 using repeated subtraction, a teacher gets 6 with 2/5 left over. Using the invert-and-multiply algorithm, she gets 6 2/3. She is confused because she can justify both answers visually but they appear mathematically contradictory.', 'fraction', 'division of fractions', 'interpreting remainders', 'in_service', 'K-6', 'JMTE_Schifter_1998_Learning', 'DEBORAH SCHIFTER', '1998', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '67, 68, 69', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Schifter_1998_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40156, 'Students struggle to recognize fractions as relational quantities, questioning the necessity of a fraction when an absolute whole number count can describe the isolated subset.', 'A student asks ''Why don''t they say the thing instead of a fraction?'' and later states, ''I still don''t get what 3/12 is... if you take it from the market and you buy it, and you have 3 of them, that''s 3 oranges.''', 'fraction', 'meaning of fractions', 'relational vs absolute quantity', 'in_service', 'K-6', 'JMTE_Schifter_1998_Learning', 'DEBORAH SCHIFTER', '1998', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '73, 74, 75, 76', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Schifter_1998_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40157, 'Teachers experience a mathematical perturbation regarding the relationship between area and perimeter, discovering with astonishment that increasing a figure''s area by adding a square does not necessarily increase its perimeter, and can actually leave it unchanged or decrease it.', 'Finding that by adding one square (area unit) to a figure, the perimeter could change by +2, 0, -2, or -4 units.', 'measurement', 'area and perimeter', 'relationship between area and perimeter', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Even_1999_Development', 'Ruhama Even', '1999', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'productive', 'secondary', '8, 9', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Even_1999_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40158, 'Prospective teachers fail to recognize when a student incorrectly uses direct argumentation disguised as an indirect proof by contradiction, instead praising the student for understanding the contradiction format.', 'A student writes a direct argument to prove a + b is irrational, and a PSMT provides feedback stating: "Your contradiction is correct."', 'rational', 'mathematical proof', 'proof by contradiction', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Bleiler_2014_Providing', 'Sarah K. Bleiler, Denisse R. Thompson, Milé Krajčevski', '2014', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '117, 120, 121, 122', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Bleiler_2014_Providing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40159, 'Students attempt to prove a general mathematical theorem by providing a single numerical example, demonstrating an empirical or inductive proof scheme.', 'Proving "if a is a factor of b and a is a factor of c, then a is a factor of b*c" by substituting a=2, b=4, and c=6.', 'whole_number', 'mathematical proof', 'empirical reasoning', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Bleiler_2014_Providing', 'Sarah K. Bleiler, Denisse R. Thompson, Milé Krajčevski', '2014', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '118, 119', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Bleiler_2014_Providing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40160, 'Prospective teachers provide incorrect or mathematically restrictive definitions for rational and irrational numbers when attempting to correct students'' arguments.', 'A PSMT incorrectly states that an irrational number b must be represented by the square root of k.', 'rational', 'number systems', 'definitions of rational and irrational', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Bleiler_2014_Providing', 'Sarah K. Bleiler, Denisse R. Thompson, Milé Krajčevski', '2014', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '116, 117', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Bleiler_2014_Providing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40161, 'Students potentially confuse irrational numbers with imaginary numbers when attempting to provide an example for a proof.', 'A student uses "i" as an example of an irrational number to prove that a + b is irrational.', 'rational', 'number systems', 'irrational vs imaginary', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Bleiler_2014_Providing', 'Sarah K. Bleiler, Denisse R. Thompson, Milé Krajčevski', '2014', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'passing', '120', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Bleiler_2014_Providing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40162, 'When learning standard written algorithms, students adopt a local view of individual columns and perform mere number manipulation, failing to interpret the numbers flexibly as a composition of tens and hundreds.', 'They identified that 3 + 5 makes 8 but did neither think of them as tens nor did they see the correspondence to the calculation on the left side (30+50 = 80).', 'whole_number', 'addition and subtraction', 'standard algorithms / place value', 'in_service', '3rd and 4th grade', 'JMTE_Scherer_2006_Noticing', 'PETRA SCHERER and HEINZ STEINBRING', '2006', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '167, 168', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Scherer_2006_Noticing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40163, 'Teachers engage in a communicative ''funnel pattern'', asking increasingly narrow, specific questions that reduce students'' mathematical activity to merely guessing the expected single word or digit, rather than fostering independent understanding and explanation.', 'When a student says ''Six plus one'' for the tens column, the teacher prompts ''What does it say there? Six...'' -> Student: ''Plus'' -> Teacher: ''Six.'' -> Student: ''Tens.'' -> Teacher: ''Plus.'' -> Student: ''Plus one.'' ... ultimately forcing the student to state ''Seven tens''.', 'whole_number', 'addition', 'communication / algorithms', 'in_service', '3rd and 4th grade', 'JMTE_Scherer_2006_Noticing', 'PETRA SCHERER and HEINZ STEINBRING', '2006', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '171, 172, 174, 175', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Scherer_2006_Noticing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40164, 'A student encounters difficulty performing subtraction when there is a zero in the minuend, stating that it ''doesn''t work'' to reach the zero.', 'Calculating 909 - 166, the student says ''From the six to the zero it''s ehm'' and is unsure how to proceed. Another student notes ''in order to reach the zero, that doesn''t work''.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'subtraction across zeroes', 'in_service', '3rd and 4th grade', 'JMTE_Scherer_2006_Noticing', 'PETRA SCHERER and HEINZ STEINBRING', '2006', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '180, 181', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Scherer_2006_Noticing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40165, 'A student finds a fraction between two given fractions by incorrectly choosing a numerator between the given numerators and a denominator between the given denominators.', 'A student answered that 2/3 is between 1/2 and 3/4 ''because 2 is between the 1 and the 3, and on the bottom the 3 lies between the 2 and the 4''.', 'fraction', 'ordering fractions', 'fractions between fractions', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Rowland_2005_Elementary', 'TIM ROWLAND, PETER HUCKSTEP and ANNE THWAITES', '2005', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '263', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Rowland_2005_Elementary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40166, 'Students struggle to interpret the word ''difference'' mathematically in a subtraction context due to its polysemy in everyday language.', 'When asked ''what''s the difference between my pond and her pond in the number of frogs?'', Jeffrey responds ''when he had a frog you only had two frogs'', failing to recognize the request for a numerical subtraction.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'vocabulary / difference', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Rowland_2005_Elementary', 'TIM ROWLAND, PETER HUCKSTEP and ANNE THWAITES', '2005', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '268', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Rowland_2005_Elementary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40167, 'When asked to find the difference between two sets, students state the total quantity of the larger set rather than the numerical difference between them.', 'When asked how many more frogs Bill has (6) compared to Martin (4), Leo incorrectly answers ''six''.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'comparison', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Rowland_2005_Elementary', 'TIM ROWLAND, PETER HUCKSTEP and ANNE THWAITES', '2005', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '268, 269', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Rowland_2005_Elementary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40168, 'When tasked with finding the difference between two randomly generated numbers, students default to adding the numbers together.', 'When dice show 3 and 5 and the teacher asks for the difference, Jeffrey sums them and answers 8. Later, when asked for the difference between 3 and 6, Jim states ''Three'' with the intention of adding the second number to it.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'difference vs sum', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Rowland_2005_Elementary', 'TIM ROWLAND, PETER HUCKSTEP and ANNE THWAITES', '2005', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '273, 274', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Rowland_2005_Elementary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40169, 'A student incorrectly decomposes a divisor additively instead of multiplicatively when using a repeated halving strategy for division, leading them to divide too many times.', 'To divide 144 by 8, the student repeatedly divides by 2 four times, reasoning that 2+2+2+2 = 8, rather than recognizing that halving three times divides by 8 (since 2*2*2=8).', 'whole_number', 'multidigit division', 'repeated halving and decomposition', 'in_service', 'grades three, four and five', 'JMTE_Empson_2004_Teachers''', 'Susan B. Empson, Debra L. Junk', '2004', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '126, 128, 132, 133', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Empson_2004_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40170, 'Teachers fail to recognize repeated halving as a valid mathematical model for division, viewing it as a completely separate and unrelated operation.', 'A teacher dismisses the student''s halving strategy by stating ''she''s splitting it in half each time, but it''s not really divided''.', 'whole_number', 'multidigit division', 'interpreting nonstandard strategies', 'in_service', 'grades three, four and five', 'JMTE_Empson_2004_Teachers''', 'Susan B. Empson, Debra L. Junk', '2004', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '134', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Empson_2004_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40171, 'Teachers misapply a measurement model of division to evaluate a student''s partitive strategy, limiting their ability to engage with the child''s actual mathematical thinking.', 'Using a measurement interpretation (''how many eights fit into 144'') to make sense of a strategy where the student is actually using a partitive model (splitting 144 into 8 equal groups by halving).', 'whole_number', 'multidigit division', 'measurement vs. partitive models', 'in_service', 'grades three, four and five', 'JMTE_Empson_2004_Teachers''', 'Susan B. Empson, Debra L. Junk', '2004', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '134', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Empson_2004_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40172, 'Students confuse the number of groups with the size of the groups when modeling division, such as representing 60 divided by 3 as two groups of three tens (30s) instead of three groups of two tens (20s). When attempting to correct this to three groups, students may move base-ten blocks haphazardly, creating unequal groups and failing to recognize that division requires equal partitioning.', 'A student splits 60 (represented by six ten-blocks) into two groups of three tens. When prompted to fix it, he moves blocks to create three unequal groups (one group of ten, one of twenty, one of thirty) and justifies it by saying ''Cause then, this would belong over here...''', 'whole_number', 'division', 'partitive division / equal groups', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Santagata_2014_Learning', 'Rossella Santagata, Cathery Yeh', '2014', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '502, 503, 505', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Santagata_2014_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40173, 'A student moves beyond simple counting to recognize the relationship between the dimensions of an array and its total area, spontaneously abstracting a multiplicative formula from a physical counting activity.', 'When finding the area by counting 12 square units, a student states: ''Well you could do something similar like 4 x 3 and then that would give you 12.''', 'measurement', 'area', 'arrays and multiplication', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Santagata_2014_Learning', 'Rossella Santagata, Cathery Yeh', '2014', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'productive', 'passing', '504, 505', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Santagata_2014_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40174, 'Prospective teachers exhibit extreme inflexibility by over-relying on the standard algorithm of finding a common denominator to compare fractions, even when the numbers make the procedure extremely unwieldy and mentally taxing.', 'For the comparison of 35/832 and 37/834, Maricela spent more than 10 min attempting to mentally compute 832 x 834, 35 x 834, and 37 x 832. She was unsuccessful.', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'inflexibility / standard algorithm', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Whitacre_2016_Investigating', 'Ian Whitacre, Susan D. Nickerson', '2016', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '66, 67, 68', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Whitacre_2016_Investigating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40175, 'Prospective teachers exhibit ''Denominator Dominance,'' focusing exclusively on the size of the fractional parts (the denominator) while ignoring the number of parts (the numerator), incorrectly concluding that the fraction with the smaller pieces is always smaller.', 'When asked to compare 8/24 with 13/39 in her first interview, Angela reasoned, ''The 39 is larger, and so it''s gonna make the pieces really small.'' She concluded that 8/24 was greater without explicitly accounting for the numerators in any way.', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'denominator dominance', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Whitacre_2016_Investigating', 'Ian Whitacre, Susan D. Nickerson', '2016', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '68', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Whitacre_2016_Investigating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40176, 'A student incorrectly attributes the 1/2 in the triangle area formula to the fact that a triangle has three sides, and the teacher accepts this explanation without probing.', 'Teacher: ''Why does it [the formula for the area of a triangle] have 1/2 in it?'' Student: ''because it [a triangle] has only three sides.'' Neither the teacher nor other students question it.', 'geometric', 'area', 'triangle area formula', 'in_service', 'grades 8-12', 'JMTE_Arbaugh_2006_Examining', 'Fran Arbaugh, John Lannin, Dustin L. Jones, Meredith Park-Rogers', '2006', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '533', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Arbaugh_2006_Examining.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40177, 'Teachers believe that students cannot succeed with a problem-based curriculum without basic skills, leading them to lower the cognitive demand of tasks by providing step-by-step procedures.', 'Instead of allowing students to construct a population model, Ms. Taylor breaks it down: ''These are your steps. You may want to put a circle around this... (1) Find the net change (2) Convert to a decimal...''', 'algebraic', 'mathematical modeling', 'iterative equations', 'in_service', 'grades 8-12', 'JMTE_Arbaugh_2006_Examining', 'Fran Arbaugh, John Lannin, Dustin L. Jones, Meredith Park-Rogers', '2006', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '530, 531, 534', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Arbaugh_2006_Examining.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40178, 'Teachers misinterpret students'' emerging understanding of fractions as numbers—where students name a rod by a fraction without explicitly stating the referent whole—as confusion or a failure to identify the unit.', 'A teacher notes that students get confused because they say ''one-half'' but don''t say precisely ''one-half of what'', concluding that the student ''couldn''t justify her thinking''.', 'fraction', 'fraction concepts', 'fractions as operators vs. fractions as numbers', 'in_service', 'elementary and middle school', 'JMTE_Francisco_2011_Teachers', 'John M. Francisco, Carolyn A. Maher', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '56', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Francisco_2011_Teachers.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40179, 'When attempting to write word problems for division by zero, students struggle to conceptualize ''two divided by zero'' and intuitively alter the wording to represent ''zero divided by two'' because it makes more sense to them.', 'The problem that was shared on the board dealt with a mother dividing two cookies among zero children. The wording was confusing to the students and they wanted to change it. Their suggested change, however, made the problem imply zero divided by two, which everyone liked better because it made sense to them.', 'whole_number', 'division', 'division by zero', 'PST', 'elementary and middle school', 'JMTE_Tyminski_2010_Teacher', 'Andrew M. Tyminski', '2010', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '300, 301', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Tyminski_2010_Teacher.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40180, 'Students falsely expect a ''oneths'' place value to exist immediately to the right of the decimal point, mirroring the ''ones'' place. This arises from an implicit ''mirror metaphor'' that incorrectly locates the point of symmetry for the base-ten system at the decimal point rather than the ones place.', 'A student asks ''Ma''am, oneths kyun nahi hota? [Teacher, why are there no oneths?]'' and another justifies that oneths would be ''one by one''.', 'decimal', 'place value', 'mirror metaphor / oneths', 'in_service', 'Grade 6', 'JMTE_Takker_2017_Knowledge', 'Shikha Takker, K. Subramaniam', '2017', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Takker_2017_Knowledge.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40181, 'Students incorrectly overgeneralize whole number rules when comparing decimal numbers, leading them to either conclude that a decimal with more digits is larger (the whole number rule) or incorrectly apply reverse thinking to conclude that a longer decimal is always smaller.', 'When comparing 3.3 and 3.300, a student (Soh) concludes that 3.3 is greater, reasoning that ''3.300 is less than 3.3 because it has more numbers [digits]''.', 'decimal', 'comparing decimals', 'longer is larger / shorter is larger', 'in_service', 'Grade 6', 'JMTE_Takker_2017_Knowledge', 'Shikha Takker, K. Subramaniam', '2017', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Takker_2017_Knowledge.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40182, 'Students struggle to understand how the placement of zero affects the value of a decimal number. Some incorrectly evaluate equivalence by just counting zeroes and ignoring the decimal point, while others falsely believe appending a zero after decimal digits acts like whole number multiplication by 10.', 'When comparing 1020.0 and 102.00, students initially state they might be the same ''because the number of zeroes are same [ignoring the decimal point].''', 'decimal', 'meaning of decimals', 'annexing zeroes', 'in_service', 'Grade 6', 'JMTE_Takker_2017_Knowledge', 'Shikha Takker, K. Subramaniam', '2017', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Takker_2017_Knowledge.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40183, 'A student incorrectly handles variable terms on both sides of a linear equation, attempting to isolate the variable by dividing early rather than using the additive properties of equality to group the variable terms together.', 'Zander solves 2x + 3 = 5x - 9 by subtracting 3, then dividing by 2 to get x = (5/2)x - 6. He then states that x is by itself on the left, but for the right side he says, ''Because it''s (5/2)x - 6 and I don''t know how to get rid of them.''', 'algebraic', 'linear equations', 'combining variable terms', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Monson_2018_Developing', 'Debra Monson, Erin Krupa, Kristin Lesseig, Stephanie Casey', '2018', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Monson_2018_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40184, 'A student solving a linear equation that results in an identity (e.g., 8 = 8) recognizes that the statement is true, but lacks a conceptual understanding of what a true statement implies about the number of solutions to the equation.', 'Taio solves 2(3x + 4) = 6x + 8, gets 8 = 8, and states ''They cancel out so 8=8, which is true.'' When the interviewer asks ''What does it mean to be true?'', Taio replies ''I dunno [don''t know].''', 'algebraic', 'linear equations', 'equations with infinitely many solutions', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Monson_2018_Developing', 'Debra Monson, Erin Krupa, Kristin Lesseig, Stephanie Casey', '2018', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Monson_2018_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40185, 'A student solving a perfect square quadratic equation believes there are two distinct zeros because the algebraic procedure yields the same numerical value twice, failing to recognize what this implies graphically.', 'For the equation x^2 - 4x + 4 = 0, the student believes there are two zeros because she has x = 2 twice. She doesn''t process that these are the same value and what that means graphically.', 'algebraic', 'quadratic equations', 'double roots', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Monson_2018_Developing', 'Debra Monson, Erin Krupa, Kristin Lesseig, Stephanie Casey', '2018', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Monson_2018_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40186, 'A student solving a quadratic equation with no real solutions does not understand the implications of calculating the square root of a negative number, failing to connect it to imaginary numbers or its graphical representation.', 'When solving x^2 - 2x + 3 = 0, the student calculates a negative square root but does not know what it means, failing to recall imaginary numbers or the implications of finding an x-value.', 'algebraic', 'quadratic equations', 'imaginary roots', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Monson_2018_Developing', 'Debra Monson, Erin Krupa, Kristin Lesseig, Stephanie Casey', '2018', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Monson_2018_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40187, 'A child divides a continuous quantity into unequal parts but treats them as equal shares by assigning one piece to each person, relying on a one-to-one correspondence strategy rather than ensuring equal area or volume.', 'A child may divide a quantity represented as a circle or square into four unequal parts and give each of four people ''one of these'' by creating a piece-to-person one-to-one correspondence.', 'fraction', 'partitioning', 'equal shares / area models', 'in_service', 'upper elementary', 'JMTE_Saxe_2001_Enhancing', 'Geoffrey B. Saxe, Maryl Gearhart and Na''ilah Suad Nasir', '2001', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '61, 62', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Saxe_2001_Enhancing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40188, 'Children struggle to coordinate different mathematical representations of the same fractional quantity or division operation, producing disconnected or conflicting solutions when using visual diagrams versus numerical algorithms.', 'A child who is asked to partition a set of 12 cookies into fair shares for eight people might produce one solution with a diagram (''one whole cookie and one of these'' [a half]) and another solution with numbers (''8 into 12 is 1 R4'').', 'fraction', 'fair sharing / division', 'multiple representations', 'in_service', 'upper elementary', 'JMTE_Saxe_2001_Enhancing', 'Geoffrey B. Saxe, Maryl Gearhart and Na''ilah Suad Nasir', '2001', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '62', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Saxe_2001_Enhancing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40189, 'Teachers inappropriately apply prior knowledge and properties of real-valued vectors to complex numbers, leading to cognitive conflicts when interpreting the geometric division of complex numbers.', 'A teacher attempts to divide complex numbers geometrically by thinking it is the ''cross product'' or by stating that ''dividing a vector by another vector is the same as repeated subtraction''.', 'algebraic', 'complex numbers', 'vector representations', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Karakok_2015_Secondary', 'Gulden Karakok, Hortensia Soto-Johnson, Stephenie Anderson Dyben', '2015', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '341, 344, 345', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Karakok_2015_Secondary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40190, 'Teachers hold a preconceptual conception of the exponential form of complex numbers, relying entirely on syntactic exponent rules rather than understanding the geometric meaning or what the symbols represent.', 'When verifying the division of complex numbers in exponential form, a teacher explains the process by saying ''subtract the exponents'' and they ''factored out the letter i'', missing the connection to magnitudes and angles.', 'algebraic', 'complex numbers', 'exponential form', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Karakok_2015_Secondary', 'Gulden Karakok, Hortensia Soto-Johnson, Stephenie Anderson Dyben', '2015', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '339', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Karakok_2015_Secondary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40191, 'Teachers initially view the arithmetic of complex numbers purely through procedural algebraic rules, lacking any geometric interpretation of the operations.', 'Teachers explain that to teach complex number operations, they tell students to ''just add and subtract like terms'' and to ''FOIL it out''.', 'algebraic', 'complex numbers', 'arithmetic operations', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Karakok_2015_Secondary', 'Gulden Karakok, Hortensia Soto-Johnson, Stephenie Anderson Dyben', '2015', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '334', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Karakok_2015_Secondary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40192, 'Teachers struggle to accept that equal-sized fractional parts of a whole can be equivalent in area but different in shape (non-congruent), initially believing that differing side lengths preclude parts from being the same size.', 'The reason why I was getting stuck was that my mind said ''that the top of the rectangle was longer than the side of the rectangle, so how were they going to produce the same size?''', 'fraction', 'equipartitioning', 'equivalence of non-congruent parts', 'in_service', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Wilson_2014_Teachers''', 'P. Holt Wilson, Paola Sztajn, Cyndi Edgington, Jere Confrey', '2014', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '164, 165, 166', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Wilson_2014_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40193, 'When sharing multiple wholes, learners and teachers exhibit ambiguity regarding the referent unit, confusing the fractional amount relative to a single whole with the fractional amount relative to the entire collection of wholes.', 'When examining a student''s answer of ''one third'' for sharing 2 pizzas among 3 friends, a teacher points out the ambiguity by asking, ''One third of what?''', 'fraction', 'equipartitioning', 'referent unit', 'in_service', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Wilson_2014_Teachers''', 'P. Holt Wilson, Paola Sztajn, Cyndi Edgington, Jere Confrey', '2014', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '166, 167', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Wilson_2014_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40194, 'Prospective teachers often accept a set of procedural steps or calculational explanations as evidence of a child''s conceptual understanding.', 'A PST states, "Erin''s process of solving is more advanced, by finding a common denominator it shows her understanding of fractions," when the child only listed algorithmic steps.', 'fraction', 'comparing fractions', 'common denominators', 'PST', 'elementary school', 'JMTE_Dyson_2013_Prospective', 'Tonya Gau Bartell • Corey Webel • Brian Bowen • Nancy Dyson', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '59, 66, 68, 70', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Dyson_2013_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40195, 'Prospective teachers equate surface-level conceptual features, such as drawing a diagram or using conceptual vocabulary like "rebundle", with conceptual understanding even if the student''s mathematical logic is flawed.', 'A PST argues a child understands fractions because the child drew a diagram, even though the child used different-sized wholes to compare the fractions.', 'fraction', 'comparing fractions', 'conceptual features', 'PST', 'elementary school', 'JMTE_Dyson_2013_Prospective', 'Tonya Gau Bartell • Corey Webel • Brian Bowen • Nancy Dyson', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '65, 66, 71', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Dyson_2013_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40196, 'Prospective teachers project their own procedural learning experiences onto students, assuming a student conceptually understands a topic simply because the student used the exact same method the PST was taught.', '"In my mind, they''re the same thing... because I understand what she''s doing and I was taught the same way so when she does the lowest common denominator I''m like, oh, that''s what I did, too."', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'common denominators', 'PST', 'elementary school', 'JMTE_Dyson_2013_Prospective', 'Tonya Gau Bartell • Corey Webel • Brian Bowen • Nancy Dyson', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '71', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Dyson_2013_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40197, 'Prospective teachers demonstrate misunderstandings about the relative size of fractions by erroneously concluding that a fraction with a smaller denominator is larger because its pieces are larger, ignoring the numerator.', '"The larger fraction is 5/6 because as a whole, the block that is divided 6 times is larger... The pieces that are in the six-piece block are larger... So when the five blocks are colored in, a greater area is consumed than if six blocks of seven were shaded in."', 'fraction', 'comparing fractions', 'relative size of fractions', 'PST', 'elementary school', 'JMTE_Dyson_2013_Prospective', 'Tonya Gau Bartell • Corey Webel • Brian Bowen • Nancy Dyson', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '62, 63', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Dyson_2013_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40198, 'Prospective teachers attempt to compare fractions by drawing models but fail to use the same-sized whole, leading to incorrect comparisons.', 'A PST draws two unaligned, differently-sized rectangles, partitioning one into sixths and one into sevenths, incorrectly showing 6/7 as physically longer than 5/6.', 'fraction', 'comparing fractions', 'same-sized whole', 'PST', 'elementary school', 'JMTE_Dyson_2013_Prospective', 'Tonya Gau Bartell • Corey Webel • Brian Bowen • Nancy Dyson', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '63, 64', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Dyson_2013_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40199, 'Prospective teachers hold misconceptions about multiplication of fractions, specifically struggling to write appropriate story problems that model the operation.', 'Writing inappropriate multiplication story problems for the expression 2/9 * 3/5.', 'fraction', 'fraction multiplication', 'story problems', 'PST', 'elementary school', 'JMTE_Dyson_2013_Prospective', 'Tonya Gau Bartell • Corey Webel • Brian Bowen • Nancy Dyson', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '64', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Dyson_2013_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40200, 'A student incorrectly compares two fractions using a diagram by treating the number of pieces shaded as whole numbers, ignoring the relationship to the whole.', '"In the two sets above you can see that 7 pieces is greater than 6 pieces, so 7/8 is larger than 6/7."', 'fraction', 'comparing fractions', 'number of pieces vs fraction size', 'PST', 'elementary school', 'JMTE_Dyson_2013_Prospective', 'Tonya Gau Bartell • Corey Webel • Brian Bowen • Nancy Dyson', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '66, 76', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Dyson_2013_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40201, 'Students may exhibit ''content universe fixation,'' artificially restricting the range of possible numbers (e.g., only considering natural numbers greater than zero) when solving open-ended arithmetic problems.', 'When asked to find two numbers whose sum is 18, students may miss 18 and 0, or they may not think of fractions because they do not consider the possibility of non-natural numbers.', 'whole_number', 'addition', 'number domains', 'mixed', 'elementary and secondary', 'JMTE_Levenson_2013_Tasks', 'Esther Levenson', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'passing', '271', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Levenson_2013_Tasks.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40202, 'Students may exhibit ''algorithmic fixation,'' rigidly adhering to an initially successful but inefficient algorithm instead of recognizing simpler mental math methods.', 'A student who is requested to calculate 20 x 20 resorts to the long multiplication algorithm, though it is clearly unnecessary in this case.', 'whole_number', 'multiplication', 'algorithmic efficiency', 'mixed', 'elementary and secondary', 'JMTE_Levenson_2013_Tasks', 'Esther Levenson', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'passing', '271', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Levenson_2013_Tasks.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40203, 'Teachers tend to approach mathematical tasks involving different representations by immediately applying procedural algebraic manipulations (treatments) to ''solve'' for standard equations, rather than anticipating or discerning the deep-level structural and geometric properties (conversions).', 'A teacher notes, ''There are items, in which I am not sure. I should really solve them… It is not a hyperbola. It is a straight line.'', demonstrating reliance on algebraic verification rather than structural anticipation.', 'geometric', 'conic sections', 'loci of points and representations', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Koichu_2016_Effects', 'Boris Koichu, Orit Zaslavsky, Lea Dolev', '2016', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'mixed', 'primary', '359, 360, 362, 363', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Koichu_2016_Effects.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40204, 'Teachers may incorrectly classify verbally described loci of points by jumping to conclusions based on isolated ''key words'' (e.g., ''ratio'') without paying attention to the specific generating elements and their spatial relationships (e.g., whether a point lies on a given line).', 'A teacher identifies an item as a hyperbola solely by noting: ''For instance, here and here there are directrix, focus and ratio. So it is a hyperbola'', incorrectly categorizing an ellipse. Another teacher later reflects, ''we missed that if the point is on the line the ratio is not important, it cannot be hyperbola or ellipse''.', 'geometric', 'conic sections', 'generating elements and locus definitions', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Koichu_2016_Effects', 'Boris Koichu, Orit Zaslavsky, Lea Dolev', '2016', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '363, 364', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Koichu_2016_Effects.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40205, 'A teacher provides an inexact definition of circumference and fails to unpack the mathematical relationship for pi, reducing the lesson to a non-mathematical craft activity.', 'Defining circumference as "how far around you can go on a circle" without specifying the boundary line, and using paper "pies" without examining the ratio of circumference to diameter.', 'geometric', 'circles', 'circumference and pi', 'in_service', 'second to sixth', 'JMTE_Project_2011_Measuring', 'Learning Mathematics for Teaching Project', '2011', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '25, 26', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Project_2011_Measuring.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40206, 'A teacher introduces an incomplete and imprecise definition of probability.', '"Probability. Just because there''s more, you''re more than likely that''s what you''re going to pick."', 'probability', 'theoretical probability', 'defining probability', 'in_service', 'second to sixth', 'JMTE_Project_2011_Measuring', 'Learning Mathematics for Teaching Project', '2011', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '36, 38', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Project_2011_Measuring.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40207, 'A teacher imprecisely uses geometric terminology by referring to the wedge-shaped sectors of a circular spinner as squares.', '"Everybody color in two more squares. Color in two more squares."', 'geometric', '2D shapes', 'sectors vs squares', 'in_service', 'second to sixth', 'JMTE_Project_2011_Measuring', 'Learning Mathematics for Teaching Project', '2011', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '38', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Project_2011_Measuring.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40208, 'A teacher accepts a student''s literal restatement of experimental results as a valid mathematical explanation for the physical characteristics of a spinner.', 'A student explains a spinner has "many" divisions because "There''s three noes and zero yeses," and the teacher praises the answer without probing for the mathematical mechanism.', 'probability', 'experimental probability', 'mathematical explanation', 'in_service', 'second to sixth', 'JMTE_Project_2011_Measuring', 'Learning Mathematics for Teaching Project', '2011', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '39, 40', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Project_2011_Measuring.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40209, 'A student incorrectly represents a two-digit number using base-10 blocks by ignoring place value.', 'Representing 51 as five little unit cubes and one little unit cube.', 'whole_number', 'place value', 'base-10 blocks representation', 'in_service', 'second to sixth', 'JMTE_Project_2011_Measuring', 'Learning Mathematics for Teaching Project', '2011', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '34', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Project_2011_Measuring.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40210, 'A teacher introduces a geometric term using an inaccurate physical referent.', 'Introducing the term "edge" by pointing to an edge of a table that is clearly rounded.', 'geometric', '3D shapes', 'edges', 'in_service', 'second to sixth', 'JMTE_Project_2011_Measuring', 'Learning Mathematics for Teaching Project', '2011', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '36', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Project_2011_Measuring.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40211, 'Prospective teachers use a direct translation strategy, jumping immediately to arithmetic by extracting numbers and units from the problem text without comprehending the real-world scenario.', 'Modeling the ''Log Problem'' by dividing 12 seconds by 3 pieces to get 4 seconds per piece, then multiplying by 4 pieces to get 16 seconds, ignoring that cuts are what take time.', 'proportional', 'word problems', 'intentionally misleading problems', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Boote_2018_Abc', 'Stacy K. Boote, David N. Boote', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '106, 107, 108', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Boote_2018_Abc.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40212, 'Prospective teachers demonstrate pervasive imprecision with units, rates, calculations, and the equals sign, often substituting expected values into intermediate calculations or dropping units inconsistently.', 'Writing the rate as ''3 pieces / 12 sec'' but calculating the answer as 4, or stating ''3 pieces = 2 cuts''.', 'measurement', 'units and rates', 'dimensional analysis and equality', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Boote_2018_Abc', 'Stacy K. Boote, David N. Boote', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '113, 114', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Boote_2018_Abc.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40213, 'Prospective teachers use multiple models but base their second model on their flawed first model, directly translating the same incorrect numbers rather than returning to the problem text to check comprehension.', 'Drawing a diagram partitioned into 3 pieces taking 4 seconds each, then writing an arithmetic equation that mirrors the same flawed logic.', 'proportional', 'mathematical modeling', 'multiple representations', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Boote_2018_Abc', 'Stacy K. Boote, David N. Boote', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '109, 110', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Boote_2018_Abc.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40214, 'Prospective teachers incorrectly take a teacher''s clear mathematical explanation and students'' compliant behaviors (such as nodding) as sufficient evidence that the students have actually achieved the mathematical learning goal.', 'On the pretest, most PTs made either a weak or strong positive claim that students had achieved the learning goal based on an excerpt where the teacher provided a good explanation of the concepts involved in the common denominator strategy while students nodded.', 'fraction', 'evaluating student understanding', 'teacher explanations vs. student thinking', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Spitzer_2011_Developing', 'Sandy M. Spitzer, Christine M. Phelps, James E. R. Beyers, Delayne Y. Johnson, Elizabeth M. Sieminski', '2011', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '70, 76, 77', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Spitzer_2011_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40215, 'Prospective teachers over-attribute understanding to correct answers, treating a student''s correct response derived from an alternative or irrelevant strategy (like using a calculator or a money analogy) as evidence that the student achieved the target conceptual learning goal.', 'When assessing if students understood the common denominator strategy, PTs highly rated a student who arrived at the correct answer by using a calculator (converting 1/4 and 2/10 to decimals .25 and .2) or by using an analogy to dimes and quarters.', 'fraction', 'comparing fractions', 'common denominators / evaluating evidence', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Spitzer_2011_Developing', 'Sandy M. Spitzer, Christine M. Phelps, James E. R. Beyers, Delayne Y. Johnson, Elizabeth M. Sieminski', '2011', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '70, 77, 78', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Spitzer_2011_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40216, 'Prospective teachers equate evidence of procedural fluency with conceptual understanding, accepting a student''s correct step-by-step procedural explanation as proof of deep conceptual grasp of the underlying ideas.', 'When a student correctly compared two fractions by listing the steps taken to find a common denominator, 88% of PTs on the pretest strongly concluded the student had achieved the conceptual learning goal.', 'fraction', 'comparing fractions', 'procedural vs conceptual understanding', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Spitzer_2011_Developing', 'Sandy M. Spitzer, Christine M. Phelps, James E. R. Beyers, Delayne Y. Johnson, Elizabeth M. Sieminski', '2011', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '70, 80, 81', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Spitzer_2011_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40217, 'Students provide overly precise, unrounded decimal answers to real-world context problems that require integer or specific rounded values, focusing on completing the calculation rather than ensuring the answer makes sense in the context.', 'When calculating the number of kittens, a student might write ''2.4 kittens'', or for the number of buses needed, a student might write ''Nine-point-two-two-five buses''.', 'decimal', 'rounding and precision', 'contextual problems', 'in_service', 'middle and high school', 'JMTE_Otten_2019_Teachers''', 'Samuel Otten, Lindsay M. Keazer, Ruveyda Karaman', '2019', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '77, 78', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Otten_2019_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40218, 'Students reflexively convert exact fractional answers into decimal approximations, failing to recognize that exact fractional representations are often more mathematically precise.', 'When solving 3x = 2, a student will immediately grab a calculator and give x = 0.6 repeating, or inexactly round to x = 0.6 or 0.7, rather than keeping the exact 2/3.', 'rational', 'representing numbers', 'fractions vs. decimals', 'in_service', 'middle and high school', 'JMTE_Otten_2019_Teachers''', 'Samuel Otten, Lindsay M. Keazer, Ruveyda Karaman', '2019', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '78', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Otten_2019_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40219, 'Students improperly string together steps of an equation using multiple equal signs in one continuous line, equating expressions that are not actually equal.', 'For the equation 2x - 5 = 13, a student writes ''2x - 5 = 13 = 2x = 18 = x = 9'', creating a long string of equal signs.', 'algebraic', 'equations', 'equal sign', 'in_service', 'middle and high school', 'JMTE_Otten_2019_Teachers''', 'Samuel Otten, Lindsay M. Keazer, Ruveyda Karaman', '2019', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '75, 80', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Otten_2019_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40220, 'Students use informal vocabulary to describe mathematical objects, rather than using precise technical terminology expected in the mathematics register.', 'When asked what type of graph a quadratic makes, a student says ''Oh, that''s a U'' instead of ''parabola'' or ''quadratic''.', 'algebraic', 'quadratics', 'vocabulary', 'in_service', 'middle and high school', 'JMTE_Otten_2019_Teachers''', 'Samuel Otten, Lindsay M. Keazer, Ruveyda Karaman', '2019', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '84', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Otten_2019_Teachers''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40221, 'Teachers holding a ''perception-based perspective'' mistakenly assume that mathematical concepts and algorithmic connections exist objectively in physical manipulatives and can be directly ''seen'' by anyone manipulating them. They fail to realize that students must already possess the requisite conceptual structures to perceive these relationships.', 'When Carol, who successfully modeled a division problem partitively with base ten blocks, stated she saw ''None'' (no relationship) between her block method and the long-division algorithm, the teacher expressed disbelief: ''I don''t believe that. I believe that if you would have thought – you would have found some kind of connection.''', 'whole_number', 'division', 'manipulatives vs algorithm', 'in_service', 'fifth grade', 'JMTE_Tzur_2001_Account', 'RON TZUR, MARTIN A. SIMON, KAREN HEINZ and MARGARET KINZEL', '2001', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '240, 242, 243, 244, 245', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Tzur_2001_Account.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40222, 'Students lack a conceptual understanding of the subtraction step in the long-division algorithm, offering procedural or superficial justifications instead of understanding that it represents removing the quantity already distributed into equal groups.', 'When asked to explain the reason for the subtraction step (14 - 14 = 0) in 140 ÷ 7, students responded ''to find the remainder,'' ''to find what is left,'' ''to check the answer,'' or ''to make the problem easier, because the number is smaller.''', 'whole_number', 'division', 'standard algorithm', 'in_service', 'fifth grade', 'JMTE_Tzur_2001_Account', 'RON TZUR, MARTIN A. SIMON, KAREN HEINZ and MARGARET KINZEL', '2001', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '237, 238', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Tzur_2001_Account.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40223, 'Students inappropriately rely on additive thinking and repeated addition when they should be using multiplicative strategies in proportional situations.', 'The children used known values to find the interval value and then added or subtracted in order to find the missing numbers.', 'proportional', 'proportional reasoning', 'additive vs. multiplicative thinking', 'in_service', 'primary (Years 3, 4, and 5)', 'JMTE_Hilton_2018_Primary', 'Annette Hilton, Geoff Hilton', '2018', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Hilton_2018_Primary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40224, 'The teacher conflates a quantitative meaning of angle measure (as the length of a subtended arc measured in proportional units) with a non-quantitative, arithmetic meaning (as a unit-less fractional part or proportion of a circle''s circumference).', 'David explained that measuring an angle involves calculating a ratio of arc length to circumference and claimed the result is unit-less because ''inches cancel inches'', treating angle measure as a mere fraction of the circle rather than a quantified attribute.', 'measurement', 'angle measure', 'meaning of radians and degrees', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Tallman_2018_Angle', 'Michael A. Tallman, Kristin M. Frank', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Tallman_2018_Angle.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40225, 'When a student proposes a shortcut for finding arc length by multiplying the radian measure by the radius, justifying it by stating ''a radian is just a length of a radius'', the teacher bypasses the opportunity to reinforce the conceptual quantitative relationship and instead validates the shortcut purely algebraically.', 'The teacher responds to the student''s suggestion by crossing out the ''2π'' terms in the expression ''0.45/(2π) · (2πr)'', stating ''So these cancel and we can see that''s the reason why.''', 'measurement', 'angle measure', 'arc length and radians', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Tallman_2018_Angle', 'Michael A. Tallman, Kristin M. Frank', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Tallman_2018_Angle.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40226, 'Students struggle with the meaning of zeros appended to the right of a decimal number, with some incorrectly believing that appending zeros makes the number larger, while others believe it has no effect because ''zero plus zero is zero'', lacking conceptual understanding of decimal place value.', 'Marco: ''But when you look at it now, these numbers are bigger... if you take two, three, seven and two, three, seven, zero, then that would be three hundred seventy would that be.''', 'decimal', 'place value', 'appended zeros', 'mixed', 'Grade 6', 'JMTE_Steinbring_1998_Elements', 'HEINZ STEINBRING', '1998', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '164, 165, 178', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Steinbring_1998_Elements.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40227, 'Students interpret the decimal point as a mere separator that divides a quantity into two independent whole numbers (e.g., reading 2.37 km as 2 kilometers and 37 meters) rather than understanding the fractional relation the decimal digits hold to the primary unit.', 'When asked what ''2.37 km'' means, a student answers ''Two kilometers and thirty seven meters'', incorrectly reading the decimal digits ''.37'' as a whole number of meters.', 'decimal', 'meaning of decimals', 'decimal point as a separator', 'mixed', 'Grade 6', 'JMTE_Steinbring_1998_Elements', 'HEINZ STEINBRING', '1998', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '162, 166, 167, 168, 175, 178', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Steinbring_1998_Elements.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40228, 'Students limit their definition of quadrilaterals to only squares and rectangles, failing to recognize that other four-sided figures with non-right angles are also quadrilaterals.', 'Students'' definitions might simply say that quadrilaterals have four sides and four angles, limiting their definitions to only some quadrilaterals and excluding those with nonright angles.', 'geometric', 'quadrilaterals', 'classification and definitions', 'other', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Fuentes_2018_Promoting', 'Sarah Quebec Fuentes, Jingjing Ma', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'passing', '369, 370', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Fuentes_2018_Promoting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40229, 'Students struggle with hierarchical, inclusive geometric definitions, specifically refusing to believe that a square is a special kind of rectangle.', 'Although it is mathematically true that squares are a special kind of rectangle, not all students will be convinced of this... they argue this idea.', 'geometric', 'quadrilaterals', 'inclusive definitions', 'other', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Fuentes_2018_Promoting', 'Sarah Quebec Fuentes, Jingjing Ma', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'passing', '369, 370', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Fuentes_2018_Promoting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40230, 'Students misapply basic algorithmic rules when multiplying fractions, such as incorrectly finding a common denominator, inverting the second fraction, or adding numerators and denominators.', 'Multiplying the numerator and denominator by ignoring the whole part of the mixed fraction (1 1/2 * 1/3 = 1 1/6), or adding the numerators and denominators (2/3 * 3/5 = 5/8).', 'fraction', 'fraction multiplication', 'algorithmic rules', 'PST', 'sixth and seventh grade', 'JMTE_Isiksal_2011_Nature', 'Mine Isiksal, Erdinc Cakiroglu', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '219, 220', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Isiksal_2011_Nature.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40231, 'Students intuitively overgeneralize properties of whole numbers to fractions, expecting the result of operations to be whole numbers without remainders.', 'To find 1/3 of 7, a student might report two as the answer, thinking that the quotient should be a whole number and ignoring the remainder since seven cannot be divided by three evenly.', 'fraction', 'fraction multiplication', 'primitive models of operations', 'PST', 'sixth and seventh grade', 'JMTE_Isiksal_2011_Nature', 'Mine Isiksal, Erdinc Cakiroglu', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '220', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Isiksal_2011_Nature.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40232, 'Students fail to perform operations correctly because they do not internalize or understand the mathematical meaning of the fraction symbol itself.', 'Students cannot perform 7 * 1/3 because they do not know what the fraction 1/3 symbolically represents.', 'fraction', 'fraction concepts', 'fraction symbolism', 'PST', 'sixth and seventh grade', 'JMTE_Isiksal_2011_Nature', 'Mine Isiksal, Erdinc Cakiroglu', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '220, 221', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Isiksal_2011_Nature.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40233, 'Students perform operations incorrectly on word problems because they misunderstand the mathematical language, confusing what information is given with what is being asked.', 'In a problem stating ''she gave 1/2 of it, which was 1 3/4 lt'', the student fails to understand the hidden statement that half of the original quantity is 1 3/4, leading to an incorrect operation.', 'fraction', 'word problems', 'mathematical language', 'PST', 'sixth and seventh grade', 'JMTE_Isiksal_2011_Nature', 'Mine Isiksal, Erdinc Cakiroglu', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '221', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Isiksal_2011_Nature.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40234, 'Prospective teachers fail to correctly interpret the meaning of a remainder in a real-life division context, inappropriately treating a fractional quotient as a literal cut object rather than recognizing the need to round up to accommodate the remaining individuals.', 'When determining the number of tables needed for 102 students (with 22 per table), a teacher states: ''The answer is not a whole number and you cannot cut a table in half to represent a decimal.''', 'whole_number', 'division', 'interpreting remainders', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Lee_2012_Prospective', 'Ji-Eun Lee', '2012', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '441', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Lee_2012_Prospective.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40235, 'A student struggles to generalize the partitive unit fractional scheme to improper fractions, confusing the required denominator (fractional unit) with the remaining number of parts needed to reach the target amount.', 'When asked to make thirty-seven tenths using fraction software, a student creates three ten-sticks to get thirty, but to get the remaining seven tenths, she divides the next stick into sevenths instead of tenths.', 'fraction', 'improper fractions', 'partitive unit fractional scheme', 'in_service', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Norton_2008_Teaching', 'Anderson Hassell Norton, Andrea McCloskey', '2008', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '291, 292', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Norton_2008_Teaching.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40236, 'Students incorrectly compute products of multiples of ten by falling back on habitual calculation patterns and ignoring proper place value scaling.', 'Ms. A asks how much is 90 70''s and students write 630 out of habit (they are used to beginning with problems like 9 70''s).', 'whole_number', 'multiplication', 'multiples of ten', 'in_service', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Norton_2008_Teaching', 'Anderson Hassell Norton, Andrea McCloskey', '2008', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '293', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Norton_2008_Teaching.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40237, 'When asked to solve the handshake problem, some prospective teachers attempt to rely on memorized calculator procedures they have forgotten rather than reasoning conceptually.', '"now is this a combination or a permutation? I forget which button to push."', 'combinatorial', 'combinations', 'handshake problem', 'other', 'K-8', 'JMTE_Appova_2017_Expert', 'Aina Appova, Cynthia E. Taylor', '2017', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Appova_2017_Expert.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40238, 'A student categorizes right triangles based on their physical resemblance to real-world objects rather than formal geometric properties.', '"There''s one kid, who has ramps, and he grouped certain triangles that looked like ramps that have right triangles."', 'geometric', '2D shapes', 'triangles', 'other', 'K-8', 'JMTE_Appova_2017_Expert', 'Aina Appova, Cynthia E. Taylor', '2017', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'productive', 'passing', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Appova_2017_Expert.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40239, 'Prospective teachers rely on rote procedures and terminology when teaching subtraction, giving directions without understanding the underlying place value concepts.', 'PTs "blindly saying [to K-8 students] ''cross out this'' and ''borrow'' from here."', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'regrouping', 'other', 'K-8', 'JMTE_Appova_2017_Expert', 'Aina Appova, Cynthia E. Taylor', '2017', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Appova_2017_Expert.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40240, 'A student incorrectly converts a fraction to a percent by treating the numerator directly as the percentage, ignoring the proportional relationship with the denominator.', 'When discussing the fraction 50/150, the student states, ''It is 50%''.', 'percent', 'fraction to percent conversion', 'part-to-whole relationships', 'in_service', 'middle school', 'JMTE_McDuffie_2018_Middle', 'Amy Roth McDuffie, Jeffrey Choppin, Corey Drake, Jon D. Davis, Jennifer Brown', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'passing', '556', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_McDuffie_2018_Middle.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40241, 'Students incorrectly assume that finding the side length of a square from its area involves dividing the area by two, overgeneralizing from the specific case where an area of 4 yields a side length of 2.', 'When given a square with an area of 9, a student suggests dividing it by 2 to get a side length of 4.5.', 'measurement', 'area of a square', 'square roots', 'in_service', 'middle school', 'JMTE_McDuffie_2018_Middle', 'Amy Roth McDuffie, Jeffrey Choppin, Corey Drake, Jon D. Davis, Jennifer Brown', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '557', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_McDuffie_2018_Middle.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40242, 'Students make errors in algebraic syntax, changing the meaning of a formula depending on how numbers and symbols are positioned.', 'A student writes C = t/4+t^2, which the teacher points out could mean C = (t/4) + t^2, C = t/(4+t^2), or (t/(4+t))^2 depending on positioning.', 'algebraic', 'algebraic expressions', 'syntax and positioning', 'PST', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Koirala_2008_Development', 'Hari P. Koirala, Marsha Davis, Peter Johnson', '2008', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'passing', '132', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Koirala_2008_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40243, 'A student incorrectly solves a difference problem by starting with two equal groups of 15 and transferring the total target difference (8) from one group to the other, failing to realize this creates a difference of 16.', 'Starting with 15 and 15, Abe moves 8 days from the dry column to the rainy column, resulting in 23 rainy days and 7 dry days, which has a difference of 16.', 'whole_number', 'word problems', 'difference', 'in_service', 'middle school', 'JMTE_Baxter_2010_Social', 'Juliet A. Baxter, Steven Williams', '2010', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'productive', 'primary', '20, 21', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Baxter_2010_Social.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40244, 'Students incorrectly apply what they call the ''Pythagorean pattern'' by finding the area of the right triangle formed by the radius rather than finding the length of the hypotenuse.', 'The student noted that the length of each leg of the triangle was 2, and explained that they had multiplied them together and divided by 2.', 'geometric', 'Pythagorean theorem', 'hypotenuse vs area', 'in_service', 'middle school', 'JMTE_Baxter_2010_Social', 'Juliet A. Baxter, Steven Williams', '2010', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '16', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Baxter_2010_Social.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40245, 'A student questions if 4/8 is equivalent to 1/2 by incorrectly reasoning that since 2 is half of 4, the equivalent fraction should be 2/4.', 'A child questions this answer and says ''isn''t 2 half of 4? Shouldn''t it be 2/4?''', 'fraction', 'equivalent fractions', 'halving', 'mixed', 'elementary', 'JMTE_KinserTraut_2018_Shared', 'Jennifer Y. Kinser-Traut, Erin E. Turner', '2018', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_KinserTraut_2018_Shared.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40246, 'Students struggle with the general algebraic form of functions, often misinterpreting symbols and function notation, translating graphs in the wrong direction, and experiencing difficulty when quadratics or polynomials are not provided in turning point form.', 'Students performed poorer on questions in which functions and equations were expressed in general form... Helen noted these misconceptions as likely to occur in grades 9 and 10: ''confusion with symbols, and reading set and function notation, difficulties with asymptotes and domain/range of these graphs, translating in wrong direction, what to do when not in TP (turning point) form''.', 'algebraic', 'functions and graphs', 'transformations and general form', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Vale_2011_Connecting', 'Colleen Vale, Alasdair McAndrew, Siva Krishnan', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'passing', '206, 207', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Vale_2011_Connecting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40247, 'Students over-rely on calculators when performing algebraic and fraction-based operations, which leads them to provide approximate decimal answers rather than exact values (like surds or fractions) and causes them to carelessly omit necessary brackets in algebraic expressions.', 'Carmel noted that students probably depended on calculators contributing to errors that students were making by providing approximate solutions instead of exact solutions and the omitting of brackets in solutions as reported by examiners.', 'algebraic', 'exact solutions', 'calculator dependence', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Vale_2011_Connecting', 'Colleen Vale, Alasdair McAndrew, Siva Krishnan', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'passing', '207, 208', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Vale_2011_Connecting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40248, 'Prospective teachers conflate variables with unknowns, treating dynamic variable quantities as fixed, static quantities that need to be solved for.', 'Creating a story for 3(x - 2) where the variable represents a fixed total number of cookies in a box, or repeatedly referring to the variable ''x'' as an ''unknown'' that they ''are trying to solve for''.', 'algebraic', 'variables and unknowns', 'dynamic vs. static quantities', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Hohensee_2017_Preparing', 'Charles Hohensee', '2017', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '245, 246', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Hohensee_2017_Preparing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40249, 'Prospective teachers struggle to bracket their knowledge of formal algebra, relying on literal symbols, formal equations, and algebraic jargon instead of pre-symbolic reasoning when tasked with creating elementary-appropriate explanations.', 'Writing the equation y = 3/4x + 2 before being able to draw an informal diagram, or explaining a solution by saying ''subtracting 4z from both sides'' rather than referring to the quantities in the story.', 'algebraic', 'pre-symbolic algebraic reasoning', 'bracketing formal algebra', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Hohensee_2017_Preparing', 'Charles Hohensee', '2017', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '246, 247, 248', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Hohensee_2017_Preparing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40250, 'Prospective teachers inaccurately represent subtraction involving variables or unknowns in informal diagrams, such as by adding units and then crossing them out, which misrepresents the original quantity.', 'Representing x - 2 by drawing ''x'' and then an additional 2 units which are crossed out, inadvertently representing x + 2 - 2 instead of 2 units less than x.', 'algebraic', 'pre-symbolic algebraic reasoning', 'informal representations of subtraction', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Hohensee_2017_Preparing', 'Charles Hohensee', '2017', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '249, 250, 251', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Hohensee_2017_Preparing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40251, 'Prospective teachers find it challenging to articulate how many and which quantities are involved when identifying functional relationships in algebraic expressions.', 'When given the expression 3(x - 2), a group identifies ''the number of groups'' and ''the amount in each group'', failing to relate the varying input amount to the total amount.', 'algebraic', 'functional relationships', 'identifying related quantities', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Hohensee_2017_Preparing', 'Charles Hohensee', '2017', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '243, 244', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Hohensee_2017_Preparing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40252, 'Prospective teachers experience a productive perturbation (meaningful insight) when discovering the relational interpretation of the equal sign, having previously held only an operational interpretation.', 'A teacher notes they always thought of the equal sign the ''operational way'' and had to switch from ''that operational side of it'' to ''being able to keep things balanced''.', 'algebraic', 'equal sign', 'relational vs. operational interpretation', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Hohensee_2017_Preparing', 'Charles Hohensee', '2017', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'productive', 'primary', '241, 242', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Hohensee_2017_Preparing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40253, 'Students struggle with unit iteration when measuring an object longer than their measuring tool, specifically not knowing where to start the next measurement or how to keep track of the number of iterations.', 'Students began disagreeing about how to start a new measurement space or how to keep track of the number of times they used their measuring tool. Some students decided to draw a pencil line or put down a finger to keep their place.', 'measurement', 'linear measurement', 'unit iteration', 'mixed', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Steele_2001_Interfacing', 'DIANA F. STEELE', '2001', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '146', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Steele_2001_Interfacing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40254, 'When determining the perimeter of a rectangle, students incorrectly add only the length and the width together (two sides) instead of calculating the distance all the way around (four sides).', 'A bunch of you added 10 + 6. Is that all the way around?', 'measurement', 'perimeter', 'rectangles', 'mixed', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Steele_2001_Interfacing', 'DIANA F. STEELE', '2001', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '153', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Steele_2001_Interfacing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40255, 'Students trained primarily with procedural manipulation rules for equations view solving as a purely mechanical application requiring no justification, leading to an inability to model real-life problems or check the plausibility of their solutions.', 'Students experience a ''total absence of control of the exactness or plausibility of solutions'' and fail to recognize ''an equation as a tool to model real life problems''.', 'algebraic', 'linear equations', 'procedural vs. structural meaning', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Mellone_2011_Influence', 'Maria Mellone', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '276, 277, 281', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Mellone_2011_Influence.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40256, 'The teacher struggles to connect students'' intuitive, context-driven method of solving linear equations (inverting arithmetic operations) with the traditional algorithmic approach (applying equivalence principles), creating a temporary cognitive conflict.', 'After students successfully solve an equation by ''going the opposite way'', the teacher experiences confusion and asks the class, ''How can the method of opposite operations be connected with the equivalence principles?''', 'algebraic', 'linear equations', 'connecting solution methods', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Mellone_2011_Influence', 'Maria Mellone', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'mixed', 'primary', '281, 282', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Mellone_2011_Influence.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40257, 'Prospective teachers attempt to explain fraction division by drawing the dividend and divisor as separate, disconnected entities, failing to conceptually model the division operation itself.', 'Drawing two lines for the dividend (2) and another line for the divisor (3/4), then quitting without knowing how to connect them.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'visual representations', 'PST', 'K-8', 'JMTE_Charalambous_2015_Working', 'Charalambos Y. Charalambous', '2015', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '435, 436', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Charalambous_2015_Working.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40258, 'Prospective teachers struggle to correctly interpret the remainder in fraction division, confusing the relative units (the divisor unit versus the original dividend whole).', 'After showing two groups of 3/4 within 2, the teacher attempts to explain the remaining 2/3 of the quotient by drawing separate rectangles partitioned into thirds, thereby ''losing the fourths'' from the original context.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'interpreting remainders and relative units', 'PST', 'K-8', 'JMTE_Charalambous_2015_Working', 'Charalambos Y. Charalambous', '2015', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '438', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Charalambous_2015_Working.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40259, 'Prospective teachers mistakenly believe that teaching fraction division conceptually with representations is impossible, instead relying entirely on rote procedural steps.', 'Outlining the steps to find the reciprocal and multiply across, and then arguing that she could not use a representation to explain this operation.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'algorithms vs conceptual understanding', 'PST', 'K-8', 'JMTE_Charalambous_2015_Working', 'Charalambos Y. Charalambous', '2015', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '437', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Charalambous_2015_Working.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40260, 'Teachers assimilate the concept of consensus building into a traditional ''show and tell'' schema, acting as the sole mathematical authority who validates student answers rather than facilitating critical student-to-student evaluation.', 'After a student presents a model for multiplying a fraction by a whole number, the teacher immediately endorses it as ''very effective'' and asks convergent questions to check if other students understood her interpretation, rather than asking students to evaluate it.', 'fraction', 'fraction multiplication', 'consensus building and representations', 'in_service', '4th and 5th grade', 'JMTE_Inoue_2011_Zen', 'Noriyuki Inoue', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '8, 9, 10', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Inoue_2011_Zen.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40261, 'Students incorrectly calculate the area of a triangle by multiplying the base and height without dividing by two when attempting to decompose a parallelogram into a rectangle and two triangles.', 'To find the area of a parallelogram, students decompose it into a rectangle and two triangles. For a triangle with width 3 and height 4, they multiply to get 12 and state ''we didn''t have to divide by 2''.', 'geometric', 'area', 'parallelograms and triangles', 'in_service', '4th and 5th grade', 'JMTE_Inoue_2011_Zen', 'Noriyuki Inoue', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'productive', 'primary', '13, 14', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Inoue_2011_Zen.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40262, 'Teachers erroneously believe that the primary goal of an inquiry lesson is simply for students to generate their own answers to an open-ended problem, failing to identify a specific mathematical realization (kizuki) to target.', 'A teacher asks ''How do you know if a number is a prime or composite number?'' without a focused goal, resulting in students randomly testing numbers and producing strategies so diverse that consensus cannot be built.', 'whole_number', 'prime and composite numbers', 'instructional goals', 'in_service', '4th and 5th grade', 'JMTE_Inoue_2011_Zen', 'Noriyuki Inoue', '2011', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '10, 11', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Inoue_2011_Zen.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40263, 'Students inappropriately use a ''distance from the whole'' (gap) strategy to compare fractions, concluding that the fraction requiring fewer additional pieces to make a whole is larger while failing to account for the size of those pieces.', 'When comparing 1/4 and 1/6, the student claims 1/4 is bigger ''Because it''s closer to four than six'', indicating that 1 is closer to 4 than 1 is to 6.', 'fraction', 'comparing fractions', 'distance from the whole / gap strategy', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Shaughnessy_2018_Complementary', 'Meghan Shaughnessy, Timothy A. Boerst, Susanna Owens Farmer', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Shaughnessy_2018_Complementary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40264, 'When comparing fractions with a common numerator, a student mistakenly concludes that the fraction with the larger denominator is the greater fraction, misapplying whole number magnitude logic.', 'When comparing 6/14 and 6/15, the student incorrectly states that 6/15 is larger ''Because 15 is greater than 14.''', 'fraction', 'comparing fractions', 'common numerators', 'PST', 'elementary', 'JMTE_Shaughnessy_2018_Complementary', 'Meghan Shaughnessy, Timothy A. Boerst, Susanna Owens Farmer', '2018', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Shaughnessy_2018_Complementary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40265, 'A teacher erroneously attempts to find the sum of interior angles of a rectangle by drawing auxiliary lines from an arbitrary point on an edge, creating three triangles and incorrectly concluding the interior angle sum is 540 degrees.', 'Drawing lines from a point on the top edge of a rectangle to the two bottom vertices, concluding there are three triangles and thus the interior angle sum is 540 degrees (180 x 3).', 'geometric', 'polygons', 'interior angle sum', 'in_service', 'K-8', 'JMTE_FerriniMundy_2007_Building', 'Joan Ferrini-Mundy, Gail Burrill, William H. Schmidt', '2007', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '321, 322', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_FerriniMundy_2007_Building.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40266, 'When creating algebraic equations to represent a sequence of fractional operations, teachers notice the variable ''x'' is inconsistently used to represent different intermediate quantities rather than the original whole.', 'In equations such as 3/4x = 24; 2/3(3/4x) = 24; 3x = 24, the ''x'' did not necessarily represent the same thing.', 'algebraic', 'equations', 'variables and units', 'in_service', 'K-8', 'JMTE_FerriniMundy_2007_Building', 'Joan Ferrini-Mundy, Gail Burrill, William H. Schmidt', '2007', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '316', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_FerriniMundy_2007_Building.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40267, 'Students may confuse angle measurement with area when a static understanding of angles is used, such as checking if one angle ''fits inside'' another.', '', 'measurement', 'angle measurement', 'static angles vs area', 'in_service', 'K-8', 'JMTE_FerriniMundy_2007_Building', 'Joan Ferrini-Mundy, Gail Burrill, William H. Schmidt', '2007', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '320', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_FerriniMundy_2007_Building.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40268, 'Teachers hold varied assumptions and partial knowledge about the base-ten number system, such as incorrectly asserting that base-10 was historically chosen because of unique mathematical patterns inherent to the number 10, rather than recognizing it as an arbitrary convention.', 'One teacher claimed that base 10 was chosen for the conventional system because of the patterns inherent in a system based on 10.', 'whole_number', 'base-ten system', 'origins and structure', 'in_service', 'elementary and middle school', 'JMTE_Remillard_2002_Supporting', 'Janine T. Remillard, Pamela Kaye Geist', '2002', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '22', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Remillard_2002_Supporting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40269, 'Children struggled to understand the size of fractions and improper fractions, believing that 1 is greater than 4/4 and that 8/5 is the same as 5/8.', 'One child claimed that 8/5 and 5/8 were the same, and another child had difficulty drawing a representation for 3/2.', 'fraction', 'improper fractions', 'magnitude and comparison', 'PST', 'elementary school', 'JMTE_Ambrose_2004_Initiating', 'Rebecca Ambrose', '2004', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'productive', 'primary', '102, 103, 104', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Ambrose_2004_Initiating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40270, 'Preservice teachers defaulted to presenting standard symbolic procedures, such as finding a common denominator for fraction addition, which confused students and lacked conceptual support.', 'I totally confused her so much... I didn''t even think of showing her with the blocks. I was just showing her the procedure way.', 'fraction', 'fraction addition', 'common denominators', 'PST', 'elementary school', 'JMTE_Ambrose_2004_Initiating', 'Rebecca Ambrose', '2004', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '113, 114', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Ambrose_2004_Initiating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40271, 'Prospective teachers initially focused on the production of correct answers using standard algorithms rather than conceptual understanding, believing standard procedures were mathematically safer.', 'Nine preferred that students exclusively use the standard algorithm for multidigit subtraction because it is ''less prone to error, faster or more of a sure thing''.', 'whole_number', 'multidigit subtraction', 'standard algorithm', 'PST', 'elementary school', 'JMTE_Ambrose_2004_Initiating', 'Rebecca Ambrose', '2004', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '112', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Ambrose_2004_Initiating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40272, 'Young children simply added the two numbers in a story problem, regardless of the semantic action or mathematical operation actually required by the problem''s context.', 'They tended simply to add the two numbers in the problems posed, regardless of the action in the problem.', 'whole_number', 'story problems', 'operations', 'PST', 'elementary school', 'JMTE_Ambrose_2004_Initiating', 'Rebecca Ambrose', '2004', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '115', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Ambrose_2004_Initiating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40273, 'Students inappropriately transfer the commutative property from addition and multiplication to division and subtraction because they accept the laws without properly associating them with the concrete meanings of the operations.', 'children accept, for example, the commutative law of addition or multiplication but failing to properly associate it with the meanings of these operations, they just go ahead and transfer this property to division or subtraction', 'whole_number', 'arithmetic operations', 'commutative property', 'PST', 'secondary or high school', 'JMTE_Moreira_2008_Academic', 'Plinio C. Moreira, Maria M. David', '2008', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '28', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Moreira_2008_Academic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40274, 'Students mistakenly link the operation of multiplication exclusively to the idea of ''increasing,'' an association that is valid for natural numbers but not for rational numbers.', 'When working with models for multiplication in Q (e.g., the area model), one is led to reconsider the link between multiplication and ''increasing'', an association valid in N but not in Q.', 'rational', 'multiplication', 'magnitude and scaling', 'PST', 'secondary or high school', 'JMTE_Moreira_2008_Academic', 'Plinio C. Moreira, Maria M. David', '2008', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '32', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Moreira_2008_Academic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40275, 'Students conceptualize infinite non-periodic decimals as ''infinite numbers'', believing that infinite digits represent a never-ending process and erroneously identifying the corresponding number with an infinitely large quantity.', 'For many students, the infinite decimals are ''infinite numbers'' and what lies behind this misconception is the idea that infinite digits represent a never-ending process and this leads to identify the corresponding number with an infinitely large quantity.', 'decimal', 'infinite non-periodic decimals', 'irrational numbers', 'PST', 'secondary or high school', 'JMTE_Moreira_2008_Academic', 'Plinio C. Moreira, Maria M. David', '2008', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '34, 35', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Moreira_2008_Academic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40276, 'Students intuitively find negative numbers unacceptable and inappropriately associate them with irrationality, similar to how they view an infinite number of decimal digits as a never-reaching total accomplishment.', 'a negative number [...] is intuitively unacceptable, and so it is associated with irrationality. An infinite number of decimal digits means something which never reaches a total accomplishment and this also is easily associated with irrationality', 'other', 'irrational numbers', 'defining irrationality', 'PST', 'secondary or high school', 'JMTE_Moreira_2008_Academic', 'Plinio C. Moreira, Maria M. David', '2008', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'passing', '35', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Moreira_2008_Academic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40277, 'Students reject irrational numbers as valid numbers because they do not fit their established schema that a number must be a ratio of integers.', 'If the students'' idea of number, in the widest sense, is a ratio of integers, an irrational, not being a ratio of integers, does not qualify to be a number.', 'other', 'irrational numbers', 'number definition', 'PST', 'secondary or high school', 'JMTE_Moreira_2008_Academic', 'Plinio C. Moreira, Maria M. David', '2008', 'Journal of Mathematics Teacher Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '35', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Moreira_2008_Academic.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40278, 'Students mistakenly move the decimal point down into the remainder during decimal division, demonstrating a lack of understanding of place value and regrouping.', 'In the division problem 11.5 / 5, a student correctly calculates the quotient 2.3 but writes the remainder as 1.5 by bringing the decimal point straight down.', 'decimal', 'decimal division', 'place value and remainders', 'in_service', 'lower, middle, and upper elementary', 'JMTE_Cai_2017_Mathematical', 'Jinfa Cai, Meixia Ding', '2017', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '19, 20, 21', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Cai_2017_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40279, 'Students erroneously assume that the presence of a zero in the middle of a dividend guarantees a zero in the middle of the quotient, or vice versa.', 'Assuming that because the dividend in 408/3 has a zero, the quotient should as well, or wondering why 624/3 = 208 has a zero in the quotient when the dividend does not.', 'whole_number', 'whole number division', 'zeros in the quotient', 'in_service', 'lower, middle, and upper elementary', 'JMTE_Cai_2017_Mathematical', 'Jinfa Cai, Meixia Ding', '2017', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'productive', 'secondary', '19', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Cai_2017_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40280, 'A student uses slightly imprecise language to explain why a rectangle''s area is larger than a parallelogram''s when transformed, but still demonstrates a productive understanding of area conservation.', '"Rectangle is bigger because we can transform the parallelogram to another rectangle. As such, the two rectangles have the same length but different width."', 'geometric', 'area', 'parallelograms and rectangles', 'in_service', 'lower, middle, and upper elementary', 'JMTE_Cai_2017_Mathematical', 'Jinfa Cai, Meixia Ding', '2017', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'productive', 'passing', '17, 18', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Cai_2017_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40281, 'Teachers and students hold incoherent meanings for trigonometric functions, viewing angle measure purely as an ''amount of turn'' and sine/cosine strictly as a ratio of triangle sides (SOH-CAH-TOA). This causes a conceptual clash when trigonometric functions are composed, as a ratio of lengths cannot logically be used as an amount of rotation.', 'If by ''degree'' one means 1/360 of a complete rotation, and by ''sin'' we mean ''opposite over hypotenuse''... this presents a clash of conceptual categories when thinking about sin(35°) as an argument to cosine: How can a ratio of two lengths be an amount of rotation?', 'measurement', 'trigonometric functions', 'angle measure and sine/cosine', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Thompson_2007_Design', 'Patrick W. Thompson, Marilyn P. Carlson, Jason Silverman', '2007', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '417, 418, 419', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Thompson_2007_Design.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40282, 'Learners hold an ''action conception'' of functions, treating formulas as a mere ''command to calculate'' specific values one at a time, rather than a ''process conception'' where the formula is seen as self-evaluating or mapping sets of values, which hinders their ability to envision graphs emerging from simultaneous covariation.', 'Students holding this view imagine function values being calculated, laboriously, one at a time. Students holding an action conception of a formula cannot envision a graph as emerging from two quantities covarying simultaneously, nor can they see it as a mapping from one set of values to another set of values.', 'algebraic', 'functions', 'action vs process conception', 'in_service', 'secondary', 'JMTE_Thompson_2007_Design', 'Patrick W. Thompson, Marilyn P. Carlson, Jason Silverman', '2007', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'primary', '424, 425, 426', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Thompson_2007_Design.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40283, 'A student inconsistently applies decimal rules during a partial products algorithm, misapplying the rule for placing the decimal point to intermediate steps while treating factors as both whole numbers and decimals.', 'When solving 0.2 x 8.0, the student sometimes treats 8.0 as 80, but records the decimal point at each intermediate step, erroneously claiming the intermediate answer is 16 but requires two digits to the right of the decimal.', 'decimal', 'decimal multiplication', 'partial products algorithm', 'in_service', 'fourth and fifth grade', 'JMTE_Es_2010_Influence', 'Elizabeth A. van Es, Miriam Gamoran Sherin', '2010', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'productive', 'primary', '165, 166', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Es_2010_Influence.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40284, 'A student incorrectly adds the numbers presented in a multiplicative word problem instead of multiplying them.', 'A student answers 14 to the problem ''there are 8 boxes of cupcakes. There are 6 cupcakes per box. How many cupcakes are there in all?''', 'whole_number', 'multiplication', 'word problems', 'in_service', 'fourth and fifth grade', 'JMTE_Es_2010_Influence', 'Elizabeth A. van Es, Miriam Gamoran Sherin', '2010', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'passing', '169', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Es_2010_Influence.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40285, 'Students incorrectly add fractions by adding the numerators together and adding the denominators together.', 'When adding a fourth of the land and a sixteenth of the land, students will try to make that into two-twentieths by just adding the numerators and denominators together.', 'fraction', 'fraction addition', 'adding numerators and denominators', 'mixed', 'middle and secondary', 'JMTE_Lannin_2013_Development', 'John K. Lannin, Matthew Webb, Kathryn Chval, Fran Arbaugh, Sarah Hicks, Cynthia Taylor, Rebecca Bruton', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '414, 415', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Lannin_2013_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40286, 'Students struggle to recognize that a variable represents a number that can be mathematically operated on.', 'A student has difficulty equating numbers and variables as equivalent, lacking the understanding that a variable can be multiplied or added to another variable.', 'algebraic', 'variables', 'variables as numbers', 'mixed', 'middle and secondary', 'JMTE_Lannin_2013_Development', 'John K. Lannin, Matthew Webb, Kathryn Chval, Fran Arbaugh, Sarah Hicks, Cynthia Taylor, Rebecca Bruton', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '420', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Lannin_2013_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40287, 'Students inappropriately apply linear graphs with constant negative slopes to represent real-world situations that model non-linear relationships.', 'When graphing the number of dancers leaving a dance floor over time, almost all students drew linear graphs with negative slopes rather than a graph showing a variable rate of change.', 'algebraic', 'graphing functions', 'non-linear relationships', 'mixed', 'middle and secondary', 'JMTE_Lannin_2013_Development', 'John K. Lannin, Matthew Webb, Kathryn Chval, Fran Arbaugh, Sarah Hicks, Cynthia Taylor, Rebecca Bruton', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'secondary', '413, 414', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Lannin_2013_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40288, 'Students struggle to understand how units can be mathematically multiplied when calculating area or volume.', 'Students do not understand that calculating area is not just a length, but that centimeters times centimeters gives centimeters squared.', 'measurement', 'units', 'multiplying units', 'mixed', 'middle and secondary', 'JMTE_Lannin_2013_Development', 'John K. Lannin, Matthew Webb, Kathryn Chval, Fran Arbaugh, Sarah Hicks, Cynthia Taylor, Rebecca Bruton', '2013', 'J Math Teacher Educ', 'deficit', 'passing', '417', 'pdfs/JMTE/JMTE_Lannin_2013_Development.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40289, 'Students erroneously hold an objectivist or conveyance metaphor for learning, believing that learning physics is primarily about receiving, memorizing, storing, and reproducing knowledge from authoritarian sources like teachers and textbooks.', 'The mind is a container; thoughts, ideas, and knowledge are entities that can be put, stored or drilled into this receptacle.', 'other', 'epistemological beliefs', 'nature of learning physics', 'student', '11th and 12th grade (high school)', 'IJSME_Langley_2006_Probing', 'DOROTHY LANGLEY and BAT-SHEVA EYLON', '2006', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'primary', '215, 216, 231, 232', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Langley_2006_Probing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40290, 'Students view physics formulas merely as technical tools or number-producing machines used to calculate values in equations, rather than as descriptions of physical relationships and regularity.', 'formulas as number producing machines', 'other', 'physics formulas', 'role of formulas', 'student', '11th and 12th grade (high school)', 'IJSME_Langley_2006_Probing', 'DOROTHY LANGLEY and BAT-SHEVA EYLON', '2006', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '220, 236', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Langley_2006_Probing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40291, 'Students believe that electrical current flows sequentially from the battery, causing components closer to the battery or its positive terminal to receive current and light up before those further away.', 'Since the electric circuit is closed and because bulb 1 is closer to the positive terminal of the battery, it lights up first because the current passes through it first and then through bulb 2.', 'other', 'simple electric circuits', 'current flow', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'IJSME_Safadi_2014_Problem', 'RAFI'' SAFADI and EDIT YERUSHALMI', '2014', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '1345, 1348, 1349, 1354, 1357, 1358', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Safadi_2014_Problem.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40292, 'Students believe that the battery is the direct source of the electrons that make up the current, rather than understanding that electrons already exist in the wires and are merely pushed by the battery.', 'The two bulbs light up at the same time since the electrons exist in the battery which provides the "push" to make the bulbs light up.', 'other', 'simple electric circuits', 'nature of electrons', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'IJSME_Safadi_2014_Problem', 'RAFI'' SAFADI and EDIT YERUSHALMI', '2014', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '1344, 1354', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Safadi_2014_Problem.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40293, 'Students believe that in a closed circuit, currents emanate from both the positive and negative terminals, flowing along their own paths until they clash at the components, causing them to function.', 'When the circuit starts working one current from the positive terminal flows along its own path and another current from the negative terminal proceeds along its own path and when they collide the bulbs light up together.', 'other', 'simple electric circuits', 'current flow', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'IJSME_Safadi_2014_Problem', 'RAFI'' SAFADI and EDIT YERUSHALMI', '2014', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '1345, 1354', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Safadi_2014_Problem.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40294, 'Students hold a cyclic model of current flow, believing that a circuit only becomes closed and functional when a sequential current has physically travelled all the way from one terminal to the other.', 'For the bulbs to light up the current should flow along a complete circle through which the electrons move until they reach the negative pole. At this time the two bulbs light up together and at the same time.', 'other', 'simple electric circuits', 'current flow', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'IJSME_Safadi_2014_Problem', 'RAFI'' SAFADI and EDIT YERUSHALMI', '2014', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '1351, 1355', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Safadi_2014_Problem.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40295, 'Teachers incorrectly apply additive strategies instead of multiplicative strategies when solving proportional missing-value problems that involve complex or non-integer number structures.', 'When given a rectangle with dimensions 5x3 cm and asked to find the new width if the new base is 12 cm, a teacher adds 7 to the width (3+7=10 cm) instead of using the proportional scale factor.', 'proportional', 'proportional reasoning', 'missing-value problems', 'in_service', 'primary level (grades 1 to 6)', 'IJSME_Ekawati_2015_Developing', 'ROOSELYNA EKAWATI, FOU-LAI LIN and KAI-LIN YANG', '2015', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', 'S15, S16', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Ekawati_2015_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40296, 'Teachers rely on visual estimation or area comparisons to judge geometric similarity, ignoring grid dimensions and failing to compare the ratio of corresponding side lengths or diagonals.', 'Teachers assume two rhombuses on a grid are similar by visual recognition or comparing areas, while ignoring the specific grid size constraints (3 cm x 2 cm) given in the problem.', 'geometric', 'similarity and congruency', 'ratio of corresponding lengths', 'in_service', 'primary level (grades 1 to 6)', 'IJSME_Ekawati_2015_Developing', 'ROOSELYNA EKAWATI, FOU-LAI LIN and KAI-LIN YANG', '2015', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', 'S18', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Ekawati_2015_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40297, 'Teachers overgeneralize proportional patterns, translating non-proportional situations (like age context problems) into algebraic proportional representations and incorrectly solving them using cross-multiplication.', 'For the problem ''When Dina is 1 year old, Budi is 2 years old. What will be Budi''s age when Dina''s age is 10 years old?'', teachers set up a proportion and find Budi''s age to be 20.', 'proportional', 'distinguishing proportional and non-proportional situations', 'constant difference vs constant ratio', 'in_service', 'primary level (grades 1 to 6)', 'IJSME_Ekawati_2015_Developing', 'ROOSELYNA EKAWATI, FOU-LAI LIN and KAI-LIN YANG', '2015', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', 'S18, S19', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Ekawati_2015_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40298, 'Teachers erroneously believe that the graphical representation of a linear ratio relation (y=kx) on a Cartesian coordinate system does not need to pass through the origin.', 'About one third of teacher participants considered that the line representing a ratio relation in the formal Cartesian coordinate system did not pass through the origin.', 'proportional', 'figural representation of ratio', 'Cartesian coordinates', 'in_service', 'primary level (grades 1 to 6)', 'IJSME_Ekawati_2015_Developing', 'ROOSELYNA EKAWATI, FOU-LAI LIN and KAI-LIN YANG', '2015', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', 'S15, S18', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Ekawati_2015_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40299, 'Students falsely believe that multiplication always results in a larger number and division always results in a smaller number.', '', 'decimal', 'multiplication and division of decimals', 'effect of operations', 'student', 'Year 5 and 6', 'IJSME_Bragg_2012_Testing', 'LEICHA A. BRAGG', '2012', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '1453, 1454, 1461', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Bragg_2012_Testing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40300, 'Students conceive of a limit as a barrier or boundary that a function dynamically approaches but can never actually reach.', 'The limit of a function at x0 is a value that the function approaches, but never arrives at.', 'calculus', 'limits', 'limit as reachable or unreachable', 'student', '12th-grade', 'IJSME_Elia_2009_Geometric', 'ILIADA ELIA, ATHANASIOS GAGATSIS, ARETI PANAOURA, THEODOSIS ZACHARIADES and FOTINI ZOULINAKI', '2009', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '767, 779, 780, 782', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Elia_2009_Geometric.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40301, 'Students erroneously equate the limit of a function at a specific point with the numerical value of the function at that exact point.', 'The limit of a function to x0 is the value of the function at the point x0.', 'calculus', 'limits', 'limit versus function value', 'student', '12th-grade', 'IJSME_Elia_2009_Geometric', 'ILIADA ELIA, ATHANASIOS GAGATSIS, ARETI PANAOURA, THEODOSIS ZACHARIADES and FOTINI ZOULINAKI', '2009', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '779, 780', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Elia_2009_Geometric.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40302, 'Students believe that a limit at a point x0 can only have meaning if x0 belongs to the domain of the function.', 'A significant proportion of students stated that the point x0 must belong to the domain of the function and that in a different case the limit has no meaning.', 'calculus', 'limits', 'relationship with domain', 'student', '12th-grade', 'IJSME_Elia_2009_Geometric', 'ILIADA ELIA, ATHANASIOS GAGATSIS, ARETI PANAOURA, THEODOSIS ZACHARIADES and FOTINI ZOULINAKI', '2009', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '780', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Elia_2009_Geometric.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40303, 'Students do not consider infinite decimals like 1.999... to be real numbers equal to an integer (2), viewing them instead as an incomplete infinite sequence.', 'When asked for the limit of a sequence of plant heights, some students responded ''the limit is 1.9999...'' because they could not understand that 1.999... is the same as the number 2.', 'calculus', 'limits of sequences', 'infinite decimals', 'student', '12th-grade', 'IJSME_Elia_2009_Geometric', 'ILIADA ELIA, ATHANASIOS GAGATSIS, ARETI PANAOURA, THEODOSIS ZACHARIADES and FOTINI ZOULINAKI', '2009', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '778, 779, 780', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Elia_2009_Geometric.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40304, 'Driven by the ''didactic contract'', students rely rigidly on stereotypic algorithmic procedures to compute limits, even when these procedures are inappropriate or a geometric interpretation provides a direct answer.', 'Although the values of the slope and the limit were evident from the information given in the graph, 15% of the students insisted on employing the stereotypic method, namely the algorithmic procedures.', 'calculus', 'limits', 'problem-solving strategies', 'student', '12th-grade', 'IJSME_Elia_2009_Geometric', 'ILIADA ELIA, ATHANASIOS GAGATSIS, ARETI PANAOURA, THEODOSIS ZACHARIADES and FOTINI ZOULINAKI', '2009', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '768, 769, 774, 781, 782', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Elia_2009_Geometric.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40305, 'Students suspend sense-making when solving division-with-remainder word problems, directly applying arithmetic operations and providing contextually inappropriate answers instead of interpreting the result according to real-world constraints.', '150 / 12 = 12.5; so, the elevator must ascend 12.5 times to get all tourists to the top of the building.', 'whole_number', 'division with remainder', 'real-world constraints', 'mixed', 'elementary school', 'IJSME_Chen_2011_Investigation', 'LIMIN CHEN, WIM VAN DOOREN, QI CHEN and LIEVEN VERSCHAFFEL', '2011', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '920, 921, 922, 929', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Chen_2011_Investigation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40306, 'Students attribute a special inherent stability to ions with full shells or noble gas configurations, believing this prevents recombination with electrons or accounts for ionization energy trends.', 'The sodium ion will not recombine with an electron to reform the sodium atom as its stable octet configuration would be disrupted.', 'other', 'chemistry', 'ionisation energy', 'student', 'Grades 11 and 12, 16-19 yr old', 'IJSME_Taber_2007_Exploring', 'KEITH S. TABER and KIM CHWEE DANIEL TAN', '2007', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematical Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '377, 381, 382', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Taber_2007_Exploring.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40307, 'Students rely exclusively on the perceived stability of fully-filled or half-filled sub-shells to explain ionization trends, ignoring other physical factors like nuclear charge.', 'The first ionisation energy of silicon is less than that of phosphorus because the 3p sub-shell of phosphorus is half-filled.', 'other', 'chemistry', 'ionisation energy', 'student', 'Grades 11 and 12, 16-19 yr old', 'IJSME_Taber_2007_Exploring', 'KEITH S. TABER and KIM CHWEE DANIEL TAN', '2007', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematical Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '381, 383', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Taber_2007_Exploring.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40308, 'Students believe that a positive nucleus produces a fixed amount of nuclear force that is shared out or redistributed among the available electrons.', 'When an electron is removed from the sodium atom, the attraction of the nucleus for the ''lost'' electron will be redistributed among the remaining electrons in the sodium ion.', 'other', 'chemistry', 'nuclear attraction', 'student', 'Grades 11 and 12, 16-19 yr old', 'IJSME_Taber_2007_Exploring', 'KEITH S. TABER and KIM CHWEE DANIEL TAN', '2007', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematical Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '377, 381, 383', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Taber_2007_Exploring.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40309, 'Students use relation-based reasoning by focusing on only one variable that they see as the ''cause'' (such as electron distance), while overlooking other co-varying influential factors (such as nuclear charge).', 'The first ionisation energy of magnesium is greater than that of aluminium because the 3p electron of aluminium is further from the nucleus compared to the 3s electrons of magnesium.', 'other', 'chemistry', 'causal reasoning', 'student', 'Grades 11 and 12, 16-19 yr old', 'IJSME_Taber_2007_Exploring', 'KEITH S. TABER and KIM CHWEE DANIEL TAN', '2007', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematical Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '382, 384', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Taber_2007_Exploring.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40310, 'Students map temporal periods on a number line using a sequential left-to-right strategy, comparing only adjacent numbers rather than viewing the scale in its totality, which leads to exaggerating the distances between smaller units.', 'Ben was one of two students who said the space between a month and a year on timeline 1 should be smaller than that between a day and a month because it only takes 12 months to make a year while it takes 30 days to make a month.', 'whole_number', 'number lines', 'scaling and magnitude', 'student', 'eighth grade, 11th grade, and university', 'IJSME_Cheek_2012_Students''', 'KIM A. CHEEK', '2012', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '1057, 1058, 1063', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Cheek_2012_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40311, 'Students fail to distinguish between the magnitudes of very large numbers, treating numbers like 100,000 and 1,000,000 as virtually the same value or mapping them logarithmically.', 'Vanessa put 100,000 and 1,000,000 at the same point on the timeline because, in her words, ''They''re the same thing.''', 'whole_number', 'large numbers', 'relative magnitude', 'student', 'eighth grade, 11th grade, and university', 'IJSME_Cheek_2012_Students''', 'KIM A. CHEEK', '2012', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '1050, 1058', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Cheek_2012_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40312, 'Students use additive reasoning rather than multiplicative reasoning when determining the proportional relationship between large numbers on a scale.', 'When trying to determine how many hundred thousand years are in 1,000,000 years, Emma correctly subtracted 100,000 from 1,000,000, but then said it would take ''about 900 years.''', 'whole_number', 'proportional reasoning', 'multiplicative relationships', 'student', 'eighth grade, 11th grade, and university', 'IJSME_Cheek_2012_Students''', 'KIM A. CHEEK', '2012', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '1059, 1060', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Cheek_2012_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40313, 'Students incorrectly assume an equation must be structurally symmetrical and simply copy a number across the equals sign to fill a missing value, ignoring the mathematical operations involved.', 'For the problem ''14 * [ ] = 0.25 * 14'', the student writes ''0.25'' in the box to make it symmetrical.', 'algebraic', 'equivalence', 'missing-value problems', 'student', 'Grades 4, 7, 9 and 11', 'IJSME_Goodchild_2009_Exploratory', 'SIMON GOODCHILD and BARBRO GREVHOLM', '2009', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '173', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Goodchild_2009_Exploratory.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40314, 'Driven by a need for closure and rote application of rules, students evaluate the expression on one side of an equation and insert the numerical result into the missing value box on the other side, completely ignoring adjacent operations.', 'For the problem ''14 * [ ] = 0.25 * 14'', the student calculates 0.25 * 14 to get 3.5 and writes ''3.5'' in the box, ignoring the ''14 *'' operation.', 'algebraic', 'equivalence', 'missing-value problems', 'student', 'Grades 4, 7, 9 and 11', 'IJSME_Goodchild_2009_Exploratory', 'SIMON GOODCHILD and BARBRO GREVHOLM', '2009', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '173, 174, 175', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Goodchild_2009_Exploratory.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40315, 'Students view algebraic expressions strictly as descriptions of chronological calculation processes rather than as mathematical objects, causing them to resist using abstract symbolic forms that obscure the sequential actions they perform.', 'When substituting ''x+1'' into the rule ''z * z = y'', Jerel rejected the notation ''(x+1)(x+1)'' because ''They [readers] would not know that I added first.''', 'algebraic', 'algebraic expressions', 'process-object duality', 'student', 'seventh grade', 'IJSME_Francisco_2012_Students''', 'JOHN M. FRANCISCO and MARKUS HAHKIONIEMI', '2012', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '1013, 1015, 1016', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Francisco_2012_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40316, 'Students'' tendency to focus primarily on recursive arithmetic patterns (finite differences) in sequential data tables limits their ability to directly formulate explicit algebraic rules for nonlinear functions.', 'Ian noticed that the y-values in a sequence increased by consecutive odd numbers (1, 3, 5, 7) and formed a partly recursive rule, but failed to generate an all-explicit rule.', 'algebraic', 'function rules', 'recursive vs explicit generalization', 'student', 'seventh grade', 'IJSME_Francisco_2012_Students''', 'JOHN M. FRANCISCO and MARKUS HAHKIONIEMI', '2012', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'productive', 'primary', '1010, 1011, 1012, 1014, 1015', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Francisco_2012_Students''.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40317, 'Students struggle with operating on negative numbers, which hinders their ability to correctly generate points for and verify the complete shape of relations across all quadrants of a Cartesian plane.', 'Hector deduced that the shape of the graph was a circle by plotting positive points and using symmetry, but he never succeeded in plotting points on the second and third quadrants due to limitations with negative multiplication.', 'integer', 'operating with negatives', 'Cartesian graphing', 'student', 'seventh grade', 'IJSME_Francisco_2012_Students''', 'JOHN M. FRANCISCO and MARKUS HAHKIONIEMI', '2012', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '1009', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Francisco_2012_Students''.pdf')).
