research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40395, 'Students inappropriately treat infinity as a distinct number or a specific point on an axis, attempting to apply standard arithmetic operations to it.', 'Almost all of the students considered infinity as a number, so they tend to find the middle point of 1 and infinity, just like finding the middle point between 1 and n.', 'calculus', 'infinity', 'infinity as an object', 'student', 'senior high-school', 'IJSME_Barahmand_2017_Boundary', 'Ali Barahmand', '2017', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '576, 582', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Barahmand_2017_Boundary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40396, 'Students erroneously conclude that 0.999... is strictly less than 1 by incorrectly generalizing the results of finite decimal sequences to an infinite context.', '0.9 < 1, 0.99 < 1, 0.999 < 1... So, by continuation of the procedure to infinity, the following conclusion is obtained: 0.99999999 ... < 1.', 'decimal', 'infinite repeating decimals', '0.999... = 1', 'student', 'senior high-school', 'IJSME_Barahmand_2017_Boundary', 'Ali Barahmand', '2017', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '580, 581', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Barahmand_2017_Boundary.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40397, 'Students conflate weightlessness with a lack of gravitational force, incorrectly believing that floating astronauts or objects in space experience zero gravity.', 'The ball stays up in space since there is no friction and no gravitation, so the initial velocity I provide the ball will cause it to fly up to the height where it stops', 'other', 'weight and gravitation', 'weightlessness and free fall', 'student', '9th grade (middle school) and 11th-12th grades (high school)', 'IJSME_Stein_2015_Impact', 'HANA STEIN and IGAL GALILI', '2015', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '1487, 1488, 1491, 1494, 1498', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Stein_2015_Impact.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40398, 'Students believe that the presence of a medium, such as air or an atmosphere, is necessary for gravitational force to act, concluding that there is no gravity in a vacuum.', 'Gravitation does not work because the astronauts are outside the layer that runs gravitation around the Earth.', 'other', 'gravitation', 'dependence on medium', 'student', '9th grade (middle school) and 11th-12th grades (high school)', 'IJSME_Stein_2015_Impact', 'HANA STEIN and IGAL GALILI', '2015', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '1494, 1498', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Stein_2015_Impact.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40399, 'Students exaggerate the inverse-square law of gravitation, believing that gravity decreases so rapidly with distance that it completely disappears at a relatively low altitude.', 'When moving away from the Moon the gravitation decreases by Newton''s formula: R grows, so F will be smaller, so the ball will reach the height where there is no gravity in space, there it will be lost.', 'other', 'gravitation', 'attenuation with distance', 'student', '9th grade (middle school) and 11th-12th grades (high school)', 'IJSME_Stein_2015_Impact', 'HANA STEIN and IGAL GALILI', '2015', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '1498, 1501', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Stein_2015_Impact.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40400, 'Students incorrectly explain weightlessness in orbit by falsely preferring more massive bodies, claiming astronauts are weightless due to negligible mass compared to the Earth, while the spaceship itself still experiences gravitational force.', 'The astronaut feels weightless because he is far from Earth and his mass is negligible compared to the Earth''s mass, but the force acts on the spaceship because its mass is not negligible', 'other', 'gravitation', 'mass dependence', 'student', '9th grade (middle school) and 11th-12th grades (high school)', 'IJSME_Stein_2015_Impact', 'HANA STEIN and IGAL GALILI', '2015', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '1498', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Stein_2015_Impact.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40401, 'Students violate Newton''s third law of motion, believing that gravitational interaction is asymmetric and that only larger or supporting bodies exert a pull.', 'The Earth is heavier than the box because the box is standing on the Earth and its force is not as strong as that of the Earth: the box doesn''t hold the Earth but the Earth holds the box!', 'other', 'gravitation', 'Newton''s third law', 'student', '9th grade (middle school) and 11th-12th grades (high school)', 'IJSME_Stein_2015_Impact', 'HANA STEIN and IGAL GALILI', '2015', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '1499', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Stein_2015_Impact.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40402, 'Students erroneously assume that changing the order of the coordinate points in the numerator and denominator of the slope formula will produce a mathematically different value.', 'I put Abel is correct because that is the only formula I was taught how to use.', 'algebraic', 'slope', 'slope formula', 'mixed', 'ninth-grade', 'IJSME_Cross_2009_Creating', 'DIONNE I. CROSS', '2009', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '914, 915, 916, 917', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Cross_2009_Creating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40403, 'Students make basic computation errors when calculating with negative integers, leading them to falsely conclude that the two valid slope formulas yield results with different signs.', 'When calculating the denominator, Kevin asked ''What is -2 - 3?'' and Eddie replied ''-6.. I mean -5'', leading to the conclusion ''You get the same thing except positive.''', 'integer', 'integer operations', 'subtraction', 'mixed', 'ninth-grade', 'IJSME_Cross_2009_Creating', 'DIONNE I. CROSS', '2009', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '915, 916, 925', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Cross_2009_Creating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40404, 'Students incorrectly compare fractions by assuming that a fraction with a larger numerator and larger denominator must be the larger fraction.', '8/9 is larger. Because the denominator 9 is larger than 6 and 8 is larger than 5.', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'unlike denominators', 'student', 'sixth grade', 'IJSME_Yang_2004_Study', 'DER-CHING YANG, CHUN-JEN HSU and MING-CHIANG HUANG', '2004', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '416, 422', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Yang_2004_Study.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40405, 'Students incorrectly compare fractions by subtracting the numerator from the denominator, believing that if the differences are the same, the fractions are equal.', '5/6 is equal to 8/9. ... Since 6 - 5 = 1 and 9 - 8 = 1, I think they are equal.', 'fraction', 'fraction comparison', 'unlike denominators', 'student', 'sixth grade', 'IJSME_Yang_2004_Study', 'DER-CHING YANG, CHUN-JEN HSU and MING-CHIANG HUANG', '2004', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '417', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Yang_2004_Study.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40406, 'Students blindly apply the procedural rule of counting decimal places to place the decimal point in a product, leading to highly unreasonable magnitudes without making sense of the quantities.', '534.6 has 1 digit and 0.545 has 3 decimal digits. So the multiplication has 4 digits. We counted from left to right 4 numbers. Hence, the answer is 29.1357.', 'decimal', 'decimal multiplication', 'placing the decimal point', 'student', 'sixth grade', 'IJSME_Yang_2004_Study', 'DER-CHING YANG, CHUN-JEN HSU and MING-CHIANG HUANG', '2004', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '419, 420, 421', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Yang_2004_Study.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40407, 'Students incorrectly rely on rote written algorithms or cross-multiplication attempts to estimate the product of fractions, rather than using benchmarks.', 'Because 32 * 16 is larger than 21 * 7 by a lot.', 'fraction', 'estimation', 'fraction multiplication', 'student', 'sixth grade', 'IJSME_Yang_2004_Study', 'DER-CHING YANG, CHUN-JEN HSU and MING-CHIANG HUANG', '2004', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '417, 418', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Yang_2004_Study.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40408, 'Students incorrectly apply the discursive routine for multiplying square roots to addition, squaring the addends individually and concluding that the sum of the square roots is the square root of the sum.', 'When asked if √2 + √3 = √5, a student agreed, explaining, "It raises to the power of two. It will be a whole number. It will be 2 + 3 = 5, so, the square root of 5 is √5."', 'algebraic', 'square roots', 'addition of irrational numbers', 'student', 'ninth grade', 'IJSME_Shinno_2018_Reification', 'Yusuke Shinno', '2018', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '306, 307, 308, 309', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Shinno_2018_Reification.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40409, 'Students specify fractional prices for individual items in a set without realizing that the sum of these fractional parts must exactly equal one whole.', 'Students priced items in a set as 1/8, 2/8, 3/8, and 4/8, not noticing until prompted that these summed to 10/8, which is more than the price of the whole set.', 'fraction', 'part-whole construct', 'sum of parts', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'IJSME_Shahbari_2017_Modelling', 'Juhaina Awawdeh Shahbari & Irit Peled', '2017', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '384', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Shahbari_2017_Modelling.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40410, 'Students struggle to understand how to use a fraction as an operator or functional operation to determine a specific portion of a total given quantity.', 'A student did not understand what a peer meant by "half of the price" when trying to compute the price of the garage for different sets.', 'fraction', 'operator construct', 'fraction of a quantity', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'IJSME_Shahbari_2017_Modelling', 'Juhaina Awawdeh Shahbari & Irit Peled', '2017', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '384, 385', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Shahbari_2017_Modelling.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40411, 'Students struggle with the reduction and expansion of fractions, finding it difficult to compare fractional parts that have unlike denominators.', 'A student did not know how to compare the prices of two items because she did not understand why 1/24 was equal to 2/48.', 'fraction', 'fraction equivalence', 'comparing fractions with unlike denominators', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'IJSME_Shahbari_2017_Modelling', 'Juhaina Awawdeh Shahbari & Irit Peled', '2017', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '385, 386', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Shahbari_2017_Modelling.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40412, 'Students interpret the place value of digits to the right of the decimal point by mirroring whole number concepts, treating the last digit as ones and assuming place value is divided by ten as they move left.', 'In 1.256, 1 represents one 0.001, 2 represents two 0.01s, 5 represents five 0.1s and 6 represent six 1s because 6 is at the back.', 'decimal', 'place value', 'mirroring whole numbers', 'student', 'grade 6', 'IJSME_Lai_2015_Hong', 'MUN YEE LAI and SARA MURRAY', '2015', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '132, 136', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Lai_2015_Hong.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40413, 'Students apply the shorter-is-larger rule when comparing decimals, incorrectly believing that more decimal places mean the overall value is smaller.', '0.32 > 0.384 because the more the decimal places, the smaller the value.', 'decimal', 'comparison of decimals', 'shorter-is-larger rule', 'student', 'grade 6', 'IJSME_Lai_2015_Hong', 'MUN YEE LAI and SARA MURRAY', '2015', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '124, 132, 138, 139', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Lai_2015_Hong.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40414, 'Students treat the sequence of digits after the decimal point as whole numbers, erroneously predicting that 0.10 comes immediately after 0.9 on a number line.', 'Answering 0.10 instead of 1.0 when asked what comes next to 0.9 on a number line.', 'decimal', 'number line', 'sequencing', 'student', 'grade 6', 'IJSME_Lai_2015_Hong', 'MUN YEE LAI and SARA MURRAY', '2015', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '132, 133', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Lai_2015_Hong.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40415, 'Students incorrectly position the decimal point when multiplying decimals due to the false generalization that multiplication must always produce a larger product.', '', 'decimal', 'multiplication of decimals', 'magnitude of product', 'student', 'grade 6', 'IJSME_Lai_2015_Hong', 'MUN YEE LAI and SARA MURRAY', '2015', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '133', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Lai_2015_Hong.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40416, 'When learning the algorithmic procedure for addition and subtraction of decimals, students incorrectly believe that failing to align the decimal points will result in a single answer containing two distinct decimal points.', 'If not, you would have two decimal points in two different places and you do not know which place is for correct answer.', 'decimal', 'addition of decimals', 'aligning decimal points', 'student', 'grade 6', 'IJSME_Lai_2015_Hong', 'MUN YEE LAI and SARA MURRAY', '2015', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '140', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Lai_2015_Hong.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40417, 'Young children believe that food does not undergo chemical change inside the body, thinking it either remains in its original form or breaks into invisible pieces that conserve their macroscopic identity.', 'prior to age 10, many children think that the body only contains the food they have eaten recently, and in their drawings, they depict the internal cavities of the body full of bread, milk and meat.', 'other', 'human nutrition', 'digestion', 'other', '', 'IJSME_CabelloGarrido_2018_Developing', 'Aurelio Cabello-Garrido & Enrique Espana-Ramos & Angel Blanco-Lopez', '2018', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '1275, 1276', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_CabelloGarrido_2018_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40418, 'Students conceive of nutrients as tangible objects perceived through senses, often equating vitamins or minerals to pills and fat to visible parts of food.', 'many of them thought that vitamins and minerals were simply pills, and the majority believed that fat in foods was something that could be seen (for example, the white part of bacon).', 'other', 'human nutrition', 'concept of nutrients', 'other', '', 'IJSME_CabelloGarrido_2018_Developing', 'Aurelio Cabello-Garrido & Enrique Espana-Ramos & Angel Blanco-Lopez', '2018', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '1276', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_CabelloGarrido_2018_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40419, 'Students classify nutrients and foods categorically as absolute good or absolute bad, ignoring dose insensitivity or dietary context.', '44% of the university students they interviewed thought their daily intake of fibre (a good nutrient) should be anywhere between 100 and more than 500 g, much more than the actual recommended amount of 20-30 g.', 'other', 'human nutrition', 'diet and classification', 'other', '', 'IJSME_CabelloGarrido_2018_Developing', 'Aurelio Cabello-Garrido & Enrique Espana-Ramos & Angel Blanco-Lopez', '2018', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '1276, 1277, 1280, 1282', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_CabelloGarrido_2018_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40420, 'Students oversimplify nutrition by assuming each nutrient serves only one physiological function or that a particular nutrient is found in only one type of food.', 'milk contains calcium, fruit has vitamins, vegetables contain minerals and meat has protein.', 'other', 'human nutrition', 'concept of nutrients', 'other', '', 'IJSME_CabelloGarrido_2018_Developing', 'Aurelio Cabello-Garrido & Enrique Espana-Ramos & Angel Blanco-Lopez', '2018', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '1277, 1281', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_CabelloGarrido_2018_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40421, 'Students confuse the scientific concept of energy in food with vitality, well-being, or a material fluid that functions like a battery for the organism.', 'water gives you lots of energy because when you are thirsty you feel weak, but as soon as you drink some water, you begin to feel stronger and full of energy.', 'other', 'human nutrition', 'concept of energy', 'other', '', 'IJSME_CabelloGarrido_2018_Developing', 'Aurelio Cabello-Garrido & Enrique Espana-Ramos & Angel Blanco-Lopez', '2018', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '1279, 1280', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_CabelloGarrido_2018_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40422, 'Students create a false dichotomy between energy, which they view positively, and calories, which they view negatively in relation to weight gain.', 'the most energy-rich foods were not the same as those most likely to lead to obesity, as if the two things were independent.', 'other', 'human nutrition', 'concept of energy', 'other', '', 'IJSME_CabelloGarrido_2018_Developing', 'Aurelio Cabello-Garrido & Enrique Espana-Ramos & Angel Blanco-Lopez', '2018', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '1280', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_CabelloGarrido_2018_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40423, 'English-speaking students conceptualize a limit processually as an unreachable boundary, believing a function or sequence approaches the limit value but can never actually equal or reach it.', 'The limit as x approaches zero the function will approach .1 but it''ll never actually reach point one.', 'calculus', 'limits', 'concept of limit', 'student', 'university students', 'IJSME_Kim_2018_Relative', 'Dong-Joong Kim & Woong Lim', '2018', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '1567, 1569, 1570, 1571, 1575', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Kim_2018_Relative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40424, 'Korean-speaking students rely heavily on a rigid syntactic mode and structural algebraic manipulation without conceptualizing dynamic limiting processes, leaving them less able to interpret visual mediators effectively to make predictions.', 'When given a task to find the limit of a function from its graph when x goes to positive infinity, students scan the written phrase as an algebraic expression rather than using geometric features.', 'calculus', 'limits', 'limit evaluation', 'student', 'university students', 'IJSME_Kim_2018_Relative', 'Dong-Joong Kim & Woong Lim', '2018', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '1574, 1575, 1576', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Kim_2018_Relative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40425, 'Students attempt to find limits using informal reasoning, incorrectly treating infinity as an ordinary number or assuming a sequence must simply round up.', 'Eventually it will have to round up because they wouldn''t make .099999 forever.', 'calculus', 'limits', 'limit evaluation', 'student', 'university students', 'IJSME_Kim_2018_Relative', 'Dong-Joong Kim & Woong Lim', '2018', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '1567, 1568', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Kim_2018_Relative.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40426, 'Students erroneously assume that if the one-dimensional side lengths of a geometric figure are enlarged by a scale factor, the area or volume of the figure increases by that exact same scale factor.', 'Most of the students implied that they just needed to multiply the area and volume by 2 when all the sides are enlarged by a scale factor of 2.', 'proportional', 'nonlinear proportional relationships', 'area and volume scaling', 'student', 'sixth, seventh, and eighth grade', 'IJSME_Ayan_2018_Middle', 'Rukiye Ayan & Mine Isiksal Bostan', '2018', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '503, 504, 512, 515', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Ayan_2018_Middle.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40427, 'Students inappropriately use additive reasoning when working with the volume of enlarged figures, adding the scale factor multiple times instead of employing a multiplicative/cubic relationship.', 'A student thought that the volume of a swimming pool would get six times larger (2 + 2 + 2 = 6) when its three dimensions are each enlarged by a scale factor of 2.', 'proportional', 'nonlinear proportional relationships', 'volume scaling', 'student', 'sixth, seventh, and eighth grade', 'IJSME_Ayan_2018_Middle', 'Rukiye Ayan & Mine Isiksal Bostan', '2018', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '504, 512, 513, 515', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Ayan_2018_Middle.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40428, 'When modeling a mixture problem, the teacher experiences a perturbation because her everyday knowledge that dissolving sugar does not increase volume conflicts with the intended mathematical model treating sugar as an equal volume part.', 'I wonder if sugar also gives some amount there? I have to think, does it really, there is no amount of sugar, it just ends up in the liquid.', 'proportional', 'mixture problems', 'mathematical modeling', 'in_service', 'grade 6', 'IJSME_Lundberg_2018_Transposition', 'Anna. L. V. Lundberg & Cecilia Kilhamn', '2018', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '569, 570, 574, 575', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Lundberg_2018_Transposition.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40429, 'Students hesitate and struggle to interpret or calculate division expressions when the quotient results in a decimal rather than an integer.', 'When evaluating 5 divided by 2, the student guesses ''three, no, ehh, is it one?''', 'rational', 'division', 'decimal quotients', 'in_service', 'grade 6', 'IJSME_Lundberg_2018_Transposition', 'Anna. L. V. Lundberg & Cecilia Kilhamn', '2018', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '571, 572', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Lundberg_2018_Transposition.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40430, 'PSTs incorrectly classified nonproportional linear additive relationships as proportional by over-attending to whether the variables simultaneously increased or decreased at a constant rate.', 'Feyza identified Graph I (y = 5 - x) as proportional ''Because it increases and decreases in a certain rate... Both of them are proportional because the increase and decrease are constant.''', 'proportional', 'differentiating proportional and nonproportional relationships', 'linear additive relationships', 'PST', 'university', 'IJSME_Arican_2018_Preservice', 'Muhammet Arican', '2018', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Arican_2018_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40431, 'PSTs incorrectly assumed an inversely proportional relationship is represented by a linear, additive decreasing graph instead of a curve.', 'Ece drew a straight, downward-sloping line to represent an inverse proportion, stating, ''Both direct and inverse proportion graphs should have straight lines.''', 'proportional', 'representing inversely proportional relationships', 'graphs', 'PST', 'university', 'IJSME_Arican_2018_Preservice', 'Muhammet Arican', '2018', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Arican_2018_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40432, 'PSTs mechanically applied cross-multiplication or across-multiplication algorithms without understanding the underlying multiplicative relationships.', '19 PSTs calculated an incorrect answer for Gear I using the across-multiplication strategy because they mistakenly assumed an inverse relationship.', 'proportional', 'solving proportion problems', 'algorithms', 'PST', 'university', 'IJSME_Arican_2018_Preservice', 'Muhammet Arican', '2018', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Arican_2018_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40433, 'PSTs failed to comprehend the mechanics of intertwined gears, mistakenly assuming that the gear rotating more times must have a greater number of smaller notches.', 'Esma rationalized her incorrect solution of 16 notches for Gear B by asserting it must have smaller-sized notches around it because it completes more revolutions.', 'proportional', 'inversely proportional relationships', 'gears', 'PST', 'university', 'IJSME_Arican_2018_Preservice', 'Muhammet Arican', '2018', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Arican_2018_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40434, 'PSTs hesitated to draw directly proportional graphs starting from the origin because the zero coordinate did not make sense within the realistic context of the problem.', 'Feyza stated, ''if the number of notches is zero, then we cannot talk about the gear. There is not a gear then.''', 'proportional', 'directly proportional relationships', 'graphs', 'PST', 'university', 'IJSME_Arican_2018_Preservice', 'Muhammet Arican', '2018', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'mixed', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Arican_2018_Preservice.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40435, 'Students interpret slope primarily as a parametric coefficient or behavior indicator rather than demonstrating a functional understanding of slope as a relationship between two covarying quantities.', 'A slope tells whether the graph of the equation is going in a negative or positive way.', 'calculus', 'slope', 'covariational reasoning', 'mixed', 'tertiary', 'IJSME_Nagle_2013_Calculus', 'COURTNEY NAGLE, DEBORAH MOORE-RUSSO, JANINE VIGLIETTI and KRISTI MARTIN', '2013', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '1491, 1501, 1507, 1510', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Nagle_2013_Calculus.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40436, 'Students struggle to apply the concept of slope in real-world contexts and infrequently evoke real-world situations as examples of how slope is used compared to their instructors.', 'When asked how slope is used, only 6% of students referenced a real-world situation compared to 23% of instructors.', 'algebraic', 'slope', 'real-world applications', 'mixed', 'tertiary', 'IJSME_Nagle_2013_Calculus', 'COURTNEY NAGLE, DEBORAH MOORE-RUSSO, JANINE VIGLIETTI and KRISTI MARTIN', '2013', 'International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education', 'sociocultural', 'secondary', '1503, 1504, 1509', 'pdfs/IJSME/IJSME_Nagle_2013_Calculus.pdf')).
