research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40574, 'Students interpret the slope (m) in a linear equation as the scale of the x-axis, assuming the interval between tick marks represents the rate of change.', 'Enrique wrote the equation y = 76 + 2x for a temperature graph because the x-axis tick marks went up by 2 (78, 80, 82), using 2 as the slope.', 'algebraic', 'linear functions', 'slope', 'student', 'high school', 'MTL_Lobato_2003_How', 'Joanne Lobato, Amy Burns Ellis, Ricardo Munoz', '2003', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '10, 11, 20, 21', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Lobato_2003_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40575, 'Students interpret the slope (m) as the difference between successive y-values (change in y) without coordinating it with the corresponding change in x-values.', 'When given a table of leaky faucet data with non-uniform time intervals, students calculated the difference between two arbitrary successive y-values (e.g., 10 oz) and used it as the slope.', 'algebraic', 'linear functions', 'slope', 'student', 'high school', 'MTL_Lobato_2003_How', 'Joanne Lobato, Amy Burns Ellis, Ricardo Munoz', '2003', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '11, 16, 17, 18, 23, 24', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Lobato_2003_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40576, 'Students view the slope formula as calculating two uncoordinated differences linked by the numerical operation of division, rather than mentally forming a ratio of covarying quantities.', 'A student might calculate the change in length as 4 and the change in weight as 20, but treat them as isolated sequences, failing to understand 4/20 as a composite unit of 0.2 cm per kg.', 'algebraic', 'linear functions', 'slope formula', 'student', 'high school', 'MTL_Lobato_2003_How', 'Joanne Lobato, Amy Burns Ellis, Ricardo Munoz', '2003', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '24, 25, 26, 27', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Lobato_2003_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40577, 'Students conflate the change in x-values of a function with the visual or numerical scale of the x-axis.', 'When setting up a graphing calculator window, a student used the table''s change in x-values (2.5) for the x-axis scale prompt (Xscl), arguing that both represented ''what it''s going up by.''', 'algebraic', 'graphing', 'scale and intervals', 'student', 'high school', 'MTL_Lobato_2003_How', 'Joanne Lobato, Amy Burns Ellis, Ricardo Munoz', '2003', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'sociocultural', 'secondary', '21, 22, 26', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Lobato_2003_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40578, 'Students treat linear inequalities as equations and incorrectly assume the solution is a single number rather than a range of values.', 'When solving 3 - 4x <= 6x - 7, a student provides the answer -5 instead of x >= 1, or for 3x <= 6, giving the answer 2.', 'algebraic', 'linear inequalities', 'solutions', 'student', 'Grade 9', 'MTL_Vaiyavutjamai_2006_Effects', 'Pongchawee Vaiyavutjamai, M. A. (Ken) Clements', '2006', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '130, 131, 132, 133, 134', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Vaiyavutjamai_2006_Effects.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40579, 'Students incorrectly reverse variables when translating verbal statements into mathematical symbols.', 'Translating the statement ''The number y is eight times the number x'' as x = 8y instead of y = 8x.', 'algebraic', 'translation', 'variables', 'student', 'Grade 9', 'MTL_Vaiyavutjamai_2006_Effects', 'Pongchawee Vaiyavutjamai, M. A. (Ken) Clements', '2006', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '137', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Vaiyavutjamai_2006_Effects.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40580, 'Students apply procedural algorithms for solving equations with fractional coefficients without relational understanding, relying on rote rules instead of mathematical principles.', 'A student explains they changed division to multiplication ''because it went to a different side'' but cannot check their answer or explain why multiplying by the least common multiple is mathematically permissible.', 'algebraic', 'linear equations', 'fractional coefficients', 'student', 'Grade 9', 'MTL_Vaiyavutjamai_2006_Effects', 'Pongchawee Vaiyavutjamai, M. A. (Ken) Clements', '2006', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '137, 138, 139, 140, 141', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Vaiyavutjamai_2006_Effects.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40581, 'When faced with an impossible task, students employ ''monster-barring'' by removing the problematic constraint or piece that causes the dead end so they can continue working, rather than concluding the task is impossible.', 'After realizing they do not have enough 2-unit tiles to fill the gaps created by the 5-unit tile, a student removes the 5-unit tile and says, ''Leave it away, anyway.''', 'geometric', 'tiling tasks', 'problem solving', 'student', 'Grades 3 and 4, Grades 7, 8, and 9', 'MTL_Stein_2006_Invisible', 'Martin Stein, Birgit Burchartz', '2006', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '71, 80', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Stein_2006_Invisible.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40582, 'Younger students formulate incomplete proofs by relying on tacit assumptions and hidden arguments, mistakenly believing that their physical actions during problem-solving adequately communicate the missing logical steps to the observer.', 'A student points at a tile and says, ''then this would have to be cut in the middle,'' tacitly assuming it makes no sense to move other tiles because they already tried that earlier.', 'other', 'mathematical argumentation', 'proofs', 'student', 'Grades 3 and 4, Grades 7, 8, and 9', 'MTL_Stein_2006_Invisible', 'Martin Stein, Birgit Burchartz', '2006', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'productive', 'primary', '81, 82, 83, 85', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Stein_2006_Invisible.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40583, 'Primary students lack confidence to declare a mathematical task impossible, leading them to either endlessly repeat senseless actions or fail on subsequent easier impossible tasks due to discouragement.', 'Students who were given a highly complex impossible big puzzle first were so discouraged that they failed to solve a much simpler small puzzle presented afterward.', 'other', 'problem solving', 'impossible tasks', 'student', 'Grades 3 and 4, Grades 7, 8, and 9', 'MTL_Stein_2006_Invisible', 'Martin Stein, Birgit Burchartz', '2006', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '77, 78, 79, 80', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Stein_2006_Invisible.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40584, 'Students apply an ''always borrow left'' syntactic bug where they borrow from the nearest non-zero column but improperly distribute the borrowed amounts across intermediate columns.', 'When solving 310 - 164, a student took 2 from the hundreds, added 9 to the tens, and 10 to the ones.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'regrouping', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'MTL_Peled_2005_It''s', 'Irit Peled, Bracha Segalis', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '218, 219', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Peled_2005_It''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40585, 'Students conceptualize decimal numbers as whole numbers and indiscriminately apply whole-number subtraction procedures without attending to place value constraints.', 'A student wrote the problem 18.2 - 1.82 vertically, added 0 to align it correctly, but then incorrectly took 2 from the ones instead of properly regrouping the tenths.', 'decimal', 'subtraction', 'regrouping', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'MTL_Peled_2005_It''s', 'Irit Peled, Bracha Segalis', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '218, 226, 227', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Peled_2005_It''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40586, 'Students do not understand that the regrouping process involves moving parts of equal value to preserve the total value of the number, failing to see the necessity or mechanics of taking from a larger unit.', 'A student struggles to subtract 7/8 from 4 1/8 because she incorrectly tries to subtract 7 from 1 without understanding how to regroup the whole number 4 to get more eighths.', 'fraction', 'subtraction', 'regrouping', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'MTL_Peled_2005_It''s', 'Irit Peled, Bracha Segalis', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '214, 215', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Peled_2005_It''s.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40587, 'Students improperly apply a buggy algorithm or incorrect splitting methods when subtracting numbers that bridge ten due to a lack of coordination between tens and ones.', 'When solving 62 - 48, a student might routinely apply the well-known buggy algorithm and incorrectly calculate it as 20 + 6.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'bridging ten', 'student', 'grade 3, grades 6 through 9, and grades 8 through 12', 'MTL_Gravemeijer_2016_Shortcomings', 'Koeno Gravemeijer, Geeke Bruin-Muurling, Jean-Marie Kraemer, Irene van Stiphout', '2016', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Gravemeijer_2016_Shortcomings.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40588, 'Students struggle to understand the relationship between fractions, multiplication, and division, treating fraction multiplication as independent isolated procedures and failing to recognize inverse operations.', 'When solving 31 x 17/31, students started by multiplying 31 and 17, apparently not realizing that multiplying by 1/31 is the inverse of multiplying by 31.', 'fraction', 'multiplication', 'inverse operations', 'student', 'grade 3, grades 6 through 9, and grades 8 through 12', 'MTL_Gravemeijer_2016_Shortcomings', 'Koeno Gravemeijer, Geeke Bruin-Muurling, Jean-Marie Kraemer, Irene van Stiphout', '2016', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Gravemeijer_2016_Shortcomings.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40589, 'Students demonstrate a limited understanding of the unit by failing to identify the fractional part of a shape if it is divided into unequal-sized pieces.', 'If a shape was divided into unequal sized parts, with the shaded part depicted as one third of one half, a large portion of students was unable to name the corresponding fraction.', 'fraction', 'the unit', 'area models', 'student', 'grade 3, grades 6 through 9, and grades 8 through 12', 'MTL_Gravemeijer_2016_Shortcomings', 'Koeno Gravemeijer, Geeke Bruin-Muurling, Jean-Marie Kraemer, Irene van Stiphout', '2016', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Gravemeijer_2016_Shortcomings.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40590, 'Students lack symbol sense and structure sense, failing to handle process-object duality and thereby incorrectly interpreting the structure of complex algebraic expressions by calculating partial values and multiplying them.', 'When simplifying the expression -6 - (5 - 4) * (-8) - 3, many students first calculated the values of -6 - (5 - 4) and (-8) - 3 yielding -7 and -11, and then multiplied both numbers.', 'algebraic', 'algebraic expressions', 'symbol sense', 'student', 'grade 3, grades 6 through 9, and grades 8 through 12', 'MTL_Gravemeijer_2016_Shortcomings', 'Koeno Gravemeijer, Geeke Bruin-Muurling, Jean-Marie Kraemer, Irene van Stiphout', '2016', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Gravemeijer_2016_Shortcomings.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40591, 'Students fail to recognize the mathematical structure of equations as extensions of previously learned forms, blindly expanding brackets instead of generalizing factorization rules like the zero product property.', 'To solve the cubic equation (x - 1)(x + 3)(x - 4) = 0, students tended to expand the brackets instead of generalizing the structure A * B = 0 to A * B * C = 0.', 'algebraic', 'solving equations', 'factorization', 'student', 'grade 3, grades 6 through 9, and grades 8 through 12', 'MTL_Gravemeijer_2016_Shortcomings', 'Koeno Gravemeijer, Geeke Bruin-Muurling, Jean-Marie Kraemer, Irene van Stiphout', '2016', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Gravemeijer_2016_Shortcomings.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40592, 'A student incorrectly applies the standard subtraction algorithm by subtracting the smaller digit from the larger digit in each column, regardless of the digits'' positions as minuend or subtrahend.', 'When subtracting 39 from 73 using the vertical algorithm, Marna subtracted 3 from 7 to get 4 in the tens column, and 3 from 9 to get 6 in the ones column, answering 46.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'standard algorithm', 'student', 'second-grade', 'MTL_Whitenack_2001_Coordinating', 'Joy Whitenack, Nancy Knipping, Sue Novinger', '2001', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'mixed', 'primary', '70, 71, 72', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Whitenack_2001_Coordinating.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40593, 'Students estimate task completion time for two combined workers or pipes by calculating the arithmetic average of their individual times, failing to convert time to rate.', 'When one pipe fills a tank in 8 hours and another in 4 hours, students estimate both working together will take 6 hours.', 'proportional', 'rates', 'task completion', 'student', 'undergraduates', 'MTL_Reed_2002_Building', 'Stephen K. Reed, Jonathan Cooke, Linda Jazo', '2002', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '317', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Reed_2002_Building.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40594, 'Students inappropriately apply proportional reasoning to estimate completion time when the fill rate changes during the task, such as when there is a side leak or a delay.', 'Using the proportion of the tank filled to estimate total completion time underestimates the time when a side leak slows the rate down.', 'proportional', 'proportional reasoning', 'varying rates', 'student', 'undergraduates', 'MTL_Reed_2002_Building', 'Stephen K. Reed, Jonathan Cooke, Linda Jazo', '2002', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '318, 328', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Reed_2002_Building.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40595, 'Students struggle to translate a complex mathematical situation, such as a tank with a leak partway up, into an appropriate pair of decomposed algebraic equations.', 'Only 14 of 34 students correctly identified that a leak 5 feet from the bottom of a 15-foot tank requires calculating the fill rate for 1/3 of the tank without loss and 2/3 of the tank with loss.', 'algebraic', 'equation construction', 'problem decomposition', 'student', 'undergraduates', 'MTL_Reed_2002_Building', 'Stephen K. Reed, Jonathan Cooke, Linda Jazo', '2002', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '326, 327', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Reed_2002_Building.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40596, 'Students erroneously believe that multiplication always results in a larger quantity, leading to incorrect operation choices in story problems.', 'Applying the ''multiplication makes bigger'' (MMB) idea to avoid multiplying by decimals less than 1.', 'decimal', 'multiplication', 'operation sense', 'mixed', 'elementary school, ninth-grade, secondary, undergraduate', 'MTL_Harel_2005_Advanced', 'Guershon Harel, Larry Sowder', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'mixed', 'primary', '43, 45, 46, 47', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Harel_2005_Advanced.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40597, 'Students compare decimals incorrectly by relying on whole number properties, such as the number of digits or the right-most place value.', 'Deciding that 4.21 is larger than 4.238 because hundredths are larger than thousandths.', 'decimal', 'comparing decimals', 'place value', 'mixed', 'elementary school, ninth-grade, secondary, undergraduate', 'MTL_Harel_2005_Advanced', 'Guershon Harel, Larry Sowder', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'passing', '41', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Harel_2005_Advanced.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40598, 'Students struggle to form equations for word problems due to an inability to coordinate unknown problem conditions with variable quantities.', 'A prospective teacher cannot algebraically express the distance of an unknown point x from 1, confusing the problem static condition with the variable expression (x + 4)/2.', 'algebraic', 'word problems', 'variables and unknowns', 'mixed', 'elementary school, ninth-grade, secondary, undergraduate', 'MTL_Harel_2005_Advanced', 'Guershon Harel, Larry Sowder', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'mixed', 'primary', '37, 38', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Harel_2005_Advanced.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40599, 'Students overgeneralize the distributive property or linear operations to inappropriate algebraic contexts.', 'Inferring that since 2(a + b) = 2a + 2b is valid, then (a + b)^2 = a^2 + b^2 must also be valid.', 'algebraic', 'distribution', 'exponents', 'mixed', 'elementary school, ninth-grade, secondary, undergraduate', 'MTL_Harel_2005_Advanced', 'Guershon Harel, Larry Sowder', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'passing', '32', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Harel_2005_Advanced.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40600, 'Students rely on an inductive proof scheme, using empirical observations, direct measurements, or numerical computations to justify a general mathematical assertion.', 'A student attempts to prove a logarithmic identity by substituting specific numbers, like checking if log(4*3*7) works, and assuming it applies generally.', 'algebraic', 'proofs', 'justification', 'mixed', 'elementary school, ninth-grade, secondary, undergraduate', 'MTL_Harel_2005_Advanced', 'Guershon Harel, Larry Sowder', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '30, 32', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Harel_2005_Advanced.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40601, 'Students apply additive reasoning in contexts that require proportional or multiplicative reasoning.', '', 'proportional', 'ratio and proportion', '', 'mixed', 'elementary school, ninth-grade, secondary, undergraduate', 'MTL_Harel_2005_Advanced', 'Guershon Harel, Larry Sowder', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'mixed', 'secondary', '34, 35, 36', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Harel_2005_Advanced.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40602, 'Students lack the units-locating and organizing-by-composites processes necessary to mentally structure a 2D array into rows and columns, causing them to count along random paths and lose track of the unit squares.', 'When trying to predict how many squares would cover a rectangle, Katy pointed and counted randomly, predicting 30, but on a second count got 24, and on a third got 27.', 'measurement', 'area', 'spatial structuring', 'student', 'elementary school, second graders, fifth graders', 'MTL_Battista_2004_Applying', 'Michael T. Battista', '2004', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'mixed', 'primary', '193, 194', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Battista_2004_Applying.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40603, 'Students lack the spatial coordination to build a mental model of 3D arrays from 2D views, causing them to double-count or triple-count cubes that appear on multiple faces of a building.', 'Jeff counted all visible cube faces on the six sides of a cube building, double-counting the edge cubes and triple-counting the corner cubes.', 'measurement', 'volume', 'spatial structuring', 'student', 'elementary school, second graders, fifth graders', 'MTL_Battista_2004_Applying', 'Michael T. Battista', '2004', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'mixed', 'primary', '194', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Battista_2004_Applying.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40604, 'Students cannot conceptualize what is actually being counted when applying the standard volume algorithm, leading to confusion about why corner cubes are not counted three times or leading to the overgeneralization of the algorithm in non-standard contexts.', 'Bethany was puzzled by the length times width times height algorithm, feeling that the corner cube gets counted once for each dimension. Other students incorrectly apply 3 x 3 x 5 to find the volume using packages of 2 cubes.', 'measurement', 'volume', 'volume formula', 'student', 'elementary school, second graders, fifth graders', 'MTL_Battista_2004_Applying', 'Michael T. Battista', '2004', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'mixed', 'primary', '200, 201', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Battista_2004_Applying.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40605, 'Students hold a ''sample space misconception'', believing that the outcomes of a random experiment are dependent on previously observed outcomes, leading them to inappropriately exclude previously obtained results from the sample space.', 'When asked what colors she could get on a second spin, Mpho wrote only red, yellow, and blue, saying that she had already obtained a green on the first trial.', 'probability', 'sample space', 'independence and randomness', 'student', '4th and 5th grades', 'MTL_Polaki_2002_Using', 'Mokaeane V. Polaki', '2002', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '300, 301, 310', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Polaki_2002_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40606, 'Students rely on a weak ''part-part schema'' when comparing probabilities across different sample spaces, erroneously comparing the absolute number of favorable outcomes without relating them to their respective total sample spaces.', 'Mpho chose Container B (5 red, 6 blue) over Container A (3 red, 3 blue) for drawing a red chip simply because ''It has 5 red chips and container A has 3 red chips,'' directly comparing the parts.', 'probability', 'comparing probabilities', 'part-part vs. part-whole reasoning', 'student', '4th and 5th grades', 'MTL_Polaki_2002_Using', 'Mokaeane V. Polaki', '2002', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '301, 302, 304, 305', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Polaki_2002_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40607, 'Students use numerical probability expressions instrumentally as qualitative labels rather than quantitative proportional measures, interpreting informal language like ''3 out of 8'' as a direct comparison between the numbers 3 and 5.', 'Lineo claimed that a coin had a better chance of winning than a die because ''It is easier to get what you want because it has only two sides, but the die has many sides'', despite previously assigning the probabilities 1/2 and 3/6.', 'probability', 'numerical probability', 'fractions as probability measures', 'student', '4th and 5th grades', 'MTL_Polaki_2002_Using', 'Mokaeane V. Polaki', '2002', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '306, 309, 310', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Polaki_2002_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40608, 'Students interpret the empty number line merely as a way of notating collection-based calculations rather than as a continuous linear-counting model, leading to varying and incompatible numerical interpretations.', 'When solving 90 - 8, some students interpreted a counting-back jump as taking away 2 or 3 candies, while others reasoned that taking away 8 from the 9th decade (which they identified as 80-89) would leave 81 candies.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'empty number line', 'student', 'first-grade', 'MTL_Gravemeijer_1999_How', 'Koeno Gravemeijer', '1999', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'productive', 'primary', '166, 167', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Gravemeijer_1999_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40609, 'Students face ambiguity over whether to mark a specified quantity on the discrete item itself or at the boundary marking the end of the interval, reflecting a tension between ordinal and cardinal interpretations of measurement.', 'When asked to mark 53 on a depicted bead string, students debated whether the mark should go on the 53rd bead or next to it.', 'measurement', 'linear measurement', 'cardinality', 'student', 'first-grade', 'MTL_Gravemeijer_1999_How', 'Koeno Gravemeijer', '1999', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'productive', 'secondary', '167, 168', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Gravemeijer_1999_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40610, 'Learners experience model-reality mismatches when their existing models fail to usefully describe or explain an experience, prompting changes to the model.', 'Learners'' naive theories of motion (similar to Aristotelian ideas) mismatch with expert Newtonian models, but are only revised when they become less than useful for the learner''s own purposes.', 'other', 'model-reality mismatch', '', 'other', '', 'MTL_Lesh_2003_Beyond', 'Richard Lesh, Helen M. Doerr, Guadalupe Carmona, Margret Hjalmarson', '2003', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'productive', 'primary', '220, 221', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Lesh_2003_Beyond.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40611, 'Learners experience within-model mismatches when integrating new elements, interpreting new quantities, or shifting attention across representational media, which drives the differentiation and development of the model.', 'Students revised their notions of a ''best worker'' when they saw the restructured representations of their data in a spreadsheet during the Summer Jobs problem.', 'statistics', 'data representation', '', 'other', '', 'MTL_Lesh_2003_Beyond', 'Richard Lesh, Helen M. Doerr, Guadalupe Carmona, Margret Hjalmarson', '2003', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'productive', 'primary', '221, 222', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Lesh_2003_Beyond.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40612, 'Learners experience between-model mismatches when distinct ways of thinking about a problem are inconsistent, prompting them to debate, select, and combine elements into a new, more robust model.', 'Three students defining a ''floater'' paper airplane debated and refined successive definitions until incorporating elements given by all team members.', 'other', 'modeling and definitions', '', 'other', '', 'MTL_Lesh_2003_Beyond', 'Richard Lesh, Helen M. Doerr, Guadalupe Carmona, Margret Hjalmarson', '2003', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'productive', 'primary', '222', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Lesh_2003_Beyond.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40613, 'A student rejects the mathematical definition that .999... equals 1 because her intuition, based on earlier division procedures, conflicts with the formal definition.', 'Stephanie argued that .333... equals 1/3 because 1 divided by 3 is .333..., but 1 divided by 1 is not .999... so they cannot be equal.', 'decimal', 'infinite decimals', 'equivalence', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'MTL_Edwards_2005_Advanced', 'Barbara S. Edwards, Ed Dubinsky, Michael A. McDonald', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '17', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Edwards_2005_Advanced.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40614, 'A student cannot conceive of an infinite union of sets as a completed process, preventing her from rigorously proving a set equality.', 'Emily asked, When do you ever reach infinity? when trying to understand the infinite union.', 'discrete', 'infinite processes', 'set theory', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'MTL_Edwards_2005_Advanced', 'Barbara S. Edwards, Ed Dubinsky, Michael A. McDonald', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '20, 21', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Edwards_2005_Advanced.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40615, 'Students struggle with the concept of limits, relying on empirical evaluation of finite processes rather than viewing the limit as a coordinated pair of processes.', 'A student trying to reason about the limit of (x^2-1)/(x-1) as x approaches 1 might plug in 1.1, 1.01, 1.001 to guess the limit is 1/2.', 'calculus', 'limits', 'infinite processes', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'MTL_Edwards_2005_Advanced', 'Barbara S. Edwards, Ed Dubinsky, Michael A. McDonald', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '21', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Edwards_2005_Advanced.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40616, 'A student is unable to see beyond an imperfect physical model of a mathematical space, erroneously assigning the physical properties of the model to the mathematical space itself.', 'Jim wrote that there were no real symmetries on the hyperbolic plane because the crocheted model shape was always changing, wobbly, and stretchy.', 'geometric', 'hyperbolic space', 'models', 'student', 'undergraduate', 'MTL_Edwards_2005_Advanced', 'Barbara S. Edwards, Ed Dubinsky, Michael A. McDonald', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '24', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Edwards_2005_Advanced.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40617, 'Teachers struggle to define the whole when multiple objects are shared, becoming confused about whether the resulting fraction should be named relative to a single object or the entire set of objects.', 'Teachers debated whether 2 pretzels shared among 6 children meant each child gets 2/6 or 2/12, at times incorrectly proposing that 2/6 and 2/12 might be equivalent fractions.', 'fraction', 'defining the whole', 'sharing multiple objects', 'in_service', 'second-, third-, and fifth-grade', 'MTL_Fernandez_2005_Lesson', 'Clea Fernandez', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '277, 278, 279, 280', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Fernandez_2005_Lesson.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40618, 'Teachers view equal sharing scenarios involving whole units exclusively as division and conceptually separate from fractions, unless the sharing explicitly forces objects to be broken into smaller fractional parts.', 'Teachers rejected the problem ''4 cookies shared among 4 people'' because they believed ''it sounds more like division than fractions.''', 'fraction', 'fractions vs. division', 'equal sharing', 'in_service', 'second-, third-, and fifth-grade', 'MTL_Fernandez_2005_Lesson', 'Clea Fernandez', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '272, 277', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Fernandez_2005_Lesson.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40619, 'Students bypass fractional thinking in equal sharing tasks by measuring the object and applying whole-number division to the total length.', 'Students measured a 30-inch pretzel rod and decided each of three children gets 10 inches, rather than conceptualizing the share as one-third of the pretzel.', 'fraction', 'equal sharing', 'measurement', 'in_service', 'second-, third-, and fifth-grade', 'MTL_Fernandez_2005_Lesson', 'Clea Fernandez', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '275, 276', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Fernandez_2005_Lesson.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40620, 'Teachers incorrectly assume that repeatedly halving a fractional piece and adding the pieces together will sum exactly to a whole in finite steps, failing to recognize the remaining missing fraction.', 'A teacher thought the pieces 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, etc., visually looked like they added up to the whole piece of licorice, until a guest pointed out that there is always a piece left out.', 'fraction', 'repeated halving', 'infinite series', 'in_service', 'second-, third-, and fifth-grade', 'MTL_Fernandez_2005_Lesson', 'Clea Fernandez', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '283, 284', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Fernandez_2005_Lesson.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40621, 'A student reverts to a less efficient counting-all strategy after briefly using counting-on, indicating a lack of genuine conceptual reflection and a rejection of the more advanced strategy when explicit modeling is introduced.', 'Felicia used counting-on for challenge items like 5 + 22, but reverted to counting-all for single-digit combinations like 3 + 5 and declared ''You can''t do it that way'' when counting-on was modeled for her.', 'whole_number', 'addition', 'counting strategies', 'other', '', 'MTL_Baroody_2004_Comments', 'Arthur J. Baroody, Michael Cibulskis, Meng-lung Lai, Xia Li', '2004', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'passing', '238', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Baroody_2004_Comments.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40622, 'Students struggle to explicitly define the whole in fraction problems and experience confusion when task requirements unexpectedly force them to switch between different wholes.', 'Laura identified 3/4 of 1/4 as 3/10, perhaps creating a new scaled-down whole instead of viewing it as a fraction of her partner''s original whole.', 'fraction', 'defining the whole', 'fraction of a fraction', 'other', '', 'MTL_Baroody_2004_Comments', 'Arthur J. Baroody, Michael Cibulskis, Meng-lung Lai, Xia Li', '2004', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'mixed', 'secondary', '241, 242', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Baroody_2004_Comments.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40623, 'Children experience confusion and fail to abstract the cardinality principle when counting objects is imposed before they understand verbal number recognition.', 'Children recount the collection when asked ''How many?'' because they do not understand the switch in meaning of the last number word from an ordinal step to a cardinal total.', 'whole_number', 'counting', 'cardinality principle', 'other', '', 'MTL_Baroody_2004_Comments', 'Arthur J. Baroody, Michael Cibulskis, Meng-lung Lai, Xia Li', '2004', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '248', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Baroody_2004_Comments.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40624, 'Students adapt a mathematical representation as a rote notational system rather than as a meaningful model of mental activity, misinterpreting the model''s structure.', 'Students interpreted an empty number line diagram for ''90 - ? = 88'' incorrectly as take away 3 by reasoning ''take away candy number 90, candy number 89, and candy number 88''.', 'whole_number', 'subtraction', 'empty number line', 'other', '', 'MTL_Baroody_2004_Comments', 'Arthur J. Baroody, Michael Cibulskis, Meng-lung Lai, Xia Li', '2004', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'mixed', 'secondary', '235', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Baroody_2004_Comments.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40625, 'Students can execute the symbolic motions of a formal proof without understanding its epistemic function, meaning the proof fails to warrant the mathematical truth for them.', 'A student can fluently reproduce a proof by induction for the sum of the first n integers, or perform algebraic manipulations, but states ''I know what to do but not what it means!''', 'other', 'mathematical proof', 'warranting', 'other', '', 'MTL_Rodd_2000_Mathematical', 'M. M. Rodd', '2000', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '224, 225, 235', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Rodd_2000_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40626, 'Students rely on empirical generalization to justify mathematical propositions, mistakenly believing that observing limited cases or procedurally generating a pattern sufficiently warrants mathematical truth.', 'Calculating that a set of terms in a sequence gets closer and closer to zero and erroneously concluding the sequence has a limit of zero.', 'other', 'justification', 'empirical generalization', 'other', '', 'MTL_Rodd_2000_Mathematical', 'M. M. Rodd', '2000', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '224, 226, 227, 228', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Rodd_2000_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40627, 'Students make errors during a-linguistic epistemic visualization, such as acquiring a false belief from a mental image or overgeneralizing from an insufficiently general visualization.', 'Mentally picturing a geometric kite as too rhombus-like and mistakenly concluding that the diagonals of its midpoint quadrilateral are parallel to the kite''s sides.', 'geometric', 'visualization', 'geometric properties', 'other', '', 'MTL_Rodd_2000_Mathematical', 'M. M. Rodd', '2000', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '239, 240', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Rodd_2000_Mathematical.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40628, 'Parents conceptualize school mathematics almost entirely as rote computational procedures, leading them to reject or fail to understand conceptual models or multiple solution strategies as valid school mathematics.', 'Betty discouraged her granddaughter from using arrays (''little sticks'') for multiplication, instructing her instead that ''multiplication is memory''.', 'whole_number', 'multiplication', 'arrays', 'other', 'kindergartners, second grade, third grade', 'MTL_Remillard_2006_Old', 'Janine T. Remillard, Kara Jackson', '2006', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '241, 242, 245, 249, 250, 251', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Remillard_2006_Old.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40629, 'Parents believe that a distributed practice (spiral) curriculum is detrimental to student learning, preferring traditional mastery-based instruction with extended repetition.', 'Beverly criticized the spiral approach by comparing it to teaching her daughter to drive a car for one day in January and then expecting her to pass the test in June.', 'other', 'pedagogical beliefs', 'spiral curriculum', 'other', 'kindergartners, second grade, third grade', 'MTL_Remillard_2006_Old', 'Janine T. Remillard, Kara Jackson', '2006', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '246, 247, 250', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Remillard_2006_Old.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40630, 'Students overgeneralize from integer operations to erroneously believe that multiplication always produces a result greater than both factors.', 'When asked whether multiplication always produces a bigger number, a student initially answers ''A bigger one'', though the class eventually realizes ''It depends on the numbers'' when decimals are involved.', 'decimal', 'multiplication', 'operation sense', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'MTL_Bonotto_2005_How', 'Cinzia Bonotto', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '318, 334, 335, 336', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Bonotto_2005_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40631, 'Students struggle to compare and order decimal numbers, failing to understand the relationship between symbols and their referents due to a rote extension of place-value conventions.', 'When asked to order the sequence 0.15; 1; 0.1; 1.5; 0.5; 1.05, the majority of elementary and middle school students made mistakes.', 'decimal', 'ordering', 'magnitude', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'MTL_Bonotto_2005_How', 'Cinzia Bonotto', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '318, 319', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Bonotto_2005_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40632, 'Students hold the misconception that in any division problem, the larger number must always be divided by the smaller number.', '', 'decimal', 'division', 'operation sense', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'MTL_Bonotto_2005_How', 'Cinzia Bonotto', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'passing', '318', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Bonotto_2005_How.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40633, 'Students use a single variable to represent time but implicitly shift the zero reference point between different moving objects, leading to contradictory equations and confusion.', 'Mr. Green used the variable t to refer to the boy''s time on the clock, but then confused it with the time taken, not realizing he was taking t to have sliding meanings.', 'algebraic', 'rate of change word problems', 'reference points', 'student', 'graduate students', 'MTL_Presmeg_2001_Visualization', 'Norma C. Presmeg, Patricia E. Balderas-Canas', '2001', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '297, 303, 304', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Presmeg_2001_Visualization.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40634, 'When setting up algebraic equations for a problem where one person gives items to another, students initially fail to account for the fact that the giver''s quantity decreases while the receiver''s quantity increases.', 'Mr. Silver initially did not take the decrease of the giver''s walnuts into account in his first equation.', 'algebraic', 'system of equations', 'transfer of quantities', 'student', 'graduate students', 'MTL_Presmeg_2001_Visualization', 'Norma C. Presmeg, Patricia E. Balderas-Canas', '2001', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '298, 301', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Presmeg_2001_Visualization.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40635, 'Students construct idiosyncratic grounding metaphors that introduce ambiguity, preventing them from exploiting the visual model''s potential to solve the problem and instead constraining their reasoning.', 'Mr. Green drew in the hands of the clocks, which did not help because the confusion lay in whether the time started with the boy''s departure or his brother''s, and the clock faces did not address the discrepancy with his equations.', 'other', 'visual reasoning', 'metaphors', 'student', 'graduate students', 'MTL_Presmeg_2001_Visualization', 'Norma C. Presmeg, Patricia E. Balderas-Canas', '2001', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '309, 310', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Presmeg_2001_Visualization.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40636, 'Teachers believe that incorporating physical manipulatives into instruction automatically aligns with reform practices, even when they direct students to use the materials in a strictly algorithmic, procedural manner without conceptual connection.', 'A teacher instructed students exactly how to divide 84 chips into three equal groups to find two-thirds of 84, bypassing any opportunity for students to explore or construct meaning for themselves.', 'other', 'pedagogical beliefs', 'use of manipulatives', 'in_service', 'fourth grade, middle school', 'MTL_Schorr_2003_Using', 'Roberta Y. Schorr, Karen Koellner-Clark', '2003', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '192, 193, 194', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Schorr_2003_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40637, 'Teachers interpret the reform practice of ''listening to student thinking'' as merely having students recite the procedural steps used to find a correct answer, rather than using it to probe conceptual understanding or fuel mathematical discourse.', 'Roger believed he was effectively using student thinking when he had a student share her step-by-step arithmetic procedure for solving a proportion problem, using it only as a barometer to check if she got the right answer.', 'other', 'pedagogical beliefs', 'student discourse', 'in_service', 'fourth grade, middle school', 'MTL_Schorr_2003_Using', 'Roberta Y. Schorr, Karen Koellner-Clark', '2003', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '202, 203, 204, 205', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Schorr_2003_Using.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40638, 'Students equate measuring with the physical act of counting steps rather than covering an amount of space, leading them to miscount the placement of the first foot.', 'Some students did not count the placement of the first foot, starting their counting with ''one'' on the second foot.', 'measurement', 'linear measurement', 'pacing', 'student', '', 'MTL_Gravemeijer_2004_Local', 'Koeno Gravemeijer', '2004', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'productive', 'primary', '122, 123', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Gravemeijer_2004_Local.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40639, 'Students are unable to mentally subdivide a measurement tool when it physically extends past the object being measured, because they view measuring as strictly tied to the physical placement of the tool.', 'When measuring with a footstrip of five paces, students ran into problems if the space to be measured was shorter than the physical extension of the footstrip, unable to mentally cut it.', 'measurement', 'linear measurement', 'subdividing units', 'student', '', 'MTL_Gravemeijer_2004_Local', 'Koeno Gravemeijer', '2004', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '123', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Gravemeijer_2004_Local.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40640, 'Students associate a number word with the sequential placement of a measurement tool rather than the accumulated amount of space covered, leading to errors when a unit extends past the item.', 'When a 10-cube Smurf bar extended beyond the item on the second iteration, students counted the extra cubes as ''21, 22, 23'' instead of ''11, 12, 13'', treating ''20'' as the word for the second placement.', 'measurement', 'linear measurement', 'iterating units', 'student', '', 'MTL_Gravemeijer_2004_Local', 'Koeno Gravemeijer', '2004', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '123', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Gravemeijer_2004_Local.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40641, 'Students incorrectly quantify the spatial gap between two numbers on a measurement strip by counting the hash marks (lines) instead of the intervals (spaces).', 'When finding the difference between two lengths, some students counted the lines between the numbers on the measurement strip rather than the spaces.', 'measurement', 'finding the difference', 'quantifying gaps', 'student', '', 'MTL_Gravemeijer_2004_Local', 'Koeno Gravemeijer', '2004', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'productive', 'secondary', '123, 124', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Gravemeijer_2004_Local.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40642, 'Students struggle to interpret the hash marks on an empty number line, unable to resolve whether they signify discrete cardinal quantities or ordinal positions.', 'Without the prior bead string activity, students lacked the means to resolve whether a hash mark labeled 38 meant 38 objects (like candies) or the thirty-eighth object.', 'whole_number', 'number line', 'cardinal and ordinal numbers', 'student', '', 'MTL_Gravemeijer_2004_Local', 'Koeno Gravemeijer', '2004', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '117, 118', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Gravemeijer_2004_Local.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40643, 'Students use a guess-and-check strategy to generate algebraic rules, relying solely on empirical validation of a few instances without understanding the general relation or connecting it to the problem context.', 'Brett guessed the rule =A6 * 4 + 2 for the theater seats problem by blindly testing numbers until it worked for the first row, but could not adapt it when the rate of change was altered.', 'algebraic', 'generalization', 'patterning', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'MTL_Lannin_2005_Generalization', 'John K. Lannin', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '233, 244, 245, 247, 251, 252', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Lannin_2005_Generalization.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40644, 'Students apply a whole-object strategy, using proportional reasoning to multiply a known term to find a larger term without adjusting for the overcounting of shared parts.', 'Angela multiplied the 42 stickers on a rod of length 10 by 2 to find the number of stickers for a rod of length 20, failing to adjust for the joined ends.', 'algebraic', 'generalization', 'explicit rules', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'MTL_Lannin_2005_Generalization', 'John K. Lannin', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '233, 234, 241, 252', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Lannin_2005_Generalization.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40645, 'Students demonstrate a shallow understanding of arithmetic operations, guessing operations to fit the numbers rather than interpreting the operations'' meaning within the problem context.', 'In the pizza sharing problem, Angela tried dividing 72 by 4, got a decimal, and then arbitrarily reversed it to 4 divided by 72 without considering the contextual meaning of sharing pizza.', 'whole_number', 'operations', 'division', 'student', 'sixth-grade', 'MTL_Lannin_2005_Generalization', 'John K. Lannin', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '246, 247, 252', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Lannin_2005_Generalization.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40646, 'Students incorrectly believe that a dropped object falls at a constant speed right from the moment it is released, rather than gradually accelerating due to gravity.', 'Hilda''s initial interpretation was that the ball dropped at a constant speed, drawing her graph so that ''as soon as it starts it is the same speed all of the way down.''', 'algebraic', 'rate of change', 'kinematics', 'student', 'precalculus', 'MTL_Doerr_1999_Understanding', 'Helen M. Doerr, Joseph S. Tripp', '1999', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '241, 246, 248, 250', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Doerr_1999_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40647, 'Students believe that a ball tossed into the air stays at its highest point for a perceptible duration of time, leading them to draw a horizontal plateau at the peak of a position-time graph.', 'Hilda drew a flat top on her graph and stated, ''when you toss a ball in the air, it kind of stays, it is in one place... it doesn''t move anywhere, but it stays there for a certain period of time.''', 'algebraic', 'graphing functions', 'position-time graphs', 'student', 'precalculus', 'MTL_Doerr_1999_Understanding', 'Helen M. Doerr, Joseph S. Tripp', '1999', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '242, 243', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Doerr_1999_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40648, 'Students struggle to correctly determine the period and frequency of a trigonometric function from its graph, confusing the counting of full cycles within an interval.', 'Mindy began by incorrectly interpreting the graph of f(x) = sin(4x) as f(x) = sin(2x) before a peer prompted her to count the ''hill and a valley'' cycles up to 2pi.', 'algebraic', 'trigonometric functions', 'period and frequency', 'student', 'precalculus', 'MTL_Doerr_1999_Understanding', 'Helen M. Doerr, Joseph S. Tripp', '1999', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '238, 239', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Doerr_1999_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40649, 'Students doubt the accuracy or reality of data presented by a graphing calculator, suspecting the continuous graphical curve might be an artifact of the technology rather than a true physical representation.', 'Hilda questioned the calculator graph by asking, ''does this really exist or is it just from looking at like the graph... just because it is in a graph doesn''t mean, like, it is a calculator.''', 'other', 'technology-based representations', 'epistemological beliefs', 'student', 'precalculus', 'MTL_Doerr_1999_Understanding', 'Helen M. Doerr, Joseph S. Tripp', '1999', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'productive', 'primary', '244, 245', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Doerr_1999_Understanding.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40650, 'Students hold the everyday intuitive model that motion implies an active force, and that without a continuous force, there is no motion.', 'The authors note that many students believe ''objects do not move without force'' and that ''motion implies a force and, equivalently, that without a force there is no motion.''', 'other', 'kinematics', 'Newtonian mechanics', 'student', 'precalculus', 'MTL_Doerr_1999_Understanding', 'Helen M. Doerr, Joseph S. Tripp', '1999', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'mixed', 'secondary', '234, 235', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Doerr_1999_Understanding.pdf')).
