research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40659, 'Students employ superficial and nonrealistic modeling strategies, blindly applying calculations to the numbers in a word problem without monitoring progress or considering real-world constraints.', 'The student glimpses the problem, quickly deciding what calculations to perform with the numbers given in the problem statement, and then proceeds with these calculations without considering any alternatives even if no progress is made at all.', 'other', 'word problems', 'mathematical modeling', 'student', 'fifth graders', 'MTL_Verschaffel_1999_Learning', 'Lieven Verschaffel, Erik De Corte, Sabien Lasure, Griet Van Vaerenbergh, Hedwig Bogaerts, Elie Ratinckx', '1999', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '196, 197, 199', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Verschaffel_1999_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40660, 'Students hold counterproductive beliefs about mathematics, such as assuming problems have only one right answer, there is only one correct way to solve a problem, and that school mathematics is disconnected from the real world.', '', 'other', 'epistemological beliefs', 'problem solving', 'student', 'fifth graders', 'MTL_Verschaffel_1999_Learning', 'Lieven Verschaffel, Erik De Corte, Sabien Lasure, Griet Van Vaerenbergh, Hedwig Bogaerts, Elie Ratinckx', '1999', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'sociocultural', 'primary', '196, 197, 203', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Verschaffel_1999_Learning.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40661, 'Students view fractions as an arrangement where a whole is partitioned into equal parts, rather than as a multiplicative quantity, leading them to not recognize that differently shaped halves of the same whole must be equal in size.', 'Students agreed that a square cut into two rectangles or two triangles both represented halves, but disagreed on whether the rectangular half and the triangular half were the same size.', 'fraction', 'fraction as a quantity', 'equivalence', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'MTL_Simon_2006_Key', 'Martin A. Simon', '2006', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '360, 361, 365', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Simon_2006_Key.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40662, 'Students understand fractions only as parts of a single whole, making them unable to conceptualize improper fractions.', 'When adding one more fifth to a whole made of five congruent parts, students refer to the six parts as 6 sixths instead of 6/5.', 'fraction', 'improper fractions', '', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'MTL_Simon_2006_Key', 'Martin A. Simon', '2006', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '361', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Simon_2006_Key.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40663, 'Students invoke an additive comparison for ratio problems when the situation does not easily trigger an identical-groups conception.', 'When asked which of three different sized lots appears most square, students use additive comparison because it is not obvious there is a recipe that can be repeated.', 'proportional', 'ratio', 'additive comparison', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'MTL_Simon_2006_Key', 'Martin A. Simon', '2006', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '368', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Simon_2006_Key.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40664, 'Students can produce a fraction of a fraction but struggle to name the resulting share as a fraction of the original whole because they are unable to reverse their partitioning operations through multiple levels.', 'Arthur partitioned a stick into seven parts, pulled out three parts, partitioned each into four parts and pulled out three for a share, but could not name the share as a fraction of the original whole.', 'fraction', 'fraction of a fraction', 'recursive partitioning', 'student', 'Grades 4 and 5', 'MTL_Olive_1999_From', 'John Olive', '1999', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'productive', 'primary', '291, 292, 293, 294', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Olive_1999_From.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40665, 'Students learn the standard algorithm for multiplying fractions as a rote whole-number procedure that is separated in their minds from their quantity-based operational models.', 'Nathan calculated 2/3 times 4/5 as 8/15 using the standard algorithm but could not connect this to his operational scheme, instead pulling out two separate fraction bars and stating he would put an ''x'' between them.', 'fraction', 'fraction multiplication', 'standard algorithm', 'student', 'Grades 4 and 5', 'MTL_Olive_1999_From', 'John Olive', '1999', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'mixed', 'secondary', '303, 304', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Olive_1999_From.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40666, 'Students inappropriately calculate a combined or weighted average by simply adding the averages of the subgroups and dividing by the number of subgroups or the total number of individuals, ignoring the relative weights and total sums.', 'In the Elevator Problem, teachers predicted students might incorrectly add the women''s average weight (120) and the men''s average weight (180) and either divide the sum by 2 or divide it by the total number of people (10).', 'statistics', 'arithmetic average', 'weighted average', 'in_service', 'sixth-grade, seventh-grade', 'MTL_Cai_2005_Chinese', 'Jinfa Cai', '2005', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '148', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Cai_2005_Chinese.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40667, 'Students interpret a decimal mean in a discrete context literally, attempting to map the fractional part to a real-world physical entity rather than understanding it as a mathematical abstraction.', 'When asked how a family could have an average of 2.3 children, students suggested it meant they had ''two full grown kids, and one''s not full grown yet'' or that the mother ''might be pregnant.''', 'statistics', 'mean', 'interpreting decimals in discrete contexts', 'student', 'Grades 3 to 9', 'MTL_Watson_2000_Longitudinal', 'Jane M. Watson, Jonathan B. Moritz', '2000', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'productive', 'primary', '20, 23, 32, 34, 35, 36', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Watson_2000_Longitudinal.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40668, 'Students attempt to calculate a weighted mean by simply adding the averages of the given subgroups and dividing by the number of subgroups, ignoring the unequal sample sizes.', 'To find the average TV viewing time for 25 country students watching 8 hours and 75 city students watching 4 hours, a student simply added 8 and 4, divided by 2, and concluded the average was 6 hours.', 'statistics', 'weighted mean', 'algorithm application', 'student', 'Grades 3 to 9', 'MTL_Watson_2000_Longitudinal', 'Jane M. Watson, Jonathan B. Moritz', '2000', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '24, 27, 34, 38, 42', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Watson_2000_Longitudinal.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40669, 'Students can apply the add-and-divide mean algorithm in a forward direction to summarize a dataset but are unable to reverse the algorithm to find the sum from a given average and sample size.', 'When given the average of 2.3 children for 10 families, students guess numbers of children for individual families but fail to multiply 2.3 by 10 to establish the necessary total of 23 children.', 'statistics', 'mean', 'reversing the algorithm', 'student', 'Grades 3 to 9', 'MTL_Watson_2000_Longitudinal', 'Jane M. Watson, Jonathan B. Moritz', '2000', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '12, 24, 25, 41, 42', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Watson_2000_Longitudinal.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40670, 'Students rely exclusively on colloquial meanings and storytelling to define average, failing to conceptualize it as a quantitative measure that mathematically summarizes a data set.', 'A student described an average of TV watching times by telling a story about disguising a camera on a student, rather than discussing the numerical measurement or combination of data.', 'statistics', 'concept of average', 'representativeness', 'student', 'Grades 3 to 9', 'MTL_Watson_2000_Longitudinal', 'Jane M. Watson, Jonathan B. Moritz', '2000', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '20, 22, 23', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Watson_2000_Longitudinal.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40671, 'Students incorrectly compute two-digit multiplication by multiplying only the tens together and the ones together, omitting the cross products (tens by ones and ones by tens).', 'For 26 x 38, Pete computed 60 + 48 = 108, likely by multiplying the tens numbers (20 and 30) to get 60, and ones to get 48, combining two errors: erring on magnitudes and omitting products of tens and ones.', 'whole_number', 'two-digit multiplication', 'partial products', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'MTL_Izsak_2004_Teaching', 'Andrew Izsak', '2004', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '64, 65, 66', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Izsak_2004_Teaching.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40672, 'Students fail to coordinate the groups of imaginary unit squares with the individual addends in numeric labels, inappropriately using the same addend for multiple sub-regions without regard to the actual dimensions of the quadrant.', 'Jodi arrived at 10 x 6 = 60 for the 6-by-30 quadrant by using the ''6'' from her 10+10+6 label and one of the ''10''s from her 10+10+10+8 label, ignoring the actual dimensions of the quadrant.', 'whole_number', 'area models', 'determining areas of quadrants', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'MTL_Izsak_2004_Teaching', 'Andrew Izsak', '2004', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '68, 69, 70, 71', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Izsak_2004_Teaching.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40673, 'Students assign partial products to regions of an area model based purely on the relative size of the quadrant and the relative size of the factors, rather than the explicit dimensions of the specific region.', 'Tom reversed the products for the 6-by-30 and 20-by-8 quadrants, explaining he used large factors for the large quadrant and small factors for the small quadrant.', 'whole_number', 'area models', 'assigning partial products', 'student', 'fourth-grade', 'MTL_Izsak_2004_Teaching', 'Andrew Izsak', '2004', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'secondary', '71, 72', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Izsak_2004_Teaching.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40674, 'Students struggle with compensation logic, failing to understand that dividing a whole into a larger number of equal parts results in smaller individual portions.', 'Children continue to struggle with this relation, for example, judging the fraction 1/6 to be larger than 1/3, and using ineffective strategies for creating partitions of the appropriate size.', 'fraction', 'partitioning', 'compensation logic', 'student', 'kindergartners, first graders', 'MTL_Wing_2004_Young', 'Rachel E. Wing, Carole R. Beal', '2004', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'deficit', 'primary', '1, 2, 7, 10', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Wing_2004_Young.pdf')).
