research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40745, 'Applying the conventional algorithm for fraction addition blindly to all situations involving fractions, without recognizing that the procedure only yields a correct mathematical representation if the addends and result share the same unit of reference.', 'Applying conventional addition to find a baseball player''s overall performance after hitting 1/2 in one game and 1/4 in a second game, rather than using vector addition.', 'fraction', 'addition of fractions', 'reference units', '', '', 'FLM_Mochon_1993_You', 'SIMON MOCHON', '1993', 'For the Learning of Mathematics', 'uncoded', 'primary', '19-20', 'pdfs/FLM/FLM_Mochon_1993_You.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40746, 'Struggling to solve a splitting task that requires recognizing the compositional relationship between partitioning and iterating operations, instead guessing unrelated numbers of partitions.', 'When given a virtual stick and told ''my stick is 4 times as long as your stick. Draw your stick'', the student guessed dividing the stick into eighths and fifths before realizing she needed to divide it into fourths.', 'fraction', 'splitting operation', 'partitioning and iterating', 'mixed', 'upper elementary (grades five and six)', 'FLM_Mccloskey_2012_Caring', 'ANDREA MCCLOSKEY', '2012', 'For the Learning of Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '29, 30', 'pdfs/FLM/FLM_Mccloskey_2012_Caring.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40747, 'Exhibiting inflexibility with referent units by treating the referent unit as a fixed value rather than recognizing it can change depending on the mathematical operation.', 'When interpreting an area model for the division problem 1 ÷ 2/3 (which yields a quotient of 3/2), a teacher claimed the drawing only showed 2/3 of one whole because she expected 3/2 to be represented as more than one unit square, failing to realize the quotient refers to a different whole.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'referent units', 'in_service', 'middle grades', 'MTL_Lee_2011_Mathematics', 'Soo Jin Lee, Rachael Eriksen Brown, Chandra Hawley Orrill', '2011', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'productive', 'primary', '210-211', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Lee_2011_Mathematics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40748, 'Judging the correctness of a drawn representation by looking for superficial perceptual features (e.g., explicit symbols or unshaded divisions) rather than using conceptual operations or quantitative reasoning.', 'Rejecting a valid area model for 1/3 of 3/4 because it did not explicitly show the whole shape divided into thirds, instead insisting on a standard ''double-shaded'' textbook format where both the 1/3 and 3/4 factors were distinctively partitioned.', 'fraction', 'fraction multiplication', 'area models', 'in_service', 'middle grades', 'MTL_Lee_2011_Mathematics', 'Soo Jin Lee, Rachael Eriksen Brown, Chandra Hawley Orrill', '2011', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'productive', 'primary', '211-212', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Lee_2011_Mathematics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40749, 'Interpreting drawn representations by first calculating the numerical solution and then selecting any diagram that matches that final quantity, regardless of whether the diagram accurately models the intended mathematical operation.', 'Selecting a number line that models subtraction (1/4 - 1/5 = 1/20) as the correct representation for a multiplication problem (1/5 * 1/4) simply because the final shaded length corresponded to the answer 1/20.', 'fraction', 'fraction operations', 'drawn representations', 'in_service', 'middle grades', 'MTL_Lee_2011_Mathematics', 'Soo Jin Lee, Rachael Eriksen Brown, Chandra Hawley Orrill', '2011', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'uncoded', 'primary', '213-214', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Lee_2011_Mathematics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40750, 'Over-relying on algorithmic knowledge to interpret drawings, trying to map the steps of an algorithm onto the diagram rather than using the diagram to understand the conceptual situation.', 'When interpreting a number line that modeled a partitive (sharing) division situation for 2/3 ÷ 1/4, teachers immediately applied the ''invert and multiply'' algorithm (2/3 * 4) and then looked for a drawing that showed four groups of 2/3.', 'fraction', 'fraction division', 'algorithmic knowledge', 'in_service', 'middle grades', 'MTL_Lee_2011_Mathematics', 'Soo Jin Lee, Rachael Eriksen Brown, Chandra Hawley Orrill', '2011', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'productive', 'secondary', '214-215', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Lee_2011_Mathematics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40751, 'Attempting to verify the mathematical correctness of a representation by physically measuring it using improvised tools, rather than relying on mathematical relationships.', 'To verify an area model showing 0.3 * 1.2, a teacher folded the bottom of the page to physically mark and count off the width of the unshaded columns.', 'decimal', 'decimal multiplication', 'drawn representations', 'in_service', 'middle grades', 'MTL_Lee_2011_Mathematics', 'Soo Jin Lee, Rachael Eriksen Brown, Chandra Hawley Orrill', '2011', 'Mathematical Thinking and Learning', 'uncoded', 'passing', '215-216', 'pdfs/MTL/MTL_Lee_2011_Mathematics.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40752, 'Performing fraction division by rote application of an algorithm without conceptual understanding of what the algorithm represents or what the numbers stand for.', 'Students ''flip'' the 1/2 in the problem 3 ÷ 1/2 but cannot provide an explanation for why they do it or what the resulting 6 stands for.', 'fraction', 'division of fractions', 'invert and multiply algorithm', 'PST', '', 'FLM_Peretz_2006_Teaching', 'Dvora Peretz', '2006', 'For the Learning of Mathematics', 'deficit', 'passing', '24', 'pdfs/FLM/FLM_Peretz_2006_Teaching.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40753, 'Making subtraction errors while attempting to execute a long division algorithm, leading to convoluted and unusual partial solutions, despite possessing a good underlying understanding of the concept of division.', 'A student (''Anonymous'') solving 3674 ÷ 87 showed correct multiplicative reasoning (e.g., 30 100''s is 3000) but the work broke down into unusual tracking steps like ''130+130+130=390'' due to difficulties with the subtraction portion.', 'whole_number', 'long division', 'subtraction', 'PST', '', 'FLM_Peretz_2006_Teaching', 'Dvora Peretz', '2006', 'For the Learning of Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '27, 28, 29', 'pdfs/FLM/FLM_Peretz_2006_Teaching.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40754, 'Naming a composed fraction in relation to the local number of mini-parts created during problem solving, rather than tracking the parts back to the original global whole.', 'After partitioning 3/4 of a yard into 15 mini-parts and isolating 6 of them, a student calls the result ''six-fifteenths'' instead of ''six-twentieths''.', 'fraction', 'fraction composition', 'identifying the whole', 'student', 'Middle school', 'FLM_Tillema_2011_Developing', 'ERIK TILLEMA, AMY HACKENBERG', '2011', 'For the Learning of Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '31-32', 'pdfs/FLM/FLM_Tillema_2011_Developing.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40755, 'Believing that a repeating decimal is not a legitimate, ''real'', or ''complete'' decimal because it does not terminate, making it impossible to enumerate its parts or perfectly convert it back into a fraction using standard place value methods.', 'Students claiming that 1/3 cannot be turned into a true decimal because the resulting 0.333... ''never ends'' and ''you don''t know how many pieces it is''.', 'decimal', 'repeating decimals', 'definition of a decimal', 'student', 'fifth grade', 'ESM_Mcoconnor_2001_Frcation', 'M.C. O''CONNOR', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '150, 151, 165, 167, 169, 170', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Mcoconnor_2001_Frcation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40756, 'Generalizing that the only fractions that can be converted into decimals are those whose given denominators are explicitly factors of powers of ten.', 'Asserting that 1/3, 1/6, and 1/8 cannot be turned into decimals because their denominators (3, 6, 8) do not divide evenly into 10, 100, or 1000.', 'fraction', 'fraction to decimal conversion', 'denominators as factors of powers of ten', 'student', 'fifth grade', 'ESM_Mcoconnor_2001_Frcation', 'M.C. O''CONNOR', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '150, 153, 154, 155', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Mcoconnor_2001_Frcation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40757, 'Making multi-step equivalency and multiplication errors while attempting to convert fractions into decimals, revealing fragile computational fluency under the cognitive load of a conceptual discussion.', 'A student correctly reducing 6/8 to 3/4, but then erroneously calculating that multiplying the numerator 3 by 20 results in 60, and thus the decimal is .60.', 'fraction', 'equivalent fractions', 'multiplication errors', 'student', 'fifth grade', 'ESM_Mcoconnor_2001_Frcation', 'M.C. O''CONNOR', '2001', 'Educational Studies in Mathematics', 'uncoded', 'secondary', '174, 175', 'pdfs/ESM/ESM_Mcoconnor_2001_Frcation.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40758, 'Confusing the reciprocal of a function (1/f(x)) with the inverse of a function (f^-1(x)) due to applying rules from negative exponents.', 'Students misinterpreting the notation f^-1(x) as 1/f(x).', 'algebraic', 'functions', 'inverse vs reciprocal notation', 'in_service', 'elementary and secondary', 'FLM_Figueiras_2011_Teachers', 'LOURDES FIGUEIRAS, MIGUEL RIBEIRO, JOSÉ CARRILLO, SAÍNZA FERNÁNDEZ, JORDI DEULOFEU', '2011', 'For the Learning of Mathematics', 'productive', 'secondary', '27', 'pdfs/FLM/FLM_Figueiras_2011_Teachers.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40759, 'Assuming that equivalent fractions are strictly characterized by one being the result of multiplying or dividing the numerator and denominator of the other by the exact same integer.', 'Failing to recognize fractions like 4/6 and 6/9 as equivalent because there is no direct integer multiple to get from one to the other.', 'fraction', 'equivalent fractions', 'generating equivalent fractions', 'in_service', 'elementary and secondary', 'FLM_Figueiras_2011_Teachers', 'LOURDES FIGUEIRAS, MIGUEL RIBEIRO, JOSÉ CARRILLO, SAÍNZA FERNÁNDEZ, JORDI DEULOFEU', '2011', 'For the Learning of Mathematics', 'uncoded', 'primary', '27, 28', 'pdfs/FLM/FLM_Figueiras_2011_Teachers.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40760, 'Refusing to accept that the recurring decimal 0.999... is equal to 1, which the authors argue is actually a structurally justified difficulty because 0.999... is inconsistent with the standard division algorithm and numeration system taught in schools.', 'Students accepting expansions such as 0.12341234... = 1234/9999, but refusing to accept that 0.999... = 1.', 'decimal', 'recurring decimals', '0.999... = 1', 'student', 'secondary school', 'FLM_Woo_2008_Revisiting', 'JEONGHO WOO, JAEHOON YIM', '2008', 'For the Learning of Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '12, 13', 'pdfs/FLM/FLM_Woo_2008_Revisiting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40761, 'Believing that rational exponents with negative bases (like (-8)^(1/3)) can be defined unproblematically, leading to contradictions because extending the exponential form to negative bases violates the exponential laws the extension relies upon.', 'Evaluating (-8)^(1/3) as (-8)^(2/6), which leads to ((-8)^2)^(1/6) = 64^(1/6) = 2, directly contradicting the initial definition of (-8)^(1/3) = -2.', 'algebraic', 'rational exponents', 'negative bases', 'student', 'secondary school', 'FLM_Woo_2008_Revisiting', 'JEONGHO WOO, JAEHOON YIM', '2008', 'For the Learning of Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '14, 15', 'pdfs/FLM/FLM_Woo_2008_Revisiting.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40762, 'Failing to identify or track changing referent units during decimal multiplication using an area model, leading to confusion over what fractional parts of the model represent.', 'When asked to draw ''four-tenths of one of these orange things'' (a 2.6-unit block), PSTs were unsure if this meant four individual long units, four-tenths of the whole 3x2.6 array, or four-tenths of a single 1x2.6 rectangle.', 'decimal', 'decimal multiplication', 'referent units / area model', 'PST', 'adult learners / pre-service elementary teachers', 'FLM_Rathouz_2010_Ambiguity', 'MARGARET RATHOUZ', '2010', 'For the Learning of Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '45, 46, 47', 'pdfs/FLM/FLM_Rathouz_2010_Ambiguity.pdf')).
research_corpus_misconception_row(row(40763, 'Struggling to distinguish whether a fraction in a word problem acts as a scalar multiplier on a whole quantity or as a specific measurement unit, causing confusion about which arithmetic operation applies.', 'Debating whether the expression 3 1/2 - 2/3 is matched by a story where a runner stops after ''2/3 of a mile'' (same units, subtraction) or ''2/3 of the way'' (scalar multiplier, requiring multiplication).', 'fraction', 'word problems', 'referent units / operations', 'PST', 'adult learners / pre-service elementary teachers', 'FLM_Rathouz_2010_Ambiguity', 'MARGARET RATHOUZ', '2010', 'For the Learning of Mathematics', 'productive', 'primary', '47, 48, 49', 'pdfs/FLM/FLM_Rathouz_2010_Ambiguity.pdf')).

